/* Pilot questions (Nov 7 2026): new Learn by system questions. Evaluators see them first; they go live for everyone once approved (see pilotStatus). */
DB.PILOT=[{"id":"qp05fce9e5","lv":"aemt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:acls","q":"In cardiac arrest, which effect of epinephrine is mainly responsible for improving coronary perfusion pressure during CPR?","a":"Alpha-adrenergic peripheral vasoconstriction","w":["Beta-2 mediated bronchodilation","Direct electrical conversion of fibrillation","Reduction of myocardial oxygen demand"],"x":"Epinephrine's alpha effect constricts peripheral vessels, raising aortic diastolic pressure and coronary perfusion during compressions. It does not convert ventricular fibrillation; defibrillation does that.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb9ac418e","lv":"aemt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:acls","q":"A patient in cardiac arrest has no peripheral IV, and attempts at access have failed. Which route is an accepted alternative for giving epinephrine during resuscitation?","a":"Intraosseous","w":["Subcutaneous","Sublingual","Oral"],"x":"The intraosseous route reaches the central circulation about as reliably as IV access and is the preferred alternative in arrest. Subcutaneous absorption is unreliable when there is no spontaneous circulation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdcdbf311","lv":"aemt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:acls","q":"For which cardiac arrest rhythm is epinephrine recommended as early as possible, because no shock is indicated?","a":"Asystole","w":["Ventricular fibrillation","Pulseless ventricular tachycardia","Sinus tachycardia"],"x":"In nonshockable rhythms such as asystole and PEA, epinephrine is given as soon as feasible. In VF and pulseless VT, defibrillation comes first and epinephrine follows after initial shocks.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7e69707f","lv":"aemt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:acls","q":"Epinephrine belongs to which drug class?","a":"Sympathomimetic catecholamine","w":["Parasympatholytic anticholinergic","Cardiac glycoside","Beta-adrenergic blocker"],"x":"Epinephrine is an endogenous catecholamine that stimulates alpha and beta adrenergic receptors. Atropine, not epinephrine, is the anticholinergic used in emergency care.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa4f9aa88","lv":"aemt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:acls","q":"After return of spontaneous circulation in a patient who received several doses of epinephrine, which finding is an expected drug effect to monitor for?","a":"Tachycardia and ventricular ectopy","w":["Bradycardia and heart block","Pinpoint pupils and miosis","Bronchospasm and wheezing"],"x":"Epinephrine's beta-1 stimulation raises heart rate and myocardial irritability, so tachycardia and ectopy are common after ROSC. It causes bronchodilation, not bronchospasm.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdc2da8f5","lv":"aemt","d":"aspirin","sys":"c:acs","q":"How does aspirin benefit a patient with a suspected acute coronary syndrome?","a":"It irreversibly inhibits platelet aggregation","w":["It dissolves the existing fibrin clot","It dilates the coronary arteries directly","It lowers myocardial oxygen demand directly"],"x":"Aspirin blocks cyclooxygenase and thromboxane A2 production, keeping platelets from clumping onto the clot. It is not a fibrinolytic and does not break down existing fibrin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc6e5ae4b","lv":"aemt","d":"aspirin","sys":"c:acs","q":"When aspirin is given for suspected acute coronary syndrome, how should the patient take the tablets?","a":"Chew them before swallowing","w":["Swallow them whole with water","Let them dissolve under the tongue","Hold them between cheek and gum"],"x":"Chewing speeds absorption through the oral and gastric mucosa so platelet inhibition begins sooner. Sublingual placement is the route for nitroglycerin, not aspirin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa102105f","lv":"aemt","d":"aspirin","sys":"c:acs","q":"A patient with chest pain suggestive of acute coronary syndrome reports which history that is a contraindication to aspirin?","a":"Known aspirin allergy with prior hives and wheezing","w":["Daily use of a cholesterol-lowering medication","Taking a baby aspirin earlier in the week","History of controlled high blood pressure"],"x":"A true aspirin hypersensitivity is a contraindication because it can trigger urticaria, bronchospasm or anaphylaxis. Prior aspirin use earlier in the week does not prevent its use now.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa1ec9217","lv":"aemt","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:acs","q":"A patient with chest pain has ST elevation in the inferior leads and is hypotensive. Why is nitroglycerin used with great caution in this patient?","a":"Possible right ventricular infarction makes the patient preload dependent","w":["Inferior infarction makes the patient resistant to vasodilators","Nitroglycerin increases the risk of ventricular fibrillation here","Nitroglycerin worsens platelet clumping in inferior infarction"],"x":"Inferior MI often involves the right ventricle, which depends on preload to fill the left heart; venodilation can cause profound hypotension. The concern is preload, not resistance to the drug.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9395e250","lv":"aemt","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:acs","q":"Before each dose of sublingual nitroglycerin for ischemic chest pain, which assessment is most essential?","a":"Blood pressure","w":["Blood glucose level","Pupil size and reactivity","Capillary refill in the toes"],"x":"Nitroglycerin's vasodilation can drop blood pressure sharply, so blood pressure is checked before every dose and reassessed after. Glucose is useful in many patients but does not determine whether nitroglycerin is safe.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8e1b9424","lv":"aemt","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:htn","q":"A hypertensive patient presents with acute pulmonary edema. What is the main way nitroglycerin relieves the respiratory distress?","a":"Venodilation reduces preload and pulmonary congestion","w":["Bronchodilation opens constricted small airways","Diuresis removes excess fluid through the kidneys","Increased contractility strengthens the left ventricle"],"x":"Nitroglycerin dilates veins, pooling blood peripherally and lowering the volume returning to the congested lungs. It is not a diuretic; fluid is redistributed, not excreted.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcfa3df06","lv":"aemt","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:htn","q":"A patient with very high blood pressure and chest pain says he took a medication for erectile dysfunction yesterday. Why is this important before nitroglycerin?","a":"Combined use can cause severe, prolonged hypotension","w":["Combined use can cause a dangerous rise in blood pressure","The combination blocks nitroglycerin from being absorbed","The combination causes rapid bleeding from the gums"],"x":"Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors such as sildenafil and tadalafil potentiate nitrate vasodilation and can cause life-threatening hypotension. The interaction lowers, not raises, blood pressure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp344eb997","lv":"aemt","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:htn","q":"Which side effect is most commonly reported after a patient takes nitroglycerin?","a":"Throbbing headache","w":["Severe constipation","Slowed respirations","Blurred yellow vision"],"x":"Dilation of cerebral blood vessels commonly produces a pounding headache. Yellow-tinted vision is associated with digoxin toxicity, not nitrates.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp75b6be65","lv":"aemt","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:htn","q":"Which teaching point helps a patient keep prescribed sublingual nitroglycerin tablets effective?","a":"Keep them in the original tightly closed container","w":["Store them in a moist bathroom cabinet","Transfer them to a plastic weekly pill box","Keep them open in a sunny window sill"],"x":"Nitroglycerin tablets lose potency with exposure to light, heat, air and moisture, so they belong in the original closed glass container. Plastic pill organizers and humid bathrooms speed degradation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp28e6b233","lv":"aemt","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:htn","q":"A patient becomes lightheaded and hypotensive a few minutes after nitroglycerin. What is the most appropriate interpretation and action?","a":"Recognize an expected vasodilator effect, place supine, and reassess","w":["Recognize an allergic reaction and give glucagon","Recognize hypoglycemia and give oral glucose","Recognize opioid toxicity and give naloxone"],"x":"Nitroglycerin-induced venodilation can cause hypotension; laying the patient flat and reassessing vital signs is appropriate, along with reporting the change. This is a predictable effect, not anaphylaxis or hypoglycemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp503706c7","lv":"aemt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"In anaphylactic shock, which epinephrine effect most directly counters the drop in blood pressure?","a":"Alpha-1 vasoconstriction","w":["Beta-2 bronchodilation","Muscarinic receptor blockade","Histamine-1 receptor blockade"],"x":"Alpha-1 stimulation tightens dilated, leaky vessels and raises systemic vascular resistance. Beta-2 effects open the airways but do not restore blood pressure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1b96dea3","lv":"aemt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"What is the preferred initial route for epinephrine in a patient with anaphylactic shock in the field?","a":"Intramuscular injection in the lateral thigh","w":["Subcutaneous injection in the upper arm","Inhaled by small-volume nebulizer","Sublingual tablet under the tongue"],"x":"Intramuscular injection into the vastus lateralis gives faster, more reliable absorption than subcutaneous injection. Nebulized epinephrine does not treat systemic shock.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa889baa0","lv":"aemt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"A patient in hemorrhagic shock remains hypotensive. Why is epinephrine not the primary treatment?","a":"The problem is lost volume, which needs bleeding control and fluids","w":["Epinephrine is absorbed too slowly in trauma patients","Epinephrine causes too much bronchodilation in trauma","The problem is vasodilation, which needs antihistamines"],"x":"Hemorrhagic shock is a volume problem; controlling bleeding and restoring circulating volume address the cause. Vasoconstrictors cannot fill an empty vascular space.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9a522554","lv":"aemt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"A patient in anaphylaxis takes a beta-blocker daily. How might this affect the response to epinephrine?","a":"The response may be blunted, causing refractory hypotension","w":["The response is usually exaggerated, causing hypertension","The beta-blocker fully prevents any alpha effects","The beta-blocker has no effect on epinephrine response"],"x":"Beta-blockade can blunt epinephrine's cardiac and bronchial effects, leading to persistent hypotension; glucagon is sometimes used in this situation. Alpha effects are not blocked by beta-blockers.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc56d0a98","lv":"aemt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"After epinephrine is given for shock, which combination of reassessments is most appropriate?","a":"Blood pressure, heart rate and rhythm, and mental status","w":["Pupil size, temperature, and capillary glucose only","Skin turgor, bowel sounds, and urine color","Grip strength, pupil size, and pain score"],"x":"Epinephrine changes blood pressure, heart rate and rhythm, and the response should show in perfusion and mental status. The other choices do not track its hemodynamic effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc9c7390d","lv":"aemt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:airway","q":"Albuterol belongs to which drug class?","a":"Short-acting beta-2 agonist","w":["Long-acting anticholinergic","Inhaled corticosteroid","Leukotriene receptor antagonist"],"x":"Albuterol is a short-acting beta-2 agonist used as rescue therapy for bronchospasm. Inhaled corticosteroids control inflammation but do not provide rapid relief.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa42da95e","lv":"aemt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:airway","q":"A patient receiving nebulized albuterol becomes shaky and reports a racing heart. How should these findings be interpreted?","a":"Common expected side effects of beta stimulation","w":["Signs of a severe allergic reaction","Signs of opioid withdrawal","Signs of acute hypoglycemia"],"x":"Tremor and tachycardia are common albuterol effects from beta-receptor stimulation; they should be monitored and reported if significant. They do not indicate an allergy.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5340568a","lv":"aemt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:airway","q":"After a nebulized albuterol treatment for wheezing, which finding best shows the treatment is working?","a":"Improved air movement and decreased work of breathing","w":["Wheezing disappears and breath sounds become absent","Heart rate rises well above baseline","Patient becomes drowsy and difficult to arouse"],"x":"Better air movement with less accessory muscle use shows reversal of bronchospasm. Wheezing that fades into silent breath sounds signals worsening obstruction, not improvement.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6b9456cb","lv":"aemt","d":"ipratropium","sys":"c:airway","q":"How does ipratropium produce bronchodilation?","a":"It blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors in the airways","w":["It stimulates beta-2 receptors in bronchial smooth muscle","It blocks histamine release from mast cells","It reduces airway inflammation like a corticosteroid"],"x":"Ipratropium is an anticholinergic that blocks vagally mediated bronchoconstriction. Beta-2 stimulation is how albuterol works, which is why the two are often combined.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp48933399","lv":"aemt","d":"ipratropium","sys":"c:airway","q":"In the emergency treatment of moderate to severe bronchospasm, ipratropium is most often given in which way?","a":"Nebulized together with albuterol","w":["Injected intramuscularly in the thigh","Chewed and swallowed as a tablet","Injected by slow intravenous push"],"x":"Ipratropium is an inhaled drug and is commonly nebulized with albuterol because the two act on different pathways. It is not given by injection.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc92faa2f","lv":"aemt","d":"ipratropium","sys":"c:airway","q":"Which side effect is most commonly reported with inhaled ipratropium?","a":"Dry mouth","w":["Hypoglycemia","Excess salivation","Slowed heart rate"],"x":"As an anticholinergic, ipratropium reduces secretions and commonly causes dry mouth. Excess salivation would be the opposite, cholinergic effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp255afa6f","lv":"aemt","d":"levalbuterol","sys":"c:airway","q":"How is levalbuterol related to albuterol?","a":"It is the active isomer of albuterol, also a beta-2 agonist","w":["It is a long-acting form used only for prevention","It is an anticholinergic combined with albuterol","It is an inhaled steroid derived from albuterol"],"x":"Levalbuterol is the R-isomer of albuterol and works the same way, relaxing bronchial smooth muscle by stimulating beta-2 receptors. It is used for acute bronchospasm, not as a steroid or anticholinergic.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7dfdd7b5","lv":"aemt","d":"levalbuterol","sys":"c:airway","q":"A patient with asthma receives nebulized levalbuterol. Which adverse effects should be monitored for?","a":"Tachycardia, tremor, and nervousness","w":["Bradycardia, miosis, and drooling","Hypertension, hives, and fever","Constipation, sedation, and slowed breathing"],"x":"Like albuterol, levalbuterol is a beta agonist and can cause tachycardia, tremor and nervousness. Bradycardia and drooling are cholinergic effects not expected with this drug.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpeb55a959","lv":"aemt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:airway","q":"A patient in severe asthma exacerbation is too fatigued to use a nebulizer effectively. Why may intramuscular epinephrine be considered under protocol?","a":"It causes bronchodilation without depending on inhalation","w":["It sedates the patient to reduce air hunger","It reduces airway inflammation over several hours","It thins mucus so it can be coughed up"],"x":"Systemic epinephrine reaches bronchial smooth muscle through the blood, so it works even when air movement is too poor to deliver inhaled drugs. It is not a sedative or anti-inflammatory.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp541eafc7","lv":"aemt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:airway","q":"A child with croup improves after nebulized epinephrine. Why is continued observation important?","a":"Stridor can return as the drug's effect wears off","w":["The drug commonly causes delayed hypoglycemia","The drug commonly causes delayed hives","Stridor typically worsens permanently after epinephrine"],"x":"Nebulized epinephrine shrinks swollen airway tissue only temporarily, so symptoms can return once it wears off. Delayed hypoglycemia and hives are not the expected concern.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6779ed83","lv":"aemt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:allergy","q":"What is the first-line medication for a patient with anaphylaxis?","a":"Epinephrine","w":["Diphenhydramine","Albuterol","Methylprednisolone"],"x":"Epinephrine reverses vasodilation, airway swelling and bronchospasm and is the only first-line drug for anaphylaxis. Antihistamines relieve itching and hives but do not reverse shock or airway compromise.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp94488968","lv":"aemt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:allergy","q":"Where is an epinephrine auto-injector given?","a":"Anterolateral thigh","w":["Deltoid muscle of the arm","Abdominal wall near the navel","Dorsal gluteal muscle"],"x":"Auto-injectors are labeled for the anterolateral thigh, which provides rapid intramuscular absorption and can be reached through clothing. The abdomen is a common site for other injected drugs, not auto-injectors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa64f28a0","lv":"aemt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:allergy","q":"A patient's hives and wheezing resolve after self-administered epinephrine. Why does the patient still need emergency department evaluation?","a":"Symptoms can return as epinephrine wears off or in a biphasic reaction","w":["Epinephrine routinely causes delayed liver injury","Epinephrine must be reversed with an antidote","Hives always progress to shock within one hour"],"x":"Epinephrine is short-acting and symptoms may recur, including in a biphasic reaction hours later, so observation is needed. There is no antidote that must be given after epinephrine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp293310bc","lv":"aemt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:allergy","q":"Which epinephrine effect relieves the upper airway swelling of anaphylaxis?","a":"Vasoconstriction that reduces mucosal edema","w":["Blocking histamine at H1 receptors","Increased mucus production in the airway","Relaxation of the skeletal muscles of the neck"],"x":"Alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction decreases capillary leak and swelling in airway tissues. Blocking H1 receptors is how antihistamines work.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5aea94bd","lv":"aemt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:allergy","q":"A patient with anaphylaxis has received epinephrine but still has wheezing. What role does albuterol play?","a":"An adjunct for persistent bronchospasm","w":["A replacement for epinephrine in anaphylaxis","The primary treatment for hypotension","A treatment for laryngeal swelling"],"x":"Albuterol can relieve lower-airway bronchospasm that persists after epinephrine. It does not raise blood pressure or reduce upper-airway edema, so it never replaces epinephrine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp868d0e50","lv":"aemt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:allergy","q":"Why is albuterol alone inadequate treatment for anaphylaxis?","a":"It does not reverse vasodilation or airway edema","w":["It worsens hives by releasing histamine","It acts too slowly to relieve wheezing","It is only effective in children"],"x":"Albuterol is a selective beta-2 agonist that relaxes bronchial smooth muscle but lacks the alpha effects needed for hypotension and swelling. It acts quickly on wheezing.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcb3812df","lv":"aemt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:allergy","q":"During nebulized albuterol for an allergic reaction, which finding should the AEMT recognize as a common drug effect rather than worsening allergy?","a":"Mild tremor of the hands","w":["New swelling of the tongue","Spreading hives on the chest","Dropping blood pressure"],"x":"Fine tremor is a common beta-agonist effect. Tongue swelling, spreading hives and hypotension suggest progression of the allergic reaction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp81fb174b","lv":"aemt","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:allergy","q":"A patient in anaphylaxis who takes a beta-blocker remains hypotensive after repeated epinephrine. Which medication may be used for this situation?","a":"Glucagon","w":["Naloxone","Ondansetron","Aspirin"],"x":"Glucagon raises heart rate and contractility through a pathway that does not use beta receptors, so it may help when beta-blockade blunts epinephrine. The other drugs do not address this problem.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpff28fd77","lv":"aemt","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:allergy","q":"Why can glucagon work when beta-blockers blunt the response to epinephrine?","a":"It increases cardiac cAMP without using beta receptors","w":["It directly blocks the beta-blocker in the blood","It stimulates alpha receptors more strongly","It speeds the liver's removal of the beta-blocker"],"x":"Glucagon binds its own receptors on heart cells and raises cAMP, bypassing the blocked beta receptors. It does not bind or clear the beta-blocker.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0080dbd5","lv":"aemt","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:allergy","q":"Which common side effect of glucagon requires the AEMT to be prepared to protect the airway?","a":"Vomiting","w":["Bronchospasm","Seizures","Jaw rigidity"],"x":"Nausea and vomiting are frequent after glucagon, so suction and positioning should be ready, especially in a patient with altered mental status. Bronchospasm is not a typical effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa7f1b80b","lv":"aemt","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"c:sedation","q":"Acetaminophen is best described by which set of properties?","a":"Analgesic and antipyretic with little anti-inflammatory effect","w":["Opioid analgesic with strong sedating effects","Anti-inflammatory that strongly inhibits platelets","Local anesthetic that blocks nerve conduction"],"x":"Acetaminophen relieves pain and fever but has minimal anti-inflammatory action and does not affect platelets. NSAIDs such as ibuprofen are the anti-inflammatory choice.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa15171c7","lv":"aemt","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"c:sedation","q":"What is the most serious toxic effect of an acetaminophen overdose?","a":"Liver injury","w":["Respiratory depression","Gastric ulcer bleeding","Bone marrow failure"],"x":"Excess acetaminophen overwhelms normal metabolism and produces a metabolite that damages the liver. Respiratory depression is typical of opioid overdose, not acetaminophen.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0a07789f","lv":"aemt","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"c:sedation","q":"Before giving acetaminophen, which question is most important to ask the patient?","a":"Whether they recently took any product containing acetaminophen","w":["Whether they have ever had a tetanus shot","Whether they are allergic to shellfish or iodine","Whether they have had recent dental work"],"x":"Many cold, sleep and prescription pain combinations contain acetaminophen, so asking prevents unintentional excess intake. Shellfish allergy has no bearing on acetaminophen.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd198ca88","lv":"aemt","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"c:sedation","q":"For a patient with a history of stomach ulcers who needs a mild pain reliever, why might acetaminophen be preferred over an NSAID?","a":"It does not irritate the stomach lining or impair platelets","w":["It is a stronger anti-inflammatory than NSAIDs","It works by coating and protecting the stomach","It is safer than NSAIDs in severe liver disease"],"x":"Acetaminophen lacks the GI irritation and antiplatelet effects of NSAIDs, which can cause ulcer bleeding. It is not safer in severe liver disease, where acetaminophen requires caution.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp910a32ea","lv":"aemt","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"c:sedation","q":"Which patient history calls for the most caution with acetaminophen?","a":"Chronic heavy alcohol use with liver disease","w":["Seasonal allergies treated with nasal spray","Recent ankle sprain with swelling","Well-controlled hypothyroidism"],"x":"Chronic alcohol use and liver disease increase the risk of acetaminophen liver toxicity. The other conditions do not meaningfully raise that risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp782890e5","lv":"aemt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"Why are dextrose-in-water solutions a poor choice for volume replacement in hemorrhagic shock?","a":"Once the sugar is metabolized, the free water leaves the vessels","w":["The sugar binds red cells and blocks oxygen delivery","The solution raises blood pressure too quickly","The solution causes immediate allergic reactions"],"x":"After the dextrose is used by cells, the remaining free water distributes into all body compartments and little stays in the vascular space. Isotonic crystalloids such as normal saline are used for volume.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp16f297e3","lv":"aemt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"Before giving concentrated IV dextrose, why must the AEMT confirm that the IV line is patent and well secured?","a":"Extravasation can cause severe tissue damage","w":["Dextrose clots quickly inside the IV catheter","Dextrose cannot be pushed through a saline lock","A patent line prevents low blood sugar from recurring"],"x":"Hypertonic dextrose is irritating, and if it leaks into tissue it can cause necrosis. Checking flow and flushing before and during administration reduces that risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbab24194","lv":"aemt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"During IV dextrose administration, the site becomes swollen, cool, and painful. What should the AEMT recognize?","a":"Infiltration of the IV","w":["An expected drug effect","Hyperglycemic crisis","Fluid volume overload"],"x":"Swelling, coolness and pain at the site indicate the solution is entering tissue rather than the vein, which must be recognized and managed per protocol. These are local signs, not systemic overload.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0c68e547","lv":"aemt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"Why is a large, proximal vein preferred when giving concentrated dextrose?","a":"Hypertonic solution irritates small veins","w":["Dextrose is absorbed only by large veins","Small veins remove dextrose too quickly","Large veins prevent rebound hyperglycemia"],"x":"Concentrated dextrose is hypertonic and can cause phlebitis; larger veins with more blood flow dilute it faster. Vein size does not change how the body absorbs glucose.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp59a863c8","lv":"aemt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"After IV dextrose is given for hypoglycemia, which assessment confirms the treatment effect?","a":"Repeat blood glucose with mental status check","w":["Repeat pupil check with grip strength","Repeat lung sounds with oxygen saturation","Repeat skin turgor with urine output"],"x":"Rechecking the glucose level and the patient's mental status shows whether hypoglycemia has been corrected. Lung sounds and urine output do not reflect the response to dextrose.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6725d8b3","lv":"aemt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:lytes","q":"How does albuterol lower serum potassium in hyperkalemia?","a":"It shifts potassium from the blood into cells","w":["It increases potassium excretion in urine","It binds potassium in the intestines","It removes potassium through the lungs"],"x":"Beta-2 stimulation drives potassium into cells, temporarily lowering serum levels. It does not remove potassium from the body, which requires the kidneys, binders or dialysis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp407b2ad9","lv":"aemt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:lytes","q":"Why is albuterol considered only a temporary measure for hyperkalemia?","a":"Total body potassium is unchanged and levels can rebound","w":["Its effect lasts only seconds after inhalation","It causes the kidneys to retain more potassium","It is quickly neutralized by stomach acid"],"x":"Albuterol moves potassium into cells but does not eliminate it, so serum levels can rise again as the effect fades. Definitive removal is needed afterward.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp583ec98e","lv":"aemt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:lytes","q":"Which patient is at highest risk for hyperkalemia, a condition sometimes treated with albuterol?","a":"A dialysis patient who missed treatments","w":["A runner with prolonged vomiting","A patient taking a loop diuretic","A patient with ongoing diarrhea"],"x":"Kidney failure with missed dialysis prevents potassium excretion, causing hyperkalemia. Vomiting, diarrhea and loop diuretics tend to cause low potassium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7aa3e173","lv":"aemt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:lytes","q":"Repeated high-dose albuterol can cause which electrolyte disturbance?","a":"Hypokalemia","w":["Hypernatremia","Hypercalcemia","Hypermagnesemia"],"x":"Because albuterol shifts potassium into cells, heavy use can lower serum potassium. It does not raise sodium, calcium or magnesium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8916d466","lv":"aemt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:lytes","q":"While albuterol is given to a patient with suspected hyperkalemia, which monitoring is most important?","a":"Continuous cardiac rhythm and heart rate","w":["Hourly temperature measurements","Pupil size and reactivity","Peak flow every few minutes"],"x":"Potassium abnormalities cause dysrhythmias, and albuterol itself raises heart rate, so cardiac monitoring is essential. Peak flow measures airflow, not the potassium effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6282570b","lv":"aemt","d":"naloxone","sys":"c:tox","q":"How does naloxone reverse opioid toxicity?","a":"It competitively blocks opioid receptors","w":["It speeds liver breakdown of the opioid","It binds the opioid in the stomach","It directly stimulates the breathing center"],"x":"Naloxone displaces opioids from mu receptors and blocks them, reversing respiratory and CNS depression. It does not change how quickly the opioid is metabolized.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1c428a0e","lv":"aemt","d":"naloxone","sys":"c:tox","q":"When giving naloxone to an unresponsive patient with suspected opioid overdose, what is the primary goal?","a":"Restore adequate spontaneous breathing","w":["Restore full alertness and orientation","Induce vomiting of any pills swallowed","Lower the heart rate to normal"],"x":"The aim is adequate ventilation; full awakening is unnecessary and risks abrupt withdrawal and agitation. Naloxone does not cause vomiting of pills.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp51d51908","lv":"aemt","d":"naloxone","sys":"c:tox","q":"After naloxone reverses an opioid overdose, why must the patient be monitored closely?","a":"Naloxone may wear off before the opioid, causing re-sedation","w":["Naloxone often causes delayed liver failure","Naloxone often causes delayed anaphylaxis","Naloxone causes a late drop in blood glucose"],"x":"Many opioids, especially long-acting or high-potency ones, outlast naloxone, so respiratory depression can return. Liver failure and hypoglycemia are not expected naloxone effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8dd603c7","lv":"aemt","d":"naloxone","sys":"c:tox","q":"A patient who uses opioids daily becomes agitated, vomits, and has goosebumps right after naloxone. What should the AEMT recognize?","a":"Acute opioid withdrawal","w":["Allergic reaction to naloxone","Serotonin syndrome","Hypoglycemic reaction"],"x":"Naloxone abruptly reverses opioid effects in a dependent patient, producing withdrawal with agitation, vomiting and piloerection. These signs are not an allergic reaction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa21f3e66","lv":"aemt","d":"activated-charcoal","sys":"c:tox","q":"How does activated charcoal reduce the absorption of an ingested poison?","a":"It adsorbs the toxin on its surface in the gut","w":["It neutralizes acids by chemical reaction","It induces vomiting of stomach contents","It blocks the toxin at its receptor site"],"x":"Activated charcoal's large surface area binds many drugs in the GI tract so less is absorbed. It is not an emetic or a receptor antagonist.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7037ae26","lv":"aemt","d":"activated-charcoal","sys":"c:tox","q":"Which patient should NOT receive activated charcoal?","a":"A drowsy patient who cannot protect the airway","w":["An alert adult who swallowed pills recently","An alert patient who took an acetaminophen overdose","An alert patient with nausea but no vomiting"],"x":"Charcoal given to a patient with decreased consciousness or an unprotected airway risks aspiration. An alert patient with a recent ingestion is the typical candidate if ordered.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc8af4c37","lv":"aemt","d":"activated-charcoal","sys":"c:tox","q":"Activated charcoal binds poorly to which ingested substance?","a":"Iron","w":["Aspirin","Acetaminophen","Tricyclic antidepressants"],"x":"Metals such as iron and lithium, alcohols and caustics are poorly adsorbed by charcoal. Aspirin, acetaminophen and tricyclics bind well.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd4d749d9","lv":"aemt","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:tox","q":"Glucagon may be used as an antidote for an overdose of which medication class?","a":"Beta-blockers","w":["Benzodiazepines","Opioids","Anticholinergics"],"x":"Glucagon raises heart rate and contractility through its own receptors, bypassing blocked beta receptors in beta-blocker overdose. Opioid overdose is reversed with naloxone.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc5f36b06","lv":"aemt","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:tox","q":"A patient with beta-blocker overdose has bradycardia and hypotension. Which effect of glucagon makes it useful?","a":"Increases heart rate and contractility","w":["Lowers heart rate and contractility","Causes peripheral vasodilation","Blocks calcium entry into cardiac cells"],"x":"Glucagon has positive chronotropic and inotropic effects independent of beta receptors, countering beta-blocker toxicity. Blocking calcium entry would worsen the problem.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3b101b0b","lv":"aemt","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:tox","q":"When glucagon is given for beta-blocker toxicity, which side effect should the AEMT be prepared to manage?","a":"Vomiting with aspiration risk","w":["Severe bronchospasm","Muscle rigidity","Pinpoint pupils"],"x":"Vomiting is a common glucagon effect, so suction and airway positioning should be ready. Bronchospasm and pinpoint pupils are not expected glucagon effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfb900d56","lv":"aemt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:endo","q":"Before IV dextrose is given to a patient with altered mental status, which finding should be confirmed?","a":"A low blood glucose reading","w":["A high blood glucose reading","A normal blood pressure","A slow pulse rate"],"x":"Dextrose is indicated for documented hypoglycemia, so a glucose check guides its use. Blood pressure and pulse do not determine the need for dextrose.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpae25f7cd","lv":"aemt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:endo","q":"Why is IV dextrose preferred over oral glucose for a hypoglycemic patient who is unresponsive?","a":"The patient cannot safely swallow, risking aspiration","w":["Oral glucose raises blood sugar too much","Oral glucose is only for children","IV dextrose lasts longer than any food"],"x":"Oral glucose requires an intact gag reflex and ability to swallow; an unresponsive patient could aspirate. IV dextrose bypasses the airway.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc6915c15","lv":"aemt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:endo","q":"A known diabetic is confused, pale, and sweaty. Which finding most strongly supports hypoglycemia as the cause?","a":"Rapid onset after skipping a meal following insulin","w":["Gradual onset over days with heavy thirst","Fruity breath with deep, rapid breathing","Frequent urination with dry mucous membranes"],"x":"Taking insulin without eating commonly causes rapid hypoglycemia with diaphoresis and confusion, which dextrose treats. Fruity breath and Kussmaul breathing suggest DKA.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp59bb0e7f","lv":"aemt","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:endo","q":"A hypoglycemic patient is unresponsive and no IV access can be obtained. How can glucagon be given?","a":"Intramuscularly or by the intranasal form","w":["Orally as a chewable tablet","Sublingually as a dissolving film","Rectally as a suppository"],"x":"Glucagon is available for IM injection and as an intranasal powder, so it can be given without IV access. It is not effective by mouth.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb5a7ec32","lv":"aemt","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:endo","q":"Why may glucagon be less effective in a malnourished patient with chronic alcohol use?","a":"Liver glycogen stores may be depleted","w":["Alcohol blocks glucagon receptors directly","Alcohol speeds glucagon excretion by kidneys","Alcohol inactivates glucagon in the blood"],"x":"Glucagon raises blood sugar by releasing glucose from liver glycogen; if those stores are low, the response is weak. IV dextrose supplies glucose directly.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc683aadc","lv":"aemt","d":"ondansetron","sys":"c:gi","q":"Ondansetron belongs to which drug class?","a":"Serotonin 5-HT3 receptor antagonist","w":["Dopamine D2 receptor agonist","Histamine H2 receptor blocker","Proton pump inhibitor"],"x":"Ondansetron blocks serotonin 5-HT3 receptors in the gut and brainstem vomiting center. H2 blockers and proton pump inhibitors reduce acid but do not treat nausea directly.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp296b6b9f","lv":"aemt","d":"ondansetron","sys":"c:gi","q":"Which cardiac effect of ondansetron is listed as a warning in its labeling?","a":"QT interval prolongation","w":["Sudden heart block","Marked hypertension","ST segment elevation"],"x":"Ondansetron can prolong the QT interval and, rarely, cause torsades de pointes, so caution is needed in patients with long QT or electrolyte problems. ST elevation is not an expected effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp862dea2a","lv":"aemt","d":"ondansetron","sys":"c:gi","q":"How should an orally disintegrating ondansetron tablet be given?","a":"Placed on the tongue to dissolve, then swallowed","w":["Crushed and inhaled through a nebulizer","Swallowed whole with a large glass of water","Chewed thoroughly with a meal"],"x":"The orally disintegrating tablet dissolves on the tongue in seconds and is swallowed with saliva, useful for nauseated patients. It does not need to be swallowed whole with water.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf623152b","lv":"aemt","d":"ondansetron","sys":"c:gi","q":"Which side effect is commonly reported after ondansetron?","a":"Headache","w":["Hallucinations","Hypoglycemia","Excess tearing"],"x":"Headache is among the most common ondansetron side effects; constipation is another. Hallucinations are not a typical effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd3c34ca4","lv":"aemt","d":"ondansetron","sys":"c:gi","q":"Which patient history calls for the most caution before ondansetron?","a":"Congenital long QT syndrome","w":["Seasonal pollen allergies","Recent knee replacement surgery","Well-controlled asthma"],"x":"Ondansetron's labeling advises avoiding it in congenital long QT syndrome because of the risk of dangerous rhythms. The other histories do not raise this risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7fc3d6be","lv":"aemt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:peds","q":"A child stung by a bee develops hives, wheezing, and lethargy. What is the priority medication?","a":"Intramuscular epinephrine","w":["Oral diphenhydramine","Nebulized ipratropium","Oral acetaminophen"],"x":"Hives with respiratory involvement and altered mental status indicate anaphylaxis, for which IM epinephrine is first-line. Antihistamines do not reverse airway or circulatory compromise.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf2c87d47","lv":"aemt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:peds","q":"When helping a parent use a child's epinephrine auto-injector, which teaching point is correct?","a":"Press firmly into the outer thigh for the labeled time","w":["Inject into the buttock for faster absorption","Remove clothing completely before injecting","Inject into the upper arm muscle"],"x":"Auto-injectors go into the anterolateral thigh and are held in place for the labeled time so the full amount is delivered; they can be given through clothing. Labeling warns against injecting into the buttocks.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcdbbe7f7","lv":"aemt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:peds","q":"An infant is jittery, poorly feeding, and has a weak cry. Which assessment should guide whether dextrose is given?","a":"Blood glucose measurement","w":["Temperature measurement","Head circumference","Pupil response"],"x":"Signs of hypoglycemia in infants are vague, so a glucose check confirms the need for dextrose. Temperature and head size do not establish hypoglycemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6c7dc5c9","lv":"aemt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:peds","q":"A school-age child with asthma is wheezing. Which device helps the child get more albuterol into the lungs from a metered-dose inhaler?","a":"A spacer attached to the inhaler","w":["A nasal cannula","A bag-valve mask with reservoir","A peak flow meter"],"x":"A spacer keeps the medication in a chamber so the child can inhale it with less need for coordination, improving delivery to the lungs. A peak flow meter measures airflow but delivers no medication.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd8cd717c","lv":"aemt","d":"naloxone","sys":"c:peds","q":"A toddler is found drowsy with slow, shallow breathing and pinpoint pupils near a grandparent's pill bottle. Which medication addresses the likely cause?","a":"Naloxone","w":["Glucagon","Ondansetron","Albuterol"],"x":"Pinpoint pupils with respiratory depression suggest opioid toxicity, which naloxone reverses; ventilation support comes first. Glucagon is for hypoglycemia or beta-blocker toxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp67622ebb","lv":"aemt","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:peds","q":"A child with type 1 diabetes is unresponsive with a low glucose reading and no IV access. Which medication can be given?","a":"Glucagon","w":["Oral glucose gel","Ondansetron","Activated charcoal"],"x":"Glucagon by IM injection or intranasally raises blood sugar without IV access. Oral glucose is unsafe because an unresponsive child cannot protect the airway.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2203974d","lv":"aemt","d":"ondansetron","sys":"c:peds","q":"Why is ondansetron given to some children with vomiting from gastroenteritis?","a":"To reduce vomiting so oral rehydration can succeed","w":["To cure the underlying viral infection","To slow diarrhea by firming the stool","To treat dehydration directly by adding fluid"],"x":"Controlling vomiting allows the child to keep down oral fluids and may avoid IV therapy. Ondansetron does not treat the infection or replace fluid itself.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc08df65e","lv":"aemt","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"c:peds","q":"Why is acetaminophen preferred over aspirin for fever in a child with a viral illness?","a":"Aspirin is linked to Reye syndrome in children","w":["Aspirin does not reduce fever in children","Acetaminophen also treats the viral infection","Acetaminophen has stronger anti-inflammatory effects"],"x":"Aspirin use in children with viral illnesses such as influenza or chickenpox is associated with Reye syndrome, a dangerous liver and brain disorder. Acetaminophen does not treat the infection.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6aa629cf","lv":"aemt","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"c:peds","q":"Which teaching point helps a parent avoid giving too much liquid acetaminophen to a child?","a":"Use the measuring device that comes with the product","w":["Use a household kitchen spoon","Mix it into a full bottle of formula","Give an adult tablet cut into pieces"],"x":"The dosing device packaged with the product matches its labeled concentration and reduces measuring errors. Kitchen spoons vary in size and commonly lead to dosing mistakes.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2e07adc9","lv":"aemt","d":"ibuprofen","sys":"c:peds","q":"A child has had several days of vomiting and diarrhea with very little urine output. Why is ibuprofen a concern?","a":"Dehydration increases its risk of kidney injury","w":["Dehydration makes it cause liver failure","It worsens diarrhea by speeding the gut","It causes dangerous drops in blood sugar"],"x":"NSAIDs reduce blood flow to the kidneys, and in a dehydrated child this can contribute to acute kidney injury. Liver failure is the major concern with acetaminophen overdose, not ibuprofen.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfdfdb9e6","lv":"cna","d":"amlodipine","sys":"cardio","q":"A resident takes amlodipine for high blood pressure. Which change is a common side effect a nursing assistant should report to the nurse?","a":"Swelling in the ankles and feet","w":["Bright orange-colored urine","Increased hunger at night","White patches inside the mouth"],"x":"Amlodipine often causes swelling (edema) in the ankles and feet, which should be reported. It does not change urine color or cause mouth patches.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6a733882","lv":"cna","d":"atorvastatin","sys":"cardio","q":"A resident who takes atorvastatin to lower cholesterol complains of new, unexplained muscle pain and weakness. What should the nursing assistant do?","a":"Report the muscle pain to the nurse","w":["Give the resident a warm bath and recheck tomorrow","Tell the resident this is normal for everyone","Encourage extra exercise to work out the soreness"],"x":"Statins like atorvastatin can rarely cause serious muscle damage, so new muscle pain or weakness must be reported. It should not be brushed off as normal soreness.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe6d2b0bf","lv":"cna","d":"digoxin","sys":"cardio","q":"A resident on digoxin says food has lost its appeal, feels sick to the stomach, and sees yellow-green halos around lights. What do these changes suggest?","a":"Possible signs of too much digoxin","w":["Expected signs the medicine is working","Normal changes from aging eyes","Signs of a mild seasonal allergy"],"x":"Nausea, loss of appetite, and vision changes such as yellow-green halos can mean digoxin levels are too high. These must be reported to the nurse right away, not treated as normal aging.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4fe1f3b4","lv":"cna","d":"furosemide","sys":"cardio","q":"Why is furosemide usually given in the morning rather than at bedtime?","a":"It causes frequent urination for several hours","w":["It works better on an empty stomach at night","It causes sleepiness during the day","It must be taken with the evening meal"],"x":"Furosemide is a strong water pill that makes the person urinate a lot for several hours. Morning timing helps avoid nighttime trips to the bathroom and falls.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa6773b7c","lv":"cna","d":"hydrochlorothiazide","sys":"cardio","q":"A resident who takes hydrochlorothiazide feels lightheaded when getting out of bed. What is the safest action for the nursing assistant?","a":"Have the resident sit on the edge of the bed before standing","w":["Help the resident stand up quickly to get it over with","Have the resident walk to the bathroom alone","Lay the resident flat and leave the room"],"x":"Water pills can lower blood pressure, especially when standing up. Sitting first and rising slowly lowers fall risk; standing quickly makes dizziness worse.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp47b4e869","lv":"cna","d":"lisinopril","sys":"cardio","q":"A resident who recently started lisinopril has a dry, tickly cough that will not go away. What should the nursing assistant know?","a":"A dry cough is a known side effect to report","w":["A dry cough means the dose is working well","A dry cough is caused only by a cold","A dry cough means the resident needs more fluids"],"x":"Lisinopril (an ACE inhibitor) commonly causes a dry, nagging cough, which should be reported to the nurse. It is not a sign the medicine is working.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf68d4d46","lv":"cna","d":"lisinopril","sys":"cardio","q":"A resident taking lisinopril suddenly has swelling of the lips, tongue, and face. What should the nursing assistant do?","a":"Call for the nurse right away and stay with the resident","w":["Offer cold water and check back in an hour","Apply lotion to the lips and face","Write it down to report at the end of the shift"],"x":"Swelling of the face, lips, or tongue (angioedema) is a rare but dangerous reaction to lisinopril that can block the airway. It is an emergency, not something to report later.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp30559f66","lv":"cna","d":"metoprolol","sys":"cardio","q":"When taking vital signs on a resident who takes metoprolol, which finding is most important to report to the nurse?","a":"A pulse that is much slower than usual","w":["A pulse that is the same as yesterday","A temperature that is normal","A blood pressure that is unchanged"],"x":"Metoprolol slows the heart rate, so an unusually slow pulse can mean too strong an effect. Readings that match the resident's normal values are not a concern.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf1ae52ba","lv":"cna","d":"metoprolol","sys":"cardio","q":"Metoprolol belongs to which group of heart medicines?","a":"Beta blockers","w":["Water pills (diuretics)","Blood thinners","Cholesterol-lowering statins"],"x":"Metoprolol is a beta blocker that slows the heart and lowers blood pressure. It is not a water pill; furosemide and hydrochlorothiazide are diuretics.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpadfbc09a","lv":"cna","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"cardio","q":"A resident with chest pain is given nitroglycerin by the nurse. Which side effect is common and should be expected?","a":"Headache and feeling lightheaded","w":["Increased appetite and thirst","Itchy rash on the arms","Dark brown urine"],"x":"Nitroglycerin widens blood vessels, which commonly causes headache and dizziness from lower blood pressure. The resident should stay seated or lying down to prevent falls.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp23004356","lv":"cna","d":"digoxin","sys":"shock","q":"What is digoxin commonly used to treat?","a":"Heart failure and certain irregular heartbeats","w":["High cholesterol and fatty blood","Asthma and other breathing problems","Low thyroid hormone levels"],"x":"Digoxin helps the heart pump more strongly and can slow some irregular heart rhythms like atrial fibrillation. It is not a cholesterol or breathing medicine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbbb9b0fa","lv":"cna","d":"digoxin","sys":"shock","q":"Before a resident's morning digoxin, the nursing assistant counts an apical pulse that is much lower than the resident's usual rate. What should the nursing assistant do?","a":"Report the pulse to the nurse before the medication pass","w":["Have the resident walk to raise the pulse","Recheck the pulse tomorrow morning","Record it without telling anyone"],"x":"Digoxin slows the heart rate, and a low pulse can be a sign of too much medicine. The nurse needs this information right away to decide what to do.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp84e8c264","lv":"cna","d":"apixaban","sys":"blood","q":"What is apixaban given for?","a":"To help prevent blood clots and stroke","w":["To raise a low red blood cell count","To lower high blood sugar","To relieve joint pain and swelling"],"x":"Apixaban is a blood thinner (anticoagulant) used to prevent clots, such as strokes in atrial fibrillation or clots in the legs and lungs. It does not treat anemia or pain.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd5677436","lv":"cna","d":"apixaban","sys":"blood","q":"A resident who takes apixaban slips and falls but says they feel fine and has no visible cuts. What should the nursing assistant do?","a":"Report the fall to the nurse right away","w":["Help the resident up and not mention it","Report it only if a bruise appears tomorrow","Give the resident an ice pack and leave"],"x":"People on blood thinners can bleed inside the body, even in the head, without visible injury. Every fall must be reported right away so the nurse can assess.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpca3de057","lv":"cna","d":"aspirin","sys":"blood","q":"A resident takes daily low-dose aspirin. Which stool change should be reported to the nurse?","a":"Black, tarry stools","w":["Soft, formed brown stools","One bowel movement a day","Stool that is light brown"],"x":"Aspirin can irritate the stomach and cause bleeding, which may show up as black, tarry stools. Normal soft brown stools are not a concern.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1ac3f792","lv":"cna","d":"aspirin","sys":"blood","q":"Why do many people with heart disease take a daily low-dose aspirin?","a":"To help keep platelets from forming clots","w":["To lower cholesterol in the blood","To make the heart beat slower","To help the body get rid of fluid"],"x":"Aspirin makes platelets less sticky, which helps prevent clots that cause heart attacks and strokes. It does not lower cholesterol or remove fluid.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp20cedac1","lv":"cna","d":"clopidogrel","sys":"blood","q":"Clopidogrel belongs to which group of medicines?","a":"Antiplatelet medicines (blood thinners)","w":["Antibiotics that fight infection","Water pills (diuretics)","Medicines that raise blood pressure"],"x":"Clopidogrel keeps platelets from sticking together, so it is an antiplatelet blood thinner. It is often given after a stent or stroke.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpec126fd4","lv":"cna","d":"clopidogrel","sys":"blood","q":"A resident on clopidogrel needs to be shaved. Which is the safest choice?","a":"Use an electric razor","w":["Use a straight-edge razor","Use a new disposable blade razor","Shave against the hair growth quickly"],"x":"People taking blood thinners bleed more easily from small cuts. An electric razor lowers the chance of nicks compared with blade razors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa4e9546e","lv":"cna","d":"ferrous-sulfate","sys":"blood","q":"A resident recently started ferrous sulfate (iron). Which change in stool is a common, expected effect of iron pills?","a":"Dark green or black color","w":["Bright red streaks of blood","Pale clay color","Very loose watery stool"],"x":"Iron pills commonly turn stool dark green or black. Bright red blood is not caused by iron and should always be reported.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc9761f2e","lv":"cna","d":"ferrous-sulfate","sys":"blood","q":"Iron pills often cause which bowel problem that the nursing assistant should watch for and report?","a":"Constipation","w":["Bowel incontinence","Bright yellow stools","Rectal itching"],"x":"Constipation is a very common side effect of iron pills. Tracking bowel movements and encouraging fluids and fiber as allowed by the care plan helps.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp725401f5","lv":"cna","d":"warfarin","sys":"blood","q":"A resident who takes warfarin has pink urine, bleeding gums, and new large bruises. What should the nursing assistant do?","a":"Report these signs of bleeding to the nurse","w":["Give the resident extra fluids and recheck later","Treat the bruises with a heating pad","Record it as a normal effect of warfarin"],"x":"Pink or red urine, bleeding gums, and unusual bruising can be signs warfarin is causing bleeding. These need to be reported, not treated as normal.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbd852c93","lv":"cna","d":"warfarin","sys":"blood","q":"A resident who takes warfarin suddenly starts eating much larger amounts of green leafy vegetables like spinach and kale. Why should this be shared with the nurse?","a":"Vitamin K in these foods can change how warfarin works","w":["These foods make warfarin cause more headaches","These foods turn warfarin into a laxative","These foods make warfarin taste bitter"],"x":"Green leafy vegetables are high in vitamin K, which works against warfarin. People on warfarin should keep the amount of these foods steady, so big changes matter.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3f8a6adc","lv":"cna","d":"albuterol","sys":"resp","q":"What does an albuterol inhaler do?","a":"Quickly relaxes and opens the airways","w":["Slowly reduces swelling over weeks","Loosens and thins thick mucus","Kills bacteria in the lungs"],"x":"Albuterol is a quick-relief (rescue) inhaler that opens tight airways within minutes. It is not a long-term anti-swelling medicine or an antibiotic.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp12fe194f","lv":"cna","d":"albuterol","sys":"resp","q":"After using an albuterol inhaler, a resident says they feel shaky and their heart is beating fast. What should the nursing assistant know?","a":"These are common side effects to report to the nurse","w":["These mean the resident is allergic to air","These mean the inhaler is empty","These are signs of low blood sugar only"],"x":"Albuterol commonly causes shakiness and a fast heartbeat. These should be reported so the nurse can check the resident, especially if they are strong or do not go away.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe75d7092","lv":"cna","d":"fluticasone","sys":"resp","q":"After a resident uses a fluticasone inhaler, why is rinsing the mouth with water and spitting encouraged?","a":"To help prevent a fungal mouth infection (thrush)","w":["To make the medicine work faster","To prevent the teeth from staining","To keep the throat from getting too cold"],"x":"Fluticasone is an inhaled steroid that can leave medicine in the mouth and lead to thrush. Rinsing and spitting lowers that risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4dedd322","lv":"cna","d":"fluticasone","sys":"resp","q":"A resident with asthma has a fluticasone inhaler and an albuterol inhaler. Which is true about the fluticasone inhaler?","a":"It is used every day to control swelling, not for sudden attacks","w":["It is the rescue inhaler for sudden attacks","It is only used when the resident has a cold","It works within seconds to open the airways"],"x":"Fluticasone is a controller medicine that reduces airway swelling over time. Albuterol, not fluticasone, is the quick-relief rescue inhaler.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe4cefa7e","lv":"cna","d":"guaifenesin","sys":"resp","q":"What is guaifenesin used for?","a":"To loosen mucus so it is easier to cough up","w":["To stop all coughing completely","To open the airways during an asthma attack","To treat a bacterial lung infection"],"x":"Guaifenesin is an expectorant that thins mucus so coughs bring it up more easily. It does not treat infection or open the airways.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpde77e684","lv":"cna","d":"guaifenesin","sys":"resp","q":"A resident is taking guaifenesin for chest congestion. Which daily care action helps this medicine work best, if allowed by the care plan?","a":"Encouraging the resident to drink fluids","w":["Keeping the resident lying flat all day","Limiting fluids to keep mucus thick","Keeping the room very dry and warm"],"x":"Drinking enough fluids helps thin mucus, which supports what guaifenesin does. Limiting fluids or lying flat makes mucus harder to clear.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp87a5e1dd","lv":"cna","d":"levocetirizine","sys":"resp","q":"What is levocetirizine mainly used to treat?","a":"Allergy symptoms like sneezing and a runny nose","w":["Chest pain from heart disease","Constipation and bloating","Pain and swelling in the joints"],"x":"Levocetirizine is an antihistamine that relieves allergy symptoms such as sneezing, runny nose, and itchy eyes. It is not a heart, bowel, or pain medicine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpaa6102af","lv":"cna","d":"levocetirizine","sys":"resp","q":"A resident who started levocetirizine seems more sleepy than usual. How might this affect daily care?","a":"The resident may need extra help to prevent falls","w":["The resident needs less help with walking","The resident should skip meals until alert","The resident will need extra salt in meals"],"x":"Levocetirizine can cause drowsiness, which raises fall risk. Sleepiness should be reported and the resident may need closer help with walking and transfers.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0e78dfff","lv":"cna","d":"prednisone","sys":"resp","q":"A resident with diabetes is taking prednisone for a lung flare-up. Which change is most likely to happen?","a":"Blood sugar levels may go up","w":["Blood sugar levels will drop very low","The resident will lose their appetite","The resident's skin will become oily"],"x":"Steroids like prednisone raise blood sugar, which is especially important in people with diabetes. Prednisone usually increases, rather than decreases, appetite.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdff9cb60","lv":"cna","d":"prednisone","sys":"resp","q":"Prednisone belongs to which group of medicines?","a":"Steroids (corticosteroids)","w":["Antibiotics","Antihistamines","Opioid pain relievers"],"x":"Prednisone is a corticosteroid that reduces swelling and calms the immune system. It is not an antibiotic, so it does not kill bacteria.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpae8f75f1","lv":"cna","d":"carbidopa-levodopa","sys":"neuro","q":"What is carbidopa/levodopa used to treat?","a":"Parkinson's disease symptoms like tremor and stiffness","w":["Memory loss from Alzheimer's disease","Seizures from epilepsy","Dizziness from inner ear problems"],"x":"Carbidopa/levodopa replaces dopamine in the brain to ease Parkinson's symptoms such as shaking, stiffness, and slow movement. It does not treat memory loss or seizures.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp30190dbf","lv":"cna","d":"carbidopa-levodopa","sys":"neuro","q":"A resident who takes carbidopa/levodopa has new jerky, twisting movements of the arms and face that they cannot control. What should the nursing assistant do?","a":"Report the new movements to the nurse","w":["Restrain the arms to keep them still","Ignore it because it is part of aging","Encourage the resident to sleep it off"],"x":"Uncontrolled jerky or twisting movements (dyskinesias) are a known side effect of carbidopa/levodopa and need to be reported. Restraining the resident is unsafe.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5bb3221a","lv":"cna","d":"donepezil","sys":"neuro","q":"What is donepezil used for?","a":"To help with memory and thinking in Alzheimer's dementia","w":["To cure Alzheimer's disease completely","To treat seizures in older adults","To help people fall asleep at night"],"x":"Donepezil may help memory and thinking symptoms of Alzheimer's dementia, but it does not cure the disease. It is not a seizure or sleep medicine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp130aa959","lv":"cna","d":"donepezil","sys":"neuro","q":"Which side effects of donepezil should a nursing assistant watch for and report?","a":"Nausea, diarrhea, and a slow pulse or fainting","w":["Increased hair growth and acne","Bright red urine and nosebleeds","Very fast pulse and sweating"],"x":"Donepezil commonly causes stomach upset such as nausea and diarrhea, and can slow the heart rate, leading to fainting. These should be reported.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfabfe7ec","lv":"cna","d":"gabapentin","sys":"neuro","q":"Gabapentin is used for which conditions?","a":"Certain seizures and nerve pain","w":["High blood pressure and heart failure","Bacterial infections of the skin","Low thyroid and weight gain"],"x":"Gabapentin is used to help control certain seizures and to treat nerve pain, such as pain after shingles. It is not a blood pressure medicine or antibiotic.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd70d2a8d","lv":"cna","d":"gabapentin","sys":"neuro","q":"An older resident who takes gabapentin is dizzy and sleepy. Which safety step is most important?","a":"Help with walking and keep the call light within reach","w":["Have the resident walk alone to stay strong","Raise the bed to its highest position","Remove the resident's walker from the room"],"x":"Gabapentin commonly causes dizziness and sleepiness, raising fall risk. Assistance with walking and a call light within reach help prevent falls.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp47f80962","lv":"cna","d":"lorazepam","sys":"neuro","q":"Lorazepam belongs to which group of medicines?","a":"Benzodiazepines","w":["Antibiotics","Antidepressants","Blood thinners"],"x":"Lorazepam is a benzodiazepine that calms the brain and can be used for anxiety or to stop seizures. It is not an antidepressant.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp413429da","lv":"cna","d":"lorazepam","sys":"neuro","q":"An older resident received lorazepam and is now confused and unsteady on their feet. What should the nursing assistant do?","a":"Report the changes and help keep the resident safe from falls","w":["Encourage the resident to walk the hallway alone","Assume it is normal and keep the usual routine","Give the resident coffee to wake them up"],"x":"Lorazepam can cause confusion, sleepiness, and poor balance, especially in older adults. These changes should be reported and fall precautions used.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb35421e3","lv":"cna","d":"meclizine","sys":"neuro","q":"A resident takes meclizine for dizziness from an inner ear problem. Which side effect makes extra fall precautions important?","a":"Drowsiness","w":["Increased energy","Faster reaction time","Better balance right away"],"x":"Meclizine is an antihistamine that commonly causes drowsiness, which can raise fall risk. It does not improve alertness.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp44098851","lv":"cna","d":"memantine","sys":"neuro","q":"A resident with Alzheimer's disease takes memantine. Which side effects should be reported to the nurse?","a":"Dizziness, headache, or new confusion","w":["Shiny skin and hair loss","Heavy sweating and shaking","Bright orange urine"],"x":"Memantine can cause dizziness, headache, and confusion. New or worse confusion should be reported, and dizziness increases fall risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp005a8e59","lv":"cna","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"pain","q":"Acetaminophen is used to relieve which problems?","a":"Pain and fever","w":["Pain and blood clots","Fever and constipation","Swelling and high blood pressure"],"x":"Acetaminophen treats mild to moderate pain and lowers fever. It does not thin the blood like aspirin, and does little for swelling.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp45b2badd","lv":"cna","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"pain","q":"Which organ can be harmed if a person takes too much acetaminophen, including from combination products?","a":"Liver","w":["Lungs","Eyes","Bladder"],"x":"Too much acetaminophen can cause serious liver damage. Many combination pain and cold products also contain acetaminophen, so the total amount matters.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6df2da88","lv":"cna","d":"gabapentin","sys":"pain","q":"A resident with burning, tingling pain in the feet from diabetes is given gabapentin. What kind of pain is gabapentin used for?","a":"Nerve pain","w":["Headache from high blood pressure","Muscle pain from a strain","Chest pain from the heart"],"x":"Gabapentin is used for nerve pain, such as burning or tingling from diabetic nerve damage or after shingles. It does not treat heart-related chest pain.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc7d02d79","lv":"cna","d":"hydrocodone","sys":"pain","q":"A resident received hydrocodone for pain and now wants to walk to the bathroom. What should the nursing assistant do?","a":"Stay with the resident and help them walk","w":["Let the resident walk alone to stay independent","Tell the resident to wait until the next shift","Have the resident walk quickly before getting sleepy"],"x":"Hydrocodone is an opioid that can cause dizziness and sleepiness, which raise fall risk. Staying with the resident and assisting keeps them safe.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3856d8ed","lv":"cna","d":"ibuprofen","sys":"pain","q":"Ibuprofen belongs to which group of medicines?","a":"Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs)","w":["Opioid pain relievers","Steroids (corticosteroids)","Antihistamines"],"x":"Ibuprofen is an NSAID that reduces pain, fever, and swelling. It is not an opioid or a steroid.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7c87847f","lv":"cna","d":"ibuprofen","sys":"pain","q":"A resident taking ibuprofen regularly complains of stomach pain and has dark, tarry stools. Why is this important?","a":"It may be a sign of stomach bleeding","w":["It means the ibuprofen is working","It is caused by drinking too much water","It means the resident needs more fiber"],"x":"Ibuprofen can irritate the stomach lining and cause ulcers and bleeding, which may show as black, tarry stools. This must be reported to the nurse.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9deb3a61","lv":"cna","d":"oxycodone","sys":"pain","q":"A resident taking oxycodone has not had a bowel movement in several days. What does this likely relate to?","a":"Opioids commonly cause constipation","w":["Oxycodone always causes diarrhea","The resident is eating too much fiber","Oxycodone makes the bladder overactive"],"x":"Constipation is one of the most common side effects of opioids like oxycodone. Tracking bowel movements and reporting constipation helps the nurse manage it.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf94525d6","lv":"cna","d":"oxycodone","sys":"pain","q":"After a dose of oxycodone, a resident is very hard to wake up and is breathing slowly and shallowly. What should the nursing assistant do?","a":"Call for the nurse immediately","w":["Let the resident sleep and check later","Offer a glass of water and leave","Turn off the lights so they can rest"],"x":"Extreme sleepiness with slow, shallow breathing can be a sign of opioid overdose and is an emergency. The nurse must be called right away.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfcf8858c","lv":"cna","d":"tramadol","sys":"pain","q":"Tramadol belongs to which group of medicines?","a":"Opioid pain relievers","w":["Antibiotics","Water pills (diuretics)","Antacids"],"x":"Tramadol is an opioid pain reliever, so it can cause sleepiness, dizziness, constipation, and slowed breathing like other opioids.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp31175bf1","lv":"cna","d":"zolpidem","sys":"pain","q":"A resident took zolpidem at bedtime and gets up in the middle of the night. Which concern is most important?","a":"Unsteadiness and falls from the sleep medicine","w":["Higher blood sugar from the sleep medicine","Sunburn from the sleep medicine","Hearing loss from the sleep medicine"],"x":"Zolpidem is a sleep medicine that can cause dizziness, confusion, and unusual sleep behaviors, raising the risk of falls at night. The resident should be helped and the call light kept close.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb3206518","lv":"cna","d":"lorazepam","sys":"psych","q":"Lorazepam is commonly used in behavioral health to treat what?","a":"Anxiety","w":["Depression that lasts for months","Schizophrenia hallucinations","Attention problems"],"x":"Lorazepam is a benzodiazepine used for short-term relief of anxiety. It is not a treatment for long-term depression or psychosis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp82426ea3","lv":"cna","d":"lorazepam","sys":"psych","q":"A resident takes both lorazepam and an opioid pain medicine. Which change is most important to report right away?","a":"Very slow breathing and being hard to wake","w":["A mild increase in appetite","Asking for an extra blanket","Wanting to watch television"],"x":"Taking a benzodiazepine like lorazepam together with an opioid can dangerously slow breathing and cause deep sedation. These signs need immediate reporting.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp98e1bab0","lv":"cna","d":"quetiapine","sys":"psych","q":"Quetiapine belongs to which group of medicines?","a":"Antipsychotics","w":["Antibiotics","Blood thinners","Stool softeners"],"x":"Quetiapine is an antipsychotic used for conditions such as schizophrenia and bipolar disorder. It is not an antibiotic or a blood thinner.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1da012c3","lv":"cna","d":"quetiapine","sys":"psych","q":"A resident who takes quetiapine gets dizzy when standing up. Which care step helps keep the resident safe?","a":"Have the resident rise slowly and assist with standing","w":["Have the resident stand up quickly","Have the resident skip meals to avoid dizziness","Have the resident walk without a walker"],"x":"Quetiapine can lower blood pressure when standing and cause sleepiness, which raises fall risk. Rising slowly with help lowers that risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe1c938cf","lv":"cna","d":"sertraline","sys":"psych","q":"A resident started sertraline for depression last week and says it is not helping yet. What should the nursing assistant know?","a":"It often takes several weeks to feel the full effect","w":["It should work within a few hours","It only works if taken with alcohol","It works only when the resident is sleeping"],"x":"Antidepressants like sertraline usually take several weeks to reach full effect. Concerns should still be shared with the nurse.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa404f7df","lv":"cna","d":"sertraline","sys":"psych","q":"A resident on sertraline says, \"Everyone would be better off without me.\" What should the nursing assistant do?","a":"Stay with the resident and report it to the nurse right away","w":["Change the subject to something more cheerful","Keep it private because the resident trusted you","Tell the resident to talk about it tomorrow"],"x":"Antidepressants carry a warning about worsening depression and suicidal thoughts. Any talk of self-harm must be reported immediately, and the resident should not be left alone.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp640d63c4","lv":"cna","d":"trazodone","sys":"psych","q":"Trazodone is an antidepressant that is also often used to help with what?","a":"Sleep","w":["Constipation","High blood pressure","Seasonal allergies"],"x":"Trazodone is an antidepressant that causes sleepiness, so it is often given at bedtime to help with sleep. It is not a laxative or allergy medicine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp43cde3d9","lv":"cna","d":"trazodone","sys":"psych","q":"A resident who takes trazodone at bedtime needs to use the bathroom during the night. Which action is safest?","a":"Keep the bed low, the call light close, and assist with walking","w":["Have the resident walk in the dark alone","Raise the bed rails and leave the bed high","Tell the resident to hurry to the bathroom"],"x":"Trazodone can cause drowsiness and dizziness on standing, so night trips to the bathroom can lead to falls. A low bed, call light, and help with walking improve safety.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4bdac6e0","lv":"cna","d":"sennosides","sys":"psych","q":"Some behavioral health medicines cause constipation, so senna may be ordered. How does senna work?","a":"It stimulates the bowel muscles to move stool","w":["It adds bulk by soaking up fat","It coats the stomach to stop acid","It slows the bowel to firm up stool"],"x":"Senna is a stimulant laxative that makes the bowel muscles push stool along. It does not coat the stomach or slow the bowel.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp953d2e03","lv":"cna","d":"bisacodyl","sys":"psych","q":"A resident received bisacodyl for constipation. Which side effect is common and may be reported if it is strong?","a":"Belly cramping","w":["Hair loss","Blurred vision","Fast weight gain"],"x":"Bisacodyl is a stimulant laxative that commonly causes belly cramping. Severe cramping, pain, or many watery stools should be reported.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa4236466","lv":"cna","d":"furosemide","sys":"renal","q":"A resident takes furosemide and is weighed every day. Which practice gives the most accurate daily weights?","a":"Same scale, same time of day, and similar clothing","w":["Different scale each day to compare","Weigh only after the resident eats","Weigh at a different time each day"],"x":"Daily weights show fluid gain or loss, so they must be taken the same way every day. Using a different scale or time makes changes hard to trust.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6c818b3b","lv":"cna","d":"furosemide","sys":"renal","q":"A resident on furosemide has a dry mouth, is very dizzy, and is urinating much less than usual. What might these signs mean?","a":"The resident may be losing too much fluid","w":["The medicine is not strong enough","The resident is getting too much potassium","These are normal signs the medicine works"],"x":"Furosemide removes fluid, and too much fluid loss can cause dry mouth, dizziness, and low urine output. These signs of dehydration should be reported.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd06e5b80","lv":"cna","d":"hydrochlorothiazide","sys":"renal","q":"A resident starts hydrochlorothiazide, a water pill. How might this affect daily care?","a":"The resident may need help getting to the bathroom more often","w":["The resident will need fewer bathroom trips","The resident should have fluids taken away","The resident will need to stay in bed all day"],"x":"Water pills increase urination, so the resident may need more frequent toileting help. Keeping the call light within reach and the path clear lowers fall risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp684b013d","lv":"cna","d":"potassium-chloride","sys":"renal","q":"Why is potassium chloride often given to people who take water pills like furosemide?","a":"To replace potassium lost in the urine","w":["To make the water pill work faster","To lower blood sugar after meals","To keep the urine from smelling"],"x":"Some water pills cause the body to lose potassium in the urine. Potassium chloride replaces it to keep levels in a safe range.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp52e7a07d","lv":"cna","d":"potassium-chloride","sys":"renal","q":"A resident takes potassium chloride. Which side effect should the nursing assistant report to the nurse?","a":"Stomach pain, vomiting, or trouble swallowing","w":["Increased hunger at mealtime","Feeling cold in a cool room","Mild dry skin on the hands"],"x":"Potassium pills can irritate the stomach and throat, causing pain, nausea, vomiting, or trouble swallowing. These should be reported.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpba0f48f3","lv":"cna","d":"nitrofurantoin","sys":"renal","q":"A resident taking nitrofurantoin has urine that looks darker yellow or brownish. What should the nursing assistant know?","a":"This color change is a known effect of the medicine","w":["This means the resident has kidney stones","This means the resident needs less water","This color means the medicine has expired"],"x":"Nitrofurantoin can turn urine dark yellow or brown, which is a known, usually harmless effect. The nursing assistant can still mention it to the nurse.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe30a3f8d","lv":"cna","d":"oxybutynin","sys":"renal","q":"What is oxybutynin used to treat?","a":"Overactive bladder with sudden urges and leaking","w":["Urinary tract infections","Kidney stones","High blood pressure"],"x":"Oxybutynin relaxes the bladder muscle to reduce sudden urges, frequent urination, and leaking. It is not an antibiotic for infection.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp89088b8e","lv":"cna","d":"oxybutynin","sys":"renal","q":"An older resident started oxybutynin and now has a dry mouth, constipation, and new confusion. What should the nursing assistant do?","a":"Report these side effects to the nurse","w":["Offer salty snacks to fix the dry mouth","Assume the confusion is unrelated","Limit fluids to reduce bathroom trips"],"x":"Oxybutynin can cause dry mouth, constipation, and confusion, especially in older adults. These changes should be reported, and limiting fluids is not appropriate.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp954c37da","lv":"cna","d":"tamsulosin","sys":"renal","q":"What is tamsulosin used for?","a":"Making urination easier in men with an enlarged prostate","w":["Treating a bladder infection","Lowering blood sugar","Increasing the amount of urine made"],"x":"Tamsulosin relaxes muscles in the prostate and bladder opening so urine flows more easily. It is not an antibiotic or a water pill.","pilot":1},{"id":"qped13d2ed","lv":"cna","d":"tamsulosin","sys":"renal","q":"A resident who takes tamsulosin gets lightheaded when standing up. What is the main safety concern?","a":"Falling from low blood pressure when standing","w":["Choking during meals","Getting a skin rash","Losing hearing"],"x":"Tamsulosin can lower blood pressure when a person stands, causing dizziness and fainting. Standing slowly with help lowers fall risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8f882bb7","lv":"cna","d":"alendronate","sys":"bone","q":"A resident takes alendronate in the morning. Why must the resident stay sitting up or standing for at least 30 minutes afterward?","a":"To help keep the pill from irritating the esophagus","w":["To help the pill lower blood sugar","To prevent the pill from causing sleepiness","To make the pill work as a laxative"],"x":"Alendronate can irritate and injure the esophagus if it stays there or backs up. Staying upright after taking it lowers this risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2c632899","lv":"cna","d":"alendronate","sys":"bone","q":"A resident who takes alendronate for osteoporosis complains of new heartburn, chest pain, and pain when swallowing. What should the nursing assistant do?","a":"Report these symptoms to the nurse","w":["Lay the resident flat to rest","Offer a large meal to settle the stomach","Wait until the next week to see if it improves"],"x":"Heartburn, chest pain, or painful swallowing can be signs alendronate has irritated the esophagus. These must be reported, and lying flat can make it worse.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9bea1b14","lv":"cna","d":"cephalexin","sys":"infect","q":"Cephalexin belongs to which group of medicines?","a":"Antibiotics","w":["Antifungals","Antihistamines","Antacids"],"x":"Cephalexin is an antibiotic (a cephalosporin) used for bacterial infections such as skin infections. It does not treat fungal infections.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp05d7a5b2","lv":"cna","d":"cephalexin","sys":"infect","q":"A resident taking cephalexin develops frequent, watery diarrhea. Why is this important to report?","a":"Antibiotics can lead to a serious bowel infection","w":["It means the infection is fully cured","It means the resident drank too much water","It shows the antibiotic is too weak"],"x":"Antibiotics can upset normal gut bacteria and allow C. difficile, a serious bowel infection, to grow. Frequent watery diarrhea must be reported.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf887bb47","lv":"cna","d":"nitrofurantoin","sys":"infect","q":"Nitrofurantoin is most often used to treat which type of infection?","a":"Bladder (urinary tract) infection","w":["Pneumonia in the lungs","Fungal infection of the mouth","Ear infection"],"x":"Nitrofurantoin concentrates in the urine, so it is used for bladder infections. It does not work well for lung or ear infections.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp939ae7aa","lv":"cna","d":"nitrofurantoin","sys":"infect","q":"An older resident taking nitrofurantoin develops a new cough, fever, and shortness of breath. What should the nursing assistant do?","a":"Report the breathing changes to the nurse right away","w":["Encourage the resident to lie flat and rest","Assume it is a normal cold and wait","Offer cough drops and recheck tomorrow"],"x":"Nitrofurantoin can rarely cause a serious lung reaction, with cough, fever, and shortness of breath. Breathing changes must be reported promptly.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0ba7fc18","lv":"cna","d":"nystatin","sys":"infect","q":"A resident has creamy white patches in the mouth that do not wipe off easily, and nystatin is ordered. What is nystatin used to treat?","a":"Fungal (yeast) infections","w":["Bacterial infections","Viral infections","Allergic reactions"],"x":"Nystatin is an antifungal used for yeast infections such as oral thrush. Antibiotics do not treat yeast and can actually make thrush more likely.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe7e7d2a1","lv":"cna","d":"nystatin","sys":"infect","q":"Nystatin powder is ordered for a red, moist rash in a resident's skin folds. Which care step supports the treatment?","a":"Keep the skin folds clean and dry","w":["Cover the area with thick wet towels","Wash the area with hot water often","Apply heavy lotion to keep it damp"],"x":"Yeast grows in warm, moist skin folds. Keeping the area clean and dry helps the antifungal powder work and prevents the rash from coming back.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf0d6cad3","lv":"cna","d":"glipizide","sys":"endo","q":"A resident with diabetes who takes glipizide becomes shaky, sweaty, and confused before lunch. What do these signs most likely mean?","a":"Low blood sugar","w":["High blood sugar","Low thyroid level","A urinary infection"],"x":"Glipizide makes the pancreas release more insulin and can cause low blood sugar. Shakiness, sweating, and confusion are classic signs and need to be reported right away.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6daac75f","lv":"cna","d":"glipizide","sys":"endo","q":"A resident who takes glipizide refuses most of breakfast. Why should the nursing assistant tell the nurse?","a":"Not eating raises the risk of low blood sugar","w":["Not eating makes glipizide cause diarrhea","Not eating makes blood pressure very high","Not eating has no effect with this medicine"],"x":"Glipizide lowers blood sugar even when a person does not eat, so a skipped meal increases the risk of low blood sugar. The nurse needs to know about poor intake.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc9305a65","lv":"cna","d":"insulin-glargine","sys":"endo","q":"Insulin glargine is which type of insulin?","a":"Long-acting insulin","w":["Rapid-acting mealtime insulin","Short-acting insulin given only by IV","Oral insulin pill"],"x":"Insulin glargine is a long-acting basal insulin that works steadily over about a day. Rapid-acting insulins like lispro are the mealtime insulins.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpec0c7999","lv":"cna","d":"insulin-lispro","sys":"endo","q":"A resident receives insulin lispro right before meals. Why is it important for the meal tray to be delivered on time?","a":"Lispro works quickly and can cause low blood sugar without food","w":["Lispro needs food to dissolve in the stomach","Lispro works only after a full night's sleep","Lispro makes the resident too full to eat"],"x":"Insulin lispro is rapid-acting and starts lowering blood sugar within minutes. If the meal is late, the resident may develop low blood sugar.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpec5a30a2","lv":"cna","d":"insulin-lispro","sys":"endo","q":"A resident received mealtime insulin lispro but ate only a few bites of lunch. What should the nursing assistant do?","a":"Report the poor intake to the nurse","w":["Remove the tray and say nothing","Offer coffee instead of food","Record the meal as fully eaten"],"x":"After rapid-acting insulin, eating very little can lead to low blood sugar. The nurse needs to know so the resident can be watched and treated if needed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpea0f9431","lv":"cna","d":"insulin-nph","sys":"endo","q":"Insulin NPH is which type of insulin?","a":"Intermediate-acting insulin","w":["Rapid-acting insulin","Long-acting insulin with no peak","Insulin taken by mouth"],"x":"NPH is an intermediate-acting insulin that has a peak several hours after it is given. It usually looks cloudy, unlike clear rapid- and long-acting insulins.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp098b5dac","lv":"cna","d":"levothyroxine","sys":"endo","q":"What is levothyroxine used to treat?","a":"Low thyroid hormone (hypothyroidism)","w":["High blood sugar (diabetes)","Overactive bladder","Allergic sneezing"],"x":"Levothyroxine replaces thyroid hormone in people whose thyroid does not make enough. It is not a diabetes or bladder medicine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpca3e4ab2","lv":"cna","d":"levothyroxine","sys":"endo","q":"A resident who takes levothyroxine has a fast heartbeat, feels nervous, and cannot sleep. What might these signs mean?","a":"Possible signs of too much thyroid medicine","w":["Expected signs of low blood sugar only","Normal signs the dose is too low","Signs of a urinary tract infection"],"x":"Too much levothyroxine can cause symptoms like a fast heartbeat, nervousness, and trouble sleeping. These should be reported to the nurse.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1769174e","lv":"cna","d":"metformin","sys":"endo","q":"Metformin is most commonly used to treat which condition?","a":"Type 2 diabetes","w":["Low thyroid","High cholesterol","Seizures"],"x":"Metformin lowers blood sugar in type 2 diabetes, mainly by reducing sugar made by the liver. It does not treat thyroid or cholesterol problems.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8885748d","lv":"cna","d":"metformin","sys":"endo","q":"Why is metformin usually given with meals?","a":"To lessen stomach upset and diarrhea","w":["To make it work as a laxative","To make the resident sleepy after eating","To prevent a fast heartbeat"],"x":"Metformin commonly causes nausea, stomach upset, and diarrhea. Taking it with meals helps reduce these side effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpac7b77a3","lv":"cna","d":"docusate","sys":"gi","q":"How does docusate help with constipation?","a":"It softens stool by letting water mix into it","w":["It strongly squeezes the bowel muscles","It neutralizes stomach acid","It slows the bowel to reduce diarrhea"],"x":"Docusate is a stool softener that lets water and fat mix into stool so it is easier to pass. Stimulant laxatives like senna are the ones that push the bowel muscles.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9f191fe7","lv":"cna","d":"docusate","sys":"gi","q":"A resident takes docusate. Which daily care action helps it work best, if allowed by the care plan?","a":"Offering fluids throughout the day","w":["Limiting fluids at meals","Keeping the resident in bed all day","Serving only low-fiber foods"],"x":"Stool softeners work better when the person drinks enough fluids. Activity and fiber, as allowed, also help prevent constipation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp74cb9fa7","lv":"cna","d":"omeprazole","sys":"gi","q":"Omeprazole belongs to which group of medicines?","a":"Proton pump inhibitors that lower stomach acid","w":["Stimulant laxatives","Antihistamines for allergies","Antibiotics for infection"],"x":"Omeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor (PPI) that reduces stomach acid for heartburn and ulcers. It is not a laxative or antibiotic.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1923f4d7","lv":"cna","d":"omeprazole","sys":"gi","q":"When is omeprazole usually taken for best effect?","a":"Before a meal, often before breakfast","w":["Right after a large dinner","Only when lying down at night","Only during a heartburn attack"],"x":"Omeprazole works best when taken before a meal, commonly before breakfast. It is not a quick-acting antacid for sudden heartburn.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp731a069b","lv":"cna","d":"polyethylene-glycol","sys":"gi","q":"How is polyethylene glycol powder usually taken?","a":"Mixed and dissolved in a glass of liquid","w":["Sprinkled dry on the tongue","Rubbed onto the skin","Inhaled with a mouthpiece"],"x":"Polyethylene glycol powder is dissolved in a beverage such as water or juice before drinking. It is not taken dry or inhaled.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0151696a","lv":"cna","d":"polyethylene-glycol","sys":"gi","q":"How does polyethylene glycol relieve constipation?","a":"It keeps water in the bowel to soften stool","w":["It irritates the bowel lining to cause cramping","It coats the stool with oil","It adds bulk by soaking up fat"],"x":"Polyethylene glycol is an osmotic laxative that keeps water in the bowel, making stool softer and easier to pass. It does not work by irritating the bowel.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4963ddb4","lv":"cna","d":"magnesium-hydroxide","sys":"gi","q":"A resident received magnesium hydroxide (milk of magnesia) and now has many loose, watery stools. What should the nursing assistant do?","a":"Report the diarrhea to the nurse","w":["Offer another laxative","Limit the resident's fluids","Ignore it because it is expected"],"x":"Magnesium hydroxide is a laxative, and too strong an effect can cause frequent watery stools and fluid loss. This should be reported.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp523340a2","lv":"cna","d":"magnesium-hydroxide","sys":"gi","q":"Magnesium hydroxide (milk of magnesia) is used for which purposes?","a":"Constipation and upset stomach from acid","w":["Nausea from motion sickness and vertigo","Allergies and itchy eyes","Pain and fever"],"x":"Milk of magnesia works as a laxative for constipation and as an antacid for acid indigestion. It does not treat motion sickness or allergies.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp185cfc2c","lv":"cna","d":"meclizine","sys":"gi","q":"Meclizine is used to prevent or treat which problem?","a":"Nausea and dizziness from motion sickness","w":["Heartburn from stomach acid","Constipation from pain pills","Diarrhea from antibiotics"],"x":"Meclizine is an antihistamine that helps prevent nausea, vomiting, and dizziness from motion sickness. It is not an antacid or laxative.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5bdcdb8e","lv":"cna","d":"calcium-carbonate","sys":"gi","q":"A resident takes calcium carbonate often for heartburn. Which side effect should the nursing assistant watch for?","a":"Constipation","w":["Diarrhea","Increased urination","Hair loss"],"x":"Calcium carbonate antacids commonly cause constipation. Magnesium-containing antacids are more likely to cause diarrhea.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe243e6ba","lv":"cna","d":"calcium-carbonate","sys":"ob","q":"A pregnant woman has heartburn. Calcium carbonate is often used for this. How does it work?","a":"It neutralizes stomach acid","w":["It speeds up the bowels","It numbs the throat","It reduces allergic swelling"],"x":"Calcium carbonate is an antacid that neutralizes stomach acid to relieve heartburn, which is common in pregnancy. It also provides calcium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8c934ad9","lv":"cna","d":"calcium-carbonate","sys":"ob","q":"Besides relieving heartburn, calcium carbonate is a source of a mineral that is important for which body part?","a":"Bones and teeth","w":["Hair and nails only","Eyes and ears","Lungs and airways"],"x":"Calcium carbonate supplies calcium, which helps build and keep strong bones and teeth. During pregnancy, calcium supports the mother's and baby's bones.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp62075332","lv":"cna","d":"ferrous-sulfate","sys":"ob","q":"Why is ferrous sulfate (iron) often given during pregnancy?","a":"To treat or prevent iron-deficiency anemia","w":["To prevent morning sickness","To lower blood pressure","To prevent urinary infections"],"x":"Pregnancy increases the body's need for iron, and low iron can cause anemia. Ferrous sulfate supplies iron to build healthy red blood cells.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp71182c54","lv":"cna","d":"ferrous-sulfate","sys":"ob","q":"Which drink taken with iron pills can help the body absorb the iron better?","a":"Orange juice","w":["Milk","Coffee","Tea"],"x":"Vitamin C in orange juice helps the body absorb iron. Milk, coffee, and tea can reduce how much iron is absorbed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc774abaa","lv":"cna","d":"nitrofurantoin","sys":"ob","q":"A woman is taking nitrofurantoin for a bladder infection. Which symptom shows the infection may not be improving and should be reported?","a":"Continued burning and urgency when urinating","w":["Urine that is pale yellow and clear","Normal number of bathroom trips","Feeling more energetic each day"],"x":"Burning, urgency, and frequent urination are signs of bladder infection. If they continue or get worse, the nurse needs to know.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9335fcf8","lv":"cna","d":"levocetirizine","sys":"immune","q":"Besides seasonal allergies, levocetirizine may also be used to relieve which skin problem?","a":"Itchy hives","w":["Pressure ulcers","Fungal skin rash","Sunburn blisters"],"x":"Levocetirizine is an antihistamine labeled for chronic hives (urticaria) as well as allergies. It does not heal pressure ulcers or fungal rashes.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpefbf4ab4","lv":"cna","d":"levocetirizine","sys":"immune","q":"Why is levocetirizine usually taken in the evening?","a":"It can cause drowsiness","w":["It must be taken with dinner","It keeps people awake","It works only in the dark"],"x":"Levocetirizine can make people sleepy, so it is labeled for evening use. Being drowsy at night is safer than during daytime activities.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1469e822","lv":"cna","d":"loratadine","sys":"immune","q":"Loratadine belongs to which group of medicines?","a":"Antihistamines","w":["Antibiotics","Steroids (corticosteroids)","Blood thinners"],"x":"Loratadine is an antihistamine that blocks histamine to relieve allergy symptoms. It is less likely to cause sleepiness than older antihistamines.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb0b4cd8b","lv":"cna","d":"loratadine","sys":"immune","q":"A resident who takes loratadine for allergies suddenly has swelling of the lips and trouble breathing. What should the nursing assistant do?","a":"Call for the nurse immediately and stay with the resident","w":["Give the resident another allergy pill","Wait to see if the swelling goes away","Have the resident lie down and rest alone"],"x":"Lip swelling and trouble breathing can be a severe allergic reaction (anaphylaxis), which is an emergency. Antihistamines like loratadine are not enough to treat it.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd0cd26c6","lv":"cna","d":"meclizine","sys":"immune","q":"Meclizine belongs to which group of medicines?","a":"Antihistamines","w":["Antibiotics","Opioid pain relievers","Water pills (diuretics)"],"x":"Meclizine is an antihistamine used for motion sickness and dizziness. Like other antihistamines, it can cause drowsiness and dry mouth.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp49519357","lv":"emt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:acls","q":"During cardiac arrest, ALS providers give epinephrine. What is its main intended benefit during CPR?","a":"Constricting blood vessels to improve blood flow to the heart and brain","w":["Dissolving the clot that caused the arrest","Converting the rhythm back to normal by itself","Opening the airway by relaxing the throat muscles"],"x":"Epinephrine's alpha effects constrict blood vessels, raising the pressure that drives blood to the heart and brain during compressions. It does not dissolve clots or reliably convert a rhythm on its own.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdddbaa4f","lv":"emt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:acls","q":"An EMT crew arrives first at a witnessed cardiac arrest. ALS with epinephrine is several minutes away. What should the crew focus on?","a":"High-quality CPR and early use of the AED","w":["Waiting for ALS before starting compressions","Giving the patient's epinephrine auto-injector first","Placing the patient in the recovery position"],"x":"Survival from cardiac arrest depends most on early high-quality CPR and defibrillation; epinephrine supports these but never replaces them. An epinephrine auto-injector is not an EMT treatment for cardiac arrest.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd8a8ade9","lv":"emt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:acls","q":"Epinephrine belongs to which group of medications?","a":"Sympathomimetics (adrenergic agonists)","w":["Beta-blockers","Opioid antagonists","Anticholinergics"],"x":"Epinephrine is a catecholamine that stimulates alpha and beta adrenergic receptors, mimicking the sympathetic nervous system. Beta-blockers have the opposite effect on beta receptors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp736f0be0","lv":"emt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:acls","q":"Which effect of epinephrine on the heart comes from stimulating beta-1 receptors?","a":"Faster heart rate and stronger contractions","w":["Slower heart rate and weaker contractions","Relaxation of the coronary smooth muscle only","No change in rate but lower blood pressure"],"x":"Beta-1 stimulation increases heart rate and the force of contraction. This raises the heart's oxygen demand, which is why chest pain or palpitations can follow a dose.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdd752aad","lv":"emt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:acls","q":"A patient with anaphylaxis improves after an epinephrine auto-injector but then reports new chest pain and a racing heart. What is the best interpretation and action?","a":"Recognize a possible cardiac side effect, reassess, and report it to ALS","w":["Expect this as proof the dose was too small and repeat it now","Treat this as anxiety and reassure the patient only","Assume a new allergic reaction and ignore the vital signs"],"x":"Epinephrine increases heart rate and myocardial oxygen demand and can cause chest pain or dysrhythmias, especially in older adults or those with heart disease. New chest pain needs reassessment and prompt reporting, not simple reassurance.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpaa856427","lv":"emt","d":"aspirin","sys":"c:acs","q":"How does aspirin help a patient with a suspected heart attack?","a":"It reduces platelet clumping so the clot is less likely to grow","w":["It dissolves the existing clot in the coronary artery","It dilates the coronary arteries to relieve chest pain","It slows the heart rate to lower oxygen demand"],"x":"Aspirin is an antiplatelet drug that blocks thromboxane production, limiting further clot growth. It does not dissolve an existing clot; that is the role of fibrinolytics or catheter procedures.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb5ccd0af","lv":"emt","d":"aspirin","sys":"c:acs","q":"Why are patients with suspected acute coronary syndrome told to chew aspirin rather than swallow it whole?","a":"Chewing allows faster absorption and quicker antiplatelet effect","w":["Chewing prevents the tablet from irritating the stomach","Chewing is needed to activate the medication","Chewing reduces the risk of an allergic reaction"],"x":"Chewing breaks the tablet up so it is absorbed more quickly, speeding the antiplatelet effect. It does not activate the drug or prevent allergic reactions.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp03796121","lv":"emt","d":"aspirin","sys":"c:acs","q":"Which finding in a patient with chest pain is a contraindication to giving aspirin?","a":"A known allergy to aspirin","w":["A history of high blood pressure","Chest pain that began while resting","A history of type 2 diabetes"],"x":"A known aspirin allergy (or active GI bleeding) is a contraindication. Hypertension, diabetes and chest pain at rest are common in heart attack patients and are not reasons to avoid aspirin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp512baace","lv":"emt","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:acs","q":"Which finding is a contraindication to assisting a chest pain patient with nitroglycerin?","a":"Recent use of an erectile dysfunction medication such as sildenafil","w":["A blood pressure higher than the patient's usual","Chest pain that radiates to the left arm","A history of a previous heart attack"],"x":"Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors such as sildenafil or tadalafil combined with nitroglycerin can cause severe, prolonged hypotension. High blood pressure and typical cardiac pain are not contraindications.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp77297552","lv":"emt","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:acs","q":"Before an EMT assists a patient with nitroglycerin, which condition must generally be met?","a":"It is prescribed to the patient and medical direction approves","w":["The patient has taken aspirin within the past day","The patient's pain has lasted longer than usual","A family member has a prescription for it"],"x":"At the EMT level, nitroglycerin is an assisted medication: it must be the patient's own prescription and given with online or protocol-based medical direction. Another person's prescription does not qualify.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7302211b","lv":"emt","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:htn","q":"Nitroglycerin lowers blood pressure and cardiac workload mainly by which action?","a":"Dilating veins so less blood returns to the heart","w":["Blocking beta receptors to slow the heart","Causing the kidneys to remove extra fluid","Constricting arteries to redirect blood flow"],"x":"Nitroglycerin is a vasodilator that mainly dilates veins, reducing preload and the heart's oxygen demand. It is not a beta-blocker or a diuretic.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0d9f86c8","lv":"emt","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:htn","q":"Shortly after taking nitroglycerin, a patient reports a pounding headache. What is the most likely explanation?","a":"An expected side effect from dilation of blood vessels","w":["A sign of an allergic reaction to the tablet","Proof that the tablets have lost their potency","A sign that the patient is having a stroke"],"x":"Headache is a common, expected side effect caused by vasodilation of blood vessels in the head. It is not a sign of allergy, and a headache actually suggests the tablet is active.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb58b59c1","lv":"emt","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:htn","q":"Which vital sign is most important for an EMT to reassess after each nitroglycerin dose?","a":"Blood pressure","w":["Body temperature","Pupil size","Blood glucose"],"x":"The main danger of nitroglycerin is hypotension from vasodilation, so blood pressure is checked before and after each dose. Temperature and glucose are not directly affected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp770dadca","lv":"emt","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:htn","q":"A patient has a very high blood pressure and a headache but no chest pain. Why is nitroglycerin not an EMT treatment here?","a":"At the EMT level it is used for suspected cardiac chest pain, not high blood pressure alone","w":["Nitroglycerin raises blood pressure and would make this worse","Nitroglycerin only works when blood pressure is low","Nitroglycerin is only given by injection in hypertension"],"x":"EMTs assist with nitroglycerin for chest pain of suspected cardiac origin under medical direction. Treating severe hypertension is an ALS and hospital decision; nitroglycerin lowers, not raises, blood pressure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpab46899f","lv":"emt","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:htn","q":"How is a nitroglycerin tablet given?","a":"Placed under the tongue and allowed to dissolve","w":["Swallowed whole with a full glass of water","Chewed and swallowed like aspirin","Placed between the cheek and gum and chewed"],"x":"Nitroglycerin tablets and spray are given sublingually for rapid absorption through the mucosa. Swallowing exposes the drug to first-pass liver breakdown, which greatly reduces its effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfa8f7ea3","lv":"emt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"Anaphylactic shock is classified as which type of shock?","a":"Distributive shock","w":["Hypovolemic shock","Cardiogenic shock","Obstructive shock"],"x":"In anaphylaxis, widespread vasodilation and leaky capillaries cause distributive shock. Hypovolemic shock comes from fluid or blood loss, and cardiogenic shock from pump failure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4c0e9643","lv":"emt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"Which action of epinephrine most directly raises blood pressure in anaphylactic shock?","a":"Alpha-1 stimulation that constricts blood vessels","w":["Beta-2 stimulation that relaxes the airways","Blocking histamine at its receptors","Increasing urine output by the kidneys"],"x":"Alpha-1 vasoconstriction reverses the vasodilation of anaphylaxis and raises blood pressure. Beta-2 bronchodilation helps breathing but does not raise blood pressure; epinephrine is not an antihistamine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5538c96f","lv":"emt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"A patient in anaphylactic shock takes a beta-blocker daily. What should the EMT anticipate after giving epinephrine?","a":"The response may be weaker, so call for ALS early","w":["The patient will be unusually sensitive to epinephrine","Epinephrine will have no effect at all on breathing","The beta-blocker will fully reverse the reaction"],"x":"Beta-blockers can blunt the response to epinephrine and make anaphylaxis harder to treat, so early ALS backup is important. The effect is reduced, not absent, and the beta-blocker does not treat the reaction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe8f87b04","lv":"emt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"A patient's blood pressure and breathing improve after an epinephrine auto-injector. Why is continued transport and monitoring still needed?","a":"Epinephrine's effects are short-lived and symptoms can return","w":["Epinephrine causes lasting low blood pressure later","The improvement shows the reaction was not anaphylaxis","A second injection is required by law after any use"],"x":"Epinephrine wears off quickly and symptoms may recur, including biphasic reactions hours later. Improvement after epinephrine supports, rather than rules out, anaphylaxis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp20f5323b","lv":"emt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"After giving epinephrine to a patient in shock, how should the EMT reassess?","a":"Repeat vital signs, mental status and skin findings frequently, as for an unstable patient","w":["Recheck vital signs once on arrival at the hospital","Reassess only if the patient reports new symptoms","Check blood glucose first, then vital signs at the hospital"],"x":"A patient in shock is unstable and needs frequent reassessment of vital signs, mental status, breathing and skin to detect improvement or deterioration. Waiting for new complaints can miss a decline.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb86c538f","lv":"emt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:airway","q":"How does albuterol relieve wheezing in an asthma attack?","a":"It stimulates beta-2 receptors to relax bronchial smooth muscle","w":["It blocks histamine to reduce airway swelling","It thins mucus so it can be coughed up","It reduces airway inflammation like a steroid"],"x":"Albuterol is a short-acting beta-2 agonist that relaxes bronchial smooth muscle and opens the airways. It does not reduce inflammation like a corticosteroid or block histamine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp00a06126","lv":"emt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:airway","q":"Which step is part of correct metered-dose inhaler technique?","a":"Breathe out fully, then press the inhaler while inhaling slowly and deeply","w":["Press the inhaler while breathing out forcefully","Take quick, shallow breaths while spraying","Spray into the air first and then breathe it in"],"x":"The patient should exhale, seal the lips on the mouthpiece or spacer, activate the inhaler during a slow deep breath, and hold the breath. Spraying during exhalation or shallow breaths delivers little drug to the lungs.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8218f3f9","lv":"emt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:airway","q":"An asthma patient who was wheezing loudly now has very quiet breath sounds, is drowsy, and is still working hard to breathe. What does this most likely mean?","a":"Airflow is worsening; prepare to assist ventilations and call ALS","w":["The inhaler has worked and the attack is ending","The patient is relaxing and can be reassured","The wheezing was caused by anxiety alone"],"x":"A quiet or silent chest with fatigue and altered mental status signals severe obstruction and impending respiratory failure. Improvement would show easier breathing and better alertness, not drowsiness.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd7b0c29e","lv":"emt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:airway","q":"Which finding in a patient having an allergic reaction shows airway involvement that calls for epinephrine?","a":"Hoarse voice, stridor, or a feeling of throat tightness","w":["Itchy hives limited to the arms","Watery eyes and a runny nose","Mild redness around an insect sting"],"x":"Hoarseness, stridor and throat tightness suggest upper airway swelling, a sign of anaphylaxis that needs epinephrine. Localized hives or mild local reactions alone do not meet anaphylaxis criteria.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp55d2a860","lv":"emt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:airway","q":"A patient in anaphylaxis is wheezing and has throat swelling. Why is epinephrine preferred over an albuterol inhaler as the first medication?","a":"Epinephrine treats airway swelling, bronchospasm and low blood pressure together","w":["Albuterol is not safe in any allergic reaction","Epinephrine works only on the lungs and acts faster","Albuterol would make the throat swelling worse"],"x":"Epinephrine counters the whole systemic reaction: airway edema, bronchospasm and vasodilation. Albuterol only relaxes bronchial muscle and does not treat swelling or shock, though it can be used as an add-on.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9bc14569","lv":"emt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:allergy","q":"Where is an epinephrine auto-injector given?","a":"Into the outer (anterolateral) thigh","w":["Into the upper arm deltoid muscle","Into the abdominal wall near the navel","Into the buttock's upper outer area"],"x":"Auto-injectors are designed for the anterolateral thigh, where absorption is reliable, and can be given through clothing. Injection into the buttock or other sites is not recommended.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdb1a7097","lv":"emt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:allergy","q":"After an epinephrine auto-injector, which set of effects should the EMT expect as common side effects?","a":"Fast heart rate, shakiness, anxiety and pale skin","w":["Slow heart rate, drowsiness and flushed skin","Pinpoint pupils and slowed breathing","Low blood sugar and sweating"],"x":"Epinephrine stimulates the sympathetic system, causing tachycardia, tremor, anxiety and pallor. Slowing of the heart or breathing is not expected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp851c8fd5","lv":"emt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:allergy","q":"Which patient best meets the criteria for anaphylaxis requiring epinephrine?","a":"Hives after a bee sting with difficulty breathing and low blood pressure","w":["Swelling and pain only at the bee sting site","Itchy rash on one arm after touching a plant","Sneezing and watery eyes during pollen season"],"x":"Anaphylaxis involves more than one body system, such as skin signs plus respiratory compromise or hypotension. Local swelling, contact rashes and seasonal allergy symptoms are not anaphylaxis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2c003f07","lv":"emt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:allergy","q":"After epinephrine, a patient with anaphylaxis still has some wheezing. What is the role of an albuterol inhaler here?","a":"An add-on for remaining bronchospasm, not a replacement for epinephrine","w":["The main treatment that makes epinephrine unnecessary","A treatment for the low blood pressure of anaphylaxis","A way to reverse the side effects of epinephrine"],"x":"Albuterol may help persistent bronchospasm, but it does not treat airway swelling or shock. Epinephrine remains the first-line drug for anaphylaxis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf59fc285","lv":"emt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:allergy","q":"After assisting an allergic patient with an albuterol inhaler, what should the EMT reassess first?","a":"Work of breathing, breath sounds and oxygen saturation","w":["Pupil size and reaction to light","Blood glucose and skin temperature","Bowel sounds and abdominal tenderness"],"x":"Albuterol targets bronchospasm, so its effect is judged by breathing effort, wheezing and oxygenation. Pupils and glucose are not the main markers of response.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3f73fad7","lv":"emt","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"c:sedation","q":"Acetaminophen is best described as which type of medication?","a":"A pain reliever and fever reducer with little anti-inflammatory effect","w":["A nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug that thins the blood","An opioid pain reliever that can slow breathing","A sedative that reduces anxiety and causes sleep"],"x":"Acetaminophen is an analgesic and antipyretic but has minimal anti-inflammatory action and does not affect platelets. It is not an NSAID or an opioid.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp90c1e6a3","lv":"emt","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"c:sedation","q":"Which organ is most at risk from acetaminophen overdose?","a":"Liver","w":["Kidneys","Lungs","Stomach lining"],"x":"Excess acetaminophen produces a toxic metabolite that damages the liver. Stomach irritation and bleeding are more typical of NSAIDs such as aspirin or ibuprofen.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6c5567d4","lv":"emt","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"c:sedation","q":"A patient took a large amount of acetaminophen a few hours ago and feels only mildly nauseated. What is important to understand?","a":"Serious liver damage can develop later even if the patient looks well now","w":["Mild symptoms mean the amount taken was not harmful","Acetaminophen overdose mainly causes immediate breathing problems","The patient can be observed at home if symptoms stay mild"],"x":"Early acetaminophen poisoning often causes few or mild symptoms, while liver injury appears a day or more later. Transport for evaluation is needed regardless of how well the patient appears.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp51e0a6e3","lv":"emt","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"c:sedation","q":"Why should an EMT ask about all cold remedies and prescription pain pills when a patient reports taking acetaminophen?","a":"Many combination products also contain acetaminophen, adding to the total amount","w":["Cold remedies block acetaminophen so it stops working","Prescription pain pills cancel out acetaminophen's effects","Acetaminophen cannot be taken with any other medication"],"x":"Acetaminophen is in many cold and flu products and some opioid combination pills, so accidental overdose is common. These products add to the total exposure rather than block it.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8df6ce70","lv":"emt","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"c:sedation","q":"Which medication is used in the hospital as the antidote for acetaminophen poisoning?","a":"Acetylcysteine","w":["Naloxone","Flumazenil","Atropine"],"x":"Acetylcysteine replenishes the liver's protective glutathione and prevents liver injury. Naloxone reverses opioids, not acetaminophen, even in combination products.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpadb3b8c7","lv":"emt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"ALS arrives for an unresponsive patient with a very low blood sugar. Which form of dextrose is appropriate for this patient?","a":"Intravenous dextrose","w":["Oral glucose gel between cheek and gum","Intramuscular dextrose into the thigh","Dextrose tablets dissolved under the tongue"],"x":"An unresponsive patient cannot protect the airway, so oral glucose risks aspiration; ALS gives dextrose intravenously. Dextrose is not given intramuscularly.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpca7aec01","lv":"emt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"Why is it important to watch the IV site closely when concentrated dextrose is given?","a":"Leakage into surrounding tissue can cause serious tissue damage","w":["Dextrose often causes a severe allergic rash at the site","Dextrose makes the vein clot and stops all flow","Dextrose leaking out causes the blood sugar to drop"],"x":"Concentrated dextrose is irritating, and extravasation can injure or kill surrounding tissue. Allergy to dextrose is not a typical concern.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2ebdf7b6","lv":"emt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"Before giving glucose to a diabetic patient with altered mental status, which assessment should be done if local protocol allows?","a":"Check the blood glucose level with a glucometer","w":["Check the core body temperature","Measure the patient's urine output","Listen for bowel sounds in each quadrant"],"x":"A glucometer reading confirms hypoglycemia and gives a baseline to judge the response to treatment. Temperature and bowel sounds do not guide glucose administration.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6995d18d","lv":"emt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"A diabetic patient takes a long-acting oral diabetes medication such as glipizide. After oral glucose, the patient is alert and wants to refuse transport. What is the main concern?","a":"Blood sugar may fall again hours later as the medication keeps working","w":["The glucose will cause a dangerous rise in blood pressure","The medication will make the glucose ineffective right away","Glipizide causes an allergy to glucose after treatment"],"x":"Sulfonylureas such as glipizide can cause prolonged or recurrent hypoglycemia that outlasts a single glucose dose. This is why medical direction and hospital observation are strongly encouraged.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp01dbcb48","lv":"emt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"A known diabetic is confused but can swallow, and blood sugar cannot be measured. Why is giving oral glucose still considered reasonable?","a":"A small amount of sugar will not harm a high-sugar patient but may help a low-sugar one","w":["Oral glucose lowers blood sugar if it is already too high","Confusion in diabetics is always caused by high blood sugar","Oral glucose has no effect unless blood sugar is low"],"x":"The brief rise from oral glucose is unlikely to harm a hyperglycemic patient, while untreated hypoglycemia can quickly cause brain injury. Glucose does not lower blood sugar.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp496a2bcf","lv":"emt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:lytes","q":"What effect does albuterol have on blood potassium levels?","a":"It lowers blood potassium by shifting potassium into the cells","w":["It raises blood potassium by releasing it from cells","It removes potassium from the body through the urine","It has no effect on potassium at any amount"],"x":"Beta-2 stimulation drives potassium into cells, temporarily lowering the blood level. It does not remove potassium from the body.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3344510e","lv":"emt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:lytes","q":"Hospitals sometimes give nebulized albuterol for high potassium. What is a limitation of this treatment?","a":"The effect is temporary because potassium is moved, not removed","w":["It permanently removes potassium from the body","It only works if the patient is wheezing","It raises potassium briefly before lowering it for good"],"x":"Albuterol shifts potassium into cells for a short time, so other treatments such as dialysis are needed to remove it from the body. It does not need wheezing to work.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3035de0a","lv":"emt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:lytes","q":"ALS may give albuterol for suspected high potassium. Which patient history most suggests this problem?","a":"A dialysis patient who missed treatments and now has weakness and a slow pulse","w":["A young athlete with leg cramps after a long run","A patient with vomiting and diarrhea for several days","A patient taking a water pill who feels thirsty"],"x":"Missed dialysis allows potassium to build up, which can cause weakness and dangerous slow rhythms. Vomiting, diarrhea and diuretics more often cause low potassium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp647747c9","lv":"emt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:lytes","q":"A patient with severe asthma has used albuterol repeatedly for hours. Which electrolyte problem might cause muscle weakness and irregular heartbeats?","a":"Low potassium","w":["High potassium","High calcium","Low sodium"],"x":"Repeated albuterol doses shift potassium into cells and can cause hypokalemia, leading to weakness and dysrhythmias. Albuterol does not raise potassium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa0c1ea65","lv":"emt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:lytes","q":"Which medication taken together with frequent albuterol most increases the risk of low potassium?","a":"Furosemide, a loop diuretic","w":["Spironolactone, a potassium-sparing diuretic","Lisinopril, an ACE inhibitor","Acetaminophen, a pain reliever"],"x":"Loop diuretics like furosemide cause potassium loss in the urine, adding to albuterol's potassium-lowering effect. Spironolactone and ACE inhibitors tend to raise potassium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0aef3a45","lv":"emt","d":"naloxone","sys":"c:tox","q":"How does naloxone reverse an opioid overdose?","a":"It blocks opioid receptors and displaces the opioid from them","w":["It speeds up the liver's breakdown of opioids","It stimulates the breathing center directly like caffeine","It binds the opioid in the stomach so it is not absorbed"],"x":"Naloxone is a competitive opioid receptor antagonist that pushes opioids off their receptors. Binding drugs in the gut describes activated charcoal, not naloxone.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa619ce73","lv":"emt","d":"naloxone","sys":"c:tox","q":"A patient wakes and breathes normally after naloxone, then refuses care. Why is continued monitoring important?","a":"Naloxone can wear off before the opioid, so breathing may slow again","w":["Naloxone causes delayed breathing problems of its own","The patient will now be immune to further opioid effects","Naloxone permanently removes the opioid from the body"],"x":"Many opioids, especially long-acting or potent ones, last longer than naloxone, so re-sedation and respiratory depression can return. Naloxone does not remove the opioid.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9df3bbf5","lv":"emt","d":"naloxone","sys":"c:tox","q":"A patient with a suspected opioid overdose is breathing a few shallow breaths per minute and is cyanotic. What should the EMT do first?","a":"Open the airway and ventilate with a bag-valve mask and oxygen","w":["Give naloxone and wait for the patient to wake up","Place the patient in the recovery position and observe","Give activated charcoal to absorb the opioid"],"x":"Inadequate breathing is immediately treated with airway management and ventilation; naloxone follows but takes time to work. Charcoal is unsafe in a patient who cannot protect the airway.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp34c27a95","lv":"emt","d":"activated-charcoal","sys":"c:tox","q":"How does activated charcoal work in some oral poisonings?","a":"It adsorbs the poison in the gut so less is absorbed","w":["It neutralizes poisons already in the bloodstream","It causes vomiting to empty the stomach","It coats the stomach to stop pain and burning"],"x":"Activated charcoal binds many substances in the gastrointestinal tract, reducing absorption. It does not act on poison already absorbed and is not an emetic.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp968ceee6","lv":"emt","d":"activated-charcoal","sys":"c:tox","q":"Which patient is NOT a candidate for activated charcoal?","a":"A drowsy patient who cannot protect the airway","w":["An alert adult who swallowed pills recently","An alert patient who agrees to drink it","An alert patient with a known ingestion and stable vitals"],"x":"Charcoal given to a patient with altered mental status risks vomiting and aspiration into the lungs. It is considered only for alert patients who can swallow and protect the airway, per medical direction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7f7e06fe","lv":"emt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:endo","q":"Which patient meets the usual criteria for oral glucose?","a":"A known diabetic with altered mental status who can swallow","w":["An unresponsive diabetic with no gag reflex","A diabetic who is alert with no complaints","A confused patient who is actively seizing"],"x":"Oral glucose is for a patient with a history of diabetes and altered mental status who can swallow and protect the airway. Unresponsive or seizing patients risk aspiration.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp844e662c","lv":"emt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:endo","q":"Why is oral glucose contraindicated in a patient who cannot swallow?","a":"The patient could aspirate the gel into the airway","w":["The gel will not absorb unless it is swallowed whole","It will raise blood sugar dangerously fast","It causes vomiting in unresponsive patients"],"x":"A patient who cannot swallow or protect the airway may aspirate the gel. The danger is airway compromise, not a rapid rise in blood sugar.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp321955c2","lv":"emt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:endo","q":"How is oral glucose gel given?","a":"Placed between the cheek and gum or on the tongue in small portions","w":["Injected into the muscle of the outer thigh","Sprayed into the nostrils with an atomizer","Swallowed all at once as a single large mouthful"],"x":"Oral glucose is placed between the cheek and gum (or on a tongue depressor) a little at a time so it can be absorbed and swallowed safely. Giving it all at once raises the risk of choking.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpeac8f897","lv":"emt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:endo","q":"Several minutes after oral glucose, a diabetic patient's mental status has not improved. What should the EMT consider?","a":"Other causes of altered mental status and the need for ALS","w":["That the glucose was absorbed and no change is expected","That the patient must have high blood sugar instead","That transport is unnecessary since glucose was given"],"x":"Lack of improvement suggests the hypoglycemia is severe or something else, such as stroke, head injury or overdose, is causing the altered mental status. ALS and further assessment are needed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpabf18051","lv":"emt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:endo","q":"Which presentation in a diabetic patient most suggests low blood sugar rather than high blood sugar?","a":"Sudden confusion with cool, pale, sweaty skin","w":["Gradual onset with warm, dry skin and fruity breath","Deep rapid breathing and several days of thirst","Frequent urination and slowly worsening fatigue"],"x":"Hypoglycemia comes on quickly with sympathetic signs such as cool, clammy skin. Gradual onset, fruity breath and deep rapid breathing suggest diabetic ketoacidosis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp301b8e2a","lv":"emt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:peds","q":"A child with a peanut allergy has hives, wheezing and vomiting after a snack. The child has a prescribed auto-injector. What is the best interpretation?","a":"Anaphylaxis; epinephrine is indicated per protocol","w":["A mild reaction that only needs an antihistamine","Food poisoning that needs no medication","An asthma attack that needs only an inhaler"],"x":"Skin signs plus respiratory and GI symptoms after a known allergen meet anaphylaxis criteria, and epinephrine is first-line. Antihistamines and inhalers do not treat the systemic reaction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp12adcb37","lv":"emt","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:peds","q":"While using a parent's epinephrine auto-injector, a child accidentally injects it into his own thumb. What is the main concern?","a":"Reduced blood flow to the thumb from vasoconstriction; report it","w":["A severe allergic reaction to the epinephrine itself","Immediate cardiac arrest from the injection","Low blood sugar caused by the epinephrine"],"x":"Epinephrine constricts blood vessels and can reduce circulation to a finger or thumb, so the injury should be reported and evaluated. Allergy to epinephrine is not the concern.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp24f1ef30","lv":"emt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:peds","q":"A young child with diabetes is suddenly irritable, sweaty and sleepy. What should the EMT suspect first?","a":"Low blood sugar","w":["High blood sugar","Simple tiredness","Dehydration from heat"],"x":"Sudden behavior change, sweating and lethargy in a diabetic child are classic signs of hypoglycemia. High blood sugar usually develops more gradually with thirst and frequent urination.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb5aac9c3","lv":"emt","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:peds","q":"Why should blood glucose be checked in a sick infant or child with altered mental status, even without diabetes?","a":"Children have small glucose stores and can become hypoglycemic during illness","w":["Children cannot develop low blood sugar unless diabetic","Glucose checks are required before any pediatric transport","Altered mental status in children is always from fever"],"x":"Young children have limited glycogen reserves, so poor feeding or illness can cause hypoglycemia. Checking glucose helps identify a treatable cause.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd1e1f6ad","lv":"emt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:peds","q":"Which finding in a child with asthma suggests significant respiratory distress?","a":"Retractions between the ribs and nasal flaring","w":["Clear breath sounds and normal skin color","A dry cough only at night","Talking in full sentences while playing"],"x":"Retractions, nasal flaring and use of accessory muscles show increased work of breathing. Talking in full sentences and clear lungs suggest mild or no distress.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc84c4f90","lv":"emt","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:peds","q":"A young child cannot coordinate breathing with a metered-dose inhaler. What device helps deliver the medication?","a":"A spacer with a face mask","w":["A nasal cannula","A bag-valve mask alone","A nonrebreather mask"],"x":"A spacer holds the medication so the child can inhale it over several breaths, and a mask helps with young children. Oxygen masks and cannulas do not deliver inhaler medication.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0788e2bc","lv":"emt","d":"naloxone","sys":"c:peds","q":"A toddler found near a grandparent's pain pills is very sleepy with slow, shallow breathing. Which additional finding suggests opioid poisoning?","a":"Pinpoint pupils","w":["Widely dilated pupils","Hot, flushed, dry skin","Rapid heart rate and agitation"],"x":"Constricted pupils with respiratory depression and decreased responsiveness form the classic opioid toxidrome. Dilated pupils and agitation suggest stimulant or anticholinergic poisoning.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5f743c6d","lv":"emt","d":"naloxone","sys":"c:peds","q":"What is an advantage of giving naloxone by the intranasal route?","a":"It is absorbed through the nasal lining without needing a needle","w":["It works only after it is swallowed","It lasts much longer than injected naloxone","It does not need the airway to be managed"],"x":"Intranasal naloxone is absorbed through the nasal mucosa and avoids needlestick risk. It still wears off, and airway and ventilation remain the first priorities.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp11bfc7ca","lv":"emt","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"c:peds","q":"A caregiver says a feverish child was given acetaminophen. What information is most important for the EMT to obtain?","a":"When it was given and whether other medicines with acetaminophen were used","w":["Which brand name and flavor of the product was used","Whether the child liked the taste of the medicine","Whether the bottle was bought at a pharmacy or a store"],"x":"Timing and use of multiple products help identify possible accidental overdose, which is common with children's fever and cold medicines. Brand and flavor do not change the risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp627d166d","lv":"emt","d":"ibuprofen","sys":"c:peds","q":"Compared with acetaminophen, ibuprofen also has which effect?","a":"It reduces inflammation as well as pain and fever","w":["It reverses opioid effects as well as pain","It raises blood sugar as well as fever","It causes sleep as well as reducing pain"],"x":"Ibuprofen is an NSAID that relieves pain, fever and inflammation. Acetaminophen relieves pain and fever but has little anti-inflammatory effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1de49bb8","lv":"flight","d":"amiodarone","sys":"c:acls","q":"During an amiodarone infusion for stable wide-complex tachycardia, which adverse effects are most important to watch for?","a":"Hypotension and bradycardia","w":["Hypertension and tachycardia","Hypoglycemia and seizures","Hyperkalemia and peaked T waves"],"x":"IV amiodarone commonly causes hypotension and bradycardia through vasodilation and its beta-blocking and calcium-channel effects. It does not typically cause hypertension or tachycardia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5039a317","lv":"flight","d":"amiodarone","sys":"c:acls","q":"Amiodarone is primarily classified as which type of antiarrhythmic?","a":"Class III agent that prolongs repolarization by blocking potassium channels","w":["Class IB agent that shortens repolarization by blocking sodium channels","Class II agent that works only by blocking beta-1 receptors","Class IV agent that works only by blocking L-type calcium channels"],"x":"Amiodarone is a Class III antiarrhythmic that prolongs repolarization and the refractory period, though it also has Class I, II and IV effects. Lidocaine, not amiodarone, is the Class IB agent.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp33772a20","lv":"flight","d":"adenosine","sys":"c:acls","q":"How is adenosine administered for stable narrow-complex supraventricular tachycardia?","a":"Rapid IV push followed immediately by a saline flush","w":["Slow IV push over several minutes","Intramuscular injection into the deltoid","Diluted continuous infusion titrated to rate"],"x":"Adenosine has a half-life of seconds, so it must be pushed rapidly through a proximal IV and flushed right away to reach the heart. A slow push lets it be metabolized before it reaches the AV node.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp99d7cdb3","lv":"flight","d":"adenosine","sys":"c:acls","q":"Which patient factor may blunt the effect of adenosine, so the expected response may not occur?","a":"Recent theophylline use or heavy caffeine intake","w":["Current dipyridamole therapy","History of heart transplant","Current carbamazepine therapy"],"x":"Methylxanthines such as theophylline and caffeine block adenosine receptors and can reduce its effect. Dipyridamole and a transplanted heart do the opposite and increase sensitivity to adenosine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4d89ac8c","lv":"flight","d":"diltiazem","sys":"c:acls","q":"In which patient with rapid atrial fibrillation is IV diltiazem contraindicated?","a":"Atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White pre-excitation","w":["Atrial fibrillation in a patient with COPD","Atrial fibrillation in a patient with diabetes","New atrial fibrillation with narrow QRS and normal blood pressure"],"x":"Blocking the AV node in pre-excited atrial fibrillation can speed conduction down the accessory pathway and trigger ventricular fibrillation. COPD is not a contraindication; diltiazem is often chosen over beta blockers in these patients.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe798bb49","lv":"flight","d":"procainamide","sys":"c:acls","q":"Which ECG change is most important to monitor during a procainamide infusion?","a":"Widening of the QRS complex and prolongation of the QT interval","w":["Shortening of the PR interval","New ST elevation in the inferior leads","Peaked T waves with a shortened QT interval"],"x":"Procainamide is a sodium-channel blocker with Class III effects, so it widens the QRS and lengthens the QT, raising the risk of torsades. Peaked T waves with a short QT point to hyperkalemia, not procainamide toxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp692ad273","lv":"flight","d":"calcium-chloride","sys":"c:acls","q":"Why is a central line or large, secure IV preferred when giving calcium chloride?","a":"Extravasation can cause severe tissue necrosis","w":["It precipitates when exposed to light","It is only effective when given into a central vein","It causes anaphylaxis when given peripherally"],"x":"Calcium chloride is very irritating to tissue, and extravasation can cause necrosis and sloughing. It works when given peripherally in an emergency; a secure line is chosen for safety, not effectiveness.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd11d201d","lv":"flight","d":"sodium-bicarbonate","sys":"c:acls","q":"In cardiac arrest, sodium bicarbonate is reasonable in which situation rather than as routine therapy?","a":"Known pre-existing hyperkalemia","w":["Any arrest that lasts longer than ten minutes","Asystole of unknown cause","Pulseless electrical activity from hypovolemia"],"x":"Current guidelines advise against routine bicarbonate in arrest, but it is reasonable in special cases such as hyperkalemia or sodium-channel blocker overdose. Arrest length alone is not an indication.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp69b54715","lv":"flight","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:acls","q":"The main benefit of epinephrine during cardiac arrest comes from which effect?","a":"Alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction that raises coronary perfusion pressure","w":["Beta-2 bronchodilation that improves oxygenation","Direct conversion of ventricular fibrillation to sinus rhythm","Beta-1 slowing of conduction through the AV node"],"x":"Epinephrine's alpha-1 vasoconstriction raises aortic diastolic pressure and improves coronary and cerebral perfusion during CPR. It does not convert VF by itself, and its beta-1 effect speeds rather than slows conduction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcd063446","lv":"flight","d":"atropine","sys":"c:acls","q":"Atropine is least likely to increase heart rate in which patient with symptomatic bradycardia?","a":"A heart transplant recipient with a denervated heart","w":["Sinus bradycardia during vomiting","Bradycardia from increased vagal tone","Sinus bradycardia during endotracheal suctioning"],"x":"Atropine works by blocking vagal input, and a transplanted heart has no vagal innervation, so atropine has little effect. Vagally mediated bradycardia, such as during vomiting or suctioning, usually responds well.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2addd734","lv":"flight","d":"aspirin","sys":"c:acs","q":"How does aspirin reduce clot formation in acute coronary syndrome?","a":"Irreversibly inhibits cyclooxygenase, reducing thromboxane A2","w":["Reversibly blocks the platelet P2Y12 ADP receptor","Directly inhibits thrombin in the clot","Converts plasminogen to plasmin to dissolve the clot"],"x":"Aspirin permanently inactivates platelet cyclooxygenase, lowering thromboxane A2 and platelet aggregation for the life of the platelet. P2Y12 blockade describes drugs like clopidogrel and ticagrelor.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe3994c07","lv":"flight","d":"aspirin","sys":"c:acs","q":"Why is aspirin chewed rather than swallowed whole in suspected acute coronary syndrome?","a":"Chewing speeds absorption and the onset of platelet inhibition","w":["Chewing prevents gastrointestinal bleeding","Swallowed tablets are inactivated by stomach acid","Chewing lowers the risk of an allergic reaction"],"x":"Chewing a non-enteric-coated tablet allows faster absorption through the oral and gastric mucosa, so platelet inhibition starts sooner. It does not prevent GI bleeding.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe5109916","lv":"flight","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:acs","q":"Which recent medication use is a contraindication to nitroglycerin in a patient with chest pain?","a":"Sildenafil taken for erectile dysfunction","w":["Atorvastatin taken for high cholesterol","Metformin taken for diabetes","Lisinopril taken for hypertension"],"x":"Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors such as sildenafil combined with nitrates can cause severe, refractory hypotension. Statins, metformin and ACE inhibitors are not contraindications.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp74891b60","lv":"flight","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:acs","q":"A patient has ST elevation in II, III and aVF plus ST elevation in V4R. Why is nitroglycerin used with great caution?","a":"Right ventricular infarction makes the patient preload dependent","w":["Nitroglycerin causes coronary artery vasospasm","Nitroglycerin increases left ventricular afterload","Nitroglycerin is ineffective in inferior wall ischemia"],"x":"ST elevation in V4R suggests right ventricular involvement, and these patients rely on preload to maintain output, so venodilation from nitroglycerin can cause profound hypotension. Nitroglycerin relieves, rather than causes, vasospasm.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp98953ffa","lv":"flight","d":"heparin","sys":"c:acs","q":"During transfer, a patient on a heparin infusion has a platelet count that has fallen by more than half from baseline and a new leg clot. What should be recognized?","a":"Possible heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","w":["Dilutional thrombocytopenia from IV fluids","Expected heparin effect needing no action","Immune reaction to aspirin given earlier"],"x":"A platelet drop of more than half with new thrombosis is classic for heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, which should be reported to the provider right away. It is a paradoxically prothrombotic immune reaction, not an expected effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7377d999","lv":"flight","d":"morphine","sys":"c:acs","q":"What is a concern with giving morphine for chest pain in acute coronary syndrome?","a":"It may delay absorption and onset of oral P2Y12 inhibitors","w":["It directly increases myocardial oxygen demand","It causes coronary artery vasospasm","It inactivates heparin in the IV line"],"x":"Opioids slow gastric emptying, which can delay absorption of oral P2Y12 inhibitors such as ticagrelor and clopidogrel. Morphine tends to lower, not raise, myocardial oxygen demand.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8ed902c4","lv":"flight","d":"fentanyl","sys":"c:acs","q":"Why might fentanyl be preferred over morphine for ischemic chest pain in a patient with borderline blood pressure?","a":"It causes less histamine release and less hypotension","w":["It has a longer duration so fewer doses are needed","It has added antiplatelet effects","It does not cause respiratory depression"],"x":"Fentanyl releases little histamine, so it is less likely than morphine to cause vasodilation and hypotension. It is shorter acting than morphine and still causes respiratory depression.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf944dd07","lv":"flight","d":"metoprolol","sys":"c:acs","q":"Which finding makes early IV metoprolol inappropriate in a patient with STEMI?","a":"Signs of heart failure or a low-output state","w":["Sinus tachycardia with hypertension","Ongoing ischemic chest pain","History of hyperlipidemia"],"x":"Early IV beta blockade increases the risk of cardiogenic shock in patients with heart failure, low output or other shock risk factors. Hypertension with ongoing ischemia is a setting where it may be reasonable.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe295b609","lv":"flight","d":"tenecteplase","sys":"c:acs","q":"How is tenecteplase given for STEMI?","a":"As a single weight-based IV bolus","w":["As a bolus followed by a 90-minute infusion","As a continuous infusion over a full day","As an intracoronary injection only during PCI"],"x":"Tenecteplase is given as one weight-based IV bolus, which is convenient in transport. The bolus-plus-infusion regimen describes alteplase.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp194d0785","lv":"flight","d":"alteplase","sys":"c:acs","q":"Which is an absolute contraindication to alteplase for STEMI?","a":"Any prior intracranial hemorrhage","w":["Hypertension controlled on medication","Age older than 75 years","Current daily aspirin use"],"x":"Any history of intracranial hemorrhage is an absolute contraindication to fibrinolysis. Advanced age and chronic hypertension are factors to weigh, not absolute contraindications.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpaf083809","lv":"flight","d":"nicardipine","sys":"c:htn","q":"Nicardipine belongs to which drug class?","a":"Dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","w":["Nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","Organic nitrate vasodilator","Selective beta-1 blocker"],"x":"Nicardipine is a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker that mainly dilates arterioles with little effect on the AV node. Nondihydropyridines such as diltiazem slow AV conduction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3ca53d0f","lv":"flight","d":"nicardipine","sys":"c:htn","q":"Which adverse effects should be monitored during a nicardipine infusion?","a":"Reflex tachycardia and headache","w":["Bradycardia and heart block","Bronchospasm and wheezing","Hyperkalemia and peaked T waves"],"x":"Arterial vasodilation from nicardipine can cause headache and reflex tachycardia. It has little effect on the AV node, so heart block is unlikely.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp38b0a4a7","lv":"flight","d":"labetalol","sys":"c:htn","q":"How does labetalol lower blood pressure?","a":"Blocks alpha-1 receptors and beta receptors","w":["Blocks only beta-1 receptors","Stimulates central alpha-2 receptors","Directly relaxes arteriolar smooth muscle"],"x":"Labetalol combines alpha-1 blockade, which lowers vascular resistance, with nonselective beta blockade, which limits reflex tachycardia. A pure beta-1 blocker would be something like metoprolol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbfc141cf","lv":"flight","d":"labetalol","sys":"c:htn","q":"Which history makes labetalol a poor choice for a hypertensive emergency?","a":"Severe asthma with active bronchospasm","w":["Pregnancy with severe preeclampsia","Acute aortic dissection","Acute ischemic stroke before thrombolysis"],"x":"Labetalol's nonselective beta blockade can worsen bronchospasm in asthma. It is a first-line choice in severe preeclampsia, aortic dissection and stroke blood pressure control.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp19aa2d58","lv":"flight","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:htn","q":"In which hypertensive emergency is IV nitroglycerin especially useful?","a":"Hypertensive acute pulmonary edema","w":["Eclampsia","Hypertensive intracranial hemorrhage","Aortic dissection as the only agent"],"x":"Nitroglycerin quickly reduces preload, and afterload at higher doses, which helps in hypertensive pulmonary edema. In aortic dissection it should not be used alone because it can cause reflex tachycardia that raises shear stress.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd728c2fc","lv":"flight","d":"hydralazine","sys":"c:htn","q":"How does hydralazine lower blood pressure?","a":"Directly relaxes arteriolar smooth muscle","w":["Blocks beta-1 receptors in the heart","Blocks calcium channels in the AV node","Inhibits angiotensin-converting enzyme"],"x":"Hydralazine is a direct arteriolar vasodilator, which is why reflex tachycardia is common. It does not block beta receptors or ACE.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcd6b8435","lv":"flight","d":"clevidipine","sys":"c:htn","q":"Which allergy is a contraindication to clevidipine?","a":"Allergy to soy or egg products","w":["Allergy to sulfonamide antibiotics","Allergy to penicillin","Allergy to iodinated contrast"],"x":"Clevidipine comes in a lipid emulsion that contains soy and egg components, so these allergies are contraindications. Sulfa and penicillin allergies are unrelated.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp553c2294","lv":"flight","d":"clevidipine","sys":"c:htn","q":"What property makes clevidipine easy to titrate?","a":"Ultra-short half-life from breakdown by blood and tissue esterases","w":["Long half-life that keeps levels steady","Elimination unchanged by the kidneys","Liver metabolism into long-acting active metabolites"],"x":"Clevidipine is broken down by esterases within minutes, so blood pressure responds quickly to rate changes. A long half-life would make it harder, not easier, to titrate.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp78879b61","lv":"flight","d":"esmolol","sys":"c:htn","q":"In acute aortic dissection, why is esmolol started before a vasodilator?","a":"To prevent reflex tachycardia and reduce shear stress on the aorta","w":["To increase cardiac contractility","To raise preload before vasodilation","To reverse vasodilator toxicity"],"x":"Lowering heart rate and contractility first reduces the force on the aortic wall and prevents the reflex tachycardia a vasodilator would cause. Esmolol decreases, not increases, contractility.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1724e945","lv":"flight","d":"nitroprusside","sys":"c:htn","q":"After a prolonged nitroprusside infusion, a patient develops worsening metabolic acidosis and confusion. What should be suspected?","a":"Cyanide toxicity","w":["Serotonin syndrome","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Thyroid storm"],"x":"Nitroprusside releases cyanide as it is metabolized, and accumulation causes metabolic acidosis and altered mental status. Report these findings to the provider promptly.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9204e0b3","lv":"flight","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"Per Surviving Sepsis Campaign guidelines, which vasopressor is first-line for septic shock in adults?","a":"Norepinephrine","w":["Dopamine","Phenylephrine","Vasopressin"],"x":"Norepinephrine is the recommended first-line vasopressor in septic shock. Vasopressin is usually added to norepinephrine rather than used first.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa8908547","lv":"flight","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"Norepinephrine is infusing through a peripheral IV and the site becomes blanched and cool. Which agent is infiltrated into the area?","a":"Phentolamine","w":["Hyaluronidase","Dexrazoxane","Sodium thiosulfate"],"x":"Phentolamine is an alpha blocker that reverses local vasoconstriction from norepinephrine extravasation. Hyaluronidase is used for some non-vasopressor extravasations.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbac9148a","lv":"flight","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"Which lab change is an expected effect of an epinephrine infusion and can complicate lactate interpretation?","a":"Rising lactate from beta-2 stimulated glycolysis","w":["Falling lactate from improved perfusion alone","Hypoglycemia from increased insulin release","Hyperkalemia from potassium leaving cells"],"x":"Epinephrine stimulates beta-2 receptors on muscle, increasing glycolysis and lactate even when perfusion is adequate. It tends to raise glucose and lower potassium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb5d2407d","lv":"flight","d":"dopamine","sys":"c:shock","q":"Compared with norepinephrine in shock, dopamine is associated with which outcome?","a":"More tachyarrhythmias","w":["Fewer tachyarrhythmias","Better protection of kidney function","Lower mortality in cardiogenic shock"],"x":"Trials comparing the two found more arrhythmias with dopamine, and a subgroup of cardiogenic shock patients did worse. Low-dose dopamine does not protect the kidneys.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2e09da41","lv":"flight","d":"vasopressin","sys":"c:shock","q":"How does vasopressin raise blood pressure?","a":"Stimulates V1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle","w":["Stimulates alpha-1 adrenergic receptors","Stimulates beta-1 receptors to increase contractility","Inhibits phosphodiesterase-3 in cardiac muscle"],"x":"Vasopressin acts on V1 receptors to cause vasoconstriction independently of adrenergic receptors. This is why it still works when catecholamine receptors respond poorly.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc64554ef","lv":"flight","d":"vasopressin","sys":"c:shock","q":"In septic shock, how is vasopressin typically used?","a":"Added to norepinephrine to raise MAP or reduce norepinephrine need","w":["As the first-line single vasopressor","As an inotrope for low cardiac output","As a treatment for symptomatic bradycardia"],"x":"Guidelines suggest adding vasopressin to norepinephrine rather than using it as first-line therapy. It has no inotropic effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp236f0a68","lv":"flight","d":"dobutamine","sys":"c:shock","q":"A patient in cardiogenic shock is started on dobutamine. Which effect may cause blood pressure to fall?","a":"Beta-2 mediated peripheral vasodilation","w":["Alpha-1 mediated vasoconstriction","Activation of vasopressin receptors","Negative inotropy on the myocardium"],"x":"Dobutamine is mainly a beta-1 inotrope but also has beta-2 effects that dilate vessels and can lower blood pressure. It increases, not decreases, contractility.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp903fae90","lv":"flight","d":"phenylephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"Which effect is expected after phenylephrine is given for hypotension?","a":"Reflex bradycardia as blood pressure rises","w":["Marked tachycardia from beta-1 stimulation","Bronchodilation from beta-2 stimulation","Increased myocardial contractility"],"x":"Phenylephrine is a pure alpha-1 agonist, so rising blood pressure triggers a baroreceptor reflex that slows the heart. It has essentially no beta activity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3562c341","lv":"flight","d":"milrinone","sys":"c:shock","q":"Which patient factor increases the risk of milrinone accumulation and hypotension?","a":"Renal impairment","w":["Hepatic impairment","Current beta-blocker therapy","Obesity"],"x":"Milrinone is cleared mainly by the kidneys, so it accumulates in renal impairment and can cause hypotension and arrhythmias. Because it works beyond the beta receptor, beta blockers do not raise its levels.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4ee3e83f","lv":"flight","d":"methylene-blue","sys":"c:shock","q":"Before methylene blue is given for refractory vasoplegic shock, which medication history raises concern?","a":"Use of an SSRI or other serotonergic drug","w":["Use of a statin","Use of a proton pump inhibitor","Use of a loop diuretic"],"x":"Methylene blue inhibits monoamine oxidase and carries a boxed warning for serotonin syndrome with serotonergic drugs. Statins and PPIs do not share this risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp85066edd","lv":"flight","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:airway","q":"During continuous albuterol for severe bronchospasm, which electrolyte change should be monitored?","a":"Hypokalemia","w":["Hyperkalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hypernatremia"],"x":"Beta-2 stimulation shifts potassium into cells, so repeated or continuous albuterol can lower serum potassium. Albuterol is actually used to treat hyperkalemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7fc9e646","lv":"flight","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:airway","q":"How can albuterol be delivered to an intubated patient during transport?","a":"In-line nebulizer or inhaler with spacer in the ventilator circuit","w":["Direct liquid instillation down the endotracheal tube","Continuous IV infusion through a central line","Subcutaneous injection into the abdomen"],"x":"Albuterol is delivered as an aerosol placed in the ventilator circuit, by nebulizer or an inhaler with an adapter. IV albuterol is not available in the US.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpca88ff8e","lv":"flight","d":"ipratropium","sys":"c:airway","q":"How does ipratropium produce bronchodilation?","a":"Blocks muscarinic receptors in airway smooth muscle","w":["Stimulates beta-2 receptors in airway smooth muscle","Blocks leukotriene receptors","Stabilizes mast cells to prevent histamine release"],"x":"Ipratropium is an anticholinergic that blocks vagally mediated bronchoconstriction. Beta-2 stimulation describes albuterol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp213a68ae","lv":"flight","d":"methylprednisolone","sys":"c:airway","q":"Why is a corticosteroid such as methylprednisolone given early in a severe asthma exacerbation?","a":"It reduces airway inflammation, with benefit developing over hours","w":["It causes bronchodilation within minutes","It thins and clears mucus plugs","It suppresses the cough reflex"],"x":"Steroids treat the inflammation behind the exacerbation, but the effect takes hours, so they are given early alongside bronchodilators. They are not rapid bronchodilators.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdfe72125","lv":"flight","d":"magnesium-sulfate","sys":"c:airway","q":"During an IV magnesium infusion for severe asthma, which adverse effects should be watched for?","a":"Hypotension and flushing","w":["Tremor and tachycardia","Hyperkalemia and peaked T waves","Hypertension and seizures"],"x":"Magnesium relaxes smooth muscle, including blood vessels, so hypotension and flushing are common. Tremor and tachycardia are typical of beta agonists.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfc74b939","lv":"flight","d":"racemic-epinephrine","sys":"c:airway","q":"After nebulized racemic epinephrine for croup, why must the child be observed for a period of time?","a":"Symptoms can return as the effect wears off","w":["It can cause delayed hypoglycemia","It can trigger a delayed allergic reaction","It causes late-onset bronchospasm"],"x":"Racemic epinephrine reduces airway swelling for a limited time, so stridor can come back once it wears off. That is why observation after treatment is standard.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1feea419","lv":"flight","d":"levalbuterol","sys":"c:airway","q":"Levalbuterol is best described as which of the following?","a":"The active R-enantiomer of albuterol","w":["A long-acting beta-2 agonist for maintenance","An inhaled anticholinergic bronchodilator","A methylxanthine bronchodilator"],"x":"Levalbuterol is the R-isomer of albuterol and is a short-acting beta-2 agonist used for acute bronchospasm. It is not a long-acting maintenance agent.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7b45b0df","lv":"flight","d":"terbutaline","sys":"c:airway","q":"In severe asthma when inhaled therapy is not reaching the airways, by which route is terbutaline given?","a":"Subcutaneous injection","w":["Rectal suppository","Intranasal spray","Inhaled dry powder"],"x":"Injectable terbutaline is given subcutaneously, reaching the airways through the bloodstream when bronchospasm limits inhaled delivery. It is not available as an inhaler in the US.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp27f498c5","lv":"flight","d":"dexamethasone","sys":"c:airway","q":"Why is dexamethasone used for a child with croup?","a":"It is a long-acting steroid that reduces airway swelling","w":["It causes immediate vasoconstriction of the airway","It has direct antiviral activity","It causes bronchodilation through beta-2 receptors"],"x":"Dexamethasone reduces subglottic inflammation for a prolonged period and decreases return visits and severity. Immediate vasoconstriction describes racemic epinephrine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe98099cb","lv":"flight","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:airway","q":"In anaphylaxis with wheezing, how does epinephrine relieve bronchospasm?","a":"Stimulates beta-2 receptors to relax bronchial smooth muscle","w":["Stimulates alpha-1 receptors to relax bronchial smooth muscle","Blocks muscarinic receptors in the airway","Blocks histamine-1 receptors in the airway"],"x":"Epinephrine's beta-2 effect relaxes airway smooth muscle, while its alpha-1 effect reduces mucosal swelling and raises blood pressure. Alpha-1 stimulation does not relax bronchial smooth muscle.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa50585c0","lv":"flight","d":"etomidate","sys":"c:rsi","q":"Which adverse effect is associated with etomidate?","a":"Transient adrenal suppression","w":["Profound hypotension on induction","Triggering of malignant hyperthermia","Bronchospasm from histamine release"],"x":"Etomidate inhibits 11-beta-hydroxylase, briefly reducing cortisol production. It is chosen for induction precisely because it rarely causes hypotension.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcc596e83","lv":"flight","d":"etomidate","sys":"c:rsi","q":"Why is etomidate often selected for induction in a hypotensive trauma patient?","a":"It has minimal effect on blood pressure and heart rate","w":["It provides strong analgesia","It has a long duration of sedation","It causes significant bronchodilation"],"x":"Etomidate is hemodynamically neutral, making it useful in unstable patients. It provides no analgesia and is short acting, so analgesia and sedation must follow.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd25fe021","lv":"flight","d":"ketamine","sys":"c:rsi","q":"Why is ketamine a good induction agent for a patient intubated for severe asthma?","a":"It has bronchodilating properties","w":["It reduces airway secretions","It lowers heart rate","It never causes emergence reactions"],"x":"Ketamine's sympathomimetic effects relax bronchial smooth muscle, which helps in severe bronchospasm. It increases, rather than reduces, secretions.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5989fcd3","lv":"flight","d":"rocuronium","sys":"c:rsi","q":"Which agent rapidly reverses rocuronium neuromuscular blockade?","a":"Sugammadex","w":["Flumazenil","Dantrolene","Naloxone"],"x":"Sugammadex encapsulates aminosteroid blockers such as rocuronium and vecuronium, reversing them quickly. Dantrolene treats malignant hyperthermia, not paralysis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5a0c379b","lv":"flight","d":"rocuronium","sys":"c:rsi","q":"After RSI with rocuronium, why is early post-intubation sedation and analgesia critical?","a":"Paralysis outlasts the induction agent, risking awareness","w":["Rocuronium itself lowers the seizure threshold","Rocuronium has sedative effects that wear off quickly","Sedation speeds the metabolism of rocuronium"],"x":"Rocuronium has no sedative or analgesic effect and lasts much longer than etomidate or ketamine induction doses, so a paralyzed patient may be awake. Paralysis also hides signs of distress, so sedation must be given proactively.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdc97f5e7","lv":"flight","d":"succinylcholine","sys":"c:rsi","q":"Which patient should not receive succinylcholine?","a":"A patient with a spinal cord injury from two weeks ago","w":["A patient with a femur fracture from one hour ago","A patient with a history of asthma","A patient intubated for an ongoing seizure"],"x":"After denervation injuries, burns or crush injuries older than a few days, extra acetylcholine receptors develop and succinylcholine can cause dangerous hyperkalemia. An acute fracture does not carry this risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6db47f7c","lv":"flight","d":"succinylcholine","sys":"c:rsi","q":"After succinylcholine, a patient develops jaw rigidity, a rapidly rising end-tidal CO2, tachycardia and then fever. What should be recognized?","a":"Malignant hyperthermia","w":["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Serotonin syndrome","Anaphylaxis"],"x":"Succinylcholine can trigger malignant hyperthermia in susceptible patients, and a rapid rise in end-tidal CO2 is often the earliest sign. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome follows antipsychotics and develops over days.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb0365eab","lv":"flight","d":"propofol","sys":"c:rsi","q":"Which hemodynamic effect is most common after propofol induction?","a":"Hypotension from vasodilation and myocardial depression","w":["Hypertension and tachycardia","Bradycardia with a rise in blood pressure","No significant change in blood pressure"],"x":"Propofol dilates vessels and depresses the heart, so hypotension is common, especially in volume-depleted patients. Hypertension and tachycardia are more typical of ketamine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0a27919a","lv":"flight","d":"vecuronium","sys":"c:rsi","q":"Vecuronium belongs to which drug class?","a":"Nondepolarizing neuromuscular blocker","w":["Depolarizing neuromuscular blocker","Benzodiazepine sedative","Dissociative anesthetic"],"x":"Vecuronium is a nondepolarizing aminosteroid paralytic that competes with acetylcholine at the motor end plate. Succinylcholine is the only depolarizing blocker in common use.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6b7411f0","lv":"flight","d":"atropine","sys":"c:rsi","q":"Why might atropine be given around RSI in a young infant receiving succinylcholine?","a":"To prevent or treat bradycardia from vagal stimulation","w":["To prolong the neuromuscular blockade","To prevent succinylcholine-induced hyperkalemia","To lower intracranial pressure"],"x":"Infants have high vagal tone, and laryngoscopy and succinylcholine can cause bradycardia, which atropine blocks. It has no effect on paralysis or potassium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp67f876f4","lv":"flight","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:allergy","q":"What is the preferred first route for epinephrine in anaphylaxis?","a":"Intramuscular injection into the anterolateral thigh","w":["Subcutaneous injection into the upper arm","Oral solution swallowed","Rapid IV push of the cardiac arrest formulation"],"x":"IM injection into the thigh gives faster, more reliable absorption than subcutaneous injection. IV push of the arrest concentration in a patient with a pulse risks severe hypertension and arrhythmias.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2461926e","lv":"flight","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:allergy","q":"Hypotension persists despite repeated IM epinephrine and IV fluids in anaphylaxis. What epinephrine approach is next?","a":"A titrated IV epinephrine infusion","w":["Switching to an oral antihistamine","Repeating a corticosteroid instead","Changing to subcutaneous epinephrine"],"x":"Refractory anaphylaxis is treated with a closely monitored IV epinephrine infusion. Antihistamines and steroids do not reverse shock.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc3cef75b","lv":"flight","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"c:allergy","q":"Why is diphenhydramine not a substitute for epinephrine in anaphylaxis?","a":"It relieves itching and hives but does not reverse airway swelling or shock","w":["It severely worsens hypotension","It is only available as an oral tablet","It blocks the effect of epinephrine"],"x":"H1 antihistamines treat skin symptoms but do not reverse airway obstruction, bronchospasm or shock. Epinephrine is the only first-line treatment.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp55444c91","lv":"flight","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"c:allergy","q":"Which adverse effects of diphenhydramine are most likely in an older adult?","a":"Sedation, confusion and urinary retention","w":["Bradycardia and bronchospasm","Hypoglycemia and sweating","Diarrhea and excess salivation"],"x":"Diphenhydramine is sedating and strongly anticholinergic, causing confusion and urinary retention in older adults. Salivation and diarrhea are cholinergic, the opposite effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5b096509","lv":"flight","d":"methylprednisolone","sys":"c:allergy","q":"Which statement about corticosteroids such as methylprednisolone in anaphylaxis is correct?","a":"They have a delayed onset and are adjuncts, not first-line therapy","w":["They act within minutes to reverse hypotension","They can replace epinephrine in mild cases","They should be given before epinephrine"],"x":"Steroids take hours to work and do not treat the immediate life threats of anaphylaxis. Epinephrine always comes first.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa909db50","lv":"flight","d":"methylprednisolone","sys":"c:allergy","q":"Which lab change is common after IV methylprednisolone, especially in patients with diabetes?","a":"Hyperglycemia","w":["Hypoglycemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypercalcemia"],"x":"Glucocorticoids increase glucose production and insulin resistance, raising blood glucose. They tend to lower, not raise, potassium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2b7b4248","lv":"flight","d":"dexamethasone","sys":"c:allergy","q":"How does dexamethasone compare with methylprednisolone?","a":"Longer acting with minimal mineralocorticoid activity","w":["Shorter acting with strong mineralocorticoid activity","It is an antihistamine rather than a steroid","It is a mast cell stabilizer rather than a steroid"],"x":"Dexamethasone is a long-acting glucocorticoid with almost no salt-retaining effect. Both drugs are corticosteroids.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3b5c71e2","lv":"flight","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:allergy","q":"A patient in anaphylaxis treated with IM epinephrine is still wheezing. Why is albuterol added?","a":"To treat persistent bronchospasm despite epinephrine","w":["To treat persistent hypotension","To treat laryngeal edema and stridor","To treat widespread hives"],"x":"Inhaled albuterol is an adjunct for lower-airway bronchospasm that persists after epinephrine. It does not treat shock or upper-airway swelling.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0209402c","lv":"flight","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:allergy","q":"A patient taking metoprolol has anaphylaxis that does not respond to repeated epinephrine. Which medication may help?","a":"Glucagon","w":["Additional diphenhydramine","Ondansetron","Calcium chloride"],"x":"Beta blockers can blunt the response to epinephrine, and glucagon raises heart rate and contractility through a non-beta pathway. More antihistamine will not reverse the shock.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0acc9e00","lv":"flight","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:allergy","q":"Why does glucagon work in a beta-blocked patient?","a":"It raises intracellular cAMP without using beta receptors","w":["It blocks histamine receptors","It is a potent alpha-1 agonist","It stabilizes mast cells"],"x":"Glucagon activates its own receptor, which increases cAMP and improves cardiac output even when beta receptors are blocked. Nausea and vomiting are common, so protect the airway.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpecb79761","lv":"flight","d":"lorazepam","sys":"c:neuro","q":"After IV lorazepam for status epilepticus, what is most important to monitor?","a":"Respiratory effort and airway patency","w":["Blood glucose for hyperglycemia","Blood pressure for hypertension","Heart rate for tachycardia"],"x":"Benzodiazepines can cause respiratory depression and airway loss, especially with repeated doses. Hypotension, not hypertension, is the hemodynamic concern.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2fcc0bf6","lv":"flight","d":"midazolam","sys":"c:neuro","q":"A patient is actively seizing with no IV access. Which route of midazolam has been shown to be effective?","a":"Intramuscular","w":["Oral","Subcutaneous","Transdermal"],"x":"IM midazolam stops seizures as well as IV lorazepam and is faster to give when there is no IV. Oral and transdermal routes are too slow and impractical.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2559d9de","lv":"flight","d":"levetiracetam","sys":"c:neuro","q":"Seizures continue after an adequate benzodiazepine dose. What is the role of levetiracetam?","a":"Second-line IV antiseizure medicine after benzodiazepines","w":["First-line agent given before benzodiazepines","Agent used only for alcohol withdrawal","Sedative used for intubation"],"x":"Levetiracetam is one of the second-line agents for benzodiazepine-refractory status epilepticus. Benzodiazepines remain first line.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3d352156","lv":"flight","d":"hypertonic-saline-3-percent","sys":"c:neuro","q":"How does hypertonic saline lower intracranial pressure?","a":"It creates an osmotic gradient that draws water out of brain tissue","w":["It constricts the cerebral arteries","It reduces CSF production by inhibiting carbonic anhydrase","It causes diuresis of free water by the kidneys"],"x":"Raising serum sodium pulls water from brain tissue into the blood, reducing edema and ICP. Carbonic anhydrase inhibition describes acetazolamide.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpeca1433c","lv":"flight","d":"hypertonic-saline-3-percent","sys":"c:neuro","q":"During hypertonic saline therapy for raised ICP, which lab is most important to monitor?","a":"Serum sodium","w":["Serum potassium","Serum magnesium","Serum phosphate"],"x":"Hypertonic saline works by raising serum sodium, so levels are monitored to keep them in target and avoid overcorrection. Potassium is not the main concern.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp62e813b0","lv":"flight","d":"diazepam","sys":"c:neuro","q":"How does diazepam compare with lorazepam?","a":"It has a longer half-life with active metabolites","w":["It has no active metabolites","It is much shorter acting","It is not a benzodiazepine"],"x":"Diazepam is highly lipid soluble with active metabolites that prolong its effect. Lorazepam has no active metabolites.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf8064b11","lv":"flight","d":"mannitol","sys":"c:neuro","q":"In a hypotensive patient with traumatic brain injury, why might hypertonic saline be favored over mannitol?","a":"Mannitol causes osmotic diuresis that can worsen hypotension","w":["Mannitol raises intracranial pressure","Mannitol cannot cross into the bloodstream","Mannitol causes severe hyperkalemia"],"x":"Mannitol lowers ICP but also causes diuresis and volume loss, which can worsen hypotension and lower cerebral perfusion. Hypertonic saline tends to support blood pressure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp524db50e","lv":"flight","d":"alteplase","sys":"c:neuro","q":"Before alteplase for acute ischemic stroke, blood pressure must be lowered below which level?","a":"185/110 mmHg","w":["220/120 mmHg","160/90 mmHg","140/80 mmHg"],"x":"Guidelines require blood pressure below 185/110 before thrombolysis and below 180/105 afterward to limit bleeding risk. The 220/120 threshold applies to patients not receiving thrombolytics.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8d46b882","lv":"flight","d":"alteplase","sys":"c:neuro","q":"During an alteplase infusion for stroke, the patient develops a sudden severe headache, vomiting and a falling level of consciousness. What should be recognized?","a":"Possible symptomatic intracranial hemorrhage","w":["Expected reperfusion headache","Recurrent stroke in a new vascular territory","Orolingual angioedema"],"x":"New headache, vomiting or neurologic decline during thrombolysis suggests intracranial hemorrhage and must be reported immediately. Angioedema causes tongue and lip swelling, not these signs.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp48da5d28","lv":"flight","d":"tenecteplase","sys":"c:neuro","q":"What practical advantage does tenecteplase have over alteplase for stroke transport?","a":"It is a single IV bolus with no infusion running during transport","w":["It carries no risk of bleeding","It can be given regardless of blood pressure","It does not require brain imaging first"],"x":"Tenecteplase is given as one bolus, avoiding an hour-long infusion. It still carries bleeding risk and requires imaging and blood pressure control.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6b663dd1","lv":"flight","d":"fentanyl","sys":"c:sedation","q":"After a rapid IV fentanyl push, the patient becomes very hard to ventilate with a rigid chest. What should be recognized?","a":"Opioid-induced chest wall rigidity","w":["Bronchospasm from histamine release","Anaphylaxis to fentanyl","Malignant hyperthermia"],"x":"Rapid, large fentanyl doses can cause chest wall and glottic rigidity that makes ventilation difficult. Fentanyl releases little histamine, so bronchospasm is unlikely.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp620e7c32","lv":"flight","d":"morphine","sys":"c:sedation","q":"Why is morphine used cautiously in renal failure?","a":"Active metabolites accumulate, prolonging sedation and respiratory depression","w":["It is directly toxic to the kidneys","It causes severe hyperkalemia","It is eliminated unchanged only by the liver"],"x":"Morphine's active metabolites are cleared by the kidneys and build up in renal failure. Morphine itself is not nephrotoxic.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9f913fd9","lv":"flight","d":"midazolam","sys":"c:sedation","q":"Why is flumazenil used cautiously to reverse midazolam?","a":"It can trigger seizures in patients on long-term benzodiazepines","w":["It causes respiratory depression","It worsens hypotension","It increases the effect of midazolam"],"x":"Flumazenil can provoke withdrawal seizures in benzodiazepine-dependent patients or those with seizure-prone ingestions. It reverses, rather than increases, benzodiazepine effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0e15c3df","lv":"flight","d":"ketamine","sys":"c:sedation","q":"Which adverse effect is characteristic of ketamine?","a":"Emergence reactions such as vivid dreams or agitation","w":["Profound bradycardia and hypotension","Apnea at analgesic doses","Adrenal suppression"],"x":"Ketamine can cause hallucinations, vivid dreams and agitation on emergence. It usually supports, rather than lowers, heart rate and blood pressure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpaa85545a","lv":"flight","d":"propofol","sys":"c:sedation","q":"An intubated trauma patient on propofol grimaces and becomes tachycardic when moved. Which concept applies?","a":"Propofol provides no analgesia, so pain needs separate treatment","w":["Propofol is a strong analgesic so more is needed","Tachycardia is a common propofol side effect","Propofol causes pain only on injection"],"x":"Propofol is a sedative-hypnotic without pain relief, so signs of pain call for an analgesic. Propofol more often causes bradycardia and hypotension than tachycardia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp450af2dc","lv":"flight","d":"hydromorphone","sys":"c:sedation","q":"What is a key safety point when a patient is switched from morphine to hydromorphone?","a":"Hydromorphone is several times more potent, so mix-ups can cause overdose","w":["Hydromorphone is much weaker, so pain may increase","Hydromorphone has no risk of respiratory depression","Hydromorphone must be given only by mouth"],"x":"Hydromorphone is much more potent than morphine, and confusing the two is a known cause of fatal overdoses. It still causes respiratory depression.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp11f63ce3","lv":"flight","d":"lorazepam","sys":"c:sedation","q":"A patient on a prolonged high-rate lorazepam infusion develops rising anion gap and osmolar gap. What should be suspected?","a":"Propylene glycol toxicity from the drug's diluent","w":["Methanol poisoning","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Lactic acidosis from sepsis"],"x":"IV lorazepam contains propylene glycol, which can accumulate and cause metabolic acidosis and a high osmolar gap. Report this to the provider.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpda7f19a4","lv":"flight","d":"dexmedetomidine","sys":"c:sedation","q":"Which feature distinguishes dexmedetomidine from most other sedatives?","a":"Sedation with little respiratory depression","w":["Strong amnesia through GABA receptors","Analgesia through mu-opioid receptors","Dissociation through NMDA blockade"],"x":"Dexmedetomidine is a central alpha-2 agonist that sedates while largely preserving respiratory drive. GABA-mediated amnesia describes benzodiazepines and propofol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp644b0f8a","lv":"flight","d":"ketorolac","sys":"c:sedation","q":"In which patient is ketorolac inappropriate?","a":"A trauma patient with suspected active hemorrhage","w":["A patient with renal colic and normal kidney function","A patient with musculoskeletal back pain","A patient with a migraine headache"],"x":"Ketorolac inhibits platelet function and raises bleeding risk, so it is avoided with active or suspected bleeding. Renal colic with normal kidneys is a common indication.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5a656f93","lv":"flight","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"c:sedation","q":"What is the main toxicity risk with IV acetaminophen?","a":"Liver injury, especially when combined with other sources","w":["Respiratory depression","Gastrointestinal bleeding","Platelet dysfunction"],"x":"Acetaminophen overdose, often from stacking IV and oral products, can cause liver failure. Unlike NSAIDs, it does not impair platelets or cause GI bleeding.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp31a3bc9e","lv":"flight","d":"droperidol","sys":"c:psych","q":"What does droperidol's boxed warning concern?","a":"QT prolongation and torsades de pointes","w":["Severe liver toxicity","Agranulocytosis","Serotonin syndrome"],"x":"Droperidol carries a boxed warning for QT prolongation and torsades, so ECG monitoring is important. Agranulocytosis is associated with clozapine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5d2f5207","lv":"flight","d":"droperidol","sys":"c:psych","q":"What makes droperidol useful for severe agitation in emergency settings?","a":"Rapid onset of sedation when given IM or IV","w":["It has no cardiac effects","It is a long-acting benzodiazepine","It reverses alcohol intoxication"],"x":"Droperidol, a butyrophenone, sedates quickly by IM or IV route. It does have cardiac effects, including QT prolongation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp052140b1","lv":"flight","d":"haloperidol","sys":"c:psych","q":"Hours after haloperidol, a patient has a twisted neck and eyes fixed upward. What should be recognized?","a":"Acute dystonic reaction","w":["Generalized seizure","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Serotonin syndrome"],"x":"Sustained muscle spasms of the neck, eyes or jaw are an acute dystonic reaction from dopamine blockade. The patient stays awake, unlike in a seizure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9bb7f5c8","lv":"flight","d":"haloperidol","sys":"c:psych","q":"Days after starting haloperidol, a patient has high fever, lead-pipe rigidity, confusion and unstable blood pressure. What should be recognized?","a":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","w":["Serotonin syndrome","Malignant hyperthermia","Acute dystonic reaction"],"x":"Fever, rigidity, altered mental status and autonomic instability after an antipsychotic suggest neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Serotonin syndrome more often shows clonus and hyperreflexia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp87d94a96","lv":"flight","d":"midazolam","sys":"c:psych","q":"What is an advantage of IM midazolam for an agitated patient without IV access?","a":"Rapid absorption and onset compared with other IM benzodiazepines","w":["It causes no respiratory depression","It has a very long duration of action","It requires no monitoring afterward"],"x":"Midazolam is water soluble and absorbed quickly after IM injection, while IM lorazepam is slower. It can still depress breathing, so monitoring is needed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpef4680a7","lv":"flight","d":"midazolam","sys":"c:psych","q":"An agitated patient who is heavily intoxicated with alcohol is given midazolam. What is the main risk?","a":"Additive respiratory depression","w":["Hypertensive crisis","QT prolongation","Severe hyperthermia"],"x":"Alcohol and benzodiazepines both act on GABA receptors, so together they can cause marked respiratory depression. Close airway monitoring is needed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7f90ed6f","lv":"flight","d":"ketamine","sys":"c:psych","q":"After IM ketamine for severe agitation, which complications should the crew be ready to manage?","a":"Laryngospasm and hypersalivation","w":["Bradycardia and hypotension","QT prolongation and torsades","Hypoglycemia and seizures"],"x":"Ketamine increases secretions and can rarely cause laryngospasm, so suction and airway equipment should be ready. It usually raises heart rate and blood pressure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpda8a7218","lv":"flight","d":"lorazepam","sys":"c:psych","q":"For an agitated patient with tremor and sweating from alcohol withdrawal, why is a benzodiazepine such as lorazepam preferred?","a":"It treats the withdrawal itself and lowers seizure risk","w":["It has no effect on breathing","It is an antipsychotic","It lowers the seizure threshold"],"x":"Benzodiazepines replace alcohol's GABA effect, treating withdrawal and preventing seizures. Antipsychotics can lower the seizure threshold.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd35929df","lv":"flight","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"c:psych","q":"Which medication treats an acute dystonic reaction to haloperidol?","a":"Diphenhydramine","w":["Flumazenil","Naloxone","Dantrolene"],"x":"Diphenhydramine's anticholinergic effect restores the dopamine-acetylcholine balance and relieves dystonia, often within minutes. Dantrolene is used for malignant hyperthermia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfe9c3990","lv":"flight","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"c:psych","q":"Why can diphenhydramine make agitation worse in an older adult?","a":"Its anticholinergic effects can cause delirium","w":["It is a stimulant","It causes hypoglycemia","It causes serotonin syndrome"],"x":"Older adults are sensitive to anticholinergic drugs, which can cause confusion and delirium. It is sedating, not stimulating, in most adults.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8661cf16","lv":"flight","d":"tranexamic-acid","sys":"c:trauma","q":"How does tranexamic acid reduce bleeding?","a":"Blocks plasminogen from breaking down fibrin clots","w":["Activates platelets at the injury site","Replaces depleted clotting factors","Directly inhibits thrombin"],"x":"Tranexamic acid is an antifibrinolytic that blocks lysine binding sites on plasminogen, so clots are not broken down. It does not replace clotting factors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc0615191","lv":"flight","d":"tranexamic-acid","sys":"c:trauma","q":"In trauma with significant hemorrhage, when is tranexamic acid beneficial?","a":"When given within 3 hours of injury","w":["Only when started more than 3 hours after injury","Only after surgical control of bleeding","Only on the day after injury"],"x":"Trial data show benefit when TXA is started within 3 hours of injury, with possible harm when started later. Earlier is better.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6e73cfea","lv":"flight","d":"calcium-chloride","sys":"c:trauma","q":"Why is calcium given during massive transfusion?","a":"Citrate in stored blood binds ionized calcium","w":["Blood products contain excess calcium","Calcium reverses tranexamic acid","Calcium prevents hemolytic transfusion reactions"],"x":"Citrate used to preserve blood binds calcium, and hypocalcemia worsens coagulopathy and cardiac function. Stored blood does not contain extra calcium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp14badb8b","lv":"flight","d":"calcium-chloride","sys":"c:trauma","q":"How does calcium chloride compare with calcium gluconate?","a":"It delivers more elemental calcium per volume and is more irritating to veins","w":["It delivers less calcium and is safer peripherally","It is identical in calcium content and irritation","It can only be given intramuscularly"],"x":"Calcium chloride contains about three times the elemental calcium of the same volume of gluconate but is more caustic. Gluconate is gentler on peripheral veins.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe4126596","lv":"flight","d":"calcium-gluconate","sys":"c:trauma","q":"Why should calcium gluconate not be infused through the same line as a blood product?","a":"Calcium can overcome the citrate anticoagulant and cause clotting in the line","w":["Calcium destroys red blood cells","Calcium inactivates tranexamic acid","Calcium causes potassium release from platelets"],"x":"Calcium reverses citrate's anticoagulant effect, so clots can form in the tubing. Only normal saline should be run with blood products.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8e63a4ba","lv":"flight","d":"fentanyl","sys":"c:trauma","q":"Which route of fentanyl can provide rapid analgesia when IV access is not yet established?","a":"Intranasal by atomizer","w":["Swallowed oral tablet","Rectal suppository","Transdermal patch"],"x":"Intranasal fentanyl is absorbed quickly across the nasal mucosa. A transdermal patch takes many hours to work and is not for acute pain.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp007b43e4","lv":"flight","d":"ketamine","sys":"c:trauma","q":"What is an advantage of ketamine for analgesia in a trauma patient at risk of hypotension?","a":"It tends to preserve blood pressure and respiratory drive","w":["It lowers heart rate","It causes marked vasodilation","It has an antiplatelet effect"],"x":"Ketamine's sympathomimetic effect helps maintain blood pressure, and breathing is usually preserved. It does not cause marked vasodilation like propofol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp74007da4","lv":"flight","d":"ketamine","sys":"c:trauma","q":"How does ketamine produce analgesia and dissociation?","a":"Blocks NMDA receptors","w":["Stimulates mu-opioid receptors","Enhances GABA-A receptor activity","Stimulates alpha-2 receptors"],"x":"Ketamine is an NMDA receptor antagonist. Opioids act on mu receptors and dexmedetomidine on alpha-2 receptors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb6d10f3f","lv":"flight","d":"cefazolin","sys":"c:trauma","q":"Why is cefazolin given early for an open fracture?","a":"To lower the risk of deep infection from skin organisms","w":["To cover anaerobic bowel bacteria","To treat tetanus","To cover MRSA"],"x":"Early cefazolin targets the staphylococci and streptococci that commonly infect open fractures. It does not cover MRSA or replace tetanus prophylaxis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp910f0987","lv":"flight","d":"cefazolin","sys":"c:trauma","q":"Cefazolin belongs to which antibiotic class?","a":"First-generation cephalosporin","w":["Third-generation cephalosporin","Carbapenem","Fluoroquinolone"],"x":"Cefazolin is a first-generation cephalosporin with strong gram-positive coverage. Ceftriaxone is a third-generation agent.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa95ee644","lv":"flight","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"Why should concentrated dextrose be given through a large, patent vein?","a":"It is hypertonic and can cause tissue necrosis if it extravasates","w":["It precipitates when mixed with saline","It causes immediate anaphylaxis in small veins","It is inactive unless given centrally"],"x":"Concentrated dextrose is very hypertonic and irritating, so extravasation can cause tissue injury. Confirm a patent line before and during administration.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp26153c18","lv":"flight","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"After dextrose for hypoglycemia from a sulfonylurea overdose, why are frequent glucose rechecks needed?","a":"Hypoglycemia often recurs as the long-acting drug persists","w":["Dextrose causes delayed hyperkalemia","Dextrose is metabolized into insulin","Sulfonylureas block dextrose absorption"],"x":"Sulfonylureas keep stimulating insulin release for many hours, so glucose may fall again after a bolus. Frequent checks catch recurrence early.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfae45eaa","lv":"flight","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"Why is D5W a poor choice for volume resuscitation in hemorrhagic shock?","a":"The glucose is metabolized, leaving free water that moves mostly into cells","w":["It is a colloid that stays in the bloodstream","It is hypertonic and pulls fluid from cells","It contains potassium that causes arrhythmias"],"x":"Once dextrose is metabolized, D5W acts like free water and distributes into cells, with little staying in the vessels. Blood products are the preferred replacement in hemorrhage.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7da09356","lv":"flight","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"Why is a dextrose-containing solution not run in the same line as packed red blood cells?","a":"It can cause red cell clumping and hemolysis","w":["It inactivates the citrate anticoagulant","It causes the blood to clot immediately","It raises potassium in the stored blood"],"x":"Dextrose solutions can cause red cells to clump and swell, leading to hemolysis. Normal saline is the only solution routinely run with blood.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1b4b8730","lv":"flight","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"Before giving dextrose to a patient with altered mental status, what should be confirmed?","a":"Low blood glucose on point-of-care testing","w":["Serum potassium level","Arterial blood gas results","Serum ketone level"],"x":"A quick glucose check confirms hypoglycemia and gives a baseline to judge the response. Waiting for lab tests would delay treatment.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa3f63780","lv":"flight","d":"calcium-chloride","sys":"c:lytes","q":"In hyperkalemia with ECG changes, how does calcium help?","a":"It stabilizes the cardiac membrane without lowering potassium","w":["It shifts potassium into cells","It increases kidney excretion of potassium","It binds potassium in the gut"],"x":"Calcium protects the heart against potassium's effects but does not change the potassium level. Other therapies such as insulin and albuterol shift potassium into cells.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe94fdd69","lv":"flight","d":"calcium-gluconate","sys":"c:lytes","q":"Which ECG finding suggests hypocalcemia that calcium gluconate would treat?","a":"Prolonged QT interval","w":["Peaked T waves","Prominent U waves","Shortened QT interval"],"x":"Low calcium lengthens the ST segment and QT interval. Peaked T waves suggest hyperkalemia and U waves suggest hypokalemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcd0f6f78","lv":"flight","d":"calcium-gluconate","sys":"c:lytes","q":"In hyperkalemia with a widening QRS, how long does calcium's protective effect last?","a":"It begins within minutes but is temporary","w":["It takes several hours to begin","It is permanent once given","It begins only after dialysis"],"x":"Calcium works within minutes but wears off, so it may need repeating while potassium is lowered by other means. It does not remove potassium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7d2ba59b","lv":"flight","d":"magnesium-sulfate","sys":"c:lytes","q":"A patient with a prolonged QT develops polymorphic VT with a twisting pattern. Which medication is first-line?","a":"Magnesium sulfate","w":["Calcium chloride","Sodium bicarbonate","Adenosine"],"x":"Magnesium is the treatment of choice for torsades de pointes, even with a normal magnesium level. Adenosine has no role in polymorphic VT.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8b6552bd","lv":"flight","d":"magnesium-sulfate","sys":"c:lytes","q":"During a magnesium infusion, the patient's patellar reflexes disappear. What does this indicate?","a":"Rising magnesium levels approaching toxicity","w":["The expected therapeutic effect","Improving hypocalcemia","Developing hypokalemia"],"x":"Loss of deep tendon reflexes is an early sign of magnesium toxicity and can be followed by respiratory depression. Report it to the provider promptly.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp18a94cd6","lv":"flight","d":"sodium-bicarbonate","sys":"c:lytes","q":"Why should calcium and sodium bicarbonate not be given together in the same IV line without flushing?","a":"They form a calcium carbonate precipitate","w":["They cancel each other's cardiac effects","They cause hemolysis in the line","They form a toxic gas"],"x":"Calcium and bicarbonate combine to form an insoluble precipitate that can block the line. Flush well between them.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf0cbc8dc","lv":"flight","d":"furosemide","sys":"c:lytes","q":"How does furosemide cause diuresis?","a":"Inhibits the sodium-potassium-chloride cotransporter in the loop of Henle","w":["Inhibits sodium reabsorption in the distal tubule","Blocks aldosterone in the collecting duct","Inhibits carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule"],"x":"Furosemide is a loop diuretic acting on the thick ascending limb. Distal tubule action describes thiazides.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdf7b4282","lv":"flight","d":"furosemide","sys":"c:lytes","q":"Which adverse effect is associated with rapid high-dose IV furosemide?","a":"Hearing loss or tinnitus","w":["Hyperkalemia","Hypoglycemia","Bradycardia"],"x":"Rapid IV injection of large furosemide doses can cause ototoxicity. Furosemide causes potassium loss, not hyperkalemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4f9c097a","lv":"flight","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:lytes","q":"How does nebulized albuterol lower serum potassium?","a":"Stimulates the sodium-potassium pump to shift potassium into cells","w":["Increases kidney excretion of potassium","Binds potassium in the gut","Stabilizes the cardiac membrane"],"x":"Beta-2 stimulation activates Na-K-ATPase, moving potassium into cells temporarily. Total body potassium is unchanged.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp71c405c7","lv":"flight","d":"regular-insulin","sys":"c:lytes","q":"After IV regular insulin with dextrose for hyperkalemia, what is most important to monitor?","a":"Blood glucose for delayed hypoglycemia","w":["Serum calcium","Serum magnesium","Urine output for diuresis"],"x":"Hypoglycemia is a common complication, sometimes hours after the insulin dose, especially with kidney disease. Glucose should be checked repeatedly.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe512f7dd","lv":"flight","d":"naloxone","sys":"c:tox","q":"Why is a patient observed after naloxone reverses an opioid overdose?","a":"Naloxone may wear off before the opioid, causing recurrent respiratory depression","w":["Naloxone causes a delayed allergic reaction","Naloxone causes delayed hypotension","Withdrawal always starts hours later"],"x":"Many opioids last longer than naloxone, so sedation and hypoventilation can return. Withdrawal, if it occurs, starts quickly rather than hours later.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcdb9e12c","lv":"flight","d":"sodium-bicarbonate","sys":"c:tox","q":"In tricyclic antidepressant overdose with a widening QRS and hypotension, how does sodium bicarbonate help?","a":"The sodium load and alkalinization overcome sodium-channel blockade","w":["It binds the drug in the gut","It speeds kidney elimination of the drug","It blocks muscarinic receptors"],"x":"Tricyclics block cardiac sodium channels, and bicarbonate counters this with extra sodium and a higher pH. It does not meaningfully increase elimination.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6c270bc1","lv":"flight","d":"activated-charcoal","sys":"c:tox","q":"Activated charcoal is ineffective for which ingestion?","a":"Lithium","w":["Aspirin","Acetaminophen","Carbamazepine"],"x":"Charcoal does not bind metals such as lithium and iron or alcohols. Aspirin, acetaminophen and carbamazepine are well adsorbed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp41701485","lv":"flight","d":"hydroxocobalamin","sys":"c:tox","q":"Which effect is expected after hydroxocobalamin for suspected cyanide poisoning?","a":"Red discoloration of skin and urine","w":["Blue discoloration of skin","Green discoloration of urine","Yellow discoloration of skin"],"x":"Hydroxocobalamin is deep red and colors skin and urine, and it can interfere with some lab tests. This is expected and not a sign of harm.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp338e7afa","lv":"flight","d":"atropine","sys":"c:tox","q":"What is the endpoint for atropine in organophosphate poisoning?","a":"Drying of bronchial secretions and improved oxygenation","w":["Full dilation of the pupils","Resolution of muscle fasciculations","Return of normal muscle strength"],"x":"Atropine is titrated to clear airway secretions and reverse bronchorrhea. It does not treat nicotinic effects such as fasciculations or weakness.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe8dd8e5a","lv":"flight","d":"pralidoxime","sys":"c:tox","q":"What is the role of pralidoxime in organophosphate poisoning?","a":"It reactivates acetylcholinesterase, improving muscle weakness","w":["It blocks muscarinic receptors to dry secretions","It binds the organophosphate in the gut","It stops seizures through GABA receptors"],"x":"Pralidoxime restores acetylcholinesterase before the bond ages, helping nicotinic effects such as weakness. Drying secretions is atropine's job.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6968ff13","lv":"flight","d":"fomepizole","sys":"c:tox","q":"How does fomepizole treat ethylene glycol or methanol poisoning?","a":"It inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase, preventing toxic metabolites","w":["It speeds kidney elimination of the toxic alcohol","It binds the toxic alcohol in the blood","It directly corrects metabolic acidosis"],"x":"Blocking alcohol dehydrogenase stops conversion of the parent alcohol into its toxic acids. Dialysis may still be needed to remove the alcohol itself.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp98fcc650","lv":"flight","d":"physostigmine","sys":"c:tox","q":"When should physostigmine be avoided in suspected anticholinergic toxicity?","a":"When the ECG shows QRS widening suggesting a tricyclic ingestion","w":["When the patient is agitated","When the skin is dry and flushed","When there is urinary retention"],"x":"Physostigmine can cause bradycardia, asystole and seizures in tricyclic overdose. Agitation and dry flushed skin are typical anticholinergic findings it may treat.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf57f669c","lv":"flight","d":"digoxin-immune-fab","sys":"c:tox","q":"Which finding in acute digoxin overdose supports giving digoxin immune fab?","a":"A life-threatening dysrhythmia or marked hyperkalemia","w":["Mild nausea only","Yellow-tinted vision alone","Low potassium with a normal rhythm"],"x":"Digoxin immune fab is indicated for dangerous dysrhythmias or significant hyperkalemia, which signals severe acute toxicity. Mild symptoms alone do not require it.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp05392b01","lv":"flight","d":"n-acetylcysteine","sys":"c:tox","q":"How does N-acetylcysteine protect the liver in acetaminophen overdose?","a":"It replenishes glutathione to detoxify the toxic metabolite NAPQI","w":["It binds acetaminophen in the blood","It blocks acetaminophen absorption","It speeds kidney excretion of acetaminophen"],"x":"NAC restores glutathione, which neutralizes the toxic metabolite NAPQI. It works best when started early after ingestion.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp835eccaa","lv":"flight","d":"cefazolin","sys":"c:sepsis","q":"How does cefazolin kill bacteria?","a":"It inhibits cell wall synthesis by binding penicillin-binding proteins","w":["It inhibits protein synthesis at the 30S ribosome","It inhibits bacterial DNA gyrase","It blocks bacterial folate synthesis"],"x":"Cefazolin is a beta-lactam that disrupts cell wall formation. Ribosome inhibition describes drugs such as aminoglycosides.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1460d06c","lv":"flight","d":"cefazolin","sys":"c:sepsis","q":"Which organism is not reliably covered by cefazolin?","a":"Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus","w":["Methicillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus pyogenes","Streptococcus agalactiae"],"x":"Cefazolin covers MSSA and streptococci well but not MRSA. Suspected MRSA needs an agent such as vancomycin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfafe4656","lv":"flight","d":"cefazolin","sys":"c:sepsis","q":"Several days after a course of cefazolin, a patient has frequent watery diarrhea and abdominal cramps. What should be recognized?","a":"Possible Clostridioides difficile infection","w":["Expected mild stomach upset","A new food allergy","An unrelated thyroid problem"],"x":"Antibiotics disrupt normal gut flora and can allow C. difficile to overgrow. Frequent watery diarrhea after antibiotics should be reported.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp18dc4598","lv":"flight","d":"cefazolin","sys":"c:sepsis","q":"Cefazolin is mainly eliminated by which route, which matters in kidney disease?","a":"Excretion by the kidneys","w":["Metabolism by the liver","Excretion in bile","Exhalation by the lungs"],"x":"Cefazolin is cleared unchanged by the kidneys, so dosing is adjusted in kidney impairment. It undergoes little liver metabolism.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc899cf80","lv":"flight","d":"cefazolin","sys":"c:sepsis","q":"When cefazolin is ordered for suspected infection, what is best practice regarding blood cultures?","a":"Obtain cultures before the first dose if this does not delay it","w":["Obtain cultures only after the first day of therapy","Skip cultures because they do not change care","Wait for culture results before giving the antibiotic"],"x":"Cultures drawn before antibiotics are more likely to identify the organism, but antibiotics should not be meaningfully delayed. Waiting for results would delay treatment.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5eb7054b","lv":"flight","d":"regular-insulin","sys":"c:endo","q":"Before an insulin infusion is started for diabetic ketoacidosis, which lab must be confirmed?","a":"That serum potassium is not low","w":["That blood glucose has normalized","That ketones have cleared","That serum calcium is normal"],"x":"Insulin shifts potassium into cells, so starting it with low potassium can cause dangerous hypokalemia and arrhythmias. Potassium is replaced first if low.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc62efac0","lv":"flight","d":"regular-insulin","sys":"c:endo","q":"During DKA treatment, glucose falls toward the target range but the anion gap is still open. What is the usual approach?","a":"Add dextrose to IV fluids while the insulin infusion continues","w":["Switch to subcutaneous insulin right away","Give a bolus of sodium bicarbonate","Give a large bolus of IV insulin"],"x":"Insulin is needed to clear ketoacidosis, so dextrose is added to prevent hypoglycemia while insulin keeps closing the gap. Bicarbonate is not routine in DKA.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3d76c207","lv":"flight","d":"regular-insulin","sys":"c:endo","q":"During transport of a patient on an insulin infusion for DKA, how often is glucose usually checked?","a":"About every hour by point-of-care testing","w":["Only once on arrival","About every six hours","Only if the patient has symptoms"],"x":"Insulin infusions can lower glucose quickly, so hourly checks are standard. Waiting for symptoms would miss hypoglycemia in a sedated or altered patient.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4d5512da","lv":"flight","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:endo","q":"A hypoglycemic patient wakes up after IV dextrose. What is the next step?","a":"Recheck glucose and give a longer-acting carbohydrate when able to eat","w":["Assume the problem is resolved","Give insulin to prevent rebound","Give a second bolus without rechecking"],"x":"IV dextrose works quickly but briefly, so glucose is rechecked and complex carbohydrates are given when safe. Insulin would worsen hypoglycemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe7258a81","lv":"flight","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:endo","q":"Why is unnecessary hyperglycemia from dextrose-containing fluids avoided in acute stroke or brain injury?","a":"Hyperglycemia is linked to worse neurologic outcomes","w":["Hyperglycemia lowers intracranial pressure","Hyperglycemia improves outcomes","Glucose level has no effect on the brain"],"x":"High glucose is associated with larger infarcts and worse outcomes after stroke and brain injury. Treat confirmed hypoglycemia but avoid unneeded dextrose.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbd137b0a","lv":"flight","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:endo","q":"A hypoglycemic patient is unconscious and has no IV access. Which routes can be used for glucagon?","a":"Intramuscular, subcutaneous or intranasal","w":["Oral, sublingual or buccal","Rectal or transdermal only","Inhaled by nebulizer only"],"x":"Glucagon is approved for IM, SC and, as a nasal powder, intranasal use. An unconscious patient should never receive anything by mouth.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6bbf0c1b","lv":"flight","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:endo","q":"In which patient is glucagon less likely to raise blood glucose?","a":"A patient with chronic alcohol use and poor nutrition","w":["A patient with type 1 diabetes on insulin","A patient who ate a meal recently","A school-age child"],"x":"Glucagon works by releasing stored liver glycogen, which is depleted in starvation and chronic alcohol use. IV dextrose is preferred in these patients.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcbe2ba3a","lv":"flight","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:endo","q":"Which adverse effect is common after glucagon, and how should the crew prepare?","a":"Nausea and vomiting, so protect the airway","w":["Bradycardia, so prepare atropine","Hypokalemia, so prepare potassium","Bronchospasm, so prepare albuterol"],"x":"Vomiting is common after glucagon, especially as the patient wakes up. Positioning and suction reduce aspiration risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9ad2a29f","lv":"flight","d":"thiamine","sys":"c:endo","q":"A malnourished patient with alcohol use disorder has confusion, unsteady gait and abnormal eye movements. Which deficiency should be suspected?","a":"Thiamine deficiency causing Wernicke encephalopathy","w":["Vitamin B12 deficiency causing anemia","Folate deficiency causing neural tube defects","Vitamin K deficiency causing bleeding"],"x":"Confusion, ataxia and eye movement abnormalities are the classic triad of Wernicke encephalopathy from thiamine deficiency. It is treated with IV thiamine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdf70baa5","lv":"flight","d":"thiamine","sys":"c:endo","q":"A hypoglycemic patient with chronic alcohol use needs dextrose. How should thiamine fit in?","a":"Give thiamine as well, without delaying dextrose","w":["Delay dextrose until thiamine levels return","Give dextrose only, since thiamine has no role","Give thiamine only, since dextrose is harmful"],"x":"Glucose metabolism uses thiamine, so at-risk patients should receive thiamine too, but hypoglycemia is treated right away. Delaying dextrose is more dangerous.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp03163893","lv":"flight","d":"ondansetron","sys":"c:gi","q":"How does ondansetron prevent nausea and vomiting?","a":"Blocks serotonin 5-HT3 receptors","w":["Blocks dopamine D2 receptors","Blocks histamine H1 receptors","Blocks neurokinin-1 receptors"],"x":"Ondansetron blocks 5-HT3 receptors in the gut and brainstem. Dopamine blockade describes metoclopramide and droperidol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9f16b5c9","lv":"flight","d":"ondansetron","sys":"c:gi","q":"Which adverse effect warrants caution with ondansetron in a patient with long QT syndrome?","a":"QT prolongation","w":["Extrapyramidal symptoms","Tissue necrosis at the IV site","Hypoglycemia"],"x":"Ondansetron can prolong the QT interval, and its labeling advises avoiding it in congenital long QT syndrome. Extrapyramidal effects are more typical of dopamine blockers.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb52a554d","lv":"flight","d":"ondansetron","sys":"c:gi","q":"Which adverse effects of ondansetron are most common?","a":"Headache and constipation","w":["Diarrhea and excess salivation","Drowsiness and dry mouth","Restlessness and muscle spasms"],"x":"Headache and constipation are the most frequent side effects. Restlessness and spasms are typical of metoclopramide.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa53aab4b","lv":"flight","d":"metoclopramide","sys":"c:gi","q":"How does metoclopramide relieve nausea?","a":"Blocks dopamine D2 receptors and increases gut motility","w":["Blocks serotonin 5-HT3 receptors only","Blocks histamine H1 receptors","Blocks muscarinic receptors"],"x":"Metoclopramide blocks dopamine in the chemoreceptor trigger zone and speeds gastric emptying. 5-HT3 blockade describes ondansetron.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpef8725b8","lv":"flight","d":"metoclopramide","sys":"c:gi","q":"After rapid IV metoclopramide, a patient feels intense restlessness and cannot sit still. What should be recognized?","a":"Akathisia","w":["Panic attack","Serotonin syndrome","Hypoglycemia"],"x":"Akathisia is an extrapyramidal effect of dopamine blockade and is more common with rapid IV push. Report it so it can be treated.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6af14d12","lv":"flight","d":"metoclopramide","sys":"c:gi","q":"In which patient is metoclopramide contraindicated?","a":"A patient with a suspected mechanical bowel obstruction","w":["A patient with diabetic gastroparesis","A patient with a migraine headache","A patient with nausea after surgery"],"x":"Metoclopramide increases gut motility, which can be harmful with obstruction, perforation or GI bleeding. Gastroparesis is an approved indication.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5894a3b2","lv":"flight","d":"promethazine","sys":"c:gi","q":"What does the boxed warning for injectable promethazine describe?","a":"Severe tissue injury, including gangrene, from extravasation or arterial injection","w":["Severe liver failure","Torsades de pointes","Serotonin syndrome"],"x":"Injectable promethazine is very irritating and can cause severe tissue injury and gangrene. Deep IM injection is the preferred route.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3cc16a60","lv":"flight","d":"promethazine","sys":"c:gi","q":"Promethazine is contraindicated in which population?","a":"Children younger than 2 years","w":["Adults older than 65 years","Pregnant patients","Patients with motion sickness"],"x":"Promethazine has a boxed warning for fatal respiratory depression in children under 2. Motion sickness is one of its indications.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4fc0d09b","lv":"flight","d":"droperidol","sys":"c:gi","q":"Which adverse effects can droperidol share with metoclopramide?","a":"Akathisia and other extrapyramidal symptoms","w":["Constipation and headache","Red skin and urine","Bradycardia and bronchospasm"],"x":"Both drugs block dopamine receptors and can cause akathisia and dystonia. Constipation and headache are typical of ondansetron.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp32f391a5","lv":"flight","d":"droperidol","sys":"c:gi","q":"Where does droperidol act to relieve nausea?","a":"Dopamine receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone","w":["Serotonin receptors in the gut wall","Histamine receptors in the inner ear","Neurokinin receptors in the vomiting center"],"x":"Droperidol is a butyrophenone that blocks dopamine in the chemoreceptor trigger zone. Serotonin blockade in the gut describes ondansetron.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0cd9e0b3","lv":"flight","d":"oxytocin","sys":"c:ob","q":"How does oxytocin control postpartum hemorrhage?","a":"It stimulates uterine contraction, compressing bleeding vessels","w":["It promotes clotting factor production","It relaxes the uterus to reduce bleeding","It causes widespread systemic vasoconstriction"],"x":"Most postpartum hemorrhage is from uterine atony, and oxytocin makes the uterus contract and clamp down on vessels. It does not affect clotting factors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2bc97338","lv":"flight","d":"oxytocin","sys":"c:ob","q":"After a prolonged oxytocin infusion, a patient develops headache, confusion and low sodium. What should be suspected?","a":"Water intoxication from oxytocin's antidiuretic effect","w":["Preeclampsia with severe features","Amniotic fluid embolism","Magnesium toxicity"],"x":"Oxytocin resembles vasopressin and in prolonged infusions can cause water retention and hyponatremia. Report these findings promptly.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3300ba2e","lv":"flight","d":"magnesium-sulfate","sys":"c:ob","q":"What is the main purpose of magnesium sulfate in severe preeclampsia?","a":"To prevent eclamptic seizures","w":["To lower blood pressure as the main agent","To strengthen uterine contractions","To prevent postpartum hemorrhage"],"x":"Magnesium is the drug of choice for preventing and treating eclamptic seizures. Separate antihypertensives are used for blood pressure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpaadc2cb4","lv":"flight","d":"calcium-gluconate","sys":"c:ob","q":"A patient on a magnesium infusion has a respiratory rate of 8 and absent reflexes. Which medication reverses magnesium toxicity?","a":"Calcium gluconate","w":["Naloxone","Flumazenil","Sodium bicarbonate"],"x":"Calcium antagonizes magnesium at the neuromuscular junction and heart. Naloxone reverses opioids, not magnesium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe5035535","lv":"flight","d":"terbutaline","sys":"c:ob","q":"Which maternal adverse effects are most important to monitor after terbutaline?","a":"Tachycardia, chest pain and arrhythmias","w":["Bradycardia and hypotension","Hypoglycemia and sweating","Respiratory depression"],"x":"Terbutaline is a beta agonist, and its boxed warning cites serious maternal heart problems with prolonged tocolysis. It raises, not lowers, glucose.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbd9c9c7d","lv":"flight","d":"tranexamic-acid","sys":"c:ob","q":"What is the role of tranexamic acid in postpartum hemorrhage?","a":"An adjunct to uterotonics that slows clot breakdown","w":["A replacement for oxytocin","A drug that contracts the uterus","A reversal agent for anticoagulants"],"x":"TXA reduces fibrinolysis and is given alongside uterotonics, not instead of them. It has no effect on uterine tone.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb2aaa8be","lv":"flight","d":"methylergonovine","sys":"c:ob","q":"Which condition is a contraindication to methylergonovine?","a":"Preeclampsia or chronic hypertension","w":["Asthma","Type 2 diabetes","Prior cesarean delivery"],"x":"Methylergonovine causes vasoconstriction and can raise blood pressure sharply. Asthma is the main contraindication for carboprost.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp41a53bb9","lv":"flight","d":"carboprost","sys":"c:ob","q":"Which condition is a contraindication to carboprost?","a":"Asthma","w":["Chronic hypertension","Type 2 diabetes","Prior cesarean delivery"],"x":"Carboprost is a prostaglandin F2-alpha analog that can cause bronchospasm. Hypertension is the main concern with methylergonovine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp360fbd7a","lv":"flight","d":"misoprostol","sys":"c:ob","q":"Misoprostol belongs to which drug class?","a":"Prostaglandin E1 analog","w":["Prostaglandin F2-alpha analog","Ergot alkaloid","Synthetic oxytocin"],"x":"Misoprostol is a prostaglandin E1 analog. Carboprost is the F2-alpha analog and methylergonovine is the ergot alkaloid.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp32185f42","lv":"flight","d":"labetalol","sys":"c:ob","q":"A patient at 34 weeks gestation has severe-range blood pressure. Which IV agent is first-line?","a":"Labetalol","w":["Nitroprusside","Enalaprilat","Furosemide"],"x":"Labetalol, hydralazine and nifedipine are first-line for severe hypertension in pregnancy. ACE inhibitors such as enalaprilat are contraindicated because of fetal harm.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5aa8b932","lv":"flight","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:peds","q":"An infant has a heart rate below 60 with poor perfusion despite effective oxygenation and ventilation. Along with CPR, which medication is given first?","a":"Epinephrine","w":["Atropine","Adenosine","Amiodarone"],"x":"Pediatric bradycardia is usually caused by hypoxia, and epinephrine is the first drug when it persists despite good ventilation. Atropine is reserved for vagal causes or primary AV block.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp564408cb","lv":"flight","d":"atropine","sys":"c:peds","q":"In which child is atropine preferred over epinephrine for bradycardia?","a":"A child whose bradycardia is from increased vagal tone during intubation","w":["A child whose bradycardia is from hypoxia","A child in pulseless electrical activity","A child in ventricular fibrillation"],"x":"Atropine treats bradycardia from vagal stimulation or primary AV block. Hypoxic bradycardia is treated with ventilation and epinephrine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1146506b","lv":"flight","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:peds","q":"Why is glucose checked often in a sick neonate during transport?","a":"Neonates have small glycogen stores and become hypoglycemic quickly","w":["Neonates cannot metabolize glucose","Dextrose is contraindicated in neonates","Neonates are prone to severe hyperkalemia"],"x":"Newborns, especially when sick or stressed, use up limited glycogen stores fast. Frequent checks catch hypoglycemia before it causes harm.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7a481e40","lv":"flight","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:peds","q":"A child with asthma has less wheezing after albuterol but now has poor air movement and is tired. What does this suggest?","a":"Worsening obstruction with impending respiratory failure","w":["Improvement from the albuterol","Expected albuterol side effect","Anxiety now resolving"],"x":"A quiet chest with poor air movement and fatigue means too little air is moving to wheeze. This is an ominous sign that needs immediate reporting.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfed9ef4c","lv":"flight","d":"midazolam","sys":"c:peds","q":"A child becomes agitated and combative after receiving midazolam. What should be recognized?","a":"A paradoxical reaction to the benzodiazepine","w":["Expected effect of correct sedation","A sign of opioid withdrawal","An allergic reaction to midazolam"],"x":"Benzodiazepines can cause paradoxical agitation, especially in children. It is not an allergy and should be reported.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1f99ece1","lv":"flight","d":"naloxone","sys":"c:peds","q":"Why is naloxone avoided in a newborn whose mother uses opioids long term?","a":"It can precipitate acute withdrawal, including seizures","w":["It causes severe neonatal hypoglycemia","It is ineffective in newborns","It worsens respiratory depression"],"x":"Infants exposed to opioids in the womb can have abrupt, severe withdrawal and seizures after naloxone. Ventilatory support is the priority.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcdcf37cd","lv":"flight","d":"adenosine","sys":"c:peds","q":"An infant has a regular, narrow-complex heart rate too fast to count with no visible P waves. Why is adenosine given?","a":"To briefly block the AV node and end the SVT","w":["To slow sinus tachycardia from fever","To convert ventricular fibrillation","To increase cardiac contractility"],"x":"These findings suggest SVT, and adenosine briefly blocks AV conduction to break the reentry circuit. Sinus tachycardia is treated by addressing the cause.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8a784261","lv":"flight","d":"ondansetron","sys":"c:peds","q":"What is the benefit of ondansetron for a child with vomiting from gastroenteritis?","a":"It reduces vomiting so oral rehydration is more likely to succeed","w":["It cures the viral infection","It replaces lost electrolytes","It stops diarrhea completely"],"x":"Ondansetron decreases vomiting and the need for IV fluids or admission. It does not treat the infection or diarrhea.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa311bca1","lv":"flight","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"c:peds","q":"Which antipyretic is appropriate for an infant younger than 6 months?","a":"Acetaminophen","w":["Ibuprofen","Aspirin","Naproxen"],"x":"Acetaminophen can be used in young infants, while ibuprofen is generally not used under 6 months. Aspirin is avoided in children because of Reye syndrome.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc3ecaa11","lv":"flight","d":"ibuprofen","sys":"c:peds","q":"In which child should ibuprofen be used with caution?","a":"A child who is significantly dehydrated with poor urine output","w":["A child with a mild ear infection","A child with a sprained ankle","A child with a sore throat"],"x":"NSAIDs reduce kidney blood flow and can cause kidney injury in dehydrated children. The other children are typical candidates.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp413843e9","lv":"flight","d":"propofol","sys":"c:icu","q":"A patient on a prolonged high-rate propofol infusion develops metabolic acidosis, rising CK, hyperkalemia and new arrhythmias. What should be suspected?","a":"Propofol infusion syndrome","w":["Malignant hyperthermia","Serotonin syndrome","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome"],"x":"Propofol infusion syndrome causes acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalemia and cardiac failure and can be fatal. Report these findings immediately.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpac196535","lv":"flight","d":"propofol","sys":"c:icu","q":"Because propofol comes in a lipid emulsion, which lab is monitored during prolonged infusion?","a":"Triglyceride level","w":["Serum ammonia","Thyroid-stimulating hormone","Serum lithium"],"x":"Propofol's lipid vehicle can raise triglycerides and lead to pancreatitis. The emulsion also supports bacterial growth, so strict aseptic handling is required.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8c2ba49c","lv":"flight","d":"dexmedetomidine","sys":"c:icu","q":"Which adverse effects are most common with a dexmedetomidine infusion?","a":"Bradycardia and hypotension","w":["Apnea and respiratory arrest","Hyperkalemia and peaked T waves","Seizures and myoclonus"],"x":"Alpha-2 stimulation lowers sympathetic tone, causing bradycardia and hypotension. Respiratory drive is relatively preserved.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp17ec1d84","lv":"flight","d":"esmolol","sys":"c:icu","q":"Why is esmolol useful for rate control in an unstable transport patient?","a":"Its very short duration allows rapid offset if hypotension develops","w":["Its long duration means fewer adjustments","It increases contractility while slowing rate","It has no effect on blood pressure"],"x":"Esmolol is broken down quickly by red cell esterases, so its effects fade within minutes when the rate is changed. It does reduce contractility and can lower blood pressure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5ef9b2d7","lv":"flight","d":"milrinone","sys":"c:icu","q":"How does milrinone increase contractility and cause vasodilation?","a":"Inhibits phosphodiesterase-3, raising cAMP","w":["Stimulates beta-1 receptors","Stimulates alpha-1 receptors","Inhibits the sodium-potassium pump"],"x":"Milrinone blocks the breakdown of cAMP in heart and vascular muscle, making it an inodilator. Sodium-potassium pump inhibition describes digoxin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qped1b44ab","lv":"flight","d":"nitroprusside","sys":"c:icu","q":"Why is a nitroprusside infusion protected from light?","a":"It breaks down when exposed to light","w":["Light makes it more potent","Light turns it into an opioid","Light causes it to crystallize"],"x":"Nitroprusside is light sensitive, so the bag is wrapped in an opaque cover. Degraded solution may be discolored.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc2744ad7","lv":"flight","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"c:icu","q":"During interfacility transport on norepinephrine, what is the best way to monitor its effect?","a":"Continuous arterial line MAP with perfusion assessment","w":["Urine output alone","Pulse oximetry alone","Hourly cuff blood pressure"],"x":"An arterial line gives beat-to-beat pressure for titration, and perfusion signs show whether organs are benefiting. Cuff readings every hour are too infrequent for a titrated vasopressor.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpda1928c6","lv":"flight","d":"heparin","sys":"c:icu","q":"Which agent reverses unfractionated heparin in major bleeding?","a":"Protamine sulfate","w":["Vitamin K","Idarucizumab","Andexanet alfa"],"x":"Protamine binds and neutralizes heparin. Vitamin K reverses warfarin and idarucizumab reverses dabigatran.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9c23ed27","lv":"flight","d":"nicardipine","sys":"c:icu","q":"When nicardipine is given through a peripheral IV, which complication should be monitored at the site?","a":"Phlebitis at the infusion site","w":["Hyperkalemia","Bronchospasm","Bleeding at the site"],"x":"Peripheral nicardipine infusions often irritate veins, and the labeling advises rotating peripheral sites regularly. It does not affect potassium or clotting.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf427d2a2","lv":"flight","d":"vasopressin","sys":"c:icu","q":"Which adverse effect should be monitored during a vasopressin infusion?","a":"Ischemia of the fingers, gut or heart","w":["Hypokalemia","Bronchospasm","Severe hyperglycemia"],"x":"Vasopressin's strong vasoconstriction can reduce blood flow to the digits, bowel and heart. Check distal perfusion and report changes.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp75bf7733","lv":"flightrn","d":"adenosine","sys":"cardio","q":"A stable patient in narrow-complex SVT is to receive IV adenosine during transport. How should it be given?","a":"Rapid IV push through a proximal site, followed immediately by a saline flush","w":["Slow IV push over several minutes to limit flushing and chest discomfort","Intramuscular injection into the deltoid, repeated if the rhythm persists","Diluted continuous infusion that is titrated to the patient's heart rate"],"x":"Adenosine has a half-life of only seconds, so it must reach the heart as a rapid bolus chased by a flush. A slow push lets it be metabolized before it can act on the AV node.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp42838c8d","lv":"flightrn","d":"adenosine","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient with SVT takes theophylline daily. What effect should the flight nurse anticipate when adenosine is given?","a":"A reduced response, because methylxanthines block adenosine receptors","w":["An exaggerated response with prolonged asystole after the first push","Severe hyperkalemia, because both drugs shift potassium out of cells","No change in response, because the two drugs share no common pathway"],"x":"Theophylline and caffeine are adenosine receptor antagonists, so adenosine may be less effective. Prolonged asystole is a concern with dipyridamole or carbamazepine and in heart transplant patients, not with methylxanthines.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp46029b94","lv":"flightrn","d":"amiodarone","sys":"cardio","q":"During an IV amiodarone infusion after conversion of ventricular tachycardia, which finding most needs prompt reporting?","a":"Hypotension and new bradycardia","w":["Mild headache and fatigue","Transient metallic taste","Increased urine output"],"x":"IV amiodarone commonly causes hypotension and can cause bradycardia and AV block, which can compromise perfusion. The other findings are minor or unrelated and do not threaten hemodynamic stability.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp20c8fe1f","lv":"flightrn","d":"esmolol","sys":"cardio","q":"In suspected acute aortic dissection, why is esmolol commonly started before a vasodilator such as nitroprusside?","a":"It lowers heart rate and shear force, preventing reflex tachycardia from vasodilation","w":["It directly dilates the aorta to reduce wall tension at the site of the tear","It increases myocardial contractility to keep distal organs well perfused","It reverses any anticoagulant effect that could worsen bleeding into the wall"],"x":"Beta blockade first reduces heart rate and the force of ejection (dP/dt), which limits propagation of the tear; a vasodilator alone causes reflex tachycardia. Esmolol is titratable and short acting, but it does not dilate the aorta itself.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd7d78f4b","lv":"flightrn","d":"diltiazem","sys":"cardio","q":"Which patient with a rapid irregular tachycardia should NOT receive IV diltiazem?","a":"A patient with a wide, irregular rhythm and known Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome","w":["A patient with a long history of well-controlled essential hypertension","A patient with chronic stable COPD who uses an albuterol inhaler","A patient who takes a daily statin for high cholesterol levels"],"x":"AV nodal blockers such as diltiazem can speed conduction down an accessory pathway in pre-excited atrial fibrillation and trigger ventricular fibrillation. Hypertension, COPD and statin use are not contraindications to diltiazem.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa01b7e35","lv":"flightrn","d":"clevidipine","sys":"cardio","q":"Before starting a clevidipine infusion for severe hypertension, which allergy history is most important to ask about?","a":"Allergy to soybeans or egg products","w":["Allergy to shellfish or iodine contrast","Allergy to sulfonamide antibiotics","Allergy to latex gloves or tubing"],"x":"Clevidipine is formulated in a lipid emulsion containing soybean oil and egg yolk phospholipids, so these allergies are contraindications. Shellfish and iodine allergies are a common but unrelated distractor.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa194457a","lv":"flightrn","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient with inferior STEMI has ST elevation in V4R and borderline blood pressure. Why is nitroglycerin a particular concern?","a":"Right ventricular infarction is preload dependent, so venodilation can cause profound hypotension","w":["Right ventricular infarction causes severe bronchospasm when nitrates are given","Nitroglycerin is ineffective in inferior infarction because the vessel is too small","Nitroglycerin raises heart rate so much that it extends the right-sided infarct"],"x":"The infarcted right ventricle depends on venous return to fill, and nitrates reduce preload, which can drop blood pressure sharply. Nitrates do not cause bronchospasm, and the main issue is preload, not reflex tachycardia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp884536fd","lv":"flightrn","d":"procainamide","sys":"cardio","q":"During a procainamide infusion for stable monomorphic VT, which finding should be reported to the provider right away?","a":"QRS widening by half or more from baseline","w":["Narrowing of the QRS toward a normal width","A shorter PR interval than on arrival","Return of upright P waves before each QRS"],"x":"Procainamide slows conduction, and marked QRS widening, hypotension or QT prolongation signal toxicity and are standard endpoints to report. A narrowing QRS or visible P waves suggest the rhythm is converting, not that the drug is toxic.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp551edf98","lv":"flightrn","d":"digoxin-immune-fab","sys":"cardio","q":"After digoxin immune fab is given, the total serum digoxin level rises sharply. How should the nurse interpret this?","a":"It is expected, because most measured digoxin is now bound to the fab and inactive","w":["It shows worsening toxicity, so a repeat dose of the antidote is clearly required","It reflects a lab error from hemolysis, so the sample should simply be redrawn","It means the fab caused acute kidney injury that is blocking digoxin clearance"],"x":"Standard assays measure both free and fab-bound digoxin, so total levels rise after treatment and no longer reflect toxicity. Clinical response and potassium, not the total level, guide further care.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpeee0f081","lv":"flightrn","d":"nitroprusside","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient on a prolonged nitroprusside infusion develops rising lactate and worsening metabolic acidosis. Which problem should the nurse suspect?","a":"Cyanide toxicity","w":["Thiamine deficiency","Serotonin syndrome","Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia"],"x":"Nitroprusside releases cyanide as it is metabolized, and cyanide blocks cellular oxygen use, causing lactic acidosis. Thiocyanate toxicity, more common in renal failure, presents mainly with neurologic signs rather than acidosis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp480501dc","lv":"flightrn","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"After adequate fluid resuscitation, which vasopressor do Surviving Sepsis Campaign guidelines recommend first for septic shock?","a":"Norepinephrine","w":["Dopamine","Phenylephrine","Dobutamine"],"x":"Norepinephrine is recommended as the first-line vasopressor in septic shock. Dopamine causes more arrhythmias and is no longer preferred.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4942c50d","lv":"flightrn","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"A peripheral IV running norepinephrine has infiltrated, and the surrounding skin is pale and cool. Which agent is used to infiltrate the area?","a":"Phentolamine","w":["Hyaluronidase","Atropine","Naloxone"],"x":"Phentolamine is an alpha blocker that reverses the local vasoconstriction caused by norepinephrine extravasation. Hyaluronidase is used for some non-vasopressor extravasations but does not counter alpha-mediated ischemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp68465ac7","lv":"flightrn","d":"vasopressin","sys":"shock","q":"Vasopressin is added to norepinephrine for a patient in septic shock. How does vasopressin raise blood pressure?","a":"It stimulates V1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle","w":["It stimulates alpha-1 adrenergic receptors in vessels","It inhibits phosphodiesterase-3 in vascular tissue","It stimulates beta-1 receptors to raise cardiac output"],"x":"Vasopressin causes vasoconstriction through V1 receptors, independent of adrenergic receptors, which is why it helps when catecholamine response is blunted. Alpha-1 stimulation is how norepinephrine and phenylephrine work.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp87447b44","lv":"flightrn","d":"vasopressin","sys":"shock","q":"Which adverse effect should the transport nurse watch for in a patient receiving a vasopressin infusion for shock?","a":"Digital and mesenteric ischemia","w":["Bronchospasm and wheezing","Severe hyperglycemia","Serotonin syndrome"],"x":"Strong V1-mediated vasoconstriction can reduce blood flow to the fingers, skin and gut. Vasopressin does not cause bronchospasm or serotonin toxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8a88421d","lv":"flightrn","d":"epinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"A patient on an epinephrine infusion has rising lactate and glucose, yet skin perfusion and urine output are improving. What is the best interpretation?","a":"Beta-2 stimulation can raise lactate and glucose even without worsening tissue hypoperfusion","w":["The rising lactate proves shock is worsening and more fluid is the only correct response","The rising glucose indicates new-onset diabetic ketoacidosis triggered by the infusion","The lab values reflect contamination from drawing blood above the infusion site only"],"x":"Epinephrine increases aerobic glycolysis and glycogenolysis through beta-2 receptors, so lactate and glucose can rise even when perfusion is better. Lactate must be interpreted with other perfusion markers, not alone.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp13d38489","lv":"flightrn","d":"dobutamine","sys":"shock","q":"A patient in cardiogenic shock is started on dobutamine, and the blood pressure falls. What is the most likely reason?","a":"Beta-2 mediated vasodilation lowers systemic vascular resistance","w":["Alpha-1 stimulation causes intense constriction of coronary vessels","Dobutamine directly depresses contractility of the left ventricle","Dobutamine blocks vasopressin receptors and causes fluid loss"],"x":"Dobutamine is mainly a beta-1 inotrope but also has beta-2 vasodilating effects, which can lower blood pressure, so a vasopressor may be needed. It increases, not decreases, contractility.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp32135d18","lv":"flightrn","d":"milrinone","sys":"shock","q":"Why might milrinone be preferred over dobutamine for a low-output patient who takes a beta blocker long term?","a":"It increases contractility through PDE-3 inhibition, bypassing beta receptors","w":["It is a pure vasoconstrictor that does not depend on any receptor at all","It is a beta-1 agonist that competes better against the beta blocker","It slows heart rate further and improves filling time in the ventricle"],"x":"Milrinone raises intracellular cAMP by blocking its breakdown, so its effect does not depend on beta receptors that are occupied by a beta blocker. It is an inodilator, not a vasoconstrictor.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0510c1e6","lv":"flightrn","d":"milrinone","sys":"shock","q":"Which lab value is most important to review when a patient is receiving milrinone during a long transport?","a":"Renal function, because milrinone is cleared by the kidneys and can accumulate","w":["Liver enzymes, because milrinone is cleared almost entirely by the liver","Platelet count, because milrinone commonly causes immune thrombocytopenia","Serum amylase, because milrinone is a frequent cause of acute pancreatitis"],"x":"Milrinone is eliminated mainly by the kidneys, so renal impairment leads to accumulation, hypotension and arrhythmias. It is not primarily hepatically cleared.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5d94c04a","lv":"flightrn","d":"phenylephrine","sys":"shock","q":"When a phenylephrine infusion raises a patient's blood pressure, which heart rate change is expected?","a":"Reflex bradycardia","w":["Reflex tachycardia","Sudden heart block","No change in rate"],"x":"Phenylephrine is a pure alpha-1 agonist with no beta activity, so the rise in pressure triggers a baroreceptor reflex that slows the heart. Reflex tachycardia is typical of vasodilators, not vasoconstrictors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9bd1f6a1","lv":"flightrn","d":"dopamine","sys":"shock","q":"Compared with norepinephrine in patients with shock, dopamine is associated with which outcome?","a":"More tachyarrhythmias","w":["Fewer tachyarrhythmias","Better kidney protection","Lower blood glucose levels"],"x":"Trials comparing the two agents found more arrhythmias with dopamine. So-called renal-dose dopamine does not protect the kidneys, which is a common misconception.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6a6ccd55","lv":"flightrn","d":"heparin","sys":"blood","q":"A patient on IV heparin for several days has a platelet count that has dropped by more than half and a new arm vein thrombosis. What should the nurse recognize?","a":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia with high risk of new clotting","w":["Expected dilutional thrombocytopenia from IV fluids","Heparin resistance caused by low antithrombin levels","Normal response showing heparin is working as intended"],"x":"HIT is an immune reaction that typically appears 5 to 10 days into therapy with a large platelet drop and paradoxical thrombosis. It requires notifying the provider for a non-heparin anticoagulant; it is not a sign of effective therapy.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp05b43c2e","lv":"flightrn","d":"protamine","sys":"blood","q":"Which patient history increases the risk of a serious reaction when protamine is given to reverse heparin?","a":"Previous use of NPH or other protamine-containing insulin","w":["Previous use of regular insulin by IV infusion","Previous allergic reaction to penicillin antibiotics","Previous hypersensitivity to iodine contrast media"],"x":"Prior exposure to protamine in NPH insulin, fish allergy, vasectomy and prior protamine use raise the risk of hypotension, anaphylaxis and pulmonary hypertension. Regular insulin contains no protamine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb8f286d8","lv":"flightrn","d":"prothrombin-complex-concentrate","sys":"blood","q":"When 4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate is given to reverse warfarin in an intracranial bleed, why is IV vitamin K also given?","a":"PCC's effect is temporary, while vitamin K restores the liver's own production of clotting factors","w":["Vitamin K is needed to activate the factors in PCC before they can begin to work","Vitamin K prevents the allergic reactions that commonly occur during PCC infusion","Vitamin K blocks the clotting risk of PCC so that thrombosis cannot occur at all"],"x":"Replaced factors are consumed and cleared over hours, but warfarin's effect persists, so vitamin K is needed for sustained reversal. PCC factors do not require vitamin K to function once infused.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp584f09cd","lv":"flightrn","d":"idarucizumab","sys":"blood","q":"How does idarucizumab reverse the anticoagulant effect of dabigatran?","a":"It is an antibody fragment that binds dabigatran and neutralizes it","w":["It replaces vitamin K-dependent clotting factors in the bloodstream","It binds heparin and low-molecular-weight heparin through charge","It blocks plasminogen activation to slow breakdown of formed clots"],"x":"Idarucizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody fragment that binds dabigatran with high affinity. It has no effect on factor Xa inhibitors or heparin, which protamine partly reverses.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpabf2d503","lv":"flightrn","d":"tranexamic-acid","sys":"blood","q":"Why is early timing emphasized when tranexamic acid is given to a bleeding trauma patient?","a":"Its benefit is greatest when given soon after injury, and late doses lose that benefit","w":["It must be given late so it does not interfere with early platelet plug formation","It only works after a full massive transfusion protocol has been completed","It has no timing effect, and any dose at any point gives the same benefit"],"x":"Trauma trials showed reduced bleeding death when tranexamic acid was started early, ideally within 3 hours of injury, with no benefit or possible harm afterward. It is an antifibrinolytic that protects clots already forming.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa4ecfcb1","lv":"flightrn","d":"tenecteplase","sys":"blood","q":"Why is tenecteplase practical for fibrinolysis in a STEMI patient when transport time to a PCI center is long?","a":"It is given as a single weight-based IV bolus","w":["It is given orally and absorbed within minutes","It is given as a long infusion over a full day","It is given subcutaneously in several doses"],"x":"Tenecteplase is a fibrin-specific lytic administered as one IV bolus, which simplifies use in the field. Alteplase, by contrast, requires a bolus plus infusion.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4d50158f","lv":"flightrn","d":"warfarin","sys":"blood","q":"Which clotting factors are reduced by warfarin?","a":"Factors II, VII, IX and X","w":["Factors V, VIII, XI and XII","Factors I, V, VIII and XIII","Factors III, IV, X and XII"],"x":"Warfarin inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase, lowering production of the vitamin K-dependent factors II, VII, IX and X (and proteins C and S). Factors V and VIII are not vitamin K dependent.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9d09ccd7","lv":"flightrn","d":"enoxaparin","sys":"blood","q":"Which statement about reversing enoxaparin in a bleeding patient is accurate?","a":"Protamine only partially neutralizes its anti-Xa activity","w":["Protamine completely reverses all of its anticoagulant effect","Vitamin K is the preferred antidote for this anticoagulant","Idarucizumab is the specific reversal agent for this drug"],"x":"Protamine fully reverses unfractionated heparin's anti-IIa effect but neutralizes only part of enoxaparin's anti-Xa activity. Vitamin K reverses warfarin, and idarucizumab reverses dabigatran.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp52af80f4","lv":"flightrn","d":"desmopressin","sys":"blood","q":"Desmopressin is given to a uremic patient with bleeding. Which effect explains its benefit, and which electrolyte should be monitored?","a":"It releases von Willebrand factor and factor VIII; monitor sodium for hyponatremia","w":["It activates platelets directly in bone marrow; monitor potassium for hyperkalemia","It replaces fibrinogen in the plasma; monitor calcium for transfusion hypocalcemia","It inhibits fibrinolysis in the clot; monitor magnesium for hypermagnesemia"],"x":"Desmopressin releases von Willebrand factor and factor VIII from endothelial stores, improving platelet adhesion. Its antidiuretic effect causes water retention and hyponatremia, so sodium must be watched.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp50c92ff9","lv":"flightrn","d":"tirofiban","sys":"blood","q":"During a tirofiban infusion for acute coronary syndrome, which lab change should be reported promptly?","a":"A sudden drop in platelet count","w":["A small rise in hemoglobin level","A mild fall in serum glucose","A slight increase in sodium level"],"x":"Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors can cause acute, profound thrombocytopenia, which sharply raises bleeding risk. The other changes are not typical effects of tirofiban.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfb4f0d7c","lv":"flightrn","d":"succinylcholine","sys":"airway","q":"For which patient is succinylcholine contraindicated during rapid sequence intubation?","a":"A patient with a spinal cord injury and paralysis that occurred one week ago","w":["A patient with a large burn injury that occurred within the past hour","A patient with an isolated closed femur fracture from a fall today","A patient with a suspected opioid overdose and poor respiratory effort"],"x":"After denervation, major burns or crush injury, extra junctional receptors develop over days, and succinylcholine can cause life-threatening hyperkalemia. In the first hours after a burn, this receptor upregulation has not yet occurred.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd9590943","lv":"flightrn","d":"succinylcholine","sys":"airway","q":"Shortly after succinylcholine is given, which finding is an early sign of malignant hyperthermia?","a":"An unexplained rise in end-tidal CO2 with tachycardia","w":["A sudden fall in end-tidal CO2 with bradycardia","Brief fasciculations followed by flaccid paralysis","Mild hypotension that responds to a fluid bolus"],"x":"Rising end-tidal CO2, tachycardia and muscle rigidity (including masseter spasm) are early signs; high temperature often comes later. Fasciculations followed by paralysis are the normal expected effect of succinylcholine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpaea5307e","lv":"flightrn","d":"rocuronium","sys":"airway","q":"For an RSI patient with known hyperkalemia, what is the main advantage of rocuronium over succinylcholine?","a":"It does not raise serum potassium","w":["It has a much shorter duration","It provides deep sedation as well","It lowers intracranial pressure"],"x":"Rocuronium is a nondepolarizing agent and does not cause the potassium release seen with succinylcholine. It lasts longer than succinylcholine and has no sedative effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp751b59d2","lv":"flightrn","d":"sugammadex","sys":"airway","q":"A woman of childbearing age received sugammadex to reverse rocuronium. What teaching point is important?","a":"Use a backup nonhormonal birth control method for a period, as hormonal contraception may be less effective","w":["Expect permanent loss of effect from any hormonal contraceptive and plan surgical sterilization","Double the next birth control pill dose, since sugammadex speeds hormone metabolism in the liver","No contraceptive precautions are needed, because sugammadex binds only neuromuscular blockers"],"x":"Sugammadex can bind progestogen, reducing hormonal contraceptive effect, so labeling advises a backup method for 7 days. It does not permanently alter contraception and it does bind steroids besides rocuronium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3f26bfb3","lv":"flightrn","d":"etomidate","sys":"airway","q":"A septic patient receives etomidate for intubation. Which adverse effect is a known concern with this drug?","a":"Transient adrenal suppression from inhibition of cortisol synthesis","w":["Severe hypotension from strong histamine release after injection","Marked bronchospasm in patients with reactive airway disease","Prolonged paralysis in patients with pseudocholinesterase deficiency"],"x":"Etomidate inhibits 11-beta-hydroxylase and can briefly suppress cortisol production, which is debated in sepsis. It is valued for hemodynamic stability, not for causing hypotension.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp100ce473","lv":"flightrn","d":"ketamine","sys":"airway","q":"Why is ketamine often chosen as the induction agent for a patient in status asthmaticus?","a":"It has bronchodilating effects and tends to support blood pressure","w":["It is a strong anticholinergic that fully reverses bronchospasm","It causes no increase in airway secretions or salivation at all","It is a long-acting paralytic that improves chest wall compliance"],"x":"Ketamine releases catecholamines, which relax bronchial smooth muscle and help maintain blood pressure. It can increase secretions and is not a paralytic.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb7b29fa9","lv":"flightrn","d":"propofol","sys":"airway","q":"In which patient is propofol for RSI induction most likely to cause a dangerous complication?","a":"A hypotensive trauma patient with ongoing hemorrhage","w":["A normotensive patient with an isolated seizure","A hypertensive patient with an intracranial bleed","A stable patient with an uncomplicated overdose"],"x":"Propofol causes vasodilation and myocardial depression, which can lead to profound hypotension in hypovolemic patients. In hypertensive patients, a drop in blood pressure is less likely to cause harm.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp88d2b1d3","lv":"flightrn","d":"atropine","sys":"airway","q":"Why might atropine be given to an infant before laryngoscopy for intubation?","a":"To prevent bradycardia caused by vagal stimulation","w":["To provide sedation and amnesia for the procedure","To relax skeletal muscle and improve the view","To raise blood glucose before a period of apnea"],"x":"Infants are especially prone to vagally mediated bradycardia during laryngoscopy, and atropine blocks this muscarinic response. It is not a sedative or a muscle relaxant.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfc3c2365","lv":"flightrn","d":"cisatracurium","sys":"airway","q":"Why is cisatracurium often selected for ongoing paralysis in a patient with both kidney and liver failure?","a":"It is broken down by Hofmann elimination in plasma, independent of organ function","w":["It is filtered unchanged by the kidneys at a rate that does not change in failure","It is cleared by the liver through a pathway that is preserved in liver failure","It is reversed instantly by sugammadex if any accumulation begins to occur"],"x":"Cisatracurium degrades spontaneously at normal pH and temperature (Hofmann elimination), so it does not accumulate in organ failure. Sugammadex reverses aminosteroids such as rocuronium, not benzylisoquinolines like cisatracurium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7ebb4801","lv":"flightrn","d":"fentanyl","sys":"airway","q":"After a rapid IV push of fentanyl, a patient becomes very difficult to ventilate with a bag-valve mask, and the chest feels stiff. What should the nurse recognize?","a":"Opioid-induced chest wall rigidity","w":["Acute tension pneumothorax","Severe anaphylactic bronchospasm","Malignant hyperthermia onset"],"x":"Rapid, large doses of potent opioids such as fentanyl can cause skeletal muscle rigidity of the chest wall and glottis. It is treated with a neuromuscular blocker or naloxone, whereas malignant hyperthermia is triggered by succinylcholine and volatile agents.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5db1bcb9","lv":"flightrn","d":"albuterol","sys":"resp","q":"A patient with severe asthma is on continuous nebulized albuterol. Which lab value should be monitored for a drug-related change?","a":"Serum potassium, because albuterol shifts potassium into cells","w":["Serum sodium, because albuterol causes water retention","Serum calcium, because albuterol binds calcium in plasma","Platelet count, because albuterol suppresses bone marrow"],"x":"Beta-2 agonists drive potassium into cells and can cause hypokalemia, along with tachycardia and hyperglycemia. Albuterol does not meaningfully affect sodium, calcium or platelets.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp27a1fc7c","lv":"flightrn","d":"ipratropium","sys":"resp","q":"How does ipratropium produce bronchodilation when added to albuterol for an acute exacerbation?","a":"It blocks muscarinic receptors, reducing vagal bronchoconstriction","w":["It stimulates beta-2 receptors on bronchial smooth muscle cells","It inhibits leukotriene receptors that drive airway inflammation","It stabilizes mast cells so they cannot release their histamine"],"x":"Ipratropium is an inhaled anticholinergic that blocks acetylcholine-mediated bronchoconstriction, which complements the beta-2 agonist effect of albuterol. It is not a beta agonist or leukotriene blocker.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc7c1dad9","lv":"flightrn","d":"magnesium-sulfate","sys":"resp","q":"IV magnesium sulfate is given for a severe asthma exacerbation. Which findings should the nurse monitor for?","a":"Hypotension and diminished deep tendon reflexes","w":["Hypertension and brisk deep tendon reflexes","Hyperglycemia and a new tremor of the hands","Tachycardia and severe agitation or anxiety"],"x":"Magnesium relaxes smooth muscle and can cause flushing and hypotension; high levels depress reflexes and breathing. Brisk reflexes and tremor are not signs of magnesium excess.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp88cfb5e2","lv":"flightrn","d":"terbutaline","sys":"resp","q":"A patient with severe asthma is too dyspneic to use nebulized therapy effectively. By which route can terbutaline be given in this situation?","a":"Subcutaneous injection","w":["Intrathecal injection","Transdermal patch","Rectal suppository"],"x":"Terbutaline is a beta-2 agonist that can be given subcutaneously when inhaled delivery is inadequate. It is not given by the other routes listed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp530dd758","lv":"flightrn","d":"racemic-epinephrine","sys":"resp","q":"After nebulized racemic epinephrine improves stridor in a child with croup, why is a period of observation needed?","a":"Symptoms can return as the drug's effect wears off","w":["The drug often causes delayed severe hypoglycemia","It routinely triggers seizures within a few hours","It suppresses the child's ability to swallow fluids"],"x":"Racemic epinephrine reduces airway edema through vasoconstriction, but its effect lasts only a few hours, so stridor may recur. Corticosteroids are given to provide longer-lasting relief.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp34ac2506","lv":"flightrn","d":"dexamethasone","sys":"resp","q":"Why is dexamethasone given to a child with moderate croup who has also received nebulized epinephrine?","a":"It reduces airway inflammation and provides longer-lasting relief","w":["It works within seconds to open the airway before epinephrine","It is a bronchodilator that relaxes the smooth muscle in bronchi","It treats the viral cause and shortens the infection itself"],"x":"Dexamethasone decreases subglottic inflammation over hours and reduces return visits and need for further care. It does not act within seconds and has no antiviral effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp60fd07a7","lv":"flightrn","d":"nitric-oxide-inhaled","sys":"resp","q":"Why must a backup delivery system be ready when transporting a patient on inhaled nitric oxide?","a":"Sudden interruption can cause rebound pulmonary hypertension and hypoxemia","w":["Sudden interruption causes immediate severe systemic hypertension","Interruption causes nitric oxide to convert into carbon monoxide gas","Interruption triggers dangerous methemoglobin levels within seconds"],"x":"Abrupt loss of inhaled nitric oxide can lead to rebound pulmonary vasoconstriction, worsening oxygenation and hemodynamic collapse. Methemoglobinemia is a risk of ongoing exposure, not of interruption.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa17aba3f","lv":"flightrn","d":"surfactant-beractant","sys":"resp","q":"After a preterm infant receives intratracheal surfactant, what change should the transport team anticipate?","a":"Rapid improvement in lung compliance that may require ventilator adjustments","w":["Rapid worsening of lung compliance that requires higher ventilator pressures","Gradual improvement that takes several weeks before any change is visible","No change in compliance, since surfactant acts only on the pulmonary vessels"],"x":"Surfactant lowers alveolar surface tension and can improve compliance and oxygenation quickly, so pressures and oxygen may need to be reduced to avoid overdistension and pneumothorax. Its effect is on the alveoli, not the vessels.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5996be9c","lv":"flightrn","d":"epoprostenol","sys":"resp","q":"A patient with pulmonary arterial hypertension arrives on a home continuous IV epoprostenol infusion. Which line management principle is correct?","a":"Keep it on a dedicated line and never flush or bolus that line","w":["Flush the line with saline each hour to keep the catheter patent","Piggyback other IV medications into the line to save IV access","Draw routine blood samples from the line to avoid extra needle sticks"],"x":"Epoprostenol has a half-life of minutes, so interruption can cause rebound pulmonary hypertension, and flushing delivers an unintended bolus that can cause severe hypotension. A dedicated line protects delivery.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp989b38a9","lv":"flightrn","d":"methylprednisolone","sys":"resp","q":"IV methylprednisolone is given for a severe COPD exacerbation. Which adverse effect should the nurse monitor for during care?","a":"Hyperglycemia","w":["Hypoglycemia","Bradycardia","Hyperkalemia"],"x":"Systemic corticosteroids increase gluconeogenesis and insulin resistance, often raising blood glucose. They tend to lower, not raise, potassium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6657ea1b","lv":"flightrn","d":"lorazepam","sys":"neuro","q":"An adult with IV access has been seizing continuously for 5 minutes. Which medication is a first-line choice?","a":"IV lorazepam","w":["IV levetiracetam","IV phenobarbital","IV lacosamide"],"x":"Benzodiazepines are the first-line treatment for status epilepticus, and IV lorazepam is preferred when access is available. Levetiracetam and other agents are second-line after benzodiazepines.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3c10d181","lv":"flightrn","d":"midazolam","sys":"neuro","q":"A patient is actively seizing and IV access cannot be obtained. Which option is an effective first-line treatment?","a":"Intramuscular midazolam","w":["Oral levetiracetam","Intramuscular phenytoin","Oral valproic acid"],"x":"IM midazolam is rapidly absorbed and was at least as effective as IV lorazepam in prehospital status epilepticus. Oral agents are unsafe during a seizure, and phenytoin is not given IM.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf642cbe2","lv":"flightrn","d":"fosphenytoin","sys":"neuro","q":"During an IV fosphenytoin infusion for status epilepticus, which monitoring is required?","a":"Continuous ECG and blood pressure monitoring for hypotension and dysrhythmias","w":["Hourly serum glucose checks for severe hypoglycemia from the infusion","Peak flow measurements for bronchospasm caused by the drug vehicle","Serial urine output measurements for acute crystal nephropathy"],"x":"Rapid infusion of fosphenytoin can cause hypotension and serious arrhythmias, so labeling requires cardiac and blood pressure monitoring. Hypoglycemia and bronchospasm are not typical effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8e0db436","lv":"flightrn","d":"levetiracetam","sys":"neuro","q":"Why is levetiracetam often chosen as a second-line agent for status epilepticus in critically ill patients?","a":"It has few drug interactions and little effect on blood pressure or heart rhythm","w":["It is the only agent that can be given safely as an intramuscular injection","It strongly induces liver enzymes, which helps clear other sedating drugs","It is a benzodiazepine with a longer duration than lorazepam or midazolam"],"x":"Levetiracetam is not metabolized by liver enzymes and is generally hemodynamically well tolerated. It is not a benzodiazepine, and enzyme induction is a drawback of drugs like phenytoin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa756aa09","lv":"flightrn","d":"mannitol","sys":"neuro","q":"Before giving IV mannitol for raised intracranial pressure, the nurse inspects the solution and uses an in-line filter. Why?","a":"Mannitol can crystallize, and the filter keeps crystals from being infused","w":["Mannitol is light sensitive, and the filter blocks breakdown products","Mannitol foams when shaken, and the filter removes trapped air bubbles","Mannitol binds to IV tubing, and the filter improves drug delivery"],"x":"Concentrated mannitol can form crystals, especially at low temperatures, so solutions are inspected and filtered. Light sensitivity is a feature of drugs like nitroprusside, not mannitol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpedaca351","lv":"flightrn","d":"hypertonic-saline-3-percent","sys":"neuro","q":"A hypotensive patient with traumatic brain injury shows signs of herniation. Why might hypertonic saline be chosen over mannitol?","a":"It lowers intracranial pressure without causing an osmotic diuresis","w":["It lowers intracranial pressure by causing a strong osmotic diuresis","It lowers intracranial pressure by sedating the injured brain tissue","It lowers intracranial pressure by directly constricting cerebral veins"],"x":"Hypertonic saline draws water from brain tissue while expanding intravascular volume, which supports blood pressure. Mannitol's diuresis can worsen hypovolemia and hypotension, which worsens outcomes in brain injury.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb0bfe1b3","lv":"flightrn","d":"nimodipine","sys":"neuro","q":"A patient with aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage is ordered nimodipine. By which route must it be given?","a":"Orally or through a nasogastric tube","w":["Intravenous push through a central line","Intravenous infusion through a peripheral line","Intramuscular injection into a large muscle"],"x":"Nimodipine has a boxed warning against IV administration; capsule contents given IV have caused cardiac arrest and death. If the patient cannot swallow, the contents are given by enteral tube using an oral syringe.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpeb1dacc0","lv":"flightrn","d":"alteplase","sys":"neuro","q":"During or after alteplase for acute ischemic stroke, which finding most strongly suggests intracranial hemorrhage?","a":"Sudden severe headache, worsening neurologic exam and vomiting","w":["Mild oozing at the IV site that stops with gentle pressure","A small area of bruising on the arm from the BP cuff","Mild improvement in arm strength with residual facial droop"],"x":"New headache, nausea and vomiting, acute hypertension or neurologic decline after lysis must be treated as a possible hemorrhage and reported immediately. Minor IV site oozing is common and usually managed locally.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3b78326e","lv":"flightrn","d":"nicardipine","sys":"neuro","q":"A patient with intracerebral hemorrhage is started on a nicardipine infusion for blood pressure control. Which side effects are common?","a":"Reflex tachycardia and headache","w":["Marked bradycardia and heart block","Bronchospasm and severe wheezing","Hyperkalemia and peaked T waves"],"x":"Nicardipine is a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker that dilates arterioles, which commonly causes reflex tachycardia, flushing and headache. Bradycardia and AV block are more typical of nondihydropyridines such as diltiazem.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcef15bde","lv":"flightrn","d":"valproic-acid","sys":"neuro","q":"A patient on valproic acid becomes increasingly confused, though liver enzymes are normal. Which lab abnormality should the nurse suspect?","a":"Elevated serum ammonia","w":["Low serum ammonia","Elevated serum calcium","Low serum magnesium"],"x":"Valproate can cause hyperammonemic encephalopathy even when liver function tests are normal. Checking ammonia helps identify this cause of altered mental status.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8b35d14b","lv":"flightrn","d":"fentanyl","sys":"pain","q":"Why is fentanyl often preferred over morphine for analgesia in a hemodynamically unstable patient?","a":"It causes little histamine release and less hypotension","w":["It has a much longer duration and needs fewer doses","It has no risk of respiratory depression at any dose","It has strong anti-inflammatory effects on tissue"],"x":"Morphine can release histamine, causing vasodilation and hypotension, while fentanyl has minimal histamine effect. Fentanyl still causes respiratory depression and is shorter acting.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe04c2e1d","lv":"flightrn","d":"morphine","sys":"pain","q":"A patient with acute kidney injury becomes increasingly sedated after several doses of morphine. What is the most likely explanation?","a":"Active morphine metabolites accumulate when renal function is impaired","w":["Morphine is converted into naloxone, which builds up in the kidney","Kidney injury makes the brain resistant to all opioid effects","Morphine causes acute liver failure in patients with kidney injury"],"x":"Morphine's active metabolites, especially morphine-6-glucuronide, are renally cleared and accumulate in kidney failure, causing prolonged sedation and respiratory depression. This is why other opioids are often preferred in renal impairment.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5982f4c3","lv":"flightrn","d":"propofol","sys":"pain","q":"A ventilated patient on a prolonged high-rate propofol infusion develops metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalemia and new arrhythmias. What should the nurse suspect?","a":"Propofol infusion syndrome","w":["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Serotonin syndrome","Diabetic ketoacidosis"],"x":"Propofol infusion syndrome is a rare, often fatal complication of high-dose prolonged infusion marked by acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalemia, lipemia and cardiac failure. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome follows antipsychotic use and features rigidity and fever.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2f327b10","lv":"flightrn","d":"propofol","sys":"pain","q":"Why does propofol require strict aseptic handling and timely changes of the vial and tubing?","a":"Its lipid emulsion supports rapid growth of microorganisms","w":["It breaks down into cyanide when exposed to room air","It loses all sedative potency within minutes of opening","It reacts with plastic tubing to form toxic byproducts"],"x":"Propofol is formulated in a lipid emulsion that can support bacterial growth, so labeling requires aseptic technique and limits on hang time. Cyanide release is a feature of nitroprusside.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf21a803d","lv":"flightrn","d":"dexmedetomidine","sys":"pain","q":"Which property makes dexmedetomidine useful for sedating a patient who is not intubated?","a":"It provides sedation with minimal respiratory depression","w":["It provides deep general anesthesia and complete amnesia","It provides strong analgesia equal to high-dose opioids","It provides paralysis that prevents patient-ventilator dyssynchrony"],"x":"Dexmedetomidine, a central alpha-2 agonist, produces arousable sedation with little effect on respiratory drive. It does not reliably provide amnesia or deep anesthesia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4685f2e8","lv":"flightrn","d":"dexmedetomidine","sys":"pain","q":"Which adverse effects should be monitored most closely in a patient receiving a dexmedetomidine infusion?","a":"Bradycardia and hypotension","w":["Tachycardia and hypertension","Hyperthermia and rigidity","Bronchospasm and wheezing"],"x":"By reducing central sympathetic outflow, dexmedetomidine commonly causes bradycardia and hypotension. A transient rise in blood pressure can occur with a loading dose, but sustained hypertension is not typical.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfc368404","lv":"flightrn","d":"ketamine","sys":"pain","q":"How does ketamine produce analgesia and dissociative sedation?","a":"It blocks NMDA receptors in the central nervous system","w":["It stimulates mu opioid receptors in the spinal cord","It enhances GABA-A receptor activity in the brain","It stimulates alpha-2 receptors in the brainstem"],"x":"Ketamine is an NMDA receptor antagonist, which explains its analgesic and dissociative effects. Enhancing GABA-A is the mechanism of propofol and benzodiazepines.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp882b8dd8","lv":"flightrn","d":"naloxone","sys":"pain","q":"A patient wakes after IV naloxone for a suspected long-acting opioid overdose. Why is continued monitoring essential?","a":"Naloxone may wear off before the opioid, and respiratory depression can return","w":["Naloxone causes delayed severe hypoglycemia that appears hours after a dose","Naloxone frequently triggers a delayed allergic reaction after waking","Naloxone permanently blocks opioid receptors, so pain cannot be treated"],"x":"Naloxone's duration is often shorter than that of methadone, extended-release opioids or potent synthetic opioids, so sedation and apnea can recur. Repeat doses or an infusion may be needed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa45113a6","lv":"flightrn","d":"flumazenil","sys":"pain","q":"In which patient is flumazenil most likely to cause a dangerous complication?","a":"A long-term benzodiazepine user with a mixed overdose that includes a tricyclic antidepressant","w":["A healthy adult who is slow to awaken after procedural midazolam for a brief procedure","A child who accidentally swallowed one of a grandparent's benzodiazepine tablets","A patient who received lorazepam before a short procedure and has no other ingestions"],"x":"Flumazenil can precipitate seizures in benzodiazepine-dependent patients and in overdoses involving seizure-provoking drugs such as tricyclics. Its safest use is reversing procedural sedation in patients without dependence.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp64ae1914","lv":"flightrn","d":"ketorolac","sys":"pain","q":"Which patient should NOT receive ketorolac for pain control?","a":"A trauma patient with active bleeding and acute kidney injury","w":["An adult with renal colic and normal kidney function","An adult with a musculoskeletal back strain and no history","An adult with a migraine and no bleeding or kidney risks"],"x":"Ketorolac is an NSAID that impairs platelet function and reduces renal blood flow, so active bleeding and kidney injury are contraindications. Renal colic with normal kidney function is a common appropriate use.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd5011854","lv":"flightrn","d":"haloperidol","sys":"psych","q":"Shortly after IM haloperidol for agitation, a young patient develops sustained neck twisting and upward eye deviation. What should the nurse recognize?","a":"An acute dystonic reaction","w":["A generalized tonic-clonic seizure","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Tardive dyskinesia"],"x":"Acute dystonia is an early extrapyramidal effect of high-potency antipsychotics and responds to diphenhydramine or benztropine. Tardive dyskinesia develops after long-term use, not within hours.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf1d1e374","lv":"flightrn","d":"haloperidol","sys":"psych","q":"A patient on haloperidol develops high fever, lead-pipe rigidity, confusion and unstable blood pressure. Which condition should be suspected?","a":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","w":["Serotonin syndrome","Anticholinergic toxicity","Alcohol withdrawal"],"x":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome follows dopamine blockade and features hyperthermia, lead-pipe rigidity, altered mental status and autonomic instability, often with high CK. Serotonin syndrome develops faster and typically shows clonus and hyperreflexia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9c966a33","lv":"flightrn","d":"droperidol","sys":"psych","q":"Droperidol is ordered for severe agitation. Which assessment is especially important because of its boxed warning?","a":"Cardiac monitoring for QT prolongation and torsades de pointes","w":["Serial liver enzymes for drug-induced hepatitis","Blood glucose checks for severe hypoglycemia","Platelet counts for immune thrombocytopenia"],"x":"Droperidol carries a boxed warning for QT prolongation and torsades, so ECG monitoring and attention to other QT-prolonging drugs and electrolytes are needed. Liver injury and hypoglycemia are not its main concerns.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp61c8ffc4","lv":"flightrn","d":"olanzapine","sys":"psych","q":"Which combination raises particular concern for cardiorespiratory depression in an agitated patient?","a":"IM olanzapine given close in time to a parenteral benzodiazepine","w":["IM olanzapine given along with an oral acetaminophen dose","IM olanzapine given after a dose of ondansetron for nausea","IM olanzapine given with a normal saline fluid bolus"],"x":"Labeling warns against using IM olanzapine with parenteral benzodiazepines because of reports of hypotension, bradycardia and respiratory depression. Acetaminophen and saline do not share this risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1ab13f38","lv":"flightrn","d":"ketamine","sys":"psych","q":"When ketamine is used to sedate a patient with severe agitation, which airway complication must the team be prepared to manage?","a":"Laryngospasm and increased salivation","w":["Bronchospasm and wheezing","Complete loss of muscle tone","Angioedema of the tongue"],"x":"Ketamine generally preserves airway reflexes but can cause hypersalivation and, uncommonly, laryngospasm, so suction and airway equipment should be ready. Ketamine is a bronchodilator, so bronchospasm is unlikely.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp41488961","lv":"flightrn","d":"lithium","sys":"psych","q":"Which factor is most likely to raise lithium levels and lead to toxicity?","a":"Dehydration with regular NSAID use","w":["Drinking extra fluids during illness","Adding a daily vitamin D supplement","Taking acetaminophen for a headache"],"x":"Lithium is cleared by the kidneys, and dehydration, NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors and thiazides reduce its clearance. Acetaminophen does not affect lithium elimination.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa288c58e","lv":"flightrn","d":"amitriptyline","sys":"psych","q":"A patient who overdosed on amitriptyline has a widening QRS and hypotension. Which treatment is indicated?","a":"IV sodium bicarbonate","w":["IV physostigmine","IV flumazenil","IV procainamide"],"x":"Sodium bicarbonate counteracts tricyclic sodium channel blockade by raising serum sodium and pH. Physostigmine and flumazenil can provoke seizures or arrhythmias, and procainamide worsens sodium channel blockade.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpee42dfe5","lv":"flightrn","d":"bupropion","sys":"psych","q":"What is the major toxicity concern after an overdose of extended-release bupropion?","a":"Seizures, which may be delayed","w":["Isolated severe hypoglycemia","Massive internal bleeding","Acute pulmonary fibrosis"],"x":"Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold, and seizures are common in overdose; with extended-release forms they can appear many hours later. QRS widening and cardiac toxicity can also occur in large ingestions.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc6042ec9","lv":"flightrn","d":"benztropine","sys":"psych","q":"How does benztropine relieve acute dystonia caused by an antipsychotic?","a":"It blocks central muscarinic receptors, restoring dopamine-acetylcholine balance","w":["It stimulates dopamine receptors directly, reversing the antipsychotic effect","It enhances GABA activity in the spinal cord, causing muscle relaxation","It blocks serotonin receptors, reducing excess muscle contraction"],"x":"Antipsychotic dopamine blockade leaves relative cholinergic excess in the basal ganglia, and benztropine's anticholinergic action corrects this. It is not a dopamine agonist.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp011c6096","lv":"flightrn","d":"lorazepam","sys":"psych","q":"Why is lorazepam often preferred over diazepam for alcohol withdrawal in a patient with severe liver disease?","a":"It is metabolized by glucuronidation and has no active metabolites","w":["It is excreted unchanged in the urine without any metabolism","It is the only benzodiazepine that does not cause sedation","It is a much longer-acting drug that needs fewer doses"],"x":"Lorazepam's metabolism by glucuronidation is relatively preserved in liver disease, so it accumulates less. Diazepam relies on oxidative liver metabolism and has long-acting active metabolites.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpefbdaa6f","lv":"flightrn","d":"potassium-chloride","sys":"renal","q":"Which practice is correct when giving IV potassium chloride to a patient with hypokalemia?","a":"Give it diluted as an infusion on a pump, never as an IV push","w":["Give it as a rapid IV push followed by a large saline flush","Give it as an intramuscular injection into a large muscle","Give it as an undiluted bolus through the central line port"],"x":"IV potassium given by push can cause fatal arrhythmias and cardiac arrest, so it must be diluted and infused by pump with cardiac monitoring. Even through a central line, it is never pushed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2756d1d5","lv":"flightrn","d":"calcium-gluconate","sys":"renal","q":"A patient with hyperkalemia has peaked T waves and a widening QRS. Why is IV calcium given first?","a":"It stabilizes the cardiac cell membrane but does not lower potassium","w":["It shifts potassium into the cells and lowers the serum level","It binds potassium in the gut and removes it from the body","It increases potassium excretion by the kidneys within minutes"],"x":"Calcium raises the threshold potential and protects against arrhythmias within minutes, but the potassium level is unchanged. Insulin, beta agonists and bicarbonate shift potassium, and binders or dialysis remove it.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb7b448cc","lv":"flightrn","d":"regular-insulin","sys":"renal","q":"IV regular insulin and dextrose are given for hyperkalemia. What is the most important follow-up assessment?","a":"Frequent blood glucose checks for several hours","w":["Hourly serum calcium levels for a full day","Repeat chest radiograph for pulmonary edema","Repeat liver enzymes for insulin hepatotoxicity"],"x":"Insulin shifts potassium into cells but can cause hypoglycemia, which may be delayed, especially in kidney failure. Dextrose reduces but does not remove this risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp01a242e1","lv":"flightrn","d":"sodium-bicarbonate","sys":"renal","q":"Why should sodium bicarbonate and calcium not be given together in the same IV line without flushing between them?","a":"They form a calcium carbonate precipitate in the line","w":["They cancel each other's effects on the heart","They produce carbon monoxide gas in the tubing","They cause the IV catheter to dissolve quickly"],"x":"Bicarbonate and calcium salts precipitate as calcium carbonate, which can block the line or cause embolization. Lines must be flushed between them.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4b4645bd","lv":"flightrn","d":"hypertonic-saline-3-percent","sys":"renal","q":"Chronic severe hyponatremia is being corrected with 3% saline. Which complication can result from overly rapid correction?","a":"Osmotic demyelination syndrome","w":["Acute cerebral edema","Malignant hyperthermia","Acute adrenal crisis"],"x":"Brain cells adapt to chronic hyponatremia, and raising sodium too fast can damage myelin, especially in the pons, causing quadriparesis and dysphagia. Cerebral edema is a risk of the hyponatremia itself or of correcting hypernatremia too fast.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp24f29b14","lv":"flightrn","d":"furosemide","sys":"renal","q":"Which electrolyte disturbances should be monitored in a patient receiving repeated IV furosemide?","a":"Hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia","w":["Hyperkalemia and hypermagnesemia","Hypercalcemia and hyperkalemia","Hypernatremia and hyperkalemia"],"x":"Loop diuretics increase urinary loss of potassium, magnesium and calcium. Hyperkalemia is more typical of potassium-sparing diuretics.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe4159cc1","lv":"flightrn","d":"normal-saline","sys":"renal","q":"A trauma patient received large volumes of 0.9% sodium chloride. Which acid-base disturbance is most likely to develop?","a":"Hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis","w":["Hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis","Primary respiratory alkalosis","Anion gap metabolic acidosis"],"x":"Normal saline contains more chloride than plasma, and large volumes lower bicarbonate and cause a non-anion gap acidosis. Hypochloremic alkalosis is typical of vomiting or diuretic use.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf629077b","lv":"flightrn","d":"sodium-chloride-23-4-percent","sys":"renal","q":"23.4% sodium chloride is ordered for a patient with refractory intracranial hypertension. How must it be given?","a":"Through a central venous line","w":["Through a small peripheral IV","By intramuscular injection","By the subcutaneous route"],"x":"Highly concentrated saline is extremely hyperosmolar and can cause severe vein damage and tissue necrosis, so it is given only through a central line. It is also a high-alert medication that must be clearly distinguished from standard saline.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf89099b7","lv":"flightrn","d":"albumin-human","sys":"renal","q":"How does IV albumin help expand intravascular volume?","a":"It increases plasma oncotic pressure and draws fluid into the vessels","w":["It increases plasma osmolality by adding sodium to the bloodstream","It constricts blood vessels by stimulating alpha-1 receptors","It stimulates the kidneys to retain free water and concentrate urine"],"x":"Albumin is the main contributor to plasma oncotic pressure, so infusing it pulls fluid from the interstitium into the vascular space. It is not a vasoconstrictor.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa92341b7","lv":"flightrn","d":"sodium-zirconium-cyclosilicate","sys":"renal","q":"Which statement about sodium zirconium cyclosilicate in hyperkalemia is accurate?","a":"It is an oral binder that removes potassium through the GI tract and acts more slowly than IV shifting agents","w":["It is an IV agent that stabilizes the cardiac membrane within minutes of being given to the patient","It is an IV agent that shifts potassium into cells and is given along with dextrose to the patient","It is an inhaled agent that shifts potassium into cells by stimulating the beta-2 receptors"],"x":"Sodium zirconium cyclosilicate exchanges sodium and hydrogen for potassium in the gut, lowering levels over hours. It is not suitable as the only treatment for life-threatening hyperkalemia, where calcium and shifting agents are given first.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0ba22efe","lv":"flightrn","d":"calcitonin-salmon","sys":"bone","q":"In severe hypercalcemia of malignancy, why is calcitonin often given along with a bisphosphonate?","a":"It lowers calcium within hours while the bisphosphonate takes days to work","w":["It permanently cures hypercalcemia so the bisphosphonate is only a backup","It prevents the kidney damage that bisphosphonates always cause","It is more effective over weeks, while the bisphosphonate acts in minutes"],"x":"Calcitonin acts quickly but loses effect within a couple of days due to tachyphylaxis, bridging the gap until the bisphosphonate takes effect. Bisphosphonates have a slower onset.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6133acc2","lv":"flightrn","d":"zoledronic-acid","sys":"bone","q":"Before giving zoledronic acid for hypercalcemia of malignancy, which lab value is most important to review?","a":"Serum creatinine","w":["Serum amylase","Serum ammonia","Serum troponin"],"x":"Zoledronic acid can cause kidney injury, so renal function is checked before each dose and dosing is adjusted. Amylase, ammonia and troponin are not routinely affected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe10d6edc","lv":"flightrn","d":"pamidronate","sys":"bone","q":"How does pamidronate lower serum calcium in hypercalcemia of malignancy?","a":"It inhibits osteoclast-mediated bone resorption","w":["It increases calcium excretion in the urine","It binds calcium in the gut and blocks absorption","It increases parathyroid hormone release"],"x":"Bisphosphonates such as pamidronate bind bone and inhibit osteoclasts, reducing calcium release into the blood. Increasing urinary calcium loss is the role of saline hydration.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd606e9f5","lv":"flightrn","d":"rasburicase","sys":"bone","q":"Before rasburicase is given for tumor lysis syndrome, which condition must be ruled out?","a":"G6PD deficiency","w":["Sulfa allergy","Celiac disease","Iron deficiency"],"x":"Rasburicase produces hydrogen peroxide when breaking down uric acid, which can cause severe hemolysis and methemoglobinemia in G6PD deficiency. Testing is recommended for patients at higher risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp085b4d28","lv":"flightrn","d":"rasburicase","sys":"bone","q":"Why must blood samples for uric acid be placed on ice after a patient receives rasburicase?","a":"The drug keeps breaking down uric acid in the tube, falsely lowering results","w":["The drug makes blood clot rapidly, so cooling keeps the sample liquid","The drug raises uric acid in warm samples, falsely elevating results","The drug causes hemolysis at room temperature, which ruins the sample"],"x":"Rasburicase continues to act in the sample at room temperature, so uric acid levels appear falsely low unless the sample is chilled and analyzed promptly. It does not cause clotting in the tube.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0b8f8a60","lv":"flightrn","d":"allopurinol","sys":"bone","q":"How does allopurinol differ from rasburicase in managing tumor lysis syndrome?","a":"It prevents new uric acid formation but does not break down existing uric acid","w":["It breaks down existing uric acid into a more soluble compound very rapidly","It binds uric acid in the intestine and removes it in the stool over days","It increases urinary uric acid excretion by blocking renal reabsorption"],"x":"Allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase, lowering production, while rasburicase converts existing uric acid into allantoin. Increasing renal excretion is how probenecid works.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp82cd7c31","lv":"flightrn","d":"colchicine","sys":"bone","q":"A patient taking colchicine for gout is started on another drug. Which addition most increases the risk of colchicine toxicity?","a":"Clarithromycin","w":["Acetaminophen","Amoxicillin","Famotidine"],"x":"Clarithromycin strongly inhibits CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, raising colchicine levels and causing potentially fatal toxicity. Acetaminophen, amoxicillin and famotidine do not significantly interact.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpff479d2f","lv":"flightrn","d":"denosumab","sys":"bone","q":"Which complication is a particular concern after denosumab in a patient with advanced kidney disease?","a":"Severe hypocalcemia","w":["Severe hypercalcemia","Severe hyperkalemia","Severe hypoglycemia"],"x":"Denosumab strongly inhibits bone resorption, and patients with advanced CKD are at high risk of severe, sometimes fatal hypocalcemia. Calcium and vitamin D status should be checked and monitored.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0466e576","lv":"flightrn","d":"phenobarbital","sys":"bone","q":"Phenobarbital is given for refractory seizures. Which adverse effects should be monitored most closely?","a":"Respiratory depression and hypotension","w":["Hypertension and tachycardia","Hyperthermia and muscle rigidity","Hyperglycemia and polyuria"],"x":"Phenobarbital is a barbiturate that causes sedation, respiratory depression and hypotension, especially after benzodiazepines. Airway and hemodynamic support should be ready.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4fbd1021","lv":"flightrn","d":"cinacalcet","sys":"bone","q":"A dialysis patient taking cinacalcet reports tingling around the mouth and muscle cramps. What does this most likely indicate?","a":"Hypocalcemia","w":["Hyperkalemia","Hypernatremia","Hypercalcemia"],"x":"Cinacalcet increases the sensitivity of the calcium-sensing receptor and lowers PTH and serum calcium. Perioral paresthesias and cramps are classic signs of low calcium and should be reported.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1412b7aa","lv":"flightrn","d":"hydroxocobalamin","sys":"tox","q":"After hydroxocobalamin is given to a smoke inhalation patient, the skin and urine turn red. What else should the nurse expect?","a":"Interference with some colorimetric lab tests and oximetry readings","w":["A sudden drop in blood pressure from vasodilation","Severe hemolysis that requires urgent transfusion","Rapid development of methemoglobinemia"],"x":"Hydroxocobalamin's red color is expected and can interfere with lab assays and co-oximetry. It often raises, rather than lowers, blood pressure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdcd438c3","lv":"flightrn","d":"fomepizole","sys":"tox","q":"How does fomepizole treat ethylene glycol poisoning?","a":"It inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase, blocking formation of toxic metabolites","w":["It binds ethylene glycol in the gut and prevents its absorption","It speeds kidney elimination of ethylene glycol by alkalinizing urine","It converts ethylene glycol directly into harmless carbon dioxide"],"x":"Fomepizole blocks alcohol dehydrogenase, preventing the conversion of ethylene glycol and methanol into toxic acids. Activated charcoal does not meaningfully bind toxic alcohols.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf2fc0a17","lv":"flightrn","d":"n-acetylcysteine","sys":"tox","q":"How does N-acetylcysteine protect the liver in acetaminophen overdose?","a":"It replenishes glutathione, which detoxifies the toxic acetaminophen metabolite","w":["It blocks absorption of acetaminophen from the stomach and small intestine","It binds acetaminophen in the blood and carries it to the kidneys for excretion","It inhibits the liver enzyme that would otherwise clear acetaminophen safely"],"x":"N-acetylcysteine restores glutathione, which neutralizes NAPQI, the toxic metabolite. It works best when started early, ideally within 8 hours of ingestion.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd1551083","lv":"flightrn","d":"atropine","sys":"tox","q":"In organophosphate poisoning, which clinical endpoint best guides further atropine dosing?","a":"Drying of bronchial secretions and easier breathing","w":["Return of normal pupil size in both eyes","Heart rate falling to a normal range","Resolution of muscle weakness and twitching"],"x":"Atropine is titrated to clear bronchorrhea and improve oxygenation; tachycardia is not a reason to limit it. Atropine does not reverse nicotinic effects such as weakness, which require pralidoxime.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdb05d548","lv":"flightrn","d":"pralidoxime","sys":"tox","q":"In organophosphate poisoning, what does pralidoxime add to treatment with atropine?","a":"It reactivates acetylcholinesterase and improves nicotinic muscle weakness","w":["It blocks muscarinic receptors and dries respiratory secretions","It binds the organophosphate in the gut and prevents absorption","It sedates the patient and controls organophosphate seizures"],"x":"Pralidoxime regenerates acetylcholinesterase before the enzyme bond ages, helping reverse weakness and paralysis. Atropine blocks muscarinic effects, and benzodiazepines treat seizures.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa5b249d7","lv":"flightrn","d":"methylene-blue","sys":"tox","q":"Methylene blue is ordered for methemoglobinemia. Which medication history raises the most concern?","a":"Current use of an SSRI antidepressant","w":["Current use of a statin for cholesterol","Current use of a daily proton pump inhibitor","Current use of a thiazide for blood pressure"],"x":"Methylene blue inhibits monoamine oxidase and has a boxed warning for serotonin syndrome with serotonergic drugs. G6PD deficiency is also an important concern.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3eba6345","lv":"flightrn","d":"lipid-emulsion","sys":"tox","q":"During a nerve block with bupivacaine, a patient has a seizure and then cardiovascular collapse. Which antidote is indicated?","a":"IV lipid emulsion","w":["IV flumazenil","IV naloxone","IV fomepizole"],"x":"Lipid emulsion is the specific therapy for local anesthetic systemic toxicity and is given alongside airway support and seizure control. Flumazenil and naloxone do not reverse local anesthetic toxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp053528d3","lv":"flightrn","d":"glucagon","sys":"tox","q":"Why is glucagon used for symptomatic beta-blocker overdose?","a":"It raises intracellular cAMP through its own receptor, bypassing beta receptors","w":["It competitively displaces the beta blocker from beta receptors in the heart","It enhances hepatic metabolism of the beta blocker into inactive products","It blocks calcium channels to reduce the beta blocker's toxic effects"],"x":"Glucagon stimulates its own receptor to increase cAMP, improving heart rate and contractility. Nausea and vomiting are common, so airway protection matters.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc6d8c5ca","lv":"flightrn","d":"physostigmine","sys":"tox","q":"Before giving physostigmine for suspected anticholinergic toxicity, which ECG finding is a major concern?","a":"A wide QRS suggesting tricyclic ingestion","w":["A narrow-complex sinus tachycardia","Peaked T waves from mild hyperkalemia","A normal sinus rhythm with no changes"],"x":"Physostigmine can cause bradyasystole and seizures in tricyclic overdose, which commonly shows QRS widening. Sinus tachycardia is an expected finding in anticholinergic toxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpabb8ed83","lv":"flightrn","d":"crotalidae-polyvalent-immune-fab","sys":"tox","q":"After antivenom for rattlesnake envenomation, why are follow-up coagulation labs needed after discharge?","a":"Venom effects can recur as antivenom is cleared","w":["Antivenom itself causes delayed liver failure","Antivenom destroys platelets for several weeks","Antivenom triggers heparin-like anticoagulation"],"x":"Fab fragments clear faster than some venom components, so coagulopathy and low platelets can recur days later. Delayed serum sickness can also occur.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3b9471d4","lv":"flightrn","d":"vancomycin","sys":"infect","q":"During a rapid vancomycin infusion, the patient develops flushing and itching of the face, neck and upper chest. What should the nurse recognize?","a":"An infusion-related reaction linked to rapid infusion","w":["A true IgE-mediated penicillin cross allergy","Early signs of Stevens-Johnson syndrome","Red man rash caused by acute kidney injury"],"x":"Vancomycin infusion reaction (formerly red man syndrome) is caused by histamine release with rapid infusion and is typically managed by slowing the rate and giving an antihistamine. It is not an IgE allergy or a sign of kidney injury.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp31ee818c","lv":"flightrn","d":"vancomycin","sys":"infect","q":"Which monitoring is most important during prolonged IV vancomycin therapy for sepsis?","a":"Serum creatinine and drug levels for nephrotoxicity","w":["Serum glucose for drug-induced hypoglycemia","Serum amylase for drug-induced pancreatitis","Pulmonary function tests for lung fibrosis"],"x":"Vancomycin can cause kidney injury, especially at high exposures or with other nephrotoxins such as piperacillin-tazobactam, so creatinine and levels guide dosing. Pancreatitis and lung fibrosis are not typical effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9d9927d9","lv":"flightrn","d":"cefepime","sys":"infect","q":"A patient with acute kidney injury on cefepime becomes confused, with myoclonus. What should the nurse suspect?","a":"Cefepime-induced neurotoxicity","w":["Expected sepsis-related delirium only","Vancomycin infusion reaction","Hypoglycemia from the antibiotic"],"x":"Cefepime can accumulate in renal impairment and cause encephalopathy, myoclonus and nonconvulsive seizures. Recognizing it matters because it can be mistaken for septic delirium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9c93a8e6","lv":"flightrn","d":"ceftriaxone","sys":"infect","q":"Why is IV ceftriaxone used with special caution in neonates?","a":"It can precipitate with IV calcium and displace bilirubin","w":["It causes severe hyperkalemia in newborn kidneys","It is inactivated by breast milk in the infant's gut","It produces gray baby syndrome in premature infants"],"x":"Ceftriaxone is contraindicated in hyperbilirubinemic neonates and in neonates receiving IV calcium because of fatal precipitates. Gray baby syndrome is caused by chloramphenicol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2b9f0f23","lv":"flightrn","d":"meropenem","sys":"infect","q":"A patient with a seizure disorder controlled on valproic acid is started on meropenem. What interaction should the nurse anticipate?","a":"Valproate levels drop sharply, raising seizure risk","w":["Valproate levels rise sharply, causing toxicity","Meropenem levels rise, causing kidney damage","No interaction is expected between the two drugs"],"x":"Carbapenems rapidly and markedly lower valproic acid levels, which can lead to breakthrough seizures, and increasing the valproate dose does not overcome it. An alternative antibiotic or anticonvulsant is generally needed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd52d7b0e","lv":"flightrn","d":"gentamicin","sys":"infect","q":"Which adverse effects should be monitored during gentamicin therapy?","a":"Kidney injury and hearing or balance loss","w":["Liver failure and jaundice","Bone marrow suppression","Severe hypertension"],"x":"Aminoglycosides are nephrotoxic and ototoxic, and the hearing damage can be permanent. Levels and renal function guide dosing.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9d20fd74","lv":"flightrn","d":"acyclovir","sys":"infect","q":"IV acyclovir is started for suspected HSV encephalitis. Which nursing measure helps prevent a known adverse effect?","a":"Ensure adequate hydration to prevent crystal nephropathy","w":["Limit fluids to prevent drug-induced cerebral edema","Give with calcium to prevent drug-induced hypocalcemia","Keep the patient supine to prevent orthostatic hypotension"],"x":"Acyclovir can crystallize in the renal tubules, especially with dehydration or rapid infusion, so adequate hydration is important. Fluid restriction would increase this risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc7fbfa43","lv":"flightrn","d":"linezolid","sys":"infect","q":"A patient taking sertraline is started on linezolid. Which complication should the nurse watch for?","a":"Serotonin syndrome","w":["Red man syndrome","Disulfiram reaction","Gray baby syndrome"],"x":"Linezolid is a weak monoamine oxidase inhibitor and can cause serotonin syndrome with SSRIs and other serotonergic drugs. Red man syndrome is a vancomycin infusion reaction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp48c13583","lv":"flightrn","d":"clindamycin","sys":"infect","q":"Why is clindamycin often added to beta-lactam therapy for necrotizing fasciitis or toxic shock syndrome?","a":"It suppresses bacterial toxin production","w":["It provides coverage for Pseudomonas","It prevents C. difficile infection","It reduces nephrotoxicity of other drugs"],"x":"Clindamycin inhibits protein synthesis and reduces toxin production by streptococci and staphylococci. It does not cover Pseudomonas and actually raises the risk of C. difficile.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp065a542c","lv":"flightrn","d":"piperacillin-tazobactam","sys":"infect","q":"Which best describes piperacillin-tazobactam as an empiric sepsis antibiotic?","a":"An antipseudomonal penicillin combined with a beta-lactamase inhibitor","w":["A carbapenem combined with a renal enzyme inhibitor","A glycopeptide that targets resistant gram-positive cocci","A third-generation cephalosporin without antipseudomonal activity"],"x":"Piperacillin is an extended-spectrum penicillin with Pseudomonas activity, and tazobactam protects it from many beta-lactamases. It does not cover MRSA, which is why vancomycin is often added.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe720bf39","lv":"flightrn","d":"regular-insulin","sys":"endo","q":"Before starting an IV insulin infusion for diabetic ketoacidosis, which lab result must be reviewed?","a":"Serum potassium","w":["Serum calcium","Serum amylase","Serum albumin"],"x":"Insulin drives potassium into cells, and starting it when potassium is low can cause life-threatening hypokalemia. Guidelines call for potassium replacement first if the level is low.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdec33904","lv":"flightrn","d":"insulin-glargine","sys":"endo","q":"When a DKA patient is ready to transition off the insulin infusion, why is subcutaneous basal insulin such as glargine given before the drip ends?","a":"It prevents a gap in insulin coverage that could cause rebound ketoacidosis","w":["It is needed to counter hypoglycemia caused by the IV insulin infusion","It speeds clearance of the remaining IV insulin from the bloodstream","It lowers serum potassium more quickly than IV insulin alone"],"x":"IV regular insulin has a very short half-life, so subcutaneous basal insulin is overlapped for a period to keep insulin levels steady. Without this overlap, hyperglycemia and ketosis can return quickly.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe4165087","lv":"flightrn","d":"hydrocortisone","sys":"endo","q":"Why is hydrocortisone the preferred steroid for adrenal crisis?","a":"It provides both glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid activity","w":["It has no glucocorticoid activity and only replaces aldosterone","It is the only steroid that can be given by the IV route","It has the longest half-life of all available corticosteroids"],"x":"High-dose hydrocortisone replaces cortisol and has enough mineralocorticoid effect to support sodium and volume. Dexamethasone has no meaningful mineralocorticoid activity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe9acf09b","lv":"flightrn","d":"hydrocortisone","sys":"endo","q":"In septic shock, which situation is the usual indication for adding IV hydrocortisone?","a":"Shock that requires ongoing vasopressor therapy despite fluids","w":["Any fever above normal with a positive blood culture","Sepsis without hypotension in a patient with mild confusion","Shock that resolves completely after the first fluid bolus"],"x":"Guidelines suggest IV hydrocortisone for adults with septic shock and an ongoing vasopressor requirement. It is not used for sepsis without shock.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc550d89b","lv":"flightrn","d":"dextrose","sys":"endo","q":"Concentrated dextrose is given for hypoglycemia through a small peripheral vein. Which complication should the nurse watch for?","a":"Phlebitis and tissue injury if it extravasates","w":["Rebound hypercalcemia within several minutes","Immediate severe bronchospasm from the solution","Hemolysis from the low osmolality of the fluid"],"x":"Concentrated dextrose is hypertonic and irritating to veins, and extravasation can cause tissue damage, so a patent, large vein is preferred. Diluted concentrations are used in children for the same reason.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5f2e17a9","lv":"flightrn","d":"glucagon","sys":"endo","q":"Why might IM glucagon be less effective for hypoglycemia in a patient with chronic alcohol use and poor nutrition?","a":"Liver glycogen stores may be depleted","w":["Alcohol blocks glucagon receptors directly","Glucagon is inactivated by alcohol in muscle","Malnutrition prevents absorption from muscle"],"x":"Glucagon raises glucose by breaking down liver glycogen, so it works poorly when glycogen stores are low. IV dextrose is preferred in these patients.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp76c2140b","lv":"flightrn","d":"levothyroxine","sys":"endo","q":"In myxedema coma, why is IV hydrocortisone commonly given before or with IV levothyroxine?","a":"Thyroid hormone can precipitate adrenal crisis if adrenal insufficiency coexists","w":["Hydrocortisone is needed to convert levothyroxine into its active form","Levothyroxine causes severe hyperglycemia that steroids prevent","Hydrocortisone prevents the allergic reactions levothyroxine often causes"],"x":"Restoring thyroid hormone increases metabolism and cortisol clearance, which can trigger adrenal crisis in a patient with unrecognized adrenal insufficiency. Steroids are not needed to activate levothyroxine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp46d4b035","lv":"flightrn","d":"propylthiouracil","sys":"endo","q":"Which property makes propylthiouracil a preferred thionamide in thyroid storm?","a":"It also blocks peripheral conversion of T4 to T3","w":["It destroys thyroid tissue within several hours","It blocks beta receptors to control heart rate","It releases stored thyroid hormone into the blood"],"x":"Both thionamides block new hormone synthesis, but propylthiouracil also inhibits peripheral conversion of T4 to the more active T3. Beta blockers such as propranolol are used to control adrenergic symptoms.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp77ce5992","lv":"flightrn","d":"methimazole","sys":"endo","q":"A patient taking methimazole develops fever and a severe sore throat. What should the nurse recognize?","a":"Possible agranulocytosis that requires urgent evaluation","w":["An expected mild side effect with no need to report","A sign the thyroid dose is too low and must be raised","An early symptom of drug-induced hypothyroidism only"],"x":"Thionamides can cause agranulocytosis, which presents with fever and sore throat and requires prompt notification and a white blood cell count. It is not a sign of underdosing.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0c31aaa3","lv":"flightrn","d":"vasopressin","sys":"endo","q":"A brain-dead organ donor has very high, dilute urine output and a rising serum sodium. Why is vasopressin used?","a":"To treat diabetes insipidus by increasing renal free water reabsorption","w":["To treat SIADH by blocking water reabsorption in the kidney","To treat hyperkalemia by shifting potassium into the cells","To treat adrenal crisis by replacing missing cortisol levels"],"x":"Loss of pituitary function after brain death commonly causes central diabetes insipidus, and vasopressin replaces antidiuretic hormone while also supporting blood pressure. SIADH causes the opposite picture.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp391f8e70","lv":"flightrn","d":"ondansetron","sys":"gi","q":"Before giving IV ondansetron to a patient with a history of heart disease, which assessment is most important?","a":"Review the ECG and electrolytes for QT prolongation risk","w":["Check the blood glucose for drug-induced hypoglycemia","Check peak flow for possible bronchospasm from the drug","Review liver enzymes for acute drug-induced hepatitis"],"x":"Ondansetron can prolong the QT interval, and risk rises with low potassium or magnesium and other QT-prolonging drugs. It does not commonly affect glucose or airway tone.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9d329356","lv":"flightrn","d":"metoclopramide","sys":"gi","q":"In which patient is metoclopramide contraindicated?","a":"A patient with a suspected mechanical bowel obstruction","w":["A patient with diabetic gastroparesis and nausea","A patient with nausea from a migraine headache","A patient with postoperative nausea and vomiting"],"x":"Metoclopramide increases GI motility, which can be harmful when there is mechanical obstruction, perforation or GI bleeding. Gastroparesis is one of its labeled uses.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp546809f3","lv":"flightrn","d":"promethazine","sys":"gi","q":"Why does promethazine carry a boxed warning about the injectable form?","a":"It can cause severe tissue injury, including gangrene, if extravasated or given intra-arterially","w":["It can cause sudden severe hypertension if given faster than recommended through a vein","It can cause fatal hyperkalemia when given to patients with mild kidney disease","It can cause rapid hemolysis in patients with low levels of vitamin B12"],"x":"Injectable promethazine is a vesicant, and perivascular extravasation or intra-arterial injection can cause gangrene; deep IM injection is the preferred route. A separate boxed warning covers respiratory depression in children under 2.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2181a2f0","lv":"flightrn","d":"pantoprazole","sys":"gi","q":"What is the main purpose of IV pantoprazole in a patient with an upper GI bleed from a peptic ulcer?","a":"It reduces gastric acid, which helps stabilize the clot over the ulcer","w":["It constricts splanchnic vessels to reduce portal venous pressure","It neutralizes acid already present by direct chemical reaction","It coats the ulcer base with a protective barrier against acid"],"x":"Proton pump inhibitors raise gastric pH, which helps platelet aggregation and clot stability. Reducing portal pressure is the role of octreotide in variceal bleeding, and coating the ulcer is sucralfate's action.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb36b510e","lv":"flightrn","d":"lactulose","sys":"gi","q":"How does lactulose improve hepatic encephalopathy?","a":"It acidifies the colon, trapping ammonia as ammonium for excretion","w":["It binds ammonia in the blood and carries it to the kidneys","It blocks the liver from producing ammonia from protein","It kills all ammonia-producing bacteria in the colon"],"x":"Bacterial breakdown of lactulose lowers colonic pH, converting ammonia to ammonium, which is not absorbed and is removed in the stool. Rifaximin reduces ammonia-producing bacteria.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6a887876","lv":"flightrn","d":"activated-charcoal","sys":"gi","q":"Activated charcoal does NOT effectively adsorb which ingested substance?","a":"Lithium","w":["Aspirin","Carbamazepine","Acetaminophen"],"x":"Charcoal binds poorly to metals and ions such as lithium and iron, and to alcohols. It also requires a protected airway because aspiration is a serious risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5cba7142","lv":"flightrn","d":"scopolamine-transdermal","sys":"gi","q":"Which teaching is correct for a patient using a scopolamine patch for motion sickness?","a":"Wash hands after handling the patch to avoid touching the eyes","w":["Apply the patch only after nausea and vomiting have started","Place the patch directly over the stomach for faster absorption","Expect increased saliva and sweating as common side effects"],"x":"Scopolamine on the fingers can enter the eye and cause pupil dilation and blurred vision, so hand washing is important. The patch goes behind the ear several hours before travel, and it causes dry mouth, not increased saliva.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc7ff6ce7","lv":"flightrn","d":"famotidine","sys":"gi","q":"How does famotidine reduce gastric acid?","a":"It blocks histamine H2 receptors on parietal cells","w":["It irreversibly blocks the proton pump in parietal cells","It neutralizes stomach acid by direct chemical reaction","It blocks muscarinic receptors on the gastric lining"],"x":"Famotidine is an H2 receptor antagonist. Irreversible blockade of the proton pump is how proton pump inhibitors such as pantoprazole work.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5d5fecdc","lv":"flightrn","d":"prochlorperazine","sys":"gi","q":"Shortly after IV prochlorperazine, a patient becomes very restless and cannot sit still. What should the nurse recognize?","a":"Akathisia","w":["Hypoglycemia","Serotonin syndrome","Alcohol withdrawal"],"x":"Prochlorperazine is a dopamine antagonist that can cause akathisia, an extrapyramidal effect of inner restlessness. It often responds to diphenhydramine and should be reported.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp82fbd681","lv":"flightrn","d":"meclizine","sys":"gi","q":"Which side effects are most common with meclizine taken for motion sickness or vertigo?","a":"Drowsiness and dry mouth","w":["Insomnia and diarrhea","Hypertension and tremor","Excess saliva and sweating"],"x":"Meclizine is an antihistamine with anticholinergic effects, so sedation and dry mouth are common. Patients should be cautious with driving and other sedating drugs.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp073004c4","lv":"flightrn","d":"magnesium-sulfate","sys":"ob","q":"A patient with preeclampsia is receiving IV magnesium sulfate. Which finding is often the first sign of magnesium toxicity?","a":"Loss of deep tendon reflexes","w":["Hyperactive deep tendon reflexes","Increased urine output","Rising blood pressure"],"x":"Loss of patellar reflexes appears before respiratory depression and cardiac arrest as magnesium levels rise. Hyperreflexia is a sign of worsening preeclampsia, not magnesium toxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb18fdfc0","lv":"flightrn","d":"calcium-gluconate","sys":"ob","q":"A patient on magnesium sulfate for preeclampsia has slow, shallow respirations and absent reflexes. Which medication is the antidote?","a":"Calcium gluconate","w":["Naloxone","Flumazenil","Protamine sulfate"],"x":"IV calcium gluconate antagonizes magnesium at the neuromuscular junction and heart. Naloxone reverses opioids, not magnesium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp39436880","lv":"flightrn","d":"oxytocin","sys":"ob","q":"During an oxytocin infusion, which finding is most important to report promptly?","a":"Uterine tachysystole with fetal heart rate changes","w":["Mild cramping with each uterine contraction","A small rise in maternal temperature","Mild nausea without vomiting or pain"],"x":"Excess oxytocin can cause too-frequent contractions that reduce placental blood flow and lead to fetal distress or uterine rupture. Mild cramping is expected with contractions.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc40300e6","lv":"flightrn","d":"oxytocin","sys":"ob","q":"A patient has received prolonged high-dose oxytocin with large volumes of hypotonic fluid and now has a headache and confusion. What should be suspected?","a":"Water intoxication with hyponatremia","w":["Hypercalcemia from the infusion","Hypoglycemia from uterine activity","Serotonin syndrome from oxytocin"],"x":"Oxytocin has an antidiuretic effect similar to vasopressin, and prolonged infusion with excess fluid can cause water intoxication, hyponatremia and seizures. Hypercalcemia and serotonin syndrome are not associated with oxytocin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4be4f0e8","lv":"flightrn","d":"methylergonovine","sys":"ob","q":"Methylergonovine is being considered for postpartum hemorrhage. Which patient history is a contraindication?","a":"Preeclampsia or chronic hypertension","w":["Asthma that is well controlled","Gestational diabetes on diet only","A prior cesarean delivery"],"x":"Methylergonovine causes vasoconstriction and can trigger severe hypertension, stroke or seizures in hypertensive patients. Asthma is a contraindication for carboprost, not methylergonovine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd30ecd70","lv":"flightrn","d":"carboprost","sys":"ob","q":"For postpartum hemorrhage, which patient history makes carboprost a poor choice?","a":"Asthma","w":["Migraine","Anemia","Diabetes"],"x":"Carboprost is a prostaglandin F2-alpha analog that can cause bronchospasm, so it is avoided in asthma. Its common side effects include diarrhea, fever and vomiting.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc0870422","lv":"flightrn","d":"labetalol","sys":"ob","q":"How does IV labetalol lower blood pressure in severe-range hypertension of pregnancy?","a":"It blocks alpha-1 and beta receptors","w":["It blocks calcium channels in arteries","It directly relaxes arterial smooth muscle","It inhibits angiotensin-converting enzyme"],"x":"Labetalol combines alpha-1 blockade with nonselective beta blockade, lowering vascular resistance without reflex tachycardia. Direct arteriolar relaxation describes hydralazine, and ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in pregnancy.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe4c003d4","lv":"flightrn","d":"terbutaline","sys":"ob","q":"Terbutaline is given to a patient in preterm labor. Which maternal finding should be reported right away?","a":"Chest pain with marked tachycardia","w":["Mild hand tremor and nervousness","A slight rise in blood glucose","A dry mouth and mild thirst"],"x":"Terbutaline has a boxed warning against prolonged use for preterm labor because of serious maternal cardiac events, including arrhythmias, ischemia and pulmonary edema. Tremor and mild hyperglycemia are common, expected effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfaa585b7","lv":"flightrn","d":"betamethasone","sys":"ob","q":"Why is betamethasone given to a patient at risk of preterm delivery?","a":"To accelerate fetal lung maturity","w":["To stop uterine contractions","To prevent maternal seizures","To prevent group B strep infection"],"x":"Antenatal corticosteroids speed fetal surfactant production and lung maturation, lowering respiratory distress syndrome and neonatal death. Tocolytics slow contractions, and magnesium prevents eclamptic seizures.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpddfa88c2","lv":"flightrn","d":"nifedipine","sys":"ob","q":"Immediate-release nifedipine is used for severe-range hypertension in pregnancy. By which route is it given?","a":"Orally, swallowed whole as a tablet or capsule","w":["Sublingually, after the capsule is punctured","Intravenously, as a slow push over minutes","Intramuscularly, as a deep gluteal injection"],"x":"Oral immediate-release nifedipine is an accepted first-line option for acute severe hypertension in pregnancy. Biting or puncturing the capsule for sublingual use is not recommended because it can cause unpredictable, sudden drops in blood pressure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp374f14f5","lv":"flightrn","d":"epinephrine-auto-injector","sys":"immune","q":"Where should an epinephrine auto-injector be given for anaphylaxis?","a":"The anterolateral thigh, intramuscularly","w":["The deltoid muscle, subcutaneously","The abdomen, into the subcutaneous fat","The forearm, as an intradermal injection"],"x":"IM injection into the anterolateral thigh gives faster, higher peak epinephrine levels than subcutaneous or deltoid injection. It can be given through clothing.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd8a4b671","lv":"flightrn","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"immune","q":"What is the role of diphenhydramine in the treatment of anaphylaxis?","a":"An adjunct for hives and itching that does not relieve airway obstruction or shock","w":["The first-line drug that reverses airway swelling and hypotension within minutes","A replacement for epinephrine in patients with a history of heart disease","A drug that prevents biphasic reactions when given in place of epinephrine"],"x":"Antihistamines help skin symptoms but do not treat bronchospasm, airway edema or shock. IM epinephrine is the first-line treatment for anaphylaxis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp88ed59d2","lv":"flightrn","d":"intravenous-immune-globulin","sys":"immune","q":"Which serious adverse effects are included in the boxed warning for intravenous immune globulin?","a":"Thrombosis and acute kidney injury","w":["Severe hypoglycemia and seizures","Hepatic failure and jaundice","Pulmonary fibrosis and cough"],"x":"IVIG can cause thrombosis, renal dysfunction and acute renal failure, especially in older, dehydrated or high-risk patients. Adequate hydration and slower infusion help reduce risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9cf3bb50","lv":"flightrn","d":"tacrolimus","sys":"immune","q":"A transplant patient on tacrolimus is started on fluconazole. What effect should the nurse anticipate?","a":"Increased tacrolimus levels with risk of toxicity","w":["Decreased tacrolimus levels with risk of rejection","Inactivation of fluconazole by the tacrolimus","No change in either drug's blood level"],"x":"Fluconazole inhibits CYP3A4, which metabolizes tacrolimus, raising levels and the risk of nephrotoxicity and neurotoxicity. Level monitoring is needed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb73632ce","lv":"flightrn","d":"cyclosporine","sys":"immune","q":"Which adverse effects are most important to monitor in a transplant patient on cyclosporine?","a":"Nephrotoxicity, hypertension and hyperkalemia","w":["Hypotension, hypokalemia and hypoglycemia","Bradycardia, hypothermia and hypocalcemia","Hepatic failure, hyponatremia and anemia"],"x":"Cyclosporine constricts renal vessels, causing kidney injury, hypertension and potassium retention. Levels and renal function are monitored closely.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcb1f1a88","lv":"flightrn","d":"tocilizumab","sys":"immune","q":"Why is tocilizumab given to a patient with cytokine release syndrome after CAR T-cell therapy?","a":"It blocks the interleukin-6 receptor, which drives the syndrome","w":["It destroys the CAR T cells so the reaction ends at once","It blocks histamine release from activated mast cells","It replaces depleted immunoglobulins in the plasma"],"x":"Interleukin-6 is a key mediator of cytokine release syndrome, and tocilizumab is FDA approved for its treatment. It does not eliminate the CAR T cells.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp28c10aca","lv":"flightrn","d":"eculizumab","sys":"immune","q":"A patient receiving eculizumab develops a fever and headache. Why is this especially urgent?","a":"Complement blockade greatly raises the risk of meningococcal infection","w":["Eculizumab commonly causes aseptic meningitis that needs surgery","Fever means the eculizumab has stopped working and must be doubled","Eculizumab causes a cytokine storm that appears weeks after dosing"],"x":"Eculizumab inhibits terminal complement, which is needed to fight Neisseria meningitidis, and has a boxed warning for life-threatening meningococcal infection. Patients need vaccination and prompt evaluation of fever.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc7c830be","lv":"flightrn","d":"nivolumab","sys":"immune","q":"A patient receiving nivolumab develops frequent watery diarrhea and abdominal pain. What should the nurse recognize?","a":"A possible immune-related colitis","w":["An expected effect needing no report","A sign of tumor lysis syndrome","An allergic reaction to the infusion"],"x":"Checkpoint inhibitors can cause immune-mediated inflammation of the colon, lungs, liver and endocrine glands, often treated with corticosteroids. New diarrhea should be reported promptly, as it can become severe.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4e26304f","lv":"flightrn","d":"antithymocyte-globulin","sys":"immune","q":"During an antithymocyte globulin infusion, the patient develops fever, chills and hypotension. What is the most likely cause?","a":"An infusion-related reaction with cytokine release","w":["Acute rejection of the transplanted organ","A delayed transfusion hemolytic reaction","Expected septic shock from the transplant"],"x":"Antithymocyte globulin commonly causes cytokine release with fever, chills and hypotension, so patients are premedicated and monitored closely. These signs develop during the infusion and are not organ rejection.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8485e408","lv":"flightrn","d":"nirsevimab","sys":"immune","q":"How does nirsevimab protect infants against RSV?","a":"It provides passive immunity with a monoclonal antibody","w":["It triggers the infant to make long-term antibodies","It blocks viral replication inside infected cells","It boosts interferon release from immune cells"],"x":"Nirsevimab is a long-acting monoclonal antibody against the RSV F protein, giving immediate passive protection through the season. It is not a vaccine and does not stimulate active immunity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe929214b","lv":"flightrn","d":"arsenic-trioxide","sys":"onc","q":"Which monitoring is essential for a patient receiving arsenic trioxide for acute promyelocytic leukemia?","a":"ECG for QT prolongation, plus potassium and magnesium levels","w":["Pulmonary function tests for drug-induced lung scarring","Daily hearing tests for drug-induced hearing loss","Daily urinalysis for drug-induced hemorrhagic cystitis"],"x":"Arsenic trioxide prolongs the QT interval and can cause torsades, so ECGs and electrolytes are monitored. It also carries a boxed warning for differentiation syndrome.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp61050ff8","lv":"flightrn","d":"tretinoin","sys":"onc","q":"A patient on tretinoin for acute promyelocytic leukemia develops fever, dyspnea, weight gain and pulmonary infiltrates. What should be suspected?","a":"Differentiation syndrome","w":["Tumor lysis syndrome","Serum sickness","Hepatic encephalopathy"],"x":"Differentiation syndrome is a boxed warning for tretinoin, featuring fever, respiratory distress, weight gain, effusions and hypotension, and is treated with corticosteroids. Tumor lysis syndrome causes electrolyte disturbances rather than these findings.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3fbc68f3","lv":"flightrn","d":"axicabtagene-ciloleucel","sys":"onc","q":"A few days after CAR T-cell infusion with axicabtagene ciloleucel, a patient develops high fever, hypotension and hypoxia. What is the most likely cause?","a":"Cytokine release syndrome","w":["Tumor lysis syndrome","Graft-versus-host disease","Transfusion hemolysis"],"x":"Cytokine release syndrome is a boxed warning for CAR T-cell products and presents with fever, hypotension and hypoxia. It is treated with tocilizumab and, if needed, corticosteroids.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc28f5faf","lv":"flightrn","d":"axicabtagene-ciloleucel","sys":"onc","q":"After CAR T-cell therapy with axicabtagene ciloleucel, which finding suggests immune effector cell-associated neurotoxicity?","a":"New confusion, word-finding difficulty or tremor","w":["New rash on the palms and soles of the feet","New dry cough with clear lungs on exam","New joint pain in both knees and ankles"],"x":"Neurologic toxicity can include encephalopathy, aphasia, tremor and seizures, and is a boxed warning. Frequent neurologic checks, including handwriting and naming tasks, help detect it early.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe93077c0","lv":"flightrn","d":"blinatumomab","sys":"onc","q":"Which describes the drug class and mechanism of blinatumomab?","a":"A bispecific T-cell engager that binds CD19 and CD3","w":["A kinase inhibitor that blocks BCR-ABL signaling","An alkylating agent that cross-links tumor DNA","A monoclonal antibody that blocks PD-1 receptors"],"x":"Blinatumomab links T cells (CD3) to CD19-positive B cells, directing T cells to kill leukemic cells. This mechanism explains its risk of cytokine release syndrome and neurotoxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp60a8961c","lv":"flightrn","d":"ifosfamide","sys":"onc","q":"Why is mesna given along with ifosfamide?","a":"To prevent hemorrhagic cystitis","w":["To prevent cardiotoxicity","To prevent peripheral neuropathy","To prevent tumor lysis syndrome"],"x":"Ifosfamide's metabolite acrolein damages the bladder lining, and mesna binds and inactivates it in the urine. Ifosfamide can also cause encephalopathy, which is not prevented by mesna.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp03e2154b","lv":"flightrn","d":"methotrexate","sys":"onc","q":"Why is leucovorin given after high-dose methotrexate?","a":"It bypasses the blocked folate pathway to rescue normal cells","w":["It increases methotrexate uptake into the tumor cells","It binds methotrexate in the blood and removes it","It prevents allergic reactions to the methotrexate"],"x":"Methotrexate inhibits dihydrofolate reductase, and leucovorin is a reduced folate that does not need this enzyme, so it protects normal cells. Glucarpidase, not leucovorin, breaks down methotrexate in the blood.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp826fb0bd","lv":"flightrn","d":"ibrutinib","sys":"onc","q":"A trauma patient takes ibrutinib for chronic lymphocytic leukemia. Which concern is most relevant?","a":"Increased bleeding risk from impaired platelet function","w":["Increased clotting risk from activated platelets","Severe hypoglycemia from increased insulin release","Hyperkalemia from the drug's effect on kidneys"],"x":"Ibrutinib inhibits Bruton's tyrosine kinase and impairs platelet function, increasing bleeding, especially with anticoagulants or antiplatelets. It is also associated with atrial fibrillation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb07af632","lv":"flightrn","d":"vinblastine","sys":"onc","q":"By which route must vinblastine be given?","a":"Intravenous only","w":["Intrathecal injection","Intramuscular injection","Subcutaneous injection"],"x":"Vinca alkaloids are fatal if given intrathecally, and labeling requires warnings that the drug is for IV use only. It is also a vesicant, so extravasation must be avoided.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp98638428","lv":"flightrn","d":"venetoclax","sys":"onc","q":"Which lab pattern indicates tumor lysis syndrome in a patient starting venetoclax?","a":"High potassium, high phosphate, low calcium and high uric acid","w":["Low potassium, low phosphate, high calcium and low uric acid","High sodium, low potassium, high calcium and low uric acid","Low sodium, low phosphate, low calcium and low uric acid"],"x":"Rapid cell death releases potassium, phosphate and nucleic acids (which form uric acid), and phosphate binds calcium, lowering it. Venetoclax requires gradual dose increases and TLS monitoring for this reason.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2ab475a1","lv":"flightrn","d":"silver-sulfadiazine","sys":"skin","q":"For which burn patient is silver sulfadiazine contraindicated?","a":"A newborn infant in the first weeks of life","w":["A school-age child with a partial-thickness burn","An adult with a small partial-thickness burn","An older adult with a scald burn on the forearm"],"x":"Sulfonamides can displace bilirubin and cause kernicterus, so silver sulfadiazine is contraindicated in premature infants and newborns in the first months of life and in pregnancy near term. It is also avoided with sulfonamide allergy.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9fd33dd9","lv":"flightrn","d":"mupirocin-nasal","sys":"skin","q":"Why is intranasal mupirocin used for some patients before surgery or during an ICU stay?","a":"To decolonize the nares of Staphylococcus aureus, including MRSA","w":["To treat acute bacterial sinusitis caused by gram-negative organisms","To prevent nosebleeds by forming a protective barrier in the nose","To reduce nasal swelling before placement of a nasogastric tube"],"x":"Nasal mupirocin eliminates S. aureus carriage, lowering the risk of later infection. It is not a decongestant or a treatment for sinusitis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp31371cab","lv":"flightrn","d":"proparacaine","sys":"skin","q":"After proparacaine drops are used to examine an injured eye, which teaching point is most important?","a":"Avoid rubbing the eye until the numbness wears off","w":["Use the drops at home to control ongoing eye pain","Apply the drops hourly to speed corneal healing","Expect permanent loss of corneal sensation"],"x":"The anesthetized cornea cannot sense injury, so rubbing can cause further damage. Repeated use of topical anesthetics delays healing and can damage the cornea, so they are not used for ongoing home pain control.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2e3fad51","lv":"flightrn","d":"fluorescein","sys":"skin","q":"Why is fluorescein applied to the eye during evaluation of an eye injury?","a":"To reveal corneal abrasions under cobalt blue light","w":["To numb the cornea before the eye examination","To dilate the pupil and view the retina","To lower intraocular pressure before transport"],"x":"Fluorescein stains areas where the corneal epithelium is disrupted, which glow green under cobalt blue light. It has no anesthetic or pupil-dilating effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5e9ff064","lv":"flightrn","d":"acetazolamide","sys":"skin","q":"How does acetazolamide help prevent acute mountain sickness during ascent to high altitude?","a":"It causes a mild metabolic acidosis that stimulates breathing","w":["It directly increases red blood cell production","It dilates pulmonary arteries to improve oxygenation","It raises oxygen affinity of hemoglobin in the lungs"],"x":"By inhibiting carbonic anhydrase, acetazolamide increases bicarbonate excretion, and the resulting acidosis increases ventilation and speeds acclimatization. It does not directly stimulate red cell production.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp24d0bc83","lv":"flightrn","d":"acetazolamide","sys":"skin","q":"Which side effect is common with acetazolamide and is generally not dangerous?","a":"Tingling in the fingers, toes and around the mouth","w":["Sudden severe high blood pressure readings","Marked weight gain from fluid retention","Excess salivation and watery eyes"],"x":"Paresthesias, altered taste of carbonated drinks and increased urination are common with acetazolamide. It is a diuretic, so fluid retention is not expected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf1c5b71e","lv":"flightrn","d":"timolol-ophthalmic","sys":"skin","q":"Before starting timolol eye drops, which patient history is most important to identify?","a":"Asthma or severe COPD","w":["Seasonal allergic rhinitis","Iron deficiency anemia","Osteoarthritis of the knee"],"x":"Timolol is a nonselective beta blocker, and enough is absorbed systemically to cause bronchospasm, bradycardia and heart block. Asthma is a contraindication.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp737dccc7","lv":"flightrn","d":"lidocaine-prilocaine","sys":"skin","q":"Which serious adverse effect is associated with lidocaine-prilocaine cream, especially in infants or with large application areas?","a":"Methemoglobinemia","w":["Hypercalcemia","Hyperglycemia","Thrombocytosis"],"x":"Prilocaine metabolites oxidize hemoglobin to methemoglobin, reducing oxygen delivery and causing cyanosis that does not improve with oxygen. Infants and patients on other oxidizing drugs are at higher risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe9b82cc9","lv":"flightrn","d":"cyclopentolate","sys":"skin","q":"Which complication can cyclopentolate eye drops trigger in a patient with narrow anterior chamber angles?","a":"Acute angle-closure glaucoma","w":["Acute bacterial conjunctivitis","Retinal detachment in both eyes","Corneal abrasion from dry eye"],"x":"Cyclopentolate dilates the pupil, which can block aqueous outflow in narrow angles and sharply raise intraocular pressure. Eye pain, halos, headache and vomiting after dilation should be reported.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa286205d","lv":"flightrn","d":"silver-sulfadiazine","sys":"skin","q":"Which lab change is a known, usually transient effect of silver sulfadiazine on large burns?","a":"A drop in white blood cell count","w":["A rise in serum potassium level","A drop in serum sodium level","A rise in serum calcium level"],"x":"Transient leukopenia is reported with silver sulfadiazine, typically within days of starting, and usually recovers. The white blood cell count should be monitored and changes reported.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp62609671","lv":"im","d":"iopamidol","sys":"cardio","q":"Iopamidol is used for many CT studies. Which category of contrast agent is it?","a":"Nonionic low-osmolar iodinated contrast","w":["Ionic high-osmolar iodinated contrast","Macrocyclic gadolinium-based contrast","Barium sulfate oral suspension"],"x":"Iopamidol is a nonionic, low-osmolar iodinated agent, which causes fewer adverse reactions than older ionic high-osmolar agents. It contains iodine, not gadolinium, so it is not an MRI agent.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb0f6524e","lv":"im","d":"iodixanol","sys":"cardio","q":"What property sets iodixanol apart from most other iodinated contrast agents used in cardiac catheterization?","a":"It has the same osmolality as blood","w":["It contains gadolinium for MRI use","It is given only by the oral route","It is an ionic high-osmolar monomer"],"x":"Iodixanol is a nonionic dimer that is iso-osmolar to blood, which can reduce discomfort during intra-arterial injection. It is an iodinated agent given intravascularly, not a gadolinium or high-osmolar ionic agent.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp13be4230","lv":"im","d":"perflutren","sys":"cardio","q":"An echocardiography lab uses perflutren lipid microspheres to opacify the left ventricle. What must be readily available whenever it is given?","a":"Resuscitation equipment and trained personnel","w":["A radiation dosimeter for the sonographer","A lead apron for the patient","A gadolinium spill kit and gloves"],"x":"Perflutren carries a boxed warning for serious cardiopulmonary reactions, most occurring within about half an hour of injection, so resuscitation equipment and trained staff must be on hand. It is an ultrasound agent, so radiation shielding is not relevant.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3e0c728f","lv":"im","d":"sulfur-hexafluoride","sys":"cardio","q":"Besides IV use for echocardiography and liver imaging, sulfur hexafluoride lipid-type A microspheres are approved for which pediatric ultrasound exam?","a":"Intravesical use to evaluate vesicoureteral reflux","w":["Intrathecal use to evaluate spinal canal flow","Oral use to outline the small bowel lumen","Intra-articular use to evaluate the hip joint"],"x":"Sulfur hexafluoride microspheres can be instilled into the bladder through a catheter for voiding ultrasonography to detect vesicoureteral reflux in children. It is not approved for intrathecal, oral or intra-articular use.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp491c2941","lv":"im","d":"regadenoson","sys":"cardio","q":"Regadenoson is used for pharmacologic stress in myocardial perfusion imaging. How does it work?","a":"It stimulates adenosine A2A receptors to dilate coronary arteries","w":["It stimulates beta-1 receptors to raise heart rate and contractility","It blocks calcium channels to slow conduction through the AV node","It blocks adenosine receptors to prevent bronchospasm"],"x":"Regadenoson is a selective A2A adenosine receptor agonist that causes coronary vasodilation, revealing differences in perfusion. Raising heart rate and contractility through beta-1 receptors describes dobutamine stress.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp02996654","lv":"im","d":"adenosine","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient is scheduled for an adenosine nuclear stress test. Why are patients taught to avoid caffeine beforehand?","a":"Caffeine blocks adenosine receptors and can blunt the stress effect","w":["Caffeine binds the radiotracer and lowers image quality","Caffeine increases the risk of a contrast allergy","Caffeine speeds kidney clearance of the radiotracer"],"x":"Caffeine and other methylxanthines are adenosine receptor antagonists, so they can reduce the vasodilator response and cause a falsely normal study. Caffeine does not bind radiotracers or cause contrast allergy.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp73c0e787","lv":"im","d":"aminophylline","sys":"cardio","q":"During a regadenoson stress test, a patient develops persistent chest discomfort and shortness of breath. Which medicine may be given to reverse the adenosine-receptor effects?","a":"Aminophylline","w":["Atropine","Protamine","Flumazenil"],"x":"Aminophylline is a methylxanthine that blocks adenosine receptors and can reverse adverse effects of adenosine, regadenoson or dipyridamole. Atropine treats bradycardia from vagal tone, not adenosine-receptor stimulation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfbe4880b","lv":"im","d":"metoprolol","sys":"cardio","q":"Why may metoprolol be given before coronary CT angiography?","a":"To slow the heart rate and reduce motion artifact","w":["To dilate the coronary arteries for better filling","To protect the kidneys from contrast injury","To prevent an allergic reaction to contrast"],"x":"A slower, steadier heart rate gives a longer quiet phase in the cardiac cycle, improving coronary image quality. Coronary dilation before CT angiography is the job of nitroglycerin, not metoprolol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf9c9a346","lv":"im","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"cardio","q":"Sublingual nitroglycerin is often given before coronary CT angiography. Which recent medicine use must be reported to the provider before it is given?","a":"A phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor such as sildenafil","w":["A proton pump inhibitor such as omeprazole","A statin such as atorvastatin","An antihistamine such as cetirizine"],"x":"Nitrates combined with phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors such as sildenafil or tadalafil can cause severe, prolonged hypotension. Statins, proton pump inhibitors and antihistamines do not cause this dangerous interaction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe2d523e4","lv":"im","d":"tc99m-sestamibi","sys":"cardio","q":"On a myocardial perfusion scan with technetium Tc 99m sestamibi, what does an area of decreased tracer uptake in the heart muscle suggest?","a":"Reduced blood flow or scarred myocardium","w":["Increased blood flow from inflammation","Normal uptake in a healthy ventricle","Active bone formation in the sternum"],"x":"Sestamibi distributes to heart muscle in proportion to blood flow, so a defect suggests ischemia or infarction. A defect that appears only with stress suggests ischemia, while a fixed defect suggests scar.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa93f329d","lv":"im","d":"epinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"Minutes after IV contrast, a patient has diffuse hives, wheezing and a falling blood pressure. Which medicine is the first-line treatment?","a":"Epinephrine","w":["Diphenhydramine","Methylprednisolone","Famotidine"],"x":"This is anaphylaxis, and epinephrine is the first-line medicine because it reverses both bronchospasm and hypotension. Antihistamines and steroids are adjuncts that do not treat airway compromise or shock quickly enough.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp903bdd73","lv":"im","d":"epinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"When IV access is not available, which route and site are preferred for epinephrine in an adult with anaphylaxis?","a":"Intramuscular injection in the anterolateral thigh","w":["Subcutaneous injection in the upper abdomen","Intramuscular injection in the gluteal muscle","Intradermal injection in the inner forearm"],"x":"Intramuscular injection into the anterolateral thigh gives faster, more reliable absorption than subcutaneous injection. Gluteal and intradermal routes are not recommended for anaphylaxis treatment.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp91e197aa","lv":"im","d":"epinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"How does epinephrine help a patient in anaphylactic shock?","a":"It constricts blood vessels and relaxes airway smooth muscle","w":["It blocks histamine at H1 receptors in the skin","It suppresses the immune response over several hours","It slows the heart rate and lowers blood pressure"],"x":"Epinephrine stimulates alpha receptors to raise blood pressure and beta-2 receptors to open the airways. Blocking H1 receptors describes antihistamines, and slow immune suppression describes corticosteroids.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9ef5afd3","lv":"im","d":"dobutamine","sys":"shock","q":"Dobutamine is used in cardiogenic shock and for stress echocardiography. What is its main action?","a":"It stimulates beta-1 receptors to increase cardiac contractility","w":["It stimulates alpha-1 receptors to cause strong vasoconstriction","It blocks beta-1 receptors to slow the heart rate","It dilates coronary arteries through adenosine receptors"],"x":"Dobutamine is mainly a beta-1 agonist that increases contractility and heart rate. Strong alpha-1 vasoconstriction describes drugs like norepinephrine or phenylephrine, not dobutamine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp588426fb","lv":"im","d":"dobutamine","sys":"shock","q":"During a dobutamine infusion, which adverse effect should the imaging team watch for most closely on the monitor?","a":"Tachycardia and new arrhythmias","w":["Profound bradycardia and heart block","Severe hypertension with bradycardia","Prolonged apnea and low oxygen levels"],"x":"Because dobutamine stimulates the heart, it can cause excessive tachycardia, ectopy and arrhythmias. Bradycardia and heart block are not typical effects of a beta-1 agonist.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa658066a","lv":"im","d":"heparin","sys":"blood","q":"A patient on a continuous unfractionated heparin infusion is scheduled for an interventional procedure. Which lab test is commonly used to monitor this heparin?","a":"Activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT)","w":["International normalized ratio (INR)","Hemoglobin A1c","Serum lipase"],"x":"Unfractionated heparin is commonly monitored with the aPTT (or an anti-Xa level). The INR is used mainly to monitor warfarin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp10937e3c","lv":"im","d":"heparin","sys":"blood","q":"Several days into heparin therapy, a patient's platelet count drops sharply and a new clot is found. Which complication should be recognized?","a":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","w":["Iron deficiency anemia","Contrast-induced nephropathy","Vitamin K deficiency bleeding"],"x":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia is an immune reaction that lowers platelets and paradoxically increases clotting risk. It must be reported promptly to the provider.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp99cf7902","lv":"im","d":"protamine","sys":"blood","q":"After an interventional procedure, the provider wants to reverse the effect of unfractionated heparin. Which medicine is used?","a":"Protamine sulfate","w":["Vitamin K","Flumazenil","Naloxone"],"x":"Protamine binds heparin and neutralizes its anticoagulant effect. Vitamin K reverses warfarin, not heparin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2de71db6","lv":"im","d":"protamine","sys":"blood","q":"Which history increases a patient's risk of a serious reaction to protamine?","a":"Prior use of NPH insulin, which contains protamine","w":["Prior use of metformin for type 2 diabetes","Prior reaction to a gadolinium contrast agent","Prior treatment with an inhaled bronchodilator"],"x":"Earlier exposure to protamine, including protamine-containing insulin such as NPH, can sensitize patients to severe hypotension or anaphylaxis. Fish allergy and vasectomy are other listed risk factors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp113365ac","lv":"im","d":"alteplase","sys":"blood","q":"Alteplase is used in interventional radiology for catheter-directed thrombolysis. How does it work?","a":"It converts plasminogen to plasmin, which breaks down fibrin","w":["It inhibits thrombin directly to prevent new clots","It blocks vitamin K-dependent clotting factors","It prevents platelets from sticking together"],"x":"Alteplase is a tissue plasminogen activator, so it dissolves existing clot by activating plasmin. Blocking vitamin K-dependent factors describes warfarin, which prevents clots but does not dissolve them.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb71c4338","lv":"im","d":"alteplase","sys":"blood","q":"During an alteplase infusion through an interventional catheter, which finding should be reported to the provider right away?","a":"Oozing at the puncture site or new blood in the urine","w":["Mild warmth at the catheter insertion site","A slight decrease in anxiety after reassurance","A heart rate that stays within the normal range"],"x":"Bleeding is the major risk with thrombolytics, so new oozing, hematuria or other bleeding signs must be reported promptly. Normal vital signs and reduced anxiety are reassuring findings.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9b407468","lv":"im","d":"ferumoxytol","sys":"blood","q":"A patient received IV ferumoxytol for iron deficiency anemia last month and is now scheduled for an MRI. Why should the radiologist be told?","a":"Ferumoxytol can alter MRI signal for months after infusion","w":["Ferumoxytol makes iodinated contrast reactions more likely","Ferumoxytol causes nephrogenic systemic fibrosis","Ferumoxytol must be reversed before any MRI is done"],"x":"Ferumoxytol is a superparamagnetic iron oxide that can change MRI signal intensity for up to several months, which can affect interpretation. It does not contain gadolinium and is not linked to nephrogenic systemic fibrosis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd7752f68","lv":"im","d":"ferumoxytol","sys":"blood","q":"Ferumoxytol carries a boxed warning. What should be monitored during and after the infusion?","a":"Signs of hypersensitivity, including low blood pressure","w":["Signs of hypoglycemia, including sweating","Signs of hyperkalemia, including peaked T waves","Signs of thyroid storm, including fever"],"x":"Ferumoxytol can cause fatal hypersensitivity reactions, so blood pressure, pulse and signs of a reaction are monitored during the infusion and for a period afterward. Hypoglycemia and hyperkalemia are not its key risks.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp17f20368","lv":"im","d":"dipyridamole","sys":"blood","q":"Dipyridamole is used as a pharmacologic stress agent in nuclear cardiology. How does it produce coronary vasodilation?","a":"It blocks cellular reuptake of adenosine, raising adenosine levels","w":["It directly stimulates beta-1 receptors in the heart muscle","It blocks calcium entry into vascular smooth muscle cells","It releases nitric oxide from the vessel endothelium"],"x":"Dipyridamole inhibits adenosine reuptake and breakdown, so the body's own adenosine increases and dilates coronary arteries. Beta-1 stimulation describes dobutamine, not dipyridamole.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdd455be4","lv":"im","d":"dipyridamole","sys":"blood","q":"A patient taking oral dipyridamole for stroke prevention is scheduled for an adenosine stress test. Which interaction should be recognized?","a":"Dipyridamole can enhance and prolong the effects of adenosine","w":["Dipyridamole blocks adenosine and causes a false-normal test","Dipyridamole binds the radiotracer and blocks heart uptake","Dipyridamole has no known interaction with adenosine"],"x":"Because dipyridamole raises adenosine levels, it can make adenosine effects stronger and longer, so this must be reported to the provider. Blocking adenosine receptors is the effect of caffeine and other methylxanthines.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc1081829","lv":"im","d":"atropine","sys":"airway","q":"After contrast injection, a patient becomes pale and sweaty with hypotension and a slow heart rate. Leg elevation and IV fluids have been started. Which medicine may be given if bradycardia persists?","a":"Atropine","w":["Epinephrine","Diphenhydramine","Labetalol"],"x":"Hypotension with bradycardia suggests a vasovagal reaction, which is treated with leg elevation, fluids and atropine if needed. Epinephrine is first-line when hypotension is accompanied by tachycardia from anaphylaxis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp88e9c2a7","lv":"im","d":"atropine","sys":"airway","q":"How does atropine increase the heart rate?","a":"It blocks vagal (muscarinic) effects on the heart","w":["It stimulates beta-1 receptors directly","It blocks calcium channels in the AV node","It releases stored norepinephrine from nerves"],"x":"Atropine is an anticholinergic that blocks muscarinic receptors, removing the slowing effect of the vagus nerve. It does not directly stimulate beta receptors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbd458f75","lv":"im","d":"atropine","sys":"airway","q":"Besides treating bradycardia, why is atropine sometimes given before airway procedures?","a":"To reduce oral and airway secretions","w":["To relax the vocal cords for intubation","To produce amnesia during the procedure","To numb the throat and suppress the gag"],"x":"Atropine's anticholinergic effect dries salivary and airway secretions. Amnesia comes from benzodiazepines, and numbing the throat comes from topical local anesthetics like lidocaine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp31ddeab0","lv":"im","d":"fentanyl","sys":"airway","q":"Rapid IV push of fentanyl can cause a rare but serious airway problem. Which one?","a":"Chest wall rigidity that makes ventilation difficult","w":["Laryngeal edema from histamine release","Excess airway secretions and drooling","Bronchospasm from beta-receptor blockade"],"x":"Rapid administration of potent opioids like fentanyl can cause rigid chest wall muscles, making it hard to ventilate the patient. Fentanyl causes little histamine release compared with morphine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp91ba8085","lv":"im","d":"fentanyl","sys":"airway","q":"During moderate sedation with fentanyl, which capnography finding is an early sign of hypoventilation?","a":"Rising end-tidal carbon dioxide","w":["Falling end-tidal carbon dioxide","Rising oxygen saturation","Rising heart rate variability"],"x":"Opioid respiratory depression reduces ventilation, so exhaled carbon dioxide builds up and end-tidal CO2 rises, often before oxygen saturation falls. Oxygen saturation can stay normal for a while, especially with supplemental oxygen.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6bfdc2c5","lv":"im","d":"lidocaine","sys":"airway","q":"A patient's throat was numbed with topical lidocaine before a swallowing or endoscopic procedure. What is the main safety concern afterward?","a":"Aspiration until the gag reflex returns","w":["Hyperglycemia until the lidocaine wears off","Hypertension until the throat sensation returns","Bleeding until the mucosa heals completely"],"x":"Topical lidocaine suppresses the gag and cough reflexes, so patients are at risk for aspiration until sensation returns. It does not cause hyperglycemia or bleeding.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb4277b8a","lv":"im","d":"lidocaine","sys":"airway","q":"Which group of symptoms suggests early local anesthetic systemic toxicity after lidocaine?","a":"Numbness around the mouth, metallic taste and ringing in the ears","w":["Hives, itching and flushing of the chest and neck","Pinpoint pupils, slow breathing and deep sleep","Dry mouth, flushed skin and urinary retention"],"x":"Early local anesthetic toxicity often shows as circumoral numbness, metallic taste, tinnitus and lightheadedness, which can progress to seizures and arrhythmias. Hives and itching suggest an allergic reaction instead.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd60c226f","lv":"im","d":"midazolam","sys":"airway","q":"Midazolam carries a boxed warning about combining it with which type of medicine?","a":"Opioids, because of profound respiratory depression","w":["Antibiotics, because of reduced antibiotic effect","Iodinated contrast, because of kidney injury","Antacids, because of reduced sedative absorption"],"x":"Benzodiazepines plus opioids can cause severe respiratory depression, sedation, coma and death. That is why patients receiving both need continuous monitoring of breathing and oxygenation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp85bc35b1","lv":"im","d":"midazolam","sys":"airway","q":"Which patient history raises the risk of airway obstruction during midazolam sedation in the imaging suite?","a":"Obstructive sleep apnea","w":["Seasonal allergic rhinitis","Well-controlled hypothyroidism","Prior appendectomy"],"x":"Sedatives relax upper airway muscles, so patients with obstructive sleep apnea are more likely to obstruct and desaturate. The other histories do not meaningfully increase airway risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4a18170f","lv":"im","d":"midazolam","sys":"airway","q":"How is IV midazolam usually given for moderate sedation?","a":"In small increments, allowing time to assess the effect","w":["As one large rapid bolus at the start","As an undiluted push into an artery","Only after the procedure has finished"],"x":"Titrating midazolam slowly in small increments, with time to see the peak effect, lowers the risk of oversedation and respiratory depression. Rapid large boluses increase that risk, and intra-arterial injection is dangerous.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7d9b6dc3","lv":"im","d":"albuterol","sys":"resp","q":"After contrast injection, a patient develops mild wheezing with stable blood pressure and oxygen saturation. Which medicine is commonly given first by inhaler?","a":"Albuterol","w":["Prednisone","Famotidine","Cetirizine"],"x":"Mild bronchospasm is commonly treated with oxygen and an inhaled short-acting beta-2 agonist such as albuterol. Oral steroids act too slowly to relieve acute wheezing.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6054f764","lv":"im","d":"albuterol","sys":"resp","q":"Which side effects are common after an albuterol inhaler is used for bronchospasm?","a":"Tremor and a faster heart rate","w":["Drowsiness and slow breathing","Constipation and urinary retention","Bradycardia and low blood pressure"],"x":"Albuterol stimulates beta receptors, which commonly causes tremor, nervousness and tachycardia. Drowsiness and slow breathing are typical of sedatives and opioids, not albuterol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp27bfd683","lv":"im","d":"epinephrine","sys":"resp","q":"After IV contrast, a patient reports throat tightness and has a hoarse voice and stridor. Which medicine is the priority?","a":"Epinephrine","w":["Diphenhydramine","Albuterol","Hydrocortisone"],"x":"Hoarseness and stridor suggest laryngeal edema, a potentially life-threatening reaction treated first with epinephrine. Albuterol opens lower airways but does not reduce upper airway swelling.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5bf9adad","lv":"im","d":"epinephrine","sys":"resp","q":"A patient taking a beta-blocker has a severe contrast reaction with bronchospasm. Why might the reaction be harder to treat?","a":"Beta-blockade can blunt the response to epinephrine","w":["Beta-blockers bind the contrast and prolong its effect","Beta-blockers make epinephrine more likely to cause seizures","Beta-blockers speed the breakdown of all rescue medicines"],"x":"Beta-blockers occupy the receptors that epinephrine and albuterol need, so bronchospasm and hypotension can be resistant. Glucagon may be considered in this situation because it acts independently of beta receptors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp69283422","lv":"im","d":"methylprednisolone","sys":"resp","q":"A patient with a prior moderate reaction to iodinated contrast needs another contrast CT. Why is methylprednisolone part of an elective premedication regimen?","a":"It reduces the chance of a repeat allergic-like reaction","w":["It treats an anaphylactic reaction within minutes","It protects the kidneys from contrast injury","It increases the brightness of the contrast"],"x":"Corticosteroid premedication lowers, but does not eliminate, the risk of a repeat allergic-like contrast reaction. It does not protect the kidneys or change image quality.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1b174e5d","lv":"im","d":"methylprednisolone","sys":"resp","q":"Why is IV methylprednisolone not relied on as the first treatment for acute bronchospasm during a contrast reaction?","a":"Its anti-inflammatory effect takes hours to develop","w":["It cannot be given by the intravenous route","It causes immediate bronchospasm as a side effect","It is reversed by any iodinated contrast agent"],"x":"Corticosteroids work by changing gene expression, which takes hours, so they do not relieve acute airway symptoms. Fast-acting bronchodilators and epinephrine are used first.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp269a1854","lv":"im","d":"prednisone","sys":"resp","q":"In a common elective oral premedication regimen for contrast allergy, prednisone is combined with which medicine shortly before the scan?","a":"Diphenhydramine","w":["Furosemide","Ondansetron","Metoprolol"],"x":"Standard oral premedication uses a corticosteroid such as prednisone given in several doses beforehand, plus diphenhydramine about an hour before contrast. Furosemide and metoprolol have no role in preventing allergic-like reactions.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp09f4dccc","lv":"im","d":"prednisone","sys":"resp","q":"A patient completed oral prednisone premedication before a contrast CT. Which statement about the patient's reaction risk is accurate?","a":"A breakthrough reaction can still occur, so monitoring continues","w":["Premedication fully prevents any reaction from occurring","Premedication means no rescue medicines need to be ready","Premedication eliminates the need for patient observation"],"x":"Premedication reduces but does not eliminate risk, and breakthrough reactions do happen, often similar in severity to the prior reaction. Staff must still monitor the patient and have treatment ready.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4fbbcbbd","lv":"im","d":"aminophylline","sys":"resp","q":"Aminophylline is a methylxanthine bronchodilator with a narrow therapeutic range. Which signs suggest toxicity?","a":"Persistent vomiting, arrhythmias and seizures","w":["Pinpoint pupils and slow breathing","Hives, itching and flushing","Muscle weakness and slow heart rate"],"x":"Theophylline-type toxicity causes nausea and vomiting, tachyarrhythmias and seizures. Pinpoint pupils and slow breathing suggest opioid toxicity instead.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6ad32f16","lv":"im","d":"potassium-iodide","sys":"resp","q":"Why may potassium iodide be given before imaging with a radioiodine-labeled agent such as iobenguane I 123?","a":"To block thyroid uptake of free radioactive iodine","w":["To increase tumor uptake of the radiotracer","To protect the kidneys from the radiotracer","To prevent an allergic reaction to the tracer"],"x":"Stable iodine fills the thyroid so it takes up less of any free radioiodine released from the tracer, lowering thyroid radiation dose. It does not enhance tumor uptake or protect the kidneys.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp25ed293c","lv":"im","d":"gadobutrol","sys":"neuro","q":"Gadobutrol is used for brain MRI. How is it classified?","a":"A macrocyclic gadolinium-based contrast agent","w":["A linear iodinated contrast agent","A superparamagnetic iron oxide agent","A technetium-labeled radiopharmaceutical"],"x":"Gadobutrol is a macrocyclic gadolinium-based agent, a structure that holds gadolinium tightly. Macrocyclic agents are associated with less gadolinium retention than linear agents.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd0eac64c","lv":"im","d":"gadobutrol","sys":"neuro","q":"Before giving gadobutrol, which patient condition most raises concern for nephrogenic systemic fibrosis?","a":"Severe chronic kidney disease or acute kidney injury","w":["Mild seasonal allergies to pollen","Well-controlled high blood pressure","A previous reaction to iodinated contrast"],"x":"Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis is linked to gadolinium exposure in patients with severely reduced kidney function, including those on dialysis or with acute kidney injury. A prior iodinated contrast reaction is a separate allergic-like risk, not an NSF risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf4031b33","lv":"im","d":"gadoterate","sys":"neuro","q":"Which statement about gadolinium retention after agents such as gadoterate meglumine is accurate?","a":"Small amounts can remain in the brain and bone for months to years","w":["All gadolinium is cleared within one hour of injection","Retention occurs only in patients with liver disease","Retained gadolinium has proven to cause dementia"],"x":"FDA warns that gadolinium can be retained in the brain, bone and skin for months to years, more with linear than macrocyclic agents. No harmful effects of brain retention have been proven in patients with normal kidney function.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp74a16c36","lv":"im","d":"gadoterate","sys":"neuro","q":"How is gadoterate meglumine mainly eliminated from the body?","a":"Through the kidneys into the urine","w":["Through the liver into the bile","Through the lungs in exhaled air","Through the skin in sweat"],"x":"Gadoterate is cleared mainly unchanged by glomerular filtration, which is why kidney function matters for gadolinium agents. Significant biliary excretion is a feature of certain other agents, such as gadoxetate.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbdb09b15","lv":"im","d":"gadobenate","sys":"neuro","q":"What elimination feature makes gadobenate dimeglumine different from many other gadolinium agents?","a":"A small portion is excreted through the liver into bile","w":["It is removed entirely by exhalation through the lungs","It is eliminated only by hemodialysis","It is broken down completely in skeletal muscle"],"x":"Gadobenate is cleared mostly by the kidneys but also has a small hepatobiliary component. It is not exhaled or metabolized in muscle.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd063b000","lv":"im","d":"gadopiclenol","sys":"neuro","q":"What is a distinctive feature of gadopiclenol compared with other gadolinium-based contrast agents?","a":"High relaxivity that allows a lower gadolinium dose","w":["It contains iodine for use in CT scanning","It is given orally to outline the bowel","It is a linear agent with the lowest stability"],"x":"Gadopiclenol is a high-relaxivity macrocyclic agent, so it produces good enhancement with about half the gadolinium of standard agents. It is not iodinated and is not a linear agent.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4c9a6c4a","lv":"im","d":"alteplase","sys":"neuro","q":"A patient arrives with acute stroke symptoms and may receive alteplase. Which imaging study is typically done first?","a":"Noncontrast head CT to rule out hemorrhage","w":["Contrast MRI of the lumbar spine","Nuclear bone scan of the skull","Ultrasound of the abdominal aorta"],"x":"Alteplase is contraindicated in intracranial hemorrhage, so a rapid noncontrast head CT is done to exclude bleeding before thrombolysis. The other studies do not evaluate stroke.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcdbc61bd","lv":"im","d":"alteplase","sys":"neuro","q":"During or shortly after alteplase for stroke, a patient develops a sudden severe headache, vomiting and worsening weakness. What should be suspected?","a":"Intracranial hemorrhage","w":["Contrast-induced nephropathy","A mild vasovagal reaction","Expected recovery from the stroke"],"x":"New headache, vomiting or neurologic decline after a thrombolytic suggests bleeding in the brain and must be reported to the provider immediately, usually with urgent head CT. These are not signs of recovery or a vasovagal event.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp157b290b","lv":"im","d":"labetalol","sys":"neuro","q":"Labetalol may be used to lower blood pressure in acute stroke before thrombolysis. Which class does it belong to?","a":"Combined alpha- and beta-adrenergic blocker","w":["Calcium channel blocker","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor","Loop diuretic"],"x":"Labetalol blocks alpha-1 and beta receptors, lowering blood pressure without a reflex rise in heart rate. Nicardipine is the calcium channel blocker often used in the same setting.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcaf8c65e","lv":"im","d":"midazolam","sys":"neuro","q":"A patient has a prolonged seizure in the imaging department and no IV access. Which route allows midazolam to be given quickly?","a":"Intramuscular injection","w":["Intrathecal injection","Topical skin application","Intra-arterial injection"],"x":"Midazolam is well absorbed after intramuscular injection and is approved by this route for status epilepticus, so treatment need not wait for IV access. Intrathecal and intra-arterial routes are not used.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfbaca79b","lv":"im","d":"flumazenil","sys":"pain","q":"A patient is oversedated with slow, shallow breathing after midazolam. Which medicine reverses the benzodiazepine effect?","a":"Flumazenil","w":["Naloxone","Protamine","Atropine"],"x":"Flumazenil is a benzodiazepine receptor antagonist that reverses midazolam sedation. Naloxone reverses opioids, not benzodiazepines.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9a16bc4b","lv":"im","d":"flumazenil","sys":"pain","q":"In which patient does flumazenil carry an increased risk of seizures?","a":"A patient with long-term benzodiazepine dependence","w":["A patient with a single prior dental anesthesia","A patient with mild seasonal allergies","A patient with well-controlled hypertension"],"x":"Flumazenil can precipitate withdrawal seizures in patients dependent on benzodiazepines or who have a seizure disorder controlled by them. This is a boxed warning on its label.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp168b917e","lv":"im","d":"naloxone","sys":"pain","q":"After fentanyl sedation, a patient responds to naloxone. Why must monitoring continue afterward?","a":"Naloxone can wear off before the opioid, causing resedation","w":["Naloxone causes delayed bleeding hours after it is given","Naloxone raises blood glucose to dangerous levels","Naloxone builds up and causes kidney failure"],"x":"Naloxone's effect can be shorter than that of the opioid, so breathing can slow again after it wears off. Bleeding and hyperglycemia are not effects of naloxone.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9964da27","lv":"im","d":"naloxone","sys":"pain","q":"Which effect may occur when naloxone is given to a patient who takes opioids long term?","a":"Sudden withdrawal with pain, agitation and vomiting","w":["Deep sedation with very slow breathing","Severe bradycardia with heart block","Rapid onset of hives and wheezing"],"x":"Naloxone abruptly blocks opioid receptors, which can trigger acute withdrawal and the return of pain in opioid-dependent patients. Deep sedation and slow breathing are opioid effects, not naloxone effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpeeaac85b","lv":"im","d":"lidocaine","sys":"pain","q":"Lidocaine is injected for local anesthesia before a biopsy. How does it block pain?","a":"It blocks sodium channels in nerve membranes","w":["It stimulates opioid receptors in the brain","It blocks prostaglandin production in tissue","It enhances GABA activity in the spinal cord"],"x":"Lidocaine is an amide local anesthetic that blocks sodium channels, stopping nerve impulse conduction. Opioid receptor stimulation describes medicines like fentanyl.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4ce57791","lv":"im","d":"bupivacaine","sys":"pain","q":"Compared with lidocaine, what is a key feature of bupivacaine as a local anesthetic?","a":"Slower onset but a longer duration of action","w":["Faster onset and a much shorter duration","Useful only on the surface of the skin","No risk of toxicity if absorbed systemically"],"x":"Bupivacaine takes longer to start working than lidocaine but provides longer-lasting anesthesia. It also carries a higher risk of cardiac toxicity if it reaches the bloodstream in excess.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8e835a78","lv":"im","d":"bupivacaine","sys":"pain","q":"A patient develops seizures and an arrhythmia shortly after a local injection of bupivacaine. Which specific treatment may be used for local anesthetic systemic toxicity?","a":"IV lipid emulsion","w":["IV naloxone","IV flumazenil","IV protamine"],"x":"Lipid emulsion therapy is used for local anesthetic systemic toxicity, along with airway support and seizure control. Naloxone and flumazenil reverse opioids and benzodiazepines, not local anesthetics.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp53e0e075","lv":"im","d":"dexmedetomidine","sys":"pain","q":"Dexmedetomidine is sometimes used for procedural sedation. What is its mechanism?","a":"It stimulates central alpha-2 adrenergic receptors","w":["It enhances GABA effects at benzodiazepine sites","It stimulates mu opioid receptors in the brain","It blocks NMDA receptors in the spinal cord"],"x":"Dexmedetomidine is a selective alpha-2 agonist that reduces sympathetic activity and produces sedation. Enhancing GABA at benzodiazepine sites describes midazolam.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4d0890b8","lv":"im","d":"fentanyl","sys":"pain","q":"Why is fentanyl often chosen as the opioid for moderate sedation in interventional radiology?","a":"It has a rapid onset and short duration after IV use","w":["It has no risk of respiratory depression","It can be reversed by flumazenil","It provides long-lasting local numbness"],"x":"IV fentanyl acts within minutes and wears off fairly quickly, which suits short procedures. It can still cause respiratory depression and is reversed by naloxone, not flumazenil.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfa5a5814","lv":"im","d":"midazolam","sys":"pain","q":"After midazolam sedation, a patient may not remember the discharge teaching. Which effect explains this?","a":"Anterograde amnesia","w":["Retrograde hypnosis","Peripheral neuropathy","Delayed hypoglycemia"],"x":"Midazolam commonly causes anterograde amnesia, so patients may not recall events or instructions after it is given. Written instructions and a responsible adult escort help with safe discharge.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6549010b","lv":"im","d":"midazolam","sys":"psych","q":"Midazolam may be ordered for a patient with claustrophobia before MRI. How does it reduce anxiety?","a":"It enhances the calming effect of GABA in the brain","w":["It blocks dopamine receptors in the brain","It stimulates alpha-2 receptors in the brainstem","It blocks histamine receptors in the brain"],"x":"Midazolam is a benzodiazepine that increases GABA activity, producing anxiolysis and sedation. Alpha-2 stimulation describes dexmedetomidine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp94e0158f","lv":"im","d":"midazolam","sys":"psych","q":"After IV midazolam, an older adult becomes agitated, restless and combative instead of calm. What should be recognized?","a":"A paradoxical reaction to the benzodiazepine","w":["An expected sign of adequate sedation","An allergic reaction to the contrast agent","Opioid withdrawal from the sedation"],"x":"Benzodiazepines can cause paradoxical agitation and combativeness, more often in older adults and children. This should be reported to the provider rather than treated as adequate sedation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp154bc362","lv":"im","d":"midazolam","sys":"psych","q":"How is midazolam commonly given to a young child for anxiety before an imaging procedure when no IV is in place?","a":"By mouth as a liquid","w":["As a skin patch","As a rectal enema of contrast","By intrathecal injection"],"x":"Oral midazolam syrup is commonly used for preprocedure anxiolysis in children. Skin patches and intrathecal routes are not used for midazolam.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpca0de774","lv":"im","d":"midazolam","sys":"psych","q":"Why are older adults given extra caution with midazolam sedation?","a":"They are more sensitive to oversedation and respiratory depression","w":["They clear midazolam much faster than younger adults","They cannot receive midazolam by the IV route","They are immune to the amnesic effects of midazolam"],"x":"Older adults often have slower drug clearance and greater sensitivity, increasing the risk of oversedation and breathing problems. They do not clear midazolam faster.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp708b248a","lv":"im","d":"midazolam","sys":"psych","q":"A patient taking a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor, such as ketoconazole, needs sedation with midazolam. Which interaction should be recognized?","a":"Midazolam levels and sedation may be increased","w":["Midazolam will have little or no effect at all","Midazolam will cause severe hypertension","Midazolam will turn the urine dark red"],"x":"Midazolam is broken down by CYP3A4, so strong inhibitors raise its levels and can deepen and prolong sedation. The provider should be aware of these medicines before sedation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp771e781f","lv":"im","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"psych","q":"An older adult becomes confused and disoriented after IV diphenhydramine for a contrast reaction. What explains this?","a":"Its anticholinergic and sedating effects on the brain","w":["Its stimulation of beta receptors in the brain","Its blockade of serotonin reuptake in the brain","Its direct toxic effect on the kidneys"],"x":"Diphenhydramine is a first-generation antihistamine with strong anticholinergic effects that can cause confusion or delirium, especially in older adults. It does not affect serotonin reuptake.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpec544780","lv":"im","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"psych","q":"Instead of drowsiness, which unexpected effect can diphenhydramine cause in young children?","a":"Excitement and restlessness","w":["Severe hypoglycemia","Pinpoint pupils","Slow heart rate"],"x":"Antihistamines such as diphenhydramine can cause paradoxical excitation in young children. Pinpoint pupils are typical of opioids, not antihistamines.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5b4f7b84","lv":"im","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"psych","q":"An outpatient received IV diphenhydramine as part of contrast premedication. What teaching point applies?","a":"Do not drive because drowsiness and slowed reactions are likely","w":["Expect increased alertness for several hours afterward","Drink coffee to fully reverse the antihistamine effect","Expect the medicine to turn the urine bright green"],"x":"Diphenhydramine causes significant drowsiness and impaired coordination, so patients need a ride home after IV doses. Caffeine does not reliably reverse its effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfc53186f","lv":"im","d":"dexmedetomidine","sys":"psych","q":"Why may dexmedetomidine be chosen to sedate an anxious child for MRI?","a":"It sedates with relatively little respiratory depression","w":["It produces complete paralysis for motion control","It provides deep general anesthesia by itself","It reverses any prior benzodiazepine effect"],"x":"Dexmedetomidine produces a calm, arousable sedation while largely preserving the drive to breathe. It is not a paralytic and does not reverse benzodiazepines.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp13d4bd5d","lv":"im","d":"dexmedetomidine","sys":"psych","q":"Which adverse effects should be monitored during dexmedetomidine sedation?","a":"Bradycardia and hypotension","w":["Tachycardia and severe hypertension","Hyperthermia and muscle rigidity","Hyperglycemia and excessive thirst"],"x":"By reducing sympathetic activity, dexmedetomidine commonly lowers heart rate and blood pressure. A brief rise in blood pressure can occur with rapid loading, but bradycardia and hypotension are the main concerns.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0f92ff32","lv":"im","d":"furosemide","sys":"renal","q":"Furosemide may be given during a technetium Tc 99m mertiatide (MAG3) renal scan. What is its purpose?","a":"To help tell true obstruction from a dilated but unobstructed system","w":["To increase tracer uptake in the bones","To prevent an allergic reaction to the tracer","To slow urine flow so the kidney fills completely"],"x":"In diuretic renography, furosemide increases urine flow; a dilated system that drains well is not obstructed, while poor washout suggests obstruction. It increases, not slows, urine flow.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp25d96a54","lv":"im","d":"furosemide","sys":"renal","q":"Where in the kidney does furosemide act?","a":"The thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle","w":["The proximal convoluted tubule only","The glomerular capillary membrane","The collecting duct aldosterone receptors"],"x":"Furosemide is a loop diuretic that blocks sodium-potassium-chloride transport in the thick ascending limb. Blocking aldosterone receptors in the collecting duct describes spironolactone.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpaa22287a","lv":"im","d":"furosemide","sys":"renal","q":"Which lab result should be watched in a patient receiving furosemide?","a":"Low serum potassium","w":["High serum potassium","Low serum glucose","High platelet count"],"x":"Loop diuretics increase potassium loss in the urine, which can cause hypokalemia and arrhythmias. High potassium is more typical of potassium-sparing diuretics.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcc19613c","lv":"im","d":"furosemide","sys":"renal","q":"Which adverse effect is associated with rapid IV administration of furosemide, especially in patients with kidney impairment?","a":"Hearing loss or ringing in the ears","w":["Discoloration of the teeth","Pinpoint pupils and sedation","Excessive salivation and tearing"],"x":"Furosemide can cause ototoxicity, with tinnitus or hearing loss, particularly with rapid injection, high doses or kidney impairment. It is not associated with sedation or tooth discoloration.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp11a871a1","lv":"im","d":"furosemide","sys":"renal","q":"Furosemide is contraindicated in which patient?","a":"A patient with anuria who makes no urine","w":["A patient with mild ankle swelling","A patient with congestive heart failure","A patient with high blood pressure"],"x":"Furosemide requires functioning nephrons to work and is contraindicated in anuria. Edema, heart failure and hypertension are conditions it is used to treat.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp45a51376","lv":"im","d":"atropine","sys":"tox","q":"In organophosphate pesticide poisoning, atropine is given until which clinical endpoint is reached?","a":"Drying of bronchial secretions","w":["Constriction of the pupils","Return of normal bowel sounds","Onset of deep sedation"],"x":"Atropine blocks excess muscarinic stimulation, and the key endpoint is clearing of airway secretions so breathing improves. Pupil size is not a reliable endpoint.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3edbb742","lv":"im","d":"epinephrine","sys":"tox","q":"A patient with heart disease develops anaphylaxis after contrast. Which statement about epinephrine is accurate?","a":"There is no absolute contraindication to epinephrine in anaphylaxis","w":["Heart disease is an absolute contraindication to epinephrine","Epinephrine should be replaced by diphenhydramine in heart disease","Epinephrine is safe only for patients younger than middle age"],"x":"Anaphylaxis is life-threatening, and the benefit of epinephrine outweighs its risks even in patients with cardiovascular disease. Antihistamines cannot replace epinephrine for airway compromise or shock.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdf7e245d","lv":"im","d":"flumazenil","sys":"tox","q":"A sedated patient received both fentanyl and midazolam and is breathing slowly. Which statement about flumazenil is accurate?","a":"It reverses only the benzodiazepine, not the opioid","w":["It reverses both the opioid and the benzodiazepine","It reverses only the opioid, not the benzodiazepine","It reverses neither medicine and is a stimulant"],"x":"Flumazenil blocks benzodiazepine receptors and has no effect on opioid respiratory depression, which requires naloxone. Both may be needed when both drug types were given.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6c779aae","lv":"im","d":"glucagon","sys":"tox","q":"A patient on a beta-blocker has a severe contrast reaction that is not responding to epinephrine. Which medicine may be added?","a":"Glucagon","w":["Metoprolol","Flumazenil","Protamine"],"x":"Glucagon raises heart rate and contractility through a pathway that bypasses beta receptors, so it can help when beta-blockade blunts epinephrine. Metoprolol is a beta-blocker and would worsen the problem.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf82fe8fb","lv":"im","d":"glucagon","sys":"tox","q":"After IV glucagon is given, which common side effect should staff be prepared for?","a":"Nausea and vomiting","w":["Severe diarrhea and cramping","Pinpoint pupils","Profound bradycardia"],"x":"Nausea and vomiting are common after glucagon, so patients should be positioned to protect the airway. Glucagon tends to increase, not decrease, heart rate.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp39ed0cce","lv":"im","d":"naloxone","sys":"tox","q":"Which group of findings suggests opioid toxicity that may respond to naloxone?","a":"Pinpoint pupils, slow breathing and decreased responsiveness","w":["Dilated pupils, fast heart rate and hot dry skin","Hives, wheezing and swelling of the lips","Muscle twitching, sweating and drooling"],"x":"Miosis, respiratory depression and decreased level of consciousness are the classic opioid toxidrome. Dilated pupils with hot dry skin suggests anticholinergic toxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6a2e7395","lv":"im","d":"naloxone","sys":"tox","q":"By which routes can naloxone be given when opioid overdose is suspected?","a":"Intravenous, intramuscular or intranasal","w":["Oral tablet only","Topical skin cream only","Inhaled through a nebulizer only"],"x":"Naloxone works quickly by the IV, IM and intranasal routes, so it can be given even without IV access. Oral naloxone is poorly absorbed and not used for overdose.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp755a393b","lv":"im","d":"potassium-iodide","sys":"tox","q":"Potassium iodide may be recommended after a radiation emergency involving radioactive iodine. What does it protect?","a":"Only the thyroid gland from radioactive iodine","w":["The whole body from all types of radiation","The bone marrow from radioactive cesium","The lungs from inhaled radioactive particles"],"x":"Potassium iodide saturates the thyroid with stable iodine so it absorbs less radioactive iodine. It does not protect other organs or guard against other radioactive materials.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe39b02eb","lv":"im","d":"protamine","sys":"tox","q":"How does protamine reverse heparin?","a":"It binds heparin to form an inactive complex","w":["It activates vitamin K-dependent clotting factors","It breaks down fibrin in existing clots","It stimulates the bone marrow to make platelets"],"x":"Protamine is positively charged and binds negatively charged heparin, neutralizing it. Activating vitamin K-dependent factors describes vitamin K reversal of warfarin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2ec87d9d","lv":"im","d":"protamine","sys":"tox","q":"Which statement about protamine and low-molecular-weight heparins such as enoxaparin is accurate?","a":"Protamine only partially reverses their anticoagulant effect","w":["Protamine fully reverses them faster than heparin","Protamine has no effect on them at all","Protamine increases their anticoagulant effect"],"x":"Protamine neutralizes the anti-thrombin activity of low-molecular-weight heparins but only partly reverses their anti-Xa effect. It reverses unfractionated heparin much more completely.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp12cb92b3","lv":"im","d":"metformin","sys":"endo","q":"When a patient taking metformin has acute kidney injury and receives iodinated contrast, what is the main concern?","a":"Lactic acidosis from metformin accumulation","w":["Severe hypoglycemia from contrast","Thyroid storm from the iodine load","Gadolinium deposition in the brain"],"x":"Contrast can worsen kidney function, and metformin can then build up and cause lactic acidosis. Metformin alone rarely causes severe hypoglycemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf155f64e","lv":"im","d":"metformin","sys":"endo","q":"Per ACR guidance, a patient with severely reduced kidney function who takes metformin receives iodinated contrast. What is reassessed before metformin is restarted?","a":"Kidney function, such as eGFR","w":["Hemoglobin A1c level","Thyroid hormone level","Liver enzyme levels"],"x":"For patients with acute kidney injury or severe chronic kidney disease, kidney function is rechecked after contrast before metformin is resumed. Hemoglobin A1c reflects long-term glucose control, not drug safety after contrast.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa3bcc1d4","lv":"im","d":"glucagon","sys":"endo","q":"Why may glucagon be given during some GI fluoroscopy or abdominal MRI exams?","a":"To temporarily relax the bowel and reduce peristalsis","w":["To increase bowel motion and speed contrast transit","To prevent an allergic reaction to the contrast","To raise blood pressure during the study"],"x":"Glucagon temporarily relaxes smooth muscle of the GI tract, reducing motion artifact and improving distension. It does not prevent contrast reactions.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpaa17a39a","lv":"im","d":"glucagon","sys":"endo","q":"Glucagon used as a diagnostic aid is contraindicated in a patient with which condition?","a":"Pheochromocytoma","w":["Hypothyroidism","Asthma","Osteoporosis"],"x":"Glucagon can trigger catecholamine release from a pheochromocytoma and cause a sudden, dangerous rise in blood pressure. Insulinoma is another labeled contraindication.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd90d94fd","lv":"im","d":"hydrocortisone","sys":"endo","q":"An inpatient with a prior contrast reaction needs an urgent contrast CT and cannot wait for an oral regimen. Which IV corticosteroid may be used in an accelerated premedication regimen?","a":"Hydrocortisone","w":["Dexamethasone eye drops","Fludrocortisone","Budesonide inhaler"],"x":"ACR accelerated premedication uses an IV corticosteroid such as methylprednisolone or hydrocortisone with diphenhydramine. Eye drops, inhaled steroids and fludrocortisone are not used for contrast premedication.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbd6c0ac3","lv":"im","d":"hydrocortisone","sys":"endo","q":"Hydrocortisone is the treatment of choice for adrenal crisis. Which hormone does it replace?","a":"Cortisol","w":["Insulin","Thyroxine","Epinephrine"],"x":"Hydrocortisone is chemically identical to cortisol, the hormone lacking in adrenal insufficiency. Epinephrine comes from the adrenal medulla and is not replaced by hydrocortisone.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5d253995","lv":"im","d":"secretin","sys":"endo","q":"Secretin is sometimes used to improve visualization of the pancreatic duct. How does it work?","a":"It stimulates the pancreas to release bicarbonate-rich fluid","w":["It relaxes the bowel to reduce peristalsis","It increases bile flow from the gallbladder","It enhances the signal of gadolinium in the pancreas"],"x":"Secretin stimulates pancreatic secretion of bicarbonate-rich fluid, which distends the ducts and makes them easier to see. Bowel relaxation is the role of glucagon.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp46cbb4e9","lv":"im","d":"methylprednisolone","sys":"endo","q":"A patient with diabetes received methylprednisolone as contrast premedication. Which effect should be anticipated?","a":"A temporary rise in blood glucose","w":["A sudden drop in blood glucose","A sudden drop in blood pressure","A sharp rise in serum potassium"],"x":"Corticosteroids raise blood glucose, which can be significant in patients with diabetes. They do not cause hypoglycemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpba74fdec","lv":"im","d":"prednisone","sys":"endo","q":"A patient has taken prednisone daily for many months. Which effect makes abrupt changes risky?","a":"Suppression of the body's own cortisol production","w":["Permanent loss of insulin production","Overproduction of thyroid hormone","Excess production of aldosterone"],"x":"Long-term corticosteroid use suppresses the adrenal glands, so sudden changes can lead to adrenal insufficiency. It does not destroy insulin production.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7916ab59","lv":"im","d":"potassium-iodide","sys":"endo","q":"Which adverse effect is possible with repeated use of potassium iodide?","a":"Thyroid dysfunction, either underactive or overactive","w":["Permanent hearing loss","Bone marrow failure","Tendon rupture"],"x":"Excess iodide can disturb thyroid hormone production, especially with repeated doses or existing thyroid disease. Hearing loss, marrow failure and tendon rupture are not linked to potassium iodide.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpaf30b8c1","lv":"im","d":"ondansetron","sys":"gi","q":"How does ondansetron prevent nausea?","a":"It blocks serotonin 5-HT3 receptors","w":["It blocks histamine H2 receptors","It stimulates dopamine D2 receptors","It blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors"],"x":"Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 receptor antagonist that acts in the gut and brain to reduce nausea. Blocking H2 receptors describes famotidine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp57c7c5f2","lv":"im","d":"ondansetron","sys":"gi","q":"Which cardiac effect of ondansetron should be recognized, especially in patients with electrolyte problems?","a":"QT interval prolongation","w":["Complete heart block","Severe hypertension","Coronary artery spasm"],"x":"Ondansetron can prolong the QT interval, raising the risk of dangerous arrhythmias, particularly with low potassium or magnesium or other QT-prolonging drugs. Hypertension is not a typical effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa1d85602","lv":"im","d":"ondansetron","sys":"gi","q":"A patient taking an SSRI antidepressant receives ondansetron. Which interaction should be recognized?","a":"Serotonin syndrome","w":["Lactic acidosis","Heparin resistance","Hypoglycemia"],"x":"Combining ondansetron with other serotonergic drugs can rarely cause serotonin syndrome, with agitation, fever, tremor and fast heart rate. Lactic acidosis is linked to metformin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6467bf1f","lv":"im","d":"ondansetron","sys":"gi","q":"Which side effects are most commonly reported with ondansetron?","a":"Headache and constipation","w":["Diarrhea and hair loss","Cough and hoarseness","Tremor and fast heart rate"],"x":"Headache and constipation are the most common adverse effects of ondansetron. Tremor and tachycardia are more typical of albuterol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd05eb483","lv":"im","d":"famotidine","sys":"gi","q":"To which class does famotidine belong?","a":"Histamine H2 receptor antagonist","w":["Proton pump inhibitor","Serotonin 5-HT3 antagonist","First-generation H1 antihistamine"],"x":"Famotidine blocks H2 receptors on stomach cells to reduce acid secretion. Proton pump inhibitors, such as omeprazole, work by a different mechanism.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3386e38a","lv":"im","d":"famotidine","sys":"gi","q":"Why might famotidine be added to an H1 antihistamine for allergic hives?","a":"Histamine acts on both H1 and H2 receptors in the skin","w":["Famotidine directly reverses bronchospasm","Famotidine is a strong sedative for itching","Famotidine replaces epinephrine in anaphylaxis"],"x":"Skin blood vessels have both H1 and H2 receptors, so adding an H2 blocker can add relief for urticaria. Famotidine does not treat bronchospasm or replace epinephrine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcf83acc8","lv":"im","d":"famotidine","sys":"gi","q":"Which patients are more likely to have confusion or other CNS effects from famotidine?","a":"Patients with reduced kidney function","w":["Patients with mild seasonal allergies","Young adults with normal kidneys","Patients with well-controlled asthma"],"x":"Famotidine is cleared by the kidneys, so it can accumulate with kidney impairment and cause confusion, especially in older adults. Its labeling calls for adjustment in moderate to severe renal impairment.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7ed76fab","lv":"im","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"gi","q":"Besides allergy relief, diphenhydramine is labeled for which GI-related use?","a":"Preventing and treating motion sickness","w":["Healing stomach ulcers","Treating bacterial diarrhea","Reducing bowel peristalsis for imaging"],"x":"Diphenhydramine's antihistamine and anticholinergic effects help prevent motion sickness nausea. Reducing bowel peristalsis for imaging is typically done with glucagon.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4c125fd4","lv":"im","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"gi","q":"An older man with an enlarged prostate receives diphenhydramine. Which adverse effect should he be monitored for?","a":"Urinary retention","w":["Urinary incontinence","Excessive salivation","Pinpoint pupils"],"x":"Diphenhydramine's anticholinergic effects can prevent bladder emptying, especially with prostate enlargement. Excess salivation is the opposite of its drying effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0ef374a2","lv":"im","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"gi","q":"Which pair of side effects is commonly expected after a dose of diphenhydramine?","a":"Dry mouth and drowsiness","w":["Excess tearing and alertness","Diarrhea and insomnia","Increased sweating and tremor"],"x":"As a sedating first-generation antihistamine with anticholinergic action, diphenhydramine commonly causes dry mouth and drowsiness. Insomnia and diarrhea are not typical.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb0738d37","lv":"im","d":"labetalol","sys":"ob","q":"A pregnant patient with preeclampsia has severely elevated blood pressure. Labetalol is one first-line choice. Which class is it?","a":"Combined alpha- and beta-blocker","w":["Angiotensin receptor blocker","Thiazide diuretic","Direct thrombin inhibitor"],"x":"IV labetalol, a combined alpha- and beta-blocker, is a first-line treatment for severe hypertension in pregnancy. Angiotensin receptor blockers are avoided in pregnancy because of fetal harm.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpffd351be","lv":"im","d":"labetalol","sys":"ob","q":"Which patient history should be reported to the provider before labetalol is given?","a":"Asthma with frequent bronchospasm","w":["Seasonal allergic rhinitis","Well-controlled hypothyroidism","Iron deficiency anemia"],"x":"Labetalol blocks beta-2 receptors in the airways and can trigger bronchospasm; bronchial asthma is a labeled contraindication. The other histories are not contraindications.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3b885632","lv":"im","d":"labetalol","sys":"ob","q":"After IV labetalol, a patient feels dizzy when sitting up. What does this suggest?","a":"Orthostatic hypotension from the medicine","w":["An allergic reaction to the medicine","Low blood sugar from the medicine","Expected increase in blood pressure"],"x":"Labetalol's alpha-blocking effect can cause a drop in blood pressure on standing, so patients should stay lying down for a time after IV doses and change position slowly. Dizziness here is not an allergic sign.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4ca4c04e","lv":"im","d":"labetalol","sys":"ob","q":"If labetalol is given to a pregnant patient near delivery, which effects should the care team watch for in the newborn?","a":"Slow heart rate and low blood sugar","w":["Fast heart rate and high blood sugar","Rash and high fever","Jaundice and hearing loss"],"x":"Labetalol crosses the placenta, and newborns may show bradycardia, hypotension or hypoglycemia. High blood sugar and tachycardia would be the opposite of beta-blocker effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4aca08c5","lv":"im","d":"labetalol","sys":"ob","q":"Why does labetalol usually lower blood pressure without causing a reflex fast heart rate?","a":"Its beta-blocking action limits the heart rate response","w":["It stimulates the vagus nerve directly","It blocks calcium entry into the heart","It increases the heart's contractility"],"x":"Labetalol dilates vessels through alpha-1 blockade while its beta-blockade prevents the reflex tachycardia seen with pure vasodilators. It does not stimulate the vagus nerve or block calcium channels.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp06bd4d89","lv":"im","d":"cetirizine","sys":"immune","q":"Why does cetirizine usually cause less drowsiness than diphenhydramine?","a":"It is a second-generation antihistamine with less entry into the brain","w":["It is a corticosteroid that does not affect histamine","It blocks H2 receptors rather than H1 receptors","It is a stimulant that offsets antihistamine sedation"],"x":"Cetirizine is a second-generation H1 antihistamine that crosses into the brain less than diphenhydramine, though some patients still feel drowsy. It is not an H2 blocker or a steroid.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc4d0b1ba","lv":"im","d":"cetirizine","sys":"immune","q":"A patient calls a day after a contrast CT with a mild itchy rash. For which symptom is cetirizine used?","a":"Hives and itching","w":["Wheezing and chest tightness","Low blood pressure","Throat swelling"],"x":"Oral antihistamines such as cetirizine relieve hives and itching from mild delayed skin reactions. Wheezing, hypotension and throat swelling are signs of more serious reactions needing other treatment.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcfac55e2","lv":"im","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"immune","q":"After IV contrast, a patient develops a few scattered hives with normal vital signs and no breathing difficulty. If medicine is needed, which is commonly used?","a":"Diphenhydramine","w":["Epinephrine","Atropine","Naloxone"],"x":"Mild urticaria is often only observed, but an H1 antihistamine such as diphenhydramine may be given for symptom relief. Epinephrine is reserved for more severe or progressing reactions.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp04446e7b","lv":"im","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"immune","q":"Which class does diphenhydramine belong to?","a":"First-generation H1 antihistamine","w":["Second-generation H1 antihistamine","H2 receptor antagonist","Leukotriene receptor antagonist"],"x":"Diphenhydramine is a first-generation H1 antihistamine, which is why it is sedating and anticholinergic. Cetirizine is a second-generation agent.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp03a943ae","lv":"im","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"immune","q":"In anaphylaxis after contrast, what is the role of diphenhydramine?","a":"An adjunct for skin symptoms after epinephrine is given","w":["The first-line treatment for low blood pressure","The main treatment for airway swelling","A replacement for epinephrine in older adults"],"x":"Diphenhydramine relieves itching and hives but does not reverse hypotension or airway swelling. Epinephrine remains the first-line treatment for anaphylaxis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf68d0d13","lv":"im","d":"tc99m-medronate","sys":"bone","q":"Why does technetium Tc 99m medronate (MDP) show areas such as fractures and bone metastases on a bone scan?","a":"It localizes where there is increased bone turnover","w":["It binds to red blood cells in the marrow","It is taken up by active white blood cells","It concentrates only in fatty bone marrow"],"x":"MDP adsorbs onto bone mineral, with more uptake where osteoblastic activity and blood flow are increased. Labeled white blood cells, not MDP, are used to image infection directly.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp56a120d6","lv":"im","d":"tc99m-medronate","sys":"bone","q":"After the injection for a bone scan, what is a patient commonly taught to do during the waiting period?","a":"Drink fluids and empty the bladder often","w":["Avoid all fluids until imaging is done","Lie flat without moving until imaging","Eat a high-calcium meal right away"],"x":"Hydration and frequent voiding help clear unbound tracer and reduce radiation dose to the bladder. Restricting fluids would increase bladder dose.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp10eb68a3","lv":"im","d":"tc99m-medronate","sys":"bone","q":"Why is there usually a delay of a few hours between MDP injection and bone imaging?","a":"To let tracer clear from soft tissue and build up in bone","w":["To let the radioactivity decay completely","To wait for the patient's fasting period to end","To allow the contrast allergy window to pass"],"x":"The delay allows soft-tissue background to clear through the kidneys while bone uptake increases, improving image contrast. The tracer still needs to be radioactive for imaging.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd80e0557","lv":"im","d":"tc99m-medronate","sys":"bone","q":"On a normal bone scan with Tc 99m medronate, why are the kidneys and bladder usually visible?","a":"The tracer is excreted through the urinary tract","w":["The tracer is metabolized in the kidneys","It indicates a kidney infection is present","It shows tracer leaking from a vein"],"x":"MDP that does not bind to bone is excreted in the urine, so the kidneys and bladder are normally seen. Their presence alone does not suggest disease.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8b261784","lv":"im","d":"tc99m-medronate","sys":"bone","q":"If Tc 99m medronate leaks out of the vein at the injection site, what problem can it cause on the images?","a":"A hot spot that may hide or mimic disease nearby","w":["Complete absence of uptake in the skeleton","A severe allergic reaction to technetium","Permanent damage to the injection arm"],"x":"Extravasated tracer creates an intense focal area at the injection site and lowers the amount reaching bone, which can affect interpretation. The site should be noted for the reader.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp63aed1bd","lv":"im","d":"cefazolin","sys":"infect","q":"Cefazolin is commonly used for antibiotic prophylaxis before some interventional procedures. Which class does it belong to?","a":"First-generation cephalosporin","w":["Fluoroquinolone","Aminoglycoside","Macrolide"],"x":"Cefazolin is a first-generation cephalosporin often used for surgical and procedural prophylaxis. Fluoroquinolones and macrolides are different antibiotic classes.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp09edaaec","lv":"im","d":"cefazolin","sys":"infect","q":"Before placing an implanted port, why is cefazolin often chosen for prophylaxis?","a":"It covers common skin bacteria such as staphylococci","w":["It covers fungal infections of the bloodstream","It covers viral infections of the skin","It covers anaerobic bacteria in the colon"],"x":"Cefazolin is active against many gram-positive skin organisms such as methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus and streptococci, the usual sources of device infection. It has no antifungal or antiviral activity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp55ee658f","lv":"im","d":"cefazolin","sys":"infect","q":"When is prophylactic IV cefazolin usually given for a procedure?","a":"Within the hour before the skin incision","w":["The morning after the procedure","Only if a fever develops later","During the week after discharge"],"x":"Giving prophylaxis shortly before the incision ensures adequate tissue levels when bacteria may enter. Waiting for fever is treatment, not prevention.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpebcfa5a6","lv":"im","d":"cefazolin","sys":"infect","q":"A patient scheduled for cefazolin prophylaxis reports a penicillin allergy. What should be done?","a":"Report the allergy and the type of reaction to the provider","w":["Give the cefazolin without telling anyone","Assume the patient is allergic to all antibiotics","Replace cefazolin with a dose of diphenhydramine"],"x":"Cross-reactivity between penicillin and cefazolin is low, but the provider needs to know the reaction history to decide safely. The type of past reaction, such as anaphylaxis versus mild rash, guides the decision.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbfec97ac","lv":"im","d":"cefazolin","sys":"infect","q":"Days after receiving cefazolin, a patient develops frequent watery diarrhea. Which complication should be considered and reported?","a":"Clostridioides difficile infection","w":["Contrast-induced nephropathy","Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis"],"x":"Antibiotics disrupt normal gut flora and can allow Clostridioides difficile to overgrow, causing diarrhea and colitis. The other conditions are not caused by antibiotics.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp79914d5f","lv":"intern","d":"amiodarone","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient on long-term warfarin for atrial fibrillation is started on amiodarone for rhythm control. What change in anticoagulation should the intern anticipate?","a":"The INR rises because amiodarone inhibits warfarin metabolism","w":["The INR falls because amiodarone induces hepatic enzymes","The INR is unchanged because the drugs share no pathway","The INR falls because amiodarone binds warfarin in the gut"],"x":"Amiodarone inhibits CYP2C9 and other enzymes that clear warfarin, so the INR rises over the following weeks and needs closer monitoring. It is an enzyme inhibitor, not an inducer, so a falling INR is not expected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp69690302","lv":"intern","d":"amiodarone","sys":"cardio","q":"Before long-term oral amiodarone is started, which set of baseline studies is most appropriate?","a":"Thyroid tests, liver tests, chest imaging, and pulmonary function","w":["Lipid panel, uric acid, and hemoglobin A1c","Amylase, lipase, and creatine kinase","Ammonia, lactate, and urine drug screen"],"x":"Amiodarone can cause thyroid dysfunction, hepatotoxicity, and pulmonary toxicity, so these organs are assessed at baseline and periodically. The other panels do not target its major organ toxicities.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc94bf01f","lv":"intern","d":"digoxin","sys":"cardio","q":"An older patient on digoxin is admitted with acute kidney injury, nausea, yellow-tinged vision, and new bidirectional ventricular tachycardia. What is the most likely explanation?","a":"Digoxin toxicity from reduced renal clearance","w":["Hypercalcemia from a thiazide diuretic","Hepatic failure impairing digoxin metabolism","Hypermagnesemia from an antacid"],"x":"Digoxin is cleared mainly by the kidneys, so AKI raises levels; GI symptoms, visual changes, and bidirectional VT are classic for toxicity. Digoxin is not primarily cleared by the liver, so hepatic failure is the less likely cause.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp38b429f5","lv":"intern","d":"lisinopril","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient on lisinopril develops swelling of the lips and tongue without hives or itching. What mechanism best explains this reaction?","a":"Accumulation of bradykinin from ACE inhibition","w":["IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation","Complement activation by drug immune complexes","Direct histamine release from basophils"],"x":"ACE also breaks down bradykinin, so ACE inhibition lets bradykinin build up and cause angioedema, often without urticaria. Because it is not histamine- or IgE-driven, antihistamines and steroids work less well than they do for allergic angioedema.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6a9827bf","lv":"intern","d":"sacubitril-valsartan","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction on lisinopril is being switched to sacubitril-valsartan. Which precaution is required?","a":"Allow a washout after the last ACE inhibitor dose","w":["Give the first dose with the last ACE inhibitor dose","Add a potassium supplement with the first dose","Overlap both drugs for several days before switching"],"x":"Combining neprilysin inhibition with an ACE inhibitor greatly increases angioedema risk because both raise bradykinin, so a washout period is required. Overlapping the drugs is contraindicated for the same reason.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp89581a21","lv":"intern","d":"spironolactone","sys":"cardio","q":"Spironolactone is added to lisinopril for a patient with heart failure and chronic kidney disease. Which labs are most important to follow after starting it?","a":"Serum potassium and creatinine","w":["Serum sodium and glucose","Liver enzymes and bilirubin","Uric acid and calcium"],"x":"Spironolactone is potassium-sparing, and with an ACE inhibitor and reduced kidney function the risk of hyperkalemia and worsening renal function is high. Sodium can change, but potassium is the dangerous abnormality to watch.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp89f34c9e","lv":"intern","d":"diltiazem","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient with atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response also has acutely decompensated heart failure with reduced ejection fraction. Why is IV diltiazem a poor choice for rate control?","a":"Its negative inotropy can worsen systolic heart failure","w":["It speeds conduction through the AV node","It causes reflex tachycardia from vasodilation","It is contraindicated in patients with diabetes"],"x":"Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers reduce contractility and can worsen decompensated HFrEF. Diltiazem slows, rather than speeds, AV nodal conduction, which is why it is used for rate control in other patients.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp31d89521","lv":"intern","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"cardio","q":"Before giving sublingual nitroglycerin for chest pain, which home medication is most important to ask about?","a":"A phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor such as sildenafil","w":["A proton pump inhibitor such as omeprazole","A statin such as atorvastatin","An SSRI such as sertraline"],"x":"Nitrates combined with PDE-5 inhibitors can cause profound, refractory hypotension, so recent use must be asked about. The other drugs do not have a dangerous hemodynamic interaction with nitrates.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp553455ab","lv":"intern","d":"clopidogrel","sys":"cardio","q":"Clopidogrel must undergo which process before it can inhibit platelets?","a":"Hepatic activation, largely through CYP2C19","w":["Renal conversion to an active metabolite","Cleavage by gut bacteria in the colon","None; the parent drug is fully active"],"x":"Clopidogrel is a prodrug that needs CYP2C19-dependent activation in the liver, so poor metabolizers and some interacting drugs reduce its effect. It is not activated by the kidney or by gut flora.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd0cfd8ed","lv":"intern","d":"furosemide","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient receiving aggressive IV furosemide for volume overload is most likely to develop which pattern of lab changes?","a":"Hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis","w":["Hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis","Hypercalcemia and hypermagnesemia","Hyperkalemia and hypercalcemia"],"x":"Loop diuretics increase potassium and hydrogen ion loss and cause contraction alkalosis. They also increase calcium and magnesium excretion, so high calcium or magnesium would not be expected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp76172803","lv":"intern","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"A patient with septic shock remains hypotensive after adequate fluid resuscitation. Which vasopressor is recommended first-line?","a":"Norepinephrine","w":["Dopamine","Phenylephrine","Isoproterenol"],"x":"Norepinephrine is the first-line vasopressor in septic shock because it raises vascular tone with modest inotropy and fewer arrhythmias than dopamine. Phenylephrine is an alternative in select cases, not the routine first choice.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf584bdbd","lv":"intern","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"A peripheral IV running norepinephrine infiltrates, and the surrounding skin becomes pale and cool. Which agent is used locally to limit tissue injury?","a":"Phentolamine infiltrated into the affected area","w":["Hyaluronidase injected into the affected area","Protamine injected into the affected area","Ice packs applied over the affected area"],"x":"Phentolamine is an alpha-adrenergic blocker that reverses the local vasoconstriction caused by extravasated norepinephrine. Hyaluronidase is used for some non-vasoactive extravasations, and cold would worsen vasoconstriction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbd3d75e7","lv":"intern","d":"vasopressin","sys":"shock","q":"Vasopressin added to norepinephrine in septic shock raises blood pressure mainly by acting on which receptor?","a":"V1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle","w":["V2 receptors in the renal collecting duct","Alpha-1 adrenergic receptors on arterioles","Beta-1 adrenergic receptors on the heart"],"x":"Vasopressin causes vasoconstriction through V1 receptors, independent of adrenergic receptors, which is why it can reduce catecholamine needs. V2 receptors mediate water reabsorption, not vascular tone.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp442af662","lv":"intern","d":"epinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"A hospitalized patient develops anaphylaxis with wheezing and hypotension after a new antibiotic. What is the preferred initial route for epinephrine?","a":"Intramuscular injection into the anterolateral thigh","w":["Subcutaneous injection into the upper arm","Nebulized inhalation through a face mask","Oral administration as a liquid solution"],"x":"Intramuscular epinephrine in the thigh gives faster, more reliable absorption than subcutaneous injection and is the first-line initial treatment for anaphylaxis. Inhaled or oral routes do not treat the systemic reaction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2acb947f","lv":"intern","d":"epinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"A patient on an epinephrine infusion has a rising lactate despite improving blood pressure and perfusion. What best explains this?","a":"Beta-2 stimulation increases aerobic glycolysis and lactate production","w":["Epinephrine is metabolized directly into lactic acid","Epinephrine always causes mesenteric ischemia","Alpha-1 stimulation blocks lactate uptake by the liver"],"x":"Epinephrine's beta-2 effects drive glycolysis in muscle and raise lactate even when tissue perfusion is adequate. Lactate is not a metabolite of epinephrine itself, so that explanation is wrong.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8fda9e75","lv":"intern","d":"phenylephrine","sys":"shock","q":"A patient becomes hypotensive and receives IV phenylephrine. Which heart rate response is expected?","a":"Reflex slowing of the heart rate","w":["Marked sinus tachycardia from beta-1 stimulation","No change because it acts only on veins","New atrial fibrillation from atrial stimulation"],"x":"Phenylephrine is a pure alpha-1 agonist; the rise in blood pressure triggers a baroreceptor reflex that slows the heart. It has no meaningful beta-1 activity, so tachycardia is not expected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp25062da9","lv":"intern","d":"dobutamine","sys":"shock","q":"Dobutamine is started for low-output cardiogenic shock. Which adverse effects should the team watch for most closely?","a":"Tachyarrhythmias and possible hypotension","w":["Bradycardia and complete heart block","Severe hypertension from alpha stimulation","Hyperkalemia and peaked T waves"],"x":"Dobutamine is mainly a beta-1 agonist with some beta-2 vasodilation, so it can cause tachycardia, arrhythmias, and a drop in blood pressure. It does not cause strong alpha-mediated vasoconstriction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp808e292b","lv":"intern","d":"milrinone","sys":"shock","q":"Milrinone is being considered for a patient with low-output heart failure and acute kidney injury. What is the main concern?","a":"It accumulates because it is renally cleared, raising hypotension and arrhythmia risk","w":["It is hepatically cleared and causes liver failure in kidney injury","It is cleared by plasma esterases, so kidney injury has no effect","It accumulates and causes hypertensive crisis in kidney injury"],"x":"Milrinone is eliminated mainly by the kidneys, so it builds up in renal impairment and its vasodilating and proarrhythmic effects become stronger. It lowers, not raises, blood pressure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcf90dc3c","lv":"intern","d":"dopamine","sys":"shock","q":"Compared with norepinephrine as the first vasopressor in shock, dopamine is associated with which outcome?","a":"More arrhythmic events","w":["Better protection of kidney function","Lower overall mortality","Less tachycardia"],"x":"Trials comparing the two found more arrhythmias with dopamine and no renal benefit, which is why norepinephrine is preferred. The idea that dopamine protects the kidneys has not been supported.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb2a016c1","lv":"intern","d":"midodrine","sys":"shock","q":"A patient is started on midodrine for symptomatic orthostatic hypotension. Which counseling point is most important?","a":"Take doses during the day, not near bedtime, to limit supine hypertension","w":["Take the last dose at bedtime to prevent morning dizziness","Take each dose lying flat to improve absorption","Take doses only when dizzy to avoid tolerance"],"x":"Midodrine is an alpha-1 agonist prodrug that can cause significant supine hypertension, so doses are timed while the patient is upright during the day. Bedtime dosing is exactly what raises that risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpca6eae16","lv":"intern","d":"argatroban","sys":"blood","q":"For a patient with heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, argatroban is generally preferred over bivalirudin when the patient has which condition?","a":"Severe kidney impairment","w":["Severe hepatic impairment","Active gastrointestinal bleeding","Planned percutaneous coronary intervention"],"x":"Argatroban is cleared by the liver, so it is a good choice when kidney function is poor. In hepatic impairment it accumulates, which is where bivalirudin is often favored.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb0b1a648","lv":"intern","d":"heparin","sys":"blood","q":"On day seven of unfractionated heparin, a patient's platelet count falls by more than half and a new DVT appears. What process best explains this?","a":"Antibodies to platelet factor 4-heparin complexes activate platelets","w":["Heparin directly suppresses megakaryocytes in the marrow","Platelets are diluted by large-volume IV fluids","Platelets are sequestered in an enlarged spleen"],"x":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia is an immune reaction against PF4-heparin complexes that activates platelets, causing both low counts and thrombosis. Marrow suppression would not explain the new clot.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd2934aa5","lv":"intern","d":"enoxaparin","sys":"blood","q":"Enoxaparin is ordered for VTE prophylaxis in a patient with severely reduced kidney function. What is the main concern?","a":"It accumulates and raises bleeding risk without adjustment","w":["It is inactivated faster and loses its effect","It causes hyperkalemia through aldosterone activation","It requires conversion by the kidney to work"],"x":"Enoxaparin is cleared by the kidneys, so it accumulates in severe renal impairment and needs adjustment or an alternative such as unfractionated heparin. It does not lose effect in renal failure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpeba0d956","lv":"intern","d":"warfarin","sys":"blood","q":"A patient on warfarin is prescribed trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for a urinary tract infection. What effect is expected?","a":"A higher INR and increased bleeding risk","w":["A lower INR and increased clot risk","No change in INR but more bruising","A lower INR from increased vitamin K intake"],"x":"Sulfamethoxazole inhibits CYP2C9, which clears the more potent warfarin isomer, so the INR rises. This is one of the most common clinically important warfarin interactions.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5f139af7","lv":"intern","d":"warfarin","sys":"blood","q":"A patient has a mechanical mitral valve and needs long-term anticoagulation. Which oral agent is appropriate?","a":"Warfarin","w":["Apixaban","Rivaroxaban","Dabigatran"],"x":"Warfarin is the oral anticoagulant used for mechanical heart valves. Direct oral anticoagulants are not approved for mechanical valves, and dabigatran caused more clots and bleeding in a trial in this group.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpae3f7838","lv":"intern","d":"prothrombin-complex-concentrate","sys":"blood","q":"A patient on warfarin has an intracranial hemorrhage and an elevated INR. Which approach reverses the anticoagulation most rapidly?","a":"Four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate with IV vitamin K","w":["Fresh frozen plasma alone without vitamin K","Protamine sulfate with IV vitamin K","Oral vitamin K alone"],"x":"Four-factor PCC replaces factors II, VII, IX, and X within minutes, and IV vitamin K keeps the correction going. Protamine reverses heparin, not warfarin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb292f522","lv":"intern","d":"dabigatran","sys":"blood","q":"Which oral anticoagulant's clearance depends most heavily on kidney function?","a":"Dabigatran","w":["Apixaban","Rivaroxaban","Warfarin"],"x":"Dabigatran is cleared mostly by the kidneys, so its levels rise sharply in renal impairment. Apixaban has the least renal clearance of the direct oral anticoagulants.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2ca427ee","lv":"intern","d":"idarucizumab","sys":"blood","q":"How does idarucizumab reverse the effect of dabigatran?","a":"It is an antibody fragment that binds dabigatran tightly","w":["It supplies clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X","It acts as a decoy factor Xa that binds inhibitors","It neutralizes heparin through ionic binding"],"x":"Idarucizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody fragment that binds dabigatran with very high affinity. A decoy factor Xa describes a different reversal agent for factor Xa inhibitors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7fa2cb59","lv":"intern","d":"protamine","sys":"blood","q":"Protamine most completely reverses the anticoagulant effect of which agent?","a":"Unfractionated heparin","w":["Enoxaparin","Fondaparinux","Apixaban"],"x":"Protamine binds unfractionated heparin and fully neutralizes it. It only partially reverses enoxaparin and has no meaningful effect on fondaparinux or apixaban.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb7f2f25e","lv":"intern","d":"rivaroxaban","sys":"blood","q":"A patient on rivaroxaban for VTE is started on rifampin. What is the expected effect?","a":"Lower rivaroxaban levels and higher risk of clotting","w":["Higher rivaroxaban levels and higher risk of bleeding","No change because rivaroxaban is renally cleared only","Higher levels because rifampin inhibits CYP3A4"],"x":"Rifampin strongly induces both CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, which lowers rivaroxaban exposure and can lead to treatment failure. Rifampin is an inducer, not an inhibitor.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp57c0cc9c","lv":"intern","d":"succinylcholine","sys":"airway","q":"Which patient is at highest risk of life-threatening hyperkalemia if succinylcholine is used for intubation?","a":"A patient with a crush injury several days ago","w":["A patient with a COPD exacerbation","A patient with acute appendicitis","A patient with an asthma attack"],"x":"After burns, crush injuries, or denervation, extra acetylcholine receptors spread across muscle, and succinylcholine can release large amounts of potassium. The other conditions do not upregulate these receptors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0bd32f2c","lv":"intern","d":"succinylcholine","sys":"airway","q":"Shortly after intubation with succinylcholine, a patient develops jaw rigidity, rising end-tidal CO2, tachycardia, and fever. What should be recognized?","a":"Malignant hyperthermia","w":["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Serotonin syndrome","Anaphylaxis"],"x":"Succinylcholine and volatile anesthetics can trigger malignant hyperthermia, a hypermetabolic crisis treated with dantrolene. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome develops over days after dopamine blockers, not minutes after anesthesia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp63ae8390","lv":"intern","d":"sugammadex","sys":"airway","q":"How does sugammadex reverse rocuronium-induced paralysis?","a":"It encapsulates rocuronium and removes it from the receptor","w":["It inhibits acetylcholinesterase at the junction","It blocks muscarinic receptors to limit side effects","It competes with rocuronium as a direct agonist"],"x":"Sugammadex is a modified cyclodextrin that binds steroidal blockers like rocuronium and vecuronium directly. Acetylcholinesterase inhibition is how neostigmine works, not sugammadex.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp135fe0bb","lv":"intern","d":"sugammadex","sys":"airway","q":"A patient who takes an oral hormonal contraceptive received sugammadex during surgery. Which counseling point applies?","a":"Use a nonhormonal backup method for a period afterward","w":["No change is needed because there is no interaction","Double the next contraceptive dose to compensate","Switch permanently to an intrauterine device"],"x":"Sugammadex can bind progestogen and reduce contraceptive exposure, similar to missing a dose, so backup contraception is advised for a period. Doubling doses is not the recommended approach.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf29f4219","lv":"intern","d":"rocuronium","sys":"airway","q":"For rapid sequence intubation in a patient with severe hyperkalemia, why is rocuronium preferred over succinylcholine?","a":"It is nondepolarizing and does not raise serum potassium","w":["It has a much shorter duration than succinylcholine","It directly lowers serum potassium","It does not need to be reversed"],"x":"Rocuronium blocks the receptor without depolarizing the muscle, so it does not release potassium. It actually lasts longer than succinylcholine, which is why a reversal plan matters.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2cdc5974","lv":"intern","d":"etomidate","sys":"airway","q":"Which adverse effect is a key concern with etomidate used for induction?","a":"Transient adrenal suppression from blocked cortisol synthesis","w":["Profound hypotension in most patients","Severe bronchospasm from histamine release","Malignant hyperthermia triggering"],"x":"Etomidate inhibits 11-beta-hydroxylase and temporarily suppresses cortisol production. It is chosen in unstable patients precisely because it usually preserves blood pressure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4fa8e47d","lv":"intern","d":"ketamine","sys":"airway","q":"Ketamine is a useful induction agent for a patient with hypotension or severe bronchospasm mainly because it:","a":"Supports blood pressure and causes bronchodilation","w":["Lowers blood pressure and reduces airway secretions","Causes profound bradycardia and bronchoconstriction","Has no effect on heart rate or airway tone"],"x":"Ketamine's sympathomimetic effects tend to maintain blood pressure, and it relaxes bronchial smooth muscle. It increases, rather than reduces, airway secretions.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5d07d697","lv":"intern","d":"propofol","sys":"airway","q":"Which lab should be monitored periodically during a prolonged propofol infusion in the ICU?","a":"Serum triglycerides","w":["Serum ammonia","Serum uric acid","Serum amylase only"],"x":"Propofol is delivered in a lipid emulsion, so prolonged infusions can cause hypertriglyceridemia and pancreatitis. Triglycerides are the standard lab to follow.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcb924db1","lv":"intern","d":"midazolam","sys":"airway","q":"A sedated ICU patient with kidney failure stays oversedated long after a midazolam infusion is turned off. What best explains this?","a":"An active glucuronide metabolite accumulates in kidney failure","w":["Midazolam is converted to a longer-acting opioid","Kidney failure speeds midazolam clearance","Midazolam inhibits its own metabolism permanently"],"x":"Midazolam's active metabolite, alpha-hydroxymidazolam glucuronide, is renally cleared and builds up in kidney failure, prolonging sedation. Kidney failure slows, rather than speeds, its elimination.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2a434717","lv":"intern","d":"cisatracurium","sys":"airway","q":"Why is cisatracurium often chosen for neuromuscular blockade in patients with liver and kidney failure?","a":"It undergoes Hofmann elimination, independent of organ function","w":["It is excreted unchanged by the kidneys","It is cleared only by hepatic metabolism","It is reversed by sugammadex"],"x":"Cisatracurium breaks down spontaneously in plasma by Hofmann elimination, so it does not depend on the liver or kidneys. Sugammadex does not reverse benzylisoquinolines like cisatracurium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9268a265","lv":"intern","d":"albuterol","sys":"resp","q":"A patient receives frequent nebulized albuterol during a severe asthma exacerbation. Which lab change is most expected?","a":"Hypokalemia from potassium shifting into cells","w":["Hyperkalemia from potassium release from cells","Hypercalcemia from bone resorption","Hyponatremia from water retention"],"x":"Beta-2 agonists stimulate the sodium-potassium pump and shift potassium into cells, lowering serum potassium. This is the same effect used to treat hyperkalemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2135163f","lv":"intern","d":"ipratropium","sys":"resp","q":"Ipratropium is added to albuterol during a COPD exacerbation. How does it improve airflow?","a":"It blocks muscarinic receptors to reduce bronchoconstriction","w":["It stimulates beta-2 receptors on airway smooth muscle","It blocks leukotriene receptors in the airway","It inhibits phosphodiesterase in airway cells"],"x":"Ipratropium is a short-acting muscarinic antagonist that blocks vagal bronchoconstriction, adding to the effect of a beta-2 agonist. Beta-2 stimulation is how albuterol, not ipratropium, works.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4c25e1a3","lv":"intern","d":"prednisone","sys":"resp","q":"A patient with type 2 diabetes is started on oral prednisone for a COPD exacerbation. Which problem is most likely?","a":"Hyperglycemia, often most prominent later in the day","w":["Hypoglycemia, especially overnight","Hyperkalemia from mineralocorticoid effects","Hypercalcemia from increased absorption"],"x":"Glucocorticoids raise glucose by increasing insulin resistance and gluconeogenesis, and with morning dosing the rise peaks in the afternoon and evening. Hypoglycemia is not expected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp11c38558","lv":"intern","d":"methylprednisolone","sys":"resp","q":"A patient admitted for a COPD exacerbation can swallow and absorb oral medications. How does oral prednisone compare with IV methylprednisolone?","a":"Oral therapy is generally as effective as IV therapy","w":["IV therapy is required for any hospitalized patient","Oral therapy works only after several weeks","IV therapy has fewer side effects at any dose"],"x":"In patients who can take oral medications, oral corticosteroids work as well as IV for COPD exacerbations. Hospitalization alone does not require the IV route.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpddb136c6","lv":"intern","d":"theophylline","sys":"resp","q":"A patient on chronic theophylline is started on ciprofloxacin and develops nausea, tachycardia, and a seizure. What is the most likely cause?","a":"Ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2, raising theophylline levels","w":["Ciprofloxacin induces CYP1A2, lowering theophylline levels","Theophylline raises ciprofloxacin levels into the toxic range","The drugs bind each other and form a toxic compound"],"x":"Theophylline has a narrow therapeutic range and is cleared by CYP1A2, which ciprofloxacin inhibits, so levels rise and cause GI, cardiac, and seizure toxicity. Ciprofloxacin is an inhibitor, not an inducer.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2e59a2c6","lv":"intern","d":"magnesium-sulfate","sys":"resp","q":"IV magnesium sulfate is given during a severe asthma exacerbation. How does it help?","a":"It relaxes bronchial smooth muscle by opposing calcium","w":["It reduces airway inflammation like a corticosteroid","It thins mucus by breaking disulfide bonds","It blocks leukotriene synthesis in the airway"],"x":"Magnesium inhibits calcium entry into airway smooth muscle, producing bronchodilation in severe exacerbations. It does not have direct anti-inflammatory steroid-like effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp06b2236f","lv":"intern","d":"tiotropium","sys":"resp","q":"For which patient should tiotropium be used with particular caution?","a":"A patient with narrow-angle glaucoma or urinary retention","w":["A patient with well-controlled hypertension","A patient with type 2 diabetes","A patient with osteoarthritis of the knee"],"x":"Tiotropium is a long-acting muscarinic antagonist, and anticholinergic effects can worsen narrow-angle glaucoma and urinary retention from prostate enlargement. The other conditions are not specific concerns.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2570ecfc","lv":"intern","d":"montelukast","sys":"resp","q":"Which serious adverse effect carries a boxed warning for montelukast?","a":"Neuropsychiatric events such as agitation, depression, or suicidal thoughts","w":["Liver failure requiring transplantation","Severe hypokalemia and arrhythmias","Bone marrow suppression with agranulocytosis"],"x":"Montelukast carries a boxed warning for serious neuropsychiatric events, so patients and families should report mood or behavior changes. Liver injury is rare and is not the boxed warning.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8ffa17c6","lv":"intern","d":"azithromycin","sys":"resp","q":"For a patient hospitalized with community-acquired pneumonia, azithromycin is commonly added to a beta-lactam mainly to cover which organisms?","a":"Atypical organisms such as Legionella and Mycoplasma","w":["Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Anaerobes from aspiration"],"x":"Beta-lactams do not cover atypical organisms, so a macrolide is added for Legionella, Mycoplasma, and Chlamydophila. Azithromycin does not reliably cover MRSA or Pseudomonas.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp448f22bc","lv":"intern","d":"budesonide-formoterol","sys":"resp","q":"A patient is discharged on budesonide-formoterol inhaler. Which teaching point helps prevent a common local side effect?","a":"Rinse the mouth with water and spit after each use","w":["Use a nasal decongestant before each dose","Take each dose with a full meal","Swallow the dose quickly without inhaling deeply"],"x":"Inhaled corticosteroids can cause oral thrush and hoarseness, and rinsing and spitting reduces deposition in the mouth. Taking it with meals does not reduce this risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp066bae52","lv":"intern","d":"lorazepam","sys":"neuro","q":"A patient with IV access is in convulsive status epilepticus. Which medication is the preferred first-line treatment?","a":"Intravenous lorazepam","w":["Intravenous levetiracetam","Intravenous phenobarbital","Oral lamotrigine"],"x":"A benzodiazepine such as IV lorazepam is first-line for status epilepticus. Levetiracetam, fosphenytoin, or valproate are second-line agents if seizures continue.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc191ff4d","lv":"intern","d":"fosphenytoin","sys":"neuro","q":"What is an advantage of fosphenytoin over IV phenytoin?","a":"Lower risk of infusion-site injury such as purple glove syndrome","w":["No need for cardiac monitoring during infusion","No risk of hypotension during infusion","It does not convert to phenytoin in the body"],"x":"Fosphenytoin is a water-soluble prodrug that is less irritating to veins, reducing tissue injury. It is still converted to phenytoin and can still cause hypotension and arrhythmias, so monitoring is still needed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp619a0969","lv":"intern","d":"phenytoin","sys":"neuro","q":"A malnourished patient with low serum albumin has a total phenytoin level within the usual range but shows nystagmus and ataxia. What explains this?","a":"The unbound fraction is higher than the total level suggests","w":["Low albumin lowers the unbound phenytoin fraction","The level must be falsely high from lab interference","Nystagmus is unrelated to phenytoin levels"],"x":"Phenytoin is highly protein bound, so low albumin raises the free, active fraction even when the total level looks acceptable. A free phenytoin level or corrected value gives a better picture.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1e883e8d","lv":"intern","d":"valproic-acid","sys":"neuro","q":"A patient on valproic acid becomes lethargic and confused, but liver enzymes are normal. Which lab should be checked?","a":"Serum ammonia","w":["Serum lipase","Serum magnesium","Serum creatine kinase"],"x":"Valproate can cause hyperammonemic encephalopathy even when liver tests are normal. Pancreatitis is another valproate toxicity, but it presents with abdominal pain rather than isolated confusion.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9a8684cb","lv":"intern","d":"valproic-acid","sys":"neuro","q":"A patient whose seizures are controlled on valproic acid is started on meropenem and then has breakthrough seizures. Why?","a":"Carbapenems sharply lower valproic acid levels","w":["Meropenem directly lowers the seizure threshold alone","Valproic acid inactivates meropenem in the blood","Meropenem raises valproic acid to toxic levels"],"x":"Carbapenems can drop valproate concentrations rapidly and substantially, causing loss of seizure control; the combination is generally avoided. Simply increasing the valproate dose does not reliably overcome this interaction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa37ff83e","lv":"intern","d":"levetiracetam","sys":"neuro","q":"Which adverse effect should patients and families be told to report when levetiracetam is started?","a":"Irritability, mood changes, or behavioral symptoms","w":["Gingival overgrowth and coarse facial features","Weight gain and hair loss","Kidney stones and metabolic acidosis"],"x":"Levetiracetam commonly causes behavioral and mood effects, including irritability and, rarely, suicidal thoughts. Gingival overgrowth is a phenytoin effect, not a levetiracetam effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa474b544","lv":"intern","d":"carbamazepine","sys":"neuro","q":"Before starting carbamazepine in a patient of Asian ancestry, which test is recommended?","a":"HLA-B*15:02 to assess risk of Stevens-Johnson syndrome","w":["TPMT activity to assess risk of myelosuppression","G6PD activity to assess risk of hemolysis","CYP2C19 genotype to assess drug activation"],"x":"HLA-B*15:02, which is more common in many Asian populations, is strongly linked to carbamazepine-induced SJS and TEN. TPMT testing applies to thiopurines such as azathioprine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp593bc0c9","lv":"intern","d":"nimodipine","sys":"neuro","q":"How is nimodipine given to a patient after aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage?","a":"Orally or by feeding tube, never intravenously","w":["Intravenously as a continuous infusion","Intrathecally through a ventricular drain","Intramuscularly into the deltoid"],"x":"Nimodipine is given by mouth or feeding tube; injecting capsule contents IV has caused cardiac arrest and death. It is used to improve neurologic outcomes after aneurysmal SAH.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd69486ce","lv":"intern","d":"thiamine","sys":"neuro","q":"A malnourished patient with chronic alcohol use is confused and hypoglycemic. Why is thiamine given before or with dextrose?","a":"To prevent precipitating Wernicke encephalopathy","w":["To speed absorption of the dextrose","To prevent rebound hypoglycemia","To reverse alcohol intoxication directly"],"x":"Glucose metabolism uses thiamine, and giving glucose to a thiamine-deficient patient can precipitate or worsen Wernicke encephalopathy. Thiamine does not change how fast dextrose is absorbed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbbf87d10","lv":"intern","d":"alteplase","sys":"neuro","q":"Which finding is an absolute contraindication to IV alteplase for acute ischemic stroke?","a":"Intracranial hemorrhage on head CT","w":["History of well-controlled hypertension","Mild headache at symptom onset","Daily statin use at home"],"x":"Any hemorrhage on imaging rules out thrombolysis because it would worsen bleeding. Controlled hypertension and statin use are not contraindications.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf13ef7fa","lv":"intern","d":"morphine","sys":"pain","q":"Why is morphine a poor choice for a patient with severe kidney failure?","a":"Active metabolites accumulate and cause sedation and neurotoxicity","w":["It is inactive until converted by the kidneys","Kidney failure speeds its clearance and shortens its effect","It directly damages the remaining nephrons"],"x":"Morphine's glucuronide metabolites are renally cleared and accumulate in kidney failure, causing prolonged sedation, respiratory depression, and myoclonus. It is not a renal prodrug.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpee1c313f","lv":"intern","d":"fentanyl","sys":"pain","q":"Which opioid is generally preferred in severe kidney impairment because it lacks clinically important active metabolites?","a":"Fentanyl","w":["Morphine","Codeine","Meperidine"],"x":"Fentanyl is metabolized by the liver to inactive metabolites, making it a safer choice in kidney failure. Meperidine forms normeperidine, which accumulates and can cause seizures.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4a5f3a42","lv":"intern","d":"naloxone","sys":"pain","q":"A patient with opioid-induced respiratory depression responds well to naloxone but becomes drowsy and hypoventilates again later. Why?","a":"Naloxone's effect can wear off before the opioid's effect","w":["Naloxone itself causes delayed respiratory depression","The opioid was converted into naloxone","Naloxone increases opioid absorption from the gut"],"x":"Naloxone's duration is often shorter than that of long-acting or extended-release opioids, so sedation can return. Patients need ongoing monitoring and may need repeat doses or an infusion.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp260eab57","lv":"intern","d":"methadone","sys":"pain","q":"A patient's methadone was recently titrated up for pain, and several days later they become very sedated. What best explains the delay?","a":"Its long, variable half-life lets it accumulate over days","w":["It is a prodrug that activates only after a week","It causes delayed allergic reactions","Its effect is limited to the first dose"],"x":"Methadone's long and unpredictable half-life means levels keep rising for days after a dose change, so respiratory depression can appear late. That is why titration is slow and monitoring continues after changes.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8252e3bb","lv":"intern","d":"tramadol","sys":"pain","q":"A patient on sertraline is prescribed tramadol after surgery and develops agitation, tremor, hyperreflexia, and fever. What should be recognized?","a":"Serotonin syndrome","w":["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Opioid withdrawal","Anticholinergic toxicity"],"x":"Tramadol inhibits serotonin reuptake, and combined with an SSRI it can cause serotonin syndrome with clonus and hyperreflexia. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome features rigidity and slow reflexes after dopamine blockers.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb38fa300","lv":"intern","d":"ketorolac","sys":"pain","q":"Which hospitalized patient is the poorest candidate for IV ketorolac?","a":"A patient with acute kidney injury","w":["A patient with renal colic and normal kidney function","A patient with postoperative pain and normal labs","A patient with a migraine and normal labs"],"x":"Ketorolac is an NSAID that reduces renal blood flow and can worsen kidney injury, so it is contraindicated in renal impairment or volume depletion. It is commonly used for renal colic when kidney function is normal.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8ceac084","lv":"intern","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"pain","q":"A patient's home medications include hydrocodone-acetaminophen and an over-the-counter cold product. What is the main concern?","a":"Unintended cumulative acetaminophen intake causing liver injury","w":["Severe kidney injury from hydrocodone","Additive bleeding risk from acetaminophen","Serotonin syndrome from the combination"],"x":"Many combination and OTC products contain acetaminophen, and taking several can exceed safe daily totals and cause hepatotoxicity. Acetaminophen does not affect platelets the way NSAIDs do.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd63e9a3e","lv":"intern","d":"gabapentin","sys":"pain","q":"An older patient with chronic kidney disease on gabapentin becomes increasingly drowsy and confused with myoclonus. What is the most likely cause?","a":"Gabapentin accumulation from reduced renal clearance","w":["Gabapentin-induced liver failure","Serotonin syndrome from gabapentin","Gabapentin-induced hypoglycemia"],"x":"Gabapentin is cleared unchanged by the kidneys, so it accumulates in CKD and causes sedation, confusion, and myoclonus. It is not hepatically metabolized.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp26d76533","lv":"intern","d":"dexmedetomidine","sys":"pain","q":"A patient is sedated with dexmedetomidine in the ICU. Which adverse effects need the closest monitoring?","a":"Bradycardia and hypotension","w":["Respiratory depression and apnea","Hyperthermia and muscle rigidity","Hypertensive crisis and tachycardia"],"x":"Dexmedetomidine is a central alpha-2 agonist that lowers sympathetic tone, causing bradycardia and hypotension. It causes little respiratory depression, which is one of its advantages.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp501e0d79","lv":"intern","d":"flumazenil","sys":"pain","q":"Why is flumazenil used cautiously in a patient with long-term benzodiazepine use or an unknown mixed overdose?","a":"It can precipitate withdrawal seizures","w":["It causes profound respiratory depression","It worsens opioid toxicity","It has no effect on benzodiazepines"],"x":"Flumazenil competitively blocks the benzodiazepine receptor and can trigger seizures in dependent patients or those who took proconvulsant drugs such as tricyclics. It does not cause respiratory depression.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0d76ec16","lv":"intern","d":"haloperidol","sys":"psych","q":"Before giving IV haloperidol for agitated delirium, which assessment is most important?","a":"An ECG for the QTc interval and electrolytes","w":["A lipid panel and hemoglobin A1c","A urine pregnancy test only","Thyroid function tests"],"x":"IV haloperidol can prolong the QT interval and cause torsades de pointes, so a baseline ECG and correction of potassium and magnesium are important. Metabolic labs matter for long-term antipsychotic use, not this acute decision.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp096c2e24","lv":"intern","d":"haloperidol","sys":"psych","q":"Two days after starting haloperidol, a patient develops fever, lead-pipe rigidity, confusion, unstable blood pressure, and a very high CK. What should be recognized?","a":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","w":["Serotonin syndrome","Malignant hyperthermia","Acute dystonic reaction"],"x":"Fever, rigidity, altered mental status, autonomic instability, and high CK after a dopamine blocker point to NMS. Serotonin syndrome develops faster and features clonus and hyperreflexia instead of lead-pipe rigidity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp27799904","lv":"intern","d":"lithium","sys":"psych","q":"A patient on stable lithium is started on hydrochlorothiazide for hypertension. What is the main risk?","a":"Lithium toxicity from reduced renal lithium clearance","w":["Subtherapeutic lithium from increased excretion","Hypokalemia that blocks lithium's effect","Hepatotoxicity from the combination"],"x":"Thiazides increase proximal sodium and lithium reabsorption, raising lithium levels; ACE inhibitors and NSAIDs do the same. Lithium is not hepatically cleared.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpae5e5d35","lv":"intern","d":"clozapine","sys":"psych","q":"A patient on clozapine is admitted with fever and a sore throat. Which lab is most urgent?","a":"Absolute neutrophil count","w":["Serum lithium level","Serum ammonia","Thyroid-stimulating hormone"],"x":"Clozapine can cause severe neutropenia and agranulocytosis, and fever or sore throat may be the first sign. The absolute neutrophil count guides urgent management.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfae167d7","lv":"intern","d":"olanzapine","sys":"psych","q":"Which combination raises the most concern for cardiorespiratory depression in an agitated patient?","a":"IM olanzapine given with a parenteral benzodiazepine","w":["Oral olanzapine given with oral acetaminophen","IM olanzapine given with oral docusate","Oral olanzapine given with a nicotine patch"],"x":"Labeling advises against combining IM olanzapine with parenteral benzodiazepines because of reports of hypotension, bradycardia, and respiratory depression. The other combinations do not carry this risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp82aab6d2","lv":"intern","d":"quetiapine","sys":"psych","q":"A patient with Parkinson disease develops distressing hallucinations in the hospital. Why is quetiapine often preferred over haloperidol?","a":"Its weak dopamine D2 blockade causes less motor worsening","w":["It increases dopamine release in the striatum","It has strong D2 blockade that reduces tremor","It works without crossing the blood-brain barrier"],"x":"Quetiapine binds D2 receptors loosely, so it is less likely to worsen parkinsonism. Haloperidol's strong D2 blockade can markedly worsen motor symptoms.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpaf1025e4","lv":"intern","d":"sertraline","sys":"psych","q":"A patient taking sertraline needs linezolid for a resistant infection. Which risk should the team watch for?","a":"Serotonin syndrome because linezolid inhibits monoamine oxidase","w":["Lithium toxicity from reduced renal clearance","Hypertensive crisis from tyramine in hospital food","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome from dopamine blockade"],"x":"Linezolid is a weak MAO inhibitor, and combining it with an SSRI can cause serotonin syndrome. When the combination is needed, the patient is monitored closely for agitation, clonus, and fever.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3ad03a2e","lv":"intern","d":"lorazepam","sys":"psych","q":"A patient with cirrhosis is admitted in alcohol withdrawal. Why is lorazepam often chosen over chlordiazepoxide?","a":"It is cleared by glucuronidation without active metabolites","w":["It has a much longer half-life than chlordiazepoxide","It is the only benzodiazepine that treats seizures","It undergoes extensive oxidative liver metabolism"],"x":"Lorazepam is conjugated directly and has no active metabolites, so it accumulates less in liver disease. Chlordiazepoxide relies on oxidative metabolism and has long-acting active metabolites.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4210a929","lv":"intern","d":"bupropion","sys":"psych","q":"For which patient is bupropion contraindicated?","a":"A patient with a seizure disorder","w":["A patient with well-controlled hypertension","A patient who smokes cigarettes","A patient with seasonal depression"],"x":"Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold and is contraindicated with seizure disorders, eating disorders, and abrupt alcohol or sedative withdrawal. It is used for smoking cessation and seasonal depression.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8bc5400d","lv":"intern","d":"buprenorphine","sys":"psych","q":"A patient who recently used heroin develops sudden severe withdrawal after the first dose of buprenorphine. What explains this?","a":"Its high-affinity partial agonism displaces full agonists","w":["It is a full agonist that causes overdose","It blocks serotonin reuptake","It is converted to naloxone in the liver"],"x":"Buprenorphine binds opioid receptors tightly but activates them only partly, so it can push off a full agonist and precipitate withdrawal. That is why induction usually waits for withdrawal signs or uses a low-dose start.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa54d637f","lv":"intern","d":"calcium-gluconate","sys":"renal","q":"A patient with hyperkalemia has peaked T waves on ECG. What is the purpose of IV calcium gluconate?","a":"It stabilizes the cardiac membrane without lowering potassium","w":["It shifts potassium into cells","It removes potassium through the gut","It increases urinary potassium excretion"],"x":"Calcium protects the heart from arrhythmia within minutes but does not change serum potassium. Insulin and beta-agonists shift potassium into cells, and binders or diuretics remove it.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9942a160","lv":"intern","d":"regular-insulin","sys":"renal","q":"When IV regular insulin is used to treat hyperkalemia, what is the main risk and how is it addressed?","a":"Hypoglycemia, so dextrose is given and glucose is checked","w":["Hypercalcemia, so calcium levels are checked","Hypernatremia, so sodium levels are checked","Hyperkalemia rebound, so insulin is repeated hourly"],"x":"Insulin shifts potassium into cells but also lowers glucose, so dextrose is usually given unless glucose is high, and glucose is monitored for hours. Insulin does not cause hypercalcemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp45de9881","lv":"intern","d":"sodium-zirconium-cyclosilicate","sys":"renal","q":"Which adverse effect is most important to monitor in a patient with heart failure receiving sodium zirconium cyclosilicate?","a":"Edema from its sodium content","w":["Intestinal necrosis","Hypercalcemia","Severe constipation and bowel obstruction"],"x":"Sodium zirconium cyclosilicate exchanges sodium for potassium, and edema is a recognized adverse effect, especially in fluid-sensitive patients. Intestinal necrosis is linked to sodium polystyrene sulfonate.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4b2d6673","lv":"intern","d":"patiromer","sys":"renal","q":"A patient is started on patiromer for chronic hyperkalemia and takes several other oral medications. Which teaching point is most important?","a":"Separate patiromer from other oral medications","w":["Take patiromer with all other medications at once","Take patiromer only on an empty stomach at bedtime","Crush other medications into the patiromer suspension"],"x":"Patiromer can bind other oral drugs in the gut and reduce their absorption, so doses are spaced apart. Taking everything together is exactly what causes the interaction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1b5ff34b","lv":"intern","d":"sodium-polystyrene-sulfonate","sys":"renal","q":"Which serious adverse effect is associated with sodium polystyrene sulfonate?","a":"Intestinal necrosis","w":["Severe hypercalcemia","Acute pancreatitis","Hemorrhagic cystitis"],"x":"Sodium polystyrene sulfonate has been linked to colonic necrosis, particularly with sorbitol or in patients with bowel dysfunction. It does not cause hypercalcemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbb254473","lv":"intern","d":"potassium-chloride","sys":"renal","q":"A patient's hypokalemia does not improve despite repeated potassium chloride replacement. Which other electrolyte should be checked and corrected?","a":"Magnesium","w":["Phosphate","Calcium","Chloride"],"x":"Low magnesium increases renal potassium wasting, so hypokalemia is often refractory until magnesium is repleted. Phosphate and calcium do not have this direct effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5d901b70","lv":"intern","d":"hypertonic-saline-3-percent","sys":"renal","q":"When hypertonic saline is used for symptomatic chronic hyponatremia, what is the main risk of correcting sodium too quickly?","a":"Osmotic demyelination syndrome","w":["Cerebral edema from water shifting into neurons","Severe hypokalemia from sodium loading","Acute kidney injury from tubular necrosis"],"x":"Brain cells adapt to chronic hyponatremia, and overly rapid correction pulls water out of cells and damages myelin. Cerebral edema is the danger of hyponatremia itself or overly rapid lowering of sodium, not of correction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp17a8cd71","lv":"intern","d":"normal-saline","sys":"renal","q":"A patient receives large volumes of 0.9% sodium chloride during resuscitation. Which acid-base disorder is most likely?","a":"Hyperchloremic non-anion gap metabolic acidosis","w":["High anion gap metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"x":"Normal saline has more chloride than plasma, and large volumes lower bicarbonate, causing a hyperchloremic acidosis with a normal anion gap. It does not add unmeasured anions.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp31346988","lv":"intern","d":"desmopressin","sys":"renal","q":"An older patient taking desmopressin for nocturia is admitted with confusion and nausea. Which lab abnormality is most likely?","a":"Hyponatremia","w":["Hypernatremia","Hyperkalemia","Hypercalcemia"],"x":"Desmopressin is a V2 agonist that increases free water reabsorption and can cause hyponatremia, especially in older adults. Hypernatremia is what it is used to prevent in diabetes insipidus.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb6869df9","lv":"intern","d":"nitrofurantoin","sys":"renal","q":"Why is nitrofurantoin a poor choice for cystitis in a patient with markedly reduced kidney function?","a":"Urine levels are inadequate while systemic toxicity rises","w":["It is converted to a nephrotoxin by the liver","It causes severe hyperkalemia in kidney disease","It is only active in alkaline urine"],"x":"Nitrofurantoin needs adequate kidney function to reach effective urine concentrations, and accumulation raises the risk of neuropathy and other toxicity. Hyperkalemia is a trimethoprim concern, not nitrofurantoin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp06c17752","lv":"intern","d":"allopurinol","sys":"bone","q":"A patient taking azathioprine after a transplant is started on allopurinol for gout. What is the main risk?","a":"Severe myelosuppression from reduced azathioprine breakdown","w":["Gout flares from rapid uric acid lowering only","Transplant rejection from lower azathioprine levels","Kidney stones from increased uric acid excretion"],"x":"Allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase, which inactivates azathioprine and mercaptopurine, so levels rise and can cause life-threatening marrow suppression. Azathioprine levels rise, not fall.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp542814ec","lv":"intern","d":"allopurinol","sys":"bone","q":"Before starting allopurinol in a patient of Korean descent with chronic kidney disease, which test is recommended?","a":"HLA-B*58:01 to assess risk of severe skin reactions","w":["HLA-B*15:02 to assess risk of severe skin reactions","G6PD activity to assess risk of hemolysis","TPMT activity to assess risk of myelosuppression"],"x":"HLA-B*58:01 is strongly linked to allopurinol hypersensitivity syndrome, SJS, and TEN and is more common in some Asian and African American populations. HLA-B*15:02 is the marker used for carbamazepine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0047a9f9","lv":"intern","d":"colchicine","sys":"bone","q":"A patient on colchicine for gout is started on clarithromycin and develops diarrhea, weakness, and low blood counts. What is the most likely cause?","a":"Colchicine toxicity from CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein inhibition","w":["Clostridioides difficile infection from clarithromycin alone","Allergic reaction to clarithromycin","Colchicine levels falling below the effective range"],"x":"Clarithromycin strongly inhibits CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, raising colchicine to toxic levels that affect the GI tract, muscles, and marrow. Low counts are not explained by C. difficile.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc0701635","lv":"intern","d":"colchicine","sys":"bone","q":"An older patient with chronic kidney disease on long-term colchicine develops proximal muscle weakness and an elevated CK. What should be recognized?","a":"Colchicine-induced neuromyopathy from accumulation","w":["Polymyalgia rheumatica unrelated to medications","Expected gout progression","Hypothyroidism from colchicine"],"x":"Colchicine is partly renally cleared, and accumulation in CKD can cause myopathy and neuropathy, especially with statins or interacting drugs. Polymyalgia rheumatica does not raise the CK.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp17d56a50","lv":"intern","d":"febuxostat","sys":"bone","q":"Which boxed warning applies to febuxostat?","a":"Higher cardiovascular death in patients with established cardiovascular disease","w":["Severe hypersensitivity linked to HLA-B*58:01","Fatal hemolysis in G6PD deficiency","Osteonecrosis of the jaw"],"x":"In a trial against allopurinol, febuxostat was associated with higher cardiovascular death in patients with known cardiovascular disease. HLA-B*58:01 hypersensitivity is an allopurinol concern.","pilot":1},{"id":"qped1a61c6","lv":"intern","d":"rasburicase","sys":"bone","q":"Before giving rasburicase for tumor lysis syndrome, which condition must be screened for?","a":"G6PD deficiency","w":["TPMT deficiency","HLA-B*58:01 carrier status","Pseudocholinesterase deficiency"],"x":"Rasburicase generates hydrogen peroxide while breaking down uric acid, which can cause severe hemolysis and methemoglobinemia in G6PD deficiency. TPMT affects thiopurine drugs.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp16b54f92","lv":"intern","d":"alendronate","sys":"bone","q":"Which teaching point is most important for a patient starting oral alendronate?","a":"Take it with plain water on an empty stomach and stay upright afterward","w":["Take it with milk and a meal to protect the stomach","Take it at bedtime while lying down","Take it with calcium and iron for better effect"],"x":"Alendronate is poorly absorbed and can cause esophageal injury, so it is taken fasting with plain water while remaining upright. Food, calcium, and iron block its absorption.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp424fd899","lv":"intern","d":"zoledronic-acid","sys":"bone","q":"Before IV zoledronic acid is given, which assessment is most important?","a":"Kidney function and calcium status","w":["Thyroid function and lipid panel","Liver enzymes and ammonia","Platelet count and INR"],"x":"Zoledronic acid can cause kidney injury and hypocalcemia, so renal function is checked and low calcium or vitamin D is corrected first. Liver tests are not the main concern.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp66332dcf","lv":"intern","d":"denosumab","sys":"bone","q":"A patient with advanced chronic kidney disease receives denosumab. Which adverse effect is of greatest concern?","a":"Severe hypocalcemia","w":["Severe hypercalcemia","Hyperkalemia","Acute kidney injury from the drug itself"],"x":"Denosumab blocks RANKL and bone resorption, and patients with advanced CKD are at high risk of severe hypocalcemia. It is not cleared by the kidneys and is not directly nephrotoxic.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf12cf64a","lv":"intern","d":"calcitonin-salmon","sys":"bone","q":"In severe hypercalcemia of malignancy, what is the role of calcitonin?","a":"Rapid but short-lived calcium lowering due to tachyphylaxis","w":["Slow but durable calcium lowering over weeks","Calcium lowering by increasing gut absorption","Permanent cure by blocking tumor PTHrP release"],"x":"Calcitonin lowers calcium within hours, but its effect fades within a few days, so it is paired with IV fluids and a bisphosphonate for durable control. Its benefit is speed, not durability.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp03166dba","lv":"intern","d":"n-acetylcysteine","sys":"tox","q":"How does N-acetylcysteine protect the liver after an acetaminophen overdose?","a":"It replenishes glutathione to detoxify the toxic metabolite","w":["It blocks acetaminophen absorption in the gut","It inhibits CYP2E1 permanently","It increases renal excretion of acetaminophen"],"x":"NAC restores glutathione, which binds NAPQI, the toxic metabolite of acetaminophen. Activated charcoal, not NAC, is what limits gut absorption.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp90c2c035","lv":"intern","d":"fomepizole","sys":"tox","q":"How does fomepizole treat ethylene glycol or methanol poisoning?","a":"It inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase, blocking toxic metabolite formation","w":["It binds the alcohol directly in the bloodstream","It speeds conversion to the toxic metabolites","It increases renal excretion of the toxic acids"],"x":"Fomepizole competitively inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase so ethylene glycol and methanol are not converted to toxic acids. Hemodialysis, not fomepizole, removes the toxins and acids.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfa2d901d","lv":"intern","d":"digoxin-immune-fab","sys":"tox","q":"In acute digoxin overdose, which finding is an indication for digoxin immune Fab?","a":"A life-threatening arrhythmia or marked hyperkalemia","w":["Mild nausea with a normal ECG","Isolated first-degree AV block","A single loose stool"],"x":"Digoxin Fab is indicated for life-threatening arrhythmias, significant hyperkalemia from acute toxicity, or end-organ effects. Mild symptoms with a normal ECG are usually observed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7c492c39","lv":"intern","d":"sodium-bicarbonate","sys":"tox","q":"A patient with a tricyclic antidepressant overdose has a widened QRS on ECG. Which treatment is first-line?","a":"IV sodium bicarbonate","w":["IV procainamide","IV physostigmine","IV flumazenil"],"x":"Sodium loading and alkalinization overcome tricyclic sodium channel blockade and narrow the QRS. Procainamide is also a sodium channel blocker, and physostigmine can cause asystole in tricyclic toxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7215d495","lv":"intern","d":"octreotide","sys":"tox","q":"A patient with sulfonylurea overdose keeps becoming hypoglycemic despite dextrose. How does octreotide help?","a":"It inhibits insulin release from the pancreas","w":["It increases hepatic glucose output directly","It blocks sulfonylurea absorption in the gut","It increases renal excretion of the sulfonylurea"],"x":"Sulfonylureas trigger insulin release, and dextrose can drive even more; octreotide suppresses that release and prevents recurrent hypoglycemia. Glucagon, not octreotide, raises hepatic glucose output.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd21d6b52","lv":"intern","d":"glucagon","sys":"tox","q":"How does glucagon help in beta-blocker overdose with bradycardia and hypotension?","a":"It raises cardiac cAMP through a non-beta receptor pathway","w":["It directly stimulates beta-1 receptors","It blocks calcium channels in the heart","It increases vagal tone to the heart"],"x":"Glucagon activates its own receptor on cardiac cells, raising cAMP and contractility even when beta receptors are blocked. It does not act on beta receptors itself.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpaa37ea56","lv":"intern","d":"methylene-blue","sys":"tox","q":"A patient taking an SSRI needs methylene blue for methemoglobinemia. Which risk should the team anticipate?","a":"Serotonin syndrome from its MAO inhibition","w":["Hypertensive crisis from tyramine release","Severe hypoglycemia from insulin release","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome"],"x":"Methylene blue is a potent MAO-A inhibitor and can cause serotonin syndrome with serotonergic drugs. It can also cause hemolysis in G6PD deficiency.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe6968597","lv":"intern","d":"hydroxocobalamin","sys":"tox","q":"A smoke-inhalation patient with suspected cyanide poisoning receives hydroxocobalamin. How does it work?","a":"It binds cyanide to form cyanocobalamin, which is excreted","w":["It induces methemoglobin, which binds cyanide","It speeds conversion of cyanide to thiocyanate","It displaces carbon monoxide from hemoglobin"],"x":"Hydroxocobalamin combines with cyanide to form cyanocobalamin, which the kidneys excrete. Inducing methemoglobin is how nitrites work, which is risky in smoke inhalation with carbon monoxide.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa55a5b26","lv":"intern","d":"physostigmine","sys":"tox","q":"Before giving physostigmine for suspected anticholinergic delirium, which finding should be ruled out?","a":"QRS widening suggesting tricyclic antidepressant toxicity","w":["Dilated pupils on exam","Dry, flushed skin","Mild sinus tachycardia"],"x":"Physostigmine can cause bradycardia and asystole in tricyclic toxicity, so a widened QRS is a reason to avoid it. Dilated pupils, dry skin, and tachycardia are expected features of anticholinergic toxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpefe83cc1","lv":"intern","d":"phytonadione","sys":"tox","q":"Which route of vitamin K is least reliable and generally not recommended for reversing warfarin?","a":"Subcutaneous","w":["Oral","Intravenous","Through a feeding tube"],"x":"Subcutaneous vitamin K is absorbed unpredictably, so oral is used for non-urgent correction and IV for serious bleeding. IV is reliable and fast but carries a small risk of anaphylactoid reactions.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc79c8a36","lv":"intern","d":"vancomycin","sys":"infect","q":"Combining IV vancomycin with piperacillin-tazobactam is associated with which problem?","a":"Higher rates of acute kidney injury","w":["Higher rates of hearing loss only","Lower rates of kidney injury than vancomycin alone","Severe liver injury in most patients"],"x":"Observational data show more AKI with vancomycin plus piperacillin-tazobactam than with vancomycin plus other beta-lactams, so kidney function is followed closely. The combination does not protect the kidneys.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp870fc15f","lv":"intern","d":"vancomycin","sys":"infect","q":"For treatment of Clostridioides difficile infection, how must vancomycin be given?","a":"Orally, because IV vancomycin does not reach the colon","w":["Intravenously, because oral vancomycin is not absorbed","Intramuscularly into the gluteal muscle","Topically as a rectal cream"],"x":"Oral vancomycin stays in the gut lumen where C. difficile lives; IV vancomycin is not excreted into the colon. Poor oral absorption is exactly why the oral route works for this infection.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd0d474d3","lv":"intern","d":"cefepime","sys":"infect","q":"A patient with kidney injury on cefepime becomes confused with myoclonus. What should be recognized?","a":"Cefepime neurotoxicity from accumulation","w":["Expected sepsis-associated delirium only","Cefepime-induced hypoglycemia","Serotonin syndrome from cefepime"],"x":"Cefepime is renally cleared and can accumulate, causing encephalopathy, myoclonus, and nonconvulsive seizures. Unadjusted dosing in kidney injury is the classic setup.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3f8539b9","lv":"intern","d":"ceftriaxone","sys":"infect","q":"Which property makes ceftriaxone convenient in patients with kidney failure?","a":"Dual biliary and renal elimination, so routine renal adjustment is not needed","w":["Complete renal elimination, requiring dialysis dosing","No elimination by any route, so it lasts weeks","Elimination only through the lungs"],"x":"Ceftriaxone is eliminated through both bile and urine, so it generally does not need renal adjustment. Most other cephalosporins such as cefepime do require adjustment.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8336e84f","lv":"intern","d":"daptomycin","sys":"infect","q":"Why is daptomycin not used to treat pneumonia?","a":"It is inactivated by pulmonary surfactant","w":["It does not cover Staphylococcus aureus","It causes bronchospasm when given IV","It is only available as an oral tablet"],"x":"Surfactant binds and inactivates daptomycin in the alveoli, so it fails in pneumonia despite good activity against S. aureus. That activity makes it useful for bacteremia and skin infections.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4656ff1a","lv":"intern","d":"linezolid","sys":"infect","q":"A patient will receive linezolid for several weeks. Which lab should be monitored regularly?","a":"Complete blood count for thrombocytopenia","w":["Serum creatinine for nephrotoxicity","Thyroid function for hypothyroidism","Serum uric acid for gout"],"x":"Linezolid can cause myelosuppression, especially thrombocytopenia, with longer courses, so the CBC is monitored. It does not need renal adjustment and is not typically nephrotoxic.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb5491b47","lv":"intern","d":"trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","sys":"infect","q":"A patient on lisinopril and spironolactone is started on trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. Which risk is most important?","a":"Hyperkalemia from trimethoprim's amiloride-like effect","w":["Hypokalemia from increased potassium excretion","Hypoglycemia from increased insulin release","Hypercalcemia from increased bone resorption"],"x":"Trimethoprim blocks sodium channels in the collecting duct like amiloride, reducing potassium excretion; with an ACE inhibitor and spironolactone, hyperkalemia can be severe. TMP-SMX can cause hypoglycemia, but hyperkalemia is the key danger here.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa2f15342","lv":"intern","d":"gentamicin","sys":"infect","q":"Which toxicities are most important to monitor with gentamicin?","a":"Nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity","w":["Hepatotoxicity and pancreatitis","Cardiotoxicity and QT prolongation","Myelosuppression and hemolysis"],"x":"Aminoglycosides damage the proximal tubule and the inner ear, so kidney function and drug levels are followed and hearing or balance changes are reported. Liver toxicity is not a main concern.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp11adcce4","lv":"intern","d":"levofloxacin","sys":"infect","q":"An older patient taking prednisone is prescribed levofloxacin. Which adverse effect is of particular concern?","a":"Tendinitis and tendon rupture","w":["Hemorrhagic cystitis","Pulmonary fibrosis","Gingival overgrowth"],"x":"Fluoroquinolones carry a boxed warning for tendinitis and tendon rupture, and the risk is higher in older adults and with corticosteroids. Patients should report heel or tendon pain.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1f979120","lv":"intern","d":"acyclovir","sys":"infect","q":"A patient receiving high-dose IV acyclovir for HSV encephalitis develops a rising creatinine. What is the likely mechanism?","a":"Crystal nephropathy from drug precipitating in the tubules","w":["Immune-mediated glomerulonephritis","Prerenal injury from vasoconstriction","Rhabdomyolysis from muscle toxicity"],"x":"IV acyclovir can crystallize in renal tubules, especially with dehydration, so adequate hydration and renal monitoring are important. Glomerulonephritis is not the typical mechanism.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5442fee9","lv":"intern","d":"metformin","sys":"endo","q":"A patient on metformin is admitted with sepsis and acute kidney injury. Which complication is the main concern?","a":"Lactic acidosis from metformin accumulation","w":["Hypoglycemia from increased insulin secretion","Diabetic ketoacidosis from insulin deficiency","Hyperkalemia from aldosterone blockade"],"x":"Metformin is renally cleared, and AKI plus hypoperfusion increases the risk of lactic acidosis. Metformin alone rarely causes hypoglycemia because it does not stimulate insulin secretion.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfb8ecda7","lv":"intern","d":"empagliflozin","sys":"endo","q":"A patient on empagliflozin is admitted after surgery with nausea, an anion gap acidosis, and a glucose only mildly elevated. What should be recognized?","a":"Euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis","w":["Lactic acidosis from tissue hypoperfusion","Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state","Starvation ketosis without acidosis"],"x":"SGLT2 inhibitors can cause DKA with near-normal glucose, particularly around surgery, illness, or reduced intake. Hyperosmolar state involves very high glucose without significant ketoacidosis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf6938b4b","lv":"intern","d":"glyburide","sys":"endo","q":"Compared with glipizide, why is glyburide riskier in a patient with chronic kidney disease?","a":"Its active metabolites are renally cleared, prolonging hypoglycemia","w":["It does not stimulate insulin release","It causes more weight loss than glipizide","It is cleared only by the kidneys unchanged"],"x":"Glyburide has long-acting active metabolites that accumulate in CKD and cause prolonged hypoglycemia. Glipizide's metabolites are inactive, making it the safer sulfonylurea in CKD.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbe071067","lv":"intern","d":"insulin-glargine","sys":"endo","q":"A hospitalized patient with type 2 diabetes has persistently high glucose and is eating. Which regimen is preferred?","a":"Scheduled basal insulin with prandial and correction insulin","w":["Correction-only sliding scale insulin","Starting a new sulfonylurea","Oral metformin despite kidney injury"],"x":"Inpatient guidelines favor scheduled basal insulin, plus prandial and correction doses when eating, over sliding scale alone, which reacts to highs and lows rather than preventing them.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp06fbf106","lv":"intern","d":"regular-insulin","sys":"endo","q":"Before starting an IV insulin infusion for diabetic ketoacidosis, which lab must be checked?","a":"Serum potassium","w":["Serum magnesium","Serum lipase","Serum calcium"],"x":"Insulin drives potassium into cells, and DKA patients often have total body potassium depletion; if potassium is low, it must be replaced first to avoid dangerous hypokalemia. Magnesium matters but is not the key gating lab.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8169fdef","lv":"intern","d":"levothyroxine","sys":"endo","q":"A patient's medication list includes levothyroxine and calcium carbonate, taken together each morning. Which counseling point applies?","a":"Separate the doses because calcium reduces levothyroxine absorption","w":["Take them together because calcium improves absorption","Take levothyroxine only with a high-fat meal","Take calcium before bed with levothyroxine"],"x":"Calcium, iron, and some binders reduce levothyroxine absorption, so doses are spaced apart and levothyroxine is taken on an empty stomach. Food reduces, rather than improves, absorption.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp40463019","lv":"intern","d":"hydrocortisone","sys":"endo","q":"A patient on long-term prednisone is admitted with vomiting, confusion, and hypotension that does not respond to fluids. What should be recognized?","a":"Adrenal crisis from a suppressed HPA axis","w":["Primary hyperaldosteronism","Thyroid storm","Pheochromocytoma crisis"],"x":"Long-term glucocorticoids suppress the HPA axis, and illness, vomiting, or missed doses can cause adrenal crisis. It is treated with stress-dose IV hydrocortisone and fluids.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpee5c83c1","lv":"intern","d":"propylthiouracil","sys":"endo","q":"In thyroid storm, why is propylthiouracil often preferred over methimazole?","a":"It also blocks peripheral conversion of T4 to T3","w":["It is safer for the liver than methimazole","It works by destroying thyroid tissue","It has a much longer duration of action"],"x":"Both drugs block thyroid hormone synthesis, but PTU also inhibits peripheral conversion of T4 to active T3. PTU carries a boxed warning for severe liver injury, so it is not safer for the liver.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp14f758ef","lv":"intern","d":"methimazole","sys":"endo","q":"A patient on methimazole presents with fever and a sore throat. Which lab is most urgent?","a":"Complete blood count with differential","w":["Thyroid-stimulating hormone","Serum calcium","Hemoglobin A1c"],"x":"Methimazole can cause agranulocytosis, and fever or sore throat may be the first sign, so neutrophil counts are checked right away. The TSH does not identify this emergency.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6c833294","lv":"intern","d":"semaglutide","sys":"endo","q":"A patient on weekly semaglutide is admitted with severe epigastric pain radiating to the back and vomiting. What should be considered?","a":"Acute pancreatitis","w":["Diabetic ketoacidosis from insulin excess","Hypoglycemic seizure","Thyroid storm"],"x":"GLP-1 receptor agonists have been associated with acute pancreatitis, which presents with epigastric pain radiating to the back. The finding should be reported to the provider and a lipase checked; DKA would not be caused by insulin excess.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe9d779e5","lv":"intern","d":"ondansetron","sys":"gi","q":"Which adverse effect should be considered before giving IV ondansetron to a patient already on several QT-prolonging drugs?","a":"QT prolongation","w":["Tardive dyskinesia","Hemorrhagic cystitis","Agranulocytosis"],"x":"Ondansetron prolongs the QT interval in a dose-related way, and the risk adds up with other QT-prolonging drugs and electrolyte problems. Tardive dyskinesia is a concern with metoclopramide.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc13347b2","lv":"intern","d":"metoclopramide","sys":"gi","q":"A patient with Parkinson disease has postoperative nausea. Why is metoclopramide a poor choice?","a":"Its dopamine blockade can worsen parkinsonism","w":["It causes severe constipation","It strongly prolongs the QT interval more than any antiemetic","It blocks serotonin receptors in the gut"],"x":"Metoclopramide blocks central D2 receptors and can worsen parkinsonism or cause other extrapyramidal effects, including tardive dyskinesia with prolonged use. It promotes, rather than slows, GI motility.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2dcd26a3","lv":"intern","d":"pantoprazole","sys":"gi","q":"Which ICU patient has an established indication for stress ulcer prophylaxis?","a":"A mechanically ventilated patient with coagulopathy","w":["A ward patient with community-acquired pneumonia","A stable patient admitted for cellulitis","A postoperative patient eating a regular diet"],"x":"Prolonged mechanical ventilation and coagulopathy are major risk factors for stress-related GI bleeding. Routine prophylaxis in low-risk ward patients increases risks such as C. difficile without clear benefit.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0984c97d","lv":"intern","d":"pantoprazole","sys":"gi","q":"How does IV pantoprazole reduce acid secretion in upper GI bleeding?","a":"It irreversibly inhibits the parietal cell H+/K+ ATPase","w":["It blocks histamine H2 receptors on parietal cells","It coats the ulcer base with a protective barrier","It neutralizes acid already in the stomach"],"x":"Proton pump inhibitors bind the H+/K+ ATPase covalently, raising gastric pH and helping clot stability. H2 receptor blockade describes famotidine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp291ca628","lv":"intern","d":"lactulose","sys":"gi","q":"A patient with cirrhosis is on lactulose for hepatic encephalopathy. What is the usual titration target?","a":"Two to three soft stools per day","w":["One formed stool every other day","Six or more watery stools per day","No stool output until confusion resolves"],"x":"Lactulose is titrated to two to three soft stools daily to reduce ammonia absorption. Excessive diarrhea can cause dehydration and electrolyte problems that worsen encephalopathy.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp41a74fae","lv":"intern","d":"loperamide","sys":"gi","q":"In which situation is loperamide inappropriate for diarrhea?","a":"Suspected C. difficile infection or bloody invasive diarrhea","w":["Chronic diarrhea after a confirmed negative workup","Traveler's diarrhea without fever or blood","Diarrhea from irritable bowel syndrome"],"x":"Slowing gut motility in C. difficile or invasive infection can trap toxins and organisms, raising the risk of toxic megacolon. Uncomplicated noninfectious or mild traveler's diarrhea is an appropriate use.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd38c2c7e","lv":"intern","d":"promethazine","sys":"gi","q":"Which serious risk is associated with IV promethazine?","a":"Severe tissue injury and gangrene if it extravasates","w":["Hemorrhagic cystitis","Pulmonary fibrosis","Severe hypercalcemia"],"x":"Promethazine carries a boxed warning for severe tissue injury, including gangrene, with perivascular extravasation or intra-arterial injection. Deep IM injection is the preferred parenteral route.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp104cd969","lv":"intern","d":"sucralfate","sys":"gi","q":"A patient takes sucralfate and is prescribed oral levofloxacin. What is the concern?","a":"Sucralfate reduces levofloxacin absorption","w":["Sucralfate raises levofloxacin to toxic levels","Levofloxacin dissolves the sucralfate coating","The two drugs cause severe hyperkalemia"],"x":"Sucralfate contains aluminum, which binds fluoroquinolones in the gut and lowers their absorption, so doses are separated. It does not raise fluoroquinolone levels.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa9c8ce5d","lv":"intern","d":"famotidine","sys":"gi","q":"An older patient with chronic kidney disease on IV famotidine becomes confused. What should be considered?","a":"CNS effects from famotidine accumulation","w":["Hepatic encephalopathy from famotidine","Famotidine-induced hypoglycemia","Serotonin syndrome from famotidine"],"x":"Famotidine is renally cleared, and accumulation in CKD or older age can cause confusion and delirium. It does not cause hepatic encephalopathy.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5380c5c0","lv":"intern","d":"misoprostol","sys":"gi","q":"Misoprostol is prescribed to prevent NSAID-induced ulcers in a patient of reproductive potential. Which counseling point is essential?","a":"It can cause miscarriage, so pregnancy must be avoided","w":["It causes severe constipation in most patients","It increases the risk of tendon rupture","It must be taken with antacids to work"],"x":"Misoprostol is a prostaglandin E1 analog that can cause uterine contractions and pregnancy loss, so it is contraindicated in pregnancy for ulcer prevention. Its common GI side effect is diarrhea, not constipation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp895e8bf2","lv":"intern","d":"magnesium-sulfate","sys":"ob","q":"A patient with preeclampsia is receiving IV magnesium sulfate. Which finding is typically the earliest sign of magnesium toxicity?","a":"Loss of deep tendon reflexes","w":["Respiratory arrest","Cardiac arrest","Seizure activity"],"x":"Reflexes fade before respiratory depression and cardiac effects, so reflexes, breathing, and urine output are checked regularly. Seizures are what magnesium prevents, not a sign of toxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp540a0ac7","lv":"intern","d":"calcium-gluconate","sys":"ob","q":"A patient receiving magnesium sulfate for preeclampsia develops respiratory depression. Which agent is used to counteract magnesium?","a":"IV calcium gluconate","w":["IV naloxone","IV flumazenil","IV sodium bicarbonate"],"x":"Calcium antagonizes the neuromuscular and cardiac effects of magnesium. Naloxone reverses opioids and has no effect on magnesium toxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpefcf4792","lv":"intern","d":"methylergonovine","sys":"ob","q":"For postpartum hemorrhage from uterine atony, methylergonovine is contraindicated in which patient?","a":"A patient with preeclampsia or hypertension","w":["A patient with asthma","A patient with group B strep colonization","A patient with gestational diabetes"],"x":"Methylergonovine causes vasoconstriction and can trigger severe hypertension, stroke, or seizures in hypertensive patients. Asthma is the main contraindication for carboprost, not methylergonovine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf75443e6","lv":"intern","d":"carboprost","sys":"ob","q":"For postpartum hemorrhage, carboprost should be avoided in which patient?","a":"A patient with asthma","w":["A patient with chronic hypertension","A patient with gestational diabetes","A patient with hypothyroidism"],"x":"Carboprost is a prostaglandin F2-alpha analog that can cause bronchospasm, so asthma is a key contraindication. Hypertension is the main contraindication for methylergonovine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4d1753dc","lv":"intern","d":"labetalol","sys":"ob","q":"Which IV medication is a first-line option for acute severe hypertension in pregnancy?","a":"Labetalol","w":["Nitroprusside","Enalaprilat","Esmolol"],"x":"IV labetalol, IV hydralazine, and oral immediate-release nifedipine are first-line for severe hypertension in pregnancy. ACE inhibitors such as enalaprilat are contraindicated because of fetal harm.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp66f132fd","lv":"intern","d":"betamethasone","sys":"ob","q":"Betamethasone is given to a patient at risk of preterm delivery. What is the main purpose?","a":"Accelerate fetal lung maturity","w":["Stop uterine contractions","Prevent maternal seizures","Prevent neonatal group B strep infection"],"x":"Antenatal corticosteroids speed fetal lung development and reduce respiratory distress and other neonatal complications. They are not tocolytics.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5ba8d2ce","lv":"intern","d":"rho-d-immune-globulin","sys":"ob","q":"An RhD-negative patient delivers an RhD-positive infant. What is the purpose of Rho(D) immune globulin?","a":"Prevent maternal alloimmunization against fetal RhD","w":["Treat hemolytic disease in the newborn","Prevent postpartum hemorrhage","Boost the newborn's immune response"],"x":"Rho(D) immune globulin clears fetal RhD-positive cells from the mother's blood before she makes anti-D antibodies, protecting future pregnancies. It is given to the mother, not used to treat the newborn.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa6894867","lv":"intern","d":"oxytocin","sys":"ob","q":"Which adverse effect can occur with prolonged high-dose oxytocin infusion with large volumes of IV fluid?","a":"Hyponatremia from its antidiuretic effect","w":["Hypernatremia from sodium retention","Hyperkalemia from cell lysis","Hypercalcemia from bone resorption"],"x":"Oxytocin is structurally similar to vasopressin and can cause water retention and hyponatremia, which may lead to seizures. Uterine tachysystole is another key risk to monitor.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4123c74a","lv":"intern","d":"tranexamic-acid","sys":"ob","q":"Tranexamic acid is given early in postpartum hemorrhage. How does it work?","a":"It blocks plasminogen activation to reduce fibrinolysis","w":["It increases uterine muscle contraction","It replaces fibrinogen and clotting factors","It activates platelets directly"],"x":"Tranexamic acid is an antifibrinolytic that blocks plasminogen's conversion to plasmin, stabilizing clots. It does not contract the uterus, so uterotonics are still needed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa11636d3","lv":"intern","d":"penicillin-g","sys":"ob","q":"Why is IV penicillin G given during labor to a patient colonized with group B streptococcus?","a":"Prevent early-onset GBS infection in the newborn","w":["Treat maternal urinary tract infection","Prevent postpartum endometritis only","Speed cervical ripening"],"x":"Intrapartum penicillin reduces transmission of GBS to the newborn and prevents early-onset sepsis. It is not given to treat or prevent maternal infection.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp094b8bdf","lv":"intern","d":"cyclosporine","sys":"immune","q":"A transplant patient on cyclosporine is started on rifampin. What is the main concern?","a":"Lower cyclosporine levels and risk of rejection","w":["Higher cyclosporine levels and nephrotoxicity","Severe hyperkalemia from the combination","No interaction because they use different pathways"],"x":"Rifampin strongly induces CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, lowering cyclosporine levels and raising the risk of rejection. Levels are monitored closely and adjusted.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8a217dcf","lv":"intern","d":"tacrolimus","sys":"immune","q":"A transplant patient on tacrolimus is started on fluconazole. Which effect should the team anticipate?","a":"Higher tacrolimus levels with nephrotoxicity and tremor","w":["Lower tacrolimus levels with rejection","No change in tacrolimus levels","Lower fluconazole levels with fungal relapse"],"x":"Azole antifungals inhibit CYP3A4, raising tacrolimus levels and the risk of kidney injury, tremor, hyperkalemia, and hyperglycemia. Rejection would be the concern with an inducer, not an inhibitor.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdc3c2d80","lv":"intern","d":"methotrexate","sys":"immune","q":"A patient on weekly low-dose methotrexate for rheumatoid arthritis is given trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. What is the main risk?","a":"Pancytopenia from additive antifolate effects","w":["Loss of methotrexate efficacy","Severe hypoglycemia","Acute hepatic failure within hours"],"x":"Trimethoprim also inhibits dihydrofolate reductase and sulfamethoxazole reduces methotrexate clearance, so marrow toxicity can be severe. This combination is generally avoided.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf81098b2","lv":"intern","d":"azathioprine","sys":"immune","q":"Before starting azathioprine, which testing helps identify patients at high risk of severe myelosuppression?","a":"TPMT and NUDT15 activity or genotype","w":["HLA-B*58:01 genotype","G6PD activity","CYP2C19 genotype"],"x":"Low TPMT or NUDT15 activity causes thiopurine metabolites to build up and can cause life-threatening marrow suppression. HLA-B*58:01 relates to allopurinol hypersensitivity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6b02780e","lv":"intern","d":"infliximab","sys":"immune","q":"Before starting infliximab, which screening is required?","a":"Testing for latent tuberculosis","w":["Testing for HLA-B*15:02","Baseline pulmonary function tests","Baseline bone density scan"],"x":"TNF inhibitors raise the risk of reactivating latent TB, so screening is required and latent TB is treated before or during therapy. Hepatitis B screening is also recommended.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7f8d4746","lv":"intern","d":"rituximab","sys":"immune","q":"Before rituximab is given, which screening is required because of a boxed warning?","a":"Hepatitis B surface antigen and core antibody","w":["Thyroid function tests","Hemoglobin A1c","Urine drug screen"],"x":"Rituximab can reactivate hepatitis B, causing fulminant hepatitis, so HBsAg and anti-HBc are checked before treatment. The other tests are not tied to a boxed warning.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1e9d653c","lv":"intern","d":"mycophenolate","sys":"immune","q":"Which counseling point is essential for a patient of reproductive potential starting mycophenolate?","a":"It causes birth defects and pregnancy loss, so reliable contraception is required","w":["It causes hyperthyroidism, so thyroid tests are needed monthly","It causes tendon rupture, so exercise must be limited","It causes photosensitivity only, so sunscreen is enough"],"x":"Mycophenolate carries a boxed warning for embryofetal toxicity, including miscarriage and congenital malformations. Pregnancy testing and contraception are required.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf8235b08","lv":"intern","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"immune","q":"Why is diphenhydramine a poor choice as a hospital sleep aid for an older adult?","a":"Its anticholinergic effects contribute to delirium and urinary retention","w":["It causes severe hypertension in older adults","It is ineffective at causing drowsiness","It causes hyperkalemia in older adults"],"x":"Diphenhydramine is strongly anticholinergic and is on the Beers list because it causes confusion, constipation, and urinary retention in older adults. It does cause drowsiness, which is why it is often misused for sleep.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp84ff7752","lv":"intern","d":"pembrolizumab","sys":"immune","q":"A patient on pembrolizumab develops several days of watery diarrhea and abdominal pain. What should be recognized?","a":"Immune-mediated colitis","w":["Expected tumor response","Lactose intolerance unrelated to therapy","Neutropenic fever"],"x":"Checkpoint inhibitors can cause immune-related adverse events, and colitis is a common one that may need corticosteroids. It should be reported promptly rather than treated as routine diarrhea.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp30ec62c9","lv":"intern","d":"hydroxychloroquine","sys":"immune","q":"Which monitoring is recommended for a patient on long-term hydroxychloroquine?","a":"Baseline and periodic eye exams for retinal toxicity","w":["Monthly liver biopsies","Periodic bone marrow biopsies","Annual pulmonary function tests only"],"x":"Hydroxychloroquine can cause irreversible retinopathy, so screening exams are done at baseline and periodically. It can also prolong the QT interval.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf4ffc553","lv":"intern","d":"cisplatin","sys":"onc","q":"Which toxicity is most important to prevent and monitor with cisplatin?","a":"Nephrotoxicity with magnesium wasting","w":["Hemorrhagic cystitis","Cardiomyopathy","Pulmonary fibrosis"],"x":"Cisplatin damages renal tubules, so aggressive hydration and monitoring of creatinine and magnesium are standard. Hemorrhagic cystitis is linked to cyclophosphamide and ifosfamide.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp88bb28b3","lv":"intern","d":"doxorubicin","sys":"onc","q":"Before doxorubicin is started, which baseline study is most important?","a":"Assessment of left ventricular ejection fraction","w":["Pulmonary function testing","Audiometry","Bone density scan"],"x":"Doxorubicin causes cumulative, dose-related cardiomyopathy, so ejection fraction is checked at baseline and during treatment. Pulmonary function testing is used for bleomycin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb1fa90d5","lv":"intern","d":"cyclophosphamide","sys":"onc","q":"Which toxicity of high-dose cyclophosphamide is reduced by giving mesna and hydration?","a":"Hemorrhagic cystitis","w":["Peripheral neuropathy","Cardiomyopathy","Ototoxicity"],"x":"The metabolite acrolein irritates the bladder lining; mesna binds it in the urine. Neuropathy and ototoxicity are not prevented by mesna.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3d7c8a37","lv":"intern","d":"vincristine","sys":"onc","q":"How must vincristine be given?","a":"Intravenously only, because intrathecal use is fatal","w":["Intrathecally only, to reach the CSF","Intramuscularly into a large muscle","Subcutaneously into the abdomen"],"x":"Vincristine given intrathecally causes fatal ascending neurotoxicity, so it carries strict labeling and is often prepared in minibags. Its common dose-limiting toxicity is peripheral neuropathy.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4d910b35","lv":"intern","d":"bleomycin","sys":"onc","q":"Which toxicity is most characteristic of bleomycin?","a":"Pulmonary toxicity that can progress to fibrosis","w":["Hemorrhagic cystitis","Cumulative cardiomyopathy","Severe myelosuppression"],"x":"Bleomycin causes pneumonitis and fibrosis, so pulmonary function and new cough or dyspnea are watched closely. Unlike most chemotherapies, it causes little myelosuppression.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4df0ebb3","lv":"intern","d":"capecitabine","sys":"onc","q":"Shortly after starting capecitabine, a patient develops severe mucositis, diarrhea, and neutropenia. Which enzyme deficiency should be suspected?","a":"Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase","w":["Thiopurine methyltransferase","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase","Pseudocholinesterase"],"x":"DPD breaks down fluorouracil, the active form of capecitabine, so deficiency causes severe, sometimes fatal toxicity. TPMT deficiency affects thiopurines, not fluoropyrimidines.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp93fdd88b","lv":"intern","d":"tamoxifen","sys":"onc","q":"A patient taking tamoxifen is admitted with leg swelling. Which serious adverse effect should be considered?","a":"Venous thromboembolism","w":["Hemorrhagic cystitis","Pulmonary fibrosis","Peripheral neuropathy"],"x":"Tamoxifen has estrogen-like effects on clotting and raises the risk of DVT and PE, along with endometrial cancer. Leg swelling in this setting warrants evaluation for DVT.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp64727a49","lv":"intern","d":"leucovorin","sys":"onc","q":"What is the purpose of leucovorin after high-dose methotrexate?","a":"It bypasses dihydrofolate reductase to rescue normal cells","w":["It speeds renal excretion of methotrexate","It neutralizes methotrexate in the blood","It prevents methotrexate-induced nausea"],"x":"Leucovorin is a reduced folate that does not need DHFR, so it restores folate-dependent processes in healthy cells. It does not remove methotrexate; glucarpidase breaks down methotrexate in delayed clearance.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp47d3bf7a","lv":"intern","d":"methotrexate","sys":"onc","q":"Which factor most increases the risk of toxicity from high-dose methotrexate?","a":"Reduced kidney function","w":["Mildly elevated blood glucose","A history of hypothyroidism","Well-controlled hypertension"],"x":"Methotrexate is cleared mainly by the kidneys, so impaired renal function causes delayed clearance and severe marrow, mucosal, and kidney toxicity. Alkalinization, hydration, and level monitoring are used to protect clearance.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1c8afa44","lv":"intern","d":"irinotecan","sys":"onc","q":"During an irinotecan infusion, a patient develops cramping, diarrhea, sweating, and salivation. Which treatment is appropriate?","a":"Atropine for the acute cholinergic syndrome","w":["Loperamide for delayed diarrhea","Ondansetron for nausea","Diphenhydramine for allergy"],"x":"Irinotecan inhibits acetylcholinesterase and can cause early cholinergic symptoms that respond to atropine. Loperamide is used for the separate delayed diarrhea that starts later.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbf99a85d","lv":"intern","d":"timolol-ophthalmic","sys":"skin","q":"An older patient is admitted with symptomatic bradycardia. Which home eye drop could be contributing?","a":"Timolol ophthalmic solution","w":["Latanoprost ophthalmic solution","Artificial tear drops","Ketotifen ophthalmic solution"],"x":"Ophthalmic timolol is absorbed systemically and can cause bradycardia, heart block, and bronchospasm. Prostaglandin analogs such as latanoprost do not cause beta-blocker effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb75b7517","lv":"intern","d":"acetazolamide","sys":"skin","q":"A patient on acetazolamide for glaucoma has an abnormal chemistry panel. Which acid-base change is expected?","a":"Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis","w":["Metabolic alkalosis","High anion gap metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis"],"x":"Acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule, causing bicarbonate loss and a hyperchloremic acidosis, along with hypokalemia. It does not add unmeasured anions.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa0789ada","lv":"intern","d":"silver-sulfadiazine","sys":"skin","q":"A burn patient treated with silver sulfadiazine cream has a low white blood cell count a few days later. What should be recognized?","a":"Transient leukopenia associated with silver sulfadiazine","w":["Expected sign of burn wound sepsis only","Allergic reaction requiring epinephrine","Lab error caused by the cream"],"x":"Silver sulfadiazine is associated with a transient drop in white cells that often recovers. It should be recognized and monitored rather than assumed to be lab error.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1d82e499","lv":"intern","d":"mupirocin-nasal","sys":"skin","q":"Before certain surgeries, nasal mupirocin may be applied. What is its purpose?","a":"Decolonize the nose of Staphylococcus aureus to reduce infection","w":["Treat bacterial sinusitis","Relieve nasal congestion","Prevent viral upper respiratory infections"],"x":"Intranasal mupirocin reduces S. aureus carriage, including MRSA, which lowers surgical site and other infection risk. It is not a treatment for sinusitis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf5ea342f","lv":"intern","d":"permethrin","sys":"skin","q":"Scabies is diagnosed in a hospitalized adult. Which teaching about permethrin cream is correct?","a":"Apply to the whole body from the neck down and treat close contacts","w":["Apply only to itchy areas and wash off after a minute","Apply to the face and scalp only","Take it by mouth with food"],"x":"Permethrin is applied to all skin from the neck down, left on for the recommended time, and close contacts are treated at the same time. Treating only itchy spots misses mites elsewhere.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4e8ee27e","lv":"intern","d":"lidocaine-patch","sys":"skin","q":"Why is a topical lidocaine patch useful for localized pain in an older inpatient?","a":"It gives local pain relief with minimal systemic absorption","w":["It provides strong systemic opioid-like effects","It reduces inflammation like an oral NSAID","It is safe to apply to broken or open skin"],"x":"Lidocaine patches act locally on peripheral nerves with little systemic absorption, avoiding sedation and kidney effects. They should be applied to intact skin, not open wounds.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa4fd07f6","lv":"intern","d":"clobetasol","sys":"skin","q":"Which areas should generally be avoided when applying clobetasol, a superpotent topical steroid?","a":"The face, groin, and armpits","w":["The palms and soles","The elbows and knees","The scalp"],"x":"Thin skin absorbs more steroid and is prone to atrophy and striae, so superpotent steroids are generally avoided on the face and skin folds. Thick-skinned areas like palms and soles often need stronger steroids.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4d539ecd","lv":"intern","d":"isotretinoin","sys":"skin","q":"What is the most important safety requirement for a patient of reproductive potential prescribed isotretinoin?","a":"Pregnancy prevention under a restricted program","w":["Annual chest radiography","Weekly kidney function testing","Avoiding all dairy products"],"x":"Isotretinoin is a potent teratogen and is available only through the iPLEDGE program with pregnancy testing and contraception. The other measures are not required.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpaeb6caa3","lv":"intern","d":"proparacaine","sys":"skin","q":"Why is proparacaine eye drop not given to patients for home use?","a":"Repeated use damages the corneal epithelium and masks injury","w":["It causes systemic hypertension","It is ineffective after a single dose","It permanently dilates the pupil"],"x":"Topical ocular anesthetics delay healing and can cause corneal ulceration with repeated use, while removing pain that signals worsening injury. They are used only for brief exams and procedures.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpff4a5c49","lv":"intern","d":"nystatin-topical","sys":"skin","q":"Which skin finding in an obese inpatient is best treated with topical nystatin?","a":"Beefy red intertrigo with satellite pustules in skin folds","w":["Honey-crusted lesions on the face","Silvery plaques on the elbows","Painful vesicles in a band on one side"],"x":"Bright red rash in skin folds with satellite lesions suggests Candida, which nystatin treats. Honey-crusted lesions suggest impetigo, which needs an antibacterial.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe303ce8a","lv":"lvn","d":"lisinopril","sys":"cardio","q":"A resident taking lisinopril reports a persistent dry, hacking cough. How should the LVN interpret this?","a":"A common ACE inhibitor side effect to report to the provider","w":["An expected sign of the drug working that needs no follow-up","An early sign of digoxin toxicity to report to the provider","A symptom of low potassium that needs a dietary change"],"x":"ACE inhibitors such as lisinopril commonly cause a dry cough from bradykinin buildup, and it should be reported so the provider can decide on therapy. It is not a sign of the drug working or of low potassium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp025747f9","lv":"lvn","d":"metoprolol","sys":"cardio","q":"Before giving oral metoprolol to a resident, which assessment is most important?","a":"Apical pulse and blood pressure","w":["Blood glucose and temperature","Serum potassium and urine output","Pain level and bowel sounds"],"x":"Metoprolol is a beta blocker that slows heart rate and lowers blood pressure, so both are checked before each dose. Potassium and urine output matter more for diuretics.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb979e518","lv":"lvn","d":"warfarin","sys":"cardio","q":"Which lab value does the LVN check to monitor the effect of warfarin?","a":"International normalized ratio (INR)","w":["Activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT)","Platelet count","Serum potassium"],"x":"Warfarin is monitored with the PT/INR. The aPTT is the test used for unfractionated heparin, which makes it the most tempting wrong choice.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa402cf3e","lv":"lvn","d":"furosemide","sys":"cardio","q":"Which electrolyte imbalance is the most common concern with furosemide?","a":"Hypokalemia","w":["Hyperkalemia","Hypernatremia","Hypercalcemia"],"x":"Furosemide is a loop diuretic that causes loss of potassium in the urine, so low potassium is the classic concern. High potassium is a concern with potassium-sparing diuretics such as spironolactone, not loop diuretics.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9dc2f151","lv":"lvn","d":"atorvastatin","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient taking atorvastatin reports new, unexplained muscle pain and weakness. What is the best action by the LVN?","a":"Report the symptoms promptly to the RN or provider","w":["Reassure the patient that this is expected and harmless","Apply a heating pad and recheck the next week","Suggest taking an extra dose at bedtime instead"],"x":"Statins can cause myopathy and, rarely, rhabdomyolysis, so unexplained muscle pain or weakness must be reported. It should not be dismissed as harmless.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2227e2b2","lv":"lvn","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient with angina uses sublingual nitroglycerin. Which other medicine can cause a dangerous drop in blood pressure if taken with it?","a":"Sildenafil","w":["Atorvastatin","Acetaminophen","Metformin"],"x":"Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors such as sildenafil greatly increase the blood-pressure-lowering effect of nitrates and the combination is contraindicated. Atorvastatin is often taken by the same patients but does not cause this interaction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfe63863f","lv":"lvn","d":"spironolactone","sys":"cardio","q":"Which teaching is most important for a patient taking spironolactone?","a":"Avoid salt substitutes that contain potassium","w":["Eat extra bananas and oranges every day","Take a potassium supplement with each dose","Limit fluids to prevent low sodium"],"x":"Spironolactone is potassium-sparing and can cause hyperkalemia, so potassium-based salt substitutes and extra potassium should be avoided. Encouraging high-potassium foods is teaching for loop or thiazide diuretics.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpda0e56ae","lv":"lvn","d":"apixaban","sys":"cardio","q":"Apixaban belongs to which drug class?","a":"Direct factor Xa inhibitor","w":["Vitamin K antagonist","Direct thrombin inhibitor","P2Y12 platelet inhibitor"],"x":"Apixaban directly blocks factor Xa. Dabigatran is the oral direct thrombin inhibitor, and warfarin is the vitamin K antagonist.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp63c27d3e","lv":"lvn","d":"digoxin","sys":"cardio","q":"A resident taking digoxin reports loss of appetite, nausea, and seeing yellow-green halos around lights. How should the LVN interpret this?","a":"Possible digoxin toxicity that needs prompt reporting","w":["Expected mild side effects that will pass","Signs of an allergic reaction to digoxin","Signs that the digoxin level is too low"],"x":"Anorexia, nausea, and visual changes such as yellow-green halos are classic early signs of digoxin toxicity. They are not normal side effects to ignore or signs of a low level.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3d3aa028","lv":"lvn","d":"amlodipine","sys":"cardio","q":"Which side effect is common with amlodipine and should be assessed at each visit?","a":"Swelling of the ankles and feet","w":["Persistent dry cough","High blood sugar","Low serum potassium"],"x":"Amlodipine is a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker that commonly causes peripheral edema. A dry cough is linked to ACE inhibitors, not calcium channel blockers.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe258667b","lv":"lvn","d":"digoxin","sys":"shock","q":"How long should the LVN count the apical pulse before giving digoxin?","a":"A full minute","w":["Fifteen seconds, then multiply by four","Thirty seconds, then multiply by two","Ten seconds, then multiply by six"],"x":"The apical pulse is counted for a full minute before digoxin because the drug slows heart rate and can cause irregular rhythms. Shorter counts can miss irregular beats.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1a4f605b","lv":"lvn","d":"digoxin","sys":"shock","q":"Which lab finding increases a patient's risk of digoxin toxicity?","a":"Low serum potassium","w":["High serum sodium","Low hemoglobin","High blood glucose"],"x":"Hypokalemia makes the heart more sensitive to digoxin and raises the risk of toxicity, which is why patients on diuretics need potassium monitoring. Sodium, hemoglobin, and glucose do not have this effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa48525da","lv":"lvn","d":"droxidopa","sys":"shock","q":"Droxidopa is given to treat which condition?","a":"Symptomatic neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","w":["Resistant essential hypertension","Atrial fibrillation with a rapid rate","Heart failure with fluid overload"],"x":"Droxidopa is converted to norepinephrine and raises blood pressure in patients who become dizzy on standing from neurogenic orthostatic hypotension. It would worsen hypertension, not treat it.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5166e5bd","lv":"lvn","d":"droxidopa","sys":"shock","q":"Which teaching helps reduce the risk named in droxidopa's boxed warning?","a":"Rest and sleep with the head of the bed elevated","w":["Lie flat for an hour after each dose","Take the last daily dose at bedtime","Take each dose with a salty snack"],"x":"Droxidopa carries a boxed warning for supine hypertension, so patients should rest and sleep with the head raised and take the last dose well before bedtime. Lying flat after a dose would raise the risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb2fa3a81","lv":"lvn","d":"epinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"In anaphylaxis, epinephrine improves the patient's condition mainly by doing what?","a":"Constricting blood vessels and opening the airways","w":["Blocking histamine at H1 receptors","Reducing airway swelling over several hours","Slowing the heart to improve filling"],"x":"Epinephrine's alpha effects raise blood pressure and its beta-2 effects relax airway muscle, which is why it is first-line for anaphylaxis. Blocking histamine is how antihistamines work, and they act too slowly to treat shock.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1f268600","lv":"lvn","d":"epinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"After an epinephrine auto-injector relieves an allergic reaction, why does the patient still need emergency care?","a":"Symptoms can return as the drug wears off","w":["The injection usually causes low blood sugar","A second dose is always needed for the vaccine record","The injection site must be stitched closed"],"x":"Epinephrine is short-acting, and anaphylaxis symptoms can return, so the patient needs further monitoring and care. It does not typically cause low blood sugar; it tends to raise it.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf1866eed","lv":"lvn","d":"milrinone","sys":"shock","q":"Milrinone belongs to which drug class?","a":"Phosphodiesterase-3 inhibitor","w":["Beta-1 adrenergic agonist","Cardiac glycoside","Alpha-1 adrenergic agonist"],"x":"Milrinone inhibits phosphodiesterase-3, which strengthens heart contraction and dilates blood vessels. Dobutamine, not milrinone, is the beta-1 agonist inotrope.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0fa74cfb","lv":"lvn","d":"milrinone","sys":"shock","q":"For a patient receiving IV milrinone, which adverse effects should the nurse watch for?","a":"Low blood pressure and ventricular arrhythmias","w":["High blood pressure and slow heart rate","Low blood sugar and seizures","Wheezing and hives"],"x":"Because milrinone dilates blood vessels and increases heart contractility, hypotension and ventricular arrhythmias are its main risks. It does not usually cause high blood pressure with a slow rate, which is more typical of phenylephrine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp23ddcb24","lv":"lvn","d":"phenylephrine","sys":"shock","q":"How does phenylephrine raise blood pressure?","a":"It stimulates alpha-1 receptors to constrict vessels","w":["It stimulates beta-1 receptors to speed the heart","It inhibits phosphodiesterase in heart muscle","It increases blood volume by holding sodium"],"x":"Phenylephrine is a nearly pure alpha-1 agonist that raises blood pressure by vasoconstriction. It has little beta-1 effect, so it does not directly speed up the heart.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1402bdc8","lv":"lvn","d":"phenylephrine","sys":"shock","q":"A patient on IV phenylephrine has a rising blood pressure and a slowing heart rate. What does this most likely represent?","a":"Reflex bradycardia from the blood pressure rise","w":["A severe allergic reaction to the drug","The drug's direct effect on beta-1 receptors","Early signs of digoxin toxicity"],"x":"When phenylephrine raises blood pressure, the body's baroreceptor reflex slows the heart. It does not stimulate beta-1 receptors, so a direct cardiac effect would not explain this.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2b1b4ce4","lv":"lvn","d":"warfarin","sys":"blood","q":"Which dietary teaching is correct for a patient taking warfarin?","a":"Keep intake of leafy green vegetables consistent","w":["Eat as many leafy green vegetables as possible","Avoid every food that contains vitamin K","Double the next dose if one is missed"],"x":"Vitamin K reduces warfarin's effect, so a steady day-to-day intake keeps the INR stable. Avoiding all vitamin K is unnecessary and can make the INR swing when intake changes.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa7750282","lv":"lvn","d":"warfarin","sys":"blood","q":"Which agent is used to reverse the effect of warfarin?","a":"Phytonadione (vitamin K)","w":["Protamine sulfate","Idarucizumab","Andexanet alfa"],"x":"Vitamin K restores the clotting factors that warfarin blocks. Protamine reverses heparin, idarucizumab reverses dabigatran, and andexanet alfa reverses factor Xa inhibitors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp970ef873","lv":"lvn","d":"heparin","sys":"blood","q":"Which agent is the antidote for an unfractionated heparin overdose?","a":"Protamine sulfate","w":["Phytonadione (vitamin K)","Idarucizumab","Naloxone"],"x":"Protamine binds heparin and neutralizes it. Vitamin K is the reversal agent for warfarin, not heparin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp97ecfe63","lv":"lvn","d":"enoxaparin","sys":"blood","q":"When giving subcutaneous enoxaparin from a prefilled syringe, what should the nurse do?","a":"Leave the air bubble in the syringe before injecting","w":["Massage the site well after injecting","Inject into the deltoid muscle","Aspirate for blood before injecting"],"x":"The labeling says not to expel the air bubble from the prefilled syringe, and the injection is given subcutaneously, usually in the abdomen. Massaging or aspirating increases bruising.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp90d613b2","lv":"lvn","d":"clopidogrel","sys":"blood","q":"Which teaching is most important for a patient taking clopidogrel?","a":"Report black, tarry stools or unusual bruising","w":["Expect bright orange urine during treatment","Take it with antacids to protect the stomach","Report any increase in appetite"],"x":"Clopidogrel blocks platelet activation, so bleeding is the key risk and signs like black stools or easy bruising must be reported. Orange urine is linked to drugs like phenazopyridine, not clopidogrel.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbe3bc8a2","lv":"lvn","d":"ferrous-sulfate","sys":"blood","q":"Which statement about oral ferrous sulfate is accurate teaching?","a":"Stools may turn dark green or black","w":["Take it with an antacid to prevent upset stomach","Take it with milk to improve absorption","Urine may turn bright orange"],"x":"Iron commonly darkens the stool, and patients should know this is expected. Antacids and milk reduce iron absorption, so taking iron with them is incorrect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4dcda06f","lv":"lvn","d":"epoetin-alfa","sys":"blood","q":"Before giving epoetin alfa, which assessment is the priority?","a":"Blood pressure and the most recent hemoglobin","w":["Apical pulse and serum digoxin level","Blood glucose and hemoglobin A1C","Respiratory rate and oxygen saturation"],"x":"Epoetin alfa can raise blood pressure, and a hemoglobin that is too high increases the risk of heart attack, stroke, and clots. Glucose monitoring is not specifically needed for this drug.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb64ea2fe","lv":"lvn","d":"dabigatran","sys":"blood","q":"Which teaching about dabigatran capsules is correct?","a":"Swallow the capsules whole without opening them","w":["Open the capsules and sprinkle on applesauce","Crush the capsules if swallowing is hard","Chew the capsules for faster absorption"],"x":"Opening or chewing dabigatran capsules greatly increases absorption and the risk of bleeding, so they must be swallowed whole. Sprinkling on applesauce is acceptable for some other drugs but not this one.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp85c82923","lv":"lvn","d":"filgrastim","sys":"blood","q":"Which side effect is common with filgrastim?","a":"Bone pain","w":["Hair loss","Slow heart rate","Low blood sugar"],"x":"Filgrastim stimulates the bone marrow to make neutrophils, which commonly causes bone pain. Hair loss is associated with chemotherapy, not filgrastim.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa228c16d","lv":"lvn","d":"tranexamic-acid","sys":"blood","q":"Which history should be reported before a patient starts tranexamic acid for heavy menstrual bleeding?","a":"A history of blood clots, such as a DVT","w":["Iron-deficiency anemia","Seasonal allergies","Recent mild sinus infection"],"x":"Tranexamic acid prevents clot breakdown, so it is contraindicated with active or prior thromboembolic disease. Iron-deficiency anemia is common in these patients and is not a reason to avoid it.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp46e649a2","lv":"lvn","d":"onabotulinumtoxina","sys":"airway","q":"After onabotulinumtoxinA injections, which symptom should the patient report right away?","a":"New trouble swallowing or breathing","w":["Mild tenderness at the injection sites","A small bruise at an injection site","Slight tiredness on the day of treatment"],"x":"Botulinum toxins carry a boxed warning that the effect can spread beyond the injection site and weaken swallowing and breathing muscles. Mild tenderness or bruising at the site is expected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp726a0604","lv":"lvn","d":"onabotulinumtoxina","sys":"airway","q":"OnabotulinumtoxinA relaxes muscles by doing what?","a":"Blocking acetylcholine release at nerve endings","w":["Blocking dopamine receptors in the brain","Increasing calcium release in muscle cells","Stimulating GABA receptors in the spinal cord"],"x":"Botulinum toxin prevents acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction, which weakens the injected muscle. GABA stimulation in the spinal cord is how baclofen works, not botulinum toxin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb83659cc","lv":"lvn","d":"abobotulinumtoxina","sys":"airway","q":"By which route is abobotulinumtoxinA given for cervical dystonia?","a":"Intramuscular injection","w":["Oral tablet","Intravenous infusion","Inhaled powder"],"x":"AbobotulinumtoxinA is injected directly into the affected muscles. It is not given orally, intravenously, or by inhalation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp582b9e73","lv":"lvn","d":"abobotulinumtoxina","sys":"airway","q":"Which group of symptoms after abobotulinumtoxinA suggests the toxin effect has spread?","a":"Hoarseness, double vision, and trouble swallowing","w":["Redness and itching at the injection sites","Mild headache and a stuffy nose","Brief soreness in the treated muscle"],"x":"Distant toxin spread can cause generalized weakness, double vision, drooping eyelids, hoarseness, and swallowing or breathing problems, which must be reported promptly. Local redness or soreness is expected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp77fc0e27","lv":"lvn","d":"daxibotulinumtoxin","sys":"airway","q":"DaxibotulinumtoxinA works by doing what?","a":"Blocking acetylcholine release at nerve endings","w":["Blocking histamine release from mast cells","Stimulating collagen growth in the skin","Blocking pain signals in sensory nerves"],"x":"Like other botulinum toxins, daxibotulinumtoxinA prevents acetylcholine release so the injected muscle relaxes, smoothing lines or easing dystonia. It does not work by building collagen.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp19400aa0","lv":"lvn","d":"daxibotulinumtoxin","sys":"airway","q":"Which finding is a contraindication to daxibotulinumtoxinA injection?","a":"Infection at the planned injection site","w":["Mild seasonal allergies","Well-controlled high blood pressure","A history of migraine headaches"],"x":"The labeling lists infection at the injection site and hypersensitivity as contraindications. Controlled hypertension and seasonal allergies do not prevent treatment.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp069f638b","lv":"lvn","d":"fentanyl","sys":"airway","q":"During and after IV fentanyl, which assessment is the priority?","a":"Respiratory rate and oxygen saturation","w":["Blood glucose and temperature","Bowel sounds and urine output","Pupil size and reflexes only"],"x":"Fentanyl is a potent opioid whose most dangerous effect is respiratory depression, so breathing and oxygen levels are watched closely. Constipation and urinary retention matter but are not the priority.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp23e62841","lv":"lvn","d":"fentanyl","sys":"airway","q":"Which drug reverses respiratory depression caused by fentanyl?","a":"Naloxone","w":["Flumazenil","Atropine","Protamine sulfate"],"x":"Naloxone is an opioid antagonist that reverses fentanyl's effects. Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines, not opioids.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdb34e70d","lv":"lvn","d":"pyrantel","sys":"airway","q":"Over-the-counter pyrantel pamoate is used to treat which condition?","a":"Pinworm infection","w":["Head lice","Scabies","Vaginal yeast infection"],"x":"Pyrantel is an anthelmintic that paralyzes intestinal worms such as pinworms so they pass in the stool. Lice and scabies are treated with topical products such as permethrin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2fc71763","lv":"lvn","d":"pyrantel","sys":"airway","q":"When one family member is treated with pyrantel for pinworms, what does the product labeling advise?","a":"Treat the entire household unless told otherwise","w":["Treat only the person with symptoms","Treat only the children in the home","Treat household pets instead of people"],"x":"Pinworms spread easily within households, so the OTC labeling advises treating everyone in the home unless a provider says otherwise. Pets do not carry human pinworms.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1301f4d0","lv":"lvn","d":"albuterol","sys":"resp","q":"Which side effects are expected after using an albuterol inhaler?","a":"Shakiness and a faster heart rate","w":["Drowsiness and a slower heart rate","Oral thrush and hoarseness","Dry cough and swollen lips"],"x":"Albuterol is a beta-2 agonist that can also stimulate the heart and skeletal muscle, causing tremor and tachycardia. Thrush and hoarseness are linked to inhaled corticosteroids.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpff4ad3ec","lv":"lvn","d":"albuterol","sys":"resp","q":"Albuterol belongs to which drug class?","a":"Short-acting beta-2 agonist","w":["Long-acting beta-2 agonist","Inhaled corticosteroid","Long-acting muscarinic antagonist"],"x":"Albuterol is a short-acting beta-2 agonist used as a rescue inhaler. Salmeterol and formoterol are long-acting beta-2 agonists.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp19bca1c9","lv":"lvn","d":"fluticasone","sys":"resp","q":"Which teaching helps prevent oral thrush with an inhaled fluticasone inhaler?","a":"Rinse the mouth with water and spit after each use","w":["Use it only during an asthma attack","Swallow a glass of milk after each use","Hold the breath as briefly as possible"],"x":"Rinsing and spitting removes steroid left in the mouth and lowers the risk of candidiasis. Inhaled steroids are maintenance medicines, not rescue inhalers.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4f139681","lv":"lvn","d":"tiotropium","sys":"resp","q":"Which teaching about tiotropium capsules used with a dry powder inhaler device is correct?","a":"Place the capsule in the device; never swallow it","w":["Swallow the capsule with a full glass of water","Open the capsule and mix it with water","Dissolve the capsule under the tongue"],"x":"Tiotropium capsules for the dry powder device are pierced inside the device and inhaled. They are not meant to be swallowed or dissolved.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbae29b79","lv":"lvn","d":"montelukast","sys":"resp","q":"Montelukast carries a boxed warning about which type of adverse effect?","a":"Neuropsychiatric events such as mood changes","w":["Severe liver failure","Life-threatening bleeding","Tendon rupture"],"x":"Montelukast has a boxed warning for serious neuropsychiatric events, including agitation, depression, sleep disturbance, and suicidal thoughts. Tendon rupture is a fluoroquinolone warning.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp53a5e9be","lv":"lvn","d":"salmeterol","sys":"resp","q":"A patient asks whether salmeterol can be used during a sudden asthma attack. What is the correct response?","a":"No; it is long-acting and not for quick relief","w":["Yes; it is the preferred rescue inhaler","Yes; but only if albuterol is unavailable","No; it is used only for allergies"],"x":"Salmeterol is a long-acting beta-2 agonist with a slow onset, so it is not used for acute symptoms. A short-acting agent such as albuterol is the rescue inhaler.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd701cac8","lv":"lvn","d":"benzonatate","sys":"resp","q":"Which teaching about benzonatate capsules is correct?","a":"Swallow them whole without chewing or sucking","w":["Chew them to coat the throat faster","Dissolve them in the mouth like a lozenge","Open them and mix with warm tea"],"x":"Chewing or sucking benzonatate can numb the mouth and throat and cause choking or serious reactions. The capsules must be swallowed whole.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp49ff8e58","lv":"lvn","d":"oseltamivir","sys":"resp","q":"When is oseltamivir most effective for treating influenza?","a":"When started within 48 hours of symptom onset","w":["Only after a positive culture returns","After the fever has lasted a full week","Only once pneumonia has developed"],"x":"Oseltamivir is labeled to start within 48 hours of flu symptoms, when viral replication is highest. Waiting for complications reduces its benefit.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8a4afeb4","lv":"lvn","d":"pseudoephedrine","sys":"resp","q":"Which patient should talk with the provider before using pseudoephedrine?","a":"A patient with uncontrolled high blood pressure","w":["A patient with seasonal allergies","A patient with a mild sinus headache","A patient with a recent common cold"],"x":"Pseudoephedrine is a sympathomimetic decongestant that can raise blood pressure and heart rate. Allergies and colds are common reasons it is used, not reasons for caution.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5ad84c18","lv":"lvn","d":"prednisone","sys":"resp","q":"A resident with diabetes starts a course of prednisone. Which assessment should the LVN expect to increase?","a":"Blood glucose checks","w":["Apical pulse checks","Serum digoxin levels","Peak flow readings only"],"x":"Corticosteroids such as prednisone raise blood glucose, so closer glucose monitoring is needed in diabetes. Apical pulse checks are linked to drugs like digoxin and beta blockers.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp69110c5c","lv":"lvn","d":"phenytoin","sys":"neuro","q":"Which teaching is important for a patient on long-term phenytoin?","a":"Brush, floss, and get regular dental care","w":["Expect the urine to turn bright orange","Eat extra protein at every meal","Avoid all dairy products"],"x":"Phenytoin commonly causes gingival hyperplasia, and good oral hygiene helps limit it. Orange urine is linked to drugs like rifampin and phenazopyridine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4d1d96e1","lv":"lvn","d":"levetiracetam","sys":"neuro","q":"Which effect should a patient taking levetiracetam report?","a":"New irritability, aggression, or depression","w":["Mild drowsiness during the first days","A small increase in appetite","Slightly darker yellow urine"],"x":"Levetiracetam can cause behavioral and mood changes, including suicidal thoughts, which should be reported. Mild drowsiness early in therapy is common and expected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp98a9fcde","lv":"lvn","d":"carbamazepine","sys":"neuro","q":"Which symptoms in a patient taking carbamazepine should be reported promptly?","a":"Sore throat, fever, or easy bruising","w":["Mild dizziness when first starting","Slight nausea that improves with food","Mild drowsiness in the evening"],"x":"Carbamazepine can cause serious blood disorders such as agranulocytosis and aplastic anemia, which show up as infection or bleeding signs. Mild dizziness and nausea are common early effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe293955d","lv":"lvn","d":"lamotrigine","sys":"neuro","q":"Which finding should a patient taking lamotrigine report right away?","a":"Any new skin rash","w":["Mild headache","Occasional dizziness","Slight weight change"],"x":"Lamotrigine has a boxed warning for serious rashes, including Stevens-Johnson syndrome, so any rash needs prompt evaluation. Headache and dizziness are common, less urgent side effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2e934206","lv":"lvn","d":"valproic-acid","sys":"neuro","q":"Which lab values are monitored in a patient taking valproic acid?","a":"Liver function tests and platelet count","w":["Serum potassium and magnesium","Thyroid levels and hemoglobin A1C","Serum amylase only"],"x":"Valproic acid can cause liver injury and low platelets, so liver tests and platelet counts are monitored. Pancreatitis is also a risk, but monitoring amylase alone would miss the liver and bleeding risks.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp62dc3cdb","lv":"lvn","d":"carbidopa-levodopa","sys":"neuro","q":"Which teaching about carbidopa-levodopa is correct?","a":"Large high-protein meals can reduce its effect","w":["It must be taken with a high-protein shake","It works best when taken with iron pills","It cures Parkinson disease over time"],"x":"Dietary protein competes with levodopa for absorption, so large protein meals can weaken its effect. Iron supplements also reduce absorption, and the drug treats symptoms but does not cure Parkinson disease.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd2baa01a","lv":"lvn","d":"donepezil","sys":"neuro","q":"Which finding in a resident taking donepezil should the LVN report?","a":"Fainting spells with a slow pulse","w":["Mild forgetfulness about recent events","Improved interest in daily activities","Occasional mild nausea after meals"],"x":"Donepezil is a cholinesterase inhibitor that can slow the heart and cause fainting, which should be reported. Mild nausea is a common, less urgent side effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp66269e80","lv":"lvn","d":"rizatriptan","sys":"neuro","q":"Which history should be reported before a patient takes rizatriptan for migraine?","a":"Coronary artery disease or prior heart attack","w":["Seasonal allergic rhinitis","Mild osteoarthritis of the knees","A history of motion sickness"],"x":"Triptans constrict blood vessels and are contraindicated in ischemic heart disease. Allergies and arthritis are not reasons to avoid them.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1894d0c8","lv":"lvn","d":"baclofen","sys":"neuro","q":"Baclofen is used to treat which problem?","a":"Muscle spasticity from multiple sclerosis or spinal cord injury","w":["Partial seizures in adults and children","Tremor from Parkinson disease","Nerve pain from shingles"],"x":"Baclofen is a GABA-B agonist muscle relaxant used for spasticity from MS and spinal cord conditions. It is not used to treat seizures or Parkinson tremor.","pilot":1},{"id":"qped0d5005","lv":"lvn","d":"topiramate","sys":"neuro","q":"Which teaching helps reduce the risk of kidney stones with topiramate?","a":"Drink plenty of fluids throughout the day","w":["Limit fluids in the evening","Take it with calcium supplements","Eat a high-protein diet"],"x":"Topiramate can cause kidney stones, and good hydration lowers that risk. Limiting fluids would increase the risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp35f04ad8","lv":"lvn","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"pain","q":"Taking too much acetaminophen mainly damages which organ?","a":"Liver","w":["Kidneys","Stomach lining","Heart"],"x":"Acetaminophen overdose causes liver injury, which can be fatal. Stomach bleeding is more typical of NSAIDs such as ibuprofen.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa247f0a6","lv":"lvn","d":"ibuprofen","sys":"pain","q":"Which teaching helps reduce stomach upset from ibuprofen?","a":"Take it with food or milk","w":["Take it on an empty stomach","Take it with an alcoholic drink","Take it lying down"],"x":"Taking ibuprofen with food or milk reduces GI irritation. Alcohol increases the risk of stomach bleeding with NSAIDs.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc9d1d082","lv":"lvn","d":"morphine","sys":"pain","q":"Before giving oral morphine to a resident, which assessment is the priority?","a":"Respiratory rate and level of sedation","w":["Blood glucose and temperature","Apical pulse and potassium level","Bowel sounds only"],"x":"Respiratory depression is morphine's most dangerous effect, and increasing sedation often comes first. Constipation is common but is not the priority before a dose.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp462f26ba","lv":"lvn","d":"hydrocodone-acetaminophen","sys":"pain","q":"Which teaching is most important for a patient taking hydrocodone-acetaminophen?","a":"Avoid other products that contain acetaminophen","w":["Take extra doses if the pain is severe","Take it with alcohol to improve sleep","Expect the urine to turn green"],"x":"Many OTC cold and pain products contain acetaminophen, and adding them can cause liver damage. Alcohol adds to opioid sedation and liver risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp27a5bb14","lv":"lvn","d":"fentanyl","sys":"pain","q":"Which teaching is important for a patient using a fentanyl transdermal patch?","a":"Avoid heating pads, hot tubs, and electric blankets","w":["Cut the patch to adjust the effect","Apply a heating pad to speed relief","Rub the patch to warm it before use"],"x":"Heat increases fentanyl absorption from the patch and can cause a fatal overdose. Patches must not be cut.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8b69b91d","lv":"lvn","d":"naloxone","sys":"pain","q":"After naloxone reverses an opioid overdose, why must the patient be monitored closely?","a":"The opioid may outlast naloxone and sedation may return","w":["Naloxone often causes very low blood pressure","Naloxone commonly causes low blood sugar","Naloxone often causes bleeding"],"x":"Many opioids last longer than naloxone, so breathing problems can return after it wears off. Low blood sugar and bleeding are not typical naloxone effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp906a7135","lv":"lvn","d":"tramadol","sys":"pain","q":"Taking tramadol with which drug raises the risk of serotonin syndrome?","a":"Sertraline","w":["Acetaminophen","Docusate","Loratadine"],"x":"Tramadol has serotonergic activity, and combining it with an SSRI such as sertraline can cause serotonin syndrome. Acetaminophen is often combined with tramadol and does not cause this.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp865f5393","lv":"lvn","d":"zolpidem","sys":"pain","q":"Which effect of zolpidem should be reported right away?","a":"Sleepwalking or driving while not fully awake","w":["Feeling sleepy shortly after taking it","Falling asleep faster than before","Mild dry mouth in the morning"],"x":"Zolpidem has a boxed warning for complex sleep behaviors such as sleepwalking and sleep-driving, which can cause serious injury. Sleepiness after the dose is the intended effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp98a9ed46","lv":"lvn","d":"lorazepam","sys":"pain","q":"For an older adult taking lorazepam, which safety concern is the priority?","a":"Falls from sedation and unsteady gait","w":["High blood pressure","Low blood sugar","Bleeding gums"],"x":"Benzodiazepines cause drowsiness, confusion, and poor balance in older adults, increasing fall risk. They do not typically cause high blood pressure or low blood sugar.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp09a4f592","lv":"lvn","d":"lidocaine-patch","sys":"pain","q":"Which teaching about the lidocaine patch is correct?","a":"Apply it only to intact skin, not open wounds","w":["Cover it with a heating pad to work faster","Apply it over broken skin for best relief","Cut it into pieces and swallow if needed"],"x":"Lidocaine patches go on intact skin; broken skin increases absorption and toxicity. Heat also increases absorption, so heating pads should not be used over the patch.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2a4c50fa","lv":"lvn","d":"lithium","sys":"psych","q":"Which symptoms suggest early lithium toxicity?","a":"Vomiting, diarrhea, and a coarse hand tremor","w":["A fine hand tremor and increased thirst","Constipation and urinary retention","High fever and rigid muscles"],"x":"GI upset with a coarse tremor and drowsiness are early signs of lithium toxicity. A fine tremor and thirst are common expected side effects at therapeutic levels.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6d8a294e","lv":"lvn","d":"lithium","sys":"psych","q":"Which over-the-counter drug can raise lithium levels?","a":"Ibuprofen","w":["Acetaminophen","Loratadine","Docusate"],"x":"NSAIDs such as ibuprofen reduce lithium clearance by the kidneys and can cause toxicity. Acetaminophen is generally the safer OTC pain reliever for patients on lithium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9a0d3218","lv":"lvn","d":"sertraline","sys":"psych","q":"Which teaching is most important for a young adult starting sertraline?","a":"Report new or worsening suicidal thoughts right away","w":["Expect full benefit within the first day","Take it with grapefruit juice for best effect","Expect the urine to turn dark brown"],"x":"Antidepressants carry a boxed warning for increased suicidal thinking in children and young adults, especially early in therapy. Full benefit usually takes several weeks.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp88f283bb","lv":"lvn","d":"clozapine","sys":"psych","q":"Which lab value must be monitored regularly in a patient taking clozapine?","a":"Absolute neutrophil count","w":["Serum lithium level","Serum potassium","Thyroid-stimulating hormone"],"x":"Clozapine can cause severe neutropenia, so the absolute neutrophil count is monitored. Lithium levels are not related to clozapine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa41759d0","lv":"lvn","d":"haloperidol","sys":"psych","q":"A resident on long-term haloperidol has repetitive lip smacking and tongue movements. What is this most likely?","a":"Tardive dyskinesia","w":["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Serotonin syndrome","Acute dystonic reaction"],"x":"Tardive dyskinesia is a late effect of antipsychotics marked by involuntary face, lip, and tongue movements and should be reported. Acute dystonia is a sudden muscle spasm that usually occurs early in therapy.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1c8cb362","lv":"lvn","d":"olanzapine","sys":"psych","q":"Which assessments are important for a patient taking olanzapine long term?","a":"Weight, blood glucose, and lipid levels","w":["Serum lithium and sodium levels","INR and platelet count","Peak flow and oxygen saturation"],"x":"Olanzapine commonly causes weight gain, high blood sugar, and high lipids, so metabolic monitoring is needed. INR monitoring is for warfarin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp146b7e9a","lv":"lvn","d":"tranylcypromine","sys":"psych","q":"Which foods should a patient taking tranylcypromine avoid?","a":"Aged cheeses and cured or fermented meats","w":["Fresh fruits and vegetables","Plain rice and pasta","Fresh chicken and fish"],"x":"Tranylcypromine is an MAO inhibitor, and tyramine-rich foods such as aged cheese and cured meats can cause a hypertensive crisis. Fresh foods are low in tyramine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0b17e94e","lv":"lvn","d":"bupropion","sys":"psych","q":"Which history is a contraindication to bupropion?","a":"A seizure disorder","w":["Seasonal allergies","Mild osteoarthritis","Smoking cigarettes"],"x":"Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold and is contraindicated in seizure disorders and in bulimia or anorexia. It is actually used to help people stop smoking.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8c0d3fbb","lv":"lvn","d":"trazodone","sys":"psych","q":"Which adverse effect of trazodone requires immediate reporting in a male patient?","a":"A prolonged, painful erection","w":["Mild morning drowsiness","Dry mouth","A mild headache"],"x":"Trazodone can cause priapism, a medical emergency. Drowsiness and dry mouth are common and expected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb2b744a1","lv":"lvn","d":"methylphenidate","sys":"psych","q":"Which teaching helps reduce insomnia from methylphenidate?","a":"Give doses early in the day","w":["Give the dose at bedtime","Give it with a caffeinated drink","Give it only after a large evening meal"],"x":"Methylphenidate is a stimulant, so late doses can interfere with sleep. Caffeine adds to stimulant effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5ca51bd0","lv":"lvn","d":"potassium-chloride","sys":"renal","q":"Which teaching about extended-release potassium chloride tablets is correct?","a":"Swallow them whole with food and a full glass of water","w":["Crush them and mix with a small sip of water","Chew them on an empty stomach","Dissolve them under the tongue"],"x":"Extended-release potassium tablets are swallowed whole with meals and plenty of water to reduce GI irritation and injury. Crushing destroys the extended-release design.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp84a2eb28","lv":"lvn","d":"furosemide","sys":"renal","q":"When is the best time to give once-daily oral furosemide?","a":"In the morning","w":["At bedtime","In the middle of the night","Right before an afternoon nap"],"x":"Giving a diuretic in the morning keeps the peak urine output during waking hours and prevents nighttime urination and falls. A bedtime dose would disrupt sleep.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9952b1b1","lv":"lvn","d":"hydrochlorothiazide","sys":"renal","q":"Which teaching is appropriate for a patient taking hydrochlorothiazide?","a":"Use sunscreen and protective clothing outdoors","w":["Avoid all foods that contain potassium","Take it at bedtime for best effect","Drink grapefruit juice with each dose"],"x":"Hydrochlorothiazide can increase sun sensitivity and is linked to a higher risk of some skin cancers. Because it lowers potassium, avoiding potassium-rich foods would be wrong.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpef28ae90","lv":"lvn","d":"tamsulosin","sys":"renal","q":"Which teaching is important when a patient starts tamsulosin?","a":"Rise slowly from lying or sitting to prevent dizziness","w":["Expect the urine to turn bright red","Limit fluids to reduce urine output","Expect improved vision at night"],"x":"Tamsulosin is an alpha-1 blocker that can cause orthostatic hypotension, especially early in therapy. Red urine is not an expected effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0f5b1fc0","lv":"lvn","d":"finasteride","sys":"renal","q":"Which safety teaching is correct for finasteride?","a":"Women who are or may become pregnant should not handle broken tablets","w":["The tablets should be crushed before giving","Women should take it to prevent hair loss in pregnancy","It is safe for anyone to handle in any form"],"x":"Finasteride can be absorbed through the skin and may harm a male fetus, so pregnant women should avoid crushed or broken tablets. It is not used in pregnancy.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp41ec55e6","lv":"lvn","d":"oxybutynin","sys":"renal","q":"Which side effects are expected with oxybutynin?","a":"Dry mouth and constipation","w":["Diarrhea and excess saliva","Urinary frequency and urgency","Slow heart rate and wheezing"],"x":"Oxybutynin is anticholinergic, so dry mouth and constipation are common. Diarrhea and excess saliva are cholinergic effects, the opposite of what oxybutynin does.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp30204250","lv":"lvn","d":"nitrofurantoin","sys":"renal","q":"Which teaching about nitrofurantoin is correct?","a":"Take it with food; urine may turn brown","w":["Take it on an empty stomach; urine may turn blue","Take it with antacids; urine may turn green","Take it at bedtime only; urine will be clear"],"x":"Food improves nitrofurantoin absorption and reduces stomach upset, and the drug may harmlessly darken urine to yellow or brown. Magnesium-containing antacids reduce its absorption.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp86767e65","lv":"lvn","d":"phenazopyridine","sys":"renal","q":"Which statement shows understanding of phenazopyridine teaching?","a":"This relieves burning but does not cure the infection","w":["This will cure the infection in a single day","This should turn my urine light green","This replaces the need for an antibiotic"],"x":"Phenazopyridine is a urinary analgesic that eases burning and urgency but has no antibacterial effect. It turns urine reddish-orange, not green.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1f59a19a","lv":"lvn","d":"sevelamer","sys":"renal","q":"When should sevelamer be given to a patient with chronic kidney disease?","a":"With meals, to bind dietary phosphate","w":["At bedtime on an empty stomach","Only when serum calcium is low","One hour after the evening meal"],"x":"Sevelamer is a phosphate binder that must be in the gut with food to bind phosphate. Giving it apart from meals greatly reduces its effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7e85c567","lv":"lvn","d":"triamterene","sys":"renal","q":"Which lab finding is the main concern in a patient taking triamterene?","a":"A high serum potassium level","w":["A low serum potassium level","A high serum calcium level","A low blood glucose level"],"x":"Triamterene is potassium-sparing and carries a boxed warning for hyperkalemia. Low potassium is a concern with loop and thiazide diuretics instead.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp178c2f4e","lv":"lvn","d":"alendronate","sys":"bone","q":"How should oral alendronate be taken?","a":"First thing in the morning with plain water, staying upright","w":["With breakfast and a glass of orange juice","At bedtime with a glass of milk","With a calcium supplement and coffee"],"x":"Alendronate must be taken on an empty stomach with plain water, and staying upright reduces the risk of esophageal irritation. Food, juice, coffee, milk, and calcium all reduce absorption.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp91b83a82","lv":"lvn","d":"alendronate","sys":"bone","q":"Which symptom should a patient taking alendronate report?","a":"Jaw pain or a tooth socket that does not heal","w":["Mild constipation","A slight headache","Mild muscle soreness after exercise"],"x":"Bisphosphonates have been linked to osteonecrosis of the jaw, which can show up as jaw pain or poor healing after dental work. Mild constipation and headache are less concerning.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp882914f6","lv":"lvn","d":"allopurinol","sys":"bone","q":"How does allopurinol lower uric acid?","a":"It blocks xanthine oxidase to reduce uric acid production","w":["It increases uric acid excretion by the kidneys","It blocks neutrophil movement into the joint","It breaks down uric acid with an enzyme"],"x":"Allopurinol is a xanthine oxidase inhibitor that reduces how much uric acid the body makes. Increasing kidney excretion is how probenecid works.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9fdb6795","lv":"lvn","d":"allopurinol","sys":"bone","q":"Which finding in a patient taking allopurinol should be reported right away?","a":"Any new skin rash","w":["Mild stomach upset","Slightly darker yellow urine","Mild drowsiness"],"x":"Allopurinol can cause severe skin reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome, which often begin as a rash. Mild stomach upset is a less urgent side effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd5768521","lv":"lvn","d":"colchicine","sys":"bone","q":"Which symptoms are often the first signs of colchicine toxicity?","a":"Nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea","w":["Dry mouth and constipation","Rash and itching","Headache and blurred vision"],"x":"GI symptoms are the most common early signs of colchicine toxicity and should be reported. Constipation is not a typical colchicine effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp69533d78","lv":"lvn","d":"colchicine","sys":"bone","q":"Which drug can greatly raise colchicine levels and cause toxicity?","a":"Clarithromycin","w":["Acetaminophen","Docusate","Loratadine"],"x":"Clarithromycin strongly inhibits the enzymes and transporters that clear colchicine, and fatal toxicity has occurred. Acetaminophen does not have this interaction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb4b0603d","lv":"lvn","d":"febuxostat","sys":"bone","q":"Febuxostat carries a boxed warning for which risk?","a":"Cardiovascular death","w":["Tendon rupture","Lactic acidosis","Severe hypoglycemia"],"x":"Febuxostat's boxed warning is for a higher rate of cardiovascular death than allopurinol in gout patients with heart disease. Tendon rupture is a fluoroquinolone warning.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4b7eb3b9","lv":"lvn","d":"calcitonin-salmon","sys":"bone","q":"Which teaching is correct for calcitonin-salmon nasal spray?","a":"Alternate nostrils daily and report nasal sores or nosebleeds","w":["Use the same nostril every day for consistency","Swallow the spray after it reaches the throat","Spray it into the mouth if the nose is congested"],"x":"Alternating nostrils reduces nasal irritation, and rhinitis, sores, and nosebleeds are known side effects that should be reported. The spray is meant to be absorbed through the nasal lining.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe26e9bc0","lv":"lvn","d":"cholecalciferol","sys":"bone","q":"How does cholecalciferol (vitamin D3) support bone health?","a":"It increases calcium absorption from the gut","w":["It blocks cells that break down bone","It directly builds new bone protein","It increases calcium loss in the urine"],"x":"Vitamin D increases intestinal absorption of calcium and phosphate, supporting bone mineralization. Blocking bone breakdown is how bisphosphonates work.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc6a8f509","lv":"lvn","d":"cinacalcet","sys":"bone","q":"Which symptoms suggest low calcium in a patient taking cinacalcet?","a":"Tingling around the mouth and muscle cramps","w":["Increased thirst and constipation","Dry mouth and blurred vision","Flushing and a fast heart rate"],"x":"Cinacalcet lowers parathyroid hormone and calcium, and hypocalcemia causes tingling, cramps, and spasms. Thirst and constipation suggest high calcium instead.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp59ea5e4c","lv":"lvn","d":"naloxone","sys":"tox","q":"Naloxone reverses an overdose of which type of drug?","a":"Opioids such as fentanyl or oxycodone","w":["Benzodiazepines such as lorazepam","Acetaminophen","Beta blockers such as metoprolol"],"x":"Naloxone is an opioid receptor antagonist. Benzodiazepine overdose is reversed by flumazenil, not naloxone.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1f2a0038","lv":"lvn","d":"naloxone","sys":"tox","q":"After naloxone is given to a patient who takes opioids daily, which response should the nurse anticipate?","a":"Acute withdrawal with agitation, sweating, and vomiting","w":["Deep sleep with very slow breathing","A severe allergic rash and hives","Very low blood sugar and confusion"],"x":"In opioid-dependent patients, naloxone can bring on sudden withdrawal symptoms. Slow breathing is what naloxone is given to reverse.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpab1b0e7f","lv":"lvn","d":"n-acetylcysteine","sys":"tox","q":"Acetylcysteine is the antidote for an overdose of which drug?","a":"Acetaminophen","w":["Aspirin","Iron","Digoxin"],"x":"Acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione and protects the liver after acetaminophen overdose. Aspirin overdose is managed differently, without a specific antidote like this.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2636d7be","lv":"lvn","d":"n-acetylcysteine","sys":"tox","q":"When is acetylcysteine most effective after an acetaminophen overdose?","a":"When started within the first 8 hours after ingestion","w":["Only after liver enzymes begin to rise","Only after jaundice appears","After waiting a full day for the drug to clear"],"x":"Acetylcysteine works best when started early, ideally within 8 hours, before liver damage develops. Waiting for liver tests to rise or jaundice to appear loses much of its benefit.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1ee421c2","lv":"lvn","d":"glucagon","sys":"tox","q":"In which situation is glucagon used?","a":"Severe low blood sugar in a person who cannot swallow","w":["Mild low blood sugar in an alert person","High blood sugar with ketones in the urine","Routine daily control of type 2 diabetes"],"x":"Glucagon raises blood glucose by releasing liver glycogen and is used when a person is unconscious or cannot safely take sugar by mouth. An alert person with mild low blood sugar should take fast-acting oral carbohydrates.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp91668bfd","lv":"lvn","d":"glucagon","sys":"tox","q":"After glucagon is given for severe low blood sugar and the person wakes up, what should happen next?","a":"Give oral carbohydrates once the person can swallow","w":["Give a dose of rapid-acting insulin","Have the person lie flat and fast for several hours","Give nothing by mouth for the rest of the day"],"x":"Glucagon's effect is brief, so the person should eat carbohydrates once able to swallow safely to prevent another low. Insulin would lower the blood sugar again.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4edef02a","lv":"lvn","d":"phytonadione","sys":"tox","q":"Why is IV phytonadione given slowly with close monitoring?","a":"It can cause severe, sometimes fatal hypersensitivity reactions","w":["It commonly causes dangerous low blood sugar","It often causes sudden seizures","It frequently causes kidney failure"],"x":"Phytonadione has a boxed warning for severe hypersensitivity and anaphylactic reactions with IV and IM use, so other routes are preferred when possible. Low blood sugar and seizures are not its known risks.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdf751246","lv":"lvn","d":"nalmefene","sys":"tox","q":"Nalmefene belongs to which drug class?","a":"Opioid receptor antagonist","w":["Opioid receptor agonist","Benzodiazepine receptor antagonist","Partial opioid agonist"],"x":"Nalmefene, like naloxone, blocks opioid receptors and reverses opioid overdose. Flumazenil is the benzodiazepine receptor antagonist.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9ad1b2f9","lv":"lvn","d":"pyridoxine","sys":"tox","q":"Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) is used as an antidote for seizures caused by an overdose of which drug?","a":"Isoniazid","w":["Lithium","Digoxin","Acetaminophen"],"x":"Isoniazid overdose depletes vitamin B6 and causes seizures that respond to pyridoxine. Acetaminophen overdose is treated with acetylcysteine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1065f735","lv":"lvn","d":"deferiprone","sys":"tox","q":"Which symptoms should a patient taking deferiprone report right away?","a":"Fever, sore throat, or other signs of infection","w":["Reddish-brown urine","Mild nausea after doses","A mild increase in appetite"],"x":"Deferiprone has a boxed warning for agranulocytosis, so signs of infection must be reported and the neutrophil count checked. Reddish-brown urine is an expected, harmless effect of the iron complex.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3dfe898e","lv":"lvn","d":"amoxicillin","sys":"infect","q":"Before giving the first dose of amoxicillin, what should the LVN ask about?","a":"Any prior allergic reaction to penicillin","w":["Any prior reaction to iodine or shellfish","Any history of kidney stones","Any recent use of acetaminophen"],"x":"Amoxicillin is a penicillin, so a penicillin allergy can cause a serious reaction. Shellfish or iodine reactions are not related to penicillins.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp129df469","lv":"lvn","d":"ciprofloxacin","sys":"infect","q":"Which symptom should a patient taking ciprofloxacin report right away?","a":"Pain or swelling in the back of the heel","w":["Mild loose stools for a day","A slight metallic taste","Mild drowsiness"],"x":"Fluoroquinolones carry a boxed warning for tendinitis and tendon rupture, often of the Achilles tendon. Mild GI upset is a less urgent side effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc213a5d7","lv":"lvn","d":"metronidazole","sys":"infect","q":"Which teaching is most important for a patient taking metronidazole?","a":"Avoid alcohol during treatment and for several days after","w":["Take it with grapefruit juice","Avoid dairy products for a week","Expect the urine to turn bright orange"],"x":"Metronidazole with alcohol can cause a disulfiram-like reaction with flushing, nausea, and vomiting. It may darken urine, but bright orange urine is typical of other drugs.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp84416e91","lv":"lvn","d":"doxycycline","sys":"infect","q":"Which teaching about oral doxycycline is correct?","a":"Separate it from antacids and iron supplements","w":["Take it lying down with a small sip of water","Take it together with an antacid","Take it with an iron pill to prevent upset"],"x":"Antacids and iron bind doxycycline and reduce absorption, and the drug should be taken with plenty of water while upright to prevent esophageal injury. Taking it lying down increases that risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe6ad59c8","lv":"lvn","d":"trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","sys":"infect","q":"Which teaching is important for a patient taking trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole?","a":"Drink plenty of fluids during treatment","w":["Limit fluids to concentrate the drug","Take it with an antacid","Avoid all fruits and vegetables"],"x":"Good fluid intake helps prevent crystals from forming in the urine with sulfonamides. Limiting fluids increases that risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8c6cb1fb","lv":"lvn","d":"clindamycin","sys":"infect","q":"Which symptom in a patient taking oral clindamycin should be reported?","a":"Frequent watery diarrhea","w":["A mild metallic taste","Mild stomach discomfort","Slightly darker yellow urine"],"x":"Clindamycin has a boxed warning for C. difficile-associated diarrhea, which can be severe. Mild stomach discomfort is common and less urgent.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfa3b6a18","lv":"lvn","d":"vancomycin","sys":"infect","q":"During IV vancomycin, a patient develops flushing and a red rash on the face, neck, and upper body. What does this most likely represent?","a":"An infusion reaction often related to a fast infusion rate","w":["An expected sign that the infection is clearing","A sign of vancomycin-induced kidney stones","Early signs of hearing loss"],"x":"Vancomycin infusion reaction causes flushing and rash of the upper body, often with rapid infusion, and should be reported. It is not a sign of the infection improving.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9356d4b5","lv":"lvn","d":"fluconazole","sys":"infect","q":"A patient on warfarin is prescribed fluconazole. What interaction is the concern?","a":"Increased bleeding risk from a higher INR","w":["Lower INR and higher clot risk","Higher blood sugar from both drugs","Lower potassium from both drugs"],"x":"Fluconazole inhibits the enzymes that clear warfarin, raising the INR and bleeding risk. It does not lower the INR.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp180760e1","lv":"lvn","d":"zoster-recombinant-vaccine","sys":"infect","q":"Which statement about the recombinant zoster (shingles) vaccine is accurate?","a":"It is non-live and given as a two-dose intramuscular series","w":["It is live and given once subcutaneously","It is non-live and given once intradermally","It is live and given as a two-dose oral series"],"x":"The recombinant zoster vaccine is a non-live, adjuvanted vaccine given intramuscularly in two doses. The older live zoster vaccine was a single subcutaneous dose and is no longer used in the US.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9995d58d","lv":"lvn","d":"valacyclovir","sys":"infect","q":"When should a patient start valacyclovir for a cold sore outbreak?","a":"At the first sign of tingling or burning","w":["After the blisters have crusted over","Only after the sore has lasted a week","Once the sore becomes infected"],"x":"Antivirals for cold sores work best when started at the earliest symptoms, before blisters fully form. Starting after crusting gives little benefit.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp26e51715","lv":"lvn","d":"metformin","sys":"endo","q":"Which side effects are common when a patient starts metformin?","a":"Diarrhea, nausea, and stomach upset","w":["Weight gain and fluid retention","Frequent low blood sugar when used alone","Genital yeast infections"],"x":"GI upset is the most common side effect of metformin and is reduced by taking it with meals. Metformin alone rarely causes low blood sugar; weight gain and fluid retention are linked to pioglitazone.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8b61eba1","lv":"lvn","d":"insulin-glargine","sys":"endo","q":"Which statement about insulin glargine is correct?","a":"It should not be mixed with other insulins in the same syringe","w":["It is cloudy and must be rolled before use","It is given only right before each meal","It can be mixed with regular insulin in one syringe"],"x":"Insulin glargine is a clear, long-acting insulin that must not be mixed or diluted with other insulins. NPH is the cloudy insulin that is rolled to mix.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe413bcb2","lv":"lvn","d":"insulin-lispro","sys":"endo","q":"When is insulin lispro given in relation to meals?","a":"Within about 15 minutes before a meal or right after eating","w":["Once a day at the same time without regard to meals","At bedtime regardless of food intake","About an hour after finishing a meal"],"x":"Insulin lispro is rapid-acting and is timed to meals to cover the rise in blood glucose. Once-daily dosing without regard to meals describes basal insulin such as glargine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp02bc5584","lv":"lvn","d":"insulin-nph","sys":"endo","q":"How should NPH insulin be prepared before drawing up or injecting?","a":"Gently roll the vial or pen to evenly mix the cloudy suspension","w":["Shake it hard until it foams","Use only if the liquid is completely clear","Warm it in a microwave before use"],"x":"NPH is a cloudy suspension that must be gently mixed for a consistent dose. Shaking hard causes foaming, and heating can damage the insulin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpca432e9c","lv":"lvn","d":"glipizide","sys":"endo","q":"What is the main adverse effect to watch for with glipizide?","a":"Low blood sugar, especially if a meal is skipped","w":["High blood sugar after meals","Genital yeast infections","Lactic acidosis"],"x":"Glipizide is a sulfonylurea that stimulates insulin release and can cause hypoglycemia, especially in older adults or with missed meals. Lactic acidosis is a rare concern with metformin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf4f9091b","lv":"lvn","d":"levothyroxine","sys":"endo","q":"How should levothyroxine be taken?","a":"On an empty stomach with water, before breakfast","w":["With breakfast and a calcium supplement","At bedtime with a glass of milk","With an iron pill to improve absorption"],"x":"Levothyroxine is best absorbed on an empty stomach, usually 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast. Calcium, iron, and food reduce absorption.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0ad57584","lv":"lvn","d":"methimazole","sys":"endo","q":"How does methimazole treat hyperthyroidism?","a":"It blocks the thyroid gland from making new thyroid hormone","w":["It replaces missing thyroid hormone","It destroys thyroid tissue with radiation","It blocks the effects of thyroid hormone on the heart"],"x":"Methimazole is an antithyroid drug that inhibits thyroid hormone synthesis. Replacing hormone is what levothyroxine does, and blocking cardiac effects describes beta blockers.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb898d0c9","lv":"lvn","d":"semaglutide","sys":"endo","q":"Which history is a contraindication to semaglutide?","a":"Personal or family history of medullary thyroid cancer","w":["Seasonal allergies","Mild osteoarthritis","A history of migraine"],"x":"GLP-1 agonists such as semaglutide carry a boxed warning for thyroid C-cell tumors and are contraindicated with a history of medullary thyroid carcinoma or MEN 2. Allergies and arthritis are not contraindications.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp934578e7","lv":"lvn","d":"empagliflozin","sys":"endo","q":"Which side effect is common with empagliflozin?","a":"Genital yeast infections","w":["Weight gain","Frequent low blood sugar when used alone","Dry cough"],"x":"SGLT2 inhibitors increase glucose in the urine, which raises the risk of genital yeast infections and urinary tract infections. They usually cause slight weight loss, not gain.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp55a03179","lv":"lvn","d":"pioglitazone","sys":"endo","q":"Which symptoms in a patient taking pioglitazone should be reported?","a":"Rapid weight gain, swelling, and shortness of breath","w":["Mild headache in the morning","Mild nasal congestion","A slight increase in appetite"],"x":"Pioglitazone causes fluid retention and has a boxed warning for heart failure, so these symptoms must be reported. Mild congestion is a less urgent side effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpec3555f4","lv":"lvn","d":"ondansetron","sys":"gi","q":"Ondansetron belongs to which drug class?","a":"Serotonin 5-HT3 receptor antagonist","w":["Dopamine receptor antagonist","Histamine H2 receptor antagonist","Proton pump inhibitor"],"x":"Ondansetron blocks 5-HT3 serotonin receptors to prevent nausea and vomiting. Metoclopramide and prochlorperazine are the dopamine antagonist antiemetics.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbdde3ebe","lv":"lvn","d":"metoclopramide","sys":"gi","q":"Which symptom in a patient taking metoclopramide should be reported?","a":"Involuntary movements of the face or tongue","w":["Mild loose stools","Mild fatigue","Slight increase in appetite"],"x":"Metoclopramide has a boxed warning for tardive dyskinesia, which may be irreversible. Mild fatigue is a common, less urgent side effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp984e3fc3","lv":"lvn","d":"omeprazole","sys":"gi","q":"When is oral omeprazole usually given for best effect?","a":"Before a meal, typically before breakfast","w":["After the largest meal of the day","At bedtime on a full stomach","With an antacid to speed action"],"x":"Proton pump inhibitors work best when taken before a meal, when acid pumps are being activated. Taking it after eating reduces its effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbd4e0def","lv":"lvn","d":"sucralfate","sys":"gi","q":"Which teaching about sucralfate is correct?","a":"Take it on an empty stomach, apart from other oral medicines","w":["Take it with every meal and with other pills","Take it with an antacid at the same time","Chew it with milk for faster action"],"x":"Sucralfate forms a protective coating and works best on an empty stomach; it can bind other drugs and reduce their absorption. Taking it together with antacids reduces its effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9902cf16","lv":"lvn","d":"lactulose","sys":"gi","q":"How does lactulose help a patient with hepatic encephalopathy?","a":"It traps ammonia in the colon so it passes in the stool","w":["It helps the liver make more albumin","It kills all bacteria in the bowel","It increases protein absorption from food"],"x":"Lactulose acidifies the colon, converting ammonia to a form that is trapped and excreted, which lowers blood ammonia. It does not improve albumin production.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3f5924b4","lv":"lvn","d":"loperamide","sys":"gi","q":"Which finding should be reported before a patient uses loperamide for diarrhea?","a":"Bloody stools with a fever","w":["Two loose stools in one day","Mild stomach cramping","A recent change in diet"],"x":"Bloody diarrhea with fever can signal an invasive infection, and slowing the bowel may make it worse. Mild cramping with loose stools is a common reason for using loperamide.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0d44ba8e","lv":"lvn","d":"bisacodyl","sys":"gi","q":"Which teaching about bisacodyl enteric-coated tablets is correct?","a":"Do not take within an hour of antacids or milk","w":["Chew the tablets for faster relief","Take them with milk to protect the stomach","Crush them and mix into applesauce"],"x":"Antacids and milk can dissolve the enteric coating early, causing stomach irritation and cramping. The tablets must be swallowed whole.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd9b56a01","lv":"lvn","d":"promethazine","sys":"gi","q":"A patient receiving IV promethazine reports burning pain at the IV site. How should the LVN interpret this?","a":"Possible severe tissue injury that must be reported right away","w":["An expected sensation that needs no follow-up","A sign the drug is working on nausea","A mild allergy best treated with ice"],"x":"Promethazine has a boxed warning for severe tissue injury, including gangrene, from IV use or extravasation. Burning or pain at the site must be reported promptly.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2e198846","lv":"lvn","d":"pancrelipase","sys":"gi","q":"Which teaching about pancrelipase capsules is correct?","a":"Take with every meal and snack, swallowing capsules whole","w":["Take once a day at bedtime on an empty stomach","Crush and chew the capsule contents","Take only when stools become loose"],"x":"Pancrelipase replaces digestive enzymes, so it must be taken with food. Crushing or chewing the beads can irritate the mouth and reduce effectiveness.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp10851af3","lv":"lvn","d":"misoprostol","sys":"gi","q":"Before a patient starts misoprostol to prevent NSAID-related ulcers, what is most important to confirm?","a":"That the patient is not pregnant","w":["That the patient has no seasonal allergies","That the patient avoids dairy products","That the patient has normal vision"],"x":"Misoprostol can cause miscarriage and birth defects and has a boxed warning against use in pregnancy for ulcer prevention. Allergies and dairy are not relevant.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp12159d68","lv":"lvn","d":"folic-acid","sys":"ob","q":"Why is folic acid recommended before and during early pregnancy?","a":"To reduce the risk of neural tube defects","w":["To prevent morning sickness","To reduce the risk of preterm labor","To prevent gestational diabetes"],"x":"Adequate folic acid before conception and early in pregnancy lowers the risk of neural tube defects such as spina bifida. It does not treat nausea or prevent gestational diabetes.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8dfe6b0a","lv":"lvn","d":"doxylamine-pyridoxine","sys":"ob","q":"Which teaching is important for a patient taking doxylamine-pyridoxine for nausea in pregnancy?","a":"It can cause drowsiness, so avoid driving until effects are known","w":["It commonly causes insomnia, so take it in the morning","It must be chewed for best absorption","It causes the urine to turn blue"],"x":"Doxylamine is a sedating antihistamine, so drowsiness is common. The tablets are delayed-release and should be swallowed whole, not chewed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfb4b789d","lv":"lvn","d":"levonorgestrel","sys":"ob","q":"When is levonorgestrel emergency contraception most effective?","a":"When taken as soon as possible after unprotected sex","w":["When taken a week after unprotected sex","When taken only after a missed period","When taken daily for a full month"],"x":"Levonorgestrel emergency contraception works mainly by delaying ovulation, so it is most effective when taken as soon as possible. It is not effective once pregnancy is established.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3dc38668","lv":"lvn","d":"labetalol","sys":"ob","q":"Labetalol belongs to which drug class?","a":"Combined alpha-1 and nonselective beta blocker","w":["Calcium channel blocker","ACE inhibitor","Direct arterial vasodilator"],"x":"Labetalol blocks both alpha-1 and beta receptors and is commonly used for hypertension in pregnancy. Hydralazine is the direct vasodilator, and ACE inhibitors are avoided in pregnancy.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp30b369e0","lv":"lvn","d":"terbutaline","sys":"ob","q":"Terbutaline carries a boxed warning about which use?","a":"Prolonged use to prevent preterm labor","w":["Short-term use for an asthma attack","Use for chronic bronchitis","Use in patients with seasonal allergies"],"x":"Terbutaline should not be used for prolonged tocolysis, and oral terbutaline should not be used for preterm labor, because of serious maternal heart problems and death. It is approved for bronchospasm.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp92aa355e","lv":"lvn","d":"drospirenone-ethinyl-estradiol","sys":"ob","q":"Which factor increases the risk of serious cardiovascular events with drospirenone-ethinyl estradiol?","a":"Cigarette smoking, especially over age 35","w":["Taking it at the same time each day","A history of seasonal allergies","Drinking caffeinated coffee"],"x":"Combined hormonal contraceptives carry a boxed warning that smoking increases the risk of heart attack, stroke, and clots, especially in women over 35. Taking the pill at the same time daily is correct use.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpafb3168e","lv":"lvn","d":"ethinyl-estradiol-norgestimate","sys":"ob","q":"Which symptom in a patient taking an ethinyl estradiol-norgestimate pill should be reported right away?","a":"Sudden pain and swelling in one calf","w":["Mild breast tenderness in the first month","Light spotting between periods early on","Mild nausea that improves with food"],"x":"Calf pain and swelling may signal a deep vein thrombosis, a serious risk with estrogen-containing contraceptives. Breast tenderness, spotting, and mild nausea are common early side effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbaa8259d","lv":"lvn","d":"erythromycin-ophthalmic","sys":"ob","q":"Why is erythromycin ophthalmic ointment given to newborns?","a":"To prevent gonococcal eye infection","w":["To prevent newborn jaundice","To treat blocked tear ducts","To prevent vitamin K deficiency bleeding"],"x":"Erythromycin eye ointment is given shortly after birth to prevent ophthalmia neonatorum from gonorrhea. Vitamin K deficiency bleeding is prevented with an injection of vitamin K.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa3ed82c0","lv":"lvn","d":"fezolinetant","sys":"ob","q":"Which lab tests are checked before and during treatment with fezolinetant?","a":"Liver function tests","w":["Thyroid function tests","Serum potassium","Hemoglobin A1C"],"x":"Fezolinetant has a boxed warning for liver injury, so liver tests are checked before starting and periodically during treatment. Thyroid and potassium tests are not specifically required.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1fb56a16","lv":"lvn","d":"ferrous-sulfate","sys":"ob","q":"Which teaching improves absorption of ferrous sulfate in a pregnant patient?","a":"Take it with a source of vitamin C, such as orange juice","w":["Take it with milk or yogurt","Take it with a calcium-based antacid","Take it with a cup of tea"],"x":"Vitamin C helps the body absorb iron. Milk, calcium antacids, and tea reduce iron absorption.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd377ac68","lv":"lvn","d":"epinephrine-auto-injector","sys":"immune","q":"Where is an epinephrine auto-injector given?","a":"The outer thigh, through clothing if needed","w":["The upper arm deltoid muscle only","The abdomen, at least two inches from the navel","Into a vein in the forearm"],"x":"Epinephrine auto-injectors are injected into the anterolateral thigh, and can be given through clothing. Abdominal injection describes subcutaneous drugs such as enoxaparin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa31b5203","lv":"lvn","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"immune","q":"Which adverse effects make diphenhydramine risky for older adults?","a":"Confusion and urinary retention","w":["Diarrhea and excess saliva","High blood sugar and weight loss","Muscle pain and dark urine"],"x":"Diphenhydramine is strongly anticholinergic and sedating, causing confusion, urinary retention, and falls in older adults. Diarrhea and excess saliva are cholinergic effects, the opposite.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5bf8decb","lv":"lvn","d":"loratadine","sys":"immune","q":"How does loratadine compare with diphenhydramine?","a":"It is a second-generation antihistamine that causes less drowsiness","w":["It is more sedating and used mainly for sleep","It works by blocking leukotriene receptors","It is a nasal steroid that reduces swelling"],"x":"Loratadine is a second-generation H1 antihistamine that crosses into the brain less and causes little drowsiness. Blocking leukotrienes is how montelukast works.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd05908f6","lv":"lvn","d":"cyclosporine","sys":"immune","q":"Which item should a patient taking oral cyclosporine avoid?","a":"Grapefruit and grapefruit juice","w":["Fresh green vegetables","Plain yogurt","Whole-grain bread"],"x":"Grapefruit inhibits the enzymes that break down cyclosporine and can raise levels to toxic amounts. The other foods do not have this effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0118dcab","lv":"lvn","d":"tacrolimus","sys":"immune","q":"Which monitoring is routine for a patient taking oral tacrolimus after a transplant?","a":"Trough blood levels and kidney function","w":["Peak flow and oxygen saturation","INR and platelet count only","Thyroid levels and vitamin D"],"x":"Tacrolimus has a narrow therapeutic range and can harm the kidneys, so trough levels and kidney function are checked regularly. INR monitoring is for warfarin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6e65902f","lv":"lvn","d":"mycophenolate","sys":"immune","q":"Mycophenolate carries a boxed warning for which risk?","a":"Pregnancy loss and birth defects","w":["Tendon rupture","Severe hypoglycemia","Hearing loss"],"x":"Mycophenolate has boxed warnings for embryo-fetal toxicity, serious infections, and malignancy, so pregnancy prevention is required. Tendon rupture is a fluoroquinolone warning.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa2c137f3","lv":"lvn","d":"azathioprine","sys":"immune","q":"Which lab test is monitored regularly in a patient taking azathioprine?","a":"Complete blood count","w":["Serum lithium level","Hemoglobin A1C","Serum amylase"],"x":"Azathioprine can suppress the bone marrow, so the CBC is checked for low white cells, red cells, and platelets. Lithium levels are not related.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd3ccee5b","lv":"lvn","d":"golimumab","sys":"immune","q":"Which screening is done before a patient starts golimumab?","a":"Testing for latent tuberculosis","w":["A bone density scan","An eye pressure check","A hearing test"],"x":"TNF blockers such as golimumab carry a boxed warning for serious infections, including reactivation of tuberculosis, so TB testing is done first. Bone density is not routinely required.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp21bb0fce","lv":"lvn","d":"dupilumab","sys":"immune","q":"Which side effect is common with dupilumab?","a":"Eye redness, itching, or irritation","w":["Severe low blood sugar","Hair loss","Slow heart rate"],"x":"Conjunctivitis and other eye problems are among the most common side effects of dupilumab and should be reported if new or worsening. Low blood sugar and slow heart rate are not typical.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp102804df","lv":"lvn","d":"leflunomide","sys":"immune","q":"Which lab tests are monitored in a patient taking leflunomide?","a":"Liver function tests","w":["Serum digoxin level","Thyroid function tests","Serum lithium level"],"x":"Leflunomide has a boxed warning for liver injury, so liver tests are monitored. It also requires pregnancy prevention because of fetal harm.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp277ad818","lv":"lvn","d":"capecitabine","sys":"onc","q":"Which symptom in a patient taking capecitabine should be reported?","a":"Redness, swelling, or pain of the palms and soles","w":["Mild darkening of the urine","A mild increase in appetite","Slight dryness of the lips"],"x":"Capecitabine commonly causes hand-foot syndrome, which can worsen without dose changes by the provider. The other findings are not typical concerns.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc10d4b0a","lv":"lvn","d":"letrozole","sys":"onc","q":"Letrozole belongs to which drug class?","a":"Aromatase inhibitor","w":["Selective estrogen receptor modulator","Antiandrogen","Tyrosine kinase inhibitor"],"x":"Letrozole blocks aromatase, lowering estrogen production in postmenopausal women. Tamoxifen is the selective estrogen receptor modulator.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp76f71b77","lv":"lvn","d":"abiraterone","sys":"onc","q":"Which assessments are monitored in a patient taking abiraterone with prednisone?","a":"Blood pressure, serum potassium, and swelling","w":["Peak flow and oxygen saturation","Vision and hearing","Blood glucose and ketones only"],"x":"Abiraterone can cause mineralocorticoid excess with high blood pressure, low potassium, and fluid retention, which is why prednisone is given with it. Liver tests are also monitored.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp25e9314f","lv":"lvn","d":"megestrol","sys":"onc","q":"Which symptom in a patient taking megestrol should be reported?","a":"Swelling and pain in one leg","w":["A mild increase in appetite","Slight weight gain","Mild hot flashes"],"x":"Megestrol can cause blood clots, and leg pain with swelling may signal a DVT. Increased appetite and weight gain are the intended effects when used for cachexia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2c410389","lv":"lvn","d":"temozolomide","sys":"onc","q":"Which symptoms in a patient taking temozolomide should be reported?","a":"Fever, unusual bruising, or bleeding","w":["Mild tiredness after doses","Slight decrease in appetite","Mild headache"],"x":"Temozolomide suppresses the bone marrow, causing low white cells and platelets, so infection and bleeding signs need prompt reporting. Mild fatigue is common.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp219543e7","lv":"lvn","d":"lenalidomide","sys":"onc","q":"Why is lenalidomide available only through a restricted program?","a":"It can cause severe birth defects","w":["It is highly addictive","It must be given only by IV in hospitals","It causes allergic reactions in most patients"],"x":"Lenalidomide is a thalidomide analog with a boxed warning for embryo-fetal toxicity, so a REMS program requires pregnancy testing and contraception. It is an oral drug and not addictive.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8bf86acc","lv":"lvn","d":"ibrutinib","sys":"onc","q":"Which findings in a patient taking ibrutinib should be reported?","a":"Unusual bleeding or an irregular heartbeat","w":["Mild constipation","Mild dry skin","Slight increase in appetite"],"x":"Ibrutinib increases bleeding risk and can cause atrial fibrillation, so these findings need prompt reporting. Mild dry skin is not a priority concern.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0461b0e8","lv":"lvn","d":"everolimus","sys":"onc","q":"Which side effect is very common with everolimus?","a":"Mouth sores and ulcers","w":["Excess salivation","Hearing loss","Blue-tinted vision"],"x":"Stomatitis is one of the most common everolimus side effects, and alcohol-free mouth care is encouraged. Hearing loss and blue-tinted vision are not typical.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1dac1179","lv":"lvn","d":"erlotinib","sys":"onc","q":"Which side effect is common with erlotinib?","a":"An acne-like rash on the face and chest","w":["Hair darkening","Weight gain from fluid retention","Very slow heart rate"],"x":"EGFR inhibitors such as erlotinib commonly cause an acneiform rash, along with diarrhea. The other effects are not typical of this drug.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp122b8e75","lv":"lvn","d":"enzalutamide","sys":"onc","q":"Which history should be reported before a patient starts enzalutamide?","a":"A history of seizures","w":["Seasonal allergies","Mild osteoarthritis","Lactose intolerance"],"x":"Enzalutamide can lower the seizure threshold, so a seizure history or seizure risk factors must be reported. Allergies and arthritis do not affect its use.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5e7f74d6","lv":"lvn","d":"latanoprost","sys":"skin","q":"Which effect should a patient be taught to expect with latanoprost eye drops?","a":"Gradual, possibly permanent darkening of the iris","w":["Constricted pupils and dim vision at night","Bright orange discoloration of tears","Rapid loss of eyelashes"],"x":"Prostaglandin analogs such as latanoprost can increase brown pigment in the iris and lengthen and darken eyelashes. Constricted pupils with poor night vision are typical of pilocarpine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbeec990c","lv":"lvn","d":"timolol-ophthalmic","sys":"skin","q":"Which history should be reported before a patient starts timolol eye drops?","a":"Asthma or severe COPD","w":["Seasonal allergic rhinitis","Mild osteoarthritis","Recent sunburn"],"x":"Timolol is a nonselective beta blocker that can be absorbed into the bloodstream and trigger bronchospasm or slow the heart. It is contraindicated in asthma and severe COPD.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpad4c0852","lv":"lvn","d":"permethrin","sys":"skin","q":"How is permethrin cream applied to an adult for scabies?","a":"Over the whole body from the neck to the soles of the feet","w":["Only on the visible bumps and burrows","On the scalp only, then rinsed off quickly","Only on the hands and wrists"],"x":"For scabies, permethrin is massaged into all skin from the neck down and washed off after the labeled time. Short scalp application with rinsing is how it is used for head lice.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1479e6e5","lv":"lvn","d":"silver-sulfadiazine","sys":"skin","q":"Which history is a contraindication to silver sulfadiazine cream?","a":"A known allergy to sulfa drugs","w":["A known allergy to penicillin","A history of type 2 diabetes","A history of seasonal allergies"],"x":"Silver sulfadiazine is a sulfonamide, so sulfa allergy is a concern. Penicillin allergy is not a cross-reaction with sulfonamides.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5b70d58f","lv":"lvn","d":"isotretinoin","sys":"skin","q":"Which effect of isotretinoin should be reported promptly?","a":"Depressed mood or thoughts of self-harm","w":["Dry, chapped lips","Mild dry skin","Mild nosebleeds in dry weather"],"x":"Isotretinoin has been linked to depression and suicidal thoughts, which require prompt reporting. Dry lips and skin are very common and expected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa43d0610","lv":"lvn","d":"tretinoin-topical","sys":"skin","q":"Which teaching is correct for a patient using topical tretinoin?","a":"Use sunscreen daily since the skin burns more easily","w":["Apply it to wet skin right after washing","Use it with a tanning bed for faster results","Apply a thick layer several times a day"],"x":"Topical tretinoin increases sun sensitivity, so sunscreen and sun protection are important. Applying it to wet skin or using thick layers increases irritation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7575ca48","lv":"lvn","d":"clobetasol","sys":"skin","q":"Which teaching is correct for clobetasol cream?","a":"Avoid applying it to the face, groin, or underarms","w":["Apply it thickly under a tight bandage daily","Use it long term as an everyday moisturizer","Apply it to the eyelids for eczema"],"x":"Clobetasol is a super-high-potency steroid that can thin skin, so it is avoided on the face, groin, and underarms and used for limited periods. It is not used as a moisturizer.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpececd881","lv":"lvn","d":"pilocarpine-ophthalmic","sys":"skin","q":"Which effect should a patient using pilocarpine eye drops expect?","a":"Trouble seeing in dim light, such as night driving","w":["Darkening of the iris color","Dilated pupils and sensitivity to light","Yellow discoloration of the skin"],"x":"Pilocarpine constricts the pupil, which reduces vision in dim light. Dilated pupils are the opposite of its effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp17eab748","lv":"lvn","d":"lidocaine-prilocaine","sys":"skin","q":"How is lidocaine-prilocaine cream applied before a needle procedure?","a":"As a thick layer under an occlusive dressing, ahead of time","w":["Rubbed in thoroughly right before the needle stick","Applied only after the procedure for pain","Swabbed on and rinsed off right away"],"x":"The cream needs time under an occlusive dressing to numb the skin. Rubbing it in just before the needle stick does not allow enough time for it to work.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp837a552a","lv":"lvn","d":"mupirocin","sys":"skin","q":"Mupirocin ointment is used to treat which condition?","a":"Impetigo caused by staph or strep bacteria","w":["Ringworm and other fungal skin infections","Cold sores caused by herpes virus","Scabies infestation"],"x":"Mupirocin is a topical antibacterial used for impetigo. Fungal infections such as ringworm need an antifungal.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa928b600","lv":"ms1","d":"digoxin","sys":"cardio","q":"What is the primary mechanism by which digoxin increases myocardial contractility?","a":"Inhibition of the Na+/K+ ATPase, raising intracellular calcium","w":["Blockade of L-type calcium channels in myocytes","Stimulation of cardiac beta-1 adrenergic receptors","Inhibition of phosphodiesterase-3 in cardiac muscle"],"x":"Digoxin inhibits the Na+/K+ ATPase, which raises intracellular sodium, slows the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger, and leaves more calcium for contraction. Phosphodiesterase-3 inhibition is how milrinone works, not digoxin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp549ddeb1","lv":"ms1","d":"digoxin","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient taking digoxin for atrial fibrillation is started on a loop diuretic. Which electrolyte change most increases the risk of digoxin toxicity?","a":"Hypokalemia","w":["Hyperkalemia","Hypernatremia","Hypophosphatemia"],"x":"Digoxin competes with potassium at the Na+/K+ ATPase, so low potassium enhances its binding and toxicity. Hyperkalemia can be a result of acute digoxin overdose, but it does not predispose to toxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp97053789","lv":"ms1","d":"amiodarone","sys":"cardio","q":"Which group of tests is classically monitored during long-term amiodarone therapy because of its organ toxicities?","a":"Thyroid function, liver enzymes, and pulmonary function","w":["Serum uric acid, amylase, and lipase","Creatine kinase, audiometry, and serum calcium","Complete blood count, ferritin, and vitamin B12"],"x":"Amiodarone is iodine-rich and lipophilic, causing hypo- or hyperthyroidism, hepatotoxicity, and pulmonary fibrosis. Creatine kinase monitoring is associated with statins, not amiodarone.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6ad965b2","lv":"ms1","d":"atorvastatin","sys":"cardio","q":"Atorvastatin lowers LDL cholesterol primarily by which mechanism?","a":"Inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase in the liver","w":["Blocking intestinal NPC1L1 cholesterol transporters","Activating PPAR-alpha to increase lipoprotein lipase","Binding PCSK9 to prevent LDL receptor degradation"],"x":"Statins inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting step of cholesterol synthesis, which upregulates hepatic LDL receptors. Blocking NPC1L1 is the mechanism of ezetimibe.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5ffccc40","lv":"ms1","d":"lisinopril","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient develops a persistent dry cough after starting lisinopril. What is the mechanism of this adverse effect?","a":"Accumulation of bradykinin due to reduced breakdown by ACE","w":["Direct blockade of the angiotensin II type 1 receptor","Histamine release from airway mast cells","Beta-2 receptor blockade causing bronchoconstriction"],"x":"ACE also degrades bradykinin, so ACE inhibitors raise bradykinin levels, causing cough and contributing to angioedema. AT1 receptor blockade is how ARBs work, and ARBs rarely cause cough.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp68ee79d5","lv":"ms1","d":"ezetimibe","sys":"cardio","q":"What is the mechanism of action of ezetimibe?","a":"Blocks the NPC1L1 transporter, reducing intestinal cholesterol absorption","w":["Inhibits HMG-CoA reductase, reducing hepatic cholesterol synthesis","Binds bile acids in the gut, interrupting enterohepatic circulation","Inhibits hepatic VLDL secretion by reducing lipolysis in fat tissue"],"x":"Ezetimibe blocks NPC1L1 on the intestinal brush border, decreasing cholesterol absorption. Binding bile acids describes bile acid sequestrants such as cholestyramine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp60274ce4","lv":"ms1","d":"verapamil","sys":"cardio","q":"Which adverse effect is classically associated with verapamil?","a":"Constipation","w":["Dry cough","Hirsutism","Hypoglycemia"],"x":"Verapamil, a nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, commonly causes constipation by relaxing intestinal smooth muscle. Dry cough is associated with ACE inhibitors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp67a7bf54","lv":"ms1","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"cardio","q":"Why is nitroglycerin contraindicated in a patient who recently took sildenafil?","a":"Both raise cGMP, risking severe hypotension","w":["Sildenafil blocks the conversion of nitroglycerin to nitric oxide","Both prolong the QT interval, risking torsades de pointes","Sildenafil inhibits CYP3A4, causing nitroglycerin to accumulate"],"x":"Nitrates increase cGMP production through nitric oxide, and PDE5 inhibitors block cGMP breakdown, so together they cause profound vasodilation and hypotension. The interaction is pharmacodynamic, not a CYP3A4 effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4613ad9d","lv":"ms1","d":"hydralazine","sys":"cardio","q":"Hydralazine shares which classic adverse effect with procainamide and isoniazid?","a":"Drug-induced lupus-like syndrome","w":["Angioedema","Gingival hyperplasia","Ototoxicity"],"x":"Hydralazine, procainamide, and isoniazid are classic causes of drug-induced lupus, especially in slow acetylators, often with anti-histone antibodies. Angioedema is linked to ACE inhibitors instead.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe3c979fd","lv":"ms1","d":"niacin","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient starting niacin for dyslipidemia reports facial flushing and warmth. What explains this, and what can reduce it?","a":"Prostaglandin-mediated vasodilation, reduced by taking aspirin beforehand","w":["Histamine release from mast cells, reduced by taking an antihistamine","Serotonin release from platelets, reduced by taking ondansetron","Bradykinin accumulation in skin, reduced by taking an ARB"],"x":"Niacin-induced flushing is mediated by prostaglandins, so pretreatment with aspirin reduces it. It is not primarily histamine-mediated, so antihistamines are not the classic answer.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp34d8f5fb","lv":"ms1","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"Which receptor profile best describes norepinephrine?","a":"Strong alpha-1 and beta-1 agonist with little beta-2 activity","w":["Strong beta-1 and beta-2 agonist with little alpha activity","Selective alpha-1 agonist with no beta activity","Selective beta-1 agonist with no alpha activity"],"x":"Norepinephrine potently stimulates alpha-1 (vasoconstriction) and beta-1 (inotropy) receptors but has minimal beta-2 effect. A selective alpha-1 agonist describes phenylephrine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp86b4557e","lv":"ms1","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"Norepinephrine extravasates from a peripheral IV, causing local blanching and ischemia. Which agent is classically infiltrated into the area?","a":"Phentolamine","w":["Hyaluronidase","Atropine","Dexrazoxane"],"x":"Phentolamine is a nonselective alpha antagonist that reverses local vasoconstriction from vasopressor extravasation. Hyaluronidase is used for some other extravasations but does not counter alpha-mediated vasoconstriction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp031fb59d","lv":"ms1","d":"phenylephrine","sys":"shock","q":"IV phenylephrine raises blood pressure in a hypotensive patient. What change in heart rate is expected?","a":"Decreased heart rate from a baroreceptor reflex","w":["Increased heart rate from direct beta-1 stimulation","Increased heart rate from beta-2 mediated vasodilation","No change because it has no effect on vascular tone"],"x":"Phenylephrine is a selective alpha-1 agonist; the rise in blood pressure triggers a baroreceptor-mediated reflex bradycardia. It has no meaningful beta-1 activity to raise heart rate directly.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1d62cc59","lv":"ms1","d":"dobutamine","sys":"shock","q":"Dobutamine is used in cardiogenic shock mainly because of which action?","a":"Beta-1 agonism that increases myocardial contractility","w":["Alpha-1 agonism that strongly raises vascular resistance","V1 receptor agonism that constricts vascular smooth muscle","Phosphodiesterase-3 inhibition that increases cAMP"],"x":"Dobutamine is predominantly a beta-1 agonist that increases contractility and cardiac output. Phosphodiesterase-3 inhibition is the mechanism of milrinone.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe38b0cf7","lv":"ms1","d":"dopamine","sys":"shock","q":"Besides adrenergic receptors, dopamine stimulates which receptor type in the renal and mesenteric vasculature?","a":"Dopamine D1 receptors","w":["Vasopressin V1 receptors","Histamine H2 receptors","Angiotensin AT1 receptors"],"x":"Dopamine activates D1 receptors in renal and splanchnic vessels, as well as beta-1 and alpha-1 receptors depending on dose. Vasopressin V1 receptors are the target of vasopressin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp74d37940","lv":"ms1","d":"vasopressin","sys":"shock","q":"In vasodilatory shock, vasopressin raises blood pressure mainly by stimulating which receptor?","a":"V1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle","w":["V2 receptors on renal collecting duct cells","Alpha-1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle","Beta-1 receptors on cardiac myocytes"],"x":"Vasopressin causes vasoconstriction through Gq-coupled V1 receptors, independent of adrenergic receptors. V2 receptors in the collecting duct mediate its antidiuretic effect, not its pressor effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5ab862a9","lv":"ms1","d":"milrinone","sys":"shock","q":"What is the mechanism of action of milrinone?","a":"Inhibits phosphodiesterase-3, increasing cAMP in heart and vessels","w":["Stimulates beta-1 receptors, increasing cAMP in the heart","Inhibits the Na+/K+ ATPase, increasing cardiac calcium","Blocks alpha-1 receptors, relaxing vascular smooth muscle"],"x":"Milrinone inhibits PDE3, raising cAMP to increase contractility and cause vasodilation, so it is called an inodilator. Beta-1 stimulation also raises cAMP but is how dobutamine works.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp16dd31e6","lv":"ms1","d":"epinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"For anaphylaxis outside a monitored setting, what is the preferred route of epinephrine?","a":"Intramuscular injection into the anterolateral thigh","w":["Subcutaneous injection into the upper arm","Intravenous push through a peripheral line","Inhaled through a nebulizer mask"],"x":"IM epinephrine in the anterolateral thigh gives faster, more reliable absorption than subcutaneous injection. IV push epinephrine is reserved for monitored settings because it can cause dangerous arrhythmias and hypertension.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8212e832","lv":"ms1","d":"midodrine","sys":"shock","q":"Midodrine is used for symptomatic orthostatic hypotension. Which adverse effect is most characteristic?","a":"Supine hypertension","w":["Reflex tachycardia","Hypoglycemia","Hypokalemia"],"x":"Midodrine's active metabolite is an alpha-1 agonist, so it can raise blood pressure excessively when the patient lies down. It tends to cause reflex bradycardia, not tachycardia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc439757e","lv":"ms1","d":"ephedrine","sys":"shock","q":"Why does repeated dosing of ephedrine produce a progressively smaller blood pressure response?","a":"It acts partly by releasing stored norepinephrine, which becomes depleted","w":["It irreversibly downregulates alpha-1 receptors after one exposure","It is rapidly metabolized by plasma cholinesterase after each dose","It induces its own hepatic metabolism through CYP enzymes"],"x":"Ephedrine is a mixed-acting sympathomimetic that releases norepinephrine from nerve terminals, so stores deplete and tachyphylaxis develops. It is not metabolized by plasma cholinesterase.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7ac3e57f","lv":"ms1","d":"warfarin","sys":"blood","q":"What is the mechanism of action of warfarin?","a":"Inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase, reducing active factors II, VII, IX, and X","w":["Enhances antithrombin activity against thrombin and factor Xa","Directly and reversibly inhibits factor Xa","Irreversibly acetylates platelet cyclooxygenase"],"x":"Warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase, preventing gamma-carboxylation of factors II, VII, IX, X and proteins C and S. Enhancing antithrombin is how heparin works.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc1d68891","lv":"ms1","d":"warfarin","sys":"blood","q":"A patient with a stable INR on warfarin starts fluconazole. What effect on the INR is expected?","a":"INR rises because fluconazole inhibits CYP2C9","w":["INR falls because fluconazole induces CYP2C9","INR falls because fluconazole binds warfarin in the gut","INR is unchanged because the drugs share no pathway"],"x":"S-warfarin is mainly metabolized by CYP2C9, which fluconazole inhibits, so warfarin levels and INR rise. Induction leading to a lower INR is the classic effect of rifampin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp94124842","lv":"ms1","d":"heparin","sys":"blood","q":"Unfractionated heparin produces its anticoagulant effect mainly by which mechanism?","a":"Binding antithrombin and accelerating inactivation of thrombin and factor Xa","w":["Inhibiting vitamin K-dependent carboxylation of clotting factors","Directly binding the active site of thrombin without a cofactor","Blocking the platelet P2Y12 ADP receptor"],"x":"Heparin works as a cofactor, greatly enhancing antithrombin's inactivation of thrombin and factor Xa; its effect is monitored with the aPTT. Direct thrombin binding without a cofactor describes argatroban or dabigatran.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8d29c9e9","lv":"ms1","d":"heparin","sys":"blood","q":"Several days into heparin therapy, a patient's platelet count falls by half and a new deep vein thrombosis develops. What is the underlying mechanism?","a":"IgG antibodies against heparin-platelet factor 4 complexes activate platelets","w":["Heparin directly suppresses megakaryocytes in the bone marrow","Heparin causes vitamin K deficiency, impairing clotting factors","Antibodies against GPIIb/IIIa cause platelet sequestration in the spleen"],"x":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia is caused by IgG antibodies to heparin-PF4 complexes that activate platelets, causing both thrombocytopenia and thrombosis. Marrow suppression would not explain the new clot.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp18b80691","lv":"ms1","d":"enoxaparin","sys":"blood","q":"Compared with unfractionated heparin, how does enoxaparin differ in mechanism?","a":"It acts more selectively against factor Xa than thrombin","w":["It directly inhibits thrombin without needing antithrombin","It inhibits vitamin K-dependent factor synthesis","It acts more selectively against thrombin than factor Xa"],"x":"Low-molecular-weight heparins still work through antithrombin, but their shorter chains favor factor Xa inhibition over thrombin. They still require antithrombin, so they are not direct thrombin inhibitors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp868e447d","lv":"ms1","d":"dabigatran","sys":"blood","q":"Which agent specifically reverses the anticoagulant effect of dabigatran?","a":"Idarucizumab","w":["Andexanet alfa","Protamine sulfate","Phytonadione"],"x":"Idarucizumab is a monoclonal antibody fragment that binds dabigatran, a direct thrombin inhibitor. Andexanet alfa reverses factor Xa inhibitors such as apixaban and rivaroxaban.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp300082a7","lv":"ms1","d":"rivaroxaban","sys":"blood","q":"Rivaroxaban belongs to which anticoagulant class?","a":"Direct factor Xa inhibitor","w":["Direct thrombin inhibitor","Vitamin K antagonist","Indirect factor Xa inhibitor"],"x":"Rivaroxaban binds and inhibits factor Xa directly without needing antithrombin. Fondaparinux is the classic indirect factor Xa inhibitor because it works through antithrombin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf1159726","lv":"ms1","d":"clopidogrel","sys":"blood","q":"A patient with reduced-function CYP2C19 alleles takes clopidogrel after a coronary stent. What is the main concern?","a":"Less conversion to the active metabolite, reducing antiplatelet effect","w":["Accumulation of the active metabolite, increasing bleeding risk","Faster renal clearance of the drug, shortening its action","Increased conversion to a hepatotoxic metabolite"],"x":"Clopidogrel is a prodrug activated by CYP2C19 to irreversibly block the P2Y12 receptor; poor metabolizers form less active drug and have higher risk of stent thrombosis. Bleeding from accumulation would occur with a drug inactivated, not activated, by the enzyme.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp72ac3a04","lv":"ms1","d":"alteplase","sys":"blood","q":"What is the mechanism of action of alteplase?","a":"Converts plasminogen to plasmin, which breaks down fibrin","w":["Inhibits plasminogen activation to stabilize clots","Blocks glycoprotein IIb/IIIa receptors on platelets","Directly inhibits thrombin bound within the clot"],"x":"Alteplase is a recombinant tissue plasminogen activator that converts fibrin-bound plasminogen to plasmin, which degrades fibrin. Inhibiting plasminogen activation is the action of antifibrinolytics such as aminocaproic acid.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa69ab3f8","lv":"ms1","d":"aspirin","sys":"blood","q":"Why does a single dose of aspirin affect platelet function for the life of the platelet?","a":"It irreversibly acetylates COX-1, and platelets cannot make new enzyme","w":["It reversibly binds COX-2, and platelets clear it slowly","It irreversibly blocks the P2Y12 receptor on platelets","It permanently depletes platelet stores of vitamin K"],"x":"Aspirin irreversibly acetylates cyclooxygenase, blocking thromboxane A2 synthesis, and anucleate platelets cannot synthesize new enzyme. Irreversible P2Y12 blockade describes clopidogrel and prasugrel.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp805f10b6","lv":"ms1","d":"succinylcholine","sys":"airway","q":"Succinylcholine is avoided in a patient several days after a major burn injury because of which risk?","a":"Severe hyperkalemia from upregulated acetylcholine receptors","w":["Severe hypoglycemia from increased insulin release","Prolonged paralysis from reduced liver metabolism","Severe hypocalcemia from muscle calcium uptake"],"x":"Burns, crush injuries, and denervation upregulate extrajunctional acetylcholine receptors, so depolarization releases large amounts of potassium. Prolonged paralysis relates to pseudocholinesterase deficiency, not liver metabolism.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpce29215e","lv":"ms1","d":"succinylcholine","sys":"airway","q":"A patient remains paralyzed for hours after a standard intubating dose of succinylcholine. Which inherited condition best explains this?","a":"Pseudocholinesterase deficiency","w":["Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency","Slow N-acetyltransferase activity","Thiopurine methyltransferase deficiency"],"x":"Succinylcholine is hydrolyzed by plasma (pseudo)cholinesterase; atypical or deficient enzyme leads to prolonged apnea. Slow acetylation affects drugs like isoniazid and hydralazine, not succinylcholine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4dac6441","lv":"ms1","d":"rocuronium","sys":"airway","q":"How does rocuronium produce neuromuscular blockade?","a":"Competitively blocks nicotinic receptors at the motor end plate","w":["Persistently depolarizes the motor end plate","Inhibits acetylcholine release from motor nerve terminals","Blocks calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum"],"x":"Rocuronium is a nondepolarizing agent that competes with acetylcholine at nicotinic receptors. Persistent depolarization describes succinylcholine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc253d78f","lv":"ms1","d":"sugammadex","sys":"airway","q":"Sugammadex reverses rocuronium-induced blockade by which mechanism?","a":"Encapsulating rocuronium molecules in the plasma","w":["Inhibiting acetylcholinesterase at the end plate","Directly stimulating nicotinic receptors","Increasing acetylcholine release from nerve terminals"],"x":"Sugammadex is a modified cyclodextrin that binds and encapsulates rocuronium or vecuronium, removing it from the neuromuscular junction. Acetylcholinesterase inhibition is how neostigmine reverses blockade.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe5975078","lv":"ms1","d":"propofol","sys":"airway","q":"Propofol produces sedation and anesthesia mainly by which action?","a":"Potentiating GABA-A receptor chloride currents","w":["Blocking NMDA glutamate receptors","Stimulating central alpha-2 adrenergic receptors","Activating mu opioid receptors"],"x":"Propofol enhances GABA-A receptor activity, increasing inhibitory chloride currents. NMDA receptor antagonism is the mechanism of ketamine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp811b861c","lv":"ms1","d":"ketamine","sys":"airway","q":"Which mechanism is primarily responsible for ketamine's dissociative anesthesia?","a":"NMDA receptor antagonism","w":["GABA-A receptor potentiation","Alpha-2 adrenergic receptor agonism","Kappa opioid receptor antagonism"],"x":"Ketamine blocks NMDA glutamate receptors, producing dissociative anesthesia while largely preserving airway reflexes and respiratory drive. GABA-A potentiation is how propofol and benzodiazepines act.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb7468765","lv":"ms1","d":"etomidate","sys":"airway","q":"Which adverse effect of etomidate results from inhibition of 11-beta-hydroxylase?","a":"Adrenal suppression with reduced cortisol","w":["Malignant hyperthermia","Severe hypotension from vasodilation","Hyperkalemia from potassium release"],"x":"Etomidate inhibits adrenal 11-beta-hydroxylase, transiently reducing cortisol synthesis. It is chosen for its hemodynamic stability, so marked hypotension is less typical than with propofol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp20d89e4e","lv":"ms1","d":"midazolam","sys":"airway","q":"How does midazolam modify GABA-A receptor function?","a":"Increases the frequency of chloride channel opening","w":["Increases the duration of chloride channel opening","Directly opens the channel without GABA present","Blocks GABA reuptake into presynaptic neurons"],"x":"Benzodiazepines increase the frequency of GABA-A chloride channel opening and need GABA present. Increasing the duration of opening is the classic description of barbiturates.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp582ca022","lv":"ms1","d":"sevoflurane","sys":"airway","q":"During sevoflurane anesthesia, a patient develops rising end-tidal CO2, muscle rigidity, and hyperthermia. What is the underlying mechanism?","a":"Uncontrolled calcium release through a defective ryanodine receptor","w":["Excess serotonin activity in the central nervous system","Dopamine D2 receptor blockade in the hypothalamus","Direct thyroid hormone release from the gland"],"x":"Malignant hyperthermia is caused by inherited ryanodine receptor defects that release excess calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum when exposed to volatile anesthetics or succinylcholine; dantrolene treats it. D2 blockade describes neuroleptic malignant syndrome.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb6cdd617","lv":"ms1","d":"cisatracurium","sys":"airway","q":"Why is cisatracurium often chosen for patients with both liver and kidney failure?","a":"It undergoes Hofmann elimination independent of organ function","w":["It is cleared entirely by the kidneys unchanged","It is metabolized by hepatic CYP3A4 to inactive products","It is hydrolyzed by plasma pseudocholinesterase"],"x":"Cisatracurium breaks down spontaneously in plasma by Hofmann elimination, which depends on pH and temperature rather than the liver or kidneys. Pseudocholinesterase hydrolysis describes succinylcholine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa25c0419","lv":"ms1","d":"albuterol","sys":"resp","q":"Albuterol relieves acute bronchospasm through which mechanism?","a":"Beta-2 agonism that raises cAMP and relaxes bronchial smooth muscle","w":["Muscarinic antagonism that blocks bronchial smooth muscle contraction","Leukotriene receptor blockade that reduces airway inflammation","Phosphodiesterase-4 inhibition that reduces neutrophil activity"],"x":"Albuterol stimulates beta-2 receptors, increasing cAMP and relaxing airway smooth muscle; tremor and tachycardia are common effects. Muscarinic antagonism is the mechanism of ipratropium and tiotropium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp90e64af3","lv":"ms1","d":"salmeterol","sys":"resp","q":"In asthma, how should salmeterol be used according to its labeling?","a":"Only together with an inhaled corticosteroid","w":["As the first-line rescue inhaler for acute attacks","As monotherapy for mild intermittent asthma","Only during an acute exacerbation requiring hospitalization"],"x":"Long-acting beta agonist monotherapy in asthma has been linked to asthma-related deaths, so LABAs are used with an inhaled corticosteroid. Its slow onset also makes it unsuitable as a rescue inhaler.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp31f3e233","lv":"ms1","d":"tiotropium","sys":"resp","q":"Tiotropium improves airflow in COPD by which mechanism?","a":"Long-acting antagonism of M3 muscarinic receptors in airway smooth muscle","w":["Long-acting agonism of beta-2 receptors in airway smooth muscle","Inhibition of 5-lipoxygenase in airway inflammatory cells","Stabilization of mast cells to block mediator release"],"x":"Tiotropium is a long-acting muscarinic antagonist that blocks acetylcholine-mediated bronchoconstriction. Long-acting beta-2 agonism describes salmeterol or formoterol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0ea4baf8","lv":"ms1","d":"ipratropium","sys":"resp","q":"Which adverse effect is most expected with inhaled ipratropium?","a":"Dry mouth","w":["Oral candidiasis","Hypokalemia","Hyperglycemia"],"x":"Ipratropium is a muscarinic antagonist, so local anticholinergic effects such as dry mouth are common. Oral candidiasis is associated with inhaled corticosteroids.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpee05920d","lv":"ms1","d":"montelukast","sys":"resp","q":"What is the mechanism of action of montelukast?","a":"Blocks the cysteinyl leukotriene CysLT1 receptor","w":["Inhibits 5-lipoxygenase, blocking leukotriene synthesis","Binds free IgE, preventing mast cell sensitization","Inhibits phosphodiesterase-4 in inflammatory cells"],"x":"Montelukast is a leukotriene receptor antagonist; its labeling carries a boxed warning about neuropsychiatric events. Inhibiting 5-lipoxygenase is the mechanism of zileuton.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbd5a26c9","lv":"ms1","d":"zileuton","sys":"resp","q":"Zileuton reduces asthma symptoms by inhibiting which enzyme, and which lab test is monitored during therapy?","a":"5-lipoxygenase; liver enzymes","w":["Cyclooxygenase-2; serum creatinine","Phosphodiesterase-4; blood glucose","Phospholipase A2; complete blood count"],"x":"Zileuton inhibits 5-lipoxygenase, blocking leukotriene synthesis, and can cause hepatotoxicity, so liver enzymes are monitored. Phosphodiesterase-4 inhibition is the mechanism of roflumilast.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp388cd02a","lv":"ms1","d":"theophylline","sys":"resp","q":"A patient on theophylline is started on ciprofloxacin and develops tremor, tachycardia, and a seizure. What explains this?","a":"Ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2, raising theophylline levels","w":["Ciprofloxacin induces CYP1A2, producing a toxic metabolite","Theophylline raises ciprofloxacin levels by blocking renal secretion","Both drugs block adenosine reuptake in the brain"],"x":"Theophylline has a narrow therapeutic index and is cleared by CYP1A2, which ciprofloxacin inhibits, causing toxicity with arrhythmias and seizures. CYP induction would lower, not raise, theophylline levels.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa5e5212f","lv":"ms1","d":"fluticasone","sys":"resp","q":"What should a patient be taught to reduce the risk of oral thrush with inhaled fluticasone?","a":"Rinse the mouth and spit after each use","w":["Use the inhaler only during acute attacks","Take the inhaler with a full meal","Inhale a short-acting beta agonist afterward"],"x":"Inhaled corticosteroids deposit in the oropharynx and suppress local immunity, so rinsing and spitting reduces candidiasis. Inhaled corticosteroids are controller medicines, not rescue medicines for acute attacks.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp915b3b4a","lv":"ms1","d":"omalizumab","sys":"resp","q":"Omalizumab is used in allergic asthma because of which mechanism?","a":"Binds free IgE, preventing it from attaching to mast cell receptors","w":["Blocks the IL-5 cytokine, reducing eosinophil survival","Blocks the IL-4 receptor alpha subunit on T cells","Inhibits leukotriene synthesis in mast cells"],"x":"Omalizumab is an anti-IgE monoclonal antibody that lowers free IgE and prevents binding to FcεRI on mast cells and basophils. Blocking IL-5 describes mepolizumab, a different biologic.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp12cdac19","lv":"ms1","d":"roflumilast","sys":"resp","q":"Roflumilast is used to reduce COPD exacerbations. What is its mechanism?","a":"Inhibits phosphodiesterase-4, increasing cAMP in inflammatory cells","w":["Inhibits phosphodiesterase-5, increasing cGMP in pulmonary vessels","Antagonizes M3 muscarinic receptors in airway smooth muscle","Stimulates beta-2 receptors in airway smooth muscle"],"x":"Roflumilast selectively inhibits PDE4, raising cAMP in inflammatory cells and reducing lung inflammation. PDE5 inhibition describes sildenafil used for pulmonary hypertension.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp184142f5","lv":"ms1","d":"phenytoin","sys":"neuro","q":"What is the primary mechanism of phenytoin's antiseizure effect?","a":"Blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in the inactivated state","w":["Blocks T-type calcium channels in thalamic neurons","Increases the frequency of GABA-A chloride channel opening","Binds synaptic vesicle protein SV2A to reduce release"],"x":"Phenytoin stabilizes inactivated sodium channels, limiting high-frequency repetitive firing. T-type calcium channel blockade is the mechanism of ethosuximide.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa7378f07","lv":"ms1","d":"phenytoin","sys":"neuro","q":"A small increase in phenytoin dosing causes a large rise in serum level and toxicity. Which pharmacokinetic property explains this?","a":"Saturable metabolism that shifts to zero-order kinetics","w":["Very high renal clearance of unchanged drug","Strong induction of its own metabolism over time","Large first-pass effect that varies with meals"],"x":"Phenytoin's hepatic metabolism saturates at therapeutic levels, so elimination becomes zero-order and levels rise disproportionately. Autoinduction is the classic property of carbamazepine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6879969b","lv":"ms1","d":"carbamazepine","sys":"neuro","q":"Before starting carbamazepine in patients of Asian ancestry, which genetic test is recommended to reduce the risk of Stevens-Johnson syndrome?","a":"HLA-B*15:02","w":["HLA-B*57:01","HLA-B*58:01","HLA-B27"],"x":"HLA-B*15:02 carriers have a much higher risk of carbamazepine-induced SJS and toxic epidermal necrolysis. HLA-B*57:01 screening is used before abacavir.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0c6100e9","lv":"ms1","d":"valproic-acid","sys":"neuro","q":"Valproic acid use in early pregnancy is most classically associated with which birth defect?","a":"Neural tube defects such as spina bifida","w":["Ebstein anomaly of the tricuspid valve","Tooth discoloration and enamel defects","Gray baby syndrome"],"x":"Valproate is strongly teratogenic and classically causes neural tube defects. Ebstein anomaly is classically linked to lithium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp913d761c","lv":"ms1","d":"ethosuximide","sys":"neuro","q":"Ethosuximide is effective for absence seizures because of which mechanism?","a":"Blocks T-type calcium channels in thalamic neurons","w":["Blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in cortical neurons","Irreversibly inhibits GABA transaminase","Antagonizes AMPA glutamate receptors"],"x":"Absence seizures arise from thalamocortical oscillations driven by T-type calcium currents, which ethosuximide blocks. Irreversible GABA transaminase inhibition is the mechanism of vigabatrin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp347defba","lv":"ms1","d":"lamotrigine","sys":"neuro","q":"Lamotrigine must be titrated slowly, especially when given with valproate, to reduce the risk of which serious reaction?","a":"Stevens-Johnson syndrome","w":["Aplastic anemia","Acute pancreatitis","Kidney stones"],"x":"Rapid titration and valproate (which raises lamotrigine levels) increase the risk of serious rashes such as SJS. Kidney stones are associated with topiramate.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd5bbf00c","lv":"ms1","d":"carbidopa-levodopa","sys":"neuro","q":"Why is carbidopa combined with levodopa in Parkinson disease?","a":"It blocks peripheral DOPA decarboxylase so more levodopa reaches the brain","w":["It blocks central MAO-B so dopamine breakdown slows","It directly stimulates dopamine D2 receptors in the striatum","It inhibits COMT so levodopa is converted to dopamine faster"],"x":"Carbidopa does not cross the blood-brain barrier; it inhibits peripheral conversion of levodopa to dopamine, increasing CNS delivery and reducing nausea. COMT inhibition is the mechanism of entacapone, a different add-on.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1828d294","lv":"ms1","d":"selegiline","sys":"neuro","q":"Selegiline is used in Parkinson disease because it inhibits which enzyme?","a":"Monoamine oxidase type B","w":["Catechol-O-methyltransferase","Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase","Acetylcholinesterase"],"x":"Selegiline selectively inhibits MAO-B, reducing dopamine breakdown in the brain. COMT inhibition is the action of entacapone.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpda668673","lv":"ms1","d":"donepezil","sys":"neuro","q":"Donepezil treats symptoms of Alzheimer disease by which mechanism?","a":"Inhibiting acetylcholinesterase in the brain","w":["Blocking NMDA glutamate receptors","Stimulating muscarinic receptors directly","Inhibiting monoamine oxidase type B"],"x":"Donepezil is a centrally acting acetylcholinesterase inhibitor that increases synaptic acetylcholine. NMDA receptor blockade describes memantine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp360624bc","lv":"ms1","d":"sumatriptan","sys":"neuro","q":"Sumatriptan is contraindicated in a patient with coronary artery disease because of which effect?","a":"5-HT1B receptor agonism can cause coronary vasoconstriction","w":["5-HT3 receptor blockade can cause QT prolongation","Dopamine D2 receptor blockade can cause arrhythmias","Beta-1 receptor blockade can worsen heart failure"],"x":"Triptans are 5-HT1B/1D agonists; 5-HT1B activity constricts blood vessels, including coronary arteries, so they are contraindicated in ischemic heart disease. 5-HT3 blockade describes ondansetron.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp42460bc8","lv":"ms1","d":"morphine","sys":"pain","q":"Patients taking morphine long term develop tolerance to many of its effects. To which effects does tolerance typically NOT develop?","a":"Constipation and miosis","w":["Euphoria and sedation","Respiratory depression and nausea","Analgesia and itching"],"x":"Tolerance develops to analgesia, euphoria, sedation, nausea, and respiratory depression, but not to constipation or pupillary constriction. Respiratory depression shows tolerance, which is why opioid-naive patients are at greater risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp40bac490","lv":"ms1","d":"naloxone","sys":"pain","q":"After naloxone reverses a methadone overdose, why must the patient be observed for several hours?","a":"Naloxone's effect can wear off before methadone's does","w":["Naloxone can cause delayed respiratory depression by itself","Naloxone must be converted to an active metabolite over hours","Methadone blocks naloxone from binding opioid receptors"],"x":"Naloxone has a shorter duration than many opioids, especially methadone, so sedation and respiratory depression can return. Naloxone is a pure antagonist and does not cause respiratory depression itself.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp72da479f","lv":"ms1","d":"buprenorphine","sys":"pain","q":"A patient dependent on heroin takes buprenorphine shortly after last use and develops abrupt withdrawal. What property of buprenorphine explains this?","a":"Partial mu agonist with high receptor affinity that displaces full agonists","w":["Full mu agonist with a very short half-life","Pure mu antagonist with low receptor affinity","Kappa agonist that directly triggers withdrawal pathways"],"x":"Buprenorphine binds mu receptors tightly but activates them only partially, so it displaces full agonists and can precipitate withdrawal. A pure antagonist such as naltrexone also precipitates withdrawal but is not buprenorphine's mechanism.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf2f45077","lv":"ms1","d":"methadone","sys":"pain","q":"Which cardiac adverse effect is especially associated with methadone?","a":"QT interval prolongation and torsades de pointes","w":["Atrioventricular block from calcium channel blockade","Coronary vasospasm from serotonin receptor activation","Dilated cardiomyopathy from cumulative myocardial injury"],"x":"Methadone blocks cardiac hERG potassium channels, prolonging the QT interval and risking torsades. Coronary vasospasm is associated with triptans, not methadone.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4dabc4e3","lv":"ms1","d":"meperidine","sys":"pain","q":"Repeated meperidine dosing in a patient with kidney disease causes tremor and seizures. Which explains this?","a":"Accumulation of the metabolite normeperidine","w":["Accumulation of the metabolite morphine-6-glucuronide","Direct NMDA receptor activation by meperidine","Formation of NAPQI from hepatic metabolism"],"x":"Normeperidine is a renally cleared neurotoxic metabolite that lowers the seizure threshold. Morphine-6-glucuronide accumulation causes prolonged sedation with morphine, not meperidine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcc99bd84","lv":"ms1","d":"codeine","sys":"pain","q":"Why is codeine dangerous in a person who is a CYP2D6 ultrarapid metabolizer?","a":"Codeine is converted to morphine faster, risking opioid toxicity","w":["Codeine is inactivated faster, so pain is not relieved","Codeine is converted to a hepatotoxic metabolite faster","Codeine accumulates unchanged, causing seizures"],"x":"Codeine is a prodrug activated to morphine by CYP2D6; ultrarapid metabolizers can develop life-threatening respiratory depression, which is why it is contraindicated in young children. Poor metabolizers, not ultrarapid ones, get little pain relief.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6bc8dcc5","lv":"ms1","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"pain","q":"In acetaminophen overdose, which metabolite causes liver injury?","a":"N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine (NAPQI)","w":["Acetaminophen glucuronide","Acetaminophen sulfate","Salicylic acid"],"x":"When glucuronidation and sulfation pathways are saturated, more drug is oxidized by CYP2E1 to NAPQI, which depletes glutathione and damages hepatocytes. Glucuronide and sulfate conjugates are nontoxic.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb82653b4","lv":"ms1","d":"ibuprofen","sys":"pain","q":"In a patient with heart failure, ibuprofen can reduce kidney function by which mechanism?","a":"Blocking prostaglandin-mediated dilation of the afferent arteriole","w":["Blocking angiotensin II-mediated constriction of the efferent arteriole","Directly damaging the glomerular basement membrane","Precipitating as crystals in the renal tubules"],"x":"When renal perfusion is low, prostaglandins dilate the afferent arteriole to maintain GFR; NSAIDs block this. Reducing efferent constriction is how ACE inhibitors lower GFR.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc28f8415","lv":"ms1","d":"celecoxib","sys":"pain","q":"Compared with nonselective NSAIDs, celecoxib has which characteristic?","a":"Less GI ulceration but an increased risk of cardiovascular thrombosis","w":["More GI ulceration but no cardiovascular risk","Irreversible platelet inhibition similar to aspirin","No effect on kidney function in at-risk patients"],"x":"Selective COX-2 inhibition spares gastroprotective COX-1 prostaglandins but reduces prostacyclin without blocking thromboxane, raising thrombotic risk. It does not inhibit platelets like aspirin and still affects the kidneys.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdbe5be7a","lv":"ms1","d":"bupivacaine","sys":"pain","q":"During a nerve block with bupivacaine, a patient develops seizures followed by ventricular arrhythmias. Which therapy is specifically used for this toxicity?","a":"Intravenous lipid emulsion","w":["Intravenous flumazenil","Intravenous naloxone","Intravenous physostigmine"],"x":"Local anesthetic systemic toxicity, especially from lipophilic bupivacaine, is treated with lipid emulsion along with standard resuscitation. Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines and has no role here.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe85abad6","lv":"ms1","d":"fluoxetine","sys":"psych","q":"A patient taking fluoxetine is started on phenelzine and develops agitation, hyperthermia, diaphoresis, and inducible clonus. Which condition is most likely?","a":"Serotonin syndrome","w":["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Malignant hyperthermia","Anticholinergic toxicity"],"x":"Combining an SSRI with an MAOI causes excess serotonin, producing the triad of mental status change, autonomic instability, and neuromuscular hyperactivity such as clonus. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome features lead-pipe rigidity and slow onset rather than clonus.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe78e947c","lv":"ms1","d":"bupropion","sys":"psych","q":"Bupropion is contraindicated in patients with a current or past eating disorder such as bulimia because of which risk?","a":"Lowered seizure threshold","w":["Severe weight gain","QT interval prolongation","Hypertensive crisis with tyramine"],"x":"Bupropion, a norepinephrine-dopamine reuptake inhibitor, lowers the seizure threshold, and patients with eating disorders have higher seizure risk. It tends to cause weight loss, not gain.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1b05929b","lv":"ms1","d":"venlafaxine","sys":"psych","q":"Which adverse effect of venlafaxine reflects its norepinephrine reuptake inhibition and is dose related?","a":"Elevated blood pressure","w":["Orthostatic hypotension","Hypothyroidism","Agranulocytosis"],"x":"Venlafaxine is an SNRI, and its noradrenergic effect can raise blood pressure, especially at higher doses. Orthostatic hypotension is more typical of tricyclics and alpha-1 blocking drugs.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpda2e9e8a","lv":"ms1","d":"amitriptyline","sys":"psych","q":"In amitriptyline overdose with a widened QRS complex, what is the mechanism of the QRS change, and which therapy is used?","a":"Cardiac sodium channel blockade; sodium bicarbonate","w":["Cardiac potassium channel blockade; magnesium sulfate","Muscarinic receptor blockade; physostigmine","Alpha-1 receptor blockade; norepinephrine"],"x":"Tricyclic antidepressants block fast sodium channels, widening the QRS; sodium bicarbonate overcomes the blockade through sodium loading and alkalinization. Physostigmine is avoided in TCA overdose because it can worsen conduction problems.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp30ef330a","lv":"ms1","d":"phenelzine","sys":"psych","q":"A patient taking phenelzine eats aged cheese and develops a severe headache with markedly elevated blood pressure. What explains this?","a":"Dietary tyramine is not broken down and releases norepinephrine","w":["Dietary tyramine blocks serotonin reuptake in the brain","Phenelzine binds calcium in cheese, causing vasospasm","Dietary histamine directly constricts cerebral vessels"],"x":"MAOIs block intestinal and hepatic breakdown of tyramine, which then acts as an indirect sympathomimetic, releasing norepinephrine and causing hypertensive crisis. It is not a serotonin reuptake effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4de54d4b","lv":"ms1","d":"lithium","sys":"psych","q":"A patient stable on lithium is started on hydrochlorothiazide. What change is expected?","a":"Lithium levels rise because of increased proximal tubule reabsorption","w":["Lithium levels fall because of increased renal excretion","Lithium levels fall because of reduced intestinal absorption","Lithium levels stay the same because thiazides act distally"],"x":"Thiazides cause volume contraction, and the proximal tubule reabsorbs more sodium and lithium, raising lithium levels and toxicity risk. NSAIDs and ACE inhibitors raise lithium levels too, so none of these lowers them.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp73b02411","lv":"ms1","d":"lithium","sys":"psych","q":"A patient on long-term lithium develops polyuria and polydipsia with dilute urine that does not concentrate after desmopressin. What is the most likely cause?","a":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","w":["Central diabetes insipidus","Primary polydipsia","Syndrome of inappropriate ADH"],"x":"Lithium impairs collecting duct response to ADH, causing nephrogenic diabetes insipidus that does not respond to desmopressin. Central diabetes insipidus would improve with desmopressin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpce71067b","lv":"ms1","d":"haloperidol","sys":"psych","q":"Hours after receiving haloperidol, a patient develops sustained painful twisting of the neck and upward eye deviation. Which drug class is used to treat this?","a":"An anticholinergic agent such as benztropine","w":["A dopamine agonist such as bromocriptine","A muscle relaxant such as dantrolene","A beta blocker such as propranolol"],"x":"Acute dystonia from D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway responds rapidly to anticholinergics such as benztropine or diphenhydramine. Bromocriptine and dantrolene are associated with neuroleptic malignant syndrome, and propranolol with akathisia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp00fc897d","lv":"ms1","d":"clozapine","sys":"psych","q":"What monitoring is required during clozapine therapy because of its most serious hematologic risk?","a":"Absolute neutrophil count for severe neutropenia","w":["Platelet count for thrombocytopenia","Hemoglobin for hemolytic anemia","Coagulation studies for bleeding"],"x":"Clozapine can cause severe neutropenia (agranulocytosis), so the ANC is monitored on a set schedule. Thrombocytopenia is not its classic hematologic risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4d5a89bf","lv":"ms1","d":"olanzapine","sys":"psych","q":"Which adverse effect profile is most characteristic of olanzapine?","a":"Weight gain, hyperglycemia, and dyslipidemia","w":["Weight loss, hypoglycemia, and low triglycerides","Agranulocytosis requiring frequent blood counts","Marked QT prolongation with minimal weight effects"],"x":"Olanzapine, like clozapine, carries a high risk of metabolic syndrome, so weight, glucose, and lipids are monitored. Agranulocytosis requiring scheduled counts is the hallmark of clozapine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd6a125a0","lv":"ms1","d":"furosemide","sys":"renal","q":"Furosemide produces diuresis by inhibiting which transporter?","a":"Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter in the thick ascending limb","w":["Na+/Cl- cotransporter in the distal convoluted tubule","Epithelial sodium channel in the collecting duct","Carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule"],"x":"Loop diuretics inhibit NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb, which reabsorbs a large share of filtered sodium. The Na+/Cl- cotransporter in the distal tubule is the thiazide target.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcb14de9f","lv":"ms1","d":"furosemide","sys":"renal","q":"Combining furosemide with gentamicin raises the risk of which shared toxicity?","a":"Ototoxicity","w":["Hepatotoxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis","Peripheral neuropathy"],"x":"Loop diuretics and aminoglycosides are both ototoxic, and the combination increases the risk of hearing loss. Hepatotoxicity is not a classic effect of either.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp033d2e60","lv":"ms1","d":"hydrochlorothiazide","sys":"renal","q":"Hydrochlorothiazide is sometimes used in patients with recurrent calcium kidney stones because of which effect?","a":"It increases calcium reabsorption in the distal tubule","w":["It increases calcium excretion in the urine","It acidifies the urine to dissolve calcium crystals","It binds dietary calcium in the intestine"],"x":"Thiazides enhance distal tubule calcium reabsorption, lowering urinary calcium and risk of stones, and can cause hypercalcemia. Increasing calcium excretion is an effect of loop diuretics.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc195b7ea","lv":"ms1","d":"spironolactone","sys":"renal","q":"A man taking spironolactone for heart failure develops breast enlargement. What explains this effect?","a":"Antiandrogenic activity at androgen receptors","w":["Direct stimulation of prolactin release","Inhibition of the epithelial sodium channel","Excess conversion of cortisol to estrogen"],"x":"Spironolactone blocks aldosterone receptors but also androgen receptors, causing gynecomastia; eplerenone is more selective. ENaC inhibition describes amiloride and triamterene, which do not cause gynecomastia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9911c1ff","lv":"ms1","d":"acetazolamide","sys":"renal","q":"Which acid-base disturbance is expected with ongoing acetazolamide therapy?","a":"Hyperchloremic non-anion gap metabolic acidosis","w":["Hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis","Anion gap metabolic acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis with high bicarbonate"],"x":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibition in the proximal tubule causes bicarbonate wasting, producing a non-anion gap metabolic acidosis. Metabolic alkalosis is the typical effect of loop and thiazide diuretics.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4e252abc","lv":"ms1","d":"mannitol","sys":"renal","q":"In which patient is mannitol contraindicated?","a":"A patient with anuria from established kidney failure","w":["A patient with raised intracranial pressure","A patient with elevated intraocular pressure","A patient with normal kidney function before surgery"],"x":"Mannitol is an osmotic diuretic that needs to be filtered and excreted; in anuria it stays in the circulation, expanding volume and risking pulmonary edema. It is used to lower intracranial and intraocular pressure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf24ea9dd","lv":"ms1","d":"amiloride","sys":"renal","q":"Amiloride spares potassium by which mechanism?","a":"Blocking epithelial sodium channels in the collecting duct","w":["Competitively antagonizing aldosterone receptors","Inhibiting the Na+/Cl- cotransporter in the distal tubule","Inhibiting carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule"],"x":"Amiloride directly blocks ENaC in principal cells, reducing sodium reabsorption and the potassium secretion that depends on it. Aldosterone receptor antagonism is how spironolactone works.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpea86bdc1","lv":"ms1","d":"sodium-polystyrene-sulfonate","sys":"renal","q":"How does sodium polystyrene sulfonate lower serum potassium?","a":"Exchanges sodium for potassium in the colon for fecal excretion","w":["Shifts potassium into cells by stimulating Na+/K+ ATPase","Increases potassium secretion in the collecting duct","Stabilizes cardiac membranes against potassium effects"],"x":"Sodium polystyrene sulfonate is a cation-exchange resin that binds potassium in the gut in exchange for sodium. Shifting potassium into cells describes insulin and beta-2 agonists.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdc570f7c","lv":"ms1","d":"tamsulosin","sys":"renal","q":"Tamsulosin relieves urinary symptoms of benign prostatic hyperplasia by which mechanism?","a":"Blocking alpha-1A receptors in prostate and bladder neck smooth muscle","w":["Inhibiting 5-alpha reductase to shrink prostate tissue","Blocking M3 muscarinic receptors in the bladder wall","Stimulating beta-3 receptors in the detrusor muscle"],"x":"Tamsulosin selectively blocks alpha-1A receptors, relaxing smooth muscle and improving urine flow; it is also linked to intraoperative floppy iris syndrome. Shrinking the prostate is the action of finasteride.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3cac49f9","lv":"ms1","d":"finasteride","sys":"renal","q":"Finasteride reduces prostate size by which mechanism?","a":"Inhibiting conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone","w":["Blocking androgen receptors in prostate tissue","Suppressing pituitary LH release","Blocking alpha-1 receptors in the prostate"],"x":"Finasteride inhibits type 2 5-alpha reductase, lowering DHT and shrinking prostate tissue over months. Androgen receptor blockade describes drugs like flutamide.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc889b7ca","lv":"ms1","d":"alendronate","sys":"bone","q":"What is the mechanism of action of alendronate?","a":"Binds bone mineral and inhibits osteoclast-mediated resorption","w":["Stimulates osteoblasts as a parathyroid hormone analog","Binds RANKL to prevent osteoclast formation","Acts as an estrogen agonist on bone"],"x":"Bisphosphonates bind hydroxyapatite and are taken up by osteoclasts, where they inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase and resorption. RANKL binding is the mechanism of denosumab.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf37716da","lv":"ms1","d":"alendronate","sys":"bone","q":"Which instruction best reduces the risk of esophageal injury with oral alendronate?","a":"Take it on an empty stomach with plain water and stay upright afterward","w":["Take it with a full meal and milk to protect the stomach","Take it at bedtime lying down so it stays in the stomach","Take it together with a calcium supplement and orange juice"],"x":"Alendronate can cause esophagitis if it lodges in the esophagus, and food, calcium, and other drinks block its absorption. Lying down after the dose increases esophageal injury risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6005f01b","lv":"ms1","d":"allopurinol","sys":"bone","q":"Why is the combination of allopurinol and azathioprine risky?","a":"Allopurinol blocks xanthine oxidase, raising active thiopurine levels and marrow toxicity","w":["Allopurinol induces CYP3A4, lowering azathioprine to ineffective levels","Azathioprine blocks allopurinol absorption, causing gout flares","Both drugs cause severe hyperkalemia through renal effects"],"x":"Xanthine oxidase metabolizes 6-mercaptopurine (azathioprine's active product), so inhibiting it raises levels and risks severe bone marrow suppression. The interaction is enzyme inhibition, not CYP induction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpedfba7c8","lv":"ms1","d":"colchicine","sys":"bone","q":"Colchicine reduces gout inflammation by which mechanism?","a":"Inhibiting microtubule polymerization, reducing neutrophil migration","w":["Inhibiting xanthine oxidase, reducing uric acid production","Inhibiting renal urate reabsorption, increasing excretion","Converting uric acid to allantoin"],"x":"Colchicine binds tubulin and impairs microtubule-dependent neutrophil chemotaxis and degranulation. It does not lower uric acid, unlike xanthine oxidase inhibitors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7c6719fd","lv":"ms1","d":"probenecid","sys":"bone","q":"How does probenecid lower serum uric acid?","a":"Inhibits proximal tubule reabsorption of uric acid","w":["Inhibits xanthine oxidase in the liver","Converts uric acid to the soluble compound allantoin","Reduces neutrophil migration into the joint"],"x":"Probenecid is a uricosuric that blocks urate reabsorption in the proximal tubule; it also reduces tubular secretion of penicillin. Conversion to allantoin is the action of rasburicase and pegloticase.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpeb360b89","lv":"ms1","d":"febuxostat","sys":"bone","q":"Febuxostat lowers uric acid by which mechanism?","a":"Nonpurine inhibition of xanthine oxidase","w":["Purine analog inhibition of xanthine oxidase","Inhibition of renal urate reabsorption","Recombinant urate oxidase activity"],"x":"Febuxostat is a nonpurine selective xanthine oxidase inhibitor. Allopurinol is the purine analog inhibitor of the same enzyme.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2635e31d","lv":"ms1","d":"teriparatide","sys":"bone","q":"Why does intermittent teriparatide build bone rather than cause bone loss?","a":"Intermittent PTH exposure stimulates osteoblasts more than osteoclasts","w":["It irreversibly inhibits osteoclasts after each dose","It binds RANKL and prevents osteoclast formation","It acts as an estrogen agonist on bone tissue"],"x":"Teriparatide is a recombinant parathyroid hormone analog; intermittent exposure is anabolic, while continuously high PTH increases resorption. RANKL binding is how denosumab works.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6685ae9c","lv":"ms1","d":"denosumab","sys":"bone","q":"What is the target of denosumab?","a":"RANKL","w":["Sclerostin","TNF-alpha","Osteoprotegerin"],"x":"Denosumab is a monoclonal antibody that binds RANKL, mimicking osteoprotegerin and preventing osteoclast formation and activity. Sclerostin is the target of romosozumab.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf0f3a08e","lv":"ms1","d":"raloxifene","sys":"bone","q":"Raloxifene reduces osteoporotic fractures but carries which important risk?","a":"Venous thromboembolism","w":["Endometrial cancer","Breast cancer","Osteonecrosis of the jaw"],"x":"Raloxifene is a SERM with estrogen agonist effects on bone and clotting factors, increasing VTE risk; it is antagonistic in breast and does not stimulate the endometrium. Endometrial cancer risk is associated with tamoxifen.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp80eb8baa","lv":"ms1","d":"rasburicase","sys":"bone","q":"Before giving rasburicase for tumor lysis syndrome, which condition should be screened for?","a":"Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency","w":["Thiopurine methyltransferase deficiency","Pseudocholinesterase deficiency","Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase deficiency"],"x":"Rasburicase converts uric acid to allantoin and generates hydrogen peroxide, which can cause hemolysis and methemoglobinemia in G6PD deficiency. TPMT deficiency is relevant to thiopurines such as azathioprine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpefc0bedf","lv":"ms1","d":"n-acetylcysteine","sys":"tox","q":"How does N-acetylcysteine protect the liver in acetaminophen overdose?","a":"Replenishes glutathione, which detoxifies NAPQI","w":["Blocks CYP2E1 conversion of acetaminophen","Binds acetaminophen in the gut to prevent absorption","Increases renal excretion of unchanged acetaminophen"],"x":"N-acetylcysteine is a glutathione precursor and substitute that conjugates the toxic metabolite NAPQI. Binding drug in the gut describes activated charcoal.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp10ecfe2a","lv":"ms1","d":"fomepizole","sys":"tox","q":"Fomepizole treats ethylene glycol and methanol poisoning by which mechanism?","a":"Inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase, preventing toxic metabolite formation","w":["Binds the parent alcohol in the blood for renal excretion","Inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase to block acid formation","Increases conversion of the toxic acids to carbon dioxide"],"x":"Fomepizole competitively inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase, so the parent compound is not converted into toxic acids such as formic acid and oxalate. Aldehyde dehydrogenase inhibition is the mechanism of disulfiram.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp179b8a4e","lv":"ms1","d":"flumazenil","sys":"tox","q":"Why is flumazenil generally avoided in a patient with unknown overdose and long-term benzodiazepine use?","a":"It can precipitate benzodiazepine withdrawal seizures","w":["It can cause severe respiratory depression","It can cause prolonged neuromuscular paralysis","It can trigger malignant hyperthermia"],"x":"Flumazenil is a competitive GABA-A benzodiazepine-site antagonist, and abrupt reversal in dependent patients or co-ingestions such as TCAs can provoke seizures. It does not cause respiratory depression.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe6ebfc3f","lv":"ms1","d":"physostigmine","sys":"tox","q":"Physostigmine, rather than neostigmine, is used to reverse central anticholinergic delirium because physostigmine has which property?","a":"It is a tertiary amine that crosses the blood-brain barrier","w":["It is a quaternary amine that stays in the periphery","It directly stimulates muscarinic receptors","It irreversibly inhibits acetylcholinesterase"],"x":"Physostigmine's tertiary amine structure allows CNS entry, so it can reverse central anticholinergic effects. Neostigmine is a quaternary amine that does not cross well into the brain.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp03b22225","lv":"ms1","d":"pralidoxime","sys":"tox","q":"In organophosphate poisoning, why is pralidoxime given early?","a":"It reactivates acetylcholinesterase before the enzyme bond ages","w":["It blocks muscarinic receptors before secretions worsen","It binds the organophosphate in the gut before absorption","It speeds renal elimination before the toxin redistributes"],"x":"Pralidoxime regenerates phosphorylated acetylcholinesterase, but once the bond undergoes aging the enzyme cannot be reactivated. Blocking muscarinic receptors is atropine's role.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd15e99b1","lv":"ms1","d":"atropine","sys":"tox","q":"In organophosphate poisoning, which finding is NOT reversed by atropine?","a":"Muscle fasciculations and weakness","w":["Bronchorrhea and wheezing","Bradycardia","Excess salivation and sweating"],"x":"Atropine blocks muscarinic receptors but not nicotinic receptors at the neuromuscular junction, so muscle weakness and fasciculations persist; pralidoxime helps these. Bronchorrhea is muscarinic and is the main target of atropine therapy.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3a618004","lv":"ms1","d":"digoxin-immune-fab","sys":"tox","q":"How does digoxin immune Fab reverse digoxin toxicity?","a":"Binds free digoxin, forming complexes that are cleared","w":["Reactivates the Na+/K+ ATPase directly","Competitively blocks digoxin at cardiac receptors","Increases hepatic metabolism of digoxin"],"x":"Digoxin immune Fab consists of antibody fragments that bind digoxin, removing it from its target and allowing elimination. It does not act on the pump itself.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe28e045b","lv":"ms1","d":"hydroxocobalamin","sys":"tox","q":"Hydroxocobalamin treats cyanide poisoning by which mechanism?","a":"Binds cyanide to form cyanocobalamin, which is renally excreted","w":["Induces methemoglobin, which binds cyanide","Donates sulfur so rhodanese forms thiocyanate","Reactivates cytochrome oxidase by reducing iron"],"x":"Hydroxocobalamin combines with cyanide to form cyanocobalamin (vitamin B12), which is excreted in urine; it often turns skin and urine red. Inducing methemoglobin is the mechanism of sodium nitrite, and sulfur donation describes sodium thiosulfate.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0c8855a1","lv":"ms1","d":"methylene-blue","sys":"tox","q":"In a patient with methemoglobinemia, methylene blue may be ineffective and cause hemolysis if the patient has which condition?","a":"Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency","w":["Hereditary spherocytosis","Sickle cell trait","Iron deficiency anemia"],"x":"Methylene blue requires NADPH, produced by G6PD, to reduce methemoglobin; G6PD-deficient patients respond poorly and may hemolyze. Hereditary spherocytosis is a membrane defect unrelated to this pathway.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp33cd2677","lv":"ms1","d":"deferoxamine","sys":"tox","q":"Deferoxamine is the antidote for which poisoning?","a":"Iron","w":["Lead","Arsenic","Copper"],"x":"Deferoxamine chelates iron to form ferrioxamine, which is renally excreted and may color urine reddish. Lead poisoning is treated with chelators such as succimer, dimercaprol, or calcium disodium EDTA.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9ed7acca","lv":"ms1","d":"penicillin-g","sys":"infect","q":"How does penicillin G kill susceptible bacteria?","a":"Binds penicillin-binding proteins and blocks peptidoglycan cross-linking","w":["Binds D-alanyl-D-alanine and blocks cell wall precursor addition","Binds the 30S ribosomal subunit and blocks tRNA attachment","Inhibits DNA gyrase and blocks DNA supercoiling"],"x":"Beta-lactams inhibit penicillin-binding proteins (transpeptidases), blocking cross-linking of peptidoglycan. Binding D-Ala-D-Ala is the mechanism of vancomycin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa7bfce45","lv":"ms1","d":"vancomycin","sys":"infect","q":"What is the mechanism of action of vancomycin?","a":"Binds D-alanyl-D-alanine to block cell wall polymerization","w":["Binds penicillin-binding proteins to block cross-linking","Inhibits the 50S ribosomal subunit to block translocation","Disrupts the bacterial cell membrane by forming channels"],"x":"Vancomycin binds the D-Ala-D-Ala terminus of peptidoglycan precursors; resistance develops when it changes to D-Ala-D-Lac. Binding penicillin-binding proteins is how beta-lactams work.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp547c2317","lv":"ms1","d":"gentamicin","sys":"infect","q":"Which pair of toxicities is classically associated with gentamicin?","a":"Nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity","w":["Hepatotoxicity and pancreatitis","Tendon rupture and QT prolongation","Red man syndrome and thrombocytopenia"],"x":"Aminoglycosides accumulate in renal tubular cells and inner ear hair cells, causing kidney injury and hearing or vestibular loss. Tendon rupture and QT prolongation are classic fluoroquinolone effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf6e4b8bd","lv":"ms1","d":"doxycycline","sys":"infect","q":"What should a patient be taught about taking oral doxycycline?","a":"Separate it from antacids, dairy, and iron, which reduce absorption","w":["Take it with milk to prevent stomach upset","Take it at bedtime lying down to reduce nausea","Take it with an iron supplement to improve absorption"],"x":"Tetracyclines chelate divalent and trivalent cations such as calcium, magnesium, aluminum, and iron, reducing absorption. Taking it lying down increases the risk of pill esophagitis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf3efef6c","lv":"ms1","d":"ciprofloxacin","sys":"infect","q":"An older adult on corticosteroids develops sudden heel pain while taking ciprofloxacin. Which adverse effect should be recognized?","a":"Achilles tendinopathy or rupture","w":["Gouty arthritis from uric acid buildup","Plantar fasciitis from fluid retention","Osteomyelitis from bacterial resistance"],"x":"Fluoroquinolones carry a boxed warning for tendinitis and tendon rupture, with higher risk in older adults and those taking corticosteroids. Gout is not a classic fluoroquinolone effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp113e9835","lv":"ms1","d":"trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","sys":"infect","q":"How does trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole inhibit bacterial growth?","a":"Sequentially blocks folate synthesis at dihydropteroate synthase and dihydrofolate reductase","w":["Blocks cell wall cross-linking at two penicillin-binding proteins","Blocks protein synthesis at both the 30S and 50S ribosomal subunits","Blocks DNA replication at both DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV"],"x":"Sulfamethoxazole inhibits dihydropteroate synthase and trimethoprim inhibits bacterial dihydrofolate reductase, giving sequential blockade of folate synthesis. Blocking gyrase and topoisomerase IV describes fluoroquinolones.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa2283639","lv":"ms1","d":"metronidazole","sys":"infect","q":"A patient taking metronidazole drinks alcohol and develops flushing, nausea, vomiting, and palpitations. What explains this?","a":"A disulfiram-like reaction from acetaldehyde buildup","w":["An allergic reaction to the drug's sulfonamide group","Serotonin syndrome from monoamine oxidase inhibition","Red man syndrome from histamine release"],"x":"Metronidazole has been associated with a disulfiram-like reaction in which acetaldehyde accumulates after alcohol intake. Red man syndrome is linked to rapid vancomycin infusion.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7608bfca","lv":"ms1","d":"rifampin","sys":"infect","q":"A patient taking an oral contraceptive starts rifampin for tuberculosis. What is the main concern?","a":"Rifampin induces CYP enzymes, reducing contraceptive effectiveness","w":["Rifampin inhibits CYP enzymes, causing estrogen toxicity","Rifampin binds hormones in the gut, blocking absorption","The contraceptive inhibits rifampin, causing liver failure"],"x":"Rifampin is a potent inducer of CYP3A4 and other enzymes, lowering levels of hormonal contraceptives, warfarin, and many other drugs. It is an inducer, not an inhibitor.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4468a41d","lv":"ms1","d":"isoniazid","sys":"infect","q":"Why is pyridoxine (vitamin B6) commonly given with isoniazid?","a":"To prevent isoniazid-induced peripheral neuropathy","w":["To prevent isoniazid-induced hepatotoxicity","To increase isoniazid absorption from the gut","To prevent orange discoloration of body fluids"],"x":"Isoniazid interferes with pyridoxine metabolism, which can cause peripheral neuropathy, especially in at-risk patients. Pyridoxine does not prevent hepatotoxicity, which is monitored separately.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0956006d","lv":"ms1","d":"acyclovir","sys":"infect","q":"Why is acyclovir selectively active against herpes-infected cells?","a":"It is first phosphorylated by viral thymidine kinase","w":["It binds a receptor found only on infected cells","It inhibits viral neuraminidase on the cell surface","It blocks viral entry by binding the fusion protein"],"x":"Acyclovir is a guanosine analog activated by viral thymidine kinase, then by host kinases, and it inhibits viral DNA polymerase. Neuraminidase inhibition is the mechanism of oseltamivir against influenza.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpaf47d715","lv":"ms1","d":"metformin","sys":"endo","q":"What is the primary mechanism by which metformin lowers blood glucose?","a":"Decreases hepatic gluconeogenesis","w":["Stimulates insulin release from beta cells","Inhibits renal glucose reabsorption","Delays carbohydrate absorption in the gut"],"x":"Metformin, a biguanide, reduces hepatic glucose production and improves insulin sensitivity without stimulating insulin release, so it rarely causes hypoglycemia alone. Stimulating insulin release describes sulfonylureas.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc7990ccf","lv":"ms1","d":"metformin","sys":"endo","q":"Long-term metformin use is associated with deficiency of which vitamin?","a":"Vitamin B12","w":["Vitamin K","Vitamin D","Vitamin B6"],"x":"Metformin reduces ileal vitamin B12 absorption, so levels may be checked periodically, especially if neuropathy or anemia develops. Vitamin B6 deficiency is linked to isoniazid.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp998bf867","lv":"ms1","d":"glipizide","sys":"endo","q":"How does glipizide lower blood glucose?","a":"Closes ATP-sensitive potassium channels on beta cells, triggering insulin release","w":["Activates PPAR-gamma to improve peripheral insulin sensitivity","Inhibits DPP-4 to prolong incretin activity","Inhibits SGLT2 to increase urinary glucose loss"],"x":"Sulfonylureas close K-ATP channels, depolarizing beta cells and causing calcium-dependent insulin release, so hypoglycemia is a key risk. PPAR-gamma activation describes pioglitazone.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpde7c0d70","lv":"ms1","d":"pioglitazone","sys":"endo","q":"Pioglitazone carries a boxed warning for which condition?","a":"Causing or worsening heart failure","w":["Medullary thyroid carcinoma","Lactic acidosis","Severe hypoglycemia when used alone"],"x":"Thiazolidinediones cause fluid retention and can precipitate or worsen heart failure. Medullary thyroid carcinoma is the boxed warning for GLP-1 receptor agonists.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp593cdc5e","lv":"ms1","d":"sitagliptin","sys":"endo","q":"What is the mechanism of action of sitagliptin?","a":"Inhibits DPP-4, prolonging the action of endogenous incretins","w":["Activates GLP-1 receptors directly as a peptide analog","Inhibits alpha-glucosidase in the intestinal brush border","Closes ATP-sensitive potassium channels on beta cells"],"x":"Sitagliptin blocks dipeptidyl peptidase-4, so GLP-1 and GIP last longer and boost glucose-dependent insulin release. Direct GLP-1 receptor activation describes injectable agonists such as liraglutide.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp63f88276","lv":"ms1","d":"liraglutide","sys":"endo","q":"Liraglutide is contraindicated in a patient with a family history of which condition?","a":"Medullary thyroid carcinoma or MEN 2","w":["Papillary thyroid carcinoma","Type 1 diabetes in a parent","Familial hypercholesterolemia"],"x":"GLP-1 receptor agonists caused C-cell tumors in rodents, so they are contraindicated with a personal or family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma or MEN 2. Papillary thyroid cancer arises from follicular cells and is not the labeled contraindication.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp784b85a7","lv":"ms1","d":"empagliflozin","sys":"endo","q":"How does empagliflozin lower blood glucose?","a":"Inhibits SGLT2 in the proximal tubule, increasing urinary glucose excretion","w":["Inhibits DPP-4 in the plasma, increasing incretin levels","Activates PPAR-gamma in fat, increasing insulin sensitivity","Closes K-ATP channels in beta cells, increasing insulin release"],"x":"SGLT2 inhibitors block glucose reabsorption in the proximal tubule, causing glucosuria; genital mycotic infections and euglycemic DKA are key risks. DPP-4 inhibition describes sitagliptin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc8015d2e","lv":"ms1","d":"levothyroxine","sys":"endo","q":"What is important patient teaching for levothyroxine absorption?","a":"Take it on an empty stomach, separated from calcium and iron supplements","w":["Take it with a high-fiber breakfast to reduce stomach upset","Take it together with calcium to protect the bones","Take it with an iron supplement to improve uptake"],"x":"Levothyroxine absorption is reduced by food and by binding to calcium, iron, and antacids, so it is taken consistently on an empty stomach and separated from these. Taking it with calcium lowers absorption.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb30f26c7","lv":"ms1","d":"propylthiouracil","sys":"endo","q":"Which action distinguishes propylthiouracil from methimazole?","a":"It also inhibits peripheral conversion of T4 to T3","w":["It also blocks thyroid hormone receptors in tissues","It also destroys thyroid follicular cells","It also blocks TSH release from the pituitary"],"x":"Both drugs inhibit thyroid peroxidase, but PTU also inhibits peripheral 5'-deiodinase, reducing T4 to T3 conversion. Destroying follicular cells is the effect of radioactive iodine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1574fec2","lv":"ms1","d":"methimazole","sys":"endo","q":"A patient taking methimazole develops fever and a sore throat. Which serious adverse effect should be recognized and reported promptly?","a":"Agranulocytosis","w":["Thyroid storm","Hypothyroidism","Drug-induced lupus"],"x":"Thionamides can cause agranulocytosis, which often presents with fever and sore throat, so a neutrophil count is checked promptly. Hypothyroidism from overtreatment does not cause fever and sore throat.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6fa6f56e","lv":"ms1","d":"omeprazole","sys":"gi","q":"What is the mechanism of action of omeprazole?","a":"Irreversibly inhibits the H+/K+ ATPase in parietal cells","w":["Competitively blocks histamine H2 receptors on parietal cells","Neutralizes gastric acid already present in the stomach","Forms a protective barrier over the ulcer base"],"x":"Proton pump inhibitors are activated in acid and covalently inhibit the parietal cell proton pump, the final step of acid secretion. H2 receptor blockade is the mechanism of famotidine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe699db10","lv":"ms1","d":"omeprazole","sys":"gi","q":"Why might omeprazole reduce the effectiveness of clopidogrel?","a":"It inhibits CYP2C19, reducing activation of clopidogrel","w":["It induces CYP2C19, speeding clopidogrel clearance","It binds clopidogrel in the stomach, blocking absorption","It raises gastric pH, destroying clopidogrel before absorption"],"x":"Clopidogrel is a prodrug activated by CYP2C19, which omeprazole inhibits, so less active metabolite is formed. The concern is enzyme inhibition, not induction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp74b3ee0c","lv":"ms1","d":"cimetidine","sys":"gi","q":"Besides acid suppression, cimetidine is known for which pharmacologic property?","a":"Inhibition of multiple CYP450 enzymes and antiandrogenic effects","w":["Induction of multiple CYP450 enzymes and estrogenic effects","Strong anticholinergic effects causing urinary retention","Prokinetic effects through dopamine receptor blockade"],"x":"Cimetidine inhibits several CYP enzymes, raising levels of drugs like warfarin and theophylline, and can cause gynecomastia through antiandrogenic effects. Prokinetic dopamine blockade describes metoclopramide.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8bd34dda","lv":"ms1","d":"ondansetron","sys":"gi","q":"How does ondansetron prevent chemotherapy-induced nausea?","a":"Blocks 5-HT3 receptors in the gut and chemoreceptor trigger zone","w":["Blocks dopamine D2 receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone","Blocks NK1 receptors for substance P in the brainstem","Blocks histamine H1 receptors in the vestibular system"],"x":"Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist acting on vagal afferents and the chemoreceptor trigger zone; it can prolong the QT interval. NK1 antagonism is the mechanism of aprepitant.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp405dc5cb","lv":"ms1","d":"metoclopramide","sys":"gi","q":"Metoclopramide carries a boxed warning for which adverse effect with long-term use?","a":"Tardive dyskinesia","w":["Agranulocytosis","Hepatic failure","Tendon rupture"],"x":"Metoclopramide blocks dopamine D2 receptors, and prolonged use can cause irreversible tardive dyskinesia. Agranulocytosis is linked to drugs like clozapine, not metoclopramide.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp768f096e","lv":"ms1","d":"misoprostol","sys":"gi","q":"Misoprostol is used to prevent NSAID-induced ulcers. Why is it contraindicated in pregnancy when used for this purpose?","a":"As a prostaglandin E1 analog, it can cause uterine contractions and pregnancy loss","w":["As an H2 receptor antagonist, it can cause fetal growth restriction","As a proton pump inhibitor, it can cause fetal kidney damage","As a dopamine antagonist, it can cause neonatal movement disorders"],"x":"Misoprostol replaces protective prostaglandins in the stomach but also stimulates uterine contractions, so it can cause miscarriage. It is not an H2 antagonist or proton pump inhibitor.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdb39c043","lv":"ms1","d":"sucralfate","sys":"gi","q":"How does sucralfate protect against peptic ulcer injury?","a":"Forms a viscous paste in acid that binds to the ulcer base","w":["Irreversibly inhibits the parietal cell proton pump","Neutralizes acid by acting as a strong base","Replaces mucosal prostaglandins as a receptor agonist"],"x":"In an acidic environment sucralfate polymerizes into a sticky gel that adheres to damaged mucosa, shielding it from acid and pepsin. Replacing prostaglandins describes misoprostol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc3e5e45d","lv":"ms1","d":"loperamide","sys":"gi","q":"Why does loperamide slow intestinal motility with little central opioid effect at usual doses?","a":"It is a mu opioid agonist that poorly crosses the blood-brain barrier","w":["It is a muscarinic antagonist that does not enter the brain","It is a 5-HT3 antagonist that acts only on gut nerves","It is a kappa opioid antagonist that acts only in the gut"],"x":"Loperamide activates intestinal mu receptors, but P-glycoprotein efflux limits CNS entry at usual doses; very large misuse doses can cause cardiac toxicity. It is not an anticholinergic agent.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5f8383ad","lv":"ms1","d":"lactulose","sys":"gi","q":"How does lactulose lower ammonia in hepatic encephalopathy?","a":"Colonic bacteria acidify it, trapping ammonia as ammonium for excretion","w":["It directly inhibits ammonia production by the liver","It binds ammonia in the blood for renal excretion","It kills urease-producing bacteria in the colon"],"x":"Bacterial metabolism of lactulose acidifies the colon, converting ammonia to poorly absorbed ammonium and causing an osmotic laxative effect. Killing gut bacteria describes rifaximin, a different therapy.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf6aa1959","lv":"ms1","d":"aprepitant","sys":"gi","q":"What is the mechanism of action of aprepitant?","a":"Blocks NK1 receptors for substance P","w":["Blocks 5-HT3 receptors on vagal afferents","Blocks dopamine D2 receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone","Activates cannabinoid CB1 receptors in the brain"],"x":"Aprepitant is a neurokinin-1 receptor antagonist that is especially useful for delayed chemotherapy-induced nausea. 5-HT3 blockade is the mechanism of ondansetron.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp69385fa3","lv":"ms1","d":"oxytocin","sys":"ob","q":"Prolonged oxytocin infusion with large volumes of hypotonic fluid can cause which complication?","a":"Water intoxication with hyponatremia","w":["Hypernatremia from water loss","Hyperkalemia from cell lysis","Hypoglycemia from insulin release"],"x":"Oxytocin is structurally similar to ADH and has antidiuretic activity, so prolonged use can cause water retention and hyponatremia. It does not cause water loss.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa364d6d9","lv":"ms1","d":"magnesium-sulfate","sys":"ob","q":"In a patient receiving magnesium sulfate for preeclampsia, which finding is typically the earliest sign of toxicity?","a":"Loss of deep tendon reflexes","w":["Respiratory arrest","Complete heart block","Seizure activity"],"x":"Loss of patellar reflexes appears before respiratory depression and cardiac effects as magnesium levels rise. Seizures are what magnesium is given to prevent, not a sign of its toxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0f27b18a","lv":"ms1","d":"magnesium-sulfate","sys":"ob","q":"Which agent is used to counter magnesium sulfate toxicity with respiratory depression?","a":"Calcium gluconate","w":["Naloxone","Protamine sulfate","Sodium bicarbonate"],"x":"Calcium antagonizes magnesium at the neuromuscular junction and cardiac membranes, so IV calcium gluconate is the antidote. Naloxone reverses opioids, not magnesium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3b41b84b","lv":"ms1","d":"methylergonovine","sys":"ob","q":"Methylergonovine for postpartum hemorrhage is contraindicated in which patient?","a":"A patient with preeclampsia or hypertension","w":["A patient with asthma","A patient with a prior cesarean delivery","A patient who is breastfeeding"],"x":"Methylergonovine is an ergot alkaloid that causes vasoconstriction and can dangerously raise blood pressure. Asthma is the classic contraindication for carboprost, not methylergonovine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5c8ee335","lv":"ms1","d":"carboprost","sys":"ob","q":"Carboprost for postpartum hemorrhage is avoided in a patient with which condition?","a":"Asthma","w":["Hypertension","Gestational diabetes","Hypothyroidism"],"x":"Carboprost is a prostaglandin F2-alpha analog that can cause bronchospasm. Hypertension is the key contraindication for methylergonovine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4429c04b","lv":"ms1","d":"terbutaline","sys":"ob","q":"By what mechanism does terbutaline relax uterine smooth muscle?","a":"Beta-2 adrenergic agonism increasing cAMP","w":["Calcium channel blockade in myometrium","Oxytocin receptor antagonism","Prostaglandin synthesis inhibition"],"x":"Terbutaline is a beta-2 agonist that raises cAMP and relaxes myometrium; it carries a boxed warning against prolonged use for preterm labor. Calcium channel blockade describes nifedipine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp00ed7ca5","lv":"ms1","d":"rho-d-immune-globulin","sys":"ob","q":"Why is Rho(D) immune globulin given to an Rh-negative mother?","a":"To prevent maternal sensitization to fetal Rh-positive red cells","w":["To treat existing maternal anti-D antibodies","To prevent maternal anemia after delivery","To provide the fetus with passive immunity to infection"],"x":"Anti-D antibodies clear fetal Rh-positive cells from the maternal circulation before her immune system makes its own antibodies, protecting future pregnancies. It does not work once the mother is already sensitized.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp51e4f925","lv":"ms1","d":"clomiphene","sys":"ob","q":"How does clomiphene induce ovulation?","a":"Blocks hypothalamic estrogen receptors, increasing GnRH, FSH, and LH","w":["Acts as an FSH analog to stimulate follicles directly","Inhibits aromatase to lower estrogen production","Acts as a continuous GnRH agonist to suppress the pituitary"],"x":"Clomiphene is a SERM that blocks estrogen negative feedback at the hypothalamus, raising gonadotropin release and stimulating ovulation. Direct FSH activity describes gonadotropin preparations such as follitropin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp89c34fce","lv":"ms1","d":"mifepristone","sys":"ob","q":"What is the mechanism of action of mifepristone?","a":"Progesterone receptor antagonist","w":["Prostaglandin E1 analog","Estrogen receptor agonist","Oxytocin receptor agonist"],"x":"Mifepristone blocks progesterone receptors (and glucocorticoid receptors at higher exposure), causing decidual breakdown. The prostaglandin E1 analog it is commonly paired with is misoprostol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2eb949b1","lv":"ms1","d":"leuprolide","sys":"ob","q":"Why does continuous leuprolide suppress sex hormone production even though it is a GnRH agonist?","a":"Continuous stimulation downregulates pituitary GnRH receptors after an initial flare","w":["It directly blocks estrogen receptors in target tissues","It inhibits aromatase in the ovaries and fat tissue","It binds and inactivates circulating LH and FSH"],"x":"Pulsatile GnRH stimulates gonadotropins, but continuous agonist exposure desensitizes the pituitary after a short initial flare, lowering LH, FSH, and sex steroids. Aromatase inhibition describes letrozole and anastrozole.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6a8fb6c7","lv":"ms1","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"immune","q":"Why does diphenhydramine cause more sedation than loratadine?","a":"It crosses the blood-brain barrier more readily","w":["It blocks H2 receptors in the brain","It is metabolized to a benzodiazepine-like compound","It directly activates GABA-A receptors"],"x":"First-generation H1 antihistamines are lipophilic and enter the CNS, causing sedation along with anticholinergic effects. They act on H1, not H2, receptors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp87ff9f32","lv":"ms1","d":"cyclosporine","sys":"immune","q":"What is the mechanism of action of cyclosporine?","a":"Binds cyclophilin and inhibits calcineurin, reducing IL-2 transcription","w":["Binds FKBP and inhibits mTOR, blocking IL-2 signaling","Inhibits IMP dehydrogenase, reducing guanine synthesis","Blocks the IL-2 receptor alpha chain on T cells"],"x":"Cyclosporine-cyclophilin complexes inhibit calcineurin, preventing NFAT activation and IL-2 production; nephrotoxicity and gingival hyperplasia are classic effects. mTOR inhibition is the mechanism of sirolimus.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp520b401a","lv":"ms1","d":"tacrolimus","sys":"immune","q":"Tacrolimus inhibits calcineurin after binding which intracellular protein?","a":"FK-binding protein (FKBP)","w":["Cyclophilin","Calmodulin","Heat shock protein"],"x":"Tacrolimus binds FKBP, and the complex inhibits calcineurin, reducing IL-2 transcription. Cyclophilin is the binding partner for cyclosporine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe4075168","lv":"ms1","d":"sirolimus","sys":"immune","q":"How does sirolimus suppress the immune system?","a":"Inhibits mTOR, blocking T-cell proliferation in response to IL-2","w":["Inhibits calcineurin, blocking IL-2 transcription","Depletes B cells by binding CD20","Blocks the costimulatory signal between CD28 and B7"],"x":"Sirolimus binds FKBP, but the complex inhibits mTOR rather than calcineurin, blocking cell-cycle progression in response to IL-2. Calcineurin inhibition is how cyclosporine and tacrolimus act.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp660d7cca","lv":"ms1","d":"mycophenolate","sys":"immune","q":"What is the mechanism of action of mycophenolate?","a":"Inhibits IMP dehydrogenase, reducing guanine nucleotide synthesis in lymphocytes","w":["Inhibits dihydrofolate reductase, reducing thymidine synthesis","Inhibits dihydroorotate dehydrogenase, reducing pyrimidine synthesis","Acts as a purine analog incorporated into lymphocyte DNA"],"x":"Lymphocytes depend on de novo purine synthesis, so blocking IMP dehydrogenase selectively limits their proliferation. Dihydroorotate dehydrogenase inhibition describes leflunomide.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpda4eb760","lv":"ms1","d":"azathioprine","sys":"immune","q":"Before starting azathioprine, testing for which enzyme helps predict severe bone marrow suppression?","a":"Thiopurine methyltransferase (TPMT)","w":["Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD)","Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase (DPD)","Pseudocholinesterase"],"x":"Low TPMT activity leads to accumulation of active thioguanine nucleotides and severe myelosuppression. DPD deficiency predicts toxicity from fluorouracil, not azathioprine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdeee9012","lv":"ms1","d":"infliximab","sys":"immune","q":"Before starting infliximab, which screening is required?","a":"Testing for latent tuberculosis","w":["Testing for HLA-B*57:01","Testing for thiopurine methyltransferase activity","Testing for G6PD deficiency"],"x":"TNF-alpha is essential for granuloma maintenance, so anti-TNF therapy can reactivate latent tuberculosis. HLA-B*57:01 testing applies to abacavir.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2c32e45c","lv":"ms1","d":"rituximab","sys":"immune","q":"Rituximab targets which molecule?","a":"CD20 on B cells","w":["CD3 on T cells","CD25 on activated T cells","CD52 on lymphocytes"],"x":"Rituximab is an anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody that depletes B cells; it can reactivate hepatitis B. CD25 is targeted by basiliximab.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4921da27","lv":"ms1","d":"abatacept","sys":"immune","q":"What is the mechanism of action of abatacept?","a":"A CTLA-4 fusion protein that binds B7 to block T-cell costimulation","w":["A monoclonal antibody that binds TNF-alpha","A monoclonal antibody that blocks the IL-6 receptor","An IL-1 receptor antagonist protein"],"x":"Abatacept binds CD80/CD86 (B7) on antigen-presenting cells, preventing the CD28 costimulatory signal needed for T-cell activation. IL-6 receptor blockade describes tocilizumab.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp795e4e26","lv":"ms1","d":"hydroxychloroquine","sys":"immune","q":"Which monitoring is recommended for patients on long-term hydroxychloroquine?","a":"Periodic eye examinations for retinal toxicity","w":["Periodic audiometry for hearing loss","Periodic pulmonary function tests for fibrosis","Periodic echocardiograms for cardiomyopathy"],"x":"Hydroxychloroquine can cause irreversible retinopathy, so baseline and regular ophthalmologic screening is recommended. Pulmonary fibrosis monitoring is associated with drugs like amiodarone and bleomycin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpaea3e988","lv":"ms1","d":"cyclophosphamide","sys":"onc","q":"Cyclophosphamide can cause hemorrhagic cystitis. Which agent is given to help prevent it?","a":"Mesna","w":["Leucovorin","Dexrazoxane","Amifostine"],"x":"The metabolite acrolein damages bladder epithelium, and mesna binds acrolein in the urine to prevent hemorrhagic cystitis. Dexrazoxane protects against anthracycline cardiotoxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0e66e4f1","lv":"ms1","d":"cisplatin","sys":"onc","q":"Which adverse effects are most characteristic of cisplatin?","a":"Nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity","w":["Cardiotoxicity and hand-foot syndrome","Pulmonary fibrosis and skin hyperpigmentation","Hemorrhagic cystitis and alopecia"],"x":"Cisplatin, a platinum compound that cross-links DNA, classically causes kidney injury, hearing loss, and severe nausea, so aggressive hydration is used. Pulmonary fibrosis is associated with bleomycin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp389b14e3","lv":"ms1","d":"doxorubicin","sys":"onc","q":"Which cumulative toxicity is most associated with doxorubicin?","a":"Dilated cardiomyopathy","w":["Pulmonary fibrosis","Hemorrhagic cystitis","Peripheral neuropathy"],"x":"Anthracyclines generate free radicals that damage myocardium, causing dose-related dilated cardiomyopathy; dexrazoxane can reduce it. Pulmonary fibrosis is linked to bleomycin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4b7af396","lv":"ms1","d":"bleomycin","sys":"onc","q":"Which serious toxicity is classically associated with bleomycin?","a":"Pulmonary fibrosis","w":["Dilated cardiomyopathy","Hemorrhagic cystitis","Severe myelosuppression"],"x":"Bleomycin causes DNA strand breaks through free radicals and is classically linked to pulmonary fibrosis, while being relatively marrow-sparing. Dilated cardiomyopathy is linked to doxorubicin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp32d3242d","lv":"ms1","d":"vincristine","sys":"onc","q":"What is the mechanism of action of vincristine?","a":"Binds tubulin and inhibits microtubule polymerization","w":["Stabilizes microtubules and prevents their breakdown","Inhibits topoisomerase II and causes DNA breaks","Inhibits thymidylate synthase and blocks DNA synthesis"],"x":"Vinca alkaloids prevent microtubule assembly, arresting cells in M phase; peripheral neuropathy is the classic dose-limiting toxicity. Microtubule stabilization describes taxanes such as paclitaxel.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp73664d68","lv":"ms1","d":"paclitaxel","sys":"onc","q":"What is the mechanism of action of paclitaxel?","a":"Stabilizes microtubules, preventing their breakdown during mitosis","w":["Binds tubulin to prevent microtubule assembly","Inhibits topoisomerase I, causing single-strand DNA breaks","Alkylates DNA to form cross-links"],"x":"Taxanes hyperstabilize polymerized microtubules, so the mitotic spindle cannot disassemble and cells arrest in M phase. Preventing microtubule assembly describes vinca alkaloids.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc871718c","lv":"ms1","d":"methotrexate","sys":"onc","q":"Why is leucovorin given after high-intensity methotrexate therapy?","a":"It provides reduced folate that bypasses dihydrofolate reductase inhibition","w":["It directly inactivates methotrexate in the bloodstream","It inhibits thymidylate synthase to enhance tumor killing","It blocks methotrexate uptake into tumor cells"],"x":"Methotrexate inhibits dihydrofolate reductase; leucovorin (folinic acid) is already reduced, so it rescues normal cells without needing that enzyme. Enzymatic inactivation of methotrexate is the action of glucarpidase.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb997c038","lv":"ms1","d":"fluorouracil","sys":"onc","q":"What is the main mechanism of action of fluorouracil?","a":"Its metabolite inhibits thymidylate synthase, reducing thymidine synthesis","w":["It inhibits dihydrofolate reductase, reducing folate synthesis","It inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, reducing deoxyribonucleotides","It intercalates into DNA and inhibits topoisomerase II"],"x":"Fluorouracil is converted to 5-FdUMP, which forms a complex with thymidylate synthase and folate, blocking dTMP production. Ribonucleotide reductase inhibition is the mechanism of hydroxyurea.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp29ed9536","lv":"ms1","d":"imatinib","sys":"onc","q":"Imatinib is effective in chronic myeloid leukemia because it inhibits which target?","a":"The BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase","w":["The HER2 receptor tyrosine kinase","The EGFR tyrosine kinase","The VEGF signaling ligand"],"x":"CML is driven by the BCR-ABL fusion from the Philadelphia chromosome, and imatinib blocks its tyrosine kinase activity. HER2 is targeted by trastuzumab.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp575dcbcf","lv":"ms1","d":"tamoxifen","sys":"onc","q":"Long-term tamoxifen use increases the risk of which cancer?","a":"Endometrial cancer","w":["Breast cancer in the opposite breast","Ovarian cancer","Thyroid cancer"],"x":"Tamoxifen is a SERM that blocks estrogen in breast tissue but acts as a partial agonist in the endometrium, increasing endometrial hyperplasia and cancer. It reduces, rather than raises, contralateral breast cancer risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7ad653e4","lv":"ms1","d":"isotretinoin","sys":"skin","q":"Isotretinoin is distributed through a restricted program mainly because of which risk?","a":"Severe birth defects","w":["Severe hepatotoxicity","Agranulocytosis","Pulmonary fibrosis"],"x":"Isotretinoin is a potent teratogen, so the iPLEDGE program requires pregnancy testing and contraception for patients who can become pregnant. Hepatotoxicity is not the reason for the restricted program.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp344b4a4a","lv":"ms1","d":"latanoprost","sys":"skin","q":"Which adverse effect is characteristic of latanoprost eye drops?","a":"Increased brown pigmentation of the iris","w":["Systemic bronchospasm","Metabolic acidosis","Dry mouth"],"x":"Latanoprost, a prostaglandin F2-alpha analog that increases uveoscleral outflow, can permanently darken the iris and lengthen eyelashes. Systemic bronchospasm is a risk with timolol eye drops.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp513c6fc8","lv":"ms1","d":"timolol-ophthalmic","sys":"skin","q":"Why should timolol eye drops be used with caution in a patient with asthma?","a":"Systemic absorption can block bronchial beta-2 receptors and cause bronchospasm","w":["Local absorption can stimulate muscarinic receptors in the airways","It increases aqueous humor production and pulmonary secretions","It causes histamine release from conjunctival mast cells"],"x":"Timolol is a nonselective beta blocker; drops absorbed through the nasolacrimal duct can reach systemic circulation and cause bronchospasm or bradycardia. It lowers intraocular pressure by reducing aqueous production, not increasing it.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp78118404","lv":"ms1","d":"pilocarpine-ophthalmic","sys":"skin","q":"How does pilocarpine lower intraocular pressure?","a":"Muscarinic agonism contracts the ciliary muscle, opening the trabecular meshwork","w":["Muscarinic antagonism relaxes the ciliary muscle, increasing outflow","Beta blockade reduces aqueous humor production","Carbonic anhydrase inhibition reduces aqueous humor production"],"x":"Pilocarpine stimulates muscarinic receptors, causing miosis and ciliary muscle contraction that increases trabecular outflow. Muscarinic antagonists dilate the pupil and can worsen angle-closure glaucoma.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp848df7a2","lv":"ms1","d":"dorzolamide","sys":"skin","q":"What is the mechanism of dorzolamide in glaucoma?","a":"Inhibits carbonic anhydrase, reducing aqueous humor production","w":["Activates prostaglandin receptors, increasing uveoscleral outflow","Stimulates muscarinic receptors, increasing trabecular outflow","Inhibits Rho kinase, increasing trabecular outflow"],"x":"Dorzolamide is a topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitor that decreases bicarbonate and aqueous humor formation by the ciliary body. Increasing uveoscleral outflow describes prostaglandin analogs such as latanoprost.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1665d242","lv":"ms1","d":"brimonidine","sys":"skin","q":"Brimonidine eye drops belong to which drug class?","a":"Alpha-2 adrenergic agonist","w":["Beta-adrenergic antagonist","Prostaglandin analog","Muscarinic antagonist"],"x":"Brimonidine is a selective alpha-2 agonist that reduces aqueous humor production and increases uveoscleral outflow. Beta-adrenergic antagonists, such as timolol, are a separate class of glaucoma drops.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1a188187","lv":"ms1","d":"tropicamide","sys":"skin","q":"Tropicamide is used before eye exams for which effect, and what risk should be recognized?","a":"Pupil dilation; it can precipitate acute angle-closure glaucoma","w":["Pupil constriction; it can cause retinal detachment","Corneal anesthesia; it can cause corneal ulceration","Reduced eye pressure; it can cause bronchospasm"],"x":"Tropicamide is a short-acting muscarinic antagonist that causes mydriasis and cycloplegia; in narrow angles, dilation can block outflow and trigger angle closure. Pupil constriction is caused by muscarinic agonists such as pilocarpine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7cdf705f","lv":"ms1","d":"permethrin","sys":"skin","q":"How does permethrin kill scabies mites and lice?","a":"Prolongs sodium channel opening in arthropod nerve cells, causing paralysis","w":["Inhibits arthropod chitin synthesis, preventing molting","Binds glutamate-gated chloride channels, causing hyperpolarization","Inhibits acetylcholinesterase irreversibly in arthropod nerves"],"x":"Permethrin is a pyrethroid that delays sodium channel inactivation in parasite nerve membranes, leading to paralysis and death. Glutamate-gated chloride channels are the target of ivermectin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp90da016a","lv":"ms1","d":"terbinafine-topical","sys":"skin","q":"What is the mechanism of action of terbinafine?","a":"Inhibits squalene epoxidase, blocking ergosterol synthesis","w":["Inhibits lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase, blocking ergosterol synthesis","Binds ergosterol to form pores in the fungal membrane","Inhibits beta-glucan synthase in the fungal cell wall"],"x":"Terbinafine blocks squalene epoxidase, so ergosterol falls and toxic squalene builds up in fungal cells. Lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase inhibition describes azole antifungals.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe8532a94","lv":"ms1","d":"tacrolimus-topical","sys":"skin","q":"Why is topical tacrolimus useful for atopic dermatitis on the face and eyelids?","a":"It inhibits calcineurin in T cells without causing skin atrophy","w":["It is a potent corticosteroid that penetrates thin skin well","It blocks histamine H1 receptors on skin mast cells","It inhibits squalene epoxidase in skin fungi"],"x":"Topical calcineurin inhibitors reduce T-cell cytokine production and, unlike corticosteroids, do not thin the skin, making them useful on sensitive areas. It is not a corticosteroid.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe57adc6b","lv":"ms3","d":"lisinopril","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient with hypertension develops a persistent dry, nonproductive cough several weeks after starting lisinopril. Which mechanism best explains this adverse effect?","a":"Accumulation of bradykinin from reduced breakdown","w":["Direct blockade of airway beta-2 receptors","Increased aldosterone-driven airway edema","Histamine release from airway mast cells"],"x":"ACE also degrades bradykinin, so ACE inhibition lets bradykinin and substance P accumulate and trigger cough. Beta-2 blockade causes bronchospasm with beta-blockers, not ACE inhibitors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe9dfb493","lv":"ms3","d":"losartan","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient with type 2 diabetes and albuminuric chronic kidney disease develops an intolerable dry cough on enalapril. Which medication is the best replacement that preserves renal protection?","a":"Losartan","w":["Amlodipine","Hydrochlorothiazide","Metoprolol"],"x":"An ARB such as losartan blocks the angiotensin II receptor, reduces albuminuria, and does not raise bradykinin, so cough is uncommon. Amlodipine lowers blood pressure but lacks the antiproteinuric benefit of RAAS blockade.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf84e3ec5","lv":"ms3","d":"amiodarone","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient with atrial fibrillation is starting long-term amiodarone. Which baseline evaluation is most appropriate?","a":"Thyroid function, liver enzymes, chest imaging, and pulmonary function tests","w":["Serum uric acid, lipid panel, urinalysis, and bone density scan","Serum ferritin, vitamin B12 level, and peripheral smear","Fasting glucose, hemoglobin A1c, and urine albumin level"],"x":"Amiodarone can cause thyroid dysfunction, hepatotoxicity, and pulmonary toxicity, so baseline and periodic thyroid, liver, and lung assessments (plus an eye exam and ECG) are recommended. The other panels do not target amiodarone's major toxicities.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3c34479f","lv":"ms3","d":"digoxin","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient with heart failure taking digoxin and a loop diuretic presents with nausea, confusion, and yellow-tinted vision. Which electrolyte abnormality most increases the risk of this toxicity?","a":"Hypokalemia","w":["Hypernatremia","Hypophosphatemia","Hypermagnesemia"],"x":"Digoxin competes with potassium at the Na+/K+-ATPase, so diuretic-induced hypokalemia increases digoxin binding and toxicity. Hypercalcemia can also potentiate toxicity, but hypokalemia is the classic and most common precipitant in diuretic-treated patients.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpea496513","lv":"ms3","d":"eplerenone","sys":"cardio","q":"A man with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction develops painful gynecomastia on spironolactone. Which medication is the most appropriate substitute?","a":"Eplerenone","w":["Hydralazine","Triamterene","Chlorthalidone"],"x":"Eplerenone is a selective mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist with minimal anti-androgen activity and has mortality benefit in HFrEF. Triamterene is potassium sparing but does not block the mineralocorticoid receptor or improve HFrEF outcomes.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc636c8eb","lv":"ms3","d":"sacubitril-valsartan","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient with HFrEF is being transitioned to sacubitril-valsartan. Concomitant use with which drug class is contraindicated because of angioedema risk?","a":"ACE inhibitors","w":["Loop diuretics","Beta-blockers","SGLT2 inhibitors"],"x":"Neprilysin inhibition plus ACE inhibition markedly raises bradykinin and angioedema risk, so an ACE inhibitor must be stopped with a washout before starting sacubitril-valsartan. Beta-blockers and SGLT2 inhibitors are recommended alongside it in HFrEF.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp69ad37eb","lv":"ms3","d":"warfarin","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient with a stable INR on warfarin is diagnosed with a urinary tract infection. Which antibiotic is most likely to cause a marked rise in INR?","a":"Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","w":["Fosfomycin","Cephalexin","Nitrofurantoin"],"x":"Sulfamethoxazole inhibits CYP2C9, the main enzyme that clears the more potent S-warfarin, so INR can rise sharply. Fosfomycin and nitrofurantoin have little effect on warfarin metabolism.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2c790d77","lv":"ms3","d":"apixaban","sys":"cardio","q":"In which patient with atrial fibrillation is apixaban NOT an appropriate choice for stroke prevention?","a":"A patient with a mechanical mitral valve","w":["A patient older than eighty with mild anemia","A patient with well-controlled type 2 diabetes","A patient with a prior ischemic stroke"],"x":"Direct oral anticoagulants are not recommended with mechanical heart valves; warfarin is the anticoagulant of choice. Advanced age, diabetes, and prior stroke raise stroke risk and favor anticoagulation with a DOAC.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc050801b","lv":"ms3","d":"atorvastatin","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient is discharged after an acute myocardial infarction. Which lipid-lowering regimen is most appropriate to start?","a":"High-intensity atorvastatin","w":["Moderate-intensity pravastatin","Ezetimibe monotherapy","Fenofibrate monotherapy"],"x":"Patients with clinical ASCVD should receive high-intensity statin therapy, such as atorvastatin or rosuvastatin, to maximize LDL lowering and event reduction. Ezetimibe is an add-on when LDL remains above goal on a maximally tolerated statin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp85f9fdf4","lv":"ms3","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient presents with acute chest pain. Which history item is a contraindication to sublingual nitroglycerin?","a":"Use of sildenafil the previous evening","w":["Daily low-dose aspirin use","Chronic metoprolol therapy","Current atorvastatin therapy"],"x":"Nitrates combined with PDE-5 inhibitors cause synergistic vasodilation and severe, potentially fatal hypotension. Aspirin, beta-blockers, and statins are all part of standard coronary disease therapy and do not contraindicate nitroglycerin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe5ca87b7","lv":"ms3","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"A patient with septic shock remains hypotensive after adequate fluid resuscitation. Which vasopressor is recommended first-line?","a":"Norepinephrine","w":["Dopamine","Phenylephrine","Dobutamine"],"x":"Surviving Sepsis guidelines recommend norepinephrine as the first-line vasopressor for septic shock. Dopamine causes more tachyarrhythmias and is no longer preferred.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1d207388","lv":"ms3","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"A patient receiving norepinephrine through a peripheral IV develops a cool, pale, blanched area around the insertion site. Which agent is used to treat this extravasation?","a":"Phentolamine infiltrated into the affected area","w":["Hyaluronidase infiltrated into the affected area","Topical nitroglycerin applied to the hand only","Subcutaneous epinephrine near the site"],"x":"Phentolamine is an alpha-adrenergic antagonist that reverses local vasoconstriction from norepinephrine extravasation and limits tissue necrosis. Hyaluronidase is used for some non-vasopressor extravasations, not alpha-agonist injury.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8dafb4e4","lv":"ms3","d":"vasopressin","sys":"shock","q":"Vasopressin is added to norepinephrine in a patient with refractory septic shock. What is its mechanism of raising blood pressure?","a":"Activation of V1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle","w":["Stimulation of beta-1 receptors on the myocardium","Inhibition of phosphodiesterase-3 in vascular tissue","Direct activation of alpha-2 receptors in the brainstem"],"x":"Vasopressin causes vasoconstriction through V1 receptors, independent of adrenergic receptors, which helps lower catecholamine requirements. Phosphodiesterase-3 inhibition describes milrinone, which tends to lower blood pressure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp73473122","lv":"ms3","d":"epinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"A patient develops hives, wheezing, and hypotension minutes after a bee sting. What is the preferred route for the first dose of epinephrine?","a":"Intramuscular injection into the anterolateral thigh","w":["Subcutaneous injection into the upper arm","Slow intravenous push through a peripheral line","Nebulized inhalation through a face mask"],"x":"Intramuscular injection into the vastus lateralis gives faster, more reliable absorption than subcutaneous dosing and is first-line for anaphylaxis. IV bolus epinephrine is reserved for cardiac arrest or carefully titrated infusions because of arrhythmia risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5b5a97c4","lv":"ms3","d":"dobutamine","sys":"shock","q":"A patient with an acute anterior MI has cool extremities, low urine output, and low cardiac output with borderline blood pressure. Which agent primarily increases myocardial contractility through beta-1 stimulation?","a":"Dobutamine","w":["Phenylephrine","Vasopressin","Midodrine"],"x":"Dobutamine is a predominantly beta-1 agonist inotrope used in low-output cardiogenic shock. Phenylephrine is a pure alpha-1 agonist that raises afterload without improving contractility.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5a53b503","lv":"ms3","d":"milrinone","sys":"shock","q":"A patient on chronic beta-blocker therapy develops low-output decompensated heart failure. Why might milrinone be chosen over dobutamine?","a":"It inhibits phosphodiesterase-3 and bypasses beta receptors","w":["It stimulates alpha-1 receptors to raise vascular tone","It blocks the sodium-potassium ATPase in myocytes","It activates dopamine receptors to improve renal flow"],"x":"Milrinone raises cAMP by inhibiting PDE-3, so its inotropic effect does not depend on beta receptors occupied by a beta-blocker. Na+/K+-ATPase inhibition is digoxin's mechanism.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcce24a5d","lv":"ms3","d":"phenylephrine","sys":"shock","q":"Shortly after a phenylephrine infusion is started for hypotension, the patient's heart rate falls. What best explains this finding?","a":"Reflex bradycardia from increased vascular resistance","w":["Direct beta-1 receptor blockade at the sinus node","Drug-induced complete heart block from conduction toxicity","Muscarinic receptor stimulation at the AV node"],"x":"Phenylephrine is a pure alpha-1 agonist; the rise in blood pressure triggers a baroreceptor-mediated reflex slowing of heart rate. It has no beta-blocking or direct muscarinic activity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp46aa1787","lv":"ms3","d":"midodrine","sys":"shock","q":"A patient with neurogenic orthostatic hypotension is started on midodrine. Which adverse effect is most important to monitor for?","a":"Supine hypertension","w":["Severe hypokalemia","Bronchospasm","Profound bradycardia at rest"],"x":"Midodrine is an alpha-1 agonist prodrug that can cause marked supine hypertension, so patients are taught to take it during daytime upright hours and avoid lying flat afterward. Bronchospasm is a beta-blocker concern.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc996bd2f","lv":"ms3","d":"dopamine","sys":"shock","q":"Compared with norepinephrine in septic shock, dopamine is associated with which disadvantage?","a":"Higher rate of tachyarrhythmias","w":["Greater risk of digital ischemia","Inability to raise mean arterial pressure","Need for administration through an arterial line"],"x":"Trials comparing the two agents found more arrhythmic events with dopamine, which is why norepinephrine is first-line. Dopamine does raise blood pressure, and both agents can cause peripheral ischemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpee9f7f04","lv":"ms3","d":"angiotensin-ii","sys":"shock","q":"Synthetic angiotensin II is added for a patient with refractory vasodilatory shock. Which adverse effect requires concurrent prophylaxis?","a":"Venous and arterial thromboembolism","w":["Severe hypoglycemia","Acute pancreatitis","Drug-induced lupus"],"x":"Angiotensin II was associated with more thromboembolic events in its pivotal trial, so the label recommends concurrent VTE prophylaxis. Hypoglycemia and pancreatitis are not characteristic effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp701a2d33","lv":"ms3","d":"argatroban","sys":"blood","q":"On day seven of IV heparin, a patient's platelet count has fallen by more than half and a new DVT is found. Heparin has been discontinued. Which anticoagulant is most appropriate now?","a":"Argatroban","w":["Enoxaparin","Warfarin","Dalteparin"],"x":"Suspected heparin-induced thrombocytopenia requires a non-heparin anticoagulant such as the direct thrombin inhibitor argatroban. Low-molecular-weight heparins cross-react with HIT antibodies, and starting warfarin alone in acute HIT can cause venous limb gangrene.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpee5dce85","lv":"ms3","d":"protamine","sys":"blood","q":"A patient on a continuous unfractionated heparin infusion develops major bleeding. Which agent reverses the anticoagulant effect?","a":"Protamine sulfate","w":["Vitamin K","Idarucizumab","Desmopressin"],"x":"Protamine is a positively charged protein that binds and neutralizes heparin rapidly. Vitamin K reverses warfarin, and idarucizumab reverses dabigatran.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpae6667c3","lv":"ms3","d":"enoxaparin","sys":"blood","q":"Enoxaparin is being considered for VTE treatment. Which patient factor most warrants adjustment or an alternative anticoagulant?","a":"Severe renal impairment","w":["Mild hepatic steatosis","History of penicillin allergy","Well-controlled hypothyroidism"],"x":"Enoxaparin is renally cleared, so it accumulates in severe renal impairment and raises bleeding risk; dose adjustment or unfractionated heparin is used. Mild liver disease and the other conditions do not significantly alter its clearance.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp96f41fd0","lv":"ms3","d":"prothrombin-complex-concentrate","sys":"blood","q":"A patient taking warfarin presents with an intracranial hemorrhage and a markedly elevated INR. Which is the most appropriate reversal strategy?","a":"Four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate plus IV vitamin K","w":["Protamine sulfate plus fresh frozen plasma","Oral vitamin K alone with repeat INR","Idarucizumab plus IV tranexamic acid"],"x":"4F-PCC rapidly replaces factors II, VII, IX, and X, and IV vitamin K sustains reversal as the liver makes new factors. Oral vitamin K alone is far too slow for life-threatening bleeding.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8a38168c","lv":"ms3","d":"idarucizumab","sys":"blood","q":"A patient taking dabigatran needs emergency surgery for a perforated bowel. Which agent specifically reverses dabigatran?","a":"Idarucizumab","w":["Andexanet alfa","Protamine sulfate","Phytonadione"],"x":"Idarucizumab is a monoclonal antibody fragment that binds dabigatran with high affinity. Andexanet alfa reverses factor Xa inhibitors such as apixaban and rivaroxaban.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb996f108","lv":"ms3","d":"rivaroxaban","sys":"blood","q":"A patient with a DVT has triple-positive antiphospholipid syndrome. Why is rivaroxaban not recommended for long-term anticoagulation?","a":"It was linked to more thrombotic events than warfarin","w":["It cannot be given to patients with lupus anticoagulant tests","It requires frequent INR monitoring in this population","It causes heparin-induced thrombocytopenia in these patients"],"x":"In triple-positive antiphospholipid syndrome, rivaroxaban was associated with higher rates of recurrent thrombosis than warfarin, so warfarin is preferred. Rivaroxaban does not require INR monitoring.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp52f9aef0","lv":"ms3","d":"clopidogrel","sys":"blood","q":"A patient after coronary stenting has a CYP2C19 poor-metabolizer genotype. Why is clopidogrel likely to be less effective?","a":"Reduced conversion of the prodrug to its active metabolite","w":["Faster renal elimination of the active drug","Increased binding of the drug to plasma albumin","Upregulation of platelet glycoprotein IIb/IIIa receptors"],"x":"Clopidogrel is a prodrug activated mainly by CYP2C19, so poor metabolizers generate less active metabolite and get weaker P2Y12 inhibition. An alternative such as ticagrelor or prasugrel is often chosen.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6ad7463c","lv":"ms3","d":"prasugrel","sys":"blood","q":"A patient with acute coronary syndrome is undergoing PCI. Which history is a contraindication to prasugrel?","a":"Prior transient ischemic attack","w":["Prior hip replacement","Well-controlled asthma","History of gout"],"x":"Prasugrel carries a boxed warning and is contraindicated in patients with prior stroke or TIA because of increased intracranial bleeding. Asthma is a caution with ticagrelor because of dyspnea, not prasugrel.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp44fad158","lv":"ms3","d":"ferrous-sulfate","sys":"blood","q":"A patient with iron deficiency anemia is starting oral ferrous sulfate. Which counseling point is accurate?","a":"Dark stools and constipation are expected effects","w":["Take it together with calcium carbonate for absorption","Take it with milk to improve absorption","Bright red blood in stool is an expected effect"],"x":"Oral iron commonly darkens stools and causes constipation or GI upset. Calcium, antacids, and dairy decrease iron absorption, and bright red blood suggests bleeding that needs evaluation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf9931d32","lv":"ms3","d":"epoetin-alfa","sys":"blood","q":"A patient with CKD-related anemia is being evaluated for epoetin alfa. What should be assessed before starting therapy?","a":"Iron stores, including ferritin and transferrin saturation","w":["Serum uric acid and lactate dehydrogenase","Hemoglobin A2 by electrophoresis","Serum haptoglobin and direct antiglobulin test"],"x":"Erythropoiesis-stimulating agents need adequate iron to work, so iron deficiency is assessed and corrected first. Hemolysis tests and hemoglobin electrophoresis are not routine prerequisites.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7c4acff2","lv":"ms3","d":"succinylcholine","sys":"airway","q":"Rapid sequence intubation is planned. In which patient is succinylcholine contraindicated?","a":"A patient with a spinal cord injury two weeks ago","w":["A patient with a full stomach after a meal","A patient with well-controlled asthma","A patient with a history of migraine"],"x":"After denervation injury, upregulated extrajunctional acetylcholine receptors can release large amounts of potassium with succinylcholine and cause fatal hyperkalemia. A full stomach is an indication for rapid sequence intubation, not a contraindication to succinylcholine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1bd0c719","lv":"ms3","d":"succinylcholine","sys":"airway","q":"After succinylcholine for intubation, a patient develops masseter rigidity, rising end-tidal carbon dioxide, tachycardia, and fever. What is the most likely diagnosis?","a":"Malignant hyperthermia","w":["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Serotonin syndrome","Thyroid storm"],"x":"Succinylcholine and volatile anesthetics can trigger malignant hyperthermia in susceptible patients through uncontrolled RyR1 calcium release; dantrolene is the treatment. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome follows antipsychotics and develops over days.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9339e930","lv":"ms3","d":"sugammadex","sys":"airway","q":"How does sugammadex reverse rocuronium-induced neuromuscular blockade?","a":"It encapsulates rocuronium in plasma","w":["It inhibits acetylcholinesterase at the junction","It competitively blocks nicotinic receptors","It enhances renal excretion of rocuronium"],"x":"Sugammadex is a modified gamma-cyclodextrin that binds rocuronium and vecuronium, pulling them away from the neuromuscular junction. Neostigmine reverses blockade by inhibiting acetylcholinesterase.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpae53cb6e","lv":"ms3","d":"sugammadex","sys":"airway","q":"A woman using an oral hormonal contraceptive receives sugammadex after surgery. Which counseling point is most important?","a":"Use a backup nonhormonal contraceptive method for a week","w":["Expect permanent loss of contraceptive effect","Double the next contraceptive pill to compensate","No interaction with hormonal contraception occurs"],"x":"Sugammadex can bind progestogen, so the label advises treating its use like a missed pill and using backup nonhormonal contraception for the following seven days. The effect is temporary, not permanent.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2597596d","lv":"ms3","d":"etomidate","sys":"airway","q":"Etomidate is chosen as an induction agent for a hemodynamically unstable patient. Which adverse effect is characteristic of this drug?","a":"Transient adrenal suppression","w":["Malignant hyperthermia","Marked hypotension","Bronchospasm from histamine release"],"x":"Etomidate inhibits 11-beta-hydroxylase, which transiently suppresses cortisol synthesis. It is chosen precisely because it causes little hypotension.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb2235f95","lv":"ms3","d":"ketamine","sys":"airway","q":"A hypotensive trauma patient with a history of severe asthma needs rapid sequence intubation. Which induction agent is most appropriate?","a":"Ketamine","w":["Propofol","Methohexital","Thiopental"],"x":"Ketamine, an NMDA antagonist, tends to preserve blood pressure through sympathetic stimulation and causes bronchodilation. Propofol often causes significant hypotension in hemodynamically unstable patients.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc7dce9fd","lv":"ms3","d":"propofol","sys":"airway","q":"A ventilated patient has been sedated with a propofol infusion for several days. Which laboratory value should be monitored because of the drug's formulation?","a":"Serum triglycerides","w":["Serum ammonia","Serum lithium level","Serum uric acid"],"x":"Propofol is delivered in a lipid emulsion, so prolonged infusions can cause hypertriglyceridemia and pancreatitis. Ammonia monitoring relates to valproate or liver disease, not propofol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpaa7957e8","lv":"ms3","d":"midazolam","sys":"airway","q":"Midazolam is given for procedural sedation. Which effect is most characteristic of this drug?","a":"Anterograde amnesia","w":["Dissociative anesthesia with nystagmus","Prolonged neuromuscular paralysis","Significant analgesia without sedation"],"x":"Benzodiazepines such as midazolam produce sedation, anxiolysis, and anterograde amnesia but no meaningful analgesia. Dissociative anesthesia with nystagmus describes ketamine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3137c338","lv":"ms3","d":"fentanyl","sys":"airway","q":"A rapid IV bolus of fentanyl is given during induction, and bag-mask ventilation suddenly becomes very difficult. What is the most likely cause?","a":"Opioid-induced chest wall rigidity","w":["Histamine-mediated anaphylaxis","Tension pneumothorax from positive pressure","Malignant hyperthermia crisis"],"x":"Rapid administration of potent opioids such as fentanyl can cause chest wall and glottic rigidity that impairs ventilation; it responds to neuromuscular blockade or naloxone. Fentanyl causes little histamine release compared with morphine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp481dc6d4","lv":"ms3","d":"atropine","sys":"airway","q":"A patient has symptomatic sinus bradycardia with hypotension and dizziness. Which medication is first-line?","a":"Atropine","w":["Adenosine","Metoprolol","Diltiazem"],"x":"Atropine blocks vagal muscarinic tone at the sinus and AV nodes and is the first-line drug in ACLS bradycardia algorithms. Adenosine, metoprolol, and diltiazem all slow the heart rate further.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8ae77e48","lv":"ms3","d":"albuterol","sys":"resp","q":"A patient receives repeated albuterol nebulizer treatments for an asthma exacerbation. Which laboratory change is expected?","a":"Decreased serum potassium","w":["Increased serum sodium","Decreased serum glucose","Increased serum calcium"],"x":"Beta-2 stimulation activates Na+/K+-ATPase and shifts potassium into cells, which lowers serum potassium. Beta-agonists tend to raise, not lower, serum glucose.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp79d73f06","lv":"ms3","d":"budesonide-formoterol","sys":"resp","q":"Budesonide-formoterol can be used as both daily maintenance and as-needed reliever therapy in asthma. Which property of formoterol makes this possible?","a":"Rapid onset of bronchodilation","w":["Antimuscarinic action on airway smooth muscle","Absence of any systemic absorption","Inhibition of leukotriene synthesis"],"x":"Formoterol is a long-acting beta-2 agonist with a fast onset, so it relieves symptoms quickly while the paired corticosteroid treats inflammation. Salmeterol has a slower onset and is not used as a reliever.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2826e9e3","lv":"ms3","d":"fluticasone","sys":"resp","q":"A patient with persistent asthma starts inhaled fluticasone. Which counseling point best prevents its most common local adverse effect?","a":"Rinse the mouth and spit after each use","w":["Use it only during acute wheezing episodes","Take it with food to reduce stomach upset","Inhale it immediately before exercise"],"x":"Inhaled corticosteroids can cause oropharyngeal candidiasis and dysphonia; rinsing and spitting, plus using a spacer, reduces this. ICS are controllers and are not used as rescue therapy.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp622a7490","lv":"ms3","d":"salmeterol","sys":"resp","q":"Why must salmeterol be combined with an inhaled corticosteroid in a patient with asthma?","a":"LABA monotherapy in asthma increases asthma-related deaths","w":["Salmeterol is inactive without a corticosteroid present","Corticosteroids are needed to prevent salmeterol tolerance","Salmeterol monotherapy causes severe oral candidiasis"],"x":"Long-acting beta-agonist monotherapy in asthma was associated with increased severe exacerbations and asthma-related death, so it is used only with an ICS. Oral candidiasis is a corticosteroid effect, not a salmeterol effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp47080061","lv":"ms3","d":"tiotropium","sys":"resp","q":"A patient with COPD is being started on tiotropium. Which coexisting condition warrants the most caution?","a":"Urinary retention from prostatic hyperplasia","w":["Hypothyroidism on replacement therapy","Mild chronic kidney disease","Osteoarthritis of the knees"],"x":"Tiotropium is a long-acting muscarinic antagonist and can worsen urinary retention and narrow-angle glaucoma. Hypothyroidism and osteoarthritis are not affected by anticholinergic bronchodilators.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8a6dd5f9","lv":"ms3","d":"montelukast","sys":"resp","q":"A teenager with asthma and allergic rhinitis is taking montelukast. Which adverse effect carries an FDA boxed warning?","a":"Neuropsychiatric events, including suicidal thoughts","w":["Severe hepatotoxicity requiring liver transplant","Cardiac arrhythmias with QT prolongation","Agranulocytosis with severe infection"],"x":"The FDA added a boxed warning for serious neuropsychiatric events such as agitation, sleep disturbance, depression, and suicidality with montelukast. Hepatotoxicity is associated with zileuton, another leukotriene modifier.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp66cb153c","lv":"ms3","d":"theophylline","sys":"resp","q":"A patient with COPD on long-term theophylline develops nausea, tachycardia, and a seizure after starting a new antibiotic. Which antibiotic most likely caused this?","a":"Ciprofloxacin","w":["Amoxicillin","Cephalexin","Doxycycline"],"x":"Ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2, the main enzyme that metabolizes theophylline, so levels rise into the toxic range. Theophylline has a narrow therapeutic index and toxicity causes arrhythmias and seizures.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpca47d17c","lv":"ms3","d":"roflumilast","sys":"resp","q":"Roflumilast is added for a patient with severe COPD, chronic bronchitis, and frequent exacerbations. What is its mechanism of action?","a":"Selective inhibition of phosphodiesterase-4","w":["Blockade of muscarinic M3 receptors","Antagonism of cysteinyl leukotriene receptors","Binding and neutralizing circulating IgE"],"x":"Roflumilast inhibits PDE-4, increasing cAMP in inflammatory cells and reducing exacerbations. It is not a bronchodilator; M3 blockade describes tiotropium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7b447b5a","lv":"ms3","d":"omalizumab","sys":"resp","q":"A patient with severe allergic asthma and an elevated serum IgE is started on omalizumab. What should be done after each injection?","a":"Observe the patient for anaphylaxis","w":["Check a serum theophylline level","Obtain an ECG to check the QT interval","Measure blood glucose over several hours"],"x":"Omalizumab, an anti-IgE monoclonal antibody, carries a boxed warning for anaphylaxis, so patients are observed after administration and taught to recognize symptoms. It does not affect theophylline levels or the QT interval.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp57096da5","lv":"ms3","d":"elexacaftor-tezacaftor-ivacaftor","sys":"resp","q":"A patient with cystic fibrosis is starting elexacaftor-tezacaftor-ivacaftor. Which laboratory test requires routine monitoring?","a":"Liver function tests","w":["Serum lithium levels","Thyroid-stimulating hormone","Serum uric acid"],"x":"CFTR modulators can raise transaminases and bilirubin, and serious liver injury has been reported, so liver tests are checked at baseline and periodically. Thyroid and uric acid monitoring are not required.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2592c281","lv":"ms3","d":"phenytoin","sys":"neuro","q":"A patient on phenytoin for epilepsy presents with horizontal nystagmus, ataxia, and slurred speech after a recent dose increase. What is the most likely explanation?","a":"Phenytoin toxicity from saturable metabolism","w":["New posterior circulation stroke","Expected effect of subtherapeutic dosing","Vitamin B12 deficiency from phenytoin"],"x":"Phenytoin follows zero-order kinetics at therapeutic levels, so small dose increases can cause large rises in serum level and cerebellar signs such as nystagmus and ataxia. Stroke is possible but less likely given the timing with a dose change.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9bb62a4f","lv":"ms3","d":"fosphenytoin","sys":"neuro","q":"Fosphenytoin is chosen instead of IV phenytoin in a patient with status epilepticus. What is a key advantage of fosphenytoin?","a":"Less risk of local tissue injury at the infusion site","w":["No need for cardiac monitoring during infusion","No effect on hepatic enzyme metabolism","No risk of hypotension during infusion"],"x":"Fosphenytoin is a water-soluble prodrug with less infusion-site injury, including purple glove syndrome, and it can be given IM. Cardiac monitoring is still needed because hypotension and arrhythmias can occur.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp72cbef2f","lv":"ms3","d":"lorazepam","sys":"neuro","q":"A patient has a generalized tonic-clonic seizure lasting several minutes and has IV access. Which medication is the first-line treatment?","a":"Intravenous lorazepam","w":["Intravenous levetiracetam","Intravenous phenobarbital","Intravenous propofol"],"x":"Benzodiazepines such as IV lorazepam are first-line for status epilepticus. Levetiracetam, fosphenytoin, or valproate are second-line agents if seizures continue, and propofol is reserved for refractory status.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe128ae7f","lv":"ms3","d":"valproic-acid","sys":"neuro","q":"A woman of reproductive age with generalized epilepsy is considering valproic acid. Which risk is most important to discuss?","a":"Neural tube defects and other major birth defects","w":["Gingival hyperplasia and hirsutism","Kidney stones and metabolic acidosis","Stevens-Johnson syndrome in carriers of HLA-B*1502"],"x":"Valproate carries a boxed warning for major congenital malformations, especially neural tube defects, and reduced IQ after in utero exposure. Gingival hyperplasia and hirsutism are typical of phenytoin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0e7d35b3","lv":"ms3","d":"lamotrigine","sys":"neuro","q":"A patient on valproic acid has lamotrigine added. Why does this combination require especially slow lamotrigine titration?","a":"Valproate raises lamotrigine levels and serious rash risk","w":["Valproate lowers lamotrigine levels and causes seizures","Lamotrigine induces valproate metabolism and causes hepatotoxicity","The combination causes severe QT prolongation"],"x":"Valproate inhibits lamotrigine glucuronidation, roughly doubling its levels and raising the risk of Stevens-Johnson syndrome. A slower, lower titration schedule is used.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0d1d1ee2","lv":"ms3","d":"carbamazepine","sys":"neuro","q":"Before starting carbamazepine in a patient of Han Chinese ancestry, which test is recommended?","a":"HLA-B*1502 genotyping","w":["TPMT enzyme activity","G6PD enzyme activity","CYP2C19 genotyping"],"x":"Patients with HLA-B*1502, common in many Asian populations, have a much higher risk of carbamazepine-induced Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis. TPMT testing applies to thiopurines.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0b07a4eb","lv":"ms3","d":"sumatriptan","sys":"neuro","q":"Which patient with migraine has a contraindication to sumatriptan?","a":"A patient with known coronary artery disease","w":["A patient with well-controlled asthma","A patient with mild chronic kidney disease","A patient with seasonal allergic rhinitis"],"x":"Triptans are 5-HT1B/1D agonists that can cause coronary vasoconstriction, so they are contraindicated in ischemic heart disease, prior stroke, and uncontrolled hypertension. Asthma does not limit triptan use.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcdad509d","lv":"ms3","d":"carbidopa-levodopa","sys":"neuro","q":"Why is carbidopa combined with levodopa in Parkinson disease?","a":"It blocks peripheral conversion of levodopa to dopamine","w":["It directly stimulates central dopamine receptors","It inhibits monoamine oxidase B in the brain","It blocks central muscarinic receptors"],"x":"Carbidopa inhibits peripheral dopa decarboxylase and does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so more levodopa reaches the brain and peripheral nausea is reduced. MAO-B inhibition describes selegiline and rasagiline.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcf0083d6","lv":"ms3","d":"alteplase","sys":"neuro","q":"A patient presents with acute ischemic stroke symptoms within the treatment window. Which finding is an absolute contraindication to alteplase?","a":"Intracranial hemorrhage on noncontrast head CT","w":["Age older than eighty years","Mild facial droop on the affected side","History of well-controlled hypertension"],"x":"Any intracranial hemorrhage on CT rules out thrombolysis. Advanced age and controlled hypertension are not absolute contraindications, and blood pressure must simply be controlled before treatment.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9f15010f","lv":"ms3","d":"nimodipine","sys":"neuro","q":"A patient with aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage is started on nimodipine. What is the purpose of this therapy?","a":"To improve outcomes from delayed cerebral ischemia","w":["To lower blood pressure to normal levels quickly","To prevent rebleeding from the aneurysm","To prevent early post-hemorrhage seizures"],"x":"Nimodipine improves neurologic outcome after subarachnoid hemorrhage by reducing delayed ischemic deficits. It is given orally or by feeding tube only; IV administration of the capsule contents can cause cardiac arrest.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcaad175f","lv":"ms3","d":"naloxone","sys":"pain","q":"A patient with an overdose of extended-release morphine awakens after IV naloxone. Why does this patient still need prolonged observation?","a":"Naloxone's effect may wear off before the opioid's","w":["Naloxone causes delayed respiratory depression itself","Naloxone enhances later absorption of the opioid","Naloxone blocks the liver's metabolism of morphine"],"x":"Naloxone has a shorter duration of action than many opioids, especially extended-release or long-acting agents, so sedation and respiratory depression can recur. Repeat doses or an infusion may be needed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbd3e7925","lv":"ms3","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"pain","q":"Which patient factor most increases the risk of hepatotoxicity from acetaminophen within the usual dosing range?","a":"Chronic heavy alcohol use","w":["Concurrent use of omeprazole","Mild chronic kidney disease","History of peptic ulcer disease"],"x":"Chronic alcohol use induces CYP2E1 and depletes glutathione, increasing toxic NAPQI and liver injury. Peptic ulcer disease is a concern with NSAIDs, not acetaminophen.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd0d4ba96","lv":"ms3","d":"ketorolac","sys":"pain","q":"In which postoperative patient should ketorolac be avoided?","a":"A patient with acute kidney injury after surgery","w":["A patient with well-controlled hypothyroidism","A patient with mild seasonal allergies","A patient with controlled hyperlipidemia"],"x":"Ketorolac is a potent NSAID that reduces renal prostaglandins and can worsen kidney injury; it is also avoided in active GI bleeding and for perioperative pain in CABG. The other conditions do not limit its use.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfdaaeb42","lv":"ms3","d":"tramadol","sys":"pain","q":"A patient taking sertraline is prescribed tramadol for back pain. Which adverse effect is of greatest concern?","a":"Serotonin syndrome","w":["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Severe hypertensive crisis","Agranulocytosis"],"x":"Tramadol inhibits serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake in addition to its opioid effect, so combining it with an SSRI raises serotonin syndrome risk. Tramadol also lowers the seizure threshold.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9cc0aa22","lv":"ms3","d":"methadone","sys":"pain","q":"A patient is starting methadone for opioid use disorder. Which test is most important to monitor for its cardiac risk?","a":"Electrocardiogram for QT interval","w":["Echocardiogram for ejection fraction","Serum troponin for myocardial injury","Exercise stress test for ischemia"],"x":"Methadone prolongs the QT interval and can cause torsades de pointes, particularly at higher doses or with other QT-prolonging drugs. It does not reduce ejection fraction or cause ischemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp78d5e7ec","lv":"ms3","d":"buprenorphine","sys":"pain","q":"A patient with opioid use disorder who used heroin a few hours ago requests buprenorphine induction. What is the main risk of giving it now?","a":"Precipitated opioid withdrawal","w":["Severe respiratory depression","Serotonin syndrome","Malignant hyperthermia"],"x":"Buprenorphine is a high-affinity partial mu agonist that displaces full agonists and can precipitate withdrawal, so induction usually waits until mild-to-moderate withdrawal is present. Its ceiling effect makes respiratory depression less likely than with full agonists.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdbf8ab58","lv":"ms3","d":"meperidine","sys":"pain","q":"A patient with end-stage renal disease received repeated meperidine and now has tremor and myoclonus followed by a seizure. What is the cause?","a":"Accumulation of the metabolite normeperidine","w":["Accumulation of morphine-6-glucuronide","Opioid-induced hypoglycemia","Direct histamine release in the brain"],"x":"Normeperidine is a renally cleared metabolite that causes CNS excitation and seizures, which is why meperidine is avoided in renal impairment and older adults. Morphine-6-glucuronide is morphine's metabolite.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5c914e63","lv":"ms3","d":"morphine","sys":"pain","q":"A patient with advanced chronic kidney disease needs an opioid for severe pain. Why is morphine a poor choice?","a":"Its active metabolites accumulate and cause toxicity","w":["It is only available by the intravenous route","It is eliminated mainly by the liver unchanged","It has no effect on visceral pain"],"x":"Morphine's glucuronide metabolites are renally cleared and accumulate in kidney failure, causing sedation, respiratory depression, and neurotoxicity. Fentanyl or hydromorphone with caution are often preferred.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp62f81f81","lv":"ms3","d":"celecoxib","sys":"pain","q":"Compared with nonselective NSAIDs such as naproxen, celecoxib is associated with which characteristic?","a":"Lower GI ulcer risk with similar cardiovascular warnings","w":["Higher GI ulcer risk with no cardiovascular warnings","No renal effects and no cardiovascular warnings","Strong antiplatelet effect similar to aspirin"],"x":"Selective COX-2 inhibition spares gastric protective prostaglandins, but celecoxib carries the same boxed warnings for cardiovascular thrombotic events and GI bleeding as other NSAIDs. It has no meaningful antiplatelet effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpca4b6412","lv":"ms3","d":"dexmedetomidine","sys":"pain","q":"A mechanically ventilated patient is sedated with dexmedetomidine. Which adverse effect is most common?","a":"Bradycardia and hypotension","w":["Severe respiratory depression","Hypertriglyceridemia","Malignant hyperthermia"],"x":"Dexmedetomidine is a central alpha-2 agonist that reduces sympathetic outflow, causing bradycardia and hypotension with relatively little respiratory depression. Hypertriglyceridemia is a propofol effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe6eb266e","lv":"ms3","d":"lithium","sys":"psych","q":"A patient stable on lithium for bipolar disorder develops coarse tremor, confusion, and ataxia after starting a new medication for hypertension. Which medication most likely caused this?","a":"Hydrochlorothiazide","w":["Amlodipine","Hydralazine","Doxazosin"],"x":"Thiazides increase proximal sodium and lithium reabsorption, raising lithium levels into the toxic range. ACE inhibitors and NSAIDs have a similar effect, while amlodipine does not meaningfully change lithium clearance.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf3125fb2","lv":"ms3","d":"lithium","sys":"psych","q":"Which laboratory tests should be monitored periodically in a patient on long-term lithium?","a":"Renal function and thyroid function","w":["Liver enzymes and serum lipase","Complete blood count and haptoglobin","Serum prolactin and fasting lipids"],"x":"Lithium can cause hypothyroidism and chronic kidney effects, including nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, so TSH and renal function are checked along with drug levels. Lithium tends to cause leukocytosis rather than cytopenias.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp38ada1b3","lv":"ms3","d":"clozapine","sys":"psych","q":"A patient with treatment-resistant schizophrenia is starting clozapine. Which monitoring is required?","a":"Absolute neutrophil count","w":["Serum lithium level","Serum ammonia level","Thyroid antibodies"],"x":"Clozapine can cause severe neutropenia, so the absolute neutrophil count is monitored on a set schedule. Myocarditis, seizures, and metabolic effects are other important clozapine risks.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa4223e87","lv":"ms3","d":"sertraline","sys":"psych","q":"A patient has generalized anxiety disorder without other psychiatric conditions. Which medication is an appropriate first-line long-term treatment?","a":"Sertraline","w":["Alprazolam","Hydroxyzine","Propranolol"],"x":"SSRIs and SNRIs are first-line maintenance therapy for generalized anxiety disorder. Benzodiazepines such as alprazolam carry dependence and misuse risks and are not first-line long-term therapy.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd01b6cc6","lv":"ms3","d":"bupropion","sys":"psych","q":"A patient with major depression asks about bupropion. Which history is a contraindication?","a":"Current bulimia nervosa","w":["Prior SSRI-related sexual dysfunction","Current tobacco use","History of hypothyroidism"],"x":"Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold and is contraindicated in seizure disorders and in bulimia or anorexia nervosa. SSRI-related sexual dysfunction and smoking are reasons bupropion may be preferred.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3133f6ec","lv":"ms3","d":"haloperidol","sys":"psych","q":"A few hours after receiving IM haloperidol, a patient develops sustained neck twisting and upward eye deviation. Which treatment is most appropriate?","a":"Benztropine","w":["Bromocriptine","Dantrolene","Propranolol"],"x":"This is acute dystonia from dopamine blockade, treated with an anticholinergic such as benztropine or with diphenhydramine. Bromocriptine and dantrolene are used for neuroleptic malignant syndrome, and propranolol for akathisia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp85ae8ade","lv":"ms3","d":"olanzapine","sys":"psych","q":"A patient with schizophrenia is starting olanzapine. Which monitoring is most important for its major long-term adverse effects?","a":"Weight, fasting glucose, and lipid panel","w":["Absolute neutrophil count each week","Serum lithium and creatinine levels","Liver biopsy before therapy"],"x":"Olanzapine has high risk of weight gain, hyperglycemia, and dyslipidemia, so metabolic monitoring is essential. Weekly neutrophil monitoring is specific to clozapine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp12ad7a1b","lv":"ms3","d":"phenelzine","sys":"psych","q":"A patient taking phenelzine eats aged cheese and develops a severe occipital headache and very high blood pressure. What is the mechanism?","a":"Tyramine is not broken down and releases norepinephrine","w":["Serotonin reuptake is blocked in the brainstem","Dopamine receptors are blocked in the hypothalamus","Histamine is released from intestinal mast cells"],"x":"MAO inhibitors prevent breakdown of dietary tyramine in the gut and liver, and tyramine then releases norepinephrine, causing a hypertensive crisis. Patients are counseled to avoid aged, fermented, and cured foods.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpafb8ddb5","lv":"ms3","d":"acamprosate","sys":"psych","q":"A patient with alcohol use disorder and cirrhosis also takes daily oxycodone for chronic pain. Which medication for alcohol use disorder is most appropriate?","a":"Acamprosate","w":["Naltrexone","Disulfiram","Chlordiazepoxide"],"x":"Acamprosate is renally cleared and does not interact with opioids, making it suitable in liver disease. Naltrexone would precipitate opioid withdrawal, and disulfiram is risky in cirrhosis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3e3dad09","lv":"ms3","d":"varenicline","sys":"psych","q":"How does varenicline help with smoking cessation?","a":"Partial agonism at alpha-4 beta-2 nicotinic receptors","w":["Inhibition of dopamine and norepinephrine reuptake","Full agonism at nicotinic receptors in muscle","Antagonism of mu opioid receptors in the brain"],"x":"Varenicline partially stimulates nicotinic receptors to reduce cravings and withdrawal while blocking the rewarding effect of nicotine from smoking. Dopamine and norepinephrine reuptake inhibition describes bupropion.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp63db0bef","lv":"ms3","d":"calcium-gluconate","sys":"renal","q":"A patient with severe hyperkalemia has peaked T waves and a widened QRS complex. Which medication should be given first to stabilize the cardiac membrane?","a":"IV calcium gluconate","w":["Oral sodium polystyrene sulfonate","IV furosemide","Oral patiromer"],"x":"IV calcium antagonizes the membrane effects of potassium and acts within minutes but does not lower potassium. Binders and diuretics remove potassium too slowly to be the first step.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2fa96431","lv":"ms3","d":"regular-insulin","sys":"renal","q":"IV regular insulin is given with dextrose to treat hyperkalemia. Which monitoring is most important afterward?","a":"Frequent blood glucose checks for hypoglycemia","w":["Serum magnesium checks for hypermagnesemia","Urine output for osmotic diuresis","Serum calcium checks for hypercalcemia"],"x":"Insulin shifts potassium into cells but can cause delayed hypoglycemia, especially in kidney disease, so glucose is checked frequently. The other effects are not expected from this treatment.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdecce9ea","lv":"ms3","d":"patiromer","sys":"renal","q":"A patient with CKD on an ACE inhibitor is started on patiromer for chronic hyperkalemia. Which electrolyte abnormality should be monitored?","a":"Hypomagnesemia","w":["Hypernatremia","Hypercalcemia","Hyperphosphatemia"],"x":"Patiromer exchanges calcium for potassium and also binds magnesium, so hypomagnesemia is a recognized adverse effect. Other oral drugs should be separated in time from patiromer to avoid binding.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7d101802","lv":"ms3","d":"furosemide","sys":"renal","q":"A patient with heart failure on high-intensity furosemide therapy is most likely to show which laboratory pattern?","a":"Hypokalemia with metabolic alkalosis","w":["Hyperkalemia with metabolic acidosis","Hypercalcemia with metabolic acidosis","Hypernatremia with respiratory acidosis"],"x":"Loop diuretics increase distal sodium delivery and potassium and hydrogen ion loss, causing hypokalemic contraction alkalosis. They also increase calcium excretion, unlike thiazides.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd96abf1e","lv":"ms3","d":"hydrochlorothiazide","sys":"renal","q":"A patient develops an acute gout flare a few weeks after starting hydrochlorothiazide. What explains this?","a":"Decreased renal excretion of uric acid","w":["Increased purine production in the liver","Direct crystal deposition of the drug","Increased intestinal absorption of purines"],"x":"Thiazides compete with uric acid for proximal tubular secretion and cause volume contraction, both raising serum urate. Thiazides can also cause hypercalcemia, hyponatremia, and hyperglycemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp07d32240","lv":"ms3","d":"spironolactone","sys":"renal","q":"A patient with cirrhosis has new moderate ascites. Which diuretic regimen is first-line?","a":"Spironolactone with or without furosemide","w":["Hydrochlorothiazide alone","Acetazolamide alone","Mannitol with furosemide"],"x":"Secondary hyperaldosteronism drives sodium retention in cirrhosis, so the aldosterone antagonist spironolactone is the foundation, often paired with furosemide. Thiazides add hyponatremia risk without targeting the mechanism.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpab10bf23","lv":"ms3","d":"acetazolamide","sys":"renal","q":"A patient taking acetazolamide for prevention of acute mountain sickness has which expected acid-base change?","a":"Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis","w":["High anion gap metabolic acidosis","Hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis","Primary respiratory acidosis"],"x":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibition causes bicarbonate loss in the urine, producing a hyperchloremic, normal anion gap acidosis that stimulates ventilation. Hypochloremic alkalosis is seen with loop and thiazide diuretics.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3a1fffa3","lv":"ms3","d":"tamsulosin","sys":"renal","q":"A man taking tamsulosin for benign prostatic hyperplasia is scheduled for cataract surgery. What is the most important counseling point?","a":"Inform the eye surgeon about tamsulosin before surgery","w":["Expect the drug to lower intraocular pressure","Tamsulosin has no relevance to eye surgery","Expect improved pupil dilation during surgery"],"x":"Alpha-1A blockade affects the iris dilator muscle and can cause intraoperative floppy iris syndrome, so the surgeon needs to know in advance. Pupil dilation tends to be poorer, not better.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe56b2fb1","lv":"ms3","d":"finasteride","sys":"renal","q":"A man has taken finasteride for benign prostatic hyperplasia for a year. How does it affect prostate cancer screening?","a":"It lowers PSA, so measured values should be doubled","w":["It raises PSA, so measured values should be halved","It has no effect on PSA results","It makes PSA undetectable and unusable"],"x":"Finasteride, a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor, lowers PSA by about half after several months, so values are doubled for interpretation. Any confirmed PSA rise on finasteride warrants evaluation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3746bb1f","lv":"ms3","d":"mirabegron","sys":"renal","q":"An older adult with mild dementia has overactive bladder. Which medication is preferred to avoid anticholinergic cognitive effects?","a":"Mirabegron","w":["Oxybutynin","Tolterodine","Trospium"],"x":"Mirabegron is a beta-3 agonist that relaxes the detrusor without anticholinergic effects; blood pressure should be monitored. Oxybutynin is strongly anticholinergic and can worsen confusion in older adults.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3b54c075","lv":"ms3","d":"alendronate","sys":"bone","q":"A patient is starting oral alendronate for osteoporosis. Which counseling point is correct?","a":"Take with plain water on an empty stomach and stay upright","w":["Take with milk at bedtime to reduce nausea","Take with breakfast and a calcium supplement","Lie down after taking to improve absorption"],"x":"Alendronate is poorly absorbed and can cause pill esophagitis, so it is taken first thing in the morning with plain water, fasting, and the patient stays upright before eating. Calcium and food greatly reduce absorption.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7a0ca8de","lv":"ms3","d":"alendronate","sys":"bone","q":"A woman on alendronate for several years reports new dull pain in the anterior thigh. Which complication should be considered?","a":"Atypical femoral fracture","w":["Osteosarcoma of the femur","Septic arthritis of the hip","Rhabdomyolysis of the quadriceps"],"x":"Long-term bisphosphonate use is linked to atypical subtrochanteric or femoral shaft fractures, often preceded by thigh or groin pain, so imaging is warranted. Osteosarcoma was a historic concern with teriparatide, not bisphosphonates.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp102b2536","lv":"ms3","d":"allopurinol","sys":"bone","q":"A patient taking azathioprine for autoimmune hepatitis is started on allopurinol for gout. Which complication is most likely if this combination is not adjusted?","a":"Severe bone marrow suppression","w":["Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Severe hypercalcemia","Rapid loss of azathioprine effect"],"x":"Allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase, which normally breaks down azathioprine and mercaptopurine, so levels rise and can cause life-threatening myelosuppression. The thiopurine dose must be substantially reduced or the combination avoided.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp34a45a9b","lv":"ms3","d":"colchicine","sys":"bone","q":"A patient taking colchicine for gout is prescribed clarithromycin. What is the main concern?","a":"Colchicine toxicity from inhibited CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein","w":["Reduced antibacterial effect of clarithromycin","Accelerated colchicine clearance and gout flare","Severe hyperuricemia from the antibiotic"],"x":"Clarithromycin strongly inhibits CYP3A4 and P-gp, which raises colchicine levels and can cause fatal toxicity with GI symptoms, myopathy, and cytopenias. The interaction does not weaken the antibiotic.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5afc5566","lv":"ms3","d":"febuxostat","sys":"bone","q":"Febuxostat is considered for a patient with gout and established coronary artery disease. Which FDA boxed warning applies?","a":"Higher risk of cardiovascular death than allopurinol","w":["Fatal hepatic failure in most patients","Severe hypercalcemia with kidney stones","Serious neuropsychiatric events and suicide"],"x":"In a large trial, febuxostat was associated with higher cardiovascular and all-cause mortality than allopurinol in patients with cardiovascular disease, so it is reserved for those who cannot take allopurinol. Neuropsychiatric warnings apply to montelukast, not febuxostat.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfeeb7a6a","lv":"ms3","d":"probenecid","sys":"bone","q":"Which patient is the poorest candidate for probenecid to lower uric acid?","a":"A patient with a history of uric acid kidney stones","w":["A patient with normal renal function and low urate excretion","A patient who cannot tolerate xanthine oxidase inhibitors","A patient with no history of kidney stones"],"x":"Probenecid is a uricosuric that increases urinary uric acid, raising the risk of stone formation, so it is avoided with uric acid stones and urate overproduction. It works best in underexcreters with normal kidney function.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp25f47e2b","lv":"ms3","d":"denosumab","sys":"bone","q":"Before each dose of denosumab for osteoporosis, what should be assessed?","a":"Serum calcium and vitamin D status","w":["Serum potassium and magnesium only","Thyroid-stimulating hormone and free T4","Fasting glucose and lipid panel"],"x":"Denosumab inhibits RANKL and osteoclast activity and can cause significant hypocalcemia, especially in kidney disease, so calcium and vitamin D must be adequate. Patients also need to know that delaying doses can cause rebound vertebral fractures.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp27543197","lv":"ms3","d":"teriparatide","sys":"bone","q":"How does teriparatide improve bone density in severe osteoporosis?","a":"Intermittent PTH receptor stimulation increases bone formation","w":["Inhibition of RANK ligand blocks osteoclast formation","Binding to hydroxyapatite inhibits osteoclast activity","Estrogen receptor agonism in bone reduces turnover"],"x":"Teriparatide is a recombinant PTH analog; given intermittently, it favors osteoblast activity and new bone formation, making it an anabolic agent. RANKL inhibition is denosumab's mechanism, and hydroxyapatite binding describes bisphosphonates.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcc14f888","lv":"ms3","d":"raloxifene","sys":"bone","q":"Which history is a contraindication to raloxifene for postmenopausal osteoporosis?","a":"A prior deep vein thrombosis","w":["A family history of breast cancer","A history of vertebral fracture","Mild hot flashes after menopause"],"x":"Raloxifene is a SERM with a boxed warning for venous thromboembolism and fatal stroke. It lowers invasive breast cancer risk, so a family history of breast cancer may actually favor it.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp091cb8a0","lv":"ms3","d":"rasburicase","sys":"bone","q":"A patient at high risk for tumor lysis syndrome is being considered for rasburicase. Which test should be done before treatment?","a":"G6PD deficiency screening","w":["HLA-B*5801 genotyping","TPMT enzyme activity","Serum methylmalonic acid"],"x":"Rasburicase converts uric acid to allantoin and produces hydrogen peroxide, which can cause severe hemolysis and methemoglobinemia in G6PD deficiency. HLA-B*5801 testing relates to allopurinol hypersensitivity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1018c463","lv":"ms3","d":"n-acetylcysteine","sys":"tox","q":"A patient presents several hours after an intentional acetaminophen overdose. How does N-acetylcysteine prevent liver injury?","a":"It replenishes glutathione to detoxify NAPQI","w":["It blocks acetaminophen absorption from the gut","It enhances renal excretion of acetaminophen","It inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase in the liver"],"x":"NAC restores glutathione stores and serves as a substitute that conjugates the toxic metabolite NAPQI. Alcohol dehydrogenase inhibition is fomepizole's mechanism.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpebc1c31c","lv":"ms3","d":"fomepizole","sys":"tox","q":"A patient presents with a high anion gap metabolic acidosis, an elevated osmolal gap, and calcium oxalate crystals in the urine. Which antidote acts by inhibiting alcohol dehydrogenase?","a":"Fomepizole","w":["Pralidoxime","Deferoxamine","Flumazenil"],"x":"These findings suggest ethylene glycol poisoning; fomepizole blocks alcohol dehydrogenase and prevents formation of toxic metabolites. Hemodialysis may also be needed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpea9c3592","lv":"ms3","d":"physostigmine","sys":"tox","q":"Physostigmine is considered for a patient with suspected anticholinergic toxicity. Which ECG finding is a contraindication?","a":"A widened QRS suggesting tricyclic antidepressant toxicity","w":["Sinus tachycardia with a narrow QRS","Mild nonspecific T-wave flattening","Normal sinus rhythm with a narrow QRS"],"x":"Physostigmine can precipitate bradyasystole and seizures in tricyclic overdose, which is suggested by QRS widening. Sinus tachycardia is an expected anticholinergic finding and does not preclude its use.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3d945dbd","lv":"ms3","d":"sodium-bicarbonate","sys":"tox","q":"A patient with an amitriptyline overdose has hypotension and a widened QRS complex. Which treatment is indicated?","a":"IV sodium bicarbonate","w":["IV physostigmine","IV flumazenil","IV procainamide"],"x":"Sodium bicarbonate counteracts tricyclic sodium channel blockade through sodium loading and alkalinization, narrowing the QRS. Procainamide is a sodium channel blocker and would worsen toxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5808be71","lv":"ms3","d":"atropine","sys":"tox","q":"A farmworker with organophosphate poisoning is receiving repeated IV atropine. Which clinical endpoint guides further atropine doses?","a":"Drying of bronchial secretions","w":["Return of normal pupil size","Resolution of muscle fasciculations","Normalization of heart rate below normal"],"x":"Atropine is titrated to clear bronchorrhea and improve oxygenation, since airway secretions are the main cause of death. Atropine does not reverse nicotinic effects such as fasciculations or weakness; pralidoxime addresses those.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp598cd8b6","lv":"ms3","d":"pralidoxime","sys":"tox","q":"Why should pralidoxime be given early in organophosphate poisoning?","a":"It must reactivate acetylcholinesterase before aging occurs","w":["It is only absorbed well during the first hour","It must block muscarinic receptors before they upregulate","It is converted to an inactive form by the liver"],"x":"Pralidoxime regenerates acetylcholinesterase by removing the phosphate group, but once the enzyme-organophosphate bond ages it can no longer be reactivated. Muscarinic blockade is the role of atropine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp249cce7b","lv":"ms3","d":"digoxin-immune-fab","sys":"tox","q":"A patient with digoxin toxicity receives digoxin immune Fab. How should the serum digoxin level be interpreted afterward?","a":"Total digoxin rises and no longer reflects active drug","w":["Total digoxin falls to zero within minutes","Total digoxin accurately guides redosing","Total digoxin is unaffected by the antibody"],"x":"Most assays measure both free and Fab-bound digoxin, so total levels rise after treatment even though active drug is neutralized. Clinical response, not total level, guides further management.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb7ea70f3","lv":"ms3","d":"hydroxocobalamin","sys":"tox","q":"A patient rescued from a house fire has altered mental status, hypotension, and severe lactic acidosis. Which antidote is recommended?","a":"Hydroxocobalamin","w":["Methylene blue","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"],"x":"Smoke inhalation can cause cyanide poisoning, and hydroxocobalamin binds cyanide to form cyanocobalamin, which is excreted in the urine. It commonly causes red skin and urine; methylene blue treats methemoglobinemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7446d3ef","lv":"ms3","d":"methylene-blue","sys":"tox","q":"A patient develops cyanosis and chocolate-brown blood after topical benzocaine. Before giving methylene blue, which condition should be considered?","a":"G6PD deficiency","w":["Iron deficiency anemia","Hereditary hemochromatosis","Vitamin K deficiency"],"x":"Methylene blue requires NADPH from the G6PD pathway to reduce methemoglobin, and it can cause hemolysis in G6PD deficiency. It also carries serotonin syndrome risk with serotonergic drugs.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp740ce5aa","lv":"ms3","d":"deferoxamine","sys":"tox","q":"A child with severe iron poisoning receives IV deferoxamine. Which finding is expected as iron is chelated and excreted?","a":"Reddish-brown urine","w":["Bright green urine","Black, tarry urine","Blue-tinged skin"],"x":"Deferoxamine binds free iron to form ferrioxamine, which is excreted in urine and gives it a reddish-brown color. Prolonged infusion is linked to ARDS.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf90e2156","lv":"ms3","d":"vancomycin","sys":"infect","q":"During an IV vancomycin infusion, a patient develops flushing and pruritus of the face, neck, and upper chest without wheezing or hypotension. What is the most likely cause?","a":"Infusion rate-related histamine release","w":["IgE-mediated anaphylaxis","Drug-induced lupus","Stevens-Johnson syndrome"],"x":"Vancomycin infusion reaction is a non-immune histamine release linked to rapid infusion and is managed by slowing the rate and giving antihistamines. It is not a true allergy, unlike anaphylaxis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd05d4fe4","lv":"ms3","d":"ceftriaxone","sys":"infect","q":"An adult has uncomplicated urethral gonorrhea. Which treatment is recommended by current CDC guidelines?","a":"Intramuscular ceftriaxone","w":["Oral ciprofloxacin","Oral azithromycin alone","Oral amoxicillin"],"x":"CDC recommends a single intramuscular dose of ceftriaxone for uncomplicated gonorrhea, with doxycycline added if chlamydia has not been excluded. Fluoroquinolone and azithromycin monotherapy are limited by resistance.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb3ad4f14","lv":"ms3","d":"trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","sys":"infect","q":"An older adult on lisinopril is treated with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. Which electrolyte abnormality is most likely?","a":"Hyperkalemia","w":["Hypokalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hypernatremia"],"x":"Trimethoprim blocks the epithelial sodium channel in the collecting duct like amiloride, reducing potassium excretion, and risk is greater with ACE inhibitors. It can also raise serum creatinine by blocking tubular secretion.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp803aa139","lv":"ms3","d":"ciprofloxacin","sys":"infect","q":"An older adult taking chronic prednisone is prescribed ciprofloxacin. Which adverse effect is of particular concern?","a":"Achilles tendon rupture","w":["Red man syndrome","Gray baby syndrome","Ototoxicity with vestibular loss"],"x":"Fluoroquinolones carry a boxed warning for tendinitis and tendon rupture, with higher risk in older adults and those taking corticosteroids. Ototoxicity is typical of aminoglycosides.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp84c8e671","lv":"ms3","d":"metronidazole","sys":"infect","q":"A patient is prescribed metronidazole for bacterial vaginosis. Which counseling point is most important?","a":"Avoid alcohol during treatment and shortly after","w":["Take with dairy products to improve absorption","Avoid sunlight exposure for several weeks","Expect orange discoloration of urine and tears"],"x":"Metronidazole can cause a disulfiram-like reaction with flushing, nausea, and vomiting when combined with alcohol. Orange secretions are seen with rifampin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp62d2cfab","lv":"ms3","d":"linezolid","sys":"infect","q":"A patient taking escitalopram develops agitation, hyperthermia, and clonus after starting an antibiotic for MRSA pneumonia. Which antibiotic is most likely responsible?","a":"Linezolid","w":["Vancomycin","Clindamycin","Doxycycline"],"x":"Linezolid is a weak monoamine oxidase inhibitor and can cause serotonin syndrome with SSRIs. It also causes thrombocytopenia with prolonged use.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5a0f07db","lv":"ms3","d":"isoniazid","sys":"infect","q":"A patient starts isoniazid for latent tuberculosis. Which supplement is given to prevent a common adverse effect?","a":"Pyridoxine to prevent peripheral neuropathy","w":["Folic acid to prevent megaloblastic anemia","Vitamin K to prevent bleeding","Thiamine to prevent Wernicke encephalopathy"],"x":"Isoniazid increases pyridoxine excretion and can cause peripheral neuropathy, so vitamin B6 is given to at-risk patients. Liver enzymes are also monitored because isoniazid causes hepatitis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp60b8c066","lv":"ms3","d":"rifampin","sys":"infect","q":"A woman taking a combined oral contraceptive is starting rifampin. Which counseling point is most important?","a":"Use a nonhormonal backup method of contraception","w":["Increase fluid intake to prevent kidney stones","Expect blue-green discoloration of the urine","No interaction with hormonal contraception occurs"],"x":"Rifampin is a strong CYP3A4 inducer that lowers estrogen and progestin levels and can cause contraceptive failure. It also turns urine, sweat, and tears orange-red, not blue-green.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4f3127be","lv":"ms3","d":"fidaxomicin","sys":"infect","q":"An adult has a first episode of non-severe Clostridioides difficile infection. Which antibiotic is preferred by current IDSA guidelines?","a":"Oral fidaxomicin","w":["Intravenous vancomycin","Oral clindamycin","Oral ciprofloxacin"],"x":"Fidaxomicin is preferred for initial C. difficile infection because of lower recurrence, with oral vancomycin as an alternative. IV vancomycin does not reach the colon, and clindamycin is a major cause of C. difficile.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp935b15a7","lv":"ms3","d":"nitrofurantoin","sys":"infect","q":"Why is nitrofurantoin not appropriate for acute pyelonephritis?","a":"It does not reach adequate concentrations in kidney tissue","w":["It is inactive against Escherichia coli","It is only available as an intravenous form","It causes severe hyperkalemia in kidney infection"],"x":"Nitrofurantoin concentrates in the urine but achieves poor serum and renal tissue levels, so it is used only for cystitis. It is active against most E. coli.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe7d724e9","lv":"ms3","d":"metformin","sys":"endo","q":"A patient on metformin for many years develops numbness in the feet and a macrocytic anemia. Which deficiency should be evaluated?","a":"Vitamin B12 deficiency","w":["Iron deficiency","Vitamin D deficiency","Zinc deficiency"],"x":"Long-term metformin reduces vitamin B12 absorption, which can cause neuropathy and macrocytic anemia; periodic B12 checks are recommended. Iron deficiency causes a microcytic anemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdadb8ca7","lv":"ms3","d":"empagliflozin","sys":"endo","q":"A patient with type 2 diabetes and heart failure with reduced ejection fraction needs an additional glucose-lowering agent. Which drug has proven heart failure benefit?","a":"Empagliflozin","w":["Pioglitazone","Saxagliptin","Glipizide"],"x":"SGLT2 inhibitors such as empagliflozin reduce heart failure hospitalization and cardiovascular death in HFrEF. Pioglitazone causes fluid retention and can worsen heart failure, and saxagliptin was associated with increased heart failure hospitalization.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb8ed9d6b","lv":"ms3","d":"semaglutide","sys":"endo","q":"Which history is a contraindication to semaglutide?","a":"Family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma","w":["History of hypothyroidism on levothyroxine","History of type 2 diabetes with obesity","History of well-controlled hypertension"],"x":"GLP-1 receptor agonists carry a boxed warning for thyroid C-cell tumors and are contraindicated with a personal or family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma or MEN2. Hypothyroidism is not a contraindication.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp646fe3a4","lv":"ms3","d":"glyburide","sys":"endo","q":"An older adult with chronic kidney disease has type 2 diabetes. Why is glyburide a poor choice?","a":"It has active metabolites that cause prolonged hypoglycemia","w":["It causes lactic acidosis in kidney disease","It causes fluid retention and heart failure","It causes genital yeast infections"],"x":"Glyburide is long acting with renally cleared active metabolites, so hypoglycemia can be severe and prolonged in older adults and in CKD. Lactic acidosis is a metformin concern, and fluid retention is a thiazolidinedione effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp81a63324","lv":"ms3","d":"pioglitazone","sys":"endo","q":"In which patient with type 2 diabetes is pioglitazone contraindicated?","a":"A patient with NYHA class III heart failure","w":["A patient with nonalcoholic fatty liver disease","A patient with mild osteoarthritis","A patient with insulin resistance and obesity"],"x":"Thiazolidinediones cause fluid retention and carry a boxed warning against use in symptomatic heart failure. Pioglitazone may actually benefit fatty liver disease.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc2db2235","lv":"ms3","d":"insulin-glargine","sys":"endo","q":"A patient is starting insulin glargine as basal insulin. Which counseling point is correct?","a":"Do not mix it in the same syringe with other insulins","w":["Inject it immediately before each meal","Expect a sharp peak in action after injection","Shake the vial vigorously before each injection"],"x":"Insulin glargine is a long-acting, relatively peakless basal insulin with an acidic formulation, so it should not be mixed with other insulins. Mealtime coverage is provided by rapid-acting insulin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp59df214c","lv":"ms3","d":"levothyroxine","sys":"endo","q":"A patient on a stable levothyroxine dose has a rising TSH after starting a new supplement. Which supplement most likely caused this?","a":"Calcium carbonate taken at the same time","w":["Vitamin C taken with breakfast","A daily probiotic capsule","A melatonin tablet at bedtime"],"x":"Calcium, iron, and antacids bind levothyroxine in the gut and reduce absorption, so doses should be separated by several hours. Vitamin C does not decrease absorption.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpff152107","lv":"ms3","d":"propylthiouracil","sys":"endo","q":"A woman in the first trimester of pregnancy has Graves disease requiring antithyroid medication. Which drug is generally preferred during this period?","a":"Propylthiouracil","w":["Methimazole","Radioactive iodine","Liothyronine"],"x":"Methimazole is linked to embryopathy in the first trimester, so propylthiouracil is preferred then, with switching to methimazole often considered afterward because of PTU hepatotoxicity. Radioactive iodine is contraindicated in pregnancy.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp275600f4","lv":"ms3","d":"methimazole","sys":"endo","q":"A patient taking methimazole for hyperthyroidism should be taught to report which symptoms promptly?","a":"Fever and sore throat","w":["Weight gain and constipation","Increased appetite and warmth","Dry skin and hair thinning"],"x":"Methimazole can cause agranulocytosis, which presents with fever and sore throat and requires a prompt CBC. Weight gain and dry skin suggest hypothyroidism from overtreatment, which is less urgent.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb65924bb","lv":"ms3","d":"hydrocortisone","sys":"endo","q":"A patient with known primary adrenal insufficiency presents with vomiting, hypotension, and hyponatremia after a viral illness. Which medication is most appropriate immediately?","a":"IV hydrocortisone","w":["Oral fludrocortisone","IV levothyroxine","Subcutaneous octreotide"],"x":"Adrenal crisis requires parenteral hydrocortisone with IV fluids; at stress doses hydrocortisone also provides mineralocorticoid activity. Oral fludrocortisone acts too slowly and does not replace cortisol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdcfb2cce","lv":"ms3","d":"ondansetron","sys":"gi","q":"A patient with a prolonged baseline QTc needs an antiemetic. Why should ondansetron be used with caution?","a":"It can prolong the QT interval","w":["It causes tardive dyskinesia","It causes severe hypertension","It causes anticholinergic delirium"],"x":"Ondansetron, a 5-HT3 antagonist, can prolong the QT interval, especially with electrolyte abnormalities or other QT-prolonging drugs. Tardive dyskinesia is linked to metoclopramide.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp42f117b3","lv":"ms3","d":"metoclopramide","sys":"gi","q":"A patient with diabetic gastroparesis has taken metoclopramide for many months and develops involuntary lip smacking and tongue movements. What is the most likely diagnosis?","a":"Tardive dyskinesia","w":["Serotonin syndrome","Acute dystonia","Hepatic encephalopathy"],"x":"Metoclopramide blocks dopamine D2 receptors and carries a boxed warning for tardive dyskinesia, with risk rising with duration and cumulative exposure. Acute dystonia occurs within hours to days of starting therapy.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2f8526e6","lv":"ms3","d":"omeprazole","sys":"gi","q":"A patient on long-term omeprazole develops muscle cramps and arrhythmia. Which electrolyte abnormality is a recognized cause?","a":"Hypomagnesemia","w":["Hyperkalemia","Hypernatremia","Hyperphosphatemia"],"x":"Prolonged PPI use can reduce intestinal magnesium absorption and cause hypomagnesemia, sometimes with secondary hypocalcemia or hypokalemia. Other long-term risks include C. difficile infection and B12 deficiency.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdcaff503","lv":"ms3","d":"lactulose","sys":"gi","q":"How does lactulose improve hepatic encephalopathy?","a":"It acidifies the colon, trapping ammonia as ammonium","w":["It inhibits ammonia production in the liver","It increases ammonia excretion in the kidneys","It blocks ammonia from crossing the blood-brain barrier"],"x":"Colonic bacteria break down lactulose into acids, converting ammonia to non-absorbable ammonium that is excreted in stool; it is titrated to a few soft bowel movements daily. Rifaximin is added to reduce ammonia-producing bacteria.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4795c08c","lv":"ms3","d":"loperamide","sys":"gi","q":"In which patient is loperamide inappropriate?","a":"A patient with fever and bloody diarrhea","w":["A patient with mild traveler's diarrhea without fever","A patient with chronic diarrhea from irritable bowel syndrome","A patient with watery diarrhea after a viral illness"],"x":"Antimotility agents can worsen invasive bacterial infections such as Shigella or Shiga toxin-producing E. coli and can precipitate toxic megacolon. Loperamide is reasonable for nonbloody, afebrile diarrhea.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb7db88db","lv":"ms3","d":"mesalamine","sys":"gi","q":"A patient with mild-to-moderate ulcerative colitis is started on mesalamine. Which monitoring is recommended?","a":"Periodic renal function tests","w":["Monthly pulmonary function tests","Weekly absolute neutrophil counts","Periodic serum drug levels"],"x":"Mesalamine can rarely cause interstitial nephritis, so renal function is checked before and periodically during therapy. Weekly neutrophil monitoring is specific to clozapine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3bb5c077","lv":"ms3","d":"sulfasalazine","sys":"gi","q":"A man with ulcerative colitis on sulfasalazine is concerned about fertility. Which adverse effect explains this concern?","a":"Reversible oligospermia","w":["Permanent testicular atrophy","Retrograde ejaculation","Hyperprolactinemia"],"x":"The sulfapyridine component of sulfasalazine can cause reversible oligospermia. It can also cause folate deficiency and orange-yellow discoloration of urine and skin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd44d5377","lv":"ms3","d":"misoprostol","sys":"gi","q":"Misoprostol is considered to prevent NSAID-induced gastric ulcers. Which patient should not receive it for this purpose?","a":"A patient who is pregnant","w":["A patient with osteoarthritis","A patient older than sixty-five","A patient with a prior gastric ulcer"],"x":"Misoprostol is a prostaglandin E1 analog that stimulates uterine contractions and carries a boxed warning against use in pregnancy for ulcer prevention. Older age and prior ulcer are indications for gastroprotection.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp45eb9634","lv":"ms3","d":"sucralfate","sys":"gi","q":"How does sucralfate protect against peptic ulcer injury?","a":"It forms a protective barrier over the ulcer in acid","w":["It blocks the proton pump in parietal cells","It blocks histamine-2 receptors on parietal cells","It neutralizes gastric acid as a strong base"],"x":"Sucralfate polymerizes in an acidic environment and binds to exposed ulcer protein, forming a barrier against acid and pepsin. Because it binds other drugs, they should be taken at separate times.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpaee57273","lv":"ms3","d":"ursodiol","sys":"gi","q":"A middle-aged woman has fatigue, pruritus, an elevated alkaline phosphatase, and positive antimitochondrial antibodies. Which medication is first-line?","a":"Ursodiol","w":["Cholestyramine","Prednisone","Azathioprine"],"x":"Primary biliary cholangitis is treated first-line with ursodiol, which slows disease progression. Cholestyramine may relieve pruritus but does not modify disease.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5212769a","lv":"ms3","d":"magnesium-sulfate","sys":"ob","q":"A patient receiving magnesium sulfate for preeclampsia with severe features is being monitored for toxicity. Which finding is typically the earliest sign?","a":"Loss of deep tendon reflexes","w":["Respiratory arrest","Cardiac arrest","Seizure activity"],"x":"Loss of patellar reflexes appears at lower magnesium levels than respiratory depression or cardiac arrest, so reflexes are checked regularly. Seizures are what magnesium prevents.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf235a911","lv":"ms3","d":"calcium-gluconate","sys":"ob","q":"A patient on magnesium sulfate for preeclampsia has absent reflexes and a slowing respiratory rate. Which medication is the antidote?","a":"Calcium gluconate","w":["Naloxone","Flumazenil","Protamine"],"x":"IV calcium gluconate antagonizes magnesium's effects at the neuromuscular junction and myocardium. Naloxone reverses opioids, not magnesium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp22cb27fa","lv":"ms3","d":"oxytocin","sys":"ob","q":"A patient has heavy bleeding after vaginal delivery and a boggy uterus. Which uterotonic is first-line?","a":"Oxytocin","w":["Methylergonovine","Carboprost","Terbutaline"],"x":"Oxytocin is the first-line uterotonic for postpartum hemorrhage from uterine atony, alongside uterine massage. Terbutaline is a tocolytic that relaxes the uterus and would worsen bleeding.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdbe438e8","lv":"ms3","d":"methylergonovine","sys":"ob","q":"A patient with postpartum hemorrhage from uterine atony has preeclampsia. Which uterotonic is contraindicated?","a":"Methylergonovine","w":["Oxytocin","Carboprost","Misoprostol"],"x":"Methylergonovine is an ergot alkaloid that causes vasoconstriction and can provoke severe hypertension, so it is avoided in hypertension and preeclampsia. Carboprost is avoided in asthma instead.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp702184f9","lv":"ms3","d":"carboprost","sys":"ob","q":"Carboprost is considered for refractory postpartum hemorrhage. Which history is a contraindication?","a":"Asthma","w":["Chronic hypertension","Gestational diabetes","Hypothyroidism"],"x":"Carboprost is a prostaglandin F2-alpha analog that can cause bronchospasm, so it is avoided in asthma. Hypertension is the contraindication for methylergonovine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpedcc6543","lv":"ms3","d":"labetalol","sys":"ob","q":"A pregnant patient at term has a severe-range blood pressure on repeat measurement. Which medication is an appropriate first-line treatment?","a":"IV labetalol","w":["Oral lisinopril","IV nitroprusside","Oral losartan"],"x":"IV labetalol, IV hydralazine, and oral immediate-release nifedipine are first-line for acute severe hypertension in pregnancy. ACE inhibitors and ARBs are fetotoxic, and nitroprusside is reserved for refractory cases.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpce9d4ef9","lv":"ms3","d":"nifedipine","sys":"ob","q":"A pregnant patient has severe-range hypertension, and IV access cannot be obtained. Which medication is an appropriate first-line option?","a":"Oral immediate-release nifedipine","w":["Oral methyldopa","Oral hydrochlorothiazide","Oral enalapril"],"x":"ACOG lists oral immediate-release nifedipine as a first-line agent for acute severe hypertension in pregnancy, which is useful without IV access. Methyldopa is used for chronic hypertension but acts too slowly for urgent control.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1c1c94b8","lv":"ms3","d":"rho-d-immune-globulin","sys":"ob","q":"In which situation is Rh(D) immune globulin indicated?","a":"Rh-negative, unsensitized mother after delivering an Rh-positive newborn","w":["Rh-positive mother after delivering an Rh-negative newborn","Rh-negative mother already sensitized with anti-D antibodies","Rh-positive mother with a positive antibody screen"],"x":"Rh(D) immune globulin prevents alloimmunization in Rh-negative women who are not already sensitized after exposure to Rh-positive fetal blood. It does not help once anti-D antibodies have formed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd6009368","lv":"ms3","d":"betamethasone","sys":"ob","q":"A patient in preterm labor is given IM betamethasone. What is the main purpose?","a":"To accelerate fetal lung maturity","w":["To stop uterine contractions","To prevent maternal seizures","To prevent neonatal group B strep infection"],"x":"Antenatal corticosteroids speed fetal lung maturation and reduce respiratory distress syndrome, intraventricular hemorrhage, and neonatal death. Tocolytics stop contractions, and magnesium provides neuroprotection or seizure prophylaxis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb0751c44","lv":"ms3","d":"folic-acid","sys":"ob","q":"A woman planning pregnancy asks about folic acid. Which statement is most accurate?","a":"It should begin before conception to reduce neural tube defects","w":["It should begin in the third trimester to prevent anemia","It is only needed after a positive pregnancy test","It mainly prevents congenital heart defects"],"x":"Neural tube closure occurs early, often before pregnancy is recognized, so folic acid supplementation should begin before conception. Starting late misses the critical window.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp58c3fed3","lv":"ms3","d":"methotrexate","sys":"immune","q":"A patient with rheumatoid arthritis is starting weekly methotrexate. Which supplement is given to reduce adverse effects?","a":"Folic acid","w":["Vitamin D","Vitamin B12","Iron"],"x":"Folic acid reduces methotrexate adverse effects such as mouth sores, GI upset, and liver enzyme elevation without reducing efficacy in RA. Liver tests and blood counts are also monitored.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb57a3e5c","lv":"ms3","d":"adalimumab","sys":"immune","q":"Before starting adalimumab for rheumatoid arthritis, which test is most important?","a":"Screening for latent tuberculosis","w":["Serum uric acid level","Thyroid ultrasound","Bone density scan"],"x":"TNF-alpha inhibitors raise the risk of reactivating latent tuberculosis, so screening with an IGRA or TST is required before therapy. Hepatitis B screening is also recommended.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp604a93dd","lv":"ms3","d":"infliximab","sys":"immune","q":"A patient with Crohn disease is being considered for infliximab. Which comorbidity is a reason for caution?","a":"Moderate-to-severe heart failure","w":["Well-controlled hypothyroidism","Mild osteoarthritis","Iron deficiency anemia"],"x":"TNF inhibitors such as infliximab can worsen moderate-to-severe heart failure, and the label warns against higher doses in this setting. The other conditions do not limit its use.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp68551ccd","lv":"ms3","d":"hydroxychloroquine","sys":"immune","q":"A patient with systemic lupus erythematosus is starting long-term hydroxychloroquine. Which monitoring is recommended?","a":"Baseline and periodic retinal eye examinations","w":["Monthly pulmonary function tests","Weekly absolute neutrophil counts","Periodic bone marrow biopsy"],"x":"Hydroxychloroquine can cause irreversible retinopathy, so a baseline exam and annual screening after the early years of therapy are recommended. Weekly neutrophil counts apply to clozapine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6fa88ac0","lv":"ms3","d":"azathioprine","sys":"immune","q":"Before starting azathioprine, which test helps identify patients at high risk for severe myelosuppression?","a":"TPMT enzyme activity or genotype","w":["HLA-B*1502 genotype","G6PD enzyme activity","CYP2C19 genotype"],"x":"Thiopurine methyltransferase metabolizes azathioprine, and low or absent activity leads to toxic metabolite buildup and severe bone marrow suppression. HLA-B*1502 testing relates to carbamazepine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb7c25422","lv":"ms3","d":"tacrolimus","sys":"immune","q":"A kidney transplant recipient on tacrolimus has a rising creatinine and a fine hand tremor. Which adverse effect profile best explains this?","a":"Calcineurin inhibitor nephrotoxicity and neurotoxicity","w":["Antimetabolite bone marrow suppression","mTOR inhibitor impaired wound healing","Corticosteroid-induced myopathy"],"x":"Tacrolimus inhibits calcineurin and commonly causes nephrotoxicity, tremor, hyperkalemia, and hyperglycemia, so levels are monitored. Bone marrow suppression is typical of azathioprine and mycophenolate.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1abcf195","lv":"ms3","d":"cyclosporine","sys":"immune","q":"Which adverse effects help distinguish cyclosporine from tacrolimus?","a":"Gingival hyperplasia and hirsutism","w":["Nephrotoxicity and hyperkalemia","Tremor and headache","Hypertension and hyperglycemia"],"x":"Both calcineurin inhibitors cause nephrotoxicity, hypertension, and tremor, but gingival hyperplasia and hirsutism are characteristic of cyclosporine. Tacrolimus is more likely to cause hyperglycemia and neurotoxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7eef41df","lv":"ms3","d":"mycophenolate","sys":"immune","q":"A woman of reproductive age with lupus nephritis is starting mycophenolate. Which counseling point is most important?","a":"It causes birth defects and requires effective contraception","w":["It causes hearing loss and needs audiometry","It causes kidney stones and needs extra fluids","It causes red-orange urine that is harmless"],"x":"Mycophenolate carries a boxed warning for embryofetal toxicity, including miscarriage and congenital malformations, and requires reliable contraception under a REMS program. The other effects are not characteristic.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp30cdbbcc","lv":"ms3","d":"epinephrine-auto-injector","sys":"immune","q":"A patient with a severe peanut allergy is prescribed an epinephrine auto-injector. Which counseling point is correct?","a":"Inject into the outer thigh, then seek emergency care","w":["Inject into the upper arm, then rest at home if improved","Take an antihistamine first, then inject only if fainting","Inject into the abdomen, then repeat only after several hours"],"x":"The auto-injector is given into the anterolateral thigh, through clothing if needed, and the patient should still seek emergency care because symptoms can recur. Antihistamines do not treat airway or circulatory compromise and should not delay epinephrine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbcb97939","lv":"ms3","d":"rituximab","sys":"immune","q":"Before starting rituximab, which screening test is required because of a boxed warning?","a":"Hepatitis B surface antigen and core antibody","w":["Serum uric acid level","Thyroid peroxidase antibodies","Serum ceruloplasmin level"],"x":"Rituximab depletes CD20 B cells and can reactivate hepatitis B, sometimes causing fulminant hepatitis and death, so HBV screening is required. Infusion reactions and PML are other boxed warnings.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpba9525b8","lv":"ms3","d":"doxorubicin","sys":"onc","q":"A patient is about to begin doxorubicin for breast cancer. Which baseline test is most important?","a":"Assessment of left ventricular ejection fraction","w":["Pulmonary function tests with DLCO","Audiometry for high-frequency hearing","Urinalysis for microscopic hematuria"],"x":"Doxorubicin causes cumulative dose-related cardiomyopathy, so LVEF is assessed at baseline and during therapy; dexrazoxane can reduce cardiotoxicity. DLCO monitoring relates to bleomycin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc70bdd55","lv":"ms3","d":"cisplatin","sys":"onc","q":"A patient receiving cisplatin needs aggressive IV hydration. Which toxicity is this intended to prevent?","a":"Nephrotoxicity","w":["Hemorrhagic cystitis","Pulmonary fibrosis","Cardiomyopathy"],"x":"Cisplatin damages renal tubules, and pre- and post-hydration with saline reduces this risk; it also causes ototoxicity, neuropathy, and hypomagnesemia. Hemorrhagic cystitis is prevented with mesna for cyclophosphamide or ifosfamide.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp51975226","lv":"ms3","d":"bleomycin","sys":"onc","q":"A patient receiving bleomycin for Hodgkin lymphoma develops a dry cough and dyspnea. Which complication is most likely?","a":"Pulmonary fibrosis","w":["Dilated cardiomyopathy","Hemorrhagic cystitis","Peripheral neuropathy"],"x":"Bleomycin causes dose-related pulmonary toxicity that can progress to fibrosis, so pulmonary function including DLCO is monitored. High oxygen concentrations can worsen the injury.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpca9e4431","lv":"ms3","d":"vincristine","sys":"onc","q":"A patient receiving vincristine reports numbness in the fingers and severe constipation. What is the most likely cause?","a":"Vincristine neurotoxicity","w":["Vincristine hepatotoxicity","Vincristine-induced hypothyroidism","Vincristine bone marrow failure"],"x":"Vincristine disrupts microtubules in neurons, causing peripheral and autonomic neuropathy, including constipation and ileus. It is relatively marrow sparing compared with vinblastine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp642128ba","lv":"ms3","d":"cyclophosphamide","sys":"onc","q":"A patient develops painful gross hematuria during high-dose cyclophosphamide therapy. Which agent helps prevent this complication?","a":"Mesna","w":["Leucovorin","Dexrazoxane","Amifostine"],"x":"The metabolite acrolein damages the bladder, and mesna binds acrolein in the urine to prevent hemorrhagic cystitis, along with hydration. Leucovorin rescues normal cells from methotrexate.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp324e0ef9","lv":"ms3","d":"leucovorin","sys":"onc","q":"Why is leucovorin given after high-dose methotrexate?","a":"To bypass dihydrofolate reductase and rescue normal cells","w":["To enhance methotrexate binding to tumor cells","To prevent hemorrhagic cystitis from methotrexate","To increase renal reabsorption of methotrexate"],"x":"Leucovorin is a reduced folate that does not need dihydrofolate reductase, so it replenishes folate in normal cells and limits myelosuppression and mucositis. Mesna prevents hemorrhagic cystitis from cyclophosphamide.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb0d52026","lv":"ms3","d":"tamoxifen","sys":"onc","q":"A postmenopausal woman taking tamoxifen for breast cancer should be taught to report which symptom promptly?","a":"Abnormal vaginal bleeding","w":["Mild hot flashes","Increased appetite","Brief morning stiffness"],"x":"Tamoxifen has estrogen agonist activity on the endometrium and raises the risk of endometrial hyperplasia and cancer. Hot flashes are a common, expected side effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb0b461aa","lv":"ms3","d":"anastrozole","sys":"onc","q":"A postmenopausal woman starts anastrozole for hormone receptor-positive breast cancer. Which monitoring is recommended?","a":"Bone mineral density","w":["Endometrial thickness by ultrasound","Serum potassium level","Pulmonary function tests"],"x":"Aromatase inhibitors lower estrogen levels and accelerate bone loss, increasing fracture risk, so bone density is monitored. Endometrial cancer risk is a tamoxifen concern, not an aromatase inhibitor concern.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2e9b09c1","lv":"ms3","d":"imatinib","sys":"onc","q":"What is the mechanism of imatinib in chronic myeloid leukemia?","a":"Inhibition of the BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase","w":["Inhibition of the HER2 receptor","Blockade of the CD20 antigen","Inhibition of the proteasome"],"x":"Imatinib blocks the constitutively active BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase produced by the Philadelphia chromosome. HER2 is targeted by trastuzumab, CD20 by rituximab, and the proteasome by bortezomib.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2e5e5010","lv":"ms3","d":"pembrolizumab","sys":"onc","q":"A patient receiving pembrolizumab develops severe diarrhea with abdominal pain several weeks into therapy, and infection has been excluded. What is the most likely cause?","a":"Immune-mediated colitis","w":["Direct drug-induced mucositis","Neutropenic enterocolitis","Tumor lysis syndrome"],"x":"PD-1 inhibitors release immune checkpoints and can cause immune-related adverse events such as colitis, pneumonitis, hepatitis, and endocrinopathies, which are typically treated with corticosteroids. Pembrolizumab is not a cytotoxic drug and rarely causes neutropenia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcc6575fc","lv":"ms3","d":"isotretinoin","sys":"skin","q":"A woman with severe nodular acne is starting isotretinoin. Which requirement is most important?","a":"Two forms of contraception and pregnancy testing under iPLEDGE","w":["Monthly audiometry and slit-lamp examinations","Weekly complete blood counts and liver biopsies","A baseline chest X-ray and tuberculosis screening"],"x":"Isotretinoin is a potent teratogen, so the iPLEDGE REMS program requires two forms of contraception and repeated pregnancy tests. Lipids and liver enzymes are also monitored.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp40634066","lv":"ms3","d":"timolol-ophthalmic","sys":"skin","q":"A patient with open-angle glaucoma also has asthma. Why should timolol eye drops be avoided?","a":"Systemic absorption can cause bronchospasm","w":["It causes permanent darkening of the iris","It increases intraocular pressure in asthma","It interacts with inhaled corticosteroids"],"x":"Ophthalmic timolol is a nonselective beta-blocker that can be absorbed systemically and cause bronchospasm and bradycardia. Iris darkening is a prostaglandin analog effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8456e85c","lv":"ms3","d":"latanoprost","sys":"skin","q":"A patient starting latanoprost for open-angle glaucoma should be counseled about which effect?","a":"Possible permanent darkening of iris color","w":["Possible bronchospasm with first use","Possible miosis and dim vision at night","Possible metallic taste and kidney stones"],"x":"Prostaglandin analogs can increase iris pigmentation permanently and lengthen and darken eyelashes. Miosis is associated with pilocarpine, and kidney stones with carbonic anhydrase inhibitors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5636960c","lv":"ms3","d":"pilocarpine-ophthalmic","sys":"skin","q":"How does pilocarpine help in acute angle-closure glaucoma?","a":"Muscarinic stimulation constricts the pupil and opens the angle","w":["Beta-receptor blockade decreases aqueous production","Prostaglandin agonism increases uveoscleral outflow","Carbonic anhydrase inhibition reduces aqueous formation"],"x":"Pilocarpine contracts the iris sphincter, pulling the iris away from the trabecular meshwork and improving outflow. Beta blockade describes timolol, and prostaglandin agonism describes latanoprost.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1854749a","lv":"ms3","d":"permethrin","sys":"skin","q":"An adult has intensely itchy papules in the finger webs and burrows on the wrists. Which treatment is first-line?","a":"Topical permethrin","w":["Topical clotrimazole","Topical mupirocin","Topical hydrocortisone alone"],"x":"Scabies is treated first-line with topical permethrin applied from the neck down, and close contacts are treated as well. Clotrimazole treats fungal infections.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp702c0e8e","lv":"ms3","d":"clobetasol","sys":"skin","q":"Clobetasol, a super-high-potency topical corticosteroid, is prescribed for plaque psoriasis on the elbows. Which counseling point is correct?","a":"Avoid applying it to the face, groin, or armpits","w":["Apply it under occlusion over large areas for months","Use it as a daily moisturizer on all skin","Apply it to the eyelids for faster results"],"x":"Super-high-potency steroids can cause skin atrophy, striae, and systemic absorption, especially on thin skin such as the face and skin folds. Use is limited to short courses on thick plaques.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc6b3c1fc","lv":"ms3","d":"tretinoin-topical","sys":"skin","q":"A patient is starting topical tretinoin for acne. Which counseling point is accurate?","a":"Use sun protection because skin becomes photosensitive","w":["Apply it to wet skin immediately after washing","Expect immediate clearing within days","Combine it with abrasive scrubs for better effect"],"x":"Topical retinoids increase sun sensitivity and can cause irritation and an early flare; they are usually applied at night to dry skin. Improvement takes weeks, and harsh scrubs worsen irritation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfcc60f1c","lv":"ms3","d":"mupirocin","sys":"skin","q":"A child has a few localized honey-crusted lesions around the nose consistent with impetigo. Which treatment is appropriate?","a":"Topical mupirocin","w":["Topical clotrimazole","Oral fluconazole","Topical permethrin"],"x":"Limited impetigo is treated with a topical antibiotic such as mupirocin or retapamulin; oral antibiotics are used for extensive disease. Clotrimazole and fluconazole treat fungal infections.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3ae15536","lv":"ms3","d":"brimonidine","sys":"skin","q":"Brimonidine eye drops are used to lower intraocular pressure. In which patient population is brimonidine contraindicated?","a":"Infants and very young children","w":["Older adults with cataracts","Adults with type 2 diabetes","Adults with controlled hypertension"],"x":"Brimonidine is an alpha-2 agonist that can cross the immature blood-brain barrier and cause CNS depression, bradycardia, and apnea in infants. It is used routinely in adults.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5b9cd809","lv":"ms3","d":"tacrolimus-topical","sys":"skin","q":"A patient with atopic dermatitis on the eyelids needs ongoing treatment while avoiding steroid-induced skin atrophy. Which agent is appropriate?","a":"Topical tacrolimus","w":["Topical clobetasol","Topical fluorouracil","Topical podofilox"],"x":"Topical calcineurin inhibitors such as tacrolimus treat atopic dermatitis without causing skin atrophy, making them useful on the face and eyelids; burning on application is common. Super-high-potency steroids such as clobetasol should be avoided on the eyelids.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp57ab5459","lv":"paramedic","d":"amiodarone","sys":"c:acls","q":"A patient remains in ventricular fibrillation after several shocks and a dose of epinephrine. Which antiarrhythmic is a first-line choice under current ACLS guidelines?","a":"Amiodarone","w":["Adenosine","Diltiazem","Atropine"],"x":"Amiodarone is a recommended antiarrhythmic for shock-refractory VF or pulseless VT. Adenosine treats regular narrow-complex SVT and has no role in VF.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0d3ca7de","lv":"paramedic","d":"amiodarone","sys":"c:acls","q":"During an amiodarone infusion for stable monomorphic ventricular tachycardia, which adverse effects should the paramedic watch for most closely?","a":"Hypotension and bradycardia","w":["Hypertension and tachycardia","Hyperglycemia and polyuria","Bronchospasm and wheezing"],"x":"IV amiodarone commonly causes hypotension and can slow the heart rate or cause AV block. It does not typically raise blood pressure or heart rate.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf1694e2e","lv":"paramedic","d":"adenosine","sys":"c:acls","q":"How is adenosine administered for stable narrow-complex supraventricular tachycardia?","a":"Rapid IV push followed immediately by a saline flush","w":["Slow IV push over several minutes","Continuous IV infusion titrated to heart rate","Intramuscular injection into the deltoid"],"x":"Adenosine has a half-life of only seconds, so it must be pushed rapidly into a proximal IV and flushed right away to reach the heart. A slow push lets it be broken down before it acts.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa021125f","lv":"paramedic","d":"lidocaine","sys":"c:acls","q":"A patient receiving lidocaine after conversion from ventricular tachycardia becomes confused, has slurred speech and muscle twitching. What should the paramedic recognize?","a":"Signs of lidocaine toxicity","w":["Expected therapeutic response","Signs of hypoglycemia from lidocaine","An allergic reaction to the IV fluid"],"x":"Lidocaine toxicity shows up first as central nervous system effects such as confusion, slurred speech, twitching and eventually seizures. These are not expected effects and should be reported promptly.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp715ddabd","lv":"paramedic","d":"atropine","sys":"c:acls","q":"How does atropine increase the heart rate in symptomatic sinus bradycardia?","a":"It blocks vagal (muscarinic) effects on the SA and AV nodes","w":["It directly stimulates beta-1 receptors in the heart","It blocks calcium channels in the AV node","It increases sodium entry into Purkinje fibers"],"x":"Atropine is an anticholinergic that removes vagal braking on the SA and AV nodes. It does not stimulate beta receptors the way epinephrine or dopamine does.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp19b9f946","lv":"paramedic","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:acls","q":"What is the main reason epinephrine is given during cardiac arrest?","a":"Alpha-1 vasoconstriction raises coronary and cerebral perfusion pressure","w":["Beta-2 bronchodilation improves oxygen delivery","It directly converts VF back to a sinus rhythm","Vagal blockade speeds conduction through the AV node"],"x":"During CPR, epinephrine's alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction increases perfusion pressure to the heart and brain. It does not defibrillate the heart; electricity does that.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd059bcba","lv":"paramedic","d":"diltiazem","sys":"c:acls","q":"In which patient with a rapid tachycardia is IV diltiazem contraindicated?","a":"Atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White pre-excitation","w":["Stable atrial flutter with a rapid ventricular rate","Atrial fibrillation with rapid rate and normal blood pressure","Narrow-complex SVT that recurred after adenosine"],"x":"Blocking the AV node with a calcium channel blocker in pre-excited atrial fibrillation can speed conduction down the accessory pathway and trigger VF. Rate control with diltiazem is appropriate for ordinary stable atrial fibrillation or flutter.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa76df6d4","lv":"paramedic","d":"procainamide","sys":"c:acls","q":"While procainamide is infusing for stable wide-complex tachycardia, which ECG change should be reported immediately?","a":"Widening of the QRS complex or a prolonged QT interval","w":["Shortening of the PR interval","Peaked T waves in the precordial leads","Return of normal P waves before each QRS"],"x":"Procainamide slows conduction and can widen the QRS and prolong the QT, raising the risk of torsades de pointes. A return of normal P waves would suggest conversion, not toxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdf541d5f","lv":"paramedic","d":"calcium-chloride","sys":"c:acls","q":"In which cardiac arrest is calcium chloride specifically indicated rather than given routinely?","a":"Arrest in a dialysis patient with suspected hyperkalemia","w":["Witnessed VF arrest in a healthy runner","Asystole after a prolonged drowning","PEA arrest from a tension pneumothorax"],"x":"Calcium is not a routine arrest drug but is given when hyperkalemia, hypocalcemia or calcium channel blocker toxicity is suspected, as in a missed-dialysis patient. A tension pneumothorax needs decompression, not calcium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp77705d61","lv":"paramedic","d":"sodium-bicarbonate","sys":"c:acls","q":"Why should the IV line be flushed well between sodium bicarbonate and other resuscitation drugs?","a":"Bicarbonate can inactivate catecholamines and precipitate with calcium","w":["Bicarbonate makes amiodarone more toxic in the line","Bicarbonate converts epinephrine into norepinephrine","Bicarbonate causes the IV catheter to clot off quickly"],"x":"Sodium bicarbonate inactivates catecholamines such as epinephrine and forms a precipitate with calcium salts if they mix in the same line. Flushing between drugs prevents this.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpad88447c","lv":"paramedic","d":"aspirin","sys":"c:acs","q":"Why is aspirin chewed rather than swallowed whole for suspected acute coronary syndrome?","a":"Chewing speeds absorption and onset of the antiplatelet effect","w":["Chewing prevents aspirin from irritating the stomach","Chewing lowers the chance of an allergic reaction","Chewing lets aspirin dissolve the fibrin clot faster"],"x":"Chewing a non-enteric-coated tablet allows faster absorption through the oral and gastric mucosa, so platelet inhibition starts sooner. Aspirin does not dissolve existing fibrin clots.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa66fc520","lv":"paramedic","d":"aspirin","sys":"c:acs","q":"A patient with chest pain suggestive of ACS reports several medical conditions. Which finding is a contraindication to giving aspirin?","a":"Known aspirin allergy with prior anaphylaxis","w":["Daily use of a cholesterol-lowering statin","A history of type 2 diabetes","Taking a beta blocker for hypertension"],"x":"A true aspirin allergy, especially anaphylaxis, is a contraindication. Diabetes, statins and beta blockers are common in cardiac patients and do not prevent aspirin use.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp08842029","lv":"paramedic","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:acs","q":"A patient with chest pain asks for nitroglycerin. Which history is the most important reason not to give it?","a":"Took sildenafil for erectile dysfunction last night","w":["Takes a daily low-dose aspirin","Has a history of mild asthma","Takes metformin for diabetes"],"x":"Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors such as sildenafil combined with nitrates can cause severe, refractory hypotension. Aspirin, asthma and metformin do not interact dangerously with nitroglycerin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp44dd2f55","lv":"paramedic","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:acs","q":"A patient has an inferior STEMI with ST elevation in right-sided lead V4R. Why is nitroglycerin used with great caution?","a":"The right ventricle is preload dependent and profound hypotension can occur","w":["Nitroglycerin causes dangerous bradycardia in inferior infarcts","Nitroglycerin increases infarct size in the right ventricle","Nitroglycerin triggers ventricular fibrillation in right ventricular infarcts"],"x":"Right ventricular infarction makes cardiac output depend on venous return, and nitroglycerin's venodilation can drop preload and blood pressure sharply. The concern is hypotension, not a direct arrhythmia effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc366b612","lv":"paramedic","d":"morphine","sys":"c:acs","q":"After IV morphine for ischemic chest pain, which adverse effects are most important to monitor?","a":"Respiratory depression and hypotension","w":["Hypertension and tachycardia","Hyperglycemia and thirst","Diarrhea and abdominal cramping"],"x":"Opioids depress the respiratory drive, and morphine's histamine release and venodilation can lower blood pressure. It tends to slow, not speed, the heart rate.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7c7eae5c","lv":"paramedic","d":"morphine","sys":"c:acs","q":"Besides relieving pain, what effect of morphine may benefit a patient with ACS?","a":"It reduces anxiety and modestly lowers preload through venodilation","w":["It dissolves the thrombus in the coronary artery","It increases myocardial contractility and output","It blocks platelet aggregation at the plaque"],"x":"Morphine eases pain and anxiety, which lowers sympathetic drive, and its venodilation slightly reduces preload. It has no thrombolytic or antiplatelet action.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa1138811","lv":"paramedic","d":"fentanyl","sys":"c:acs","q":"Which property of IV fentanyl makes it useful for titrating chest pain relief in the field?","a":"Rapid onset with a short duration of action","w":["Slow onset with a very long duration of action","Strong antiplatelet activity in the coronaries","Direct coronary vasodilation similar to nitrates"],"x":"IV fentanyl acts within minutes and wears off relatively quickly, so it can be given in small increments and reassessed. It does not affect platelets or dilate coronary arteries.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1667dd76","lv":"paramedic","d":"fentanyl","sys":"c:acs","q":"A patient becomes apneic with pinpoint pupils after receiving fentanyl for chest pain. After supporting ventilation, which medication reverses the opioid effect?","a":"Naloxone","w":["Flumazenil","Atropine","Glucagon"],"x":"Naloxone is a competitive opioid receptor antagonist that reverses fentanyl-induced respiratory depression. Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines, not opioids.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1e005d30","lv":"paramedic","d":"metoprolol","sys":"c:acs","q":"Which finding in a patient with ACS is a reason a beta blocker such as metoprolol should not be given early?","a":"Signs of acute heart failure or cardiogenic shock","w":["Persistent hypertension with tachycardia","Ongoing ischemic chest pain","Recent use of aspirin"],"x":"Beta blockers reduce contractility and heart rate, which can worsen heart failure or shock. Hypertension with tachycardia is a situation where a beta blocker may be useful.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdfb2fb73","lv":"paramedic","d":"metoprolol","sys":"c:acs","q":"How does metoprolol help the ischemic myocardium?","a":"Selective beta-1 blockade lowers heart rate and oxygen demand","w":["Beta-2 stimulation dilates the coronary arteries","Alpha-1 blockade lowers systemic afterload","Calcium channel blockade relieves coronary spasm"],"x":"Metoprolol is a cardioselective beta-1 blocker that slows the heart and reduces contractility, lowering myocardial oxygen demand. It does not act on calcium channels.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9b937f03","lv":"paramedic","d":"labetalol","sys":"c:htn","q":"Which receptor action explains how labetalol lowers blood pressure in a hypertensive emergency?","a":"Blockade of alpha-1 and nonselective beta receptors","w":["Selective blockade of beta-1 receptors only","Blockade of calcium channels in arterioles","Release of nitric oxide in venous smooth muscle"],"x":"Labetalol blocks alpha-1 receptors, causing vasodilation, and blocks beta receptors to prevent reflex tachycardia. Metoprolol, not labetalol, is beta-1 selective.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp72ca6b20","lv":"paramedic","d":"labetalol","sys":"c:htn","q":"In which patient with severe hypertension would labetalol be a poor choice?","a":"A patient wheezing from an acute asthma exacerbation","w":["A pregnant patient with severe preeclampsia","A stroke patient needing blood pressure control","A patient with hypertension and chest pain"],"x":"Labetalol's nonselective beta blockade can trigger bronchospasm, so it is avoided in active asthma. It is commonly used in preeclampsia and acute stroke.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpad41d60b","lv":"paramedic","d":"labetalol","sys":"c:htn","q":"After IV labetalol, which precaution is most appropriate when moving the patient?","a":"Keep the patient supine or reclined due to orthostatic hypotension","w":["Have the patient walk to the stretcher to assess gait","Sit the patient upright to improve venous return","Encourage rapid position changes to prevent clots"],"x":"Labetalol's alpha blockade can cause symptomatic orthostatic hypotension, so patients should stay lying down or reclined. Sitting or standing up quickly can cause fainting.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp869e5df7","lv":"paramedic","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:htn","q":"A patient with a hypertensive emergency has acute pulmonary edema. Why is nitroglycerin useful?","a":"It reduces preload and afterload, easing pulmonary congestion","w":["It increases contractility to raise cardiac output","It acts as a diuretic to remove excess fluid","It dilates the bronchi to relieve wheezing"],"x":"Nitroglycerin dilates veins (and arteries at higher levels), lowering preload and afterload so fluid moves out of the lungs. It is not a diuretic or a bronchodilator.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8024c345","lv":"paramedic","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"c:htn","q":"A patient who received sublingual nitroglycerin reports a throbbing headache and facial flushing. What do these findings most likely represent?","a":"Expected side effects of vasodilation","w":["Early signs of a hemorrhagic stroke","An allergic reaction to nitrates","Nitroglycerin toxicity requiring an antidote"],"x":"Headache and flushing are common, expected effects of nitrate vasodilation. They should still be reassessed, but they do not by themselves indicate a stroke or allergy.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfdc85c3e","lv":"paramedic","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"A patient remains hypotensive from septic shock despite fluid resuscitation. Which vasopressor is the recommended first choice?","a":"Norepinephrine","w":["Dopamine","Dobutamine","Isoproterenol"],"x":"Norepinephrine is the first-line vasopressor in septic shock. Dopamine causes more arrhythmias, and dobutamine is an inotrope that can lower blood pressure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp492d17a7","lv":"paramedic","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"Which receptor profile best describes norepinephrine?","a":"Mainly alpha-1 vasoconstriction with some beta-1 effect","w":["Mainly beta-2 vasodilation with some alpha effect","Pure beta-1 stimulation with no vascular effect","Dopamine receptor stimulation in renal vessels"],"x":"Norepinephrine is a potent alpha-1 agonist that raises vascular tone, with moderate beta-1 inotropic effect. It has little beta-2 activity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe1db953d","lv":"paramedic","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"The skin around a norepinephrine IV site becomes pale, cool and hard. Which antidote is infiltrated into the area to limit tissue injury?","a":"Phentolamine infiltrated locally","w":["Hyaluronidase infiltrated locally","Protamine infiltrated locally","Flumazenil infiltrated locally"],"x":"Norepinephrine extravasation causes intense local vasoconstriction and tissue necrosis, and phentolamine, an alpha blocker, reverses it. Hyaluronidase is used for some non-vasopressor extravasations, not norepinephrine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp88915b0e","lv":"paramedic","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"Why is it important to address volume status before or alongside norepinephrine in hypotensive patients?","a":"Vasopressors work poorly when intravascular volume is depleted","w":["Norepinephrine causes fluid overload unless given first","Fluids inactivate norepinephrine in the IV line","Norepinephrine is only effective in cardiogenic shock"],"x":"Constricting vessels around an empty circulation does little to restore perfusion and can worsen organ ischemia. Fluids do not inactivate norepinephrine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcffd2d9e","lv":"paramedic","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"Why can an epinephrine infusion help a patient with symptomatic bradycardia and hypotension that does not respond to atropine?","a":"Beta-1 effects raise rate and contractility while alpha-1 raises vascular tone","w":["Beta-2 effects slow the heart and raise blood pressure","It blocks vagal input to the SA node like atropine","It increases preload by causing fluid retention"],"x":"Epinephrine stimulates beta-1 receptors to increase heart rate and contractility and alpha-1 receptors to constrict vessels. It does not work by vagal blockade.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp56a1ed32","lv":"paramedic","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"While a patient in shock receives an epinephrine infusion, which adverse effects are most important to watch for?","a":"Tachyarrhythmias and increased myocardial oxygen demand","w":["Bradycardia and heart block","Hypoglycemia and seizures","Respiratory depression and miosis"],"x":"Epinephrine's beta-1 stimulation can cause tachycardia, arrhythmias and myocardial ischemia. It tends to raise blood glucose, not lower it.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp41ac361d","lv":"paramedic","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:shock","q":"In which type of shock is epinephrine the first-line drug?","a":"Anaphylactic shock","w":["Hemorrhagic shock","Obstructive shock from tension pneumothorax","Hypovolemic shock from vomiting"],"x":"Epinephrine reverses the vasodilation, capillary leak and bronchospasm of anaphylaxis. Hemorrhagic and hypovolemic shock need volume and bleeding control, and obstructive shock needs the obstruction relieved.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9cc81634","lv":"paramedic","d":"dopamine","sys":"c:shock","q":"Why is dopamine described as having dose-dependent effects?","a":"Higher infusion rates add alpha vasoconstriction to its beta-1 effects","w":["Higher infusion rates switch it to a pure vasodilator","Lower infusion rates cause the strongest vasoconstriction","Its effects are identical at every infusion rate"],"x":"At moderate rates dopamine mainly stimulates beta-1 receptors, and as the rate rises alpha-1 vasoconstriction becomes dominant. It does not become a vasodilator at high rates.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa1982928","lv":"paramedic","d":"dopamine","sys":"c:shock","q":"Compared with norepinephrine in septic shock, dopamine is associated with which problem?","a":"More tachyarrhythmias","w":["More extravasation injury","More bronchospasm","More hypoglycemia"],"x":"Studies comparing the two found dopamine caused more arrhythmias, which is one reason norepinephrine is preferred. Both can cause tissue injury if they extravasate.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2d645bb5","lv":"paramedic","d":"dopamine","sys":"c:shock","q":"A hypotensive patient who takes a monoamine oxidase inhibitor (MAOI) needs a vasopressor. Why is dopamine a particular concern?","a":"MAOIs can greatly exaggerate the pressor response to dopamine","w":["MAOIs block dopamine receptors and make it ineffective","MAOIs cause dopamine to precipitate in the IV line","MAOIs convert dopamine into a sedating metabolite"],"x":"MAOIs prevent breakdown of catecholamines, so dopamine can produce a severe hypertensive response and arrhythmias. The label calls for markedly reduced dosing in these patients.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3537e9b3","lv":"paramedic","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:airway","q":"How does nebulized albuterol relieve bronchospasm in an acute asthma attack?","a":"It stimulates beta-2 receptors to relax bronchial smooth muscle","w":["It blocks muscarinic receptors to reduce secretions","It reduces airway inflammation over several hours","It stabilizes mast cells to prevent histamine release"],"x":"Albuterol is a short-acting beta-2 agonist that rapidly relaxes airway smooth muscle. Blocking muscarinic receptors describes ipratropium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb63030a4","lv":"paramedic","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:airway","q":"After several back-to-back albuterol treatments, which side effects are expected and should be monitored?","a":"Tachycardia, tremor and low potassium","w":["Bradycardia, drowsiness and high potassium","Hypotension, flushing and loss of reflexes","Dry mouth, urinary retention and constipation"],"x":"Beta-2 stimulation causes tremor and tachycardia and shifts potassium into cells, lowering serum potassium. Loss of reflexes and flushing suggest magnesium effects, not albuterol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3472e6a4","lv":"paramedic","d":"ipratropium","sys":"c:airway","q":"Ipratropium is often added to albuterol for COPD or severe asthma. How does it work?","a":"It blocks muscarinic receptors, reducing vagally mediated bronchoconstriction","w":["It stimulates beta-2 receptors in the bronchial smooth muscle","It inhibits leukotrienes that cause airway swelling","It suppresses the immune response in the airway lining"],"x":"Ipratropium is an inhaled anticholinergic that blocks acetylcholine at airway muscarinic receptors. The beta-2 agonist in the combination is albuterol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7e6c4a85","lv":"paramedic","d":"methylprednisolone","sys":"c:airway","q":"A patient with diabetes receives IV methylprednisolone for a COPD exacerbation. Which effect should be anticipated and reported?","a":"Elevated blood glucose","w":["Low blood glucose","Low blood pressure","Slow heart rate"],"x":"Corticosteroids increase glucose production and insulin resistance, so blood glucose often rises, especially in people with diabetes. They do not typically cause hypoglycemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc4eab958","lv":"paramedic","d":"magnesium-sulfate","sys":"c:airway","q":"An adult with severe asthma is not improving after repeated albuterol and ipratropium. Why might IV magnesium sulfate be given?","a":"It relaxes bronchial smooth muscle by blocking calcium entry","w":["It thins mucus so it can be coughed up more easily","It rapidly reduces airway inflammation like a steroid","It increases respiratory drive in a tiring patient"],"x":"Magnesium interferes with calcium-mediated smooth muscle contraction, producing bronchodilation in severe asthma. It is not an anti-inflammatory or a respiratory stimulant.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb13aeab7","lv":"paramedic","d":"racemic-epinephrine","sys":"c:airway","q":"A child with croup and stridor at rest improves after nebulized racemic epinephrine. What is most important afterward?","a":"Continue observation for rebound stridor as the effect wears off","w":["Release the child once stridor at rest resolves","Give a second treatment to prevent rebound","Have the child lie flat to rest the airway"],"x":"Racemic epinephrine reduces subglottic swelling through vasoconstriction, but the effect is short and symptoms can return. Children need a period of observation after treatment.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4d014fad","lv":"paramedic","d":"levalbuterol","sys":"c:airway","q":"How is levalbuterol best described?","a":"The single active isomer of albuterol, a short-acting beta-2 agonist","w":["A long-acting beta-2 agonist for daily maintenance","An inhaled anticholinergic related to ipratropium","An inhaled corticosteroid used for acute attacks"],"x":"Levalbuterol is the R-isomer of albuterol and acts as a short-acting beta-2 agonist for acute bronchospasm. It is not a maintenance controller or an anticholinergic.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0a245f6a","lv":"paramedic","d":"terbutaline","sys":"c:airway","q":"A patient in severe bronchospasm cannot effectively use inhaled therapy. By which route can terbutaline be given?","a":"Subcutaneous injection","w":["Sublingual tablet","Endotracheal instillation","Transdermal patch"],"x":"Injectable terbutaline is given subcutaneously for bronchospasm when inhaled therapy is not effective. It is not given by sublingual, endotracheal or transdermal routes.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp215342ea","lv":"paramedic","d":"dexamethasone","sys":"c:airway","q":"How does dexamethasone help a child with croup?","a":"It reduces subglottic airway inflammation and swelling","w":["It produces immediate bronchodilation via beta-2","It thins secretions in the lower airways","It blocks histamine released from mast cells"],"x":"Dexamethasone is a long-acting corticosteroid that lowers inflammation and edema in the upper airway over several hours. It is not a bronchodilator or antihistamine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3c8c85e5","lv":"paramedic","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:airway","q":"IM epinephrine is being considered for life-threatening asthma that is not responding to nebulized treatment. Which patient warrants the greatest caution?","a":"An older adult with known coronary artery disease","w":["A teenager with a history of eczema","A young adult who uses an albuterol inhaler","A child with seasonal allergies"],"x":"Epinephrine raises heart rate, blood pressure and myocardial oxygen demand, which can provoke ischemia in patients with coronary disease. Eczema and allergies do not raise the cardiac risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpadc81d2d","lv":"paramedic","d":"etomidate","sys":"c:rsi","q":"Which effect of etomidate has raised concern when it is used to intubate patients with sepsis?","a":"Transient suppression of adrenal cortisol production","w":["Prolonged hypotension from histamine release","Severe bronchospasm on injection","Sustained rise in serum potassium"],"x":"Etomidate temporarily inhibits an enzyme needed for cortisol synthesis, which is a theoretical concern in septic shock. It causes little histamine release and does not raise potassium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7800b3a5","lv":"paramedic","d":"etomidate","sys":"c:rsi","q":"Why is etomidate often chosen as the induction agent for a hypotensive trauma patient?","a":"It has minimal effect on blood pressure and heart rate","w":["It provides strong, long-lasting analgesia","It reliably paralyzes the patient for intubation","It reverses hypotension by stimulating the heart"],"x":"Etomidate is relatively hemodynamically neutral, making it useful in unstable patients. It provides no analgesia or paralysis, so a neuromuscular blocker is still needed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp659f4ed4","lv":"paramedic","d":"ketamine","sys":"c:rsi","q":"Why is ketamine a useful induction agent for a patient in status asthmaticus who needs intubation?","a":"It has bronchodilating properties","w":["It reduces airway secretions","It causes no change in heart rate","It provides prolonged paralysis"],"x":"Ketamine's sympathomimetic effects produce bronchodilation, which can help in severe bronchospasm. It actually tends to increase secretions and heart rate.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa22db46d","lv":"paramedic","d":"rocuronium","sys":"c:rsi","q":"Rocuronium belongs to which class of medications?","a":"Nondepolarizing neuromuscular blocker","w":["Depolarizing neuromuscular blocker","Dissociative anesthetic","Benzodiazepine sedative"],"x":"Rocuronium competitively blocks acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction without depolarizing the muscle. Succinylcholine is the depolarizing agent.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp664e0e33","lv":"paramedic","d":"rocuronium","sys":"c:rsi","q":"Which agent can rapidly reverse neuromuscular blockade from rocuronium?","a":"Sugammadex","w":["Naloxone","Flumazenil","Dantrolene"],"x":"Sugammadex encapsulates rocuronium and vecuronium molecules, rapidly ending the blockade. Dantrolene treats malignant hyperthermia, not rocuronium paralysis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5ab58f49","lv":"paramedic","d":"succinylcholine","sys":"c:rsi","q":"Which history most strongly contraindicates succinylcholine for RSI?","a":"Extensive burns sustained one week ago","w":["An acute asthma exacerbation today","An isolated closed femur fracture today","Hypertension controlled with medication"],"x":"After the first day or so following major burns, crush injury or denervation, extra acetylcholine receptors develop and succinylcholine can cause life-threatening hyperkalemia. A fresh fracture or asthma does not carry this risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp19f228b4","lv":"paramedic","d":"succinylcholine","sys":"c:rsi","q":"Shortly after succinylcholine, a patient develops jaw rigidity, rising end-tidal CO2, tachycardia and a climbing temperature. What should be recognized?","a":"Malignant hyperthermia","w":["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Serotonin syndrome","Expected fasciculations"],"x":"Succinylcholine can trigger malignant hyperthermia in susceptible patients, marked by rigidity, rising CO2 and hyperthermia. Fasciculations are brief and occur just before paralysis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpac885de8","lv":"paramedic","d":"vecuronium","sys":"c:rsi","q":"After intubation, a patient is kept paralyzed with vecuronium during transport. What is essential?","a":"Provide ongoing sedation and analgesia because vecuronium has none","w":["Reduce monitoring since the patient cannot move","Rely on the paralytic to provide pain control","Use the patient's lack of movement to confirm sedation"],"x":"Neuromuscular blockers paralyze without causing unconsciousness or pain relief, so a paralyzed patient can be awake and in pain. Lack of movement does not show adequate sedation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp31ffa70e","lv":"paramedic","d":"midazolam","sys":"c:rsi","q":"What is a drawback of using midazolam as the induction agent in a hypotensive patient?","a":"It can worsen hypotension","w":["It causes prolonged paralysis","It raises intracranial pressure","It triggers severe bronchospasm"],"x":"Benzodiazepines such as midazolam can reduce blood pressure, which is a problem in shock. Agents like etomidate or ketamine are often preferred in unstable patients.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0563e6b9","lv":"paramedic","d":"fentanyl","sys":"c:rsi","q":"Rapid IV push of fentanyl at high doses can cause which complication that makes bag-mask ventilation difficult?","a":"Rigid chest wall syndrome","w":["Laryngeal edema from histamine","Tension pneumothorax","Severe bronchorrhea"],"x":"Fentanyl given rapidly in large amounts can cause truncal and chest wall muscle rigidity that impairs ventilation. It causes little histamine release.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdfcc37d1","lv":"paramedic","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:allergy","q":"What is the preferred route for the first dose of epinephrine in anaphylaxis outside the hospital?","a":"Intramuscular injection into the anterolateral thigh","w":["Subcutaneous injection into the upper arm","Rapid IV push through a peripheral line","Nebulized inhalation through a mask"],"x":"IM injection in the thigh gives faster, more reliable absorption than subcutaneous injection. Undiluted IV push risks dangerous arrhythmias and hypertension.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2e41af15","lv":"paramedic","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:allergy","q":"Why is epinephrine the first-line treatment for anaphylaxis?","a":"It reverses vasodilation, bronchospasm and further mediator release","w":["It blocks histamine at H1 receptors in the skin","It suppresses the immune response over hours","It replaces fluid lost through leaky capillaries"],"x":"Epinephrine's alpha effects raise blood pressure and reduce swelling, while beta effects open the airways and reduce mast cell mediator release. Antihistamines only block H1 effects such as hives and itching.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp06d85668","lv":"paramedic","d":"dexamethasone","sys":"c:allergy","q":"Compared with other corticosteroids such as methylprednisolone, what is a notable property of dexamethasone?","a":"A long duration of action","w":["A very short duration of action","Strong mineralocorticoid (salt-retaining) activity","Immediate bronchodilating activity"],"x":"Dexamethasone is a long-acting glucocorticoid with very little mineralocorticoid effect. Like other steroids, it does not produce immediate bronchodilation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2e78a58a","lv":"paramedic","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"c:allergy","q":"Why is diphenhydramine not a substitute for epinephrine in anaphylaxis?","a":"It relieves hives and itching but does not reverse shock or bronchospasm","w":["It acts too quickly and causes rebound reactions","It worsens bronchospasm in most patients","It is only effective when given intramuscularly"],"x":"H1 antihistamines help skin symptoms but do not treat airway obstruction or hypotension. Epinephrine is the only first-line drug for anaphylaxis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1cc768d4","lv":"paramedic","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"c:allergy","q":"Which side effects are most expected after a patient receives diphenhydramine?","a":"Drowsiness and anticholinergic effects such as dry mouth","w":["Insomnia and excessive salivation","Bradycardia and increased sweating","Diarrhea and frequent urination"],"x":"Diphenhydramine is a sedating first-generation antihistamine with anticholinergic effects such as dry mouth and urinary retention. It does not increase salivation or sweating.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpaeac4a75","lv":"paramedic","d":"methylprednisolone","sys":"c:allergy","q":"What is the role of methylprednisolone in treating anaphylaxis?","a":"A slow-acting adjunct that may help with prolonged symptoms","w":["The first-line drug to reverse hypotension","A rapid bronchodilator for acute wheezing","An antidote that neutralizes the allergen"],"x":"Corticosteroids take hours to work and do not treat the immediate life threats, so they are adjuncts after epinephrine. Their effect on preventing biphasic reactions is uncertain.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpab692fb3","lv":"paramedic","d":"dexamethasone","sys":"c:allergy","q":"Dexamethasone given for an allergic reaction belongs to which class?","a":"Glucocorticoid (corticosteroid)","w":["H1 antihistamine","Beta-2 adrenergic agonist","Mast cell stabilizer"],"x":"Dexamethasone is a long-acting glucocorticoid that reduces inflammation. H1 antihistamines such as diphenhydramine are a different class.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb6c178ca","lv":"paramedic","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:allergy","q":"A patient in anaphylaxis still has wheezing after IM epinephrine. Why might nebulized albuterol be added?","a":"To relieve residual bronchospasm","w":["To treat ongoing hypotension","To clear hives on the skin","To prevent a biphasic reaction"],"x":"Albuterol is a beta-2 agonist that helps bronchospasm that persists after epinephrine. It does not treat hypotension or skin symptoms, and it is never a replacement for epinephrine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp830ac21e","lv":"paramedic","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:allergy","q":"In which anaphylaxis patient is IV glucagon most likely to be considered?","a":"A patient taking a beta blocker who does not respond to epinephrine","w":["A patient with diabetes whose glucose is normal","A child with hives and no breathing difficulty","A patient who improved after one dose of epinephrine"],"x":"Beta blockers can blunt the response to epinephrine, and glucagon raises cardiac output through a pathway that bypasses beta receptors. It is not needed when the patient responds to epinephrine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp62b1f2ff","lv":"paramedic","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:allergy","q":"Which adverse effect of IV glucagon requires the paramedic to be ready to protect the airway?","a":"Nausea and vomiting","w":["Severe bronchospasm","Laryngeal swelling","Sudden sedation"],"x":"Glucagon commonly causes nausea and vomiting, which can lead to aspiration in a patient with altered mental status. It does not cause bronchospasm or sedation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa4d575b0","lv":"paramedic","d":"lorazepam","sys":"c:neuro","q":"How does lorazepam stop seizure activity in status epilepticus?","a":"It enhances GABA-A receptor activity, increasing neuronal inhibition","w":["It blocks sodium channels to stabilize neuronal membranes","It blocks NMDA receptors to prevent excitation","It increases dopamine in the motor cortex"],"x":"Benzodiazepines bind the GABA-A receptor and increase chloride entry, making neurons harder to fire. Sodium channel blockade describes drugs such as phenytoin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp07286e81","lv":"paramedic","d":"lorazepam","sys":"c:neuro","q":"How should lorazepam injection be stored on an ambulance to preserve its potency?","a":"Refrigerated and protected from light","w":["Frozen until needed","In direct sunlight to stay warm","At any temperature without limits"],"x":"Lorazepam injection is labeled for refrigerated storage protected from light, which can be a practical issue in the field. Freezing or heat can degrade the drug.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpef2c525c","lv":"paramedic","d":"lorazepam","sys":"c:neuro","q":"After IV lorazepam stops a seizure, which effect is most important to monitor?","a":"Respiratory depression and airway compromise","w":["Hypertension and tachycardia","Hyperthermia and sweating","Increased muscle tone and rigidity"],"x":"Benzodiazepines depress respiration, especially with alcohol, opioids or repeat doses, and the postictal patient may not protect the airway. They tend to relax, not stiffen, muscles.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd64cec81","lv":"paramedic","d":"midazolam","sys":"c:neuro","q":"For an actively seizing adult without IV access, which route of midazolam has been shown effective in prehospital trials?","a":"Intramuscular injection","w":["Oral tablet","Subcutaneous injection","Transdermal patch"],"x":"IM midazolam was at least as effective as IV lorazepam for prehospital status epilepticus, largely because it can be given quickly without an IV. Oral and transdermal routes are not practical in an active seizure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbc80ebad","lv":"paramedic","d":"midazolam","sys":"c:neuro","q":"Why is flumazenil generally not used to reverse midazolam in a patient treated for status epilepticus?","a":"It can lower the seizure threshold and provoke seizures","w":["It is ineffective against midazolam","It causes prolonged respiratory depression","It only works when given intramuscularly"],"x":"Flumazenil can precipitate seizures, especially in patients with seizure disorders or chronic benzodiazepine use. Airway support is preferred for over-sedation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe3398c2c","lv":"paramedic","d":"diazepam","sys":"c:neuro","q":"Diazepam is available for caregivers to give at home for seizure clusters in which form?","a":"Rectal gel","w":["Transdermal patch","Subcutaneous pen","Chewable tablet"],"x":"Diazepam rectal gel is designed for caregiver use during seizure clusters. Patch and pen forms of diazepam are not used for seizures.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb286b5c3","lv":"paramedic","d":"diazepam","sys":"c:neuro","q":"Why is IM diazepam a poor choice for an active seizure?","a":"Absorption from muscle is slow and unpredictable","w":["It causes immediate cardiac arrest when given IM","It only works when given with an antihistamine","It is too short-acting when given into muscle"],"x":"Diazepam is poorly and erratically absorbed after IM injection, so midazolam is preferred when there is no IV. Diazepam is actually long acting.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5c75cf40","lv":"paramedic","d":"diazepam","sys":"c:neuro","q":"Why may an older adult stay sedated much longer after diazepam than after other benzodiazepines?","a":"It has a long half-life and active metabolites","w":["It is rapidly cleared by the kidneys","It has no active metabolites","It is inactivated by stomach acid"],"x":"Diazepam and its active metabolites are eliminated slowly, and older adults clear them even more slowly, leading to prolonged sedation and fall risk. It is not short acting.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8f83f581","lv":"paramedic","d":"labetalol","sys":"c:neuro","q":"A patient with an acute ischemic stroke is a fibrinolytic candidate but has markedly elevated blood pressure. Why might labetalol be given?","a":"To lower blood pressure enough to allow safe fibrinolytic therapy","w":["To dissolve the clot blocking the cerebral artery","To prevent seizures caused by the stroke","To raise cerebral perfusion pressure"],"x":"Fibrinolytics carry bleeding risk if blood pressure is too high, so it must be lowered below treatment limits before they are given. Labetalol does not dissolve clots.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpda68e9fc","lv":"paramedic","d":"labetalol","sys":"c:neuro","q":"In an acute ischemic stroke patient who is not a fibrinolytic candidate, why is aggressive blood pressure lowering with labetalol generally not done?","a":"Brain perfusion depends on blood pressure and rapid drops can extend ischemia","w":["Labetalol raises intracranial pressure in stroke","Labetalol is ineffective in patients with stroke","Low blood pressure causes hemorrhagic conversion"],"x":"The ischemic area around the stroke relies on blood pressure for flow, so pressure is usually allowed to remain elevated unless very high. Labetalol is effective; the concern is over-lowering.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp85fd617d","lv":"paramedic","d":"fentanyl","sys":"c:sedation","q":"A patient with a severe extremity injury has no IV access. By which route can fentanyl be given for rapid analgesia?","a":"Intranasal via a mucosal atomizer","w":["Rectal suppository","Endotracheal instillation","Subcutaneous pump"],"x":"Fentanyl is well absorbed across the nasal mucosa, giving rapid pain relief without an IV. Rectal and endotracheal routes are not used for prehospital fentanyl analgesia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpce55d5ad","lv":"paramedic","d":"morphine","sys":"c:sedation","q":"A patient develops flushing and itching along the arm shortly after IV morphine but has stable vital signs and no wheezing. What is the most likely cause?","a":"Histamine release, a common non-allergic effect","w":["Anaphylaxis requiring epinephrine","Opioid withdrawal","Serotonin syndrome"],"x":"Morphine directly releases histamine, which commonly causes itching and flushing without a true allergy. Anaphylaxis would include airway or circulatory involvement.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp63afcb13","lv":"paramedic","d":"midazolam","sys":"c:sedation","q":"When midazolam is combined with fentanyl for procedural sedation, which monitoring is most important?","a":"Continuous capnography for hypoventilation","w":["Hourly blood glucose checks","Serial 12-lead ECGs for ST changes","Pupil checks for dilation"],"x":"Benzodiazepines and opioids have synergistic respiratory depressant effects, and capnography detects hypoventilation earlier than pulse oximetry. Glucose and ST changes are not the main risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7d4d1588","lv":"paramedic","d":"ketamine","sys":"c:sedation","q":"What is ketamine's primary mechanism of action?","a":"NMDA receptor antagonism producing dissociative anesthesia","w":["GABA-A receptor enhancement producing sedation","Mu-opioid receptor stimulation producing analgesia","Dopamine receptor blockade producing calm"],"x":"Ketamine blocks NMDA glutamate receptors, causing dissociation and analgesia. GABA enhancement describes benzodiazepines and dopamine blockade describes antipsychotics.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp04c4a5d0","lv":"paramedic","d":"ketamine","sys":"c:sedation","q":"As ketamine wears off, a patient becomes agitated and describes vivid, frightening dreams. What should be recognized?","a":"An emergence reaction","w":["An allergic reaction","Opioid withdrawal","Hypoglycemia"],"x":"Emergence reactions with vivid dreams, confusion or agitation are known effects of ketamine as it wears off. A calm environment and, if needed, a benzodiazepine can help.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpea1aa70b","lv":"paramedic","d":"hydromorphone","sys":"c:sedation","q":"Compared with morphine, why is extra care needed when giving hydromorphone?","a":"It is several times more potent, so dosing errors are dangerous","w":["It has no risk of respiratory depression","It cannot be reversed with naloxone","It is a non-opioid analgesic"],"x":"Hydromorphone is a potent opioid, roughly several times as strong as morphine by weight, so mix-ups between the two have caused serious harm. Naloxone does reverse it.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfd246aba","lv":"paramedic","d":"ketorolac","sys":"c:sedation","q":"Ketorolac belongs to which class of analgesics?","a":"Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID)","w":["Opioid agonist","Dissociative anesthetic","Benzodiazepine"],"x":"Ketorolac is an injectable NSAID that inhibits cyclooxygenase and reduces prostaglandins. It does not act on opioid receptors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa1de73f0","lv":"paramedic","d":"ketorolac","sys":"c:sedation","q":"In which patient would ketorolac be inappropriate for pain control?","a":"A trauma patient with suspected internal bleeding","w":["A patient with an uncomplicated ankle sprain","A patient with kidney stone pain and normal kidney function","A patient with musculoskeletal back pain"],"x":"Ketorolac inhibits platelet function and increases bleeding risk, so it is contraindicated with active or suspected bleeding. Kidney stone pain with normal renal function is a common use.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8ea4fa0c","lv":"paramedic","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"c:sedation","q":"Before giving acetaminophen for pain, what should the paramedic ask the patient about?","a":"Other acetaminophen-containing products taken recently","w":["Recent use of any antibiotics","A history of asthma","Any allergy to latex"],"x":"Acetaminophen is found in many combination products, and exceeding the daily maximum can cause liver injury. Asking prevents unintentional overdose.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1dd3d0d3","lv":"paramedic","d":"diazepam","sys":"c:sedation","q":"Besides seizures and anxiety, diazepam has a labeled use as an adjunct for which condition?","a":"Skeletal muscle spasm","w":["Migraine headache","Nausea and vomiting","Neuropathic pain"],"x":"Diazepam has muscle relaxant effects and is labeled as an adjunct for skeletal muscle spasm. It is not an antiemetic or migraine treatment.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb13cb3a8","lv":"paramedic","d":"droperidol","sys":"c:psych","q":"Droperidol carries a boxed warning. Which ECG finding is the concern?","a":"QT interval prolongation","w":["ST segment elevation","Peaked T waves","PR interval shortening"],"x":"Droperidol can prolong the QT interval and has been associated with torsades de pointes, so cardiac monitoring is important. Peaked T waves suggest hyperkalemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4a5f1629","lv":"paramedic","d":"droperidol","sys":"c:psych","q":"Why is droperidol used for rapid sedation of a severely agitated patient in some EMS and emergency settings?","a":"It is a fast-acting antipsychotic that can be given intramuscularly","w":["It is an opioid with strong analgesic effects","It is a benzodiazepine reversible with flumazenil","It is a dissociative agent that blocks NMDA receptors"],"x":"Droperidol is a butyrophenone antipsychotic with rapid onset after IM injection, making it useful for agitation. It is not an opioid, benzodiazepine or dissociative.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpae48be1d","lv":"paramedic","d":"haloperidol","sys":"c:psych","q":"What is haloperidol's main mechanism of action in calming acute agitation or psychosis?","a":"Blocking dopamine D2 receptors in the brain","w":["Enhancing GABA-A receptor activity","Blocking NMDA glutamate receptors","Stimulating serotonin release"],"x":"Haloperidol is a typical antipsychotic that blocks D2 dopamine receptors. Enhancing GABA describes benzodiazepines, and NMDA blockade describes ketamine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0c299d3e","lv":"paramedic","d":"haloperidol","sys":"c:psych","q":"Days after starting haloperidol, a patient has high fever, lead-pipe muscle rigidity, confusion and unstable blood pressure. What should be recognized?","a":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","w":["Serotonin syndrome","Anticholinergic toxicity","Acute dystonic reaction"],"x":"Fever, severe rigidity, altered mental status and autonomic instability after an antipsychotic suggest neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Serotonin syndrome more often has clonus and hyperreflexia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc686f9b0","lv":"paramedic","d":"midazolam","sys":"c:psych","q":"An agitated patient who is heavily intoxicated with alcohol is given IM midazolam. What is the greatest concern?","a":"Additive respiratory depression","w":["Severe hypertension","Serotonin syndrome","Delayed onset of sedation"],"x":"Alcohol and benzodiazepines both depress the central nervous system, so their combined effect can cause hypoventilation and airway loss. Hypertension is not a typical effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc899e60e","lv":"paramedic","d":"midazolam","sys":"c:psych","q":"After chemically sedating a combative patient with midazolam, how should the patient be positioned and monitored?","a":"Supine or on the side with continuous airway and ETCO2 monitoring","w":["Prone with hands restrained behind the back","Face down with a backboard on top","Seated upright with no further monitoring"],"x":"Prone and restricted positions increase the risk of asphyxia and sudden death in sedated, restrained patients. Sedated patients need close monitoring of breathing and capnography.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5ff10b6a","lv":"paramedic","d":"ketamine","sys":"c:psych","q":"What advantage does ketamine offer when sedating a severely agitated, violent patient without IV access?","a":"Rapid onset after intramuscular injection","w":["Slow onset that avoids oversedation","It requires no further monitoring","It works only by the oral route"],"x":"Ketamine given IM produces dissociative sedation within minutes, which improves safety for patient and crew. All sedated patients still need monitoring.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp11ec4e69","lv":"paramedic","d":"ketamine","sys":"c:psych","q":"Which uncommon airway complication must be prepared for when ketamine is used for agitation?","a":"Laryngospasm","w":["Epiglottitis","Tracheal stenosis","Foreign body obstruction"],"x":"Ketamine can rarely cause laryngospasm and also increases salivation, so airway equipment must be ready. The other choices are not drug effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp987ca1e5","lv":"paramedic","d":"lorazepam","sys":"c:psych","q":"An agitated, tremulous, sweating patient is in alcohol withdrawal. Why is a benzodiazepine such as lorazepam preferred?","a":"It treats the withdrawal itself and reduces seizure risk","w":["It reverses alcohol intoxication","It prevents liver damage from alcohol","It replaces thiamine lost in alcohol use"],"x":"Benzodiazepines act on the same GABA receptors as alcohol, controlling withdrawal symptoms and lowering the risk of seizures and delirium tremens. Antipsychotics alone do not treat withdrawal.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp763af0a7","lv":"paramedic","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"c:psych","q":"Hours after haloperidol, a patient has sustained neck twisting, eyes rolled upward and tongue protrusion. Why is diphenhydramine given?","a":"To treat an acute dystonic reaction","w":["To treat an allergic reaction","To sedate further agitation","To treat neuroleptic malignant syndrome"],"x":"Acute dystonia from dopamine blockade responds to anticholinergic agents such as diphenhydramine. It is not an allergy, and diphenhydramine does not treat neuroleptic malignant syndrome.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp661c3fc4","lv":"paramedic","d":"tranexamic-acid","sys":"c:trauma","q":"How does tranexamic acid reduce bleeding in trauma?","a":"It blocks plasminogen activation, slowing breakdown of fibrin clots","w":["It replaces clotting factors lost in hemorrhage","It reverses the effect of anticoagulants like warfarin","It constricts bleeding vessels at the injury site"],"x":"TXA is an antifibrinolytic that keeps formed clots from dissolving. It does not supply clotting factors or reverse anticoagulants.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpeb55006f","lv":"paramedic","d":"tranexamic-acid","sys":"c:trauma","q":"Why does the time since injury matter when tranexamic acid is considered for a bleeding trauma patient?","a":"Benefit is greatest when given within 3 hours of injury","w":["It only works if given more than 6 hours after injury","It must be delayed until blood products are given","Its effect is the same regardless of timing"],"x":"Trials show TXA reduces bleeding deaths when started early, with the most benefit within about 3 hours, and later use may cause harm. It does not need to wait for blood products.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp545909c2","lv":"paramedic","d":"calcium-chloride","sys":"c:trauma","q":"Why can massive transfusion of stored blood cause low calcium?","a":"Citrate preservative in stored blood binds calcium","w":["Stored blood contains excess phosphate that blocks absorption","Transfused red cells absorb calcium from plasma","Cold blood causes the kidneys to excrete calcium"],"x":"Citrate anticoagulant in blood products binds ionized calcium, and low calcium worsens clotting and heart function. That is why calcium is given during large transfusions.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcf6ee30b","lv":"paramedic","d":"calcium-chloride","sys":"c:trauma","q":"Why is calcium chloride given through a large or central vein whenever possible?","a":"Extravasation can cause severe tissue injury and necrosis","w":["It is inactivated in small peripheral veins","It only works when it reaches the liver first","It causes the vein to clot when given peripherally"],"x":"Calcium chloride is very irritating, and infiltration can cause serious tissue necrosis, so a secure, large vein is preferred. It is not inactivated in peripheral veins.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8bb7674c","lv":"paramedic","d":"calcium-gluconate","sys":"c:trauma","q":"What is an advantage of calcium gluconate over calcium chloride when only a small peripheral IV is available?","a":"It is less irritating to veins and tissues","w":["It contains more elemental calcium","It works by a completely different mechanism","It never needs ECG monitoring"],"x":"Calcium gluconate is less caustic if it infiltrates, so it is preferred peripherally. Calcium chloride actually contains more elemental calcium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5a52ab28","lv":"paramedic","d":"calcium-gluconate","sys":"c:trauma","q":"How does calcium gluconate compare with an equal volume of calcium chloride of the same strength?","a":"It delivers about one-third as much elemental calcium","w":["It delivers about three times as much elemental calcium","It delivers exactly the same amount of calcium","It delivers no usable calcium until metabolized"],"x":"Calcium chloride provides roughly three times the elemental calcium of calcium gluconate per equal amount, so more gluconate is needed for the same effect. Both deliver usable calcium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6911c473","lv":"paramedic","d":"fentanyl","sys":"c:trauma","q":"Why is fentanyl often preferred over morphine for analgesia in a trauma patient with borderline blood pressure?","a":"It causes less histamine release and hypotension","w":["It raises blood pressure through vasoconstriction","It cannot cause respiratory depression","It also controls bleeding"],"x":"Fentanyl releases little histamine, so it is less likely than morphine to drop blood pressure. It can still cause respiratory depression.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp496017aa","lv":"paramedic","d":"fentanyl","sys":"c:trauma","q":"A patient with a traumatic brain injury receives fentanyl for pain. Which monitoring is most important?","a":"Ventilation and blood pressure to prevent secondary brain injury","w":["Urine output every few minutes","Skin color for signs of jaundice","Pupil response to confirm opioid effect"],"x":"Hypoventilation raises CO2 and intracranial pressure, and hypotension lowers brain perfusion; both worsen brain injury. Capnography and blood pressure should be watched closely.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5ea3d936","lv":"paramedic","d":"ketamine","sys":"c:trauma","q":"Why is ketamine useful for analgesia in a hypotensive trauma patient?","a":"It tends to maintain blood pressure and airway reflexes","w":["It strongly lowers heart rate and blood pressure","It reverses hemorrhagic shock","It causes complete muscle paralysis"],"x":"Ketamine's sympathomimetic effects usually preserve blood pressure, and patients generally keep their airway reflexes and breathing. It does not treat the shock itself.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcae5951e","lv":"paramedic","d":"ketamine","sys":"c:trauma","q":"What is a benefit of adding low-dose ketamine to opioids for severe fracture pain?","a":"It reduces the amount of opioid needed","w":["It speeds fracture healing","It eliminates the need for splinting","It prevents infection at the fracture site"],"x":"Low-dose ketamine provides analgesia through NMDA blockade and is opioid-sparing, reducing opioid side effects. It does not affect healing or replace splinting.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp90941d4f","lv":"paramedic","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"Why is 5% dextrose in water a poor fluid for resuscitating hemorrhagic shock?","a":"Once the glucose is used, it acts as free water that leaves the vessels","w":["It contains too much sodium for trauma patients","It causes the blood to clot too quickly","It is too thick to infuse rapidly"],"x":"After the dextrose is metabolized, the remaining free water distributes into all body compartments and very little stays in the bloodstream. Isotonic crystalloids or blood are used for volume.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp39c15d0d","lv":"paramedic","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"Before and during administration of concentrated IV dextrose, what is most important to confirm?","a":"A patent, free-flowing IV line","w":["A rectal temperature reading","A recent hemoglobin level","A negative pregnancy test"],"x":"Concentrated dextrose is hypertonic and can cause tissue necrosis if it infiltrates, so line patency must be confirmed. The other checks are not required before treating hypoglycemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3b1cda03","lv":"paramedic","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"When is oral glucose gel appropriate for a patient with hypoglycemia?","a":"When the patient is alert enough to swallow and protect the airway","w":["When the patient is unresponsive and has no IV","When the patient is actively seizing","When the patient has vomited several times"],"x":"Oral glucose requires an intact gag reflex and the ability to swallow, or aspiration can occur. Unresponsive or seizing patients need IV dextrose or glucagon instead.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp41cad772","lv":"paramedic","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"Why are dextrose-containing IV fluids generally not given routinely to a patient with acute stroke unless the glucose is low?","a":"High blood glucose can worsen brain injury outcomes","w":["Dextrose dissolves the clot too quickly","Dextrose raises the blood pressure dangerously","Dextrose interferes with the stroke scale"],"x":"Hyperglycemia is linked to worse outcomes after brain injury, so glucose is checked and treated only when low. Dextrose has no effect on the clot.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfc87e4d6","lv":"paramedic","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:fluids","q":"When IV insulin is used to treat hyperkalemia, why is dextrose usually given with it?","a":"To prevent hypoglycemia caused by the insulin","w":["To push potassium into the urine","To stabilize the cardiac membrane","To make the insulin work more slowly"],"x":"Insulin shifts potassium into cells but also lowers blood glucose, so dextrose is given to prevent hypoglycemia unless glucose is already high. Calcium, not dextrose, stabilizes the cardiac membrane.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd336367d","lv":"paramedic","d":"calcium-chloride","sys":"c:lytes","q":"Which finding suggests hypocalcemia that calcium chloride would treat?","a":"Carpopedal spasm and tingling around the mouth","w":["Peaked T waves and muscle weakness","Flushing and loss of deep tendon reflexes","Polyuria and excessive thirst"],"x":"Low calcium increases nerve and muscle excitability, causing tingling, cramps, carpopedal spasm and a long QT. Loss of reflexes and flushing suggest high magnesium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp95e106f2","lv":"paramedic","d":"calcium-chloride","sys":"c:lytes","q":"During IV administration of calcium chloride, what monitoring is most important?","a":"Continuous ECG for bradycardia and arrhythmias","w":["Pupil checks for constriction","Blood glucose every few minutes","Peak flow measurements"],"x":"Rapid calcium can slow the heart and cause arrhythmias, so it is given slowly with the ECG monitored. Glucose and peak flow are not affected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe014b1ed","lv":"paramedic","d":"calcium-gluconate","sys":"c:lytes","q":"A patient with hyperkalemia has wide QRS complexes and peaked T waves. What does IV calcium gluconate do?","a":"It stabilizes the cardiac membrane but does not lower potassium","w":["It removes potassium from the body through the kidneys","It shifts potassium into the cells","It binds potassium in the gut"],"x":"Calcium protects the heart from the effects of high potassium within minutes but does not change the potassium level. Other treatments such as insulin, albuterol and dialysis lower potassium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9e151642","lv":"paramedic","d":"magnesium-sulfate","sys":"c:lytes","q":"A patient with a long QT interval develops polymorphic ventricular tachycardia that twists around the baseline and has a pulse. Which drug is indicated?","a":"Magnesium sulfate","w":["Adenosine","Diltiazem","Metoprolol"],"x":"Torsades de pointes is treated with IV magnesium, which suppresses early afterdepolarizations. Adenosine and diltiazem are for narrow-complex rhythms.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp250b9102","lv":"paramedic","d":"magnesium-sulfate","sys":"c:lytes","q":"Why is low magnesium corrected in a patient whose hypokalemia does not respond to potassium replacement?","a":"Low magnesium causes ongoing potassium loss through the kidneys","w":["Magnesium is converted into potassium in the liver","Magnesium blocks potassium absorption in the gut","Low magnesium causes falsely low lab readings"],"x":"Magnesium deficiency increases renal potassium wasting, so potassium stays low until magnesium is replaced. The lab values are real, not false.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd52b3f58","lv":"paramedic","d":"sodium-bicarbonate","sys":"c:lytes","q":"How does sodium bicarbonate help lower serum potassium in severe hyperkalemia with acidosis?","a":"Raising blood pH drives potassium into the cells","w":["It binds potassium in the bloodstream","It increases potassium loss in stool","It stabilizes the cardiac membrane directly"],"x":"As pH rises, hydrogen ions leave cells and potassium moves in, lowering serum potassium. Calcium, not bicarbonate, stabilizes the cardiac membrane.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbb86b5ed","lv":"paramedic","d":"sodium-bicarbonate","sys":"c:lytes","q":"Which problems can result from giving too much sodium bicarbonate?","a":"Metabolic alkalosis and sodium overload","w":["Metabolic acidosis and dehydration","Hyperkalemia and hypoglycemia","Hypercalcemia and kidney stones"],"x":"Excess bicarbonate raises pH and delivers a large sodium load, which can cause alkalosis, low potassium and fluid overload. It treats acidosis rather than causing it.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp38299ac5","lv":"paramedic","d":"furosemide","sys":"c:lytes","q":"Furosemide belongs to which class of diuretics?","a":"Loop diuretic acting on the loop of Henle","w":["Thiazide diuretic acting on the distal tubule","Potassium-sparing diuretic acting on aldosterone","Osmotic diuretic acting throughout the nephron"],"x":"Furosemide blocks sodium and chloride reabsorption in the thick ascending loop of Henle, making it a potent loop diuretic. Thiazides act in the distal tubule.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3ef57d25","lv":"paramedic","d":"furosemide","sys":"c:lytes","q":"Which electrolyte disturbance is most important to monitor after furosemide?","a":"Low potassium","w":["High potassium","High sodium","High calcium"],"x":"Loop diuretics cause loss of potassium, magnesium and calcium in the urine, so hypokalemia is a major concern. They do not raise potassium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5934ad62","lv":"paramedic","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:lytes","q":"How does nebulized albuterol help lower potassium in hyperkalemia?","a":"Beta-2 stimulation activates the sodium-potassium pump, shifting potassium into cells","w":["It increases potassium loss through the kidneys","It binds potassium in the airway lining","It blocks potassium absorption from the gut"],"x":"Albuterol stimulates beta-2 receptors that drive the Na-K pump, moving potassium into cells temporarily. It does not remove potassium from the body.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2d0de230","lv":"paramedic","d":"naloxone","sys":"c:tox","q":"When giving naloxone to a patient with suspected opioid overdose, what is the goal of treatment?","a":"Adequate spontaneous breathing","w":["Full alertness and orientation","A heart rate above normal","Dilated pupils"],"x":"Naloxone is titrated to restore effective breathing; aiming for full wakefulness can trigger abrupt withdrawal, vomiting and agitation. Pupil size is not the treatment goal.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp228b79ad","lv":"paramedic","d":"naloxone","sys":"c:tox","q":"After a patient wakes up following naloxone, why is continued monitoring important?","a":"Naloxone may wear off before the opioid, causing re-sedation","w":["Naloxone causes delayed seizures in all patients","Naloxone causes rebound hypertension hours later","Naloxone leads to delayed liver failure"],"x":"Many opioids, especially long-acting or high-potency ones, last longer than naloxone, so breathing can decline again. Repeat doses may be needed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd2eeaaff","lv":"paramedic","d":"sodium-bicarbonate","sys":"c:tox","q":"In a tricyclic antidepressant overdose, which finding is a key indication for sodium bicarbonate?","a":"Widening of the QRS complex","w":["Sinus bradycardia","Peaked T waves","ST depression in the lateral leads"],"x":"Tricyclics block cardiac sodium channels and widen the QRS, and bicarbonate overcomes this blockade with sodium and alkalinization. Sinus tachycardia, not bradycardia, is typical.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0455f88c","lv":"paramedic","d":"sodium-bicarbonate","sys":"c:tox","q":"How does sodium bicarbonate help in salicylate (aspirin) poisoning?","a":"It alkalinizes the urine to increase salicylate excretion","w":["It binds salicylate in the stomach","It reverses the antiplatelet effect","It blocks salicylate absorption from the gut"],"x":"Alkaline urine traps salicylate in its ionized form so it is excreted, and alkaline blood keeps it out of the brain. Activated charcoal, not bicarbonate, binds drug in the gut.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd411203f","lv":"paramedic","d":"activated-charcoal","sys":"c:tox","q":"Which patient should not receive activated charcoal?","a":"A drowsy patient who cannot protect the airway","w":["An alert patient who just swallowed acetaminophen","An alert patient who took extra aspirin","An alert patient who ingested a carbamazepine overdose"],"x":"Charcoal can be aspirated by a patient with a decreased level of consciousness and no airway protection. Acetaminophen, aspirin and carbamazepine are all bound by charcoal.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4e90eb47","lv":"paramedic","d":"hydroxocobalamin","sys":"c:tox","q":"After hydroxocobalamin is given for suspected cyanide poisoning from smoke inhalation, which finding is expected?","a":"Red discoloration of the skin and urine","w":["Blue discoloration of the lips","Yellowing of the eyes","Black, tarry stools"],"x":"Hydroxocobalamin is a deep red compound that binds cyanide to form cyanocobalamin, and it turns the skin and urine red. This is expected and can interfere with some lab tests.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1c9acca3","lv":"paramedic","d":"atropine","sys":"c:tox","q":"When treating organophosphate poisoning with atropine, what is the main endpoint for giving more?","a":"Drying of bronchial secretions and easier breathing","w":["Return of normal pupil size","Resolution of muscle twitching","A heart rate below normal"],"x":"Atropine blocks muscarinic effects, so it is titrated to clear airway secretions and improve breathing. It does not reverse nicotinic effects such as muscle twitching, and pupils are an unreliable endpoint.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7eb96ab1","lv":"paramedic","d":"pralidoxime","sys":"c:tox","q":"What does pralidoxime do in organophosphate poisoning?","a":"It reactivates acetylcholinesterase if given before the bond ages","w":["It blocks muscarinic receptors to dry secretions","It binds the organophosphate in the gut","It increases acetylcholine release at synapses"],"x":"Pralidoxime removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase, restoring the enzyme and improving muscle weakness. Atropine, not pralidoxime, blocks muscarinic receptors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0e0f1f79","lv":"paramedic","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:tox","q":"Why can glucagon help a patient with a beta blocker overdose who is bradycardic and hypotensive?","a":"It raises cardiac cAMP through a pathway that bypasses beta receptors","w":["It blocks the beta blocker from binding receptors","It stimulates the vagus nerve to slow the heart","It increases absorption of the beta blocker"],"x":"Glucagon activates its own receptors on heart cells, increasing cAMP, heart rate and contractility even when beta receptors are blocked. It does not compete at the beta receptor.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3cc753df","lv":"paramedic","d":"calcium-chloride","sys":"c:tox","q":"For which overdose is calcium chloride most specifically used?","a":"Calcium channel blocker overdose with hypotension","w":["Opioid overdose with apnea","Organophosphate exposure","Cyanide toxicity from smoke"],"x":"Calcium can partly overcome calcium channel blockade, improving contractility and blood pressure. Opioid overdose needs naloxone and cyanide needs hydroxocobalamin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1c736258","lv":"paramedic","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:endo","q":"Before giving IV dextrose to a patient with altered mental status, which assessment is most important?","a":"A point-of-care blood glucose level","w":["A 12-lead ECG","A serum potassium level","A pulse oximetry reading"],"x":"Confirming hypoglycemia with a glucose check guides treatment and avoids unnecessary dextrose. The other assessments do not confirm low blood sugar.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1e83115a","lv":"paramedic","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:endo","q":"A patient with hypoglycemia from a sulfonylurea overdose improves after IV dextrose. What should be anticipated?","a":"Recurrent hypoglycemia as the dextrose is used up","w":["Permanent correction after a single dose","Severe hyperkalemia within minutes","Rebound hypertension and bradycardia"],"x":"Sulfonylureas stimulate insulin release for many hours, so glucose often drops again after dextrose is used. These patients need repeated glucose checks and observation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0ce8e06f","lv":"paramedic","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:endo","q":"Which presentation most strongly suggests hypoglycemia that should respond to dextrose?","a":"Sweating, confusion and fast heart rate in a patient using insulin","w":["Fruity breath, deep breathing and thirst in a new diabetic","Slow onset of thirst and frequent urination over days","Fever, stiff neck and a rash in a young adult"],"x":"Sweating, tachycardia and confusion reflect the adrenaline response and brain glucose shortage of hypoglycemia. Fruity breath and deep breathing suggest diabetic ketoacidosis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd362c2b4","lv":"paramedic","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:endo","q":"A combative patient with known diabetes has low blood glucose and no IV access. Which route can be used for glucagon?","a":"Intramuscular injection","w":["Oral liquid","Sublingual tablet","Rectal suppository"],"x":"Glucagon can be given IM, subcutaneously or as a nasal powder when an IV is not available. It is not given orally, sublingually or rectally.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd206acb5","lv":"paramedic","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:endo","q":"In which patient is glucagon least likely to raise blood glucose effectively?","a":"A malnourished patient with chronic alcohol use","w":["A well-nourished patient on insulin","A young adult with type 1 diabetes","An athlete who skipped one meal"],"x":"Glucagon works by releasing glycogen stores from the liver, which are depleted in starvation and chronic alcohol use. IV dextrose is more reliable in these patients.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp090b0692","lv":"paramedic","d":"glucagon","sys":"c:endo","q":"How does glucagon raise blood glucose?","a":"It stimulates the liver to break down glycogen into glucose","w":["It provides glucose directly into the bloodstream","It increases insulin release from the pancreas","It blocks glucose uptake by the kidneys"],"x":"Glucagon triggers hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, releasing stored glucose. Unlike dextrose, it does not contain glucose itself.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa8d51510","lv":"paramedic","d":"thiamine","sys":"c:endo","q":"Why is thiamine given before or with dextrose to a malnourished patient with chronic alcohol use?","a":"To help prevent Wernicke encephalopathy","w":["To prevent rebound hypoglycemia","To treat alcohol withdrawal seizures","To speed alcohol metabolism"],"x":"Glucose metabolism uses thiamine, and in thiamine-deficient patients a glucose load can precipitate or worsen Wernicke encephalopathy. Treating hypoglycemia should not be delayed, though.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1ae39b1d","lv":"paramedic","d":"thiamine","sys":"c:endo","q":"Which combination of findings is classic for Wernicke encephalopathy?","a":"Confusion, ataxia and abnormal eye movements","w":["Fever, rash and stiff neck","Tremor, sweating and hallucinations","Chest pain, dyspnea and leg swelling"],"x":"The classic triad is confusion, unsteady gait and ophthalmoplegia or nystagmus, though all three are often not present. Tremor and hallucinations suggest alcohol withdrawal.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp828788e7","lv":"paramedic","d":"thiamine","sys":"c:endo","q":"What is thiamine's role in the body?","a":"It is a cofactor for enzymes that metabolize carbohydrates","w":["It is a hormone that lowers blood glucose","It is an electrolyte that controls heart rhythm","It is a clotting factor made by the liver"],"x":"Thiamine (vitamin B1) is needed by key enzymes in glucose metabolism, so deficiency harms energy-dependent tissues like the brain. It is not a hormone or electrolyte.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe50785c0","lv":"paramedic","d":"thiamine","sys":"c:endo","q":"Besides chronic alcohol use, which patient is at risk for thiamine deficiency?","a":"A patient with prolonged vomiting after bariatric surgery","w":["A patient with well-controlled high blood pressure","A healthy adult who takes a daily multivitamin","A patient recovering from a simple ankle sprain"],"x":"Poor intake and absorption, as with persistent vomiting, bariatric surgery or hyperemesis, can deplete thiamine. Well-nourished patients are not usually at risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp130c34a5","lv":"paramedic","d":"ondansetron","sys":"c:gi","q":"How does ondansetron relieve nausea and vomiting?","a":"It blocks 5-HT3 serotonin receptors","w":["It blocks dopamine D2 receptors","It blocks histamine H1 receptors","It blocks muscarinic receptors"],"x":"Ondansetron is a selective 5-HT3 antagonist acting in the gut and the brain's vomiting center. Dopamine blockade describes metoclopramide and droperidol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9190b9e9","lv":"paramedic","d":"ondansetron","sys":"c:gi","q":"Which ECG effect is a labeled warning for ondansetron?","a":"QT interval prolongation","w":["ST segment elevation","Delta waves","Peaked T waves"],"x":"Ondansetron can prolong the QT interval, especially with other QT-prolonging drugs or electrolyte problems. Delta waves suggest an accessory pathway.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp779428ed","lv":"paramedic","d":"ondansetron","sys":"c:gi","q":"A patient taking an SSRI becomes agitated with tremor, clonus, sweating and fever after receiving ondansetron. What should be recognized?","a":"Possible serotonin syndrome","w":["An expected effect of ondansetron","Acute dystonic reaction","Hypoglycemia"],"x":"Serotonin syndrome has been reported when ondansetron is used with serotonergic drugs and shows clonus, hyperthermia and agitation. It should be reported and the patient supported.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdf85e8e3","lv":"paramedic","d":"metoclopramide","sys":"c:gi","q":"What is metoclopramide's mechanism as an antiemetic?","a":"Dopamine receptor blockade and increased gastric emptying","w":["Serotonin 5-HT3 blockade only","Histamine H1 blockade only","Opioid receptor stimulation in the gut"],"x":"Metoclopramide blocks dopamine receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone and speeds stomach and upper intestinal movement. Ondansetron is the 5-HT3 blocker.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa4abb840","lv":"paramedic","d":"metoclopramide","sys":"c:gi","q":"Soon after IV metoclopramide, a patient feels intensely restless and cannot sit still. What is the most likely cause?","a":"Akathisia","w":["Anxiety disorder","Hypoglycemia","Opioid withdrawal"],"x":"Akathisia is an extrapyramidal effect of dopamine blockers such as metoclopramide, felt as inner restlessness. It should be recognized and reported.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd93cb703","lv":"paramedic","d":"metoclopramide","sys":"c:gi","q":"In which patient is metoclopramide contraindicated?","a":"A patient with suspected bowel obstruction","w":["A patient with migraine and nausea","A patient with diabetic gastroparesis","A patient with nausea after surgery"],"x":"Metoclopramide increases gut motility and can be harmful when there is obstruction, perforation or GI bleeding. Gastroparesis and migraine are common uses.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa2fe7dd8","lv":"paramedic","d":"promethazine","sys":"c:gi","q":"Because of its boxed warning for severe tissue injury, what is the preferred route for injectable promethazine?","a":"Deep intramuscular injection","w":["Rapid IV push","Intra-arterial injection","Subcutaneous injection"],"x":"Promethazine can cause gangrene with IV extravasation or accidental intra-arterial or subcutaneous injection, so deep IM is preferred. Subcutaneous use is contraindicated.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6084946a","lv":"paramedic","d":"promethazine","sys":"c:gi","q":"Promethazine is contraindicated in which group because of fatal respiratory depression?","a":"Children younger than 2 years","w":["Adults older than 65 years","Pregnant patients","Patients with migraine"],"x":"Promethazine carries a boxed warning against use in children under 2 years because of fatal respiratory depression. Older adults need caution but are not absolutely excluded.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfc4598fc","lv":"paramedic","d":"droperidol","sys":"c:gi","q":"How does droperidol relieve nausea and vomiting?","a":"It blocks dopamine receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone","w":["It blocks serotonin receptors in the gut wall","It neutralizes stomach acid","It increases stomach emptying through cholinergic action"],"x":"Droperidol is a dopamine antagonist that acts in the brain's chemoreceptor trigger zone. Serotonin blockade describes ondansetron.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp26a2172f","lv":"paramedic","d":"droperidol","sys":"c:gi","q":"Which effect should be expected when droperidol is given with opioids or benzodiazepines?","a":"Additive sedation and central nervous system depression","w":["Reversal of the opioid's effects","Marked increase in heart rate","Excessive alertness and insomnia"],"x":"Droperidol is sedating and adds to the CNS depression of opioids and benzodiazepines, so patients need close monitoring. It does not reverse opioids.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa7397aa6","lv":"paramedic","d":"oxytocin","sys":"c:ob","q":"When is oxytocin given in the field to control postpartum bleeding?","a":"After the placenta delivers and no other fetus is present","w":["Before the baby's head is delivered","As soon as contractions begin","Before the umbilical cord is clamped"],"x":"Oxytocin can trap an undelivered twin or placenta, so it is given after delivery once another fetus is ruled out. Giving it during labor in the field can cause dangerous uterine contractions.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp818c7778","lv":"paramedic","d":"oxytocin","sys":"c:ob","q":"How does oxytocin control postpartum hemorrhage caused by uterine atony?","a":"It makes the uterine muscle contract, compressing bleeding vessels","w":["It activates clotting factors in the blood","It constricts vessels throughout the body","It relaxes the uterus so the placenta can separate"],"x":"The uterus stops bleeding mainly by contracting around its vessels, and oxytocin strengthens those contractions. It does not act on clotting factors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8b866c33","lv":"paramedic","d":"magnesium-sulfate","sys":"c:ob","q":"A pregnant patient with severe preeclampsia has a seizure. Which drug is the treatment of choice to stop and prevent eclamptic seizures?","a":"Magnesium sulfate","w":["Phenytoin","Valproic acid","Phenobarbital"],"x":"Magnesium sulfate is more effective than other anticonvulsants for preventing and treating eclamptic seizures. Phenytoin was inferior in trials.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcf7f3f12","lv":"paramedic","d":"magnesium-sulfate","sys":"c:ob","q":"While receiving magnesium sulfate for preeclampsia, which finding is often the first sign of toxicity?","a":"Loss of deep tendon reflexes","w":["Increased urine output","Rising blood pressure","Tachycardia and tremor"],"x":"Reflexes disappear before respiratory depression and cardiac arrest, so they are checked regularly. Decreased, not increased, urine output raises toxicity risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4f6a452c","lv":"paramedic","d":"terbutaline","sys":"c:ob","q":"How does terbutaline act when used to briefly slow preterm contractions?","a":"Beta-2 stimulation relaxes uterine smooth muscle","w":["Alpha-1 stimulation tightens the cervix","Calcium channel blockade stops contractions","Oxytocin receptor blockade in the uterus"],"x":"Terbutaline is a beta-2 agonist that relaxes uterine as well as bronchial smooth muscle. Its injectable form carries a boxed warning against prolonged use for preterm labor.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp696a2632","lv":"paramedic","d":"terbutaline","sys":"c:ob","q":"Which maternal adverse effects of terbutaline should be monitored and reported?","a":"Tachycardia, palpitations and chest pain","w":["Bradycardia and heart block","Hypotension from vagal stimulation","Constricted pupils and drowsiness"],"x":"Beta stimulation causes maternal tachycardia and can lead to arrhythmias, myocardial ischemia and pulmonary edema. Bradycardia is not expected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdf27ce46","lv":"paramedic","d":"tranexamic-acid","sys":"c:ob","q":"What is the role of tranexamic acid in postpartum hemorrhage?","a":"An adjunct to uterotonics and fundal massage, not a replacement","w":["The first and only drug needed for atony","A uterotonic that makes the uterus contract","A replacement for blood products in severe bleeding"],"x":"TXA reduces fibrin breakdown and lowers bleeding deaths when given early, but it does not make the uterus contract. Fundal massage and uterotonics such as oxytocin remain essential.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0345f0ba","lv":"paramedic","d":"tranexamic-acid","sys":"c:ob","q":"Why is IV tranexamic acid given slowly rather than as a rapid push?","a":"Rapid injection can cause hypotension","w":["Rapid injection causes hypoglycemia","Rapid injection inactivates the drug","Rapid injection causes severe bradycardia"],"x":"The labeling notes that hypotension has occurred with rapid IV injection of tranexamic acid. It is not inactivated by fast administration.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpde1ce1ff","lv":"paramedic","d":"labetalol","sys":"c:ob","q":"Which IV agent is a first-line choice for severe hypertension in a patient with preeclampsia?","a":"Labetalol","w":["Enalaprilat","Nitroprusside","Furosemide"],"x":"Labetalol, along with hydralazine and oral nifedipine, is first-line for severe hypertension in pregnancy. ACE inhibitors such as enalaprilat are contraindicated in pregnancy because of fetal harm.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfc9e77e8","lv":"paramedic","d":"calcium-gluconate","sys":"c:ob","q":"A patient receiving magnesium sulfate develops slow, shallow breathing and absent reflexes. Why is calcium gluconate given?","a":"It antagonizes magnesium's effects on nerves and muscle","w":["It increases kidney excretion of magnesium","It binds magnesium and inactivates it","It sedates the patient to reduce oxygen need"],"x":"Calcium counteracts magnesium at the neuromuscular junction and heart, temporarily reversing toxicity while ventilation is supported. It does not remove magnesium from the body.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp25e2f1e2","lv":"paramedic","d":"epinephrine","sys":"c:peds","q":"An infant's heart rate remains below normal with poor perfusion despite effective oxygenation and ventilation. Which drug is first-line?","a":"Epinephrine","w":["Atropine","Adenosine","Amiodarone"],"x":"Pediatric bradycardia is usually caused by hypoxia, and when it persists with poor perfusion despite good ventilation, epinephrine is the first drug. Atropine is for vagal causes or primary AV block.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp11c937dc","lv":"paramedic","d":"atropine","sys":"c:peds","q":"During intubation, a child's heart rate suddenly drops. Which cause of bradycardia is atropine most appropriate for?","a":"Increased vagal tone from airway manipulation","w":["Hypoxia from a failed airway attempt","Hypothermia after cold-water drowning","Hyperkalemia from kidney failure"],"x":"Atropine blocks vagal effects, so it is appropriate for vagally mediated bradycardia or primary AV block. Hypoxic bradycardia is treated first with oxygenation and ventilation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp25fa8f55","lv":"paramedic","d":"dextrose","sys":"c:peds","q":"Why are lower concentrations of dextrose used for infants and young children than the concentrated form used in adults?","a":"Concentrated solutions are hypertonic and can damage small veins","w":["Children cannot metabolize concentrated glucose","Concentrated dextrose causes seizures in children","Lower concentrations raise glucose faster"],"x":"Highly concentrated dextrose is very hypertonic and can injure small veins and tissues, so diluted forms are used in children. Lower concentrations do not work faster.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1d23f6ea","lv":"paramedic","d":"midazolam","sys":"c:peds","q":"A child is having a prolonged seizure and no IV can be started. Which route of midazolam is commonly used?","a":"Intranasal via a mucosal atomizer","w":["Oral liquid","Transdermal patch","Inhaled nebulizer"],"x":"Intranasal midazolam is absorbed quickly through the nasal mucosa and is effective for pediatric seizures without IV access. Oral and transdermal routes are not practical during a seizure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp79ba8c76","lv":"paramedic","d":"naloxone","sys":"c:peds","q":"Why is caution needed with naloxone in a newborn whose mother has used opioids regularly during pregnancy?","a":"It can cause abrupt, life-threatening withdrawal including seizures","w":["It has no effect in newborns","It causes severe hypoglycemia","It blocks the newborn's breathing reflex"],"x":"Abrupt reversal in an opioid-dependent newborn can trigger severe withdrawal and seizures, so ventilation support is the priority. Naloxone does work in newborns.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9bbbbbd9","lv":"paramedic","d":"adenosine","sys":"c:peds","q":"An infant has a very fast, narrow-complex rhythm. Which feature suggests SVT that may respond to adenosine rather than sinus tachycardia?","a":"Abrupt onset with a fixed rate and absent P waves","w":["Gradual rise in rate with fever and dehydration","Rate that varies with crying and activity","Visible P waves before every QRS complex"],"x":"SVT starts and stops abruptly, has little rate variability and usually lacks visible P waves. Sinus tachycardia varies with activity, has P waves and usually has a cause such as fever.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe1fc6683","lv":"paramedic","d":"ondansetron","sys":"c:peds","q":"How does ondansetron help a child with vomiting from gastroenteritis?","a":"It reduces vomiting so oral rehydration can succeed","w":["It cures the underlying viral infection","It stops diarrhea by slowing the gut","It replaces lost electrolytes"],"x":"By reducing vomiting, ondansetron helps children tolerate oral fluids and may avoid an IV. It does not treat the infection or replace fluids.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp762a17a2","lv":"paramedic","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"c:peds","q":"What should caregivers be taught when giving liquid acetaminophen to a child?","a":"Use the measuring device supplied and dose by weight","w":["Use a kitchen spoon for convenience","Combine it with adult cold medicines","Give extra doses if the fever persists"],"x":"Kitchen spoons are inaccurate and many combination products also contain acetaminophen, so mixing them risks overdose and liver injury. The supplied device and weight-based dosing reduce errors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbd7e2f47","lv":"paramedic","d":"ibuprofen","sys":"c:peds","q":"Which child should not receive ibuprofen without provider guidance?","a":"A child with severe vomiting and signs of dehydration","w":["A child with a fever and earache","A child with a sprained ankle","A child with teething discomfort"],"x":"NSAIDs reduce blood flow to the kidneys and can cause kidney injury in dehydrated children. The other children are common, appropriate uses.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp549c1901","lv":"paramedic","d":"albuterol","sys":"c:peds","q":"A child in severe asthma has diminished breath sounds and no audible wheezing after albuterol. How should this be interpreted?","a":"Worsening obstruction with very little air movement","w":["Full recovery from the asthma attack","An expected side effect of albuterol","A sign the child is ready for discharge"],"x":"A quiet or silent chest in severe asthma means too little air is moving to produce wheezing, which is ominous. Improvement would include better air movement and less work of breathing.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp63577e69","lv":"paramedic","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"c:icu","q":"During transport of a patient with norepinephrine running through a peripheral IV, what is essential?","a":"Frequent checks of the IV site for infiltration","w":["Flushing the line hourly to keep it open","Hanging it on the same line as the antibiotic","Checking the site only at arrival"],"x":"Peripheral norepinephrine is acceptable short term in a large, proximal vein, but extravasation can cause tissue necrosis, so the site must be checked often. Sharing or flushing the line risks boluses.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe3b74e31","lv":"paramedic","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"c:icu","q":"Which finding suggests excessive vasoconstriction from a norepinephrine infusion?","a":"Cold, mottled fingers and toes with poor perfusion","w":["Warm, flushed skin and bounding pulses","Low blood pressure with a slow heart rate","Increased urine output and thirst"],"x":"Intense alpha-1 vasoconstriction can reduce blood flow to the extremities and organs, causing cool, mottled skin. Warm, flushed skin suggests vasodilation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp38cdda8c","lv":"paramedic","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"c:icu","q":"Why should a norepinephrine infusion run through a dedicated line that is not flushed?","a":"Flushing would deliver an unintended bolus and spike the blood pressure","w":["Flushing would dilute it and make it inactive","Flushing would cause the drug to crystallize","Flushing would cause a drop in heart rate"],"x":"Norepinephrine is potent and short acting, so pushing the drug remaining in the line can cause a sudden rise in blood pressure. A dedicated line prevents unplanned boluses.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1825eb6f","lv":"paramedic","d":"fentanyl","sys":"c:icu","q":"Why may a fentanyl infusion have a longer effect than expected after several days?","a":"It is fat soluble and accumulates in tissues during prolonged infusion","w":["It is converted into a long-acting toxic metabolite","It is excreted unchanged only by the lungs","It loses potency the longer it is infused"],"x":"Fentanyl is highly lipophilic, so it builds up in fat and muscle and is slowly released, prolonging its effect. Its short duration applies to single doses.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9d1d9216","lv":"paramedic","d":"fentanyl","sys":"c:icu","q":"An intubated patient is fighting the ventilator. Why does an analgesia-first approach often start with fentanyl?","a":"Pain often drives agitation, and treating it may reduce sedative needs","w":["Fentanyl paralyzes the patient so they cannot fight","Fentanyl has no effect on breathing in ventilated patients","Fentanyl prevents delirium completely"],"x":"Many ventilated patients are agitated from pain, so treating pain first can reduce the need for deeper sedation. Fentanyl does not cause paralysis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5b533901","lv":"paramedic","d":"midazolam","sys":"c:icu","q":"Why may a patient with kidney failure remain sedated long after a midazolam infusion ends?","a":"An active metabolite builds up when kidney function is poor","w":["Midazolam is not removed by the liver at all","Kidney failure makes midazolam more water soluble","Midazolam converts into an opioid in kidney failure"],"x":"Midazolam's active metabolite is cleared by the kidneys and accumulates in renal failure, prolonging sedation. Prolonged infusion also causes accumulation in fat.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd17bbbd0","lv":"paramedic","d":"midazolam","sys":"c:icu","q":"What concern is linked to prolonged benzodiazepine sedation such as a midazolam infusion in critically ill patients?","a":"Increased risk of delirium and longer time on the ventilator","w":["Increased risk of high blood pressure","Increased risk of hypoglycemia","Increased risk of excessive alertness"],"x":"Critical care guidelines prefer non-benzodiazepine sedation where possible because benzodiazepines are associated with delirium and longer ventilation. They do not cause hypertension.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp01a5c27b","lv":"paramedic","d":"midazolam","sys":"c:icu","q":"How should the depth of sedation be assessed during a midazolam infusion?","a":"Use a validated sedation scale such as the RASS","w":["Rely on the heart rate alone","Check pupil size only","Assume sedation if the patient is quiet"],"x":"Validated scales such as the Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale give an objective measure to avoid under- or over-sedation. Heart rate and pupils are unreliable sedation markers.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9b3d1822","lv":"paramedic","d":"ketamine","sys":"c:icu","q":"In which patient should ketamine be used with caution due to its cardiovascular effects?","a":"A patient with severe, uncontrolled hypertension or active ischemia","w":["A patient with septic shock and low blood pressure","A patient with asthma who needs sedation","A patient with a long bone fracture"],"x":"Ketamine's sympathomimetic effects raise heart rate and blood pressure, which can worsen severe hypertension or myocardial ischemia. In hypotension or asthma these effects can be helpful.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb5fde404","lv":"paramedic","d":"ketamine","sys":"c:icu","q":"Which effect of ketamine may require more frequent suctioning?","a":"Increased salivation and airway secretions","w":["Dry mouth and thick mucus","Decreased gag reflex","Bronchospasm"],"x":"Ketamine increases salivary and airway secretions, which may need suctioning. It tends to relax, not tighten, bronchial smooth muscle.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc0680a8e","lv":"rn","d":"digoxin","sys":"cardio","q":"Before giving a scheduled dose of digoxin, which assessment is most important for the nurse to perform?","a":"Count the apical pulse for one full minute","w":["Check the radial pulse for fifteen seconds","Measure blood pressure in both arms","Auscultate bowel sounds in all four quadrants"],"x":"Digoxin slows conduction through the AV node, so a full apical count is needed to detect bradycardia before giving it. A brief radial check can miss irregular beats and undercount the rate.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf22ec7cb","lv":"rn","d":"digoxin","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient taking digoxin and furosemide reports nausea, poor appetite, and yellow-green halos around lights. What does the nurse recognize?","a":"Possible digoxin toxicity, which low potassium can worsen","w":["Expected adjustment effects of digoxin therapy","An allergic reaction to furosemide","Dehydration from drinking too much water"],"x":"GI upset and visual changes are classic signs of digoxin toxicity, and loop diuretics can lower potassium, which makes toxicity more likely. These are not expected effects and should be reported along with a digoxin level and potassium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9b880123","lv":"rn","d":"amiodarone","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient is on long-term amiodarone. Which set of tests does the nurse expect to be monitored periodically?","a":"Thyroid, liver, and lung function tests","w":["Amylase, lipase, and uric acid levels","Hemoglobin A1c, lipase, and bone density","Creatine kinase, ammonia, and lactate levels"],"x":"Amiodarone can cause thyroid dysfunction, liver injury, and pulmonary toxicity, so these organs are monitored over time. The other panels do not target its known organ toxicities.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa2e2515d","lv":"rn","d":"metoprolol","sys":"cardio","q":"Before a scheduled dose of oral metoprolol, the nurse finds a heart rate well below the patient's usual rate and the patient reports dizziness. What is the priority action?","a":"Notify the provider before giving the dose","w":["Give the dose and recheck vital signs later","Give the dose with a full glass of water","Have the patient walk to assess tolerance"],"x":"Beta blockers slow the heart rate, and symptomatic bradycardia needs provider review before more drug is given. Giving the dose and rechecking later could worsen the bradycardia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpeaeee13b","lv":"rn","d":"lisinopril","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient taking lisinopril develops sudden swelling of the lips and tongue. What does the nurse recognize?","a":"Angioedema, a possible airway emergency","w":["Expected first-dose low blood pressure","A mild contact allergy to a food","Fluid overload from heart failure"],"x":"ACE inhibitors can cause angioedema at any time during therapy, and tongue or throat swelling can block the airway. It is not a first-dose blood pressure effect and needs urgent airway assessment and provider notification.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa9d8b239","lv":"rn","d":"furosemide","sys":"cardio","q":"Which laboratory value is the nurse's priority to monitor in a patient receiving furosemide?","a":"Serum potassium, for hypokalemia","w":["Serum calcium, for hypercalcemia","Serum sodium, for hypernatremia","Blood glucose, for hypoglycemia"],"x":"Loop diuretics cause loss of potassium, and low potassium can trigger dangerous dysrhythmias. Furosemide tends to lower, not raise, sodium and calcium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6b7847e3","lv":"rn","d":"nitroglycerin","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient prescribed sublingual nitroglycerin for angina asks about other medicines. Which medicine should the nurse teach the patient to avoid combining with it?","a":"Sildenafil or other PDE-5 inhibitors","w":["Acetaminophen for headaches","Aspirin for heart protection","Atorvastatin for cholesterol"],"x":"Nitrates combined with PDE-5 inhibitors such as sildenafil can cause severe, life-threatening hypotension. Aspirin is commonly used together with nitroglycerin in coronary disease.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp57000a69","lv":"rn","d":"spironolactone","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient takes spironolactone and lisinopril. Which finding should the nurse report to the provider?","a":"High serum potassium with muscle weakness","w":["Low serum potassium with leg cramps","A mild increase in daytime urination","Mild breast tenderness that is unchanged"],"x":"Spironolactone is potassium-sparing, and ACE inhibitors also raise potassium, so hyperkalemia is the key risk. Low potassium is the concern with loop and thiazide diuretics, not this combination.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1515aa29","lv":"rn","d":"diltiazem","sys":"cardio","q":"A patient with atrial fibrillation and a rapid ventricular rate is receiving IV diltiazem. Which monitoring is the priority?","a":"Continuous heart rhythm and frequent blood pressure","w":["Hourly bedside blood glucose checks","Daily serum digoxin level","Strict intake and output only"],"x":"IV diltiazem slows AV conduction and dilates vessels, so bradycardia, heart block, and hypotension can develop quickly. Intake and output alone would miss these changes.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc60ac992","lv":"rn","d":"adenosine","sys":"cardio","q":"The nurse is preparing a patient for IV adenosine to treat SVT. Which expected effect should the nurse explain?","a":"A few seconds of very slow heartbeat with flushing or chest pressure","w":["Drowsiness that lasts for several hours","A sustained rise in blood pressure","Gradual slowing of the heart over a day"],"x":"Adenosine is given by rapid IV push with a saline flush and briefly blocks the AV node, often causing a few seconds of very slow or absent heartbeat, flushing, or chest discomfort. Its effects last seconds, not hours.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8338d7a3","lv":"rn","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"During a peripheral norepinephrine infusion, the nurse notes blanching, coolness, and swelling at the IV site. Which treatment should the nurse anticipate?","a":"Phentolamine infiltrated into the affected area","w":["Protamine given by IV push","Naloxone given intramuscularly","Vitamin K given subcutaneously"],"x":"Norepinephrine extravasation causes intense local vasoconstriction, and phentolamine, an alpha blocker, is infiltrated into the area to limit tissue damage. Protamine reverses heparin and has no role here.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf7c597d7","lv":"rn","d":"norepinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"Which route is preferred for a continuous norepinephrine infusion whenever possible?","a":"A central venous catheter","w":["A small vein in the back of the hand","An intramuscular injection site","A subcutaneous infusion site"],"x":"A central line lowers the risk of tissue injury from extravasation of this potent vasoconstrictor. Small hand veins carry the highest risk of infiltration.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp955302eb","lv":"rn","d":"epinephrine","sys":"shock","q":"A patient in anaphylactic shock needs epinephrine right away and has no IV access. Which route is recommended?","a":"Intramuscular injection into the outer thigh","w":["Subcutaneous injection into the upper arm","Undiluted IV push through a new line","A dissolving tablet under the tongue"],"x":"Intramuscular injection into the anterolateral thigh gives faster, more reliable absorption than subcutaneous injection. Undiluted IV push epinephrine can cause dangerous dysrhythmias.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7dddd792","lv":"rn","d":"dopamine","sys":"shock","q":"A patient receiving a dopamine infusion develops a new rapid, irregular heart rhythm. How does the nurse interpret this finding?","a":"A known adverse effect that needs prompt reporting","w":["An expected sign that the drug is working","A sign of low blood volume only","An allergic reaction to the IV diluent"],"x":"Dopamine stimulates beta-1 receptors and can cause tachydysrhythmias, which should be reported promptly. A new dysrhythmia is not a marker of therapeutic effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe4ffc3ca","lv":"rn","d":"dobutamine","sys":"shock","q":"Which statement best describes how dobutamine helps a patient in cardiogenic shock?","a":"It increases heart contractility through beta-1 stimulation","w":["It constricts arteries through alpha-1 stimulation","It blocks calcium channels in vascular muscle","It inhibits phosphodiesterase-3 in heart muscle"],"x":"Dobutamine is mainly a beta-1 agonist that strengthens contraction and raises cardiac output. Phosphodiesterase-3 inhibition describes milrinone, another inotrope.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp086c3d66","lv":"rn","d":"phenylephrine","sys":"shock","q":"A patient receives IV phenylephrine for hypotension. Which change in vital signs should the nurse watch for as blood pressure rises?","a":"Reflex bradycardia","w":["Marked tachycardia","Rising respiratory rate","Falling body temperature"],"x":"Phenylephrine is a pure alpha-1 agonist, and the rise in blood pressure can trigger a reflex slowing of the heart. It lacks the beta effects that would cause tachycardia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp69cf3b6c","lv":"rn","d":"vasopressin","sys":"shock","q":"How does vasopressin raise blood pressure in septic shock?","a":"It constricts blood vessels through V1 receptors","w":["It speeds the heart through beta-1 receptors","It dilates renal arteries through dopamine receptors","It boosts contractility by inhibiting PDE-3"],"x":"Vasopressin acts on V1 receptors in vascular smooth muscle, causing vasoconstriction without beta-adrenergic effects. It does not directly increase heart rate.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcae3c734","lv":"rn","d":"milrinone","sys":"shock","q":"A patient with decompensated heart failure is started on milrinone. Which assessment is the nurse's priority?","a":"Blood pressure and heart rhythm","w":["Blood glucose for hyperglycemia","Respiratory rate for depression","Temperature for malignant hyperthermia"],"x":"Milrinone is an inotrope and vasodilator, so hypotension and ventricular dysrhythmias are the main risks. It does not cause respiratory depression.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp89af2101","lv":"rn","d":"midodrine","sys":"shock","q":"Which teaching is appropriate for a patient taking midodrine for orthostatic hypotension?","a":"Take doses during daytime hours, not close to bedtime","w":["Take the last dose at bedtime for overnight effect","Lie flat for an hour after each dose","Take each dose only while lying down"],"x":"Midodrine can cause supine hypertension, so doses are taken while upright during the day and not near bedtime. Lying flat after a dose increases that risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp95b8efdc","lv":"rn","d":"droxidopa","sys":"shock","q":"Which adverse effect is the main safety concern with droxidopa, and what does the nurse teach?","a":"Supine hypertension; rest with the head of the bed raised","w":["Hypoglycemia; carry a fast-acting sugar source","Bradycardia; check the pulse before standing","Hyperkalemia; avoid salt substitutes"],"x":"Droxidopa carries a boxed warning for supine hypertension, so patients sleep with the head of the bed elevated and supine blood pressure is monitored. Hypoglycemia is not a known effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3fe377d1","lv":"rn","d":"warfarin","sys":"blood","q":"Which laboratory test does the nurse use to evaluate the therapeutic effect of warfarin?","a":"International normalized ratio (INR)","w":["Activated partial thromboplastin time","Anti-factor Xa level","Platelet count"],"x":"Warfarin is monitored with the INR, which reflects its effect on vitamin K-dependent clotting factors. The aPTT is used for unfractionated heparin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpafd55ff1","lv":"rn","d":"warfarin","sys":"blood","q":"Which dietary teaching is appropriate for a patient starting warfarin?","a":"Keep intake of leafy green vegetables consistent","w":["Avoid all green vegetables completely","Eat extra leafy greens when bruising occurs","Take a vitamin K supplement every morning"],"x":"Vitamin K in leafy greens counters warfarin, so a steady intake keeps the INR stable. Cutting out greens entirely is unnecessary and can make the INR swing when intake changes.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6a04d6b2","lv":"rn","d":"heparin","sys":"blood","q":"Several days after starting heparin, a patient's platelet count has dropped by more than half and a new clot is suspected. What does the nurse recognize?","a":"Possible heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","w":["Expected dilution from IV fluids","Vitamin K deficiency bleeding","Warfarin-induced skin necrosis"],"x":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia is an immune reaction that lowers platelets and paradoxically causes clotting, and it must be reported right away. Dilution would not explain a large drop with new thrombosis.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4016924a","lv":"rn","d":"heparin","sys":"blood","q":"Which laboratory test is commonly used to monitor a continuous IV infusion of unfractionated heparin?","a":"Activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT)","w":["International normalized ratio (INR)","Serum fibrinogen level","Bleeding time"],"x":"Unfractionated heparin is monitored with the aPTT (or an anti-Xa level by facility protocol) to keep it in the therapeutic range. The INR is used for warfarin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp90d34964","lv":"rn","d":"enoxaparin","sys":"blood","q":"Which technique is correct when giving enoxaparin from a prefilled syringe?","a":"Inject subcutaneously in the abdomen without expelling the air bubble","w":["Inject intramuscularly into the deltoid muscle","Rub the site firmly afterward to reduce bruising","Expel the air bubble, then inject into the thigh muscle"],"x":"Enoxaparin is given by deep subcutaneous injection, usually in the abdomen, and the air bubble in the prefilled syringe is not expelled. Rubbing the site increases bruising.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe466db52","lv":"rn","d":"apixaban","sys":"blood","q":"Which statement by a patient taking apixaban shows correct understanding?","a":"\"I will report black stools or unusual bruising.\"","w":["\"I need weekly INR checks to adjust my dose.\"","\"I will double my next dose if I miss one.\"","\"I can take ibuprofen freely for pain.\""],"x":"Bleeding is the main risk with apixaban, so signs like black stools or easy bruising should be reported. Apixaban does not need routine INR monitoring, unlike warfarin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe28fe0dd","lv":"rn","d":"alteplase","sys":"blood","q":"A patient with acute ischemic stroke symptoms is being considered for alteplase. Which finding must be confirmed before it is given?","a":"A head CT showing no bleeding in the brain","w":["A normal lipid panel","A recent urine culture result","That the patient has been fasting"],"x":"Alteplase dissolves clots and is contraindicated in intracranial hemorrhage, so imaging must rule out bleeding first. A lipid panel has no bearing on eligibility.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc463b486","lv":"rn","d":"protamine","sys":"blood","q":"While giving protamine to reverse heparin, which adverse effects should the nurse watch for?","a":"Low blood pressure, slow heart rate, and allergic reaction","w":["High blood pressure and seizures","High potassium and peaked T waves","Low blood glucose and sweating"],"x":"Protamine can cause hypotension, bradycardia, and anaphylaxis-like reactions, especially if given too fast. It does not cause hypertension or seizures.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7ffebc07","lv":"rn","d":"epoetin-alfa","sys":"blood","q":"A patient with chronic kidney disease receives epoetin alfa. Which finding should the nurse report?","a":"A rapid rise in hemoglobin or new high blood pressure","w":["A gradual rise in reticulocyte count","Mild tenderness at the injection site","Improved energy over several weeks"],"x":"Epoetin raises the risk of hypertension, clots, and stroke, especially when hemoglobin rises quickly or too high. A rising reticulocyte count is an expected sign it is working.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa5b8d860","lv":"rn","d":"clopidogrel","sys":"blood","q":"Which medicine can reduce the antiplatelet effect of clopidogrel and is generally avoided with it?","a":"Omeprazole","w":["Acetaminophen","Levothyroxine","Metformin"],"x":"Omeprazole inhibits CYP2C19, the enzyme that activates clopidogrel, which can reduce its effect. Acetaminophen has no meaningful interaction with clopidogrel.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1ca75a36","lv":"rn","d":"succinylcholine","sys":"airway","q":"Which patient is at the highest risk for dangerous hyperkalemia if given succinylcholine?","a":"A patient with a severe burn injury several days ago","w":["A patient with mild dehydration","A patient with well-controlled asthma","A patient who takes a daily multivitamin"],"x":"After major burns, crush injuries, or denervation, extra receptors on muscle allow succinylcholine to release large amounts of potassium. Mild dehydration does not create this risk.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp72a9b42b","lv":"rn","d":"succinylcholine","sys":"airway","q":"Shortly after succinylcholine is given, the patient develops jaw rigidity, rising end-tidal carbon dioxide, and a fast heart rate. What does the nurse recognize?","a":"Malignant hyperthermia","w":["Expected fasciculations","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Latex anaphylaxis"],"x":"Masseter rigidity with rising end-tidal CO2 and tachycardia are early signs of malignant hyperthermia, which is treated with dantrolene. Fasciculations are brief twitches, not sustained rigidity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5cb77896","lv":"rn","d":"rocuronium","sys":"airway","q":"After intubation, a patient is receiving rocuronium. Which nursing priority applies?","a":"Ensure adequate sedation and pain control, since it provides neither","w":["Reduce sedation, since rocuronium causes deep sleep","Expect the patient to breathe well without support","Ask the patient to rate pain on a numeric scale"],"x":"Neuromuscular blockers paralyze muscles but do not affect awareness or pain, so sedation and analgesia must be given. A paralyzed patient cannot breathe or report pain.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpabcf3740","lv":"rn","d":"sugammadex","sys":"airway","q":"How does sugammadex reverse neuromuscular blockade?","a":"It binds and encapsulates rocuronium or vecuronium","w":["It inhibits acetylcholinesterase at the synapse","It blocks nicotinic receptors on muscle","It stimulates muscarinic receptors in the heart"],"x":"Sugammadex forms a tight complex with rocuronium or vecuronium, removing it from the junction. Acetylcholinesterase inhibition is how neostigmine works.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpece32cbb","lv":"rn","d":"sugammadex","sys":"airway","q":"A patient who takes oral hormonal contraceptives received sugammadex during surgery. Which teaching is appropriate?","a":"Use a backup nonhormonal method for the next week","w":["No change is needed; contraceptives are unaffected","Take two contraceptive pills the next day","Skip the next week of contraceptive pills"],"x":"Sugammadex can bind progestogen and lower contraceptive effect, so labeling advises a backup method for seven days. It is not true that contraceptives are unaffected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe3e76727","lv":"rn","d":"etomidate","sys":"airway","q":"Which adverse effect is associated with etomidate used for rapid sequence intubation?","a":"Temporary adrenal suppression","w":["Severe hypertension","Histamine-related bronchospasm","Malignant hyperthermia"],"x":"Etomidate briefly inhibits cortisol production, a concern in critically ill or septic patients. It is not a trigger for malignant hyperthermia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb2e345e1","lv":"rn","d":"ketamine","sys":"airway","q":"Why may ketamine be chosen for induction in a patient with low blood pressure?","a":"It tends to maintain blood pressure and breathing drive","w":["It lowers heart rate and blood pressure","It reliably abolishes all airway reflexes","It has a specific reversal agent"],"x":"Ketamine's sympathetic stimulation usually supports blood pressure and heart rate while preserving respiratory drive. It has no specific reversal agent.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcef7968e","lv":"rn","d":"propofol","sys":"airway","q":"Which handling practice is important when giving propofol?","a":"Use strict aseptic technique, since the lipid supports bacterial growth","w":["Shake the vial and give it intramuscularly","Mix it with other drugs in the same syringe","Save an opened vial in the refrigerator for tomorrow"],"x":"Propofol is a lipid emulsion that can grow bacteria, so aseptic technique and timely disposal of opened product are required. It is given IV, not intramuscularly.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9e730571","lv":"rn","d":"midazolam","sys":"airway","q":"A patient becomes oversedated after midazolam. Which reversal agent should the nurse anticipate?","a":"Flumazenil","w":["Naloxone","Sugammadex","Neostigmine"],"x":"Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines such as midazolam. Naloxone reverses opioids, not benzodiazepines.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp13b9b099","lv":"rn","d":"fentanyl","sys":"airway","q":"After a rapid IV push of fentanyl, ventilation becomes difficult because the chest feels stiff. What does the nurse recognize?","a":"Opioid-induced chest wall rigidity","w":["A severe hypertensive reaction","Malignant hyperthermia","Hyperkalemia from muscle injury"],"x":"Rapid, large IV doses of potent opioids like fentanyl can cause chest wall rigidity that impairs ventilation. Fentanyl is not a malignant hyperthermia trigger.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp799f71bc","lv":"rn","d":"albuterol","sys":"resp","q":"Which side effects should the nurse teach a patient to expect after using albuterol?","a":"Tremor, nervousness, and a fast heart rate","w":["Drowsiness and a slow heart rate","Oral thrush and hoarseness","Constipation and dry eyes"],"x":"Albuterol is a beta-2 agonist, and some beta stimulation causes tremor and tachycardia. Thrush and hoarseness are linked to inhaled corticosteroids.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp60a00e77","lv":"rn","d":"fluticasone","sys":"resp","q":"Which teaching is appropriate for a patient using an inhaled fluticasone inhaler?","a":"Rinse the mouth and spit after each use","w":["Use it first for sudden wheezing","Swallow the rinse water after each use","Use it only on days with symptoms"],"x":"Rinsing and spitting lowers the risk of oral thrush from inhaled corticosteroids. Fluticasone is a daily controller and does not relieve sudden wheezing.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb47cbc9a","lv":"rn","d":"salmeterol","sys":"resp","q":"Which teaching is correct about salmeterol?","a":"It is not used to relieve a sudden asthma attack","w":["It works within seconds for acute wheezing","It can be used alone without a controller in asthma","It may be used several times every hour as needed"],"x":"Salmeterol is a long-acting beta agonist with slow onset for maintenance, not rescue. In asthma it should be paired with an inhaled corticosteroid, never used alone.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp942ecdab","lv":"rn","d":"tiotropium","sys":"resp","q":"Which history should the nurse report before a patient starts tiotropium?","a":"Narrow-angle glaucoma or urinary retention","w":["Seasonal allergic rhinitis","Osteoarthritis of the knees","Treated hypothyroidism"],"x":"Tiotropium is an anticholinergic that can worsen narrow-angle glaucoma and urinary retention. Seasonal allergies are not a concern.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe4ec26d5","lv":"rn","d":"montelukast","sys":"resp","q":"Which effect should the nurse teach patients and families to report while taking montelukast?","a":"Mood changes, agitation, or suicidal thoughts","w":["Black, tarry stools","Yellow-green halos around lights","Gum overgrowth"],"x":"Montelukast has a boxed warning for serious neuropsychiatric events. Yellow-green halos are a sign of digoxin toxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa1ba0b3a","lv":"rn","d":"theophylline","sys":"resp","q":"A patient on theophylline starts ciprofloxacin and then reports nausea, restlessness, and a racing heart. What does the nurse recognize?","a":"Possible theophylline toxicity from a drug interaction","w":["Expected adjustment to the antibiotic","An allergic reaction to ciprofloxacin","Worsening asthma control"],"x":"Ciprofloxacin slows theophylline clearance, and theophylline has a narrow therapeutic range, so toxicity can follow. A theophylline level should be checked and the provider notified.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp31154851","lv":"rn","d":"prednisone","sys":"resp","q":"A patient with diabetes is started on a short course of prednisone for an asthma flare. Which change should the nurse anticipate?","a":"Higher blood glucose levels","w":["Lower blood glucose levels","Higher serum potassium","Lower white blood cell count"],"x":"Corticosteroids raise blood glucose, so closer monitoring is needed in diabetes. Prednisone tends to lower potassium and raise the white cell count.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp09b46de4","lv":"rn","d":"oseltamivir","sys":"resp","q":"When is oseltamivir treatment most effective for influenza?","a":"When started within two days of symptom onset","w":["Only after a fever has lasted a full week","After a culture confirms flu several days later","Only if pneumonia has already developed"],"x":"Oseltamivir works best when started within 48 hours of symptoms, though it may still be used later for hospitalized or high-risk patients. Waiting for culture results delays benefit.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp132f7d99","lv":"rn","d":"benzonatate","sys":"resp","q":"Which teaching is essential for a patient prescribed benzonatate capsules?","a":"Swallow capsules whole without chewing or sucking","w":["Chew the capsules for faster relief","Open the capsule and mix it in applesauce","Let the capsule dissolve under the tongue"],"x":"Releasing benzonatate in the mouth numbs the throat and can cause choking or severe reactions. It should also be stored away from children, for whom accidental ingestion can be fatal.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp01794234","lv":"rn","d":"pseudoephedrine","sys":"resp","q":"Which history should the nurse report before a patient takes pseudoephedrine for congestion?","a":"Uncontrolled high blood pressure","w":["Seasonal allergies","Mild osteoarthritis","Iron deficiency"],"x":"Pseudoephedrine is a sympathomimetic decongestant that can raise blood pressure and heart rate. Seasonal allergies are a common reason it is used.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp376f3d6e","lv":"rn","d":"phenytoin","sys":"neuro","q":"Which IV fluid is compatible for flushing before and after IV phenytoin?","a":"Normal saline","w":["Dextrose in water","Lactated Ringer's with dextrose","Any fluid already running in the line"],"x":"Phenytoin precipitates in dextrose solutions, so the line is flushed with normal saline. It should not be mixed with other fluids or drugs.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe3196e51","lv":"rn","d":"phenytoin","sys":"neuro","q":"Which teaching helps a patient manage a common long-term effect of phenytoin?","a":"Brush and floss carefully and see a dentist regularly","w":["Expect hair thinning and use a mild shampoo","Avoid all dairy products to protect the liver","Take it with grapefruit juice to improve absorption"],"x":"Phenytoin causes gingival hyperplasia, and good oral hygiene reduces it. Phenytoin levels are also monitored by name since it has a narrow therapeutic range.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp848b729a","lv":"rn","d":"carbamazepine","sys":"neuro","q":"Which new symptom in a patient taking carbamazepine should be reported right away?","a":"Skin rash with blisters, fever, or mouth sores","w":["Mild drowsiness during the first days","Mild nausea relieved by taking with food","Slight dizziness that improves with time"],"x":"Carbamazepine can cause Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis, which need urgent attention. Mild early drowsiness is common and usually fades.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp99700649","lv":"rn","d":"levetiracetam","sys":"neuro","q":"Which effect should the nurse teach a patient and family to watch for with levetiracetam?","a":"Irritability, mood changes, or thoughts of self-harm","w":["Overgrowth of the gums","Excess facial and body hair","Yellowing of the skin and eyes"],"x":"Levetiracetam is linked to behavioral changes, and antiepileptics carry a warning for suicidal thoughts. Gum overgrowth is linked to phenytoin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0ee66a4b","lv":"rn","d":"lamotrigine","sys":"neuro","q":"Which instruction is most important for a patient starting lamotrigine?","a":"Report any new rash, since it may signal a serious skin reaction","w":["Expect weight gain during the first month","Have gum checks for overgrowth","Expect a fine hand tremor as it starts working"],"x":"Lamotrigine has a boxed warning for serious rashes, including Stevens-Johnson syndrome, especially early in therapy. Gum overgrowth is a phenytoin effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfdf2a7a5","lv":"rn","d":"valproic-acid","sys":"neuro","q":"Which laboratory tests does the nurse expect to be monitored for a patient taking valproic acid?","a":"Liver function tests and platelet count","w":["Thyroid panel and lipid panel","Serum lithium and sodium","Serum magnesium and phosphate"],"x":"Valproic acid can cause hepatotoxicity and thrombocytopenia, so liver tests and platelets are checked, along with valproic acid levels. A lithium level is unrelated.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa0f9789b","lv":"rn","d":"lorazepam","sys":"neuro","q":"After IV lorazepam is given for status epilepticus, which assessment is the nurse's priority?","a":"Respiratory rate and oxygen saturation","w":["Blood pressure for hypertension","Hourly bedside blood glucose","Bladder for urinary retention"],"x":"Benzodiazepines can depress breathing, especially after repeated doses, so airway and breathing are monitored closely. Hypertension is not a typical effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5af4af1d","lv":"rn","d":"carbidopa-levodopa","sys":"neuro","q":"Which teaching is appropriate for a patient taking carbidopa-levodopa?","a":"Large high-protein meals may reduce how well it works","w":["High-protein shakes boost its effect","Its full effect is felt minutes after the first dose","It works best when taken with iron supplements"],"x":"Dietary protein competes with levodopa for absorption and transport into the brain, so large protein loads near doses can blunt its effect. Iron also reduces levodopa absorption.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp48f2b178","lv":"rn","d":"sumatriptan","sys":"neuro","q":"Which history is a contraindication to sumatriptan?","a":"Coronary artery disease","w":["Seasonal allergies","Iron deficiency anemia","Osteoarthritis"],"x":"Triptans cause vasoconstriction and are contraindicated in coronary artery disease and uncontrolled hypertension. Seasonal allergies do not affect triptan use.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp11f3d2e2","lv":"rn","d":"donepezil","sys":"neuro","q":"Which finding should the nurse report in a patient taking donepezil?","a":"Slow heart rate or fainting","w":["Dry mouth and constipation","Increased appetite","Thinning hair"],"x":"As a cholinesterase inhibitor, donepezil increases vagal tone and can cause bradycardia and syncope. Dry mouth and constipation are anticholinergic effects, the opposite of its action.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6da4cb30","lv":"rn","d":"morphine","sys":"pain","q":"Before giving IV morphine, which assessment is most important?","a":"Respiratory rate and level of sedation","w":["Presence of bowel sounds","Pupil response to light only","Oral temperature"],"x":"Respiratory depression is the most dangerous opioid effect, and increasing sedation usually comes before it. Bowel sounds matter for constipation but are not the priority.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp282d89aa","lv":"rn","d":"naloxone","sys":"pain","q":"After naloxone reverses an opioid overdose, what must the nurse continue to monitor for?","a":"Return of respiratory depression as naloxone wears off","w":["Persistent hypertensive crisis","Severe hypoglycemia","Prolonged muscle paralysis"],"x":"Naloxone often wears off before longer-acting opioids, so sedation and slow breathing can return. Repeat doses may be needed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp98cde102","lv":"rn","d":"acetaminophen","sys":"pain","q":"Which teaching helps prevent accidental acetaminophen overdose?","a":"Check cold and sleep products for hidden acetaminophen","w":["Take it with alcohol to improve its effect","Any amount is safe because it is sold over the counter","Combine it freely with other pain relievers"],"x":"Many combination products contain acetaminophen, and taking several at once can cause liver injury. Over-the-counter status does not make any amount safe.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc9402633","lv":"rn","d":"ketorolac","sys":"pain","q":"Which finding should the nurse report in a patient receiving ketorolac?","a":"Decreased urine output or black stools","w":["Mild drowsiness after a dose","Improved pain scores","Slight warmth at the injection site"],"x":"Ketorolac is a potent NSAID with boxed warnings for kidney injury and GI bleeding, and its total course is limited. Improved pain is the intended effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd2d40b3e","lv":"rn","d":"fentanyl","sys":"pain","q":"Which teaching is essential for a patient using a fentanyl transdermal patch?","a":"Avoid heating pads, hot tubs, and direct heat over the patch","w":["Cut the patch in half for a smaller effect","Apply each new patch to the same spot","Cover the patch with a heating pad at night"],"x":"Heat increases fentanyl absorption from the patch and can cause a fatal overdose. Cutting the patch damages the release system.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd2c78d41","lv":"rn","d":"hydromorphone","sys":"pain","q":"Why is hydromorphone considered a high-alert medicine?","a":"It is far more potent than morphine, so mix-ups can cause overdose","w":["It has no reversal agent","It causes severe hypertension","It is given only by the intrathecal route"],"x":"Hydromorphone is several times more potent than morphine, and look-alike names and products have led to fatal errors. Naloxone does reverse it.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp40285cc5","lv":"rn","d":"methadone","sys":"pain","q":"Which adverse effect requires ECG monitoring in a patient taking methadone?","a":"QT prolongation and dysrhythmias","w":["Hyperkalemia","Gum overgrowth","Malignant hyperthermia"],"x":"Methadone can prolong the QT interval and cause torsades de pointes. It does not cause hyperkalemia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp96c5f3b3","lv":"rn","d":"ibuprofen","sys":"pain","q":"A patient taking warfarin asks about using ibuprofen for back pain. Which response is accurate?","a":"It increases bleeding risk when combined with warfarin","w":["It lowers the INR and makes warfarin weaker","It has no interaction with warfarin","It speeds up clotting and raises clot risk"],"x":"NSAIDs impair platelet function and irritate the GI tract, adding to warfarin's bleeding risk. They do not make warfarin weaker.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9b70b098","lv":"rn","d":"dexmedetomidine","sys":"pain","q":"Which adverse effects should the nurse monitor for during a dexmedetomidine infusion?","a":"Bradycardia and hypotension","w":["Hyperthermia and muscle rigidity","Hyperkalemia and peaked T waves","Severe hypertension and fast heart rate"],"x":"Dexmedetomidine is an alpha-2 agonist that lowers sympathetic tone, so bradycardia and hypotension are its most common effects. It usually causes little respiratory depression.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc1ba6915","lv":"rn","d":"tramadol","sys":"pain","q":"Which interaction is a concern when tramadol is given to a patient taking an SSRI?","a":"Serotonin syndrome","w":["Hypoglycemia","Hyperkalemia","Neutropenia"],"x":"Tramadol has serotonergic activity, and combining it with SSRIs raises the risk of serotonin syndrome and seizures. Hyperkalemia is not linked to this combination.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5f4b1c40","lv":"rn","d":"lithium","sys":"psych","q":"A patient taking lithium has vomiting, diarrhea, coarse hand tremor, and confusion. What does the nurse recognize?","a":"Possible lithium toxicity needing a lithium level","w":["Expected side effects that will pass","Anxiety that needs a benzodiazepine","Food poisoning unrelated to lithium"],"x":"GI symptoms, coarse tremor, and confusion are signs of lithium toxicity, which has a narrow therapeutic range. A fine tremor can be expected, but a coarse tremor with confusion is not.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpeb4be38f","lv":"rn","d":"lithium","sys":"psych","q":"Which teaching helps keep lithium levels stable?","a":"Keep salt and fluid intake consistent day to day","w":["Follow a very low-salt diet","Limit fluids to reduce urination","Use ibuprofen freely for headaches"],"x":"Low sodium or dehydration causes the kidneys to retain lithium and raises levels. NSAIDs such as ibuprofen also raise lithium levels.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpbd629a29","lv":"rn","d":"clozapine","sys":"psych","q":"Which symptom should a patient taking clozapine report right away?","a":"Fever, sore throat, or flu-like symptoms","w":["Mild weight gain over months","Drowsiness after evening doses","Increased saliva at night"],"x":"Clozapine can cause severe neutropenia, so signs of infection need urgent evaluation with an ANC. Weight gain and drooling are common but not emergencies.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp560271b2","lv":"rn","d":"haloperidol","sys":"psych","q":"A patient on haloperidol develops high fever, rigid muscles, confusion, and unstable blood pressure. What does the nurse recognize?","a":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","w":["Tardive dyskinesia","Acute dystonia","Akathisia"],"x":"Fever, lead-pipe rigidity, altered mental status, and autonomic instability define neuroleptic malignant syndrome, a medical emergency. Tardive dyskinesia causes involuntary movements without fever.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2c957058","lv":"rn","d":"sertraline","sys":"psych","q":"A young adult is starting sertraline for depression. Which monitoring is most important early in treatment?","a":"Watch for worsening depression or suicidal thoughts","w":["Check blood glucose before each dose","Monitor for gum overgrowth","Check a sertraline level weekly"],"x":"Antidepressants carry a boxed warning for increased suicidal thinking in children and young adults, especially early or after dose changes. Routine sertraline levels are not used.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp99ae173e","lv":"rn","d":"tranylcypromine","sys":"psych","q":"Which foods should a patient taking tranylcypromine avoid?","a":"Aged cheeses, cured meats, and tap beer","w":["Fresh chicken, rice, and apples","Plain yogurt, oatmeal, and bananas","Fresh fish, pasta, and green beans"],"x":"MAOIs block the breakdown of tyramine, and high-tyramine foods can trigger a hypertensive crisis. Fresh meats and fish are low in tyramine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf07f4b1b","lv":"rn","d":"olanzapine","sys":"psych","q":"Which monitoring is most important for a patient taking olanzapine long term?","a":"Weight, blood glucose, and lipid levels","w":["Thyroid function and calcium","Lithium level and sodium","Uric acid and creatine kinase"],"x":"Olanzapine commonly causes weight gain, hyperglycemia, and high lipids, so metabolic monitoring is routine. Lithium levels are unrelated.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp983e23ee","lv":"rn","d":"buprenorphine-naloxone","sys":"psych","q":"Which teaching is correct for a buprenorphine-naloxone sublingual film?","a":"Let the film dissolve fully under the tongue","w":["Swallow the film whole with water","Chew the film for a faster effect","Dissolve the film in juice and drink it"],"x":"The film is designed to be absorbed under the tongue, and swallowing or chewing it lowers absorption. Naloxone is included to discourage injection misuse.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc4fcbd25","lv":"rn","d":"disulfiram","sys":"psych","q":"Which teaching is essential for a patient taking disulfiram?","a":"Avoid hidden alcohol in mouthwash, cough syrup, and sauces","w":["Small amounts of beer are safe","Alcohol is fine once a week","Only hard liquor causes a reaction"],"x":"Even small amounts of alcohol, including in mouthwash or cooking, can cause flushing, vomiting, and a racing heart. Any form of alcohol can trigger the reaction.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc5d981e6","lv":"rn","d":"bupropion","sys":"psych","q":"Which history is a concern before a patient starts bupropion?","a":"An eating disorder such as bulimia","w":["Current cigarette smoking","Controlled high blood pressure","Seasonal allergic rhinitis"],"x":"Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold and is contraindicated in seizure disorders and in bulimia or anorexia nervosa. Smoking cessation is one of its uses.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb75664bf","lv":"rn","d":"potassium-chloride","sys":"renal","q":"Which practice is essential when giving IV potassium chloride?","a":"Give it only as a diluted infusion on a pump, never IV push","w":["Give it by slow IV push over a few seconds","Give it undiluted to save fluid volume","Hang it by gravity without a pump"],"x":"IV push or concentrated potassium can cause fatal cardiac arrest, so it is a high-alert medicine given diluted by infusion pump. Slow push is still unsafe.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3d45fd17","lv":"rn","d":"potassium-chloride","sys":"renal","q":"Before starting IV potassium replacement, which assessment is most important?","a":"Adequate urine output and kidney function","w":["Pupil size and reaction","Bedside blood glucose","Skin turgor on the forearm"],"x":"The kidneys excrete potassium, so poor urine output raises the risk of dangerous hyperkalemia. Pupil checks do not guide potassium safety.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5564ab14","lv":"rn","d":"patiromer","sys":"renal","q":"Which teaching is correct for a patient taking patiromer?","a":"Separate it from other oral medicines by a few hours","w":["Take it together with all other morning pills","Take it only when potassium is very low","Mix it with hot liquids before drinking"],"x":"Patiromer can bind other oral drugs, so labeling advises spacing other medicines by about three hours. It lowers, not raises, potassium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpcf0f15bf","lv":"rn","d":"calcium-gluconate","sys":"renal","q":"Why is IV calcium gluconate given to a patient with severe hyperkalemia and ECG changes?","a":"It stabilizes the heart muscle membrane against dysrhythmias","w":["It shifts potassium into the cells","It removes potassium from the body","It increases potassium loss in the urine"],"x":"Calcium protects the heart quickly but does not lower serum potassium. Insulin with dextrose shifts potassium into cells.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp54fef95e","lv":"rn","d":"regular-insulin","sys":"renal","q":"After IV regular insulin with dextrose is given for hyperkalemia, which assessment is the nurse's priority?","a":"Blood glucose, since hypoglycemia can follow","w":["Deep tendon reflexes for depression","Urine color for hematuria","Pupil size for constriction"],"x":"Insulin shifts potassium into cells, and hypoglycemia is a common complication even with dextrose. Frequent glucose checks are needed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf557a528","lv":"rn","d":"mannitol","sys":"renal","q":"Which practice is important when preparing an IV mannitol infusion?","a":"Inspect for crystals and use an in-line filter","w":["Mix it with blood products in the same line","Shake vigorously to dissolve any crystals","Give it as a rapid intramuscular injection"],"x":"Mannitol can form crystals, so it is inspected and given through a filter. It is not given intramuscularly.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4e6e73fd","lv":"rn","d":"hydrochlorothiazide","sys":"renal","q":"A patient with a history of gout is starting hydrochlorothiazide. Which effect should the nurse watch for?","a":"Raised uric acid that may trigger a gout flare","w":["Lowered blood glucose","Raised serum potassium","Lowered serum calcium"],"x":"Thiazides reduce uric acid excretion and can provoke gout. They tend to raise glucose and calcium and lower potassium.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp06d1470c","lv":"rn","d":"desmopressin","sys":"renal","q":"Which finding should the nurse report in a patient receiving desmopressin?","a":"Low serum sodium with headache or confusion","w":["High serum sodium with thirst","Low blood glucose with sweating","High potassium with weakness"],"x":"Desmopressin causes water retention, which can lead to hyponatremia and seizures. Fluid intake is monitored and sodium checked.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp47f16b68","lv":"rn","d":"normal-saline","sys":"renal","q":"A patient with heart failure is receiving a normal saline bolus. Which finding should the nurse report?","a":"New lung crackles and shortness of breath","w":["Improved urine output","A rise in blood pressure toward baseline","Moist mucous membranes"],"x":"Crackles and dyspnea suggest fluid overload, a risk with isotonic fluids in heart failure. Improved urine output is an expected response.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc441bd42","lv":"rn","d":"oxybutynin","sys":"renal","q":"Which effects should the nurse watch for in an older adult taking oxybutynin?","a":"Confusion, constipation, and dry mouth","w":["Diarrhea and excess saliva","Slow heart rate and wheezing","Low blood glucose and sweating"],"x":"Oxybutynin is anticholinergic, and older adults are especially prone to confusion and falls. Diarrhea and salivation are cholinergic effects, the opposite.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5f1d6f05","lv":"rn","d":"alendronate","sys":"bone","q":"Which teaching is correct for a patient taking oral alendronate?","a":"Take it with plain water on an empty stomach and stay upright","w":["Take it with breakfast and coffee","Lie down afterward to prevent dizziness","Take it together with a calcium supplement"],"x":"Alendronate is poorly absorbed with food or minerals and can irritate the esophagus, so it is taken with plain water and the patient remains upright. Calcium blocks its absorption.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpab0b3807","lv":"rn","d":"alendronate","sys":"bone","q":"Which symptom should a patient taking alendronate report?","a":"New heartburn or pain when swallowing","w":["Mild muscle aches after the first dose","Improved posture over time","Slight increase in appetite"],"x":"Alendronate can cause esophagitis and esophageal ulcers. Heartburn or painful swallowing should be reported promptly.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp990c72a3","lv":"rn","d":"allopurinol","sys":"bone","q":"Which teaching is appropriate for a patient starting allopurinol?","a":"Drink plenty of fluids and report any skin rash","w":["Limit fluids to reduce urination","Take it only during a gout attack","Expect a rash as a normal effect"],"x":"Fluids reduce kidney stone risk, and a rash can signal a severe hypersensitivity reaction such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome. Allopurinol prevents attacks rather than treating an acute flare.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8455248a","lv":"rn","d":"colchicine","sys":"bone","q":"Which early symptoms may signal colchicine toxicity?","a":"Nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea","w":["Constipation and bloating","High blood glucose","Excess hair growth"],"x":"GI symptoms are often the first signs of colchicine toxicity, which is more likely with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as clarithromycin. Constipation is not typical.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp627f80e2","lv":"rn","d":"febuxostat","sys":"bone","q":"Which symptoms should the nurse teach a patient taking febuxostat to report right away?","a":"Chest pain, shortness of breath, or stroke symptoms","w":["Mild joint pain early in therapy","Mild nausea after doses","Slight headache that resolves"],"x":"Febuxostat has a boxed warning for increased cardiovascular death. Gout flares can occur early in therapy and are expected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp409b8fc3","lv":"rn","d":"zoledronic-acid","sys":"bone","q":"Which lab values should be checked before an IV zoledronic acid infusion?","a":"Serum creatinine and calcium","w":["Serum lipase and amylase","Hemoglobin A1c and lipids","INR and aPTT"],"x":"Zoledronic acid can harm the kidneys and lower calcium, so kidney function and calcium are checked and the patient is well hydrated. Clotting tests are unrelated.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp387746fb","lv":"rn","d":"denosumab","sys":"bone","q":"Which adverse effect is a particular concern with denosumab, especially in patients with kidney disease?","a":"Low serum calcium","w":["High serum potassium","Low blood glucose","High serum sodium"],"x":"Denosumab can cause significant hypocalcemia, so calcium is checked and calcium with vitamin D is often given. Potassium is not affected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5616947c","lv":"rn","d":"calcitonin-salmon","sys":"bone","q":"Which teaching is correct for a patient using calcitonin salmon nasal spray?","a":"Alternate nostrils each day","w":["Use the same nostril every day","Tilt the head back and sniff hard","Use it only when bone pain occurs"],"x":"Alternating nostrils reduces nasal irritation, a common side effect. Nasal dryness, crusting, or nosebleeds should be reported.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp69f4f4ae","lv":"rn","d":"raloxifene","sys":"bone","q":"Which teaching is important for a patient taking raloxifene?","a":"Report leg pain or swelling and avoid long periods sitting still","w":["Expect leg swelling as a normal effect","Use it to relieve hot flashes","Take it with a high-fat meal for absorption"],"x":"Raloxifene raises the risk of blood clots, so signs of DVT should be reported and prolonged immobility avoided. It can cause, not relieve, hot flashes.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf70d4c6b","lv":"rn","d":"pegloticase","sys":"bone","q":"Which screening test is needed before the first pegloticase infusion?","a":"G6PD deficiency testing","w":["HLA-B*5801 genotype","Tuberculosis skin test","Hepatitis C antibody"],"x":"Pegloticase can cause hemolysis and methemoglobinemia in G6PD deficiency, so patients at risk are screened. HLA-B*5801 testing relates to allopurinol.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7c498f7e","lv":"rn","d":"n-acetylcysteine","sys":"tox","q":"Acetylcysteine is the antidote for overdose of which medicine?","a":"Acetaminophen","w":["Aspirin","Ibuprofen","Diphenhydramine"],"x":"Acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione, protecting the liver from acetaminophen's toxic metabolite. Aspirin overdose is treated with supportive care and bicarbonate.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb0bf7c7c","lv":"rn","d":"flumazenil","sys":"tox","q":"Which risk is greatest when flumazenil is given to a patient who uses benzodiazepines long term?","a":"Seizures","w":["Respiratory depression","Hypoglycemia","Bradycardia"],"x":"Flumazenil can trigger withdrawal seizures in patients with benzodiazepine dependence or mixed overdoses. It reverses rather than causes respiratory depression.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4e8070d7","lv":"rn","d":"digoxin-immune-fab","sys":"tox","q":"After digoxin immune fab is given for toxicity, which lab change should the nurse watch for?","a":"Serum potassium may fall quickly","w":["Serum sodium may rise sharply","Blood glucose may rise sharply","Platelet count may fall sharply"],"x":"As digoxin is bound, potassium shifts back into cells and can drop, so potassium is monitored closely. Total digoxin levels also become unreliable afterward.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfe9cc08f","lv":"rn","d":"phytonadione","sys":"tox","q":"Which adverse reaction is a concern when phytonadione is given intravenously?","a":"A severe hypersensitivity reaction","w":["Severe hyperglycemia","Acute kidney stones","Rapid hair loss"],"x":"IV vitamin K carries a boxed warning for severe, sometimes fatal hypersensitivity reactions, so it is infused slowly with monitoring. Other routes are preferred when practical.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfe9f78b2","lv":"rn","d":"activated-charcoal","sys":"tox","q":"Which substance is not well bound by activated charcoal?","a":"Lithium","w":["Aspirin","Acetaminophen","Carbamazepine"],"x":"Charcoal binds poorly to metals and ions such as lithium and iron, and to alcohols. Aspirin and acetaminophen are well adsorbed.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb5e62a47","lv":"rn","d":"fomepizole","sys":"tox","q":"Fomepizole is used as an antidote for ingestion of which substance?","a":"Ethylene glycol in antifreeze","w":["Iron tablets","Organophosphate insecticide","Tricyclic antidepressants"],"x":"Fomepizole blocks alcohol dehydrogenase, preventing formation of toxic metabolites of ethylene glycol and methanol. Organophosphates are treated with atropine and pralidoxime.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp5d8e5b7e","lv":"rn","d":"hydroxocobalamin","sys":"tox","q":"After hydroxocobalamin is given for cyanide poisoning, the patient's skin and urine turn red. What does the nurse recognize?","a":"An expected effect of the antidote's red color","w":["A sign of acute kidney failure","A transfusion reaction","Worsening cyanide poisoning"],"x":"Hydroxocobalamin is deep red and commonly discolors skin and urine; it can also interfere with some lab tests. It does not indicate kidney failure.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp59237f34","lv":"rn","d":"atropine","sys":"tox","q":"In organophosphate poisoning, which finding shows atropine is having the desired effect?","a":"Drying of airway secretions and easier breathing","w":["Pinpoint pupils","Increased salivation","Slower heart rate"],"x":"The goal of atropine in organophosphate poisoning is drying of bronchial secretions. Pupil size is not a reliable endpoint.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa39eef9b","lv":"rn","d":"methylene-blue","sys":"tox","q":"Before methylene blue is given for methemoglobinemia, which medication history is a concern?","a":"Use of an SSRI or other serotonergic drug","w":["Use of a daily multivitamin","Use of an inhaled corticosteroid","Use of a topical antifungal"],"x":"Methylene blue inhibits MAO-A and can cause serotonin syndrome with serotonergic drugs. G6PD deficiency is another important concern.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd8159355","lv":"rn","d":"glucagon","sys":"tox","q":"Glucagon may be used as an antidote for which overdose?","a":"Beta blocker overdose","w":["Opioid overdose","Benzodiazepine overdose","Iron overdose"],"x":"Glucagon raises cAMP in heart cells independently of beta receptors, improving heart rate and contractility. Vomiting is common, so airway protection is monitored.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp704463e9","lv":"rn","d":"vancomycin","sys":"infect","q":"During an IV vancomycin infusion, the patient develops flushing and itching of the face, neck, and upper body. What does the nurse recognize?","a":"An infusion reaction linked to fast infusion","w":["Expected effect of a working antibiotic","Ototoxicity from high drug levels","Clostridioides difficile infection"],"x":"Vancomycin infusion reaction is a histamine release related to infusion speed, managed by slowing the infusion and notifying the provider. It is not true anaphylaxis or ototoxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp995eea74","lv":"rn","d":"vancomycin","sys":"infect","q":"Which monitoring is routine for a patient on IV vancomycin?","a":"Vancomycin levels and kidney function","w":["Liver enzymes and ammonia","INR and aPTT","Blood glucose and A1c"],"x":"Vancomycin is nephrotoxic and dosed using vancomycin levels or AUC, so kidney function is followed closely. Liver toxicity is not its main concern.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4127cd58","lv":"rn","d":"gentamicin","sys":"infect","q":"Which symptom should a patient on gentamicin report?","a":"Ringing in the ears, hearing loss, or dizziness","w":["Orange-colored urine","Metallic taste","Darkening of the tongue"],"x":"Aminoglycosides can cause ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity, so hearing changes and gentamicin levels are monitored. Orange urine is linked to rifampin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa3ff342a","lv":"rn","d":"cefazolin","sys":"infect","q":"Before the first dose of IV cefazolin, which assessment is most important?","a":"Ask about drug allergies, including penicillin reactions","w":["Check a fasting blood glucose","Measure abdominal girth","Test deep tendon reflexes"],"x":"Cephalosporins can cause allergic reactions, and a history of severe penicillin reaction raises concern. Allergy assessment comes before any first antibiotic dose.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6a575167","lv":"rn","d":"metronidazole","sys":"infect","q":"Which teaching is essential for a patient taking metronidazole?","a":"Avoid alcohol during therapy and for a few days after","w":["Take it with milk to improve absorption","Expect orange urine as a harmless effect","Use extra sun exposure to speed healing"],"x":"Alcohol with metronidazole can cause a disulfiram-like reaction with flushing, nausea, and vomiting. Orange urine is caused by rifampin, not metronidazole.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpce399336","lv":"rn","d":"ciprofloxacin","sys":"infect","q":"Which symptom should a patient taking ciprofloxacin report right away?","a":"Sudden pain or swelling at the heel or a tendon","w":["Mild nausea after meals","Slight change in taste","Mild headache that resolves"],"x":"Fluoroquinolones carry a boxed warning for tendinitis and tendon rupture, especially in older adults and those on corticosteroids. Mild nausea is common.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp2bdf4837","lv":"rn","d":"isoniazid","sys":"infect","q":"Which vitamin is often given with isoniazid to prevent peripheral neuropathy?","a":"Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","w":["Cyanocobalamin (vitamin B12)","Ascorbic acid (vitamin C)","Phytonadione (vitamin K)"],"x":"Isoniazid depletes pyridoxine, which can cause numbness and tingling. Liver function is also monitored because of hepatotoxicity.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp42cf406f","lv":"rn","d":"rifampin","sys":"infect","q":"Which teaching is appropriate for a patient starting rifampin?","a":"Body fluids may turn orange, and soft contact lenses can stain","w":["Urine may turn blue-green and is a sign of toxicity","It strengthens birth control pills","It must be taken with antacids"],"x":"Rifampin harmlessly colors urine, sweat, and tears orange-red. It reduces, not strengthens, hormonal contraceptive effectiveness.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpa0997632","lv":"rn","d":"clindamycin","sys":"infect","q":"Which symptom should a patient taking clindamycin report, even weeks after finishing?","a":"Frequent watery diarrhea","w":["Mild metallic taste","Darkened urine for a day","Mild injection-site soreness"],"x":"Clindamycin carries a boxed warning for Clostridioides difficile infection, which can appear weeks after therapy. Frequent watery diarrhea needs evaluation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp877184f7","lv":"rn","d":"penicillin-g-benzathine","sys":"infect","q":"Which route is correct for penicillin G benzathine?","a":"Deep intramuscular injection only","w":["Intravenous infusion","Subcutaneous injection","Intravenous push"],"x":"Penicillin G benzathine has a boxed warning that IV use can cause cardiac arrest and death. It is given only by deep IM injection.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp38625518","lv":"rn","d":"insulin-glargine","sys":"endo","q":"Which teaching is correct for insulin glargine?","a":"Do not mix it in the same syringe with other insulins","w":["Draw it up together with regular insulin","Shake it until it looks cloudy","Give it only with high blood glucose"],"x":"Insulin glargine is a clear, long-acting basal insulin that must not be mixed with other insulins. It is given on schedule, not only for high readings.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6d70845f","lv":"rn","d":"insulin-lispro","sys":"endo","q":"When is rapid-acting insulin lispro given relative to meals?","a":"Shortly before a meal, so food follows soon after","w":["At bedtime regardless of food","An hour after finishing a meal","Only for high readings without food"],"x":"Lispro starts working quickly, so it is given shortly before or right after eating to avoid hypoglycemia. Giving it without food raises the risk of low blood glucose.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3489dc8e","lv":"rn","d":"insulin-nph","sys":"endo","q":"When drawing up regular insulin and NPH insulin in one syringe, which order is correct?","a":"Draw up the clear regular insulin before the cloudy NPH","w":["Draw up the cloudy NPH before the clear regular","Mix both in the vial before drawing up","Draw them in either order without difference"],"x":"Drawing clear before cloudy keeps NPH from contaminating the regular insulin vial. NPH is gently rolled, not shaken, before use.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfcb3a629","lv":"rn","d":"metformin","sys":"endo","q":"Which upcoming event should the nurse report to the provider for a patient taking metformin?","a":"A procedure using iodinated contrast dye","w":["A routine dental cleaning","An annual flu vaccine","A scheduled eye exam"],"x":"Iodinated contrast can impair kidney function and increase the risk of metformin-associated lactic acidosis, so the provider decides on timing of doses. Routine dental care does not affect metformin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpefbdfae1","lv":"rn","d":"glipizide","sys":"endo","q":"Which adverse effect is most important to teach a patient taking glipizide?","a":"Low blood glucose, especially if meals are skipped","w":["High blood potassium","Genital yeast infections","Swelling of the lips"],"x":"Sulfonylureas stimulate insulin release and can cause hypoglycemia, especially in older adults or with missed meals. Genital yeast infections are linked to SGLT2 inhibitors.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd5e860a5","lv":"rn","d":"levothyroxine","sys":"endo","q":"Which teaching is correct for a patient taking levothyroxine?","a":"Take it on an empty stomach, apart from calcium or iron","w":["Take it with a calcium supplement at breakfast","Take it with a large meal for absorption","Take it whenever convenient during the day"],"x":"Levothyroxine absorbs best on an empty stomach, and calcium and iron bind it. Taking it with calcium reduces its effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp75a58048","lv":"rn","d":"levothyroxine","sys":"endo","q":"Which findings may indicate that a patient's levothyroxine effect is too strong?","a":"Palpitations, heat intolerance, and weight loss","w":["Fatigue, cold intolerance, and weight gain","Dry skin, constipation, and slow pulse","Puffy face, hoarseness, and drowsiness"],"x":"Excess thyroid hormone mimics hyperthyroidism. Fatigue, cold intolerance, and weight gain suggest the effect is too weak.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc82910bd","lv":"rn","d":"methimazole","sys":"endo","q":"Which symptom should a patient taking methimazole report right away?","a":"Fever or sore throat","w":["Mild itching","Mild weight gain","Slight change in taste"],"x":"Methimazole can cause agranulocytosis, and fever or sore throat may be the first sign. A CBC is checked when these occur.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4db565e3","lv":"rn","d":"semaglutide","sys":"endo","q":"Which history is a contraindication to semaglutide?","a":"Personal or family history of medullary thyroid cancer","w":["Personal history of iron deficiency","Family history of type 2 diabetes","Personal history of seasonal allergies"],"x":"GLP-1 receptor agonists carry a boxed warning for thyroid C-cell tumors and are contraindicated with medullary thyroid cancer or MEN 2. Family history of diabetes is common in those who use it.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp06d8c07a","lv":"rn","d":"glucagon","sys":"endo","q":"After glucagon is given for severe hypoglycemia, the patient wakes and can swallow. What is the next step?","a":"Give oral carbohydrate to prevent glucose dropping again","w":["Give a second glucagon injection right away","Have the patient sleep without eating","Give rapid-acting insulin"],"x":"Glucagon's effect is short-lived, so oral carbohydrate is given once the person can swallow safely. The patient is placed on their side beforehand because vomiting is common.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9ebd78a1","lv":"rn","d":"ondansetron","sys":"gi","q":"Which ECG change is a concern with ondansetron, especially at higher doses or with other risk factors?","a":"QT interval prolongation","w":["ST segment elevation","Peaked T waves","Shortened PR interval"],"x":"Ondansetron can prolong the QT interval, increasing the risk of torsades de pointes. Electrolytes such as potassium and magnesium are corrected before use in at-risk patients.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb0450018","lv":"rn","d":"metoclopramide","sys":"gi","q":"Which finding should a patient taking metoclopramide report?","a":"Involuntary movements of the face, tongue, or limbs","w":["Mild loose stools","Mild drowsiness","Improved appetite"],"x":"Metoclopramide has a boxed warning for tardive dyskinesia, which can be irreversible. Mild drowsiness is common and less concerning.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp136ceb5c","lv":"rn","d":"promethazine","sys":"gi","q":"During IV promethazine administration, which patient complaint is the nurse's priority?","a":"Burning or pain at the IV site","w":["Mild drowsiness","Dry mouth","Mild dizziness"],"x":"Promethazine has a boxed warning for severe tissue injury, including gangrene, from extravasation or inadvertent arterial injection. Drowsiness is expected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp11e6c5e7","lv":"rn","d":"lactulose","sys":"gi","q":"Which outcome shows lactulose is effective for hepatic encephalopathy?","a":"Improved mental status with soft stools","w":["Firm stools once every few days","Rising serum ammonia level","More drowsiness and confusion"],"x":"Lactulose traps ammonia in the colon and removes it in stool, which improves mental status. A rising ammonia level shows it is not working.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp47c6d1dd","lv":"rn","d":"pantoprazole","sys":"gi","q":"Which risks are associated with long-term pantoprazole use?","a":"Low magnesium, bone fractures, and C. difficile diarrhea","w":["High potassium, gout, and kidney stones","Weight loss, tremor, and insomnia","Hair loss, gum overgrowth, and rash"],"x":"Long-term proton pump inhibitor use is linked to hypomagnesemia, fractures, and Clostridioides difficile infection. Gum overgrowth is a phenytoin effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6e65c7a4","lv":"rn","d":"sucralfate","sys":"gi","q":"Which teaching is correct for a patient taking sucralfate?","a":"Take it on an empty stomach, apart from other medicines","w":["Take it with antacids at the same time","Take it immediately after large meals","Chew it together with other pills"],"x":"Sucralfate forms a protective barrier over ulcers and can bind other drugs, reducing their absorption. Antacids taken at the same time interfere with it.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe9a34a69","lv":"rn","d":"misoprostol","sys":"gi","q":"For which patient is misoprostol for NSAID ulcer prevention contraindicated?","a":"A patient who is pregnant","w":["A patient with osteoarthritis","A patient with controlled hypertension","A patient who takes daily aspirin"],"x":"Misoprostol can cause uterine contractions and pregnancy loss, so it is contraindicated in pregnancy for ulcer prevention. Arthritis patients on NSAIDs are its main users.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp214a8e5d","lv":"rn","d":"loperamide","sys":"gi","q":"Which situation should be reported to the provider rather than self-treated with loperamide?","a":"Diarrhea with fever or blood in the stool","w":["Mild diarrhea after a new food","Loose stools for half a day","Diarrhea with mild cramping only"],"x":"Bloody diarrhea or fever can signal an invasive infection, where slowing the gut may cause harm. Mild, short-lived diarrhea is a common use for loperamide.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp05b46c53","lv":"rn","d":"bisacodyl","sys":"gi","q":"Which teaching is correct for bisacodyl enteric-coated tablets?","a":"Swallow whole, and not with milk or antacids","w":["Crush them for a faster effect","Take them with a glass of milk","Chew them with an antacid"],"x":"Milk, antacids, or crushing can dissolve the coating early, causing stomach irritation and cramping. The tablets work in the colon.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp08e89ab1","lv":"rn","d":"pancrelipase","sys":"gi","q":"Which teaching is correct for a patient taking pancrelipase capsules?","a":"Take with every meal and snack, swallowing capsules whole","w":["Take once daily at bedtime","Crush the beads and mix with milk","Take only when stools become oily"],"x":"Pancrelipase replaces pancreatic enzymes for digestion, so it must be taken with food. Crushing or chewing the beads destroys the protective coating.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp1ed94194","lv":"rn","d":"magnesium-sulfate","sys":"ob","q":"Which findings suggest magnesium toxicity in a patient receiving IV magnesium sulfate for preeclampsia?","a":"Absent deep tendon reflexes and slow breathing","w":["Brisk reflexes and ankle clonus","Rising urine output and thirst","Fast heart rate and tremor"],"x":"Loss of reflexes, respiratory depression, and low urine output are signs of magnesium toxicity, along with a high magnesium level. Brisk reflexes and clonus suggest worsening preeclampsia instead.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb86c2ebe","lv":"rn","d":"magnesium-sulfate","sys":"ob","q":"Which medicine should be readily available as the antidote for magnesium sulfate toxicity?","a":"Calcium gluconate","w":["Naloxone","Protamine","Flumazenil"],"x":"IV calcium gluconate counters the effects of magnesium on the heart and neuromuscular junction. Naloxone reverses opioids.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6be0b7eb","lv":"rn","d":"oxytocin","sys":"ob","q":"During an oxytocin infusion for labor, which finding requires immediate action?","a":"Contractions too frequent with a nonreassuring fetal heart rate","w":["Contractions every few minutes with moderate variability","Mild maternal nausea","Gradual cervical change"],"x":"Uterine tachysystole with fetal heart rate changes signals reduced placental blood flow and needs immediate intervention per protocol and provider notification. Regular contractions with moderate variability are reassuring.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd81bb577","lv":"rn","d":"oxytocin","sys":"ob","q":"With prolonged oxytocin use, which complication should the nurse monitor for?","a":"Water intoxication with low serum sodium","w":["High serum potassium","High blood glucose","Low serum calcium"],"x":"Oxytocin has an antidiuretic effect, and prolonged infusion with large amounts of fluid can cause water intoxication and hyponatremia. Headache, confusion, or seizures are warning signs.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpaf356530","lv":"rn","d":"methylergonovine","sys":"ob","q":"Which condition is a contraindication to methylergonovine for postpartum hemorrhage?","a":"Hypertension or preeclampsia","w":["Asthma","Anemia","Gestational diabetes"],"x":"Methylergonovine causes vasoconstriction that can sharply raise blood pressure. Asthma is the key contraindication for carboprost, not methylergonovine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp69b20193","lv":"rn","d":"carboprost","sys":"ob","q":"Which history is a concern before carboprost is given for postpartum hemorrhage?","a":"Asthma","w":["Hypertension","Migraine headaches","Hypothyroidism"],"x":"Carboprost, a prostaglandin, can cause bronchospasm and is avoided in asthma. Hypertension is the key concern with methylergonovine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpf03a1bb2","lv":"rn","d":"rho-d-immune-globulin","sys":"ob","q":"Which patient is a candidate for Rho(D) immune globulin after birth?","a":"An Rh-negative mother with an Rh-positive newborn","w":["An Rh-positive mother with an Rh-negative newborn","An Rh-positive mother with an Rh-positive newborn","An Rh-negative mother with an Rh-negative newborn"],"x":"Rho(D) immune globulin prevents an Rh-negative mother from forming antibodies after exposure to Rh-positive fetal blood. It is given within 72 hours of birth.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe86b20fe","lv":"rn","d":"terbutaline","sys":"ob","q":"Which maternal assessment is the priority when terbutaline is given?","a":"Heart rate, since tachycardia is common","w":["Deep tendon reflexes","Blood glucose for hypoglycemia","Urine for protein"],"x":"Terbutaline is a beta agonist that causes maternal tachycardia and can cause serious cardiac effects; it carries a boxed warning against prolonged use for preterm labor. It tends to raise, not lower, glucose.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpb0229582","lv":"rn","d":"betamethasone","sys":"ob","q":"Why is betamethasone given to a patient in preterm labor?","a":"To speed fetal lung maturity before birth","w":["To stop uterine contractions","To prevent maternal seizures","To prevent newborn eye infection"],"x":"Antenatal corticosteroids speed fetal lung maturation and reduce neonatal respiratory distress. They do not relax the uterus.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpe2cb6cb6","lv":"rn","d":"erythromycin-ophthalmic","sys":"ob","q":"Why is erythromycin ophthalmic ointment applied to the newborn's eyes after birth?","a":"To prevent gonococcal eye infection","w":["To prevent neonatal jaundice","To treat newborn conjunctival hemorrhage","To promote tear production"],"x":"Erythromycin eye ointment prevents gonococcal ophthalmia neonatorum. It does not affect jaundice.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp839252c0","lv":"rn","d":"epinephrine-auto-injector","sys":"immune","q":"Which teaching is correct for using an epinephrine auto-injector?","a":"Inject into the outer thigh, then seek emergency care","w":["Inject into the upper arm muscle, then rest","Inject into a vein for faster action","Inject only after an antihistamine fails"],"x":"The auto-injector is given into the anterolateral thigh, even through clothing, and emergency care is needed because symptoms can return. Waiting for an antihistamine delays life-saving treatment.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp91d6c157","lv":"rn","d":"diphenhydramine","sys":"immune","q":"Which effects should the nurse watch for when an older adult takes diphenhydramine?","a":"Confusion, urinary retention, and falls","w":["Diarrhea and excess saliva","Insomnia and agitation only","Low blood pressure and wheezing"],"x":"Diphenhydramine is strongly anticholinergic and sedating, which raises the risk of confusion and falls in older adults. Diarrhea and salivation are the opposite effects.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9cc34601","lv":"rn","d":"methotrexate","sys":"immune","q":"Which teaching is most important for a patient taking oral methotrexate for rheumatoid arthritis?","a":"It is usually taken once a week, not daily","w":["It is taken every day with breakfast","Extra doses help during a flare","Alcohol is safe in any amount"],"x":"Daily dosing errors with weekly methotrexate have caused fatal toxicity. Alcohol increases liver toxicity, and blood counts and liver tests are monitored.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdd573b89","lv":"rn","d":"adalimumab","sys":"immune","q":"Which screening is required before a patient starts adalimumab?","a":"Tuberculosis screening","w":["Bone density scan","Hearing test","Thyroid ultrasound"],"x":"TNF inhibitors carry a boxed warning for serious infections, including reactivation of latent tuberculosis. Hepatitis B screening is also done.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp16265d84","lv":"rn","d":"infliximab","sys":"immune","q":"During an infliximab infusion, which findings should the nurse report?","a":"Fever, chills, hives, or shortness of breath","w":["Mild fatigue after the infusion","Improved joint stiffness","Slight warmth at the IV site"],"x":"Infliximab can cause infusion reactions ranging from mild to anaphylaxis. Improved stiffness is the desired effect.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0db3bf4f","lv":"rn","d":"cyclosporine","sys":"immune","q":"Which monitoring is routine for a transplant patient taking cyclosporine?","a":"Kidney function, blood pressure, and cyclosporine levels","w":["Hearing tests and visual fields","Bone density and vitamin D","Uric acid and lipase"],"x":"Cyclosporine is nephrotoxic, raises blood pressure, and has a narrow therapeutic range, so cyclosporine levels guide therapy. Grapefruit juice can raise levels.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpba4ef89c","lv":"rn","d":"tacrolimus","sys":"immune","q":"Which adverse effects are common with tacrolimus?","a":"Tremor, high blood glucose, and kidney injury","w":["Low blood pressure and weight loss","Gum overgrowth and hair growth","Bradycardia and wheezing"],"x":"Tacrolimus can cause neurotoxicity such as tremor, new-onset diabetes, and nephrotoxicity, and tacrolimus levels are monitored. Gum overgrowth and excess hair are more typical of cyclosporine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp6b700cd7","lv":"rn","d":"hydroxychloroquine","sys":"immune","q":"Which monitoring is recommended for a patient taking hydroxychloroquine long term?","a":"Regular eye exams for retinal damage","w":["Monthly hearing tests","Weekly INR checks","Yearly bone density scans"],"x":"Hydroxychloroquine can cause irreversible retinal toxicity, so baseline and periodic eye exams are recommended. Hearing loss is not a typical concern.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpba59297f","lv":"rn","d":"mycophenolate","sys":"immune","q":"Which teaching is essential for a patient of childbearing potential taking mycophenolate?","a":"It can cause pregnancy loss and birth defects, so reliable contraception is needed","w":["It is safe during pregnancy","It improves fertility","It only matters for male patients"],"x":"Mycophenolate has a boxed warning for embryofetal toxicity and a REMS program requiring pregnancy testing and contraception. It is not safe in pregnancy.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0c17e55a","lv":"rn","d":"azathioprine","sys":"immune","q":"Which adverse effect requires monitoring of blood counts in a patient taking azathioprine?","a":"Bone marrow suppression","w":["Hyperkalemia","Hypoglycemia","Hypercalcemia"],"x":"Azathioprine can suppress the bone marrow, especially in patients with low TPMT or NUDT15 activity. CBCs are monitored regularly.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp09083541","lv":"rn","d":"vinblastine","sys":"onc","q":"Which route is correct for vinblastine?","a":"Intravenous only; it is fatal if given intrathecally","w":["Intrathecal injection into the spinal fluid","Intramuscular injection into the thigh","Subcutaneous injection into the abdomen"],"x":"Vinca alkaloids carry a boxed warning that intrathecal administration is fatal and must be labeled for IV use only. It is also a vesicant, so extravasation is watched for.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp391bde87","lv":"rn","d":"docetaxel","sys":"onc","q":"Why is a corticosteroid premedication given before docetaxel?","a":"To reduce fluid retention and hypersensitivity reactions","w":["To prevent hair loss","To raise the white blood cell count","To prevent hemorrhagic cystitis"],"x":"Corticosteroid premedication lowers the risk and severity of fluid retention and hypersensitivity reactions with docetaxel. It does not prevent hair loss.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp54d321f2","lv":"rn","d":"tamoxifen","sys":"onc","q":"Which symptom should a patient taking tamoxifen report?","a":"Abnormal vaginal bleeding or one-sided leg swelling","w":["Mild hot flashes","Mild joint stiffness","Mild nausea"],"x":"Tamoxifen increases the risk of uterine cancer and blood clots. Hot flashes are common and expected.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp365c353e","lv":"rn","d":"capecitabine","sys":"onc","q":"Which adverse effect should a patient taking capecitabine report?","a":"Redness, swelling, or pain on the palms and soles","w":["Mild fatigue","Slight loss of appetite","Darker fingernails"],"x":"Capecitabine commonly causes hand-foot syndrome, which can limit therapy if not addressed early. Diarrhea is another important effect to report.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp4302c25a","lv":"rn","d":"ifosfamide","sys":"onc","q":"Which finding is a key concern with ifosfamide?","a":"Blood in the urine","w":["Orange urine","Ringing in the ears","Gum overgrowth"],"x":"Ifosfamide can cause hemorrhagic cystitis, which mesna and hydration help prevent. Neurotoxicity such as confusion is another concern.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp7be2234b","lv":"rn","d":"nivolumab","sys":"onc","q":"Which symptoms should a patient receiving nivolumab report promptly?","a":"New diarrhea, cough, or rash","w":["Mild fatigue after infusions","Slight decrease in appetite","Mild joint aches"],"x":"Immune checkpoint inhibitors can cause immune-related inflammation of the colon, lungs, skin, and other organs. These effects can become serious if not treated early.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp3513ced6","lv":"rn","d":"irinotecan","sys":"onc","q":"Which adverse effect of irinotecan needs prompt reporting?","a":"Diarrhea, which can become severe","w":["Hiccups","Taste changes","Mild hair thinning"],"x":"Irinotecan causes early and late diarrhea that can lead to dehydration and electrolyte loss. Early cholinergic diarrhea may be treated with atropine.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp83a3ab0e","lv":"rn","d":"oxaliplatin","sys":"onc","q":"Which teaching is appropriate for a patient receiving oxaliplatin?","a":"Avoid cold drinks and cold objects for several days after","w":["Use ice chips during the infusion","Drink only cold fluids that day","Apply ice packs to the hands"],"x":"Oxaliplatin causes cold-triggered peripheral neuropathy and throat discomfort. Ice chips, used with some other drugs, can worsen these symptoms.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpdac51c26","lv":"rn","d":"trastuzumab-deruxtecan","sys":"onc","q":"Which symptoms should a patient receiving trastuzumab deruxtecan report right away?","a":"New cough, shortness of breath, or fever","w":["Mild hair thinning","Mild nausea after infusions","Mild fatigue"],"x":"Trastuzumab deruxtecan has a boxed warning for interstitial lung disease, which can be fatal. New respiratory symptoms need prompt evaluation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp9c934ea9","lv":"rn","d":"bcg-live-intravesical","sys":"onc","q":"Which teaching is appropriate after intravesical BCG treatment?","a":"Sit to urinate and add bleach to the toilet before flushing","w":["Stand to urinate and flush right away","Avoid drinking any fluids that day","Hold urine for a full day"],"x":"BCG is a live organism, so urine is disinfected with bleach for several hours after treatment to protect others. Sitting reduces splashing.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp05315dc7","lv":"rn","d":"isotretinoin","sys":"skin","q":"Which requirement applies to a patient who can become pregnant and is starting isotretinoin?","a":"Use two forms of birth control and complete pregnancy tests","w":["Use one form of birth control as needed","Take a pregnancy test only once at the end","Avoid contraception to monitor cycles"],"x":"Isotretinoin causes severe birth defects, and the iPLEDGE program requires two forms of contraception and regular pregnancy testing. A single test at the end is not enough.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp8ac5d76a","lv":"rn","d":"timolol-ophthalmic","sys":"skin","q":"Which technique reduces systemic effects of timolol eye drops?","a":"Press on the inner corner of the eye after instilling","w":["Blink rapidly after instilling the drops","Tilt the head forward after instilling","Rub the eye gently after instilling"],"x":"Punctal occlusion limits drainage into the nose and bloodstream, reducing beta-blocker effects such as bradycardia and bronchospasm. Rubbing the eye does not help.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpfb7d4178","lv":"rn","d":"latanoprost","sys":"skin","q":"Which effect should a patient using latanoprost be taught about?","a":"Permanent darkening of iris color","w":["Temporary loss of eyelashes","Constricted pupils at night","Yellow-tinted vision"],"x":"Prostaglandin analogs can increase brown pigment in the iris, which may be permanent, and can lengthen eyelashes. They do not cause eyelash loss.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp74f14275","lv":"rn","d":"silver-sulfadiazine","sys":"skin","q":"Which allergy is a concern before applying silver sulfadiazine to a burn?","a":"Sulfonamide drug allergy","w":["Latex allergy","Iodine allergy","Egg allergy"],"x":"Silver sulfadiazine is a sulfonamide and can cause reactions in sulfa-allergic patients. It can also cause transient leukopenia.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp96e094d3","lv":"rn","d":"permethrin","sys":"skin","q":"Which teaching is appropriate when permethrin cream is prescribed for scabies?","a":"Treat close contacts and wash bedding in hot water","w":["Treat only the itchy areas","Reapply every day for a month","No cleaning of bedding is needed"],"x":"Scabies spreads by close contact, so household members are treated and bedding and clothing are washed hot. Itching may persist for weeks even after successful treatment.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc613d30a","lv":"rn","d":"clobetasol","sys":"skin","q":"Which teaching is correct for clobetasol topical cream?","a":"Avoid the face, groin, and tight coverings","w":["Apply it thickly under plastic wrap","Use it on the face for best results","Use it continuously for many months"],"x":"Clobetasol is a very high-potency corticosteroid that can cause skin thinning and systemic absorption. Occlusion and thin-skin areas increase these risks.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpc851bc4f","lv":"rn","d":"tretinoin-topical","sys":"skin","q":"Which teaching is appropriate for a patient using topical tretinoin?","a":"Use sunscreen and limit sun exposure","w":["Apply it right before sunbathing","Use it with harsh scrubs for faster results","Expect no skin irritation"],"x":"Tretinoin increases sensitivity to sunlight and commonly causes irritation at first. Harsh scrubs worsen irritation.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp978533e0","lv":"rn","d":"lidocaine-patch","sys":"skin","q":"Which teaching is correct for a lidocaine patch?","a":"Avoid heat over the patch and fold used patches before disposal","w":["Cover the patch with a heating pad","Apply it to broken or irritated skin","Leave used patches where pets can reach"],"x":"Heat increases absorption, and used patches still contain drug that can harm children and pets. Patches are applied only to intact skin.","pilot":1},{"id":"qp0eb9c321","lv":"rn","d":"pilocarpine-ophthalmic","sys":"skin","q":"Which teaching is appropriate for a patient using pilocarpine eye drops?","a":"Use caution driving at night because vision may be dim","w":["Expect wider pupils and light sensitivity","Expect improved night vision","Use the drops only when eyes are dry"],"x":"Pilocarpine constricts the pupils, which reduces light entry and can make night vision dim or blurred. Pupil dilation is caused by anticholinergic drops.","pilot":1},{"id":"qpd6b4db0c","lv":"rn","d":"prednisolone-acetate-ophthalmic","sys":"skin","q":"Which teaching is correct for prednisolone acetate eye drops?","a":"Shake the bottle well before each use","w":["Store the bottle in the freezer","Rinse the eye right after instilling","Share the bottle with family members"],"x":"Prednisolone acetate is a suspension, so shaking ensures an even dose. Long-term use can raise eye pressure, so follow-up eye exams are needed.","pilot":1}];
